(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqCe=[{pn:1,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 3 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cres",st:"Resistance and resistivity",q:"A wire of length 25 m and cross-sectional area 5 mm\xB2 having resistivity of 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \u03A9 m is bent into a complete circle. The resistance between diametrically opposite points will be",o:["12.5 \u03A9","100 \u03A9","50 \u03A9","25 \u03A9"],c:3,s:`Note: the working gives 2.5 \u03A9; option (d) prints 25 \u03A9, which looks like a misprint of 2.5 \u03A9, and no key is printed.
Resistance of the whole wire: R = \u03C1L/A, because resistance grows with length and falls with area.
A = 5 mm\xB2 = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB2, so R = (2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 25)/(5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076) = 10 \u03A9.
Two diametrically opposite points split the circle into two equal halves, each of 5 \u03A9.
The two halves connect the same two points, so they are in parallel.
R(eq) = (5 \xD7 5)/(5 + 5) = 2.5 \u03A9.
So the result is 2.5 \u03A9, which matches option (d) only if it is read as 2.5 \u03A9.`},{pn:2,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cdrf",st:"Current and charge",q:"Current passing through a wire as function of time is given as I(t) = 0.02 t + 0.01 A. The charge that will flow through the wire from t = 1 s to t = 2 s is",o:["0.07 C","0.04 C","0.06 C","0.02 C"],c:1,s:`Current is the rate of flow of charge, I = dq/dt, so the charge is the area under the I-t graph: q = \u222B I dt.
q = \u222B (0.02 t + 0.01) dt from t = 1 to t = 2.
The integral of 0.02 t is 0.01 t\xB2, and the integral of 0.01 is 0.01 t.
At t = 2: 0.01 \xD7 4 + 0.01 \xD7 2 = 0.04 + 0.02 = 0.06 C.
At t = 1: 0.01 \xD7 1 + 0.01 \xD7 1 = 0.02 C.
Charge = 0.06 \u2212 0.02 = 0.04 C, which is option (b).`},{pn:3,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ccmb",st:"Series and parallel combinations",q:"From the combination of resistors with resistances values R\u2081 = R\u2082 = R\u2083 = 5 \u03A9 and R\u2084 = 10 \u03A9, which of the following combination is the best circuit to get an equivalent resistance of 6 \u03A9?",c:0,s:`Work out the equivalent resistance of each circuit: series resistances add, parallel ones add as reciprocals.
(a) R\u2081 + R\u2082 = 10 \u03A9 in one branch and R\u2083 + R\u2084 = 15 \u03A9 in the other; these two branches are in parallel.
(10 \xD7 15)/(10 + 15) = 150/25 = 6 \u03A9, which is what we want.
(b) All four in parallel: 1/5 + 1/5 + 1/5 + 1/10 = 0.7, so R = 1.43 \u03A9.
(c) R\u2081, (R\u2082 + R\u2083) = 10 \u03A9 and R\u2084 in parallel: 1/5 + 1/10 + 1/10 = 0.4, so R = 2.5 \u03A9.
(d) R\u2081 + R\u2082 + R\u2084 = 20 \u03A9 in parallel with R\u2083 = 5 \u03A9 gives (20 \xD7 5)/25 = 4 \u03A9.
Only circuit (a) gives 6 \u03A9, so the answer is option (a).`,fig:{w:400,h:300,alt:"Four combinations of the resistors R1, R2, R3 and R4: (a) R1 with R2 in series, in parallel with R3 with R4 in series; (b) all four in parallel; (c) R1 in parallel with R2 and R3 in series and with R4; (d) R1, R2 and R4 in series, in parallel with R3",d:[["t",20,74,"(a)","e"],["c",34,70,3.2],["l",37,70,44,70],["l",44,50,44,90],["l",44,50,54,50],["l",54,50,61,50],["l",91,50,98,50],["p","M61 50L66 55L71 45L76 55L81 45L86 55L91 50"],["l",98,50,105,50],["l",135,50,142,50],["p","M105 50L110 55L115 45L120 55L125 45L130 55L135 50"],["l",142,50,152,50],["l",44,90,54,90],["l",54,90,61,90],["l",91,90,98,90],["p","M61 90L66 95L71 85L76 95L81 85L86 95L91 90"],["l",98,90,105,90],["l",135,90,142,90],["p","M105 90L110 95L115 85L120 95L125 85L130 95L135 90"],["l",142,90,152,90],["l",152,50,152,90],["l",152,70,159,70],["c",162,70,3.2],["t",76,40,"R\u2081","m"],["t",120,40,"R\u2082","m"],["t",76,108,"R\u2083","m"],["t",120,108,"R\u2084","m"],["c",44,70,2.6,"F"],["c",152,70,2.6,"F"],["t",222,86,"(b)","e"],["c",236,82,3.2],["l",239,82,246,82],["l",246,38,246,126],["l",354,38,354,126],["l",354,82,361,82],["c",364,82,3.2],["c",246,82,2.6,"F"],["c",354,82,2.6,"F"],["l",246,38,256,38],["l",256,38,285,38],["l",315,38,344,38],["p","M285 38L290 43L295 33L300 43L305 33L310 43L315 38"],["l",344,38,354,38],["t",300,29,"R\u2081","m"],["l",246,66,256,66],["l",256,66,285,66],["l",315,66,344,66],["p","M285 66L290 71L295 61L300 71L305 61L310 71L315 66"],["l",344,66,354,66],["t",300,57,"R\u2082","m"],["l",246,96,256,96],["l",256,96,285,96],["l",315,96,344,96],["p","M285 96L290 101L295 91L300 101L305 91L310 101L315 96"],["l",344,96,354,96],["t",300,87,"R\u2083","m"],["l",246,126,256,126],["l",256,126,285,126],["l",315,126,344,126],["p","M285 126L290 131L295 121L300 131L305 121L310 131L315 126"],["l",344,126,354,126],["t",300,117,"R\u2084","m"],["t",20,200,"(c)","e"],["c",34,196,3.2],["l",37,196,44,196],["l",44,160,44,232],["l",152,160,152,232],["l",152,196,159,196],["c",162,196,3.2],["c",44,196,2.6,"F"],["c",152,196,2.6,"F"],["l",44,160,54,160],["l",54,160,83,160],["l",113,160,142,160],["p","M83 160L88 165L93 155L98 165L103 155L108 165L113 160"],["l",142,160,152,160],["t",98,151,"R\u2081","m"],["l",44,196,54,196],["l",54,196,61,196],["l",91,196,98,196],["p","M61 196L66 201L71 191L76 201L81 191L86 201L91 196"],["l",98,196,105,196],["l",135,196,142,196],["p","M105 196L110 201L115 191L120 201L125 191L130 201L135 196"],["l",142,196,152,196],["t",76,187,"R\u2082","m"],["t",120,187,"R\u2083","m"],["l",44,232,54,232],["l",54,232,83,232],["l",113,232,142,232],["p","M83 232L88 237L93 227L98 237L103 227L108 237L113 232"],["l",142,232,152,232],["t",98,249,"R\u2084","m"],["t",192,200,"(d)","e"],["c",206,196,3.2],["l",209,196,216,196],["l",216,170,216,222],["l",216,170,226,170],["l",226,170,233,170],["l",263,170,270,170],["p","M233 170L238 175L243 165L248 175L253 165L258 175L263 170"],["l",270,170,277,170],["l",307,170,314,170],["p","M277 170L282 175L287 165L292 175L297 165L302 175L307 170"],["l",314,170,321,170],["l",351,170,358,170],["p","M321 170L326 175L331 165L336 175L341 165L346 175L351 170"],["l",358,170,368,170],["l",368,170,368,222],["l",368,196,375,196],["c",378,196,3.2],["c",216,196,2.6,"F"],["c",368,196,2.6,"F"],["l",216,222,226,222],["l",226,222,277,222],["l",307,222,358,222],["p","M277 222L282 227L287 217L292 227L297 217L302 227L307 222"],["l",358,222,368,222],["t",248,160,"R\u2081","m"],["t",292,160,"R\u2082","m"],["t",336,160,"R\u2084","m"],["t",292,240,"R\u2083","m"]]},o:["Circuit (a)","Circuit (b)","Circuit (c)","Circuit (d)"]},{pn:4,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 22 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cres",st:"Temperature dependence",q:"Which of the following resistivity (\u03C1) v/s temperature (T) curves is most suitable to be used in wire bound standard resistors?",c:0,s:`A standard resistor must keep the same resistance when the temperature changes a little, so its resistivity should hardly depend on T.
Alloys such as manganin and constantan have a very small temperature coefficient, so their \u03C1-T graph is an almost flat line with a tiny upward slope.
Graph (a) is the nearly flat line. Graph (b) falls steadily, graph (c) rises steeply and graph (d) falls steeply, so these change too much with T.
So the most suitable curve is (a).`,fig:{w:390,h:285,alt:"Four graphs of resistivity rho against temperature T: (a) a straight line with a positive intercept and a very small upward slope; (b) a straight line falling; (c) a curve rising slowly and then steeply; (d) a curve falling steeply and then flattening",d:[["t",30,86,"(a)","e"],["l",52,120,52,32,"a"],["l",52,120,166,120,"a"],["t",47,132,"O","e"],["t",170,124,"T (K)"],["t",52,25,"\u03C1 (\u03A9 cm)","m"],["p","M52 68L152 58"],["t",210,86,"(b)","e"],["l",232,120,232,32,"a"],["l",232,120,346,120,"a"],["t",227,132,"O","e"],["t",350,124,"T (K)"],["t",232,25,"\u03C1 (\u03A9 cm)","m"],["p","M232 44L332 110"],["t",30,228,"(c)","e"],["l",52,262,52,174,"a"],["l",52,262,166,262,"a"],["t",47,274,"O","e"],["t",170,266,"T (K)"],["t",52,167,"\u03C1 (\u03A9 cm)","m"],["p","M52 256C107 254 140 236 152 188"],["t",210,228,"(d)","e"],["l",232,262,232,174,"a"],["l",232,262,346,262,"a"],["t",227,274,"O","e"],["t",350,266,"T (K)"],["t",232,167,"\u03C1 (\u03A9 cm)","m"],["p","M236 184C246 228 276 250 336 256"]]},o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"]},{pn:5,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 23 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"crc",st:"Charging and discharging of a capacitor",q:`Identify the valid statements relevant to the given circuit at the instant when the key is closed.
A. There will be no current through resistor R.
B. There will be maximum current in the connecting wires.
C. Potential difference between the capacitor plates A and B is minimum.
D. Charge on the capacitor plates is minimum.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["A, B, D only","A, C only","B, C, D only","C, D only"],c:2,s:`At the instant the key is closed the capacitor is uncharged, so it holds no charge and no potential difference.
So C and D are true: charge and potential difference across the plates are at their minimum (zero).
With zero voltage across the capacitor, the whole 5 V falls on R, so the current is I = 5/R, the largest it will ever be.
So B is true (maximum current in the wires) and A is false (current does flow through R).
As charge builds up the capacitor voltage rises and the current falls to zero.
The true statements are B, C and D, option (c).`,fig:{w:330,h:150,alt:"A series circuit: a resistor R, an open key, a 5 V cell and a capacitor with plates A (upper) and B (lower) and air between them",d:[["l",60,30,100,30],["l",100,30,110,30],["l",140,30,150,30],["p","M110 30L115 35L120 25L125 35L130 25L135 35L140 30"],["l",150,30,170,30],["c",170,30,2.6,"F"],["l",170,30,196,12],["c",200,30,3.2],["l",203,30,270,30],["l",270,30,270,62],["l",270,62,270,75],["l",270,81,270,94],["l",260,75,280,75],["l",265,81,275,81,"B"],["l",270,94,270,120],["l",60,30,60,56],["l",46,56,74,56],["l",46,68,74,68],["l",60,68,60,120],["l",60,120,270,120],["t",125,20,"R","m"],["t",188,50,"Key","m"],["t",80,61,"A","s"],["t",80,76,"B","s"],["t",41,66,"Air","e"],["t",292,82,"5 V"]]}},{pn:6,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 28 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ccmb",st:"Equivalent resistance of networks",q:"A wire of resistance R is bent into an equilateral triangle and an identical wire is bent into a square. The ratio of resistance between the two end points of an edge of the triangle to that of the square is",o:["27/32","32/27","9/8","8/9"],c:1,s:`Equal wires keep the same total resistance R, shared equally among the sides.
Triangle: each side is R/3. Between the ends of one edge, that edge (R/3) is in parallel with the other two sides in series (2R/3).
R(tri) = (R/3 \xD7 2R/3)/(R/3 + 2R/3) = (2R\xB2/9)/R = 2R/9.
Square: each side is R/4. One edge (R/4) is in parallel with the other three (3R/4).
R(sq) = (R/4 \xD7 3R/4)/R = 3R/16.
Ratio = (2R/9)/(3R/16) = 32/27, option (b).`},{pn:7,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 28 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ccmb",st:"Balanced networks and symmetry",q:"Find the equivalent resistance between two ends of the following circuit",o:["r/6","r/9","r","r/3"],c:1,s:`Look at which points are joined by plain wires: the left end is joined by a wire to the point between the second and third resistors, and the right end is joined by a wire to the point between the first and second.
Call the left end a and the right end b. Then the left end of the first resistor is at a, and its right end is joined to b.
The second resistor has its left end at b and its right end at a. The third has its left end at a and its right end at b.
So all three resistors of r/3 sit between the same two points a and b, which means they are in parallel (see the redrawn figure).
Three equal resistors r/3 in parallel give (r/3)/3 = r/9.
The answer is r/9, option (b).`,fig:{w:300,h:140,alt:"A main line with three resistors of r/3 in series; one wire joins the left end to the point between the second and third resistors, another joins the point between the first and second resistors to the right end",d:[["c",24,60,3.2],["l",27,60,50,60],["l",50,60,65,60],["l",95,60,110,60],["p","M65 60L70 65L75 55L80 65L85 55L90 65L95 60"],["l",110,60,125,60],["l",155,60,170,60],["p","M125 60L130 65L135 55L140 65L145 55L150 65L155 60"],["l",170,60,185,60],["l",215,60,230,60],["p","M185 60L190 65L195 55L200 65L205 55L210 65L215 60"],["l",230,60,253,60],["c",256,60,3.2],["l",50,60,50,24],["l",50,24,170,24],["l",170,24,170,60],["l",110,60,110,100],["l",110,100,230,100],["l",230,100,230,60],["c",50,60,2.6,"F"],["c",110,60,2.6,"F"],["c",170,60,2.6,"F"],["c",230,60,2.6,"F"],["t",80,76,"r/3","m"],["t",140,76,"r/3","m"],["t",200,50,"r/3","m"]]},sfig:{w:300,h:150,alt:"The same circuit redrawn: three resistors of r/3 in parallel between point a on the left and point b on the right",d:[["c",24,75,3.2],["t",24,62,"a","m"],["l",27,75,80,75],["l",80,30,80,120],["l",220,30,220,120],["l",220,75,273,75],["c",276,75,3.2],["t",276,62,"b","m"],["c",80,75,2.6,"F"],["c",220,75,2.6,"F"],["l",80,30,90,30],["l",90,30,135,30],["l",165,30,210,30],["p","M135 30L140 35L145 25L150 35L155 25L160 35L165 30"],["l",210,30,220,30],["t",150,20,"r/3","m"],["l",80,75,90,75],["l",90,75,135,75],["l",165,75,210,75],["p","M135 75L140 80L145 70L150 80L155 70L160 80L165 75"],["l",210,75,220,75],["t",150,65,"r/3","m"],["l",80,120,90,120],["l",90,120,135,120],["l",165,120,210,120],["p","M135 120L140 125L145 115L150 125L155 115L160 125L165 120"],["l",210,120,220,120],["t",150,110,"r/3","m"]]}},{pn:8,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cres",st:"Temperature dependence",q:"The resistances of the platinum wire of a platinum resistance thermometer at the ice point and steam point are 8 \u03A9 and 10 \u03A9 respectively. After inserting in a hot bath of temperature 400\xB0C, the resistance of platinum wire is",o:["2 \u03A9","10 \u03A9","8 \u03A9","16 \u03A9"],c:3,s:`For a wire, resistance changes linearly with temperature: R = R\u2080(1 + \u03B1t), where t is in \xB0C and R\u2080 is the resistance at 0 \xB0C (ice point).
R\u2080 = 8 \u03A9. At the steam point (100 \xB0C): 10 = 8(1 + 100\u03B1), so 100\u03B1 = 2/8 = 0.25 and \u03B1 = 1/400 per \xB0C.
At 400 \xB0C: R = 8(1 + 400/400) = 8 \xD7 2 = 16 \u03A9.
The resistance is 16 \u03A9, option (d).`},{pn:9,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 5 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ccmb",st:"Equivalent resistance of networks",q:"In the given figure R\u2081 = 10 \u03A9, R\u2082 = 8 \u03A9, R\u2083 = 4 \u03A9 and R\u2084 = 8 \u03A9. Battery is ideal with emf 12 V. Equivalent resistance of the circuit and current supplied by battery are respectively",o:["12 \u03A9 and 1 A","10.5 \u03A9 and 1 A","12 \u03A9 and 11.4 A","10.5 \u03A9 and 1.14 A"],c:0,s:`R\u2082, R\u2083 and R\u2084 all join the same top wire and the same bottom wire (R\u2084 runs diagonally between them, joining the top-right corner to the bottom node), so they are in parallel.
1/R = 1/8 + 1/4 + 1/8 = (1 + 2 + 1)/8 = 1/2, so R = 2 \u03A9.
This 2 \u03A9 is in series with R\u2081 = 10 \u03A9, so R(eq) = 10 + 2 = 12 \u03A9.
Current from the battery: I = V/R = 12/12 = 1 A.
So the answer is 12 \u03A9 and 1 A, option (a).`,fig:{w:330,h:190,alt:"A 12 V cell in series with R1; R2 and R3 are vertical resistors and R4 a slanting resistor, all three connected between the top and the bottom wire",d:[["l",60,40,60,79],["l",60,79,60,92],["l",60,98,60,111],["l",50,92,70,92],["l",55,98,65,98,"B"],["l",60,111,60,160],["l",60,160,260,160],["l",60,40,90,40],["l",90,40,105,40],["l",135,40,150,40],["p","M105 40L110 45L115 35L120 45L125 35L130 45L135 40"],["l",150,40,260,40],["l",160,40,160,83],["l",160,117,160,160],["p","M160 83L155 88.7L165 94.3L155 100L165 105.7L155 111.3L160 117"],["l",260,40,260,83],["l",260,117,260,160],["p","M260 83L255 88.7L265 94.3L255 100L265 105.7L255 111.3L260 117"],["l",260,40,220.9,86.9],["l",199.1,113.1,160,160],["p","M220.9 86.9L213.4 88.1L217.5 98.8L206.2 96.8L210.2 107.6L198.9 105.5L199.1 113.1"],["c",160,40,2.6,"F"],["c",160,160,2.6,"F"],["c",260,40,2.6,"F"],["t",120,29,"R\u2081","m"],["t",150,104,"R\u2082","e"],["t",272,104,"R\u2083"],["t",218,74,"R\u2084","e"],["t",50,90,"12 V","e"],["t",70,82,"+","s"]]},sfig:{w:300,h:150,alt:"Equivalent circuit: the 12 V cell in series with R1 = 10 ohm and one resistor of 2 ohm",d:[["l",50,30,50,55],["l",50,55,50,68],["l",50,74,50,87],["l",40,68,60,68],["l",45,74,55,74,"B"],["l",50,87,50,120],["l",50,120,250,120],["l",50,30,90,30],["l",90,30,112,30],["l",148,30,170,30],["p","M112 30L118 35L124 25L130 35L136 25L142 35L148 30"],["l",170,30,250,30],["l",250,30,250,57],["l",250,93,250,120],["p","M250 57L245 63L255 69L245 75L255 81L245 87L250 93"],["t",130,18,"10 \u03A9","m"],["t",262,80,"2 \u03A9"],["t",40,66,"12 V","e"]]}},{pn:10,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ccmb",st:"Balanced networks and symmetry",q:"The equivalent resistance between A and B is",o:["25 \u03A9","18 \u03A9","27 \u03A9","19 \u03A9"],c:3,s:`Mark the points joined by plain wires. The vertical wire joins the middle of the top row and the middle of the bottom row, so those two points are one node, T. The horizontal wire joins the middles of the left and right columns, so those are one node, L. (The arch means the two wires cross without touching.)
A connects through the 6 \u03A9 resistor to T, and B connects through the 8 \u03A9 resistor to L.
Between T and L there are three paths: 10 \u03A9 + 5 \u03A9 = 15 \u03A9 on the top right, 8 \u03A9 + 7 \u03A9 = 15 \u03A9 on the bottom right, and 11 \u03A9 + 4 \u03A9 = 15 \u03A9 on the bottom left.
Three equal 15 \u03A9 paths in parallel give 15/3 = 5 \u03A9.
Total = 6 + 5 + 8 = 19 \u03A9, option (d).`,fig:{w:340,h:240,alt:"A square network between terminals A and B at the top-left corner: top row 6 ohm and 10 ohm, right column 5 ohm and 7 ohm, bottom row 8 ohm and 11 ohm, left column 4 ohm and 8 ohm; a vertical wire joins the middle of the top and bottom rows; a horizontal wire joins the middle of the left and right columns and arches over the vertical wire",d:[["c",70,36,3.2],["c",94,36,3.2],["t",63,40,"B","e"],["t",101,40,"A"],["l",70,39,70,56],["l",94,39,94,56],["l",94,56,120,56],["l",70,56,70,60],["l",70,60,70,78],["l",70,108,70,126],["p","M70 78L65 83L75 88L65 93L75 98L65 103L70 108"],["l",70,126,70,146],["l",70,176,70,196],["p","M70 146L65 151L75 156L65 161L75 166L65 171L70 176"],["l",94,56,115,56],["l",149,56,170,56],["p","M115 56L120.7 61L126.3 51L132 61L137.7 51L143.3 61L149 56"],["l",170,56,203,56],["l",237,56,270,56],["p","M203 56L208.7 61L214.3 51L220 61L225.7 51L231.3 61L237 56"],["l",270,56,270,76],["l",270,106,270,126],["p","M270 76L265 81L275 86L265 91L275 96L265 101L270 106"],["l",270,126,270,146],["l",270,176,270,196],["p","M270 146L265 151L275 156L265 161L275 166L265 171L270 176"],["l",270,196,237,196],["l",203,196,170,196],["p","M237 196L231.3 191L225.7 201L220 191L214.3 201L208.7 191L203 196"],["l",170,196,137,196],["l",103,196,70,196],["p","M137 196L131.3 191L125.7 201L120 191L114.3 201L108.7 191L103 196"],["l",170,56,170,196],["l",70,126,154,126],["p","M154 126A16 16 0 0 1 186 126"],["l",186,126,270,126],["c",170,56,2.6,"F"],["c",170,196,2.6,"F"],["c",70,126,2.6,"F"],["c",270,126,2.6,"F"],["t",132,47,"6 \u03A9","m"],["t",220,47,"10 \u03A9","m"],["t",282,95,"5 \u03A9"],["t",282,165,"7 \u03A9"],["t",58,95,"8 \u03A9","e"],["t",58,165,"4 \u03A9","e"],["t",220,218,"8 \u03A9","m"],["t",120,218,"11 \u03A9","m"]]},sfig:{w:380,h:170,alt:"Equivalent circuit: A, then 6 ohm, then three parallel branches of 15 ohm each, then 8 ohm, then B",d:[["c",20,85,3.2],["t",20,72,"A","m"],["l",23,85,46.5,85],["l",76.5,85,100,85],["p","M46.5 85L51.5 90L56.5 80L61.5 90L66.5 80L71.5 90L76.5 85"],["t",62,73,"6 \u03A9","m"],["l",100,85,150,85],["l",150,35,150,135],["l",250,35,250,135],["c",150,85,2.6,"F"],["c",250,85,2.6,"F"],["l",150,35,160,35],["l",160,35,182,35],["l",218,35,240,35],["p","M182 35L188 40L194 30L200 40L206 30L212 40L218 35"],["l",240,35,250,35],["t",200,25,"15 \u03A9","m"],["l",150,85,160,85],["l",160,85,182,85],["l",218,85,240,85],["p","M182 85L188 90L194 80L200 90L206 80L212 90L218 85"],["l",240,85,250,85],["t",200,75,"15 \u03A9","m"],["l",150,135,160,135],["l",160,135,182,135],["l",218,135,240,135],["p","M182 135L188 140L194 130L200 140L206 130L212 140L218 135"],["l",240,135,250,135],["t",200,125,"15 \u03A9","m"],["t",122,105,"10+5","s"],["t",122,118,"11+4","s"],["t",122,131,"8+7","s"],["l",250,85,270,85],["l",270,85,293.5,85],["l",323.5,85,347,85],["p","M293.5 85L298.5 90L303.5 80L308.5 90L313.5 80L318.5 90L323.5 85"],["t",308,73,"8 \u03A9","m"],["c",350,85,3.2],["t",350,72,"B","m"]]}},{pn:11,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ccmb",st:"Balanced networks and symmetry",q:"The effective resistance between A and B, if resistance of each resistor is R, will be",o:["8R/3","2R/3","4R/3","5R/3"],c:0,s:`The network is symmetric about the vertical line through the centre, so the top corner, the centre and the bottom corner are at the same potential (each is the midpoint of the left and right corners).
No current flows between points at equal potential, so the two vertical resistors can be removed (see the redrawn figure).
Between the left and right corners there are now three parallel paths, each with two resistors in series: 2R each.
Three equal 2R paths in parallel give 2R/3.
Add the end resistors: R + 2R/3 + R = 8R/3, option (a).`,fig:{w:380,h:230,alt:"A diamond-shaped network: resistor R from A to the left corner, resistor R from the right corner to B; between the left and right corners three paths: the two upper edges, the horizontal middle line of two resistors, and the two lower edges; a vertical line of two resistors joins the top and bottom corners through the centre; every resistor is R",d:[["c",18,115,3.2],["t",18,137,"A","m"],["l",21,115,46.5,115],["l",74.5,115,100,115],["p","M46.5 115L51.2 120L55.8 110L60.5 120L65.2 110L69.8 120L74.5 115"],["l",100,115,130,115],["l",160,115,190,115],["p","M130 115L135 120L140 110L145 120L150 110L155 120L160 115"],["l",190,115,220,115],["l",250,115,280,115],["p","M220 115L225 120L230 110L235 120L240 110L245 120L250 115"],["l",280,115,307,115],["l",335,115,362,115],["p","M307 115L311.7 120L316.3 110L321 120L325.7 110L330.3 120L335 115"],["c",365,115,3.2],["t",365,137,"B","m"],["l",100,115,134.1,82.8],["l",155.9,62.2,190,30],["p","M134.1 82.8L141.2 83L137.9 72.3L148.4 76.1L145.2 65.4L155.7 69.3L155.9 62.2"],["l",190,30,224.1,62.2],["l",245.9,82.8,280,115],["p","M224.1 62.2L224.3 69.3L234.8 65.4L231.6 76.1L242.1 72.3L238.8 83L245.9 82.8"],["l",100,115,134.1,147.2],["l",155.9,167.8,190,200],["p","M134.1 147.2L134.3 154.3L144.8 150.4L141.6 161.1L152.1 157.3L148.8 168L155.9 167.8"],["l",190,200,224.1,167.8],["l",245.9,147.2,280,115],["p","M224.1 167.8L231.2 168L227.9 157.3L238.4 161.1L235.2 150.4L245.7 154.3L245.9 147.2"],["l",190,30,190,57.5],["l",190,87.5,190,115],["p","M190 57.5L185 62.5L195 67.5L185 72.5L195 77.5L185 82.5L190 87.5"],["l",190,115,190,142.5],["l",190,172.5,190,200],["p","M190 142.5L185 147.5L195 152.5L185 157.5L195 162.5L185 167.5L190 172.5"],["c",100,115,2.6,"F"],["c",280,115,2.6,"F"],["c",190,115,2.6,"F"],["c",190,30,2.6,"F"],["c",190,200,2.6,"F"],["t",60,105,"R","m"],["t",320,105,"R","m"],["t",122,107,"R","m"],["t",258,107,"R","m"],["t",180,72,"R","e"],["t",180,164,"R","e"],["t",130,62,"R","e"],["t",250,62,"R"],["t",130,176,"R","e"],["t",250,176,"R"]]},sfig:{w:380,h:210,alt:"Redrawn: R, then three parallel branches of 2R each, then R. The vertical resistors are left out because no current flows in them",d:[["c",18,105,3.2],["t",18,92,"A","m"],["l",21,105,41.5,105],["l",69.5,105,90,105],["p","M41.5 105L46.2 110L50.8 100L55.5 110L60.2 100L64.8 110L69.5 105"],["t",55,93,"R","m"],["l",90,105,130,105],["l",130,35,130,175],["l",250,35,250,175],["c",130,105,2.6,"F"],["c",250,105,2.6,"F"],["l",130,35,140,35],["l",140,35,170,35],["l",210,35,240,35],["p","M170 35L176.7 40L183.3 30L190 40L196.7 30L203.3 40L210 35"],["l",240,35,250,35],["t",190,25,"2R","m"],["l",130,105,140,105],["l",140,105,170,105],["l",210,105,240,105],["p","M170 105L176.7 110L183.3 100L190 110L196.7 100L203.3 110L210 105"],["l",240,105,250,105],["t",190,95,"2R","m"],["l",130,175,140,175],["l",140,175,170,175],["l",210,175,240,175],["p","M170 175L176.7 180L183.3 170L190 180L196.7 170L203.3 180L210 175"],["l",240,175,250,175],["t",190,165,"2R","m"],["l",250,105,290,105],["l",290,105,310.5,105],["l",338.5,105,359,105],["p","M310.5 105L315.2 110L319.8 100L324.5 110L329.2 100L333.8 110L338.5 105"],["t",325,93,"R","m"],["c",362,105,3.2],["t",362,92,"B","m"]]}},{pn:12,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 5 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cres",st:"Resistance and resistivity",q:"The radius (r), length (l) and resistance (R) of a metal wire was measured in the laboratory as r = (0.35 \xB1 0.05) cm, R = (100 \xB1 10) ohm, l = (15 \xB1 0.2) cm. The percentage error in resistivity of the material of the wire is",o:["35.6%","39.9%","25.6%","37.3%"],c:1,s:`Resistivity from R = \u03C1l/A with A = \u03C0r\xB2: \u03C1 = \u03C0r\xB2R/l.
Errors in a product or quotient add as fractions, and a power multiplies its fraction: \u0394\u03C1/\u03C1 = 2(\u0394r/r) + \u0394R/R + \u0394l/l.
2(0.05/0.35) = 0.2857, \u0394R/R = 10/100 = 0.1 and \u0394l/l = 0.2/15 = 0.0133.
Sum = 0.2857 + 0.1 + 0.0133 = 0.399, which is 39.9%.
So the answer is 39.9%, option (b).`},{pn:13,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ccel",st:"EMF and internal resistance",q:"A galvanometer (G) of 2 \u03A9 resistance is connected in the given circuit. The ratio of charge stored in C\u2081 and C\u2082 is",o:["1","2/3","3/2","1/2"],c:3,s:`In steady state a capacitor carries no current, so current flows only through 4 \u03A9, the galvanometer (2 \u03A9) and 6 \u03A9 in series.
I = 6/(4 + 2 + 6) = 0.5 A.
Take the left corner at 6 V and the right corner at 0 V. Then the top corner is at 6 \u2212 0.5 \xD7 4 = 4 V, and the bottom corner at 0 + 0.5 \xD7 6 = 3 V (see the figure).
C\u2081 lies between the left corner (6 V) and the bottom (3 V): 3 V, so Q\u2081 = 4 \u03BCF \xD7 3 V = 12 \u03BCC.
C\u2082 lies between the top (4 V) and the right corner (0 V): 4 V, so Q\u2082 = 6 \u03BCF \xD7 4 V = 24 \u03BCC.
Q\u2081/Q\u2082 = 12/24 = 1/2, option (d).`,fig:{w:360,h:248,alt:"A 6 V cell across a bridge: 4 ohm resistor on the upper-left arm, capacitor C2 of 6 microfarad on the upper-right arm, capacitor C1 of 4 microfarad on the lower-left arm, 6 ohm resistor on the lower-right arm, and a galvanometer of 2 ohm between the top and bottom corners",d:[["l",90,110,40,110],["l",40,110,40,215],["l",40,215,164,215],["l",164,215,177,215],["l",183,215,196,215],["l",177,205,177,225],["l",183,210,183,220,"B"],["l",196,215,320,215],["l",320,215,320,110],["l",320,110,270,110],["l",90,110,121.6,85.4],["l",148.4,64.6,180,40],["p","M121.6 85.4L129.1 85.9L127.5 74.5L138.1 78.9L136.4 67.6L147 72L148.4 64.6"],["l",180,40,222.2,72.9],["l",227.8,77.1,270,110],["l",227.1,66.5,217.3,79.2],["l",232.7,70.8,222.9,83.5],["l",90,110,132.2,142.9],["l",137.8,147.1,180,180],["l",137.1,136.5,127.3,149.2],["l",142.7,140.8,132.9,153.5],["l",180,180,211.6,155.4],["l",238.4,134.6,270,110],["p","M211.6 155.4L219.1 155.9L217.5 144.5L228.1 148.9L226.4 137.6L237 142L238.4 134.6"],["l",180,40,180,96],["c",180,110,12],["t",180,114,"G","m"],["l",180,122,180,180],["c",90,110,2.6,"F"],["c",270,110,2.6,"F"],["c",180,40,2.6,"F"],["c",180,180,2.6,"F"],["t",104,62,"4 \u03A9","e"],["t",262,52,"6 \u03BCF"],["t",238,84,"C\u2082"],["t",112,150,"C\u2081","e"],["t",112,166,"4 \u03BCF","e"],["t",262,164,"6 \u03A9"],["t",200,118,"2 \u03A9","s"],["t",180,240,"6 V","m"]]},sfig:{w:380,h:150,alt:"The steady current path: 6 V at the left corner, then the 4 ohm resistor to 4 V, the galvanometer of 2 ohm to 3 V, and the 6 ohm resistor to 0 V; the current is 0.5 A",d:[["c",30,75,3.2],["t",30,58,"6 V","m"],["l",33,75,58.5,75],["l",94.5,75,120,75],["p","M58.5 75L64.5 80L70.5 70L76.5 80L82.5 70L88.5 80L94.5 75"],["t",76,63,"4 \u03A9","m"],["c",120,75,2.6,"F"],["t",120,58,"4 V","m"],["l",120,75,146,75],["c",160,75,14],["t",160,79,"G","m"],["l",174,75,200,75],["c",200,75,2.6,"F"],["t",200,58,"3 V","m"],["t",160,105,"2 \u03A9","m"],["l",200,75,245,75],["l",285,75,330,75],["p","M245 75L251.7 80L258.3 70L265 80L271.7 70L278.3 80L285 75"],["t",265,63,"6 \u03A9","m"],["c",333,75,3.2],["t",333,58,"0 V","m"],["l",70,125,150,125,"a"],["t",110,142,"I = 0.5 A","m"]]}},{pn:14,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cres",st:"Stretching and shape change of wires",q:"A wire of resistance R and length L is cut into 5 equal parts. If these parts are joined parallely, then resultant resistance will be",o:["25 R","R/25","5 R","R/5"],c:1,s:`Resistance is proportional to length, so each of the 5 equal parts has resistance R/5.
For n equal resistors r in parallel, the equivalent is r/n.
R(eq) = (R/5)/5 = R/25.
So the answer is R/25, option (b).`},{pn:15,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cdrf",st:"Current and charge",q:"The electric current through a wire varies with time as I = I\u2080 + \u03B2t, where I\u2080 = 20 A and \u03B2 = 3 A/s. The amount of electric charge crossed through a section of the wire in 20 s is",o:["1000 C","800 C","80 C","1600 C"],c:0,s:`Charge is the integral of current over time: q = \u222B I dt.
q = \u222B (I\u2080 + \u03B2t) dt from 0 to 20 s = I\u2080t + \u03B2t\xB2/2.
I\u2080t = 20 \xD7 20 = 400 C.
\u03B2t\xB2/2 = 3 \xD7 400/2 = 600 C.
Total q = 400 + 600 = 1000 C, option (a).`},{pn:16,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ckir",st:"Kirchhoff's laws",q:"In the given circuit, the current in resistance R\u2083 is",o:["1.5 A","2 A","2.5 A","1 A"],c:3,s:`R\u2082 and R\u2083 are in parallel with each other, and this pair is in series with R\u2081 and R\u2084, so the same total current passes through R\u2081 and R\u2084.
R\u2082 \u2225 R\u2083 = (4 \xD7 4)/(4 + 4) = 2 \u03A9.
Total resistance = 2 + 2 + 1 = 5 \u03A9, so I = 10/5 = 2 A.
The two parallel branches have equal resistance (4 \u03A9 each), so the 2 A splits equally.
Current in R\u2083 = 1 A, option (d).`,fig:{w:380,h:200,alt:"A 10 V cell drives a loop: R1 of 2 ohm, then R2 and R3 of 4 ohm each in parallel, then R4 of 1 ohm",d:[["l",30,80,30,150],["l",30,150,174,150],["l",174,150,187,150],["l",193,150,206,150],["l",187,140,187,160],["l",193,145,193,155,"B"],["l",206,150,350,150],["l",350,150,350,80],["l",30,80,50,80],["l",50,80,77,80],["l",113,80,140,80],["p","M77 80L83 85L89 75L95 85L101 75L107 85L113 80"],["l",260,80,287,80],["l",323,80,350,80],["p","M287 80L293 85L299 75L305 85L311 75L317 85L323 80"],["l",140,80,140,40],["l",140,40,160,40],["l",160,40,180,40],["l",220,40,240,40],["p","M180 40L186.7 45L193.3 35L200 45L206.7 35L213.3 45L220 40"],["l",240,40,260,40],["l",260,40,260,80],["l",140,80,140,120],["l",140,120,160,120],["l",160,120,180,120],["l",220,120,240,120],["p","M180 120L186.7 125L193.3 115L200 125L206.7 115L213.3 125L220 120"],["l",240,120,260,120],["l",260,120,260,80],["c",140,80,2.6,"F"],["c",260,80,2.6,"F"],["t",95,68,"R\u2081","m"],["t",95,102,"2 \u03A9","m"],["t",200,28,"R\u2082","m"],["t",200,62,"4 \u03A9","m"],["t",200,110,"4 \u03A9","m"],["t",200,96,"R\u2083","m"],["t",305,68,"1 \u03A9","m"],["t",305,102,"R\u2084","m"],["t",190,183,"10 V","m"]]}},{pn:17,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cres",st:"Temperature dependence",q:"An electric toaster has resistance of 60 \u03A9 at room temperature (27\xB0C). The toaster is connected to a 220 V supply. If the current flowing through it reaches 2.75 A, the temperature attained by toaster is around (if \u03B1 = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074/\xB0C)",o:["1694\xB0C","1235\xB0C","694\xB0C","1667\xB0C"],c:0,s:`When the toaster is hot its resistance is R = V/I = 220/2.75 = 80 \u03A9.
Resistance changes with temperature as R = R\u2080(1 + \u03B1\u0394T), with R\u2080 = 60 \u03A9 at 27 \xB0C.
80 = 60(1 + 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \u0394T), so 1 + 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \u0394T = 4/3.
2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \u0394T = 1/3, so \u0394T = 1/(6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074) \u2248 1667 \xB0C.
Final temperature = 27 + 1667 \u2248 1694 \xB0C, option (a).
Note: option (d) 1667 \xB0C is only the rise \u0394T; the question asks for the temperature attained.`},{pn:18,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ckir",st:"Kirchhoff's laws",q:"A potential divider circuit is shown in figure. The output voltage V\u2080 is",o:["12 mV","2 mV","4 V","0.5 V"],c:3,s:`The chain is in series: 3.3 k\u03A9 + 7 \xD7 100 \u03A9 = 3300 + 700 = 4000 \u03A9.
Current I = 4/4000 = 1 mA.
V\u2080 is measured across the 5 resistors of 100 \u03A9 to the right of the tap = 500 \u03A9.
V\u2080 = I \xD7 500 = 10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 500 = 0.5 V, option (d).
Note: the page prints 33 k\u03A9, the book's working uses 3.3 k\u03A9 (3300 \u03A9): I = 1 mA, V\u2080 = 0.5 V.`,fig:{w:390,h:160,alt:"A 4 V cell across a chain of one 3.3 kilo-ohm resistor followed by seven 100 ohm resistors; a sliding tap after the first three resistors from the left, with the output voltage V0 measured from the tap to the right end of the chain",d:[["l",24,24,24,70],["l",24,24,174,24],["l",174,24,187,24],["l",193,24,206,24],["l",187,14,187,34],["l",193,19,193,29,"B"],["l",206,24,366,24],["l",366,24,366,70],["l",24,70,30,70],["l",60,70,66,70],["p","M30 70L35 75L40 65L45 75L50 65L55 75L60 70"],["t",45,58,"3.3 k\u03A9","ms"],["l",66,70,72,70],["l",102,70,108,70],["p","M72 70L77 75L82 65L87 75L92 65L97 75L102 70"],["t",87,58,"100 \u03A9","ms"],["l",108,70,114,70],["l",144,70,150,70],["p","M114 70L119 75L124 65L129 75L134 65L139 75L144 70"],["t",129,58,"100 \u03A9","ms"],["l",150,70,156,70],["l",186,70,192,70],["p","M156 70L161 75L166 65L171 75L176 65L181 75L186 70"],["t",171,58,"100 \u03A9","ms"],["l",192,70,198,70],["l",228,70,234,70],["p","M198 70L203 75L208 65L213 75L218 65L223 75L228 70"],["t",213,58,"100 \u03A9","ms"],["l",234,70,240,70],["l",270,70,276,70],["p","M240 70L245 75L250 65L255 75L260 65L265 75L270 70"],["t",255,58,"100 \u03A9","ms"],["l",276,70,282,70],["l",312,70,318,70],["p","M282 70L287 75L292 65L297 75L302 65L307 75L312 70"],["t",297,58,"100 \u03A9","ms"],["l",318,70,324,70],["l",354,70,360,70],["p","M324 70L329 75L334 65L339 75L344 65L349 75L354 70"],["t",339,58,"100 \u03A9","ms"],["l",360,70,366,70],["l",150,130,150,72,"a"],["l",150,130,366,130,"b"],["l",366,70,366,138],["t",258,124,"V\u2080","m"],["t",190,10,"4 V","m"]]}},{pn:19,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cres",st:"Temperature dependence",q:"Two conductors have the same resistances at 0\xB0C but their temperature coefficients of resistance are \u03B1\u2081 and \u03B1\u2082. The respective temperature coefficients for their series and parallel combinations are",o:["\u03B1\u2081 + \u03B1\u2082, (\u03B1\u2081\u03B1\u2082)/(\u03B1\u2081 + \u03B1\u2082)","(\u03B1\u2081 + \u03B1\u2082)/2, (\u03B1\u2081 + \u03B1\u2082)/2","\u03B1\u2081 + \u03B1\u2082, (\u03B1\u2081 + \u03B1\u2082)/2","(\u03B1\u2081 + \u03B1\u2082)/2, \u03B1\u2081 + \u03B1\u2082"],c:1,s:`Let each conductor have resistance R\u2080 at 0 \xB0C, so R\u2081 = R\u2080(1 + \u03B1\u2081t) and R\u2082 = R\u2080(1 + \u03B1\u2082t).
Series: R = R\u2081 + R\u2082 = 2R\u2080[1 + ((\u03B1\u2081 + \u03B1\u2082)/2) t], so \u03B1(series) = (\u03B1\u2081 + \u03B1\u2082)/2.
Parallel: R = R\u2081R\u2082/(R\u2081 + R\u2082) = (R\u2080/2)(1 + \u03B1\u2081t)(1 + \u03B1\u2082t)/(1 + ((\u03B1\u2081 + \u03B1\u2082)/2) t).
Note: the \u03B1 values are small, so the product (1 + \u03B1\u2081t)(1 + \u03B1\u2082t) \u2248 1 + (\u03B1\u2081 + \u03B1\u2082)t, and dividing by 1 + ((\u03B1\u2081 + \u03B1\u2082)/2)t leaves about 1 + ((\u03B1\u2081 + \u03B1\u2082)/2)t.
So \u03B1(parallel) = (\u03B1\u2081 + \u03B1\u2082)/2 as well.
Both are (\u03B1\u2081 + \u03B1\u2082)/2, option (b).`},{pn:20,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cres",st:"Temperature dependence",q:"The resistivity (\u03C1) of semiconductor varies with temperature. Which of the following curve represents the correct behaviour.",c:3,s:`In a semiconductor, heating frees more charge carriers, so the number of carriers rises quickly with T.
This lowers the resistivity, and the fall is fast at low T and slows at high T (exponential-type), so \u03C1 decreases and flattens out.
Curves (b) and (c) show \u03C1 increasing, which is the behaviour of a metal. Curve (a) is a straight line, but the real fall is curved.
The correct curve is (d).`,fig:{w:360,h:285,alt:"Four graphs of resistivity rho against T: (a) a straight line falling; (b) a curve rising slowly and then bending up almost vertically; (c) flat along the axis and then a straight rise; (d) a curve falling steeply and then flattening",d:[["t",30,86,"(a)","e"],["l",52,120,52,32,"a"],["l",52,120,166,120,"a"],["t",47,132,"O","e"],["t",170,124,"T"],["t",52,25,"\u03C1","m"],["p","M56 36L158 114"],["t",210,86,"(b)","e"],["l",232,120,232,32,"a"],["l",232,120,346,120,"a"],["t",227,132,"O","e"],["t",350,124,"T"],["t",232,25,"\u03C1","m"],["p","M260 117C296 112 330 92 332 40"],["t",30,228,"(c)","e"],["l",52,262,52,174,"a"],["l",52,262,166,262,"a"],["t",47,274,"O","e"],["t",170,266,"T"],["t",52,167,"\u03C1","m"],["p","M52 262L88 262L152 188"],["t",210,228,"(d)","e"],["l",232,262,232,174,"a"],["l",232,262,346,262,"a"],["t",227,274,"O","e"],["t",350,266,"T"],["t",232,167,"\u03C1","m"],["p","M236 182C246 226 278 254 340 257"]]},o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"]},{pn:21,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ccmb",st:"Balanced networks and symmetry",q:"The equivalent resistance between A and B as shown in figure is",o:["10 k\u03A9","30 k\u03A9","5 k\u03A9","20 k\u03A9"],c:2,s:`Follow the plain wires. The top wire joins A to D, so A and D are the same point. The bottom wire joins C to B, so C and B are the same point.
First 20 k\u03A9: between A and C, that is between A and B.
Second 20 k\u03A9: between C and D, that is between B and A.
10 k\u03A9: between D and B, that is between A and B. So all three are in parallel between A and B (see the redrawn figure).
1/R = 1/20 + 1/20 + 1/10 = (1 + 1 + 2)/20 = 1/5, so R = 5 k\u03A9, option (c).`,fig:{w:320,h:150,alt:"A main line from A to B with 20 kilo-ohm, 20 kilo-ohm and 10 kilo-ohm resistors in series; a wire joins A to the point D between the second and third resistors, and a wire joins the point C between the first and second resistors to B",d:[["c",22,70,3.2],["t",14,60,"A","e"],["l",25,70,50,70],["l",50,70,68,70],["l",102,70,120,70],["p","M68 70L73.7 75L79.3 65L85 75L90.7 65L96.3 75L102 70"],["l",120,70,138,70],["l",172,70,190,70],["p","M138 70L143.7 75L149.3 65L155 75L160.7 65L166.3 75L172 70"],["l",190,70,208,70],["l",242,70,260,70],["p","M208 70L213.7 75L219.3 65L225 75L230.7 65L236.3 75L242 70"],["l",260,70,285,70],["c",288,70,3.2],["t",296,66,"B"],["l",50,70,50,32],["l",50,32,190,32],["l",190,32,190,70],["l",120,70,120,112],["l",120,112,260,112],["l",260,112,260,70],["c",50,70,2.6,"F"],["c",120,70,2.6,"F"],["c",190,70,2.6,"F"],["c",260,70,2.6,"F"],["t",120,60,"C","m"],["t",196,90,"D"],["t",85,90,"20 k\u03A9","m"],["t",155,90,"20 k\u03A9","m"],["t",225,60,"10 k\u03A9","m"]]},sfig:{w:330,h:170,alt:"Redrawn: 20 kilo-ohm, 20 kilo-ohm and 10 kilo-ohm resistors in parallel between A and B",d:[["c",20,85,3.2],["t",20,72,"A","m"],["l",23,85,80,85],["l",80,35,80,135],["l",250,35,250,135],["l",250,85,297,85],["c",300,85,3.2],["t",300,72,"B","m"],["c",80,85,2.6,"F"],["c",250,85,2.6,"F"],["l",80,35,90,35],["l",90,35,145,35],["l",185,35,240,35],["p","M145 35L151.7 40L158.3 30L165 40L171.7 30L178.3 40L185 35"],["l",240,35,250,35],["t",165,25,"20 k\u03A9","m"],["l",80,85,90,85],["l",90,85,145,85],["l",185,85,240,85],["p","M145 85L151.7 90L158.3 80L165 90L171.7 80L178.3 90L185 85"],["l",240,85,250,85],["t",165,75,"20 k\u03A9","m"],["l",80,135,90,135],["l",90,135,145,135],["l",185,135,240,135],["p","M145 135L151.7 140L158.3 130L165 140L171.7 130L178.3 140L185 135"],["l",240,135,250,135],["t",165,125,"10 k\u03A9","m"]]}},{pn:22,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cdrf",st:"Drift velocity and mobility",q:"In a metallic conductor, under the effect of applied electric field, the free electrons of the conductor",o:["move in the curved paths from lower potential to higher potential","drift from higher potential to lower potential.","move in the straight line paths in the same direction","move with the uniform velocity throughout from lower potential to higher potential."],c:0,s:`Free electrons are negatively charged, so the field pushes them opposite to the field, that is from lower potential towards higher potential.
They also collide again and again with the ions, and after each collision the direction changes, so between collisions they follow short curved paths because the field accelerates them.
Their speed is not uniform: it increases after a collision and drops at the next one. Only the average (drift) velocity is steady.
So (b) is wrong (wrong direction), (c) and (d) are wrong (straight, uniform motion), and the correct statement is (a).`},{pn:23,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 11 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ckir",st:"Kirchhoff's laws",q:"The current flowing through R\u2082 is",o:["1/4 A","2/3 A","1/3 A","1/2 A"],c:2,s:`The 8 V cell is joined directly between E and A. R\u2086 sits across it, so it does not disturb the rest; the rest of the circuit is R\u2085 in series with the network between D and A.
From C to A: R\u2083 = 6 \u03A9 is in parallel with (R\u2082 + R\u2081) = 4 + 2 = 6 \u03A9, which gives 3 \u03A9.
From D to A: R\u2087 = 3 \u03A9 is in parallel with (R\u2084 + 3 \u03A9) = 3 + 3 = 6 \u03A9, which gives (3 \xD7 6)/9 = 2 \u03A9.
Total in the branch from E: R\u2085 + 2 \u03A9 = 2 + 2 = 4 \u03A9, so the current is 8/4 = 2 A and V(DA) = 2 \xD7 2 = 4 V.
Current through R\u2084 = 4/6 = 2/3 A, so V(CA) = (2/3) \xD7 3 = 2 V (see the working figure).
The branch R\u2082 + R\u2081 = 6 \u03A9 has 2 V across it, so I = 2/6 = 1/3 A through R\u2082, option (c).`,fig:{w:450,h:230,alt:"A network with an 8 V cell on the left. Nodes E, D, C, B and a bottom wire A. R5 of 2 ohm joins E to D, R6 of 4 ohm joins E to A, R7 of 3 ohm joins D to A, R4 of 3 ohm joins D to C, R3 of 6 ohm joins A to C, R1 of 2 ohm joins A to B and R2 of 4 ohm joins C to B",d:[["l",40,40,110,40],["l",40,40,40,78],["l",40,79,40,92],["l",40,98,40,111],["l",30,92,50,92],["l",35,98,45,98,"B"],["l",40,111,40,170],["l",40,170,290,170],["l",110,40,142,40],["l",178,40,210,40],["p","M142 40L148 45L154 35L160 45L166 35L172 45L178 40"],["l",210,40,270,40],["l",310,40,370,40],["p","M270 40L276.7 45L283.3 35L290 45L296.7 35L303.3 45L310 40"],["l",370,40,370,78],["l",110,40,110,87],["l",110,123,110,170],["p","M110 87L105 93L115 99L105 105L115 111L105 117L110 123"],["l",210,40,210,87],["l",210,123,210,170],["p","M210 87L205 93L215 99L205 105L215 111L205 117L210 123"],["l",290,170,290,78],["l",290,170,290,142],["l",290,106,290,78],["p","M290 142L295 136L285 130L295 124L285 118L295 112L290 106"],["l",290,78,370,78],["l",370,78,370,106],["l",370,142,370,170],["p","M370 106L365 112L375 118L365 124L375 130L365 136L370 142"],["l",290,170,310,170],["l",350,170,370,170],["p","M310 170L316.7 175L323.3 165L330 175L336.7 165L343.3 175L350 170"],["c",110,40,2.6,"F"],["c",210,40,2.6,"F"],["c",110,170,2.6,"F"],["c",210,170,2.6,"F"],["c",290,170,2.6,"F"],["c",370,78,2.6,"F"],["c",370,170,2.6,"F"],["t",106,32,"E","e"],["t",210,32,"D","m"],["t",378,72,"C"],["t",378,174,"B"],["t",150,190,"A","m"],["t",160,28,"R\u2085 = 2 \u03A9","m"],["t",310,28,"R\u2084 = 3 \u03A9","m"],["t",102,112,"R\u2086 = 4 \u03A9","e"],["t",202,112,"R\u2087 = 3 \u03A9","e"],["t",282,130,"R\u2083 = 6 \u03A9","e"],["t",378,126,"R\u2082 = 4 \u03A9"],["t",330,190,"R\u2081 = 2 \u03A9","m"],["t",30,100,"8 V","e"]]},sfig:{w:400,h:190,alt:"Working figure: D to C through R4 of 3 ohm, then from C to A the 6 ohm resistor R3 in parallel with R2 and R1 in series (4 plus 2 ohm); D is 4 V above A and C is 2 V above A",d:[["c",30,80,3.2],["t",30,62,"D","m"],["t",30,46,"4 V","m"],["l",33,80,68.5,80],["l",104.5,80,140,80],["p","M68.5 80L74.5 85L80.5 75L86.5 85L92.5 75L98.5 85L104.5 80"],["t",86,68,"R\u2084 = 3 \u03A9","m"],["c",140,80,2.6,"F"],["t",140,62,"C","m"],["t",140,46,"2 V","m"],["l",140,80,160,80],["l",160,50,160,125],["l",320,50,320,125],["c",160,80,2.6,"F"],["c",320,80,2.6,"F"],["l",320,80,347,80],["c",350,80,3.2],["t",350,62,"A","m"],["t",350,46,"0 V","m"],["l",160,50,170,50],["l",170,50,220,50],["l",260,50,310,50],["p","M220 50L226.7 55L233.3 45L240 55L246.7 45L253.3 55L260 50"],["l",310,50,320,50],["t",240,40,"R\u2083 = 6 \u03A9","m"],["l",160,125,170,125],["l",170,125,191,125],["l",219,125,240,125],["p","M191 125L195.7 130L200.3 120L205 130L209.7 120L214.3 130L219 125"],["l",240,125,261,125],["l",289,125,310,125],["p","M261 125L265.7 130L270.3 120L275 130L279.7 120L284.3 130L289 125"],["l",310,125,320,125],["t",205,145,"R\u2082 = 4 \u03A9","m"],["t",275,145,"R\u2081 = 2 \u03A9","m"]]}},{pn:24,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cres",st:"Stretching and shape change of wires",q:"A wire of resistance 160 \u03A9 is melted and drawn in a wire of one-fourth of its length. The new resistance of the wire will be",o:["640 \u03A9","40 \u03A9","10 \u03A9","16 \u03A9"],c:2,s:`Melting and redrawing keeps the volume, V = A \xD7 L, constant.
If the length becomes L/4, the area must become 4A.
R = \u03C1L/A, so R\u2032 = \u03C1(L/4)/(4A) = (1/16) \u03C1L/A = R/16.
R\u2032 = 160/16 = 10 \u03A9, option (c).`},{pn:25,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ccmb",st:"Balanced networks and symmetry",q:"The equivalent resistance between A and B of the network shown in figure is",fig:{w:350,h:165,alt:"Network between A and B: 2R from A to a node, then 9R to a second node, then 3R to B; R joins the A-side node to the second node; 6R joins the first inner node to B",d:[["c",30,90,3],["l",33,90,60,90],["p","M60 90 L77 90 L80 85 L86 95 L92 85 L98 95 L104 85 L110 95 L113 90 L130 90"],["p","M130 90 L147 90 L150 85 L156 95 L162 85 L168 95 L174 85 L180 95 L183 90 L200 90"],["p","M200 90 L217 90 L220 85 L226 95 L232 85 L238 95 L244 85 L250 95 L253 90 L270 90"],["l",270,90,317,90],["c",320,90,3],["c",60,90,2.5,"F"],["c",130,90,2.5,"F"],["c",200,90,2.5,"F"],["c",270,90,2.5,"F"],["l",60,90,60,45],["l",60,45,100,45],["p","M100 45 L112 45 L115 40 L121 50 L127 40 L133 50 L139 40 L145 50 L148 45 L160 45"],["l",160,45,200,45],["l",200,45,200,90],["l",130,90,130,135],["l",130,135,175,135],["p","M175 135 L182 135 L185 130 L191 140 L197 130 L203 140 L209 130 L215 140 L218 135 L225 135"],["l",225,135,270,135],["l",270,135,270,90],["t",30,76,"A","m"],["t",320,76,"B","m"],["t",130,36,"R","m"],["t",95,108,"2R","m"],["t",165,108,"9R","m"],["t",235,80,"3R","m"],["t",200,153,"6R","m"]]},sfig:{w:290,h:170,alt:"After removing the 9R resistor: 4R in parallel with 8R between A and B",d:[["c",30,100,3,"F"],["l",33,100,70,100],["c",70,100,2.5,"F"],["l",70,100,70,60],["l",70,100,70,140],["p","M70 60 L115 60 L118.3 55 L125 65 L131.7 55 L138.3 65 L145 55 L151.7 65 L155 60 L200 60"],["p","M70 140 L115 140 L118.3 135 L125 145 L131.7 135 L138.3 145 L145 135 L151.7 145 L155 140 L200 140"],["l",200,60,200,140],["c",200,100,2.5,"F"],["l",200,100,247,100],["c",250,100,3,"F"],["t",30,86,"A","m"],["t",250,86,"B","m"],["t",135,50,"4R (R + 3R)","m"],["t",135,158,"8R (2R + 6R)","m"]]},o:["11\u2154 R","14 R","(8)/(3) R","21 R"],c:2,s:`Look at the four resistors R, 2R, 3R and 6R: they form a Wheatstone-bridge square, with 9R as the bridge arm in the middle.
Check the balance: R/2R = 1/2 and 3R/6R = 1/2, the two ratios are equal, so the bridge is balanced.
Note: in a balanced bridge the two ends of the middle resistor are at the same potential, so no current flows through 9R and it can be removed (see the second figure).
Upper path: R + 3R = 4R. Lower path: 2R + 6R = 8R.
These two paths are in parallel between A and B: (4R \xD7 8R)/(4R + 8R) = (32R\xB2)/(12R) = (8R)/(3).
So the equivalent resistance is (8R)/(3), option (c).`},{pn:26,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ccmb",st:"Equivalent resistance of networks",q:"Equivalent resistance between the adjacent corners of a regular n-side polygon of uniform wire of resistance R would be",o:["(n \u2212 1)R/(2n \u2212 1)","(n \u2212 1)R/n","n\xB2R/(n \u2212 1)","(n \u2212 1)R/n\xB2"],c:3,s:`The whole wire has resistance R and the polygon has n equal sides, so each side has resistance R/n.
Take two adjacent corners. One path between them is the single side between them: R/n.
The other path goes round the remaining (n \u2212 1) sides in series: (n \u2212 1)R/n.
These two paths are in parallel: R_eq = [(R/n) \xD7 (n \u2212 1)R/n] / [R/n + (n \u2212 1)R/n].
The denominator is R/n + (n \u2212 1)R/n = R, the numerator is (n \u2212 1)R\xB2/n\xB2.
So R_eq = (n \u2212 1)R/n\xB2, option (d).`},{pn:27,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ccel",st:"EMF and internal resistance",q:"As shown in the figure, a network of resistors is connected to a battery of 24 V with an internal resistance of 3 \u03A9. The currents through the resistors R\u2084 and R\u2085 are I\u2084 and I\u2085 respectively. The values of I\u2084 and I\u2085 are",fig:{w:420,h:195,alt:"Battery of 24 V with 3 ohm internal resistance drives a series chain: R1 = 2 ohm in parallel with R2 = 2 ohm, then R3 = 2 ohm, then R4 = 20 ohm in parallel with R5 = 5 ohm, then R6 = 2 ohm",d:[["l",30,80,80,80],["l",30,80,30,160],["l",30,160,190,160],["l",190,149,190,171],["l",195,154,195,166,"B"],["l",195,160,390,160],["l",390,160,390,80],["c",80,80,2.5,"F"],["c",160,80,2.5,"F"],["c",230,80,2.5,"F"],["c",330,80,2.5,"F"],["l",80,80,80,50],["p","M80 50 L102 50 L105 45 L111 55 L117 45 L123 55 L129 45 L135 55 L138 50 L160 50"],["l",160,50,160,80],["l",80,80,80,110],["p","M80 110 L102 110 L105 105 L111 115 L117 105 L123 115 L129 105 L135 115 L138 110 L160 110"],["l",160,110,160,80],["p","M160 80 L177 80 L180 75 L186 85 L192 75 L198 85 L204 75 L210 85 L213 80 L230 80"],["l",230,80,230,50],["p","M230 50 L262 50 L265 45 L271 55 L277 45 L283 55 L289 45 L295 55 L298 50 L330 50"],["l",330,50,330,80],["l",230,80,230,110],["p","M230 110 L262 110 L265 105 L271 115 L277 105 L283 115 L289 105 L295 115 L298 110 L330 110"],["l",330,110,330,80],["p","M330 80 L342 80 L345 75 L351 85 L357 75 L363 85 L369 75 L375 85 L378 80 L390 80"],["t",120,42,"R\u2081 = 2 \u03A9","m"],["t",120,132,"R\u2082 = 2 \u03A9","m"],["t",195,67,"R\u2083 = 2 \u03A9","m"],["t",280,42,"R\u2084 = 20 \u03A9","m"],["t",280,132,"R\u2085 = 5 \u03A9","m"],["t",360,67,"R\u2086 = 2 \u03A9","m"],["t",180,150,"+","m"],["t",210,150,"\u2212","m"],["t",193,184,"24 V, 3 \u03A9","m"]]},sfig:{w:320,h:195,alt:"Equivalent series circuit: 1 ohm, 2 ohm, 4 ohm, 2 ohm and the 3 ohm internal resistance in series with 24 V",d:[["l",30,160,30,40],["p","M30 40 L47 40 L50 35 L56 45 L62 35 L68 45 L74 35 L80 45 L83 40 L100 40"],["p","M100 40 L117 40 L120 35 L126 45 L132 35 L138 45 L144 35 L150 45 L153 40 L170 40"],["p","M170 40 L187 40 L190 35 L196 45 L202 35 L208 45 L214 35 L220 45 L223 40 L240 40"],["l",240,40,270,40],["l",270,40,270,65],["p","M270 65 L270 77 L275 80 L265 86 L275 92 L265 98 L275 104 L265 110 L270 113 L270 125"],["l",270,125,270,160],["l",270,160,205,160],["l",200,149,200,171],["l",205,154,205,166,"B"],["l",200,160,150,160],["p","M150 160 L133 160 L130 165 L124 155 L118 165 L112 155 L106 165 L100 155 L97 160 L80 160"],["l",80,160,30,160],["t",65,28,"1 \u03A9","m"],["t",135,28,"2 \u03A9","m"],["t",205,28,"4 \u03A9","m"],["t",284,98,"2 \u03A9",""],["t",115,182,"3 \u03A9","m"],["t",203,182,"24 V","m"],["t",150,105,"I = 2 A","m"]]},o:["I\u2084 = (8)/(5) A and I\u2085 = (2)/(5) A","I\u2084 = (6)/(5) A and I\u2085 = (24)/(5) A","I\u2084 = (2)/(5) A and I\u2085 = (8)/(5) A","I\u2084 = (24)/(5) A and I\u2085 = (6)/(5) A"],c:2,s:`The battery drives the same current I through the single loop, so first find I by reducing the network to one series circuit (second figure).
R\u2081 and R\u2082 are in parallel: (2 \xD7 2)/(2 + 2) = 1 \u03A9.
R\u2084 and R\u2085 are in parallel: (20 \xD7 5)/(20 + 5) = 4 \u03A9.
Total resistance = 3 (internal) + 1 + 2 (R\u2083) + 4 + 2 (R\u2086) = 12 \u03A9.
Current from the battery: I = 24/12 = 2 A.
Voltage across the R\u2084, R\u2085 pair = 2 \xD7 4 = 8 V, and the same 8 V is across each of them because they are in parallel.
I\u2084 = 8/20 = (2)/(5) A and I\u2085 = 8/5 = (8)/(5) A (check: 0.4 + 1.6 = 2 A).
So I\u2084 = 2/5 A and I\u2085 = 8/5 A, option (c).`},{pn:28,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 25 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cres",st:"Resistance and resistivity",q:"A uniform metallic wire carries a current 2 A, when 3.4 V battery is connected across it. The mass of uniform metallic wire is 8.92 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 kg, density is 8.92 \xD7 10\xB3 kg/m\xB3 and resistivity is 1.7 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 \u03A9 m. The length of wire is",o:["l = 10 m","l = 100 m","l = 5 m","l = 6.8 m"],c:0,s:`We know V, I, the mass, the density and the resistivity, so use R = V/I and R = \u03C1l/A together with mass = density \xD7 volume.
R = V/I = 3.4/2 = 1.7 \u03A9.
Volume = A\xB7l = m/d, so A = m/(d\xB7l).
Put this in R = \u03C1l/A: R = \u03C1\xB7l\xB2\xB7d/m, so l\xB2 = R\xB7m/(\u03C1\xB7d).
l\xB2 = (1.7 \xD7 8.92 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3)/(1.7 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 \xD7 8.92 \xD7 10\xB3) = 10\u207B\xB3/10\u207B\u2075 = 100 m\xB2.
Note: the numbers 1.7 and 8.92 appear both on the top and on the bottom, so they cancel and only the powers of ten remain.
So l = 10 m, option (a).`},{pn:29,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 25 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cres",st:"Stretching and shape change of wires",q:"The resistance of a wire is 5 \u03A9. Its new resistance in ohm if stretched to 5 times of its original length will be",o:["125","5","25","625"],c:0,s:`When a wire is stretched its volume stays the same: A\xB7l is constant, so if the length becomes 5 times, the area becomes (1/5) of the original.
Resistance R = \u03C1l/A. New length 5l and new area A/5 give R\u2032 = \u03C1(5l)/(A/5) = 25 \u03C1l/A = 25R.
Note: in general, stretching by a factor n multiplies the resistance by n\xB2 (n for the longer length and n again for the thinner area).
R\u2032 = 25 \xD7 5 = 125 \u03A9, option (a).`},{pn:30,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cdrf",st:"Current and charge",q:"The charge flowing in a conductor changes with time as Q(t) = \u03B1t \u2212 \u03B2t\xB2 + \u03B3t\xB3, where \u03B1, \u03B2 and \u03B3 are constants. Minimum value of current is",o:["\u03B1 \u2212 \u03B3\xB2/(3\u03B2)","\u03B2 \u2212 \u03B1\xB2/(3\u03B3)","\u03B1 \u2212 \u03B2\xB2/(3\u03B3)","\u03B1 \u2212 3\u03B2\xB2/\u03B3"],c:2,s:`Current is the rate of flow of charge: I = dQ/dt.
I = \u03B1 \u2212 2\u03B2t + 3\u03B3t\xB2.
This is a parabola in t that opens upwards (3\u03B3 > 0), so it has a minimum where dI/dt = 0.
dI/dt = \u22122\u03B2 + 6\u03B3t = 0, so t = \u03B2/(3\u03B3).
Put this t back: I_min = \u03B1 \u2212 2\u03B2\xB7\u03B2/(3\u03B3) + 3\u03B3\xB7\u03B2\xB2/(9\u03B3\xB2) = \u03B1 \u2212 2\u03B2\xB2/(3\u03B3) + \u03B2\xB2/(3\u03B3).
I_min = \u03B1 \u2212 \u03B2\xB2/(3\u03B3), option (c).`},{pn:31,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ccmb",st:"Equivalent resistance of networks",q:"The equivalent resistance between A and B is ______ .",fig:{w:320,h:285,alt:"Four-corner network between A and B: top path 1.5 and 0.5 ohm, 12 ohm across the top, 6 ohm across the bottom, bottom path 1 and 1 ohm, and two crossing diagonals of 8 and 6 ohm, 2 and 4 ohm meeting at a centre joint; the left corners are joined to A and the right corners to B",d:[["c",30,155,3,"F"],["l",33,155,90,155],["l",90,110,90,200],["c",90,155,2.5,"F"],["l",230,110,230,200],["l",230,155,287,155],["c",230,155,2.5,"F"],["c",290,155,3,"F"],["p","M90 110 L111.3 91.7 L110.4 86 L121.4 89.7 L119.5 78.2 L130.5 81.8 L128.6 70.3 L139.6 74 L138.7 68.3 L160 50"],["p","M160 50 L181.3 68.3 L186.9 66.4 L184.9 77.9 L196 74.3 L194 85.7 L205.1 82.1 L203.1 93.6 L208.7 91.7 L230 110"],["p","M90 110 L142 110 L145 105 L151 115 L157 105 L163 115 L169 105 L175 115 L178 110 L230 110"],["p","M90 200 L142 200 L145 195 L151 205 L157 195 L163 205 L169 195 L175 205 L178 200 L230 200"],["p","M90 200 L111.3 218.3 L116.9 216.4 L114.9 227.9 L126 224.3 L124 235.7 L135.1 232.1 L133.1 243.6 L138.7 241.7 L160 260"],["p","M160 260 L181.3 241.7 L180.4 236 L191.4 239.7 L189.5 228.2 L200.5 231.8 L198.6 220.3 L209.6 224 L208.7 218.3 L230 200"],["p","M90 110 L109.9 122.8 L115.1 120.2 L114.7 131.8 L125.2 126.7 L124.8 138.3 L135.3 133.2 L134.9 144.8 L140.1 142.2 L160 155"],["p","M160 155 L179.9 167.8 L185.1 165.2 L184.7 176.8 L195.2 171.7 L194.8 183.3 L205.3 178.2 L204.9 189.8 L210.1 187.2 L230 200"],["p","M90 200 L109.9 187.2 L109.7 181.4 L120.1 186.6 L119.8 174.9 L130.2 180.1 L129.9 168.4 L140.3 173.6 L140.1 167.8 L160 155"],["p","M160 155 L179.9 142.2 L179.7 136.4 L190.1 141.6 L189.8 129.9 L200.2 135.1 L199.9 123.4 L210.3 128.6 L210.1 122.8 L230 110"],["c",160,155,2.5,"F"],["t",30,141,"A","m"],["t",290,141,"B","m"],["t",112,76,"1.5 \u03A9","e"],["t",208,76,"0.5 \u03A9",""],["t",160,97,"12 \u03A9","m"],["t",160,222,"6 \u03A9","m"],["t",112,242,"1 \u03A9","e"],["t",208,242,"1 \u03A9",""],["t",104,146,"8 \u03A9",""],["t",216,146,"4 \u03A9","e"],["t",104,172,"2 \u03A9",""],["t",216,172,"6 \u03A9","e"]]},sfig:{w:320,h:215,alt:"Five resistors in parallel between A and B: 2, 12, 4, 6 and 2 ohm",d:[["l",60,30,60,190],["l",260,30,260,190],["c",20,110,3,"F"],["l",23,110,60,110],["c",60,110,2.5,"F"],["c",300,110,3,"F"],["l",297,110,260,110],["c",260,110,2.5,"F"],["p","M60 110 L130 110 L135 105 L145 115 L155 105 L165 115 L175 105 L185 115 L190 110 L260 110"],["p","M60 30 L130 30 L135 25 L145 35 L155 25 L165 35 L175 25 L185 35 L190 30 L260 30"],["p","M60 70 L130 70 L135 65 L145 75 L155 65 L165 75 L175 65 L185 75 L190 70 L260 70"],["p","M60 150 L130 150 L135 145 L145 155 L155 145 L165 155 L175 145 L185 155 L190 150 L260 150"],["p","M60 190 L130 190 L135 185 L145 195 L155 185 L165 195 L175 185 L185 195 L190 190 L260 190"],["t",20,96,"A","m"],["t",300,96,"B","m"],["t",160,22,"2 \u03A9","m"],["t",160,62,"12 \u03A9","m"],["t",160,142,"6 \u03A9","m"],["t",160,182,"2 \u03A9","m"],["t",160,102,"4 \u03A9","m"]]},o:["(2)/(3) \u03A9","(3)/(2) \u03A9","(1)/(3) \u03A9","(1)/(2) \u03A9"],c:0,s:`The left side of the figure is one thick wire joining the two left corners to A, and the right side joins the two right corners to B. So all resistors lie between the same two potentials: the whole network is a set of parallel branches (second figure).
Top path: 1.5 + 0.5 = 2 \u03A9. Bottom path: 1 + 1 = 2 \u03A9. Also 12 \u03A9 across the top and 6 \u03A9 across the bottom.
Centre part: the two diagonals meet at the centre joint. From the A side to the joint: 8 \u03A9 \u2225 2 \u03A9 = 16/10 = 1.6 \u03A9. From the joint to the B side: 4 \u03A9 \u2225 6 \u03A9 = 24/10 = 2.4 \u03A9. In series: 1.6 + 2.4 = 4 \u03A9.
Note: the printed figure shows the diagonals crossing without a dot; the printed key (2/3 \u03A9) is obtained only when the crossing is taken as a joint, so it is treated as one here.
Conductances add in parallel: 1/2 + 1/12 + 1/4 + 1/6 + 1/2 = (6 + 1 + 3 + 2 + 6)/12 = 18/12 = 3/2.
R_eq = 2/3 \u03A9, option (a).`},{pn:32,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cdrf",st:"Drift velocity and mobility",q:"The drift velocity of electrons for a conductor connected in an electrical circuit is V_d. The conductor is now replaced by another conductor with same material and same length but double the area of cross section. The applied voltage remains same. The new drift velocity of electrons will be",o:["V_d/4","V_d/2","2V_d","V_d"],c:3,s:`Drift velocity is linked to the electric field in the conductor: v_d = (e\u03C4/m)\xB7E, and E = V/l for a uniform conductor.
The material is the same, so \u03C4, m and the electron density are unchanged. The length l and the voltage V are also unchanged.
So E = V/l does not change, and therefore v_d does not change.
Note: doubling the area halves the resistance and doubles the current, but it also doubles the number of electrons carrying it, so each electron drifts at the same speed (I = neAv_d).
The drift velocity stays V_d, option (d).`},{pn:33,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cdrf",st:"Drift velocity and mobility",q:`(A) The drift velocity of electrons decreases with the increase in the temperature of conductor.
(B) The drift velocity is inversely proportional to the area of cross-section of given conductor.
(C) The drift velocity does not depend on the applied potential difference to the conductor.
(D) The drift velocity of electron is inversely proportional to the length of the conductor.
(E) The drift velocity increases with the increase in the temperature of conductor.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["(A) and (B) only","(A) and (D) only","(B) and (E) only","(B) and (C) only"],c:1,s:`Use v_d = (e\u03C4/m)\xB7E with E = V/l, so v_d = e\u03C4V/(ml).
(A) On heating, the ions vibrate more, collisions are more frequent, \u03C4 falls, so v_d decreases. True.
(D) For a fixed potential difference v_d \u221D 1/l. True.
(B) At fixed V the formula has no area in it, so v_d does not depend on the area. False.
(C) v_d \u221D V, so it does depend on the applied potential difference. False.
(E) A temperature rise decreases v_d, it does not increase it. False.
Only (A) and (D) are correct, option (b).`},{pn:34,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cres",st:"Stretching and shape change of wires",q:"A wire of resistance R\u2081 is drawn out so that its length is increased by twice of its original length. The ratio of new resistance to original resistance is",o:["9 : 1","1 : 9","4 : 1","3 : 1"],c:0,s:`"Increased by twice of its original length" means the extra length is 2l, so the new length is l + 2l = 3l.
Volume is constant (A\xB7l = constant), so the area becomes A/3.
R = \u03C1l/A, so R\u2032 = \u03C1(3l)/(A/3) = 9\u03C1l/A = 9R\u2081.
Note: R \u221D l\xB2 when the volume is fixed, so a length factor of 3 gives 3\xB2 = 9.
R\u2032 : R\u2081 = 9 : 1, option (a).`},{pn:35,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cres",st:"Resistance and resistivity",q:"Two metallic wires of identical dimensions are connected in series. If \u03C3\u2081 and \u03C3\u2082 are the conductivities of these wires respectively, the effective conductivity of the combination is",o:["\u03C3\u2081\u03C3\u2082/(\u03C3\u2081 + \u03C3\u2082)","2\u03C3\u2081\u03C3\u2082/(\u03C3\u2081 + \u03C3\u2082)","(\u03C3\u2081 + \u03C3\u2082)/(2\u03C3\u2081\u03C3\u2082)","(\u03C3\u2081 + \u03C3\u2082)/(\u03C3\u2081\u03C3\u2082)"],c:1,s:`Conductivity is the inverse of resistivity, so a wire of length l and area A has R = l/(\u03C3A).
Let each wire have length l and area A. In series the resistances add: R = l/(\u03C3\u2081A) + l/(\u03C3\u2082A).
The combination is one wire of length 2l and the same area A, with effective conductivity \u03C3: R = 2l/(\u03C3A).
Equate: 2/\u03C3 = 1/\u03C3\u2081 + 1/\u03C3\u2082 = (\u03C3\u2081 + \u03C3\u2082)/(\u03C3\u2081\u03C3\u2082).
\u03C3 = 2\u03C3\u2081\u03C3\u2082/(\u03C3\u2081 + \u03C3\u2082), option (b).`},{pn:36,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cres",st:"Stretching and shape change of wires",q:"A 1 m long wire is broken into two unequal parts X and Y. The X part of the wire is stretched into another wire W. Length of W is twice the length of X and the resistance of W is twice that of Y. Find the ratio of length of X and Y.",o:["1 : 4","1 : 2","4 : 1","2 : 1"],c:1,s:`Let X have length x and Y have length y; both come from the same wire, so they have the same area A and resistivity \u03C1.
R_Y = \u03C1y/A.
Stretching X to twice its length halves its area (volume is constant), so R_W = \u03C1(2x)/(A/2) = 4\u03C1x/A.
Given R_W = 2R_Y: 4\u03C1x/A = 2\u03C1y/A, so 4x = 2y, i.e. y = 2x.
Therefore x : y = 1 : 2 (x = 1/3 m and y = 2/3 m, which add up to 1 m).
The ratio is 1 : 2, option (b).`},{pn:37,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 24 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ccel",st:"Grouping of cells",q:"Two identical cells each of emf 1.5 V are connected in parallel across a parallel combination of two resistors each of resistance 20 \u03A9. A voltmeter connected in the circuit measures 1.2 V. The internal resistance of each cell is",o:["2.5 \u03A9","4 \u03A9","5 \u03A9","10 \u03A9"],c:2,s:`Two identical cells in parallel act like one cell of emf 1.5 V and internal resistance r/2.
Two 20 \u03A9 resistors in parallel give the external resistance R = 10 \u03A9.
The voltmeter reads the terminal voltage across R: V = 1.2 V, so I = V/R = 1.2/10 = 0.12 A.
Terminal voltage = emf \u2212 I \xD7 (internal resistance): 1.2 = 1.5 \u2212 0.12 \xD7 (r/2).
0.12 \xD7 (r/2) = 0.3, so r/2 = 2.5 \u03A9.
Therefore r = 5 \u03A9, option (c).`},{pn:38,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 24 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ccmb",st:"Series and parallel combinations",q:`What will be the most suitable combination of three resistors A = 2 \u03A9, B = 4 \u03A9, C = 6 \u03A9, so that equivalent resistance of combination is (22/3) \u03A9?
(a) Parallel combination of A and C connected in series with B.
(b) Parallel combination of A and B connected in series with C.
(c) Series combination of A and C connected in parallel with B.
(d) Series combination of B and C connected in parallel with A.`,o:["Parallel combination of A and C connected in series with B","Parallel combination of A and B connected in series with C","Series combination of A and C connected in parallel with B","Series combination of B and C connected in parallel with A"],c:1,s:`Test each arrangement and compare with the target 22/3 \u03A9 \u2248 7.33 \u03A9.
(a) A \u2225 C = (2 \xD7 6)/(2 + 6) = 1.5 \u03A9, plus B: 5.5 \u03A9. Not equal.
(b) A \u2225 B = (2 \xD7 4)/(2 + 4) = 4/3 \u03A9, plus C: 4/3 + 6 = 22/3 \u03A9. Matches.
(c) (A + C) \u2225 B = (8 \xD7 4)/12 = 8/3 \u03A9. Not equal.
(d) (B + C) \u2225 A = (10 \xD7 2)/12 = 5/3 \u03A9. Not equal.
Only arrangement (b) gives 22/3 \u03A9, option (b).`},{pn:39,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cres",st:"Stretching and shape change of wires",q:"An aluminium wire is stretched to make its length, 0.4% larger. The percentage change in resistance is",o:["0.4%","0.2%","0.8%","0.6%"],c:2,s:`When a wire is stretched, its volume stays constant, so R = \u03C1l/A becomes R \u221D l\xB2.
For a small change, \u0394R/R = 2 \xD7 \u0394l/l (the length rise and the area fall each add the same amount).
\u0394l/l = 0.4%, so \u0394R/R = 2 \xD7 0.4% = 0.8%.
Note: exact working gives (1.004)\xB2 \u2212 1 = 0.008016, which is 0.8% to the accuracy asked.
The resistance increases by 0.8%, option (c).`},{pn:40,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ccmb",st:"Equivalent resistance of networks",q:"The equivalent resistance between points A and B in the given network is",fig:{w:290,h:205,alt:"Network between A and B: from A, two 5 ohm resistors in series reach the top node; a chain of 5 ohm resistors continues across the top, down and to B; three 10 ohm resistors run from A to the three upper nodes; a 10 ohm resistor joins A to B directly",d:[["l",45,170,45,30],["l",45,30,125,30],["p","M45 170 L45 158 L40 155 L50 149 L40 143 L50 137 L40 131 L50 125 L45 122 L45 110"],["p","M45 110 L45 98 L40 95 L50 89 L40 83 L50 77 L40 71 L50 65 L45 62 L45 50"],["p","M125 30 L157 30 L160 25 L166 35 L172 25 L178 35 L184 25 L190 35 L193 30 L225 30"],["p","M225 30 L225 47 L230 50 L220 56 L230 62 L220 68 L230 74 L220 80 L225 83 L225 100"],["p","M225 100 L225 117 L230 120 L220 126 L230 132 L220 138 L230 144 L220 150 L225 153 L225 170"],["p","M75 170 L93.9 117 L90.2 112.4 L101.7 110.2 L94.3 101.1 L105.7 98.9 L98.3 89.8 L109.8 87.6 L106.1 83 L125 30"],["p","M75 170 L136.8 112.3 L135.6 106.6 L146.8 109.8 L144.4 98.4 L155.6 101.6 L153.2 90.2 L164.4 93.4 L163.2 87.7 L225 30"],["p","M75 170 L133.7 142.6 L134.3 136.8 L144 143.3 L145.2 131.7 L154.8 138.3 L156 126.7 L165.7 133.2 L166.3 127.4 L225 100"],["l",45,170,75,170],["p","M75 170 L130 170 L133.3 165 L140 175 L146.7 165 L153.3 175 L160 165 L166.7 175 L170 170 L225 170"],["c",75,170,2.5,"F"],["c",125,30,2.5,"F"],["c",225,30,2.5,"F"],["c",225,100,2.5,"F"],["c",225,170,2.5,"F"],["t",33,85,"5 \u03A9","e"],["t",33,145,"5 \u03A9","e"],["t",175,22,"5 \u03A9","m"],["t",235,69,"5 \u03A9",""],["t",235,139,"5 \u03A9",""],["t",150,190,"10 \u03A9","m"],["t",92,108,"10 \u03A9","e"],["t",170,100,"10 \u03A9",""],["t",165,152,"10 \u03A9","m"],["t",75,186,"A","m"],["t",225,186,"B","m"]]},o:["65 \u03A9","20 \u03A9","5 \u03A9","2 \u03A9"],c:2,s:`Start from the end of the chain near A and fold the network step by step. Call the upper nodes P (first, top left), Q (next), S (the lower one on the right), then B.
Between A and P: the two 5 \u03A9 on the left are in series = 10 \u03A9, parallel with the 10 \u03A9 chord: (10 \xD7 10)/20 = 5 \u03A9.
Between A and Q: that 5 \u03A9 plus the 5 \u03A9 of the top wire = 10 \u03A9, parallel with the next 10 \u03A9 chord: 5 \u03A9.
Between A and S: 5 \u03A9 plus the next 5 \u03A9 = 10 \u03A9, parallel with the 10 \u03A9 chord: 5 \u03A9.
Between A and B: 5 \u03A9 plus the last 5 \u03A9 = 10 \u03A9, parallel with the bottom 10 \u03A9: 5 \u03A9.
Note: every step has the same pattern, "10 \u03A9 in parallel with 10 \u03A9 gives 5 \u03A9", so the answer repeats.
The equivalent resistance is 5 \u03A9, option (c).`},{pn:41,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cres",st:"Temperature dependence",q:"Resistance of the wire is measured as 2 \u03A9 and 3 \u03A9 at 10 \xB0C and 30 \xB0C respectively. Temperature coefficient of resistance of the material of the wire is",o:["0.033 \xB0C\u207B\xB9","\u22120.033 \xB0C\u207B\xB9","0.011 \xB0C\u207B\xB9","0.055 \xB0C\u207B\xB9"],c:0,s:`Resistance varies with temperature as R = R\u2080(1 + \u03B1t), where R\u2080 is the resistance at 0 \xB0C and t is in \xB0C.
At 10 \xB0C: 2 = R\u2080(1 + 10\u03B1). At 30 \xB0C: 3 = R\u2080(1 + 30\u03B1).
Divide the second by the first: 3/2 = (1 + 30\u03B1)/(1 + 10\u03B1).
Cross-multiply: 3 + 30\u03B1 = 2 + 60\u03B1, so 30\u03B1 = 1.
\u03B1 = 1/30 \u2248 0.033 \xB0C\u207B\xB9, option (a).
Note: using the other common form \u03B1 = \u0394R/(R\u2081\u0394T) would give 0.025, which is not an option; the options follow the R\u2080 (0 \xB0C) definition.`},{pn:42,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cres",st:"Resistance and resistivity",q:"What equal length of an iron wire and a copper-nickel alloy wire, each of 2 mm diameter connected parallel to give an equivalent resistance of 3 \u03A9? (Given: resistivities of iron and copper-nickel alloy wire are 12 \u03BC\u03A9 cm and 51 \u03BC\u03A9 cm respectively)",o:["97 m","110 m","90 m","82 m"],c:0,s:`Both wires have the same length l and area A = \u03C0(d/2)\xB2 = \u03C0(1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3)\xB2 = 3.14 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB2.
Convert resistivities: 12 \u03BC\u03A9 cm = 12 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 \u03A9 m = 1.2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \u03A9 m, and 51 \u03BC\u03A9 cm = 5.1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \u03A9 m.
Parallel: 1/R = A/(\u03C1\u2081l) + A/(\u03C1\u2082l), so R = (\u03C1\u2081\u03C1\u2082/(\u03C1\u2081 + \u03C1\u2082)) \xD7 (l/A).
\u03C1\u2081\u03C1\u2082/(\u03C1\u2081 + \u03C1\u2082) = (1.2 \xD7 5.1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2074)/(6.3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077) = 9.71 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 \u03A9 m.
So l = R\xB7A/(9.71 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078) = (3 \xD7 3.14 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076)/(9.71 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078) \u2248 97 m.
The length is 97 m, option (a).`},{pn:43,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Aug \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ccmb",st:"Series and parallel combinations",q:"If you are provided a set of resistances 2 \u03A9, 4 \u03A9, 6 \u03A9 and 8 \u03A9. Connect these resistances so as to obtain an equivalent resistance of (46)/(3) \u03A9.",o:["2 \u03A9 and 6 \u03A9 are in parallel with 4 \u03A9 and 8 \u03A9 in series.","4 \u03A9 and 6 \u03A9 are in parallel with 2 \u03A9 and 8 \u03A9 in series.","2 \u03A9 and 4 \u03A9 are in parallel with 6 \u03A9 and 8 \u03A9 in series.","6 \u03A9 and 8 \u03A9 are in parallel with 2 \u03A9 and 4 \u03A9 in series."],c:2,s:`The target 46/3 \u2248 15.3 \u03A9 is large, so most resistors must be in series. The wording means: one pair is joined in parallel, and this parallel pair is then put in series with the other two joined in series.
(c) 2 \u2225 4 = (2 \xD7 4)/6 = 4/3 \u03A9, and 6 + 8 = 14 \u03A9. Total = 4/3 + 14 = 46/3 \u03A9. Matches.
Check the others to be sure: (a) 2 \u2225 6 = 1.5, plus 12 = 13.5 \u03A9. (b) 4 \u2225 6 = 2.4, plus 10 = 12.4 \u03A9. (d) 6 \u2225 8 = 24/7, plus 6 = 9.43 \u03A9.
Only (c) gives 46/3 \u03A9, option (c).`},{pn:44,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Aug \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cres",st:"Colour code of resistors",q:"The colour coding on a carbon resistor is shown in the given figure. The resistance value of the given resistor is",fig:{w:330,h:170,alt:"A carbon resistor with four colour bands near one end: violet, green, red, and a separated gold band; leader lines name each band",d:[["l",20,50,60,50],["p","M60 30 A8 18 0 0 0 60 70 A8 18 0 0 0 60 30"],["l",60,30,250,30],["l",60,70,250,70],["p","M250 30 A10 20 0 0 1 250 70"],["l",260,50,310,50],["p","M130 30 A5 20 0 0 1 130 70"],["p","M148 30 A5 20 0 0 1 148 70"],["p","M166 30 A5 20 0 0 1 166 70"],["p","M200 30 A5 20 0 0 1 200 70"],["l",130,70,130,150],["l",130,150,262,150],["l",148,70,148,125],["l",148,125,262,125],["l",166,70,166,100],["l",166,100,262,100],["l",200,70,200,75],["l",200,75,262,75],["t",268,79,"Gold",""],["t",268,104,"Red",""],["t",268,129,"Green",""],["t",268,154,"Violet",""]]},o:["(7500 \xB1 375) \u03A9","(5700 \xB1 285) \u03A9","(7500 \xB1 755) \u03A9","(5700 \xB1 375) \u03A9"],c:0,s:`Read the bands from the end where they are close together. The first two bands give digits, the third is the multiplier (power of ten) and the fourth is the tolerance.
Violet = 7, Green = 5, so the digits are 75.
Red as the multiplier = 10\xB2, so R = 75 \xD7 10\xB2 = 7500 \u03A9.
Gold as tolerance = \xB15%.
5% of 7500 = 0.05 \xD7 7500 = 375 \u03A9.
So R = (7500 \xB1 375) \u03A9, option (a).`},{pn:45,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 31 Aug \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ccmb",st:"Equivalent resistance of networks",q:"The equivalent resistance of the given circuit between the terminals A and B is",fig:{w:340,h:175,alt:"Ladder circuit between A and B: top wire has two 2 ohm resistors; vertical branches of 5, 2, 3 and 3 ohm join top and bottom wires; the left end of the top wire is joined by a plain wire to the bottom wire, so the 5 ohm branch is shorted",d:[["l",30,50,30,150],["l",30,50,60,50],["l",30,150,300,150],["p","M60 50 L82 50 L85 45 L91 55 L97 45 L103 55 L109 45 L115 55 L118 50 L140 50"],["p","M140 50 L162 50 L165 45 L171 55 L177 45 L183 55 L189 45 L195 55 L198 50 L220 50"],["l",220,50,265,50],["l",60,50,60,150],["p","M60 70 L60 82 L65 85 L55 91 L65 97 L55 103 L65 109 L55 115 L60 118 L60 130"],["l",140,50,140,150],["p","M140 70 L140 82 L145 85 L135 91 L145 97 L135 103 L145 109 L135 115 L140 118 L140 130"],["l",220,50,220,150],["p","M220 70 L220 82 L225 85 L215 91 L225 97 L215 103 L225 109 L215 115 L220 118 L220 130"],["l",265,50,265,150],["p","M265 70 L265 82 L270 85 L260 91 L270 97 L260 103 L270 109 L260 115 L265 118 L265 130"],["l",265,50,300,50],["c",60,50,2.5,"F"],["c",140,50,2.5,"F"],["c",220,50,2.5,"F"],["c",265,50,2.5,"F"],["c",60,150,2.5,"F"],["c",140,150,2.5,"F"],["c",220,150,2.5,"F"],["c",265,150,2.5,"F"],["c",300,50,3,"F"],["c",300,150,3,"F"],["t",100,40,"2 \u03A9","m"],["t",180,40,"2 \u03A9","m"],["t",70,104,"5 \u03A9",""],["t",150,104,"2 \u03A9",""],["t",228,104,"3 \u03A9",""],["t",273,104,"3 \u03A9",""],["t",310,54,"A",""],["t",310,154,"B",""]]},sfig:{w:290,h:185,alt:"After folding the right and left parts: a 3 ohm branch in parallel with a 1.5 ohm branch between A and B",d:[["c",20,100,3,"F"],["l",23,100,60,100],["c",60,100,2.5,"F"],["l",60,50,60,150],["p","M60 50 L120 50 L123.3 45 L130 55 L136.7 45 L143.3 55 L150 45 L156.7 55 L160 50 L220 50"],["p","M60 150 L120 150 L123.3 145 L130 155 L136.7 145 L143.3 155 L150 145 L156.7 155 L160 150 L220 150"],["l",220,50,220,150],["c",220,100,2.5,"F"],["l",220,100,257,100],["c",260,100,3,"F"],["t",20,86,"A","m"],["t",260,86,"B","m"],["t",140,40,"3 \u03A9","m"],["t",140,170,"1.5 \u03A9","m"]]},o:["0 \u03A9","(9)/(2) \u03A9","3 \u03A9","1 \u03A9"],c:3,s:`Look at the left end of the figure: a plain wire joins the top wire to the bottom wire there. The 5 \u03A9 resistor is connected across this wire, so it is short-circuited and carries no current. Ignore it.
The left 2 \u03A9 (top) and the 2 \u03A9 (vertical) now both connect the same point to the bottom wire, so they are in parallel: (2 \xD7 2)/(2 + 2) = 1 \u03A9.
This 1 \u03A9 is in series with the second top 2 \u03A9: 1 + 2 = 3 \u03A9.
The two 3 \u03A9 resistors on the right are in parallel: 3/2 = 1.5 \u03A9.
Now 3 \u03A9 and 1.5 \u03A9 are in parallel between A and B (second figure): (3 \xD7 1.5)/(3 + 1.5) = 4.5/4.5 = 1 \u03A9.
The equivalent resistance is 1 \u03A9, option (d).`},{pn:46,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 20 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cdrf",st:"Current density",q:"A current of 5 A is passing through a non-linear magnesium wire of cross-section 0.04 m\xB2. At every point the direction of current density is at an angle of 60\xB0 with the unit vector area of cross-section. The magnitude of electric field at every point of the conductor is (Resistivity of magnesium \u03C1 = 44 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 \u03A9 m)",o:["11 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 V m\u207B\xB9","11 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 V m\u207B\xB9","11 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 V m\u207B\xB9","11 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 V m\u207B\xB9"],c:0,s:`Current is the flux of current density through the area: I = J\xB7A = J A cos \u03B8, where \u03B8 is the angle between J and the area vector.
Here \u03B8 = 60\xB0, so cos 60\xB0 = 1/2 and J = I/(A cos \u03B8) = 5/(0.04 \xD7 0.5) = 250 A m\u207B\xB2.
Ohm's law in the point form is E = \u03C1J.
E = 44 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 \xD7 250 = 11000 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 = 1.1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 V m\u207B\xB9.
1.1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 = 11 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 V m\u207B\xB9, option (a).`},{pn:47,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 22 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cres",st:"Resistance and resistivity",q:"A copper (Cu) rod of length 25 cm and cross-sectional area 3 mm\xB2 is joined with a similar aluminium (Al) rod as shown in figure. Find the resistance of the combination between the ends A and B. (Take resistivity of copper = 1.7 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 \u03A9 m, resistivity of aluminium = 2.6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 \u03A9 m).",fig:{w:345,h:170,alt:"Copper rod and aluminium rod of equal length 25 cm placed side by side, with both ends joined to terminals A and B, so they are in parallel",d:[["c",20,75,3,"F"],["l",23,75,60,75],["l",60,40,60,110],["c",60,75,2.5,"F"],["l",60,40,100,40],["l",60,110,100,110],["p","M100 25 A6 15 0 0 0 100 55 A6 15 0 0 0 100 25"],["l",100,25,240,25],["l",100,55,240,55],["p","M240 25 A6 15 0 0 1 240 55"],["p","M100 95 A6 15 0 0 0 100 125 A6 15 0 0 0 100 95"],["l",100,95,240,95],["l",100,125,240,125],["p","M240 95 A6 15 0 0 1 240 125"],["l",246,40,280,40],["l",246,110,280,110],["l",280,40,280,110],["c",280,75,2.5,"F"],["l",280,75,317,75],["c",320,75,3,"F"],["t",170,45,"Cu","m"],["t",170,115,"Al","m"],["t",20,61,"A","m"],["t",320,61,"B","m"],["l",100,142,240,142,"b"],["t",170,158,"25 cm","m"]]},o:["2.170 m\u03A9","0.858 m\u03A9","0.0858 m\u03A9","1.420 m\u03A9"],c:1,s:`In the figure both rods run from A to B side by side, so the two resistances are in parallel.
Length l = 25 cm = 0.25 m and area A = 3 mm\xB2 = 3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB2.
R_Cu = \u03C1l/A = (1.7 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 \xD7 0.25)/(3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076) = 1.417 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \u03A9 = 1.417 m\u03A9.
R_Al = (2.6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 \xD7 0.25)/(3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076) = 2.167 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \u03A9 = 2.167 m\u03A9.
Parallel: R = (1.417 \xD7 2.167)/(1.417 + 2.167) = 3.070/3.583 \u2248 0.857 m\u03A9.
This is about 0.858 m\u03A9, option (b).`},{pn:48,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cdrf",st:"Drift velocity and mobility",q:"In the given figure, a battery of emf E is connected across a conductor PQ of length l and different area of cross-section having radii r\u2081 and r\u2082 (r\u2082 < r\u2081). Choose the correct option as one moves from P to Q.",fig:{w:340,h:205,alt:"Battery E and a key K in a loop with a conductor PQ that is wide (radius r1) at P and narrow (radius r2) at Q; an arrow inside shows the current from P to Q",d:[["l",40,50,40,95],["c",40,110,14],["l",40,118,40,102,"a"],["l",40,125,40,175],["t",52,90,"\u2212",""],["t",52,133,"+",""],["l",40,175,100,175],["l",100,164,100,186],["l",105,169,105,181,"B"],["l",105,175,227,175],["c",233,175,5],["c",233,175,2.5,"F"],["l",239,175,310,175],["l",310,175,310,50],["l",255,50,310,50],["l",40,50,105,50],["p","M110 38 A5 12 0 0 0 110 62 A5 12 0 0 0 110 38"],["l",110,38,250,45],["l",110,62,250,55],["p","M250 45 A2.5 5 0 0 0 250 55 A2.5 5 0 0 0 250 45"],["l",125,50,185,50,"a"],["t",110,26,"r\u2081","m"],["t",250,34,"r\u2082","m"],["t",90,40,"P","e"],["t",270,44,"Q",""],["t",92,168,"+","e"],["t",113,168,"\u2212",""],["t",102,196,"E","m"],["t",233,196,"K","m"]]},o:["Electric field decreases.","Drift velocity of electron increases.","Electron current decreases.","All of these."],c:1,s:`The conductor is one continuous wire in a series loop, so the same current I flows through every cross-section from P to Q. Option (c) is therefore wrong.
Current density J = I/A. The area shrinks from P (radius r\u2081) to Q (radius r\u2082 < r\u2081), so J increases towards Q.
The field follows E = \u03C1J, so E increases too. Option (a) is therefore wrong.
Drift velocity v_d = I/(neA): with I and n constant and A decreasing, v_d increases from P to Q. Option (b) is right.
Note: the narrow part has the larger resistance per unit length, so the electrons must move faster there to carry the same current.
Only (b) is correct, so (d) "all of these" is also wrong.`},{pn:49,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cres",st:"Temperature dependence",q:"The resistance of a conductor at 15\xB0C is 16 \u03A9 and at 100\xB0C is 20 \u03A9. What will be the temperature coefficient of resistance of the conductor?",o:["0.042\xB0C\u207B\xB9","0.003\xB0C\u207B\xB9","0.033\xB0C\u207B\xB9","0.010\xB0C\u207B\xB9"],c:1,s:`For a small temperature range a conductor obeys R = R\u2080(1 + \u03B1\u0394T), where \u03B1 is the temperature coefficient.
Use the two readings: R\u2081 = 16 \u03A9 at 15\xB0C and R\u2082 = 20 \u03A9 at 100\xB0C.
Take R\u2081 as the reference: R\u2082 = R\u2081[1 + \u03B1(100 \u2212 15)].
20 = 16(1 + 85\u03B1), so 1 + 85\u03B1 = 20/16 = 1.25.
85\u03B1 = 0.25, so \u03B1 = 0.25/85 \u2248 2.9 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \xB0C\u207B\xB9.
Note: the nearest option is 0.003, as the question only asks for an approximate value.
So \u03B1 \u2248 0.003 \xB0C\u207B\xB9, which is option (2).`},{pn:50,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 16 Mar \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cres",st:"Stretching and shape change of wires",q:"A conducting wire of length l, area of cross-section A and electric resistivity \u03C1 is connected between the terminals of a battery. A potential difference V is developed between its ends, causing an electric current. If the length of the wire of the same material is doubled and the area of cross-section is halved, the resultant current would be",o:["(1)/(4)\xB7(VA)/(\u03C1l)","4\xB7(VA)/(\u03C1l)","(1)/(4)\xB7(\u03C1l)/(VA)","(3)/(4)\xB7(VA)/(\u03C1l)"],c:0,s:`Resistance of a wire is R = \u03C1l/A, and the current from a battery of fixed V is I = V/R.
New length is 2l and new area is A/2; the material (\u03C1) is the same.
New resistance R\u2032 = \u03C1(2l)/(A/2) = 4\u03C1l/A.
So the resistance becomes 4 times the old one.
Current I\u2032 = V/R\u2032 = V/(4\u03C1l/A) = (VA)/(4\u03C1l).
So the current is (1)/(4)\xB7(VA)/(\u03C1l), option (1).`},{pn:51,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 17 Mar \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cdrf",st:"Drift velocity and mobility",q:"A current of 10 A exists in a wire of cross-sectional area 5 mm\xB2 with a drift velocity of 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m s\u207B\xB9. The number of free electrons in each cubic metre of the wire is",o:["2 \xD7 10\u2076","625 \xD7 10\xB2\u2075","2 \xD7 10\xB2\u2075","1 \xD7 10\xB2\xB3"],c:1,s:`Current in a conductor is I = nAev_d, where n is the number of free electrons per m\xB3.
So n = I/(Aev_d).
Convert the area: A = 5 mm\xB2 = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB2.
Denominator: 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 \xD7 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077.
n = 10/(1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077) = 6.25 \xD7 10\xB2\u2077 m\u207B\xB3 = 625 \xD7 10\xB2\u2075 m\u207B\xB3.
This is option (2).`},{pn:52,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 24 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cdrf",st:"Current and charge",q:"A current through a wire depends on time as i = \u03B1\u2080t + \u03B2t\xB2, where \u03B1\u2080 = 20 A s\u207B\xB9 and \u03B2 = 8 A s\u207B\xB2. Find the charge crossed through a section of the wire in 15 s.",o:["2250 C","11250 C","21000 C","260 C"],c:1,s:`Current is the rate of flow of charge, i = dq/dt, so the charge is the integral of i over time.
q = \u222B\u2080\xB9\u2075 (\u03B1\u2080t + \u03B2t\xB2) dt = \u03B1\u2080t\xB2/2 + \u03B2t\xB3/3 evaluated from 0 to 15 s.
First term: 20 \xD7 15\xB2/2 = 10 \xD7 225 = 2250 C.
Second term: 8 \xD7 15\xB3/3 = 8 \xD7 3375/3 = 9000 C.
Total q = 2250 + 9000 = 11250 C.
This is option (2).`},{pn:53,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cres",st:"Stretching and shape change of wires",q:"A wire of 1 \u03A9 has a length of 1 m. It is stretched till its length increases by 25 %. The percentage change in resistance to the nearest integer is",o:["56%","76%","12.5%","25%"],c:0,s:`When a wire is stretched its volume stays constant: Al = constant, so the area falls as the length rises.
Then R = \u03C1l/A = \u03C1l\xB2/V, so R is proportional to l\xB2.
New length is 1.25 times the old, so R\u2032/R = (1.25)\xB2 = 1.5625.
The resistance rises to 1.5625 \u03A9, a change of 0.5625 = 56.25 %.
To the nearest integer, 56 %, option (1).
Note: the shortcut 25 % + 25 % = 50 % is only right for tiny stretches; use the square for 25 %.`},{pn:54,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 2 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cres",st:"Resistance and resistivity",q:"Consider four conducting materials copper, tungsten, mercury and aluminium with resistivity \u03C1_C, \u03C1_T, \u03C1_M and \u03C1_A respectively. Then",o:["\u03C1_C > \u03C1_A > \u03C1_T","\u03C1_M > \u03C1_A > \u03C1_C","\u03C1_A > \u03C1_T > \u03C1_C","\u03C1_A > \u03C1_M > \u03C1_C"],c:1,s:`This needs the known room-temperature resistivities (in \u03A9 m).
Copper 1.7 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078, aluminium 2.7 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078, tungsten 5.6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078, mercury 9.4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077.
So the order is \u03C1_M > \u03C1_T > \u03C1_A > \u03C1_C.
Check each option: (1) says copper is greater than aluminium, false. (3) says \u03C1_A > \u03C1_T, false. (4) says \u03C1_A > \u03C1_M, false.
Option (2), \u03C1_M > \u03C1_A > \u03C1_C, is true.`},{pn:55,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 6 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cpot",st:"Galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"A circuit to verify Ohm's law uses an ammeter and a voltmeter in series or parallel connected correctly to the resistor. In the circuit",o:["ammeter is always used in parallel and voltmeter in series","both ammeter and voltmeter must be connected in parallel","ammeter is always connected in series and voltmeter in parallel","both ammeter and voltmeter must be connected in series."],c:2,s:`An ammeter measures the current through the resistor, so the same current must pass through it: it is connected in series.
An ideal ammeter has very low resistance, so it does not change the current.
A voltmeter measures the potential difference across the resistor, so it is connected across it, in parallel.
An ideal voltmeter has very high resistance, so it draws almost no current.
So the ammeter is in series and the voltmeter in parallel, option (3).`},{pn:56,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cres",st:"Colour code of resistors",q:"A 200 \u03A9 resistor has a certain colour code. If one replaces the red colour by green in the code, the new resistance will be",o:["100 \u03A9","500 \u03A9","300 \u03A9","400 \u03A9"],c:1,s:`Colour code: first two bands give digits, the third band gives the power of ten (black 0, brown 1, red 2, orange 3, yellow 4, green 5).
200 \u03A9 = 20 \xD7 10\xB9, so the bands are red (2), black (0), brown (\xD710).
Replace the red (first band) by green: the first digit becomes 5.
The new value is 50 \xD7 10\xB9 = 500 \u03A9.
This is option (2).
Note: if the red were the multiplier band the value would differ, but 200 \u03A9 has red only as the first digit.`},{pn:57,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ckir",st:"Kirchhoff's laws",q:"In the figure shown, what is the current (in ampere) drawn from the battery? You are given: R\u2081 = 15 \u03A9, R\u2082 = 10 \u03A9, R\u2083 = 20 \u03A9, R\u2084 = 5 \u03A9, R\u2085 = 25 \u03A9, R\u2086 = 30 \u03A9, E = 15 V",o:["20/3","9/32","7/18","13/24"],c:1,s:`Read the circuit: R\u2083, R\u2084, R\u2085 are in series along the right-hand path, and this whole path is in parallel with R\u2082 (see the solution figure).
R\u2083 + R\u2084 + R\u2085 = 20 + 5 + 25 = 50 \u03A9.
Parallel of 10 \u03A9 and 50 \u03A9: (10 \xD7 50)/(10 + 50) = 500/60 = 25/3 \u03A9.
R\u2081 and R\u2086 are in series with this block, because all the current from the battery passes through them.
Total R = 15 + 25/3 + 30 = 160/3 \u03A9.
I = E/R = 15 \xD7 3/160 = 9/32 A, option (2).`,fig:{w:340,h:215,alt:"Circuit: battery E on the left; R1 in the top-left arm, R6 in the bottom-left arm, R2 in the middle vertical arm, and R3, R4, R5 in series round the right side",d:[["l",50,40,50,97],["l",39,97,61,97],["l",44,103,56,103,"B"],["l",50,103,50,170],["t",34,100,"E","e"],["p","M50 40 L90 40 L93.3 35 L100 45 L106.7 35 L113.3 45 L120 35 L126.7 45 L130 40 L170 40"],["t",110,30,"R\u2081","m"],["p","M170 40 L210 40 L213.3 35 L220 45 L226.7 35 L233.3 45 L240 35 L246.7 45 L250 40 L290 40"],["t",230,30,"R\u2083","m"],["p","M290 40 L290 85 L295 88.3 L285 95 L295 101.7 L285 108.3 L295 115 L285 121.7 L290 125 L290 170"],["t",302,108,"R\u2084",""],["p","M170 170 L210 170 L213.3 165 L220 175 L226.7 165 L233.3 175 L240 165 L246.7 175 L250 170 L290 170"],["t",230,192,"R\u2085","m"],["p","M50 170 L90 170 L93.3 165 L100 175 L106.7 165 L113.3 175 L120 165 L126.7 175 L130 170 L170 170"],["t",110,192,"R\u2086","m"],["p","M170 40 L170 85 L175 88.3 L165 95 L175 101.7 L165 108.3 L175 115 L165 121.7 L170 125 L170 170"],["t",182,108,"R\u2082",""],["c",170,40,2.6,"F"],["c",170,170,2.6,"F"]]},sfig:{w:340,h:215,alt:"Equivalent circuit: 15 ohm in series with the parallel pair 10 ohm and 50 ohm, then 30 ohm, across the 15 V battery",d:[["l",50,40,50,97],["l",39,97,61,97],["l",44,103,56,103,"B"],["l",50,103,50,170],["t",34,100,"15 V","e"],["p","M50 40 L120 40 L123.3 35 L130 45 L136.7 35 L143.3 45 L150 35 L156.7 45 L160 40 L230 40"],["t",140,30,"15 \u03A9","m"],["l",230,40,290,40],["p","M230 40 L230 85 L235 88.3 L225 95 L235 101.7 L225 108.3 L235 115 L225 121.7 L230 125 L230 170"],["t",218,108,"10 \u03A9","e"],["p","M290 40 L290 85 L295 88.3 L285 95 L295 101.7 L285 108.3 L295 115 L285 121.7 L290 125 L290 170"],["t",302,108,"50 \u03A9",""],["l",230,170,290,170],["p","M50 170 L120 170 L123.3 165 L130 175 L136.7 165 L143.3 175 L150 165 L156.7 175 L160 170 L230 170"],["t",140,192,"30 \u03A9","m"],["c",230,40,2.6,"F"],["c",230,170,2.6,"F"]]}},{pn:58,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ccmb",st:"Equivalent resistance of networks",q:"A wire of resistance R is bent to form a square ABCD as shown in the figure. The effective resistance between E and C is (E is mid-point of arm CD)",o:["(7)/(64)R","R","(1)/(16)R","(3)/(4)R"],c:0,s:`The wire is uniform, so resistance is proportional to length. Each side of the square has R/4.
E is the mid-point of CD, so the piece EC has R/8, and ED also has R/8.
From E there are two paths to C: the short one, EC = R/8.
The long one goes E \u2192 D \u2192 A \u2192 B \u2192 C: R/8 + R/4 + R/4 + R/4 = 7R/8.
These two paths are in parallel (solution figure).
R_eq = (R/8 \xD7 7R/8)/(R/8 + 7R/8) = (7R\xB2/64)/R = 7R/64, option (1).`,fig:{w:240,h:200,alt:"Square wire ABCD with A top left, B top right, C bottom right and D bottom left; E is the mid-point of the bottom side DC",d:[["r",60,30,120,120],["t",52,26,"A","e"],["t",188,26,"B",""],["t",188,168,"C",""],["t",52,168,"D","e"],["c",120,150,2.6,"F"],["t",120,172,"E","m"]]},sfig:{w:340,h:190,alt:"Between E and C: the short piece EC of resistance R/8 in parallel with the long way E-D-A-B-C of resistance 7R/8",d:[["l",40,95,80,95],["l",80,50,80,140],["p","M80 50 L145 50 L149.2 45 L157.5 55 L165.8 45 L174.2 55 L182.5 45 L190.8 55 L195 50 L260 50"],["t",170,38,"R/8","m"],["p","M80 140 L145 140 L149.2 135 L157.5 145 L165.8 135 L174.2 145 L182.5 135 L190.8 145 L195 140 L260 140"],["t",170,166,"7R/8","m"],["l",260,50,260,140],["l",260,95,300,95],["c",80,95,2.6,"F"],["c",260,95,2.6,"F"],["t",32,99,"E","e"],["t",308,99,"C",""]]}},{pn:59,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ccmb",st:"Equivalent resistance of networks",q:"A metal wire of resistance 3 \u03A9 is elongated to make a uniform wire of double its previous length. This new wire is now bent and the ends joined to make a circle. If two points on this circle make an angle 60\xB0 at the centre, the equivalent resistance between these two points will be",o:["(7)/(2) \u03A9","(5)/(2) \u03A9","(12)/(5) \u03A9","(5)/(3) \u03A9"],c:3,s:`Stretching at constant volume: length doubles, so the area halves, and R = \u03C1l/A becomes 4 times.
New resistance of the wire = 4 \xD7 3 = 12 \u03A9.
This wire is joined into a circle, so the circle has 12 \u03A9 in total, spread uniformly round it.
An angle of 60\xB0 at the centre is 60/360 = 1/6 of the circle, so the short arc has 12/6 = 2 \u03A9 and the long arc has 10 \u03A9 (solution figure).
The two arcs are in parallel between the points.
R_eq = (2 \xD7 10)/(2 + 10) = 20/12 = 5/3 \u03A9, option (4).`,sfig:{w:300,h:210,alt:"Circle of the bent wire: two points make an angle of 60 degrees at the centre; the short arc is 2 ohm and the long arc is 10 ohm",d:[["c",150,105,60],["c",150,105,2,"F"],["l",150,105,120,53,"d"],["l",150,105,180,53,"d"],["p","M142 92 A14 14 0 0 1 158 92"],["t",150,85,"60\xB0","ms"],["c",120,53,2.6,"F"],["c",180,53,2.6,"F"],["t",150,34,"2 \u03A9","m"],["t",150,188,"10 \u03A9","m"]]}},{pn:60,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cres",st:"Resistance and resistivity",q:"In a conductor, if the number of conduction electrons per unit volume is 8.5 \xD7 10\xB2\u2078 m\u207B\xB3 and mean free time is 25 fs (femto second), its approximate resistivity is (m\u2091 = 9.1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9 kg)",o:["10\u207B\u2076 \u03A9 m","10\u207B\u2077 \u03A9 m","10\u207B\u2078 \u03A9 m","10\u207B\u2075 \u03A9 m"],c:2,s:`In the Drude model the resistivity is \u03C1 = m/(ne\xB2\u03C4), where \u03C4 is the mean free time.
Values: m = 9.1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9 kg, n = 8.5 \xD7 10\xB2\u2078 m\u207B\xB3, e = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 C, \u03C4 = 25 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2075 s.
Denominator: ne\xB2\u03C4 = 8.5 \xD7 10\xB2\u2078 \xD7 (1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079)\xB2 \xD7 25 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2075 = 8.5 \xD7 10\xB2\u2078 \xD7 2.56 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\u2078 \xD7 2.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2074 \u2248 5.44 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB3.
\u03C1 = 9.1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9 / 5.44 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB3 \u2248 1.7 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 \u03A9 m.
So \u03C1 \u2248 10\u207B\u2078 \u03A9 m, option (3).`},{pn:61,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cdrf",st:"Drift velocity and mobility",q:"A current of 5 A passes through a copper conductor (resistivity = 1.7 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 \u03A9 m) of radius of cross-section 5 mm. Find the mobility of the charges if their drift velocity is 1.1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m/s.",o:["1.3 m\xB2/V s","1.0 m\xB2/V s","1.8 m\xB2/V s","1.5 m\xB2/V s"],c:1,s:`Mobility is drift speed per unit field: \u03BC = v_d/E.
Field in the wire: E = \u03C1J = \u03C1I/A.
Area A = \u03C0r\xB2 = 3.14 \xD7 (5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3)\xB2 = 7.85 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 m\xB2.
E = 1.7 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 \xD7 5/(7.85 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075) \u2248 1.08 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 V/m.
\u03BC = 1.1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3/1.08 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \u2248 1.0 m\xB2/V s, option (2).`},{pn:62,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cres",st:"Temperature dependence",q:"In an experiment, the resistance of a material is plotted as a function of temperature (in some range). As shown in the figure, ln R(T) against 1/T\xB2 is a straight line. One may conclude that",o:["R(T) = R\u2080e^(T\xB2/T\u2080\xB2)","R(T) = R\u2080e^(\u2212T\xB2/T\u2080\xB2)","R(T) = R\u2080e^(\u2212T\u2080\xB2/T\xB2)","R(T) = R\u2080/T\xB2"],c:2,s:`A straight line on the graph means ln R = (intercept) + (slope) \xD7 (1/T\xB2).
The line cuts the vertical axis above zero and falls as 1/T\xB2 rises, so the slope is negative. Call it \u2212T\u2080\xB2.
So ln R = ln R\u2080 \u2212 T\u2080\xB2/T\xB2, where ln R\u2080 is the intercept.
Taking the exponential of both sides: R(T) = R\u2080e^(\u2212T\u2080\xB2/T\xB2).
Check: as T grows, 1/T\xB2 falls, so R rises, which matches the graph read from the right to the left.
Note: the printed key is missing; working gives option (c).`,fig:{w:300,h:205,alt:"Graph of ln R(T) against 1/T squared: a straight line with positive intercept on the vertical axis and negative slope",d:[["l",50,170,270,170,"a"],["l",50,170,50,25,"a"],["t",62,36,"ln R(T)",""],["t",268,190,"1/T\xB2","e"],["t",44,184,"O","e"],["l",50,60,230,150],["c",50,60,2.6,"F"]]}},{pn:63,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ccel",st:"Grouping of cells",q:"In the given circuit, an ideal voltmeter connected across the 10 \u03A9 resistance reads 2 V. The internal resistance r of each cell is",o:["0.5 \u03A9","1.5 \u03A9","0 \u03A9","1 \u03A9"],c:0,s:`The 15 \u03A9 and 10 \u03A9 resistors are in parallel, so the voltmeter across the 10 \u03A9 also reads the voltage across the pair: 2 V.
Parallel resistance = (15 \xD7 10)/(15 + 10) = 6 \u03A9.
Current through the pair (and the whole circuit): I = 2/6 = 1/3 A.
The two cells are in series: total emf = 3 V and total internal resistance = 2r.
The 2 \u03A9 resistor is in series with the 15 \u03A9 and 10 \u03A9 pair, so total external resistance = 6 + 2 = 8 \u03A9.
3 = (1/3)(8 + 2r), so 9 = 8 + 2r.
So r = 0.5 \u03A9, option (a).`,fig:{w:340,h:215,alt:"Circuit: 15 ohm and 10 ohm resistors in parallel, in series with a 2 ohm resistor and two cells of 1.5 V, each with internal resistance r",d:[["l",60,75,110,75],["l",110,45,110,105],["l",200,45,200,105],["p","M110 45 L138 45 L140.8 40 L146.5 50 L152.2 40 L157.8 50 L163.5 40 L169.2 50 L172 45 L200 45"],["t",155,36,"15 \u03A9","m"],["p","M110 105 L138 105 L140.8 100 L146.5 110 L152.2 100 L157.8 110 L163.5 100 L169.2 110 L172 105 L200 105"],["t",155,126,"10 \u03A9","m"],["c",110,75,2.6,"F"],["c",200,75,2.6,"F"],["p","M200 75 L228 75 L230.8 70 L236.5 80 L242.2 70 L247.8 80 L253.5 70 L259.2 80 L262 75 L290 75"],["t",245,64,"2 \u03A9","m"],["l",290,75,290,160],["l",60,75,60,160],["l",60,160,147,160],["l",147,149,147,171],["l",153,154,153,166,"B"],["l",153,160,185,160],["l",185,160,190,160],["l",185,160,192,160],["l",192,149,192,171],["l",198,154,198,166,"B"],["l",198,160,290,160],["t",150,188,"1.5 V","m"],["t",150,203,"r \u03A9","m"],["t",195,188,"1.5 V","m"],["t",195,203,"r \u03A9","m"]]}},{pn:64,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cres",st:"Resistance and resistivity",q:"Space between two concentric conducting spheres of radii a and b (b > a) is filled with a medium of resistivity \u03C1. The resistance between the two spheres will be",o:["(\u03C1)/(4\u03C0)(1/a + 1/b)","(\u03C1)/(4\u03C0)(1/a \u2212 1/b)","(\u03C1)/(2\u03C0)(1/a \u2212 1/b)","(\u03C1)/(2\u03C0)(1/a + 1/b)"],c:1,s:`Cut the medium into thin spherical shells of radius r and thickness dr. Each shell has resistance dR = \u03C1 dr/(4\u03C0r\xB2), as the current crosses an area 4\u03C0r\xB2.
The shells are in series, so add them: R = \u222B from a to b of \u03C1 dr/(4\u03C0r\xB2).
R = (\u03C1/4\u03C0)[\u22121/r] from a to b = (\u03C1/4\u03C0)(1/a \u2212 1/b).
Note: b > a, so 1/a \u2212 1/b is positive, as a resistance must be.
This is option (2).`},{pn:65,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cdrf",st:"Drift velocity and mobility",q:"Drift speed of electrons, when 1.5 A of current flows in a copper wire of cross section 5 mm\xB2, is v. If the electron density in copper is 9 \xD7 10\xB2\u2078/m\xB3, the value of v in mm/s is close to (Take charge of electron to be 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 C)",o:["3","0.2","2","0.02"],c:3,s:`Use I = nAev, so v = I/(nAe).
A = 5 mm\xB2 = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB2.
nAe = 9 \xD7 10\xB2\u2078 \xD7 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 = 7.2 \xD7 10\u2074.
v = 1.5/(7.2 \xD7 10\u2074) \u2248 2.1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 m/s.
In mm/s: 2.1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 \xD7 10\xB3 = 0.021 \u2248 0.02 mm/s, option (4).`},{pn:66,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cres",st:"Colour code of resistors",q:"A resistance is shown in the figure. Its value and tolerance are given respectively by (bands from the left: Red, Violet, Orange, Silver)",o:["27 k\u03A9, 20%","270 \u03A9, 10%","270 \u03A9, 5%","27 k\u03A9, 10%"],c:3,s:`Read the bands from the end that has the bands closer together. The first two bands are digits, the third is the multiplier, the last is tolerance.
From the figure the order is Red, Violet, Orange, Silver.
Red = 2, Violet = 7, so the digits are 27.
Orange = multiplier 10\xB3, so the value is 27 \xD7 10\xB3 \u03A9 = 27 k\u03A9.
Silver tolerance is \xB110% (gold is \xB15%, no band is \xB120%).
So 27 k\u03A9, 10%, option (4).`,fig:{w:330,h:170,alt:"Carbon resistor with four colour bands: red, violet, orange and silver",d:[["l",30,90,80,90],["l",250,90,300,90],["r",80,68,170,44],["l",100,68,100,112,"B"],["l",125,68,125,112,"B"],["l",150,68,150,112,"B"],["l",215,68,215,112,"B"],["t",100,28,"Red","m"],["l",100,36,100,64,"a"],["t",150,28,"Orange","m"],["l",150,36,150,64,"a"],["t",125,150,"Violet","m"],["l",125,142,125,116,"a"],["t",215,150,"Silver","m"],["l",215,142,215,116,"a"]]}},{pn:67,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cres",st:"Stretching and shape change of wires",q:"A copper wire is stretched to make it 0.5% longer. The percentage change in its electrical resistance if its volume remains unchanged is",o:["2.0%","1.0%","0.5%","2.5%"],c:1,s:`Volume stays constant, so when the length rises the area falls and R = \u03C1l\xB2/V is proportional to l\xB2.
For a small change, \u0394R/R = 2 \u0394l/l.
\u0394l/l = 0.5%, so \u0394R/R = 2 \xD7 0.5% = 1.0%.
Exact check: (1.005)\xB2 = 1.010025, a rise of 1.0%.
So the resistance rises by about 1.0%, option (2).`},{pn:68,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cres",st:"Colour code of resistors",q:"A carbon resistance has the following colour code (bands: Green, Orange, Yellow, Golden). What is the value of the resistance?",o:["64 k\u03A9 \xB1 10%","530 k\u03A9 \xB1 5%","5.3 M\u03A9 \xB1 5%","6.4 M\u03A9 \xB1 5%"],c:1,s:`The first two bands give digits, the third gives the power of ten, the fourth gives tolerance.
Green = 5, Orange = 3, so the digits are 53.
Yellow = multiplier 10\u2074, so the value is 53 \xD7 10\u2074 \u03A9 = 530 k\u03A9.
Golden tolerance is \xB15%.
So 530 k\u03A9 \xB1 5%, option (2).`,fig:{w:330,h:150,alt:"Carbon resistor with four colour bands: green, orange, yellow and gold",d:[["l",30,90,80,90],["l",250,90,300,90],["r",80,68,170,44],["l",100,68,100,112,"B"],["l",125,68,125,112,"B"],["l",150,68,150,112,"B"],["l",215,68,215,112,"B"],["t",100,134,"G","m"],["t",125,134,"O","m"],["t",150,134,"Y","m"],["t",215,134,"Golden","m"]]}},{pn:69,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ccmb",st:"Equivalent resistance of networks",q:"A uniform metallic wire has a resistance of 18 \u03A9 and is bent into an equilateral triangle. Then, the resistance between any two vertices of the triangle is",o:["4 \u03A9","2 \u03A9","8 \u03A9","10 \u03A9"],c:0,s:`The wire is uniform, so each of the three equal sides has 18/3 = 6 \u03A9.
Between two vertices there are two paths: the direct side (6 \u03A9) and the other two sides in series (6 + 6 = 12 \u03A9).
These paths are in parallel.
R_eq = (6 \xD7 12)/(6 + 12) = 72/18 = 4 \u03A9, option (1).`},{pn:70,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"crc",st:"Charging and discharging of a capacitor",q:"The charge on a capacitor plate in a circuit, as a function of time, is shown in the figure. What is the value of current at t = 4 s?",o:["3 \u03BCA","zero","1.5 \u03BCA","2 \u03BCA"],c:1,s:`Current is the rate of change of charge, I = dq/dt, which is the slope of the q-t graph.
Between t = 2 s and t = 6 s the graph is a horizontal line at 3 \u03BCC.
A horizontal line has zero slope, so there the charge does not change.
At t = 4 s we are on this part, so I = 0.
This is option (2).
Note: the slope from 0 to 2 s would be 3 \u03BCC/2 s = 1.5 \u03BCA, but that is not the time asked.`,fig:{w:330,h:200,alt:"Graph of charge q in microcoulomb against time t in seconds: q rises from 0 to 3 at 2 s, stays at 3 until 6 s, then rises again to about 5.2 at 8 s",d:[["l",50,150,310,150,"a"],["l",50,150,50,18,"a"],["t",62,26,"q (\u03BCC)",""],["t",312,142,"t (s)","e"],["t",44,164,"O","e"],["l",46,130,50,130],["t",42,134,"1","e"],["l",46,110,50,110],["t",42,114,"2","e"],["l",46,90,50,90],["t",42,94,"3","e"],["l",46,70,50,70],["t",42,74,"4","e"],["l",46,50,50,50],["t",42,54,"5","e"],["l",46,30,50,30],["t",42,34,"6","e"],["l",110,150,110,154],["t",110,168,"2","m"],["l",170,150,170,154],["t",170,168,"4","m"],["l",230,150,230,154],["t",230,168,"6","m"],["l",290,150,290,154],["t",290,168,"8","m"],["l",50,150,110,90],["l",110,90,230,90],["l",230,90,290,46],["l",110,90,110,150,"d"],["l",170,90,170,150,"d"],["l",230,90,230,150,"d"]]}},{pn:71,y:"JEE Main 2018 \xB7 Online",tp:"ccmb",st:"Balanced networks and symmetry",q:"In the given circuit all resistances are of value R ohm each. The equivalent resistance between A and B is",o:["2R","(5R)/(2)","3R","(5R)/(3)"],c:0,s:`Look at the plain wires (no resistor) in the figure: the horizontal wire from the end of A's resistor to the middle node, the vertical wire in the middle, and the bottom wire to the end of B's resistor.
These wires join the node after A's resistor, the middle top and bottom nodes and the node after B's resistor into one single node X.
So A reaches X through one R, and B reaches X through another R.
Every other resistor starts and ends on X or on nodes that connect back only to X, so no current flows through them (solution figure).
Note: a resistor with both ends at the same potential carries no current, so it can be ignored.
R_eq = R + R = 2R, option (1).`,fig:{w:330,h:200,alt:"Network: A and B each connect through one resistor R to the left nodes; the two horizontal and the middle vertical wires join the left nodes together; the other resistors connect the upper and right nodes",d:[["p","M30 100 L51 100 L53.3 95 L58 105 L62.7 95 L67.3 105 L72 95 L76.7 105 L79 100 L100 100"],["p","M30 165 L51 165 L53.3 160 L58 170 L62.7 160 L67.3 170 L72 160 L76.7 170 L79 165 L100 165"],["t",14,104,"A","e"],["t",14,169,"B","e"],["p","M100 100 L100 113 L105 116.3 L95 122.8 L105 129.3 L95 135.8 L105 142.3 L95 148.8 L100 152 L100 165"],["l",100,100,190,100],["l",190,100,190,165],["l",100,165,190,165],["p","M100 165 L128.8 144.2 L128.6 138.2 L139.8 142.4 L139.4 130.4 L150.6 134.6 L150.2 122.6 L161.4 126.8 L161.2 120.8 L190 100"],["p","M190 100 L220 100 L223.3 95 L230 105 L236.7 95 L243.3 105 L250 95 L256.7 105 L260 100 L290 100"],["p","M190 165 L220 165 L223.3 160 L230 170 L236.7 160 L243.3 170 L250 160 L256.7 170 L260 165 L290 165"],["p","M190 165 L223.2 143.4 L223.3 137.4 L234.3 142.1 L234.5 130.1 L245.5 134.9 L245.7 122.9 L256.7 127.6 L256.8 121.6 L290 100"],["p","M290 100 L290 113 L295 116.3 L285 122.8 L295 129.3 L285 135.8 L295 142.3 L285 148.8 L290 152 L290 165"],["p","M100 100 L108 85.1 L105.2 79.8 L117.2 78.7 L111.5 68.1 L123.5 66.9 L117.8 56.3 L129.8 55.2 L127 49.9 L135 35"],["p","M135 35 L149.6 52.2 L155.6 51.5 L152.2 63.1 L164.2 61.7 L160.8 73.3 L172.8 71.9 L169.4 83.5 L175.4 82.8 L190 100"],["p","M135 35 L167.5 35 L170.8 30 L177.5 40 L184.2 30 L190.8 40 L197.5 30 L204.2 40 L207.5 35 L240 35"],["p","M240 35 L227.2 51.6 L229.1 57.3 L217.1 56.5 L221 67.9 L209 67.1 L212.9 78.5 L200.9 77.7 L202.8 83.4 L190 100"],["p","M240 35 L252.8 51.6 L258.8 51.2 L254.9 62.6 L266.9 61.8 L263.1 73.2 L275.1 72.4 L271.2 83.8 L277.2 83.4 L290 100"],["c",100,100,2.6,"F"],["c",100,165,2.6,"F"],["c",190,100,2.6,"F"],["c",190,165,2.6,"F"],["c",290,100,2.6,"F"],["c",290,165,2.6,"F"],["c",135,35,2.6,"F"],["c",240,35,2.6,"F"]]},sfig:{w:340,h:160,alt:"Simplified circuit: A, one resistor R, the joined node X, another resistor R, and B in series; the rest of the network only hangs from X",d:[["l",30,70,170,70],["p","M30 70 L80 70 L83.3 65 L90 75 L96.7 65 L103.3 75 L110 65 L116.7 75 L120 70 L170 70"],["t",100,58,"R","m"],["p","M170 70 L220 70 L223.3 65 L230 75 L236.7 65 L243.3 75 L250 65 L256.7 75 L260 70 L310 70"],["t",240,58,"R","m"],["c",170,70,2.6,"F"],["t",178,62,"X",""],["t",22,74,"A","e"],["t",318,74,"B",""],["p","M170 70 L170 100 L110 100 L110 135 L230 135 L230 100 L170 100","d"],["t",170,152,"rest of the network: no current","m"]]}},{pn:72,y:"JEE Main 2018 \xB7 Online",tp:"cdrf",st:"Drift velocity and mobility",q:"A copper rod of cross-sectional area A carries a uniform current I through it. At temperature T, if the volume charge density of the rod is \u03C1, how long will the charges take to travel a distance d?",o:["(\u03C1dA)/(I)","(\u03C1dA)/(IT)","(2\u03C1dA)/(I)","(2\u03C1dA)/(IT)"],c:0,s:`Current in terms of volume charge density: I = \u03C1Av, where v is the drift speed of the charges.
So v = I/(\u03C1A).
Time to travel a distance d: t = d/v = d \xD7 \u03C1A/I.
So t = \u03C1dA/I, option (1).
Note: T is the temperature and does not enter the answer.`},{pn:73,y:"JEE Main 2018 \xB7 Online",tp:"cres",st:"Temperature dependence",q:"A heating element has a resistance of 100 \u03A9 at room temperature. When it is connected to a supply of 220 V, a steady current of 2 A passes in it and the temperature is 500\xB0C more than room temperature. What is the temperature coefficient of resistance of the heating element?",o:["1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \xB0C\u207B\xB9","2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \xB0C\u207B\xB9","0.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \xB0C\u207B\xB9","5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \xB0C\u207B\xB9"],c:1,s:`The resistance of a metal element changes with temperature as R = R\u2080(1 + \u03B1 \u0394T), where \u03B1 is the temperature coefficient.
Find the hot resistance from Ohm's law: R = V/I = 220/2 = 110 \u03A9.
The cold resistance is R\u2080 = 100 \u03A9 and the rise is \u0394T = 500\xB0C.
110 = 100(1 + \u03B1 \xD7 500), so 1 + 500\u03B1 = 1.1.
500\u03B1 = 0.1, so \u03B1 = 0.1/500 = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \xB0C\u207B\xB9.
This is option (b).`},{pn:74,y:"JEE Main 2017 \xB7 Online",tp:"cres",st:"Stretching and shape change of wires",q:"A uniform wire of length l and radius r has a resistance of 100 \u03A9. It is recast into a wire of radius r/2. The resistance of the new wire will be",o:["400 \u03A9","100 \u03A9","200 \u03A9","1600 \u03A9"],c:3,s:`Recasting means melting and drawing again, so the volume of metal stays the same: A \xD7 l = A' \xD7 l'.
Resistance is R = \u03C1l/A, with the same material so \u03C1 does not change.
Radius is halved, so area becomes (1/2)\xB2 = 1/4 of the old area: A' = A/4.
Volume constant, so the length becomes l' = 4l.
New resistance R' = \u03C1(4l)/(A/4) = 16 \xD7 \u03C1l/A = 16 R.
R' = 16 \xD7 100 = 1600 \u03A9, option (d).`},{pn:75,y:"JEE Main 2016",tp:"cres",st:"Temperature dependence",q:"The temperature dependence of resistances of Cu and undoped Si in the temperature range 300-400 K, is best described by",o:["linear increase for Cu, linear increase for Si","linear increase for Cu, exponential increase for Si","linear increase for Cu, exponential decrease for Si","linear decrease for Cu, linear decrease for Si"],c:2,s:`Copper is a metal. Its number of free electrons is fixed and more heating only makes the electrons collide more often, so R rises almost linearly with T (R = R\u2080(1 + \u03B1\u0394T)).
Undoped silicon is a semiconductor. On heating, many more electrons are freed from bonds, so the number of charge carriers grows very fast (exponentially) with T.
More carriers means less resistance, so R of Si falls exponentially with temperature.
So: Cu rises linearly and Si falls exponentially, option (c).`},{pn:76,y:"JEE Advanced 2016",tp:"cdrf",st:"Current density",q:"An infinite line charge of uniform electric charge density \u03BB lies along the axis of an electrically conducting infinite cylindrical shell of radius R. At time t = 0, the space inside the cylinder is filled with a material of permittivity \u03B5 and electrical conductivity \u03C3. The electrical conduction in the material follows Ohm's law. Which one of the following graphs best describes the subsequent variation of the magnitude of current density j(t) at any point in the material?",o:["(a)","(b)","(c)","(d)"],c:0,s:`The charge on the axis is fixed, but the free charge in the material can drift away because the material conducts.
Ohm's law gives j = \u03C3E, and Gauss's law gives the field of the charge enclosed in a thin cylinder: E = (charge per length enclosed)/(2\u03C0 \u03B5 r).
Current flowing out of the cylinder reduces the enclosed charge: d(charge)/dt = \u2212j \xD7 area = \u2212(\u03C3/\u03B5) \xD7 charge.
So the enclosed charge falls as e^(\u2212\u03C3t/\u03B5), and so do E and j = \u03C3E.
Note: this is an RC-type decay with time constant \u03B5/\u03C3; the rate of fall is proportional to what is left, so the curve is steepest at t = 0 and flattens towards zero without reaching it.
Only graph (a), a decreasing curve that flattens and goes to zero, has this shape; (b) is a straight fall, (c) stops at a non-zero value, (d) stays flat at first.`,fig:{w:340,h:272,alt:"Four graphs of j(t) against t from the origin: (a) decaying curve that flattens to zero, (b) straight line falling to zero, (c) decaying curve that flattens to a non-zero dashed value, (d) flat at first then falling smoothly",d:[["l",40,95,40,10,"a"],["l",40,95,160,95,"a"],["t",35,16,"j(t)","e"],["t",165,99,"t"],["t",35,107,"O","e"],["p","M40 28 C46 60 72 86 150 92"],["t",95,125,"(a)","m"],["l",215,95,215,10,"a"],["l",215,95,335,95,"a"],["t",210,16,"j(t)","e"],["t",340,99,"t"],["t",210,107,"O","e"],["l",215,28,330,88],["t",270,125,"(b)","m"],["l",40,225,40,140,"a"],["l",40,225,160,225,"a"],["t",35,146,"j(t)","e"],["t",165,229,"t"],["t",35,237,"O","e"],["p","M40 158 C48 182 70 192 150 194"],["l",40,194,150,194,"d"],["t",95,255,"(c)","m"],["l",215,225,215,140,"a"],["l",215,225,335,225,"a"],["t",210,146,"j(t)","e"],["t",340,229,"t"],["t",210,237,"O","e"],["p","M215 158 C255 158 270 162 285 180 C297 194 312 204 330 206"],["t",270,255,"(d)","m"]]}},{pn:77,y:"JEE Main 2015",tp:"cdrf",st:"Drift velocity and mobility",q:"When 5 V potential difference is applied across a wire of length 0.1 m, the drift speed of electrons is 2.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 m s\u207B\xB9. If the electron density in the wire is 8 \xD7 10\xB2\u2078 m\u207B\xB3, the resistivity of the material is close to",o:["1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \u03A9 m","1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 \u03A9 m","1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 \u03A9 m","1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \u03A9 m"],c:1,s:`Link the drift speed to the resistivity: current density j = n e v_d, and also j = \u03C3E = E/\u03C1.
So \u03C1 = E/(n e v_d).
The field in the wire is E = V/l = 5/0.1 = 50 V/m.
n e v_d = 8 \xD7 10\xB2\u2078 \xD7 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 \xD7 2.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 = 3.2 \xD7 10\u2076 A/m\xB2.
\u03C1 = 50/(3.2 \xD7 10\u2076) = 1.56 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 \u03A9 m \u2248 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 \u03A9 m.
This is option (b).`},{pn:78,y:"JEE Main 2015 \xB7 Online",tp:"cres",st:"Ohm's law and V-I graphs",q:"Suppose the drift velocity v_d in a material varied with the applied electric field E as v_d \u221D \u221AE. Then the V-I graph for a wire made of such a material is best given by",o:["(a)","(b)","(c)","(d)"],c:2,s:`Current in a wire is I = n e A v_d, and n, e, A are constants, so I \u221D v_d.
Here v_d \u221D \u221AE, so I \u221D \u221AE.
The field in a wire of length l is E = V/l, so E \u221D V and therefore I \u221D \u221AV.
Square both sides: V \u221D I\xB2.
Note: an ordinary (ohmic) wire has v_d \u221D E, which would give V \u221D I, a straight line (graph a).
A graph of V against I that is V \u221D I\xB2 is a parabola from the origin that bends upwards, getting steeper as I grows. That is graph (c).`,fig:{w:340,h:272,alt:"Four V against I graphs: (a) straight line through the origin, (b) curve rising steeply then flattening, (c) curve starting flat and bending upwards, (d) curve rising to a peak and then falling",d:[["l",40,95,40,10,"a"],["l",40,95,160,95,"a"],["t",35,16,"V","e"],["t",165,99,"I"],["t",35,107,"O","e"],["l",40,95,140,28],["t",95,125,"(a)","m"],["l",215,95,215,10,"a"],["l",215,95,335,95,"a"],["t",210,16,"V","e"],["t",340,99,"I"],["t",210,107,"O","e"],["p","M215 95 C222 50 255 32 325 28"],["t",270,125,"(b)","m"],["l",40,225,40,140,"a"],["l",40,225,160,225,"a"],["t",35,146,"V","e"],["t",165,229,"I"],["t",35,237,"O","e"],["p","M40 225 C80 222 115 200 145 158"],["t",95,255,"(c)","m"],["l",215,225,215,140,"a"],["l",215,225,335,225,"a"],["t",210,146,"V","e"],["t",340,229,"I"],["t",210,237,"O","e"],["p","M215 225 C238 224 250 218 262 190 C270 168 280 158 290 162 C302 168 310 210 332 222"],["t",270,255,"(d)","m"]]}},{pn:79,y:"JEE Main 2013 \xB7 Online",tp:"ccmb",st:"Equivalent resistance of networks",q:"A letter 'A' is constructed of a uniform wire with resistance 1.0 \u03A9 per cm. The sides of the letter are 20 cm and the cross piece in the middle is 10 cm long. The apex angle is 60\xB0. The resistance between the ends of the legs is close to",o:["50.0 \u03A9","10 \u03A9","36.7 \u03A9","26.7 \u03A9"],c:3,s:`A piece of this wire has resistance 1.0 \u03A9 for every cm of length, so a length of 10 cm is 10 \u03A9.
The cross piece joins the middle points M and N of the two legs. Each half-leg is 10 cm, so X to M, N to Y, M to apex and apex to N are all 10 \u03A9, and the cross piece MN is also 10 \u03A9 (see the figure).
Between M and N there are two paths in parallel: the cross piece (10 \u03A9) and the route M to apex to N (10 + 10 = 20 \u03A9).
Their parallel value is (10 \xD7 20)/(10 + 20) = 20/3 \u2248 6.7 \u03A9.
The current from X to Y goes through X to M, then this parallel part, then N to Y, all in series.
R = 10 + 6.7 + 10 = 26.7 \u03A9, which is option (d).`,sfig:{w:260,h:200,alt:"The letter A: apex at the top, legs ending at X and Y, cross piece joining the middle points M and N; every segment is 10 ohm",d:[["l",130,22,50,160.6],["l",130,22,210,160.6],["l",90,91.3,170,91.3],["c",90,91.3,3,"F"],["c",170,91.3,3,"F"],["t",44,176.6,"X","m"],["t",216,176.6,"Y","m"],["t",82,95.3,"M","e"],["t",178,95.3,"N"],["t",130,16,"apex","m"],["t",70,65.3,"10 \u03A9","e"],["t",190,65.3,"10 \u03A9"],["t",58,130.6,"10 \u03A9","e"],["t",202,130.6,"10 \u03A9"],["t",130,107.3,"10 \u03A9","m"]]}},{pn:80,y:"JEE Main 2012 \xB7 Online",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"A 6.0 volt battery is connected to two light bulbs as shown in the figure. Light bulb 1 has resistance 3 ohm while light bulb 2 has resistance 6 ohm. Battery has negligible internal resistance. Which bulb will glow more?",o:["Bulb 1 will glow more first and then its brightness will become less than bulb 2","Bulb 1","Bulb 2","Both glow equally"],c:1,s:`In the figure the two bulbs sit on separate wires joined to the same two points of the battery, so they are in parallel.
The battery has negligible internal resistance, so each bulb has the full 6.0 V across it.
Brightness depends on power, and for a fixed voltage P = V\xB2/R.
Bulb 1: P = 6\xB2/3 = 12 W. Bulb 2: P = 6\xB2/6 = 6 W.
Note: in parallel the smaller resistance gets the larger current and power; in series it would be the other way round.
Bulb 1 gives twice the power of bulb 2, so bulb 1 glows more, option (b).`,fig:{w:340,h:210,alt:"A 6.0 volt battery connected to two lamps, Bulb 1 and Bulb 2, on two parallel branches",d:[["l",60,40,60,165],["l",60,40,114,40],["l",146,40,280,40],["l",60,95,114,95],["l",146,95,280,95],["l",280,40,280,165],["p","M114 40 A16 11 0 1 0 146 40 A16 11 0 1 0 114 40"],["p","M120 43 a2.5 5 0 0 1 5 0 a2.5 5 0 0 1 5 0 a2.5 5 0 0 1 5 0 a2.5 5 0 0 1 5 0"],["p","M114 95 A16 11 0 1 0 146 95 A16 11 0 1 0 114 95"],["p","M120 98 a2.5 5 0 0 1 5 0 a2.5 5 0 0 1 5 0 a2.5 5 0 0 1 5 0 a2.5 5 0 0 1 5 0"],["t",130,20,"Bulb 1","m"],["t",130,75,"Bulb 2","m"],["l",60,165,153,165],["l",174,165,280,165],["l",153,153,153,177],["l",160,158,160,172,"B"],["l",167,153,167,177],["l",174,158,174,172,"B"],["t",142,153,"+","e"],["t",182,153,"\u2212"],["t",163,196,"6.0 V","m"],["l",60,130,60,118,"a"],["l",280,118,280,130,"a"],["c",60,95,2.5,"F"],["c",280,95,2.5,"F"]]}},{pn:81,y:"AIEEE 2011",tp:"cres",st:"Stretching and shape change of wires",q:"If a wire is stretched to make it 0.1% longer, its resistance will",o:["increase by 0.05%","increase by 0.2%","decrease by 0.2%","decrease by 0.05%"],c:1,s:`When a wire is stretched, its volume stays constant: A \xD7 l = constant, so A \u221D 1/l.
R = \u03C1l/A = \u03C1l\xB2/(A l) = \u03C1l\xB2/V, so R \u221D l\xB2.
For a small change, \u0394R/R \u2248 2 \xD7 \u0394l/l = 2 \xD7 0.1% = 0.2%.
Note: the wire gets longer AND thinner, and both make R larger, which is why the change is twice the change in length.
Resistance increases by 0.2%, option (b).`},{pn:82,y:"AIEEE 2010",tp:"cres",st:"Temperature dependence",q:"Two conductors have the same resistance at 0\xB0C but their temperature coefficients of resistance are \u03B1\u2081 and \u03B1\u2082. The respective temperature coefficients of their series and parallel combinations are nearly",o:["(\u03B1\u2081 + \u03B1\u2082)/2, (\u03B1\u2081 + \u03B1\u2082)/2","(\u03B1\u2081 + \u03B1\u2082)/2, \u03B1\u2081 + \u03B1\u2082","\u03B1\u2081 + \u03B1\u2082, (\u03B1\u2081 + \u03B1\u2082)/2","\u03B1\u2081 + \u03B1\u2082, \u03B1\u2081\u03B1\u2082/(\u03B1\u2081 + \u03B1\u2082)"],c:0,s:`Let each resistance be R\u2080 at 0\xB0C. At temperature t the resistances are R\u2081 = R\u2080(1 + \u03B1\u2081t) and R\u2082 = R\u2080(1 + \u03B1\u2082t).
Series: R_s = R\u2081 + R\u2082 = 2R\u2080[1 + ((\u03B1\u2081 + \u03B1\u2082)/2) t], so \u03B1_s = (\u03B1\u2081 + \u03B1\u2082)/2.
Parallel: R_p = R\u2081R\u2082/(R\u2081 + R\u2082) = R\u2080(1 + \u03B1\u2081t)(1 + \u03B1\u2082t)/(2 + (\u03B1\u2081 + \u03B1\u2082)t).
Neglect the tiny \u03B1\u2081\u03B1\u2082t\xB2 term and use 1/(1 + x) \u2248 1 \u2212 x: R_p \u2248 (R\u2080/2)[1 + (\u03B1\u2081 + \u03B1\u2082)t][1 \u2212 (\u03B1\u2081 + \u03B1\u2082)t/2] \u2248 (R\u2080/2)[1 + ((\u03B1\u2081 + \u03B1\u2082)/2) t].
So \u03B1_p is also (\u03B1\u2081 + \u03B1\u2082)/2.
Both are (\u03B1\u2081 + \u03B1\u2082)/2, option (a).`},{pn:83,y:"IIT-JEE 2010",tp:"cpot",st:"Galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"To verify Ohm's law, a student is provided with a test resistor R_T, a high resistance R\u2081, a small resistance R\u2082, two identical galvanometers G\u2081 and G\u2082, and a variable voltage source V. The correct circuit to carry out the experiment is",o:["(a)","(b)","(c)","(d)"],c:2,s:`To verify Ohm's law we must measure the voltage across R_T and the current through it, using only galvanometers and the two resistors given.
A galvanometer in series with a HIGH resistance works as a voltmeter, and it must be connected across (in parallel with) R_T.
A galvanometer with a SMALL resistance in parallel (a shunt) works as an ammeter, and it must be in series with R_T.
So we need G\u2081 + R\u2081 in series across R_T, and G\u2082 with R\u2082 in parallel placed in the main circuit.
Check the options: in (a) and (b) the galvanometer has a parallel resistor across R_T, which does not make a voltmeter; in (d) the small resistance R\u2082 is in series with G\u2081, so it would draw a large current.
Only (c) has G\u2081 in series with R\u2081 across R_T and G\u2082 shunted by R\u2082 in series with the source, which is option (c).`,fig:{w:530,h:380,alt:"Four circuits (a) to (d), each with a variable source V, test resistor R_T and two galvanometers G1 and G2 with resistors R1 and R2 arranged differently",d:[["t",4,20,"(a)"],["l",38,74,38,144],["l",244,74,244,144],["l",38,144,116,144],["l",124,144,244,144],["l",116,134,116,154],["l",124,139,124,149,"B"],["l",108,156,136,132,"a"],["t",120,170,"V","m"],["l",38,74,67,74],["p","M67 74 L69.7 78 L75 70 L80.3 78 L85.7 70 L91 78 L96.3 70 L99 74"],["l",99,74,128,74],["t",83,92,"R_T","m"],["l",38,74,38,22],["l",38,22,60,22],["r",60,13,34,18],["t",77,26,"G\u2081","m"],["l",94,22,128,22],["l",128,22,128,74],["l",38,54,52,54],["c",38,54,2.2,"F"],["l",38,54,68,54],["p","M68 54 L70.5 58 L75.5 50 L80.5 58 L85.5 50 L90.5 58 L95.5 50 L98 54"],["l",98,54,128,54],["c",128,54,2.2,"F"],["t",83,45,"R\u2082","m"],["l",128,74,146,74],["r",146,65,32,18],["t",162,78,"G\u2082","m"],["l",178,74,190,74],["l",190,74,199,74],["p","M199 74 L202 78 L208 70 L214 78 L220 70 L226 78 L232 70 L235 74"],["l",235,74,244,74],["t",217,92,"R\u2081","m"],["t",266,20,"(b)"],["l",300,74,300,144],["l",506,74,506,144],["l",300,144,378,144],["l",386,144,506,144],["l",378,134,378,154],["l",386,139,386,149,"B"],["l",370,156,398,132,"a"],["t",382,170,"V","m"],["l",300,74,329,74],["p","M329 74 L331.7 78 L337 70 L342.3 78 L347.7 70 L353 78 L358.3 70 L361 74"],["l",361,74,390,74],["t",345,92,"R_T","m"],["l",300,74,300,22],["l",300,22,322,22],["r",322,13,34,18],["t",339,26,"G\u2081","m"],["l",356,22,390,22],["l",390,22,390,74],["l",300,54,314,54],["c",300,54,2.2,"F"],["l",300,54,330,54],["p","M330 54 L332.5 58 L337.5 50 L342.5 58 L347.5 50 L352.5 58 L357.5 50 L360 54"],["l",360,54,390,54],["c",390,54,2.2,"F"],["t",345,45,"R\u2081","m"],["l",390,74,408,74],["r",408,65,32,18],["t",424,78,"G\u2082","m"],["l",440,74,452,74],["l",452,74,461,74],["p","M461 74 L464 78 L470 70 L476 78 L482 70 L488 78 L494 70 L497 74"],["l",497,74,506,74],["t",479,92,"R\u2082","m"],["t",4,216,"(c)"],["l",38,270,38,340],["l",244,270,244,340],["l",38,340,116,340],["l",124,340,244,340],["l",116,330,116,350],["l",124,335,124,345,"B"],["l",108,352,136,328,"a"],["t",120,366,"V","m"],["l",38,270,67,270],["p","M67 270 L69.7 274 L75 266 L80.3 274 L85.7 266 L91 274 L96.3 266 L99 270"],["l",99,270,128,270],["t",83,288,"R_T","m"],["l",38,270,38,240],["l",38,240,54,240],["r",54,231,26,18],["t",67,244,"G\u2081","m"],["l",80,240,90,240],["l",90,240,96,240],["p","M96 240 L98.6 244 L103.8 236 L109 244 L114.2 236 L119.4 244 L122 240"],["l",122,240,128,240],["l",128,240,128,270],["t",104,230,"R\u2081","m"],["l",128,270,154,270],["c",154,270,2.2,"F"],["l",154,270,162,270],["r",162,261,36,18],["t",180,274,"G\u2082","m"],["l",198,270,244,270],["c",206,270,2.2,"F"],["l",154,270,154,298],["l",154,298,166,298],["l",166,298,171,298],["p","M171 298 L174 302 L180 294 L186 302 L192 294 L198 302 L201 298"],["l",201,298,206,298],["l",206,298,206,270],["t",186,316,"R\u2082","m"],["t",266,216,"(d)"],["l",300,270,300,340],["l",506,270,506,340],["l",300,340,378,340],["l",386,340,506,340],["l",378,330,378,350],["l",386,335,386,345,"B"],["l",370,352,398,328,"a"],["t",382,366,"V","m"],["l",300,270,329,270],["p","M329 270 L331.7 274 L337 266 L342.3 274 L347.7 266 L353 274 L358.3 266 L361 270"],["l",361,270,390,270],["t",345,288,"R_T","m"],["l",300,270,300,240],["l",300,240,316,240],["r",316,231,26,18],["t",329,244,"G\u2081","m"],["l",342,240,352,240],["l",352,240,358,240],["p","M358 240 L360.6 244 L365.8 236 L371 244 L376.2 236 L381.4 244 L384 240"],["l",384,240,390,240],["l",390,240,390,270],["t",366,230,"R\u2082","m"],["l",390,270,416,270],["c",416,270,2.2,"F"],["l",416,270,424,270],["r",424,261,36,18],["t",442,274,"G\u2082","m"],["l",460,270,506,270],["c",468,270,2.2,"F"],["l",416,270,416,298],["l",416,298,428,298],["l",428,298,433,298],["p","M433 298 L436 302 L442 294 L448 302 L454 294 L460 302 L463 298"],["l",463,298,468,298],["l",468,298,468,270],["t",448,316,"R\u2081","m"]]}},{pn:84,y:"IIT-JEE 2010",tp:"cres",st:"Resistance and resistivity",q:"Consider a thin square sheet of side L and thickness t, made of a material of resistivity \u03C1. The resistance between two opposite faces, shown by the shaded areas in the figure, is",o:["directly proportional to L","directly proportional to t","independent of L","independent of t"],c:2,s:`Current flows between the two shaded faces, so the length of the conductor along the current is L.
The cross-section the current passes through is the shaded face: width L and thickness t, so A = L \xD7 t.
Use R = \u03C1 \xD7 (length)/(area).
R = \u03C1 \xD7 L/(L \xD7 t) = \u03C1/t.
Note: L cancels, because a longer sheet is also wider, so the two effects cancel. This is why "resistance per square" is quoted for thin films.
So R depends only on \u03C1 and t (and goes as 1/t), not on L, which is option (c).`,fig:{w:260,h:155,alt:"A thin square sheet of side L and thickness t with two opposite faces shaded",d:[["p","M40 105 L80 60 L220 60 L180 105 Z","f"],["l",180,105,220,60],["r",40,105,140,16,"f"],["p","M180 105 L220 60 L220 76 L180 121"],["p","M80 60 L220 60 L220 76 L80 76 L80 60","f"],["l",46,121,54,105],["l",54,121,62,105],["l",62,121,70,105],["l",70,121,78,105],["l",78,121,86,105],["l",86,121,94,105],["l",94,121,102,105],["l",102,121,110,105],["l",110,121,118,105],["l",118,121,126,105],["l",126,121,134,105],["l",134,121,142,105],["l",142,121,150,105],["l",150,121,158,105],["l",158,121,166,105],["l",166,121,174,105],["l",174,121,182,105],["l",86,76,94,60],["l",94,76,102,60],["l",102,76,110,60],["l",110,76,118,60],["l",118,76,126,60],["l",126,76,134,60],["l",134,76,142,60],["l",142,76,150,60],["l",150,76,158,60],["l",158,76,166,60],["l",166,76,174,60],["l",174,76,182,60],["l",182,76,190,60],["l",190,76,198,60],["l",198,76,206,60],["l",206,76,214,60],["l",214,76,222,60],["l",80,60,40,105],["l",80,76,80,60,"d"],["l",40,105,40,121],["l",40,121,180,121],["l",20,86,20,103,"a"],["l",20,142,20,123,"a"],["t",20,117,"t","m"],["l",100,142,40,142,"a"],["l",120,142,180,142,"a"],["t",110,146,"L","m"]]}},{pn:85,y:"AIEEE 2007",tp:"cres",st:"Temperature dependence",q:"The resistance of a wire is 5 ohm at 50\xB0C and 6 ohm at 100\xB0C. The resistance of the wire at 0\xB0C will be",o:["3 ohm","2 ohm","1 ohm","4 ohm"],c:3,s:`For a metal wire, R = R\u2080(1 + \u03B1t), so R changes by the same amount for every equal step in temperature (a straight line in t).
From 50\xB0C to 100\xB0C (a 50\xB0C rise) the resistance rises from 5 \u03A9 to 6 \u03A9, a gain of 1 \u03A9.
Going from 0\xB0C to 50\xB0C is another 50\xB0C step, so the resistance rose by 1 \u03A9 there too.
So R\u2080 = 5 \u2212 1 = 4 \u03A9.
Check with the formula: R\u2080\u03B1 \xD7 50 = 1 and R\u2080 + 50R\u2080\u03B1 = 5, so R\u2080 + 1 = 5 and R\u2080 = 4 \u03A9.
Option (d).`},{pn:86,y:"AIEEE 2006",tp:"cres",st:"Resistance and resistivity",q:"A material B has twice the specific resistance of A. A circular wire made of B has twice the diameter of a wire made of A. Then for the two wires to have the same resistance, the ratio l_B/l_A of their respective lengths must be",o:["2","1","1/2","1/4"],c:0,s:`Use R = \u03C1l/A with A = \u03C0d\xB2/4, so R = 4\u03C1l/(\u03C0d\xB2).
Equal resistances: \u03C1_B l_B/d_B\xB2 = \u03C1_A l_A/d_A\xB2.
So l_B/l_A = (\u03C1_A/\u03C1_B) \xD7 (d_B/d_A)\xB2.
Here \u03C1_B = 2\u03C1_A and d_B = 2d_A.
l_B/l_A = (1/2) \xD7 (2)\xB2 = (1/2) \xD7 4 = 2.
Option (a).`},{pn:87,y:"AIEEE 2006",tp:"cres",st:"Temperature dependence",q:"The resistance of a bulb filament is 100 \u03A9 at a temperature of 100\xB0C. If its temperature coefficient of resistance be 0.005 per \xB0C, its resistance will become 200 \u03A9 at a temperature of",o:["200\xB0C","300\xB0C","400\xB0C","500\xB0C"],c:2,s:`The temperature coefficient \u03B1 is defined from 0\xB0C: R_t = R\u2080(1 + \u03B1t).
At 100\xB0C: 100 = R\u2080(1 + 0.005 \xD7 100) = 1.5 R\u2080, so R\u2080 = 100/1.5 = 200/3 \u03A9.
For 200 \u03A9: 200 = R\u2080(1 + 0.005t), so 1 + 0.005t = 200/R\u2080 = 3.
0.005t = 2, so t = 400\xB0C.
Note: if \u03B1 were measured from 100\xB0C instead, the answer would be 300\xB0C; the standard formula uses R\u2080 at 0\xB0C, and the printed key agrees with 400\xB0C.
Option (c).`},{pn:88,y:"IIT-JEE 2005",tp:"ckir",st:"Kirchhoff's laws",q:"Find out the value of current through the 2 \u03A9 resistance for the given circuit.",o:["zero","2 A","5 A","4 A"],c:0,s:`Look at how the two loops are joined. The left loop (10 V and 5 \u03A9) and the right loop (10 \u03A9 and 20 V) are connected only through the 2 \u03A9 resistor on the bottom wire. The top wires of the two loops are not joined.
A steady current needs a closed path. Any current entering the 2 \u03A9 resistor from the left loop would have no way back to the left loop, and the same for the right loop.
Charge cannot pile up at a node in steady state, so the current through the 2 \u03A9 resistor is zero.
Note: the 2 \u03A9 resistor is a bridge between two separate circuits; each loop runs on its own, with 2 A in the left loop and 2 A in the right loop (each needs only its own battery), but 0 A in the 2 \u03A9.
The current through 2 \u03A9 is zero, option (a).`,fig:{w:420,h:200,alt:"Two separate loops: a 10 volt cell with a 5 ohm resistor on the left and a 10 ohm resistor with a 20 volt cell on the right, joined only by a 2 ohm resistor on the bottom wire",d:[["l",50,40,150,40],["l",50,40,50,76],["l",50,76,50,96],["l",38,96,62,96],["l",44,103,56,103,"B"],["l",50,103,50,124],["t",32,106,"10 V","e"],["l",50,124,50,150],["l",50,150,150,150],["l",150,40,150,62],["p","M150 62 L145 67.5 L155 78.5 L145 89.5 L155 100.5 L145 111.5 L155 122.5 L150 128"],["l",150,128,150,150],["t",162,98,"5 \u03A9"],["l",150,150,183,150],["p","M183 150 L187.7 155 L197 145 L206.3 155 L215.7 145 L225 155 L234.3 145 L239 150"],["t",211,172,"2 \u03A9","m"],["l",239,150,272,150],["l",272,150,272,128],["p","M272 128 L277 122.5 L267 111.5 L277 100.5 L267 89.5 L277 78.5 L267 67.5 L272 62"],["l",272,62,272,40],["t",284,98,"10 \u03A9"],["l",272,40,372,40],["l",372,40,372,76],["l",372,76,372,96],["l",360,96,384,96],["l",366,103,378,103,"B"],["l",372,103,372,124],["l",372,124,372,150],["l",372,150,272,150],["t",392,104,"20 V"]]}},{pn:89,y:"AIEEE 2004",tp:"cres",st:"Temperature dependence",q:"The thermistors are usually made of",o:["metals with low temperature coefficient of resistivity","metals with high temperature coefficient of resistivity","metal oxides with high temperature coefficient of resistivity","semiconducting materials having low temperature coefficient of resistivity"],c:2,s:`A thermistor is a resistor used as a temperature sensor, so its resistance must change a lot for a small change in temperature.
That needs a HIGH temperature coefficient of resistivity, which rules out (a) and (d).
Pure metals do change resistance with temperature, but only slightly (about 0.4% per \xB0C), and they are poor sensors, so (b) is not what thermistors are made of.
Thermistors are made of mixtures of semiconducting metal oxides (such as oxides of nickel, manganese, cobalt), whose resistance changes strongly with temperature.
Option (c).`},{pn:90,y:"AIEEE 2004",tp:"ccmb",st:"Series and parallel combinations",q:"An electric current is passed through a circuit containing two wires of the same material, connected in parallel. If the lengths and radii of the wires are in the ratio of 4/3 and 2/3, then the ratio of the currents passing through the wire will be",o:["3","1/3","8/9","2"],c:1,s:`Wires in parallel have the same voltage across them, so the current in each is I = V/R, that is I \u221D 1/R.
R = \u03C1l/(\u03C0r\xB2), so 1/R \u221D r\xB2/l (same material, same \u03C1).
I\u2081/I\u2082 = (r\u2081\xB2/l\u2081) / (r\u2082\xB2/l\u2082) = (r\u2081/r\u2082)\xB2 \xD7 (l\u2082/l\u2081).
r\u2081/r\u2082 = 2/3 and l\u2081/l\u2082 = 4/3, so l\u2082/l\u2081 = 3/4.
I\u2081/I\u2082 = (4/9) \xD7 (3/4) = 1/3.
Option (b).`},{pn:91,y:"AIEEE 2004",tp:"ccmb",st:"Series and parallel combinations",q:"The resistance of the series combination of two resistances is S. When they are joined in parallel the total resistance is P. If S = nP, then the minimum possible value of n is",o:["4","3","2","1"],c:0,s:`Let the two resistances be R\u2081 and R\u2082. Then S = R\u2081 + R\u2082 and P = R\u2081R\u2082/(R\u2081 + R\u2082).
So n = S/P = (R\u2081 + R\u2082)\xB2/(R\u2081R\u2082).
Expand: n = (R\u2081\xB2 + 2R\u2081R\u2082 + R\u2082\xB2)/(R\u2081R\u2082) = R\u2081/R\u2082 + R\u2082/R\u2081 + 2.
For any positive numbers x + 1/x \u2265 2, with equality when x = 1, so R\u2081/R\u2082 + R\u2082/R\u2081 \u2265 2.
So n \u2265 2 + 2 = 4, with the minimum at R\u2081 = R\u2082.
Option (a).`},{pn:92,y:"AIEEE 2004",tp:"cres",st:"Temperature dependence",q:"A piece of copper and another of germanium are cooled from room temperature to 77 K, the resistance of",o:["each of them increases","each of them decreases","copper decreases and germanium increases","copper increases and germanium decreases"],c:2,s:`Copper is a metal. On cooling, the lattice vibrates less, electrons collide less, and the resistance falls.
Germanium is a semiconductor. On cooling, fewer electrons have enough energy to leave the bonds, so the number of carriers drops sharply and resistance rises.
So copper decreases and germanium increases, option (c).`},{pn:93,y:"IIT-JEE 2004",tp:"ccmb",st:"Equivalent resistance of networks",q:"Six identical resistors are connected as shown in the figure. The equivalent resistance will be",o:["maximum between P and R","maximum between Q and R","maximum between P and Q","all are equal"],c:2,s:`Reduce each side of the triangle first, then compare the three pairs of corners.
Side PQ is one resistor: R.
Side PR has three resistors in parallel: R/3.
Side QR has two resistors in parallel: R/2.
Between P and Q, the direct R is in parallel with the path P to R to Q = R/3 + R/2 = 5R/6, so R(5R/6)/(R + 5R/6) = 5R/11 \u2248 0.45 R.
Between Q and R: R/2 in parallel with R + R/3 = 4R/3 gives (R/2)(4R/3)/(R/2 + 4R/3) = 4R/11 \u2248 0.36 R.
Between P and R: R/3 in parallel with R + R/2 = 3R/2 gives (R/3)(3R/2)/(R/3 + 3R/2) = 3R/11 \u2248 0.27 R.
The largest value is between P and Q, option (c).`,fig:{w:340,h:270,alt:"Triangle with corners P, Q and R: side PQ is one resistor R, side PR has three resistors R in parallel, side QR has two resistors R in parallel",d:[["l",70,40,125,40],["p","M125 40 L132.5 45 L147.5 35 L162.5 45 L177.5 35 L192.5 45 L207.5 35 L215 40"],["l",215,40,270,40],["t",170,28,"R","m"],["t",58,36,"P","e"],["t",282,36,"Q"],["t",170,252,"R","m"],["l",70,40,95.6,88.7],["l",135.2,163.9,170,230],["l",95.6,88.7,123.9,73.8],["l",163.5,149,135.2,163.9],["l",123.9,73.8,130.5,86.2],["p","M130.5 86.2 L129.1 92.2 L140.6 96.9 L138 109 L149.5 113.7 L146.8 125.8 L158.3 130.5 L157 136.6"],["l",157,136.6,163.5,149],["t",131.7,115.4,"R","e"],["l",95.6,88.7,95.6,88.7],["l",135.2,163.9,135.2,163.9],["l",95.6,88.7,102.1,101.1],["p","M102.1 101.1 L100.8 107.1 L112.3 111.8 L109.7 123.9 L121.2 128.6 L118.5 140.8 L130 145.4 L128.7 151.5"],["l",128.7,151.5,135.2,163.9],["t",127.4,130.3,"R",""],["l",95.6,88.7,67.3,103.6],["l",106.9,178.8,135.2,163.9],["l",67.3,103.6,73.8,116],["p","M73.8 116 L72.5 122 L84 126.7 L81.3 138.8 L92.8 143.5 L90.2 155.7 L101.7 160.3 L100.4 166.4"],["l",100.4,166.4,106.9,178.8],["t",99.1,145.2,"R",""],["c",95.6,88.7,2.2,"F"],["c",135.2,163.9,2.2,"F"],["l",270,40,242.1,93.1],["l",204.8,163.9,170,230],["l",242.1,93.1,254.4,99.6],["l",217.2,170.4,204.8,163.9],["l",254.4,99.6,247.9,112],["p","M247.9 112 L242.4 114 L245.4 125.4 L234.3 129.3 L237.3 140.7 L226.2 144.7 L229.3 156 L223.7 158"],["l",223.7,158,217.2,170.4],["t",223.8,139,"R","e"],["l",242.1,93.1,229.7,86.6],["l",192.4,157.4,204.8,163.9],["l",229.7,86.6,223.1,99],["p","M223.1 99 L217.6 100.9 L220.6 112.3 L209.5 116.3 L212.6 127.7 L201.4 131.6 L204.5 143 L198.9 145"],["l",198.9,145,192.4,157.4],["t",223,126,"R",""],["c",242.1,93.1,2.2,"F"],["c",204.8,163.9,2.2,"F"]]},sfig:{w:300,h:230,alt:"Reduced triangle: PQ equals R, PR equals R over 3, QR equals R over 2",d:[["l",50,40,120,40],["p","M120 40 L125 45 L135 35 L145 45 L155 35 L165 45 L175 35 L180 40"],["l",180,40,250,40],["t",150,28,"R","m"],["l",50,40,86.8,98.8],["p","M86.8 98.8 L84.7 105 L97.6 106.8 L93.6 119.1 L106.4 120.9 L102.4 133.2 L115.3 135 L113.2 141.2"],["l",113.2,141.2,150,200],["t",84,126,"R/3","e"],["l",250,40,213.2,98.8],["p","M213.2 98.8 L206.8 99.7 L210.9 112.1 L198 113.8 L202 126.2 L189.1 127.9 L193.2 140.3 L186.8 141.2"],["l",186.8,141.2,150,200],["t",216,126,"R/2"],["t",38,36,"P","e"],["t",262,36,"Q"],["t",150,220,"R","m"]]}},{pn:94,y:"AIEEE 2003",tp:"cres",st:"Temperature dependence",q:"The difference in the variation of resistance with temperature in a metal and a semiconductor arises essentially due to the difference in the",o:["crystal structure","variation of the number of charge carriers with temperature","type of bonding","variation of scattering mechanism with temperature"],c:1,s:`Resistivity is \u03C1 = m/(n e\xB2 \u03C4), where n is the number of charge carriers and \u03C4 the time between collisions.
In a metal n is almost fixed, and heating shortens \u03C4, so the resistance rises.
In a semiconductor heating frees many more carriers, so n rises so much that it beats the change in \u03C4, and the resistance falls.
The essential difference is therefore how the number of charge carriers varies with temperature, option (b).`},{pn:95,y:"AIEEE 2003",tp:"cres",st:"Temperature dependence",q:"A strip of copper and another germanium are cooled from room temperature to 80 K. The resistance of",o:["each of these decreases","copper strip increases and that of germanium decreases","copper strip decreases and that of germanium increases","each of these increases"],c:2,s:`Copper is a metal: on cooling there is less thermal vibration of the lattice, so electrons scatter less and its resistance decreases.
Germanium is a semiconductor: on cooling fewer electrons are freed from the bonds, so there are fewer carriers and its resistance increases.
So copper decreases and germanium increases, option (c).`},{pn:96,y:"AIEEE 2003",tp:"cres",st:"Stretching and shape change of wires",q:"The length of a given cylindrical wire is increased by 100%. Due to the consequent decrease in diameter the change in the resistance of the wire will be",o:["200%","100%","50%","300%"],c:3,s:`Stretching keeps the volume constant, so if the length becomes 2l (100% increase), the area becomes A/2.
R = \u03C1l/A, so the new resistance is R' = \u03C1(2l)/(A/2) = 4 \xD7 \u03C1l/A = 4R.
Note: in general R \u221D l\xB2 for a stretched wire, and (2)\xB2 = 4.
Change = (4R \u2212 R)/R \xD7 100% = 300%.
Option (d).`},{pn:97,y:"AIEEE 2003",tp:"ccmb",st:"Equivalent resistance of networks",q:"A 3 volt battery with negligible internal resistance is connected in a circuit as shown in the figure. The current I in the circuit will be",fig:{w:260,h:170,alt:"A 3 V battery in series with a triangle of three 3 \u03A9 resistors; the battery top is joined to the apex and the battery bottom to the lower left corner of the triangle",d:[["l",50,40,50,80],["l",40,82,60,82],["l",44.5,88,55.5,88,"B"],["l",50,90,50,130],["t",36,90,"3 V","e"],["l",50,40,100,40],["l",104,40,122,40,"a"],["l",122,40,170,40],["t",113,31,"I","m"],["l",50,130,130,130],["p","M170,40 L161.2,59.8 L154.3,66.8 L156.9,78 L146.8,83.6 L149.5,94.8 L139.3,100.4 L138.8,110.2 L130,130"],["p","M170,40 L178.8,59.8 L179.3,69.6 L189.5,75.2 L186.8,86.4 L196.9,92 L194.3,103.2 L201.2,110.2 L210,130"],["p","M130,130 L147.6,130 L155.1,134.5 L162.5,125.5 L170,134.5 L177.5,125.5 L184.9,134.5 L192.4,130 L210,130"],["c",170,40,2.4,"F"],["c",130,130,2.4,"F"],["c",210,130,2.4,"F"],["t",140,82,"3 \u03A9","e"],["t",202,82,"3 \u03A9"],["t",170,152,"3 \u03A9","m"]]},o:["1 A","1.5 A","2 A","(1/3) A"],c:1,s:`The battery sees one equivalent resistance, so find it first and then use I = V/R.
The two upper sides of the triangle and the bottom side form two paths between the apex and the lower left corner.
Path 1 is the left 3 \u03A9 resistor alone. Path 2 is the right 3 \u03A9 and the bottom 3 \u03A9 in series, 3 + 3 = 6 \u03A9.
The two paths are in parallel: R = (3 \xD7 6)/(3 + 6) = 2 \u03A9 (see the figure below).
I = V/R = 3/2 = 1.5 A.
So the current is 1.5 A, option (b).`,sfig:{w:290,h:165,alt:"Equivalent circuit between the battery terminals: one 3 \u03A9 resistor in parallel with two 3 \u03A9 resistors in series",d:[["t",26,70,"A","m"],["c",30,80,3],["l",33,80,70,80],["c",70,80,2.4,"F"],["l",70,80,70,35],["l",70,35,100,35],["p","M100,35 L119.8,35 L128.2,39.5 L136.6,30.5 L145,39.5 L153.4,30.5 L161.8,39.5 L170.2,35 L190,35"],["l",190,35,230,35],["l",230,35,230,80],["c",230,80,2.4,"F"],["l",70,80,70,120],["l",70,120,95,120],["p","M95,120 L106,120 L113,124.5 L120,115.5 L127,124.5 L134,120 L145,120"],["p","M145,120 L156,120 L163,124.5 L170,115.5 L177,124.5 L184,120 L195,120"],["l",195,120,230,120],["l",230,120,230,80],["l",230,80,257,80],["c",260,80,3],["t",264,70,"B","m"],["t",145,22,"3 \u03A9","m"],["t",120,142,"3 \u03A9","m"],["t",170,142,"3 \u03A9","m"],["t",145,160,"3 \u03A9 \u2225 6 \u03A9 = 2 \u03A9","ms"]]}},{pn:98,y:"IIT-JEE 2003",tp:"cres",st:"Ohm's law and V-I graphs",q:"Which of the following set-ups can be used to verify Ohm's law? (The four circuits (a) to (d) are drawn in the figure.)",fig:{w:380,h:240,alt:"Four circuits (a) to (d), each with a battery, a rheostat, two resistors, an ammeter and a voltmeter connected differently",d:[["p","M40,25 L40,41.8 L35.5,47.9 L44.5,53.9 L35.5,60 L44.5,66.1 L35.5,72.1 L40,78.2 L40,95"],["p","M40,25 L50,25 L56,29.5 L62,20.5 L68,29.5 L74,20.5 L80,25 L90,25"],["l",48,34,82,14,"a"],["p","M115,25 L115,41.8 L110.5,47.9 L119.5,53.9 L110.5,60 L119.5,66.1 L110.5,72.1 L115,78.2 L115,95"],["c",115,25,2.4,"F"],["c",115,95,2.4,"F"],["l",180,25,180,95],["l",90,25,145,25],["c",145,25,2.4,"F"],["l",145,25,152.5,25],["c",162.5,25,10],["t",162.5,29,"A","m"],["l",172.5,25,180,25],["l",145,25,145,50],["c",145,60,10],["t",145,64,"V","m"],["l",145,70,145,95],["c",145,95,2.4,"F"],["l",40,95,145,95],["l",145,95,159.5,95],["l",165.5,85,165.5,105],["l",159.5,89.5,159.5,100.5,"B"],["l",165.5,95,180,95],["t",28,65,"(a)","e"],["l",225,25,225,50],["c",225,60,10],["t",225,64,"A","m"],["l",225,70,225,95],["p","M225,25 L235,25 L241,29.5 L247,20.5 L253,29.5 L259,20.5 L265,25 L275,25"],["l",233,34,267,14,"a"],["p","M300,25 L300,41.8 L295.5,47.9 L304.5,53.9 L295.5,60 L304.5,66.1 L295.5,72.1 L300,78.2 L300,95"],["c",300,25,2.4,"F"],["c",300,95,2.4,"F"],["l",365,25,365,95],["l",275,25,365,25],["c",330,25,2.4,"F"],["l",330,25,330,50],["c",330,60,10],["t",330,64,"V","m"],["l",330,70,330,95],["c",330,95,2.4,"F"],["l",225,95,330,95],["l",330,95,344.5,95],["l",350.5,85,350.5,105],["l",344.5,89.5,344.5,100.5,"B"],["l",350.5,95,365,95],["t",213,65,"(b)","e"],["p","M40,145 L40,161.8 L35.5,167.9 L44.5,173.9 L35.5,180 L44.5,186.1 L35.5,192.1 L40,198.2 L40,215"],["p","M40,145 L50,145 L56,149.5 L62,140.5 L68,149.5 L74,140.5 L80,145 L90,145"],["l",48,154,82,134,"a"],["p","M115,145 L115,161.8 L110.5,167.9 L119.5,173.9 L110.5,180 L119.5,186.1 L110.5,192.1 L115,198.2 L115,215"],["c",115,145,2.4,"F"],["c",115,215,2.4,"F"],["l",180,145,180,215],["l",90,145,115,145],["l",115,145,137.5,145],["c",147.5,145,10],["t",147.5,149,"A","m"],["l",157.5,145,180,145],["l",40,215,67.5,215],["c",77.5,215,10],["t",77.5,219,"V","m"],["l",87.5,215,115,215],["l",115,215,144.5,215],["l",150.5,205,150.5,225],["l",144.5,209.5,144.5,220.5,"B"],["l",150.5,215,180,215],["t",28,185,"(c)","e"],["p","M225,145 L225,161.8 L220.5,167.9 L229.5,173.9 L220.5,180 L229.5,186.1 L220.5,192.1 L225,198.2 L225,215"],["p","M225,145 L235,145 L241,149.5 L247,140.5 L253,149.5 L259,140.5 L265,145 L275,145"],["l",233,154,267,134,"a"],["p","M300,145 L300,161.8 L295.5,167.9 L304.5,173.9 L295.5,180 L304.5,186.1 L295.5,192.1 L300,198.2 L300,215"],["c",300,145,2.4,"F"],["c",300,215,2.4,"F"],["l",365,145,365,215],["l",275,145,300,145],["l",300,145,322.5,145],["c",332.5,145,10],["t",332.5,149,"V","m"],["l",342.5,145,365,145],["l",225,215,252.5,215],["c",262.5,215,10],["t",262.5,219,"A","m"],["l",272.5,215,300,215],["l",300,215,329.5,215],["l",335.5,205,335.5,225],["l",329.5,209.5,329.5,220.5,"B"],["l",335.5,215,365,215],["t",213,185,"(d)","e"]]},o:["Circuit (a)","Circuit (b)","Circuit (c)","Circuit (d)"],c:0,s:`To verify Ohm's law we must measure the current through a resistor with an ammeter (in series) and the potential difference across it with a voltmeter (in parallel), and vary the current with a rheostat.
An ideal ammeter has tiny resistance, so it must be in series; an ideal voltmeter has huge resistance, so it must be in parallel.
Circuit (a): the ammeter is in series with the cell and the voltmeter is across the resistor, so both readings are correct.
Circuit (b): the ammeter is only in the branch with the rheostat, so it does not read the current through the resistor.
Circuits (c) and (d): the voltmeter is connected in series (a voltmeter in series blocks the current), which is wrong.
So only circuit (a) can be used, option (a).`},{pn:99,y:"AIEEE 2002",tp:"cres",st:"Temperature dependence",q:"By increasing the temperature, the specific resistance of a conductor and a semiconductor",o:["increases for both","decreases for both","increases, decreases","decreases, increases"],c:2,s:`In a metallic conductor the number of free electrons is almost fixed, while heating makes the ions vibrate more and collide with the electrons more often. So the resistivity increases.
In a semiconductor heating frees many more charge carriers (electrons and holes), and this effect is stronger than the extra collisions. So the resistivity decreases.
Hence the conductor increases and the semiconductor decreases, option (c).`},{pn:100,y:"IIT-JEE 1997",tp:"cdrf",st:"Current density",q:"A steady current flows in a metallic conductor of non-uniform cross-section. The quantity/quantities constant along the length of the conductor is/are",o:["current, electric field and drift speed","drift speed only","current and drift speed","current only"],c:3,s:`In a steady state, charge cannot pile up anywhere, so the same charge crosses every section per second. The current I is therefore the same along the conductor.
The drift speed is v_d = I/(nAe). I, n and e are constant but the area A changes, so v_d changes (it is larger where the wire is thinner).
The electric field is E = J/\u03C3 = I/(\u03C3A), which also changes with A.
Only the current is constant, option (d).`},{pn:101,y:"IIT-JEE 1988",tp:"cres",st:"Temperature dependence",q:"A piece of copper and another of germanium are cooled from room temperature to 80 K. The resistance of",o:["each of them increases","each of them decreases","copper increases and germanium decreases","copper decreases and germanium increases"],c:3,s:`Copper is a metal. On cooling, the lattice vibrations reduce, electrons collide less, so its resistance decreases.
Germanium is a semiconductor. On cooling, fewer electrons have enough energy to jump to the conduction band, so there are fewer carriers and its resistance increases.
So copper decreases and germanium increases, option (d).`},{pn:102,y:"IIT-JEE 1983",tp:"ccmb",st:"Equivalent resistance of networks",q:"The current i in the circuit (see figure) is",fig:{w:260,h:170,alt:"A 2 V battery in series with a triangle of three 30 \u03A9 resistors; the battery top is joined to the apex and the battery bottom to the lower left corner of the triangle",d:[["l",50,40,50,80],["l",40,82,60,82],["l",44.5,88,55.5,88,"B"],["l",50,90,50,130],["t",36,90,"2 V","e"],["l",50,40,100,40],["l",104,40,122,40,"a"],["l",122,40,170,40],["t",113,31,"I","m"],["l",50,130,130,130],["p","M170,40 L161.2,59.8 L154.3,66.8 L156.9,78 L146.8,83.6 L149.5,94.8 L139.3,100.4 L138.8,110.2 L130,130"],["p","M170,40 L178.8,59.8 L179.3,69.6 L189.5,75.2 L186.8,86.4 L196.9,92 L194.3,103.2 L201.2,110.2 L210,130"],["p","M130,130 L147.6,130 L155.1,134.5 L162.5,125.5 L170,134.5 L177.5,125.5 L184.9,134.5 L192.4,130 L210,130"],["c",170,40,2.4,"F"],["c",130,130,2.4,"F"],["c",210,130,2.4,"F"],["t",140,82,"30 \u03A9","e"],["t",202,82,"30 \u03A9"],["t",170,152,"30 \u03A9","m"]]},o:["(1/45) ampere","(1/15) ampere","(1/10) ampere","(1/5) ampere"],c:2,s:`The three 30 \u03A9 resistors form a triangle; the cell is connected between the apex and the lower left corner.
Between these two points there are two paths: the left 30 \u03A9 alone, and the other two 30 \u03A9 resistors in series (60 \u03A9).
These paths are in parallel: R = (30 \xD7 60)/(30 + 60) = 20 \u03A9 (see the figure below).
i = V/R = 2/20 = 0.1 A = (1/10) A.
So the answer is option (c).`,sfig:{w:290,h:165,alt:"Equivalent circuit between the battery terminals: one 30 \u03A9 resistor in parallel with two 30 \u03A9 resistors in series",d:[["t",26,70,"A","m"],["c",30,80,3],["l",33,80,70,80],["c",70,80,2.4,"F"],["l",70,80,70,35],["l",70,35,100,35],["p","M100,35 L119.8,35 L128.2,39.5 L136.6,30.5 L145,39.5 L153.4,30.5 L161.8,39.5 L170.2,35 L190,35"],["l",190,35,230,35],["l",230,35,230,80],["c",230,80,2.4,"F"],["l",70,80,70,120],["l",70,120,95,120],["p","M95,120 L106,120 L113,124.5 L120,115.5 L127,124.5 L134,120 L145,120"],["p","M145,120 L156,120 L163,124.5 L170,115.5 L177,124.5 L184,120 L195,120"],["l",195,120,230,120],["l",230,120,230,80],["l",230,80,257,80],["c",260,80,3],["t",264,70,"B","m"],["t",145,22,"30 \u03A9","m"],["t",120,142,"30 \u03A9","m"],["t",170,142,"30 \u03A9","m"],["t",145,160,"30 \u03A9 \u2225 60 \u03A9 = 20 \u03A9","ms"]]}},{pn:103,y:"IIT-JEE 1980",tp:"cres",st:"Temperature dependence",q:"The temperature coefficient of resistance of a wire is 0.00125 per \xB0C. At 300 K, its resistance is 1 ohm. This resistance of the wire will be 2 ohm at",o:["1154 K","1100 K","1400 K","1127 K"],c:3,s:`For a wire, R = R\u2080(1 + \u03B1t), where R\u2080 is the resistance at 0 \xB0C and t is the temperature in \xB0C. Here \u03B1 = 0.00125 per \xB0C.
300 K is 27 \xB0C, so 1 = R\u2080(1 + 0.00125 \xD7 27) = R\u2080 \xD7 1.03375.
At the required temperature t: 2 = R\u2080(1 + 0.00125 t).
Divide the two equations: 1 + 0.00125 t = 2 \xD7 1.03375 = 2.0675.
0.00125 t = 1.0675, so t = 854 \xB0C.
T = 854 + 273 = 1127 K.
Note: if \u03B1 were measured from 300 K itself, a rise of 800 \xB0C would give 1100 K. The standard definition of \u03B1 uses 0 \xB0C, so the printed answer is 1127 K.
So the answer is option (d).`},{pn:104,y:"JEE Advanced 2015",tp:"ccmb",st:"Series and parallel combinations",q:"In an aluminium (Al) bar of square cross section, a square hole is drilled and is filled with iron (Fe) as shown in the figure. The electrical resistivities of Al and Fe are 2.7 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 \u03A9 m and 1.0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \u03A9 m, respectively. The electrical resistance between the two faces P and Q of the composite bar is",fig:{w:250,h:175,alt:"A square aluminium bar 7 mm by 7 mm and 50 mm long with a 2 mm by 2 mm square iron core; P on the front face and Q on the back face",d:[["l",50,70,120,70],["l",120,70,120,140],["l",120,140,50,140],["l",50,140,50,70],["l",50,70,120,30],["l",120,70,190,30],["l",120,140,190,100],["l",120,30,190,30],["l",190,30,190,100],["l",120,30,120,100,"d"],["l",120,100,190,100,"d"],["l",50,140,120,100,"d"],["r",75,95,20,20,"f"],["t",85,109,"Fe","m"],["t",58,84,"Al"],["t",85,90,"2 mm","ms"],["l",50,152,120,152,"b"],["t",85,167,"7 mm","m"],["t",126,154,"P"],["t",195,26,"Q"],["t",162,134,"50 mm","s"]]},o:["2475/64 \u03BC\u03A9","1875/64 \u03BC\u03A9","1875/49 \u03BC\u03A9","2475/132 \u03BC\u03A9"],c:1,s:`Current flows from face P to face Q along the 50 mm length. The aluminium part and the iron part both connect P to Q, so they are two resistors in parallel.
Use R = \u03C1l/A for each part, with l = 50 mm = 0.05 m.
Iron: A = 2 \xD7 2 = 4 mm\xB2 = 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB2. R_Fe = (1.0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 0.05)/(4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076) = 1.25 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \u03A9 = 1250 \u03BC\u03A9.
Aluminium: A = 7 \xD7 7 \u2212 2 \xD7 2 = 45 mm\xB2 = 45 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB2. R_Al = (2.7 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 \xD7 0.05)/(45 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076) = 3.0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 \u03A9 = 30 \u03BC\u03A9.
Parallel: R = (1250 \xD7 30)/(1250 + 30) \u03BC\u03A9 = 37500/1280 \u03BC\u03A9 = 1875/64 \u03BC\u03A9.
So the answer is option (b).`},{pn:105,y:"IIT-JEE 2012",tp:"ckir",st:"Kirchhoff's laws",q:"For the resistance network shown in the figure, choose the correct option(s).",fig:{w:400,h:250,alt:"Resistance network: a 12 V battery at the bottom; left side 2 ohm and 4 ohm to P and Q; right side 2 ohm and 4 ohm to S and T; inside, P to S 2 ohm, P to Q 1 ohm, S to T 1 ohm, Q to T 4 ohm",d:[["l",55,105,55,215],["l",55,215,197,215],["l",197,205,197,225],["l",203,209.5,203,220.5,"B"],["l",203,215,345,215],["l",345,215,345,105],["l",55,180,55,148,"a"],["t",46,162,"I\u2081","e"],["p","M55,105 L71.5,95.1 L80.3,93.9 L83.7,83.7 L94.3,85.5 L97.7,75.3 L108.3,77.1 L113.5,69.9 L130,60"],["p","M55,105 L71.5,114.9 L76.7,122.1 L87.3,120.3 L90.7,130.5 L101.3,128.7 L104.7,138.9 L113.5,140.1 L130,150"],["p","M270,60 L286.5,69.9 L291.7,77.1 L302.3,75.3 L305.7,85.5 L316.3,83.7 L319.7,93.9 L328.5,95.1 L345,105"],["p","M345,105 L328.5,114.9 L319.7,116.1 L316.3,126.3 L305.7,124.5 L302.3,134.7 L291.7,132.9 L286.5,140.1 L270,150"],["l",130,60,143,60],["l",143,60,162,60,"a"],["p","M162,60 L175,60 L188.6,64.5 L202.3,55.5 L216,64.5 L229.7,55.5 L243.4,64.5 L257,60 L270,60"],["t",152,50,"I\u2082","m"],["t",216,47,"2 \u03A9","m"],["p","M130,60 L130,79.8 L125.5,88.2 L134.5,96.6 L125.5,105 L134.5,113.4 L125.5,121.8 L130,130.2 L130,150"],["p","M270,60 L270,79.8 L265.5,88.2 L274.5,96.6 L265.5,105 L274.5,113.4 L265.5,121.8 L270,130.2 L270,150"],["p","M130,150 L160.8,150 L173.9,154.5 L186.9,145.5 L200,154.5 L213.1,145.5 L226.1,154.5 L239.2,150 L270,150"],["c",55,105,2.4,"F"],["c",345,105,2.4,"F"],["c",130,60,2.4,"F"],["c",270,60,2.4,"F"],["c",130,150,2.4,"F"],["c",270,150,2.4,"F"],["t",84,70,"2 \u03A9","e"],["t",80,146,"4 \u03A9","e"],["t",316,70,"2 \u03A9"],["t",320,146,"4 \u03A9"],["t",143,108,"1 \u03A9"],["t",257,108,"1 \u03A9","e"],["t",200,168,"4 \u03A9","m"],["t",200,238,"12 V","m"],["t",124,52,"P","e"],["t",278,52,"S"],["t",124,166,"Q","e"],["t",278,166,"T"]]},o:["The current through PQ is zero.","I\u2081 = 3 A.","The potential at S is less than that at Q.","I\u2082 = 2 A."],c:[0,1,2,3],s:`Let the left end of the battery be at 12 V (I\u2081 enters there) and the right end at 0 V. The network is a mirror image left to right (2 \u03A9 with 2 \u03A9, 4 \u03A9 with 4 \u03A9, 1 \u03A9 with 1 \u03A9), so V_S = 12 \u2212 V_P and V_T = 12 \u2212 V_Q.
Current law at P: (12 \u2212 V_P)/2 = (V_P \u2212 V_S)/2 + (V_P \u2212 V_Q)/1. With V_S = 12 \u2212 V_P this gives 5V_P \u2212 2V_Q = 24.
Current law at Q: (12 \u2212 V_Q)/4 + (V_P \u2212 V_Q)/1 = (V_Q \u2212 V_T)/4. With V_T = 12 \u2212 V_Q this gives 4V_P \u2212 7V_Q = \u221224.
Solving the two equations: V_P = V_Q = 8 V, so V_S = V_T = 4 V (see the figure below).
(a) V_P = V_Q, so no current flows through PQ. True.
(b) I\u2081 = (12 \u2212 8)/2 + (12 \u2212 8)/4 = 2 + 1 = 3 A. True.
(c) V_S = 4 V is less than V_Q = 8 V. True.
(d) I\u2082 = (V_P \u2212 V_S)/2 = (8 \u2212 4)/2 = 2 A. True.
All four statements are correct: options (a), (b), (c) and (d).`,sfig:{w:400,h:250,alt:"The same network with the potential of each junction written: A 12 V, P 8 V, Q 8 V, S 4 V, T 4 V, B 0",d:[["l",55,105,55,215],["l",55,215,197,215],["l",197,205,197,225],["l",203,209.5,203,220.5,"B"],["l",203,215,345,215],["l",345,215,345,105],["l",55,180,55,148,"a"],["t",46,162,"I\u2081","e"],["p","M55,105 L71.5,95.1 L80.3,93.9 L83.7,83.7 L94.3,85.5 L97.7,75.3 L108.3,77.1 L113.5,69.9 L130,60"],["p","M55,105 L71.5,114.9 L76.7,122.1 L87.3,120.3 L90.7,130.5 L101.3,128.7 L104.7,138.9 L113.5,140.1 L130,150"],["p","M270,60 L286.5,69.9 L291.7,77.1 L302.3,75.3 L305.7,85.5 L316.3,83.7 L319.7,93.9 L328.5,95.1 L345,105"],["p","M345,105 L328.5,114.9 L319.7,116.1 L316.3,126.3 L305.7,124.5 L302.3,134.7 L291.7,132.9 L286.5,140.1 L270,150"],["l",130,60,143,60],["l",143,60,162,60,"a"],["p","M162,60 L175,60 L188.6,64.5 L202.3,55.5 L216,64.5 L229.7,55.5 L243.4,64.5 L257,60 L270,60"],["t",152,50,"I\u2082","m"],["t",216,47,"2 \u03A9","m"],["p","M130,60 L130,79.8 L125.5,88.2 L134.5,96.6 L125.5,105 L134.5,113.4 L125.5,121.8 L130,130.2 L130,150"],["p","M270,60 L270,79.8 L265.5,88.2 L274.5,96.6 L265.5,105 L274.5,113.4 L265.5,121.8 L270,130.2 L270,150"],["p","M130,150 L160.8,150 L173.9,154.5 L186.9,145.5 L200,154.5 L213.1,145.5 L226.1,154.5 L239.2,150 L270,150"],["c",55,105,2.4,"F"],["c",345,105,2.4,"F"],["c",130,60,2.4,"F"],["c",270,60,2.4,"F"],["c",130,150,2.4,"F"],["c",270,150,2.4,"F"],["t",84,70,"2 \u03A9","e"],["t",80,146,"4 \u03A9","e"],["t",316,70,"2 \u03A9"],["t",320,146,"4 \u03A9"],["t",143,108,"1 \u03A9"],["t",257,108,"1 \u03A9","e"],["t",200,168,"4 \u03A9","m"],["t",200,238,"12 V","m"],["t",124,52,"P 8 V","e"],["t",278,52,"S 4 V"],["t",124,166,"Q 8 V","e"],["t",278,166,"T 4 V"],["t",47,98,"A 12 V","e"],["t",353,98,"B 0"]]}},{pn:106,y:"IIT-JEE 1998",tp:"ccmb",st:"Equivalent resistance of networks",q:"In the circuit shown in figure the current through",fig:{w:380,h:175,alt:"A 9 V battery with a ladder of resistors: top 3, 2, 2 ohm; bottom 2, 2, 2 ohm; two 8 ohm rungs and a 4 ohm resistor at the right end",d:[["l",50,40,50,80],["l",40,82,60,82],["l",44.5,88,55.5,88,"B"],["l",50,90,50,130],["t",42,90,"9 V","e"],["p","M50,40 L69.8,40 L78.2,44.5 L86.6,35.5 L95,44.5 L103.4,35.5 L111.8,44.5 L120.2,40 L140,40"],["p","M140,40 L159.8,40 L168.2,44.5 L176.6,35.5 L185,44.5 L193.4,35.5 L201.8,44.5 L210.2,40 L230,40"],["p","M230,40 L249.8,40 L258.2,44.5 L266.6,35.5 L275,44.5 L283.4,35.5 L291.8,44.5 L300.2,40 L320,40"],["l",320,40,320,40],["p","M50,130 L69.8,130 L78.2,134.5 L86.6,125.5 L95,134.5 L103.4,125.5 L111.8,134.5 L120.2,130 L140,130"],["p","M140,130 L159.8,130 L168.2,134.5 L176.6,125.5 L185,134.5 L193.4,125.5 L201.8,134.5 L210.2,130 L230,130"],["p","M230,130 L249.8,130 L258.2,134.5 L266.6,125.5 L275,134.5 L283.4,125.5 L291.8,134.5 L300.2,130 L320,130"],["p","M140,40 L140,59.8 L135.5,68.2 L144.5,76.6 L135.5,85 L144.5,93.4 L135.5,101.8 L140,110.2 L140,130"],["p","M230,40 L230,59.8 L225.5,68.2 L234.5,76.6 L225.5,85 L234.5,93.4 L225.5,101.8 L230,110.2 L230,130"],["p","M320,40 L320,59.8 L315.5,68.2 L324.5,76.6 L315.5,85 L324.5,93.4 L315.5,101.8 L320,110.2 L320,130"],["c",140,40,2.4,"F"],["c",230,40,2.4,"F"],["c",140,130,2.4,"F"],["c",230,130,2.4,"F"],["t",95,29,"3 \u03A9","m"],["t",185,29,"2 \u03A9","m"],["t",275,29,"2 \u03A9","m"],["t",95,152,"2 \u03A9","m"],["t",185,152,"2 \u03A9","m"],["t",275,152,"2 \u03A9","m"],["t",152,80,"8 \u03A9"],["t",242,80,"8 \u03A9"],["t",332,80,"4 \u03A9"]]},o:["the 3 \u03A9 resistor is 0.50 A.","the 3 \u03A9 resistor is 0.25 A.","the 4 \u03A9 resistor is 0.50 A.","the 4 \u03A9 resistor is 0.25 A."],c:3,s:`Reduce the ladder from the right-hand end towards the battery.
Right section: 2 + 4 + 2 = 8 \u03A9 in parallel with the 8 \u03A9 rung gives 4 \u03A9.
Next: this 4 \u03A9 with the 2 \u03A9 above and 2 \u03A9 below gives 2 + 4 + 2 = 8 \u03A9, in parallel with the first 8 \u03A9 rung gives 4 \u03A9.
Total resistance = 3 + 4 + 2 = 9 \u03A9, so the battery current is 9/9 = 1 A. This is the current in the 3 \u03A9 resistor, so (a) and (b) are false.
At each rung the current splits equally, because the rung (8 \u03A9) and the rest of the path (8 \u03A9) are equal: 1 A becomes 0.5 A, then 0.25 A (see the figure below).
The 4 \u03A9 resistor carries 0.25 A, so the answer is option (d).`,sfig:{w:380,h:175,alt:"The same circuit with the current in each branch: 1 A in the 3 ohm, 0.5 A and 0.25 A in the middle and 4 ohm parts",d:[["l",50,40,50,80],["l",40,82,60,82],["l",44.5,88,55.5,88,"B"],["l",50,90,50,130],["t",42,90,"9 V","e"],["p","M50,40 L69.8,40 L78.2,44.5 L86.6,35.5 L95,44.5 L103.4,35.5 L111.8,44.5 L120.2,40 L140,40"],["p","M140,40 L159.8,40 L168.2,44.5 L176.6,35.5 L185,44.5 L193.4,35.5 L201.8,44.5 L210.2,40 L230,40"],["p","M230,40 L249.8,40 L258.2,44.5 L266.6,35.5 L275,44.5 L283.4,35.5 L291.8,44.5 L300.2,40 L320,40"],["l",320,40,320,40],["p","M50,130 L69.8,130 L78.2,134.5 L86.6,125.5 L95,134.5 L103.4,125.5 L111.8,134.5 L120.2,130 L140,130"],["p","M140,130 L159.8,130 L168.2,134.5 L176.6,125.5 L185,134.5 L193.4,125.5 L201.8,134.5 L210.2,130 L230,130"],["p","M230,130 L249.8,130 L258.2,134.5 L266.6,125.5 L275,134.5 L283.4,125.5 L291.8,134.5 L300.2,130 L320,130"],["p","M140,40 L140,59.8 L135.5,68.2 L144.5,76.6 L135.5,85 L144.5,93.4 L135.5,101.8 L140,110.2 L140,130"],["p","M230,40 L230,59.8 L225.5,68.2 L234.5,76.6 L225.5,85 L234.5,93.4 L225.5,101.8 L230,110.2 L230,130"],["p","M320,40 L320,59.8 L315.5,68.2 L324.5,76.6 L315.5,85 L324.5,93.4 L315.5,101.8 L320,110.2 L320,130"],["c",140,40,2.4,"F"],["c",230,40,2.4,"F"],["c",140,130,2.4,"F"],["c",230,130,2.4,"F"],["t",95,29,"3 \u03A9","m"],["t",185,29,"2 \u03A9","m"],["t",275,29,"2 \u03A9","m"],["t",95,152,"2 \u03A9","m"],["t",185,152,"2 \u03A9","m"],["t",275,152,"2 \u03A9","m"],["t",152,80,"8 \u03A9"],["t",242,80,"8 \u03A9"],["t",332,80,"4 \u03A9"],["t",95,58,"1 A","m"],["t",185,58,"0.5 A","m"],["t",275,58,"0.25 A","m"],["t",152,96,"0.5 A"],["t",242,96,"0.25 A"],["t",332,96,"0.25 A"]]}},{pn:107,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 3 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cpot",st:"Galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"In the figure shown below, a resistance of 150.4 \u03A9 is connected in series to an ammeter A of resistance 240 \u03A9. A shunt resistance of 10 \u03A9 is connected in parallel with the ammeter. The reading of the ammeter is ___ mA.",fig:{w:300,h:170,alt:"A 20 V cell, a 150.4 ohm resistor and an ammeter A in series; a 10 ohm shunt resistor in parallel with the ammeter",d:[["l",40,40,40,130],["p","M40,40 L59.8,40 L68.2,44.5 L76.6,35.5 L85,44.5 L93.4,35.5 L101.8,44.5 L110.2,40 L130,40"],["l",130,40,150,40],["c",150,40,2.4,"F"],["l",150,40,185,40],["c",195,40,10],["t",195,44,"A","m"],["l",205,40,240,40],["c",240,40,2.4,"F"],["l",240,40,270,40],["l",270,40,270,130],["l",150,40,150,78],["p","M150,78 L169.8,78 L178.2,82.5 L186.6,73.5 L195,82.5 L203.4,73.5 L211.8,82.5 L220.2,78 L240,78"],["l",240,78,240,40],["l",40,130,74,130],["l",74,130,99,130],["l",105,120,105,140],["l",99,124.5,99,135.5,"B"],["l",105,130,130,130],["l",130,130,270,130],["t",85,29,"150.4 \u03A9","m"],["t",195,98,"10 \u03A9","m"],["t",104,152,"20 V","m"]]},n:5,s:`The ammeter and the shunt are in parallel, so first replace them by one resistor.
R_parallel = (240 \xD7 10)/(240 + 10) = 2400/250 = 9.6 \u03A9.
Total resistance = 150.4 + 9.6 = 160 \u03A9.
Main current I = 20/160 = 0.125 A = 125 mA.
In a parallel pair the current divides in the inverse ratio of the resistances, so the ammeter gets I \xD7 10/(240 + 10) = 125 \xD7 10/250 = 5 mA.
The ammeter reads 5 mA.`},{pn:108,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 22 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"crc",st:"Charging and discharging of a capacitor",q:"The net current flowing in the given circuit is ___ A.",fig:{w:400,h:175,alt:"A 2 volt cell with a ladder of resistors: top 2 ohm, 2.5 ohm in series with a 1 microfarad capacitor, 1 ohm; rungs 3, 5 and 4 ohm; diagonal resistors 4, 6 and 8 ohm",d:[["l",50,40,50,85],["l",40,87,60,87],["l",44.5,93,55.5,93,"B"],["l",50,95,50,150],["t",41,95,"2 V","e"],["l",50,150,340,150],["p","M50,40 L69.8,40 L78.2,44.5 L86.6,35.5 L95,44.5 L103.4,35.5 L111.8,44.5 L120.2,40 L140,40"],["p","M140,40 L151,40 L158,44.5 L165,35.5 L172,44.5 L179,40 L190,40"],["l",190,40,202,40],["l",202,30,202,50],["l",208,30,208,50],["l",208,40,240,40],["p","M240,40 L262,40 L271.3,44.5 L280.7,35.5 L290,44.5 L299.3,35.5 L308.7,44.5 L318,40 L340,40"],["p","M140,40 L140,64.2 L136.5,74.5 L143.5,84.7 L136.5,95 L143.5,105.3 L136.5,115.5 L140,125.8 L140,150"],["p","M240,40 L240,64.2 L236.5,74.5 L243.5,84.7 L236.5,95 L243.5,105.3 L236.5,115.5 L240,125.8 L240,150"],["p","M340,40 L340,64.2 L336.5,74.5 L343.5,84.7 L336.5,95 L343.5,105.3 L336.5,115.5 L340,125.8 L340,150"],["p","M50,40 L69.8,64.2 L75.5,76.7 L89.3,82.5 L92.3,97.2 L106.1,103.1 L109.1,117.7 L120.2,125.8 L140,150"],["p","M140,40 L162,64.2 L168.7,76.8 L183.3,82.4 L187.4,97.4 L201.9,102.9 L206.1,117.9 L218,125.8 L240,150"],["p","M240,40 L262,64.2 L268.7,76.8 L283.3,82.4 L287.4,97.4 L301.9,102.9 L306.1,117.9 L318,125.8 L340,150"],["c",140,40,2.4,"F"],["c",240,40,2.4,"F"],["c",140,150,2.4,"F"],["c",240,150,2.4,"F"],["c",50,40,2.4,"F"],["t",95,28,"2 \u03A9","m"],["t",165,28,"2.5 \u03A9","m"],["t",206,28,"1 \u03BCF","m"],["t",290,28,"1 \u03A9","m"],["t",148,132,"3 \u03A9"],["t",248,132,"5 \u03A9"],["t",348,100,"4 \u03A9"],["t",112,128,"4 \u03A9","e"],["t",176,92,"6 \u03A9","e"],["t",276,92,"8 \u03A9","e"]]},n:1,s:`In steady state a capacitor blocks direct current, so the branch with 2.5 \u03A9 and 1 \u03BCF carries no current. Everything to its right then carries no current either.
What remains (see the figure below): the 4 \u03A9 diagonal connects the cell terminals directly, and the 2 \u03A9 resistor is in series with the 3 \u03A9 and 6 \u03A9 pair, whose lower ends are both on the cell's lower wire.
First path: 4 \u03A9 across 2 V gives I\u2081 = 2/4 = 0.5 A.
Second path: 3 \u03A9 \u2225 6 \u03A9 = (3 \xD7 6)/9 = 2 \u03A9, in series with 2 \u03A9 gives 4 \u03A9, so I\u2082 = 2/4 = 0.5 A.
Net current = 0.5 + 0.5 = 1 A.
Note: the bottom wire is one single node, so the resistors on it join the cell's lower terminal directly.`,sfig:{w:340,h:165,alt:"Steady state: the capacitor branch carries no current; the cell drives a 4 ohm resistor in parallel with 2 ohm in series with 2 ohm",d:[["l",40,35,40,78],["l",30,80,50,80],["l",34.5,86,45.5,86,"B"],["l",40,88,40,125],["t",31,88,"2 V","e"],["l",40,35,210,35],["l",40,125,210,125],["p","M130,35 L130,54.8 L125.5,63.2 L134.5,71.6 L125.5,80 L134.5,88.4 L125.5,96.8 L130,105.2 L130,125"],["c",130,35,2.4,"F"],["c",130,125,2.4,"F"],["t",140,84,"4 \u03A9"],["c",210,35,2.4,"F"],["p","M210,35 L210,44.9 L205.5,49.1 L214.5,53.3 L205.5,57.5 L214.5,61.7 L205.5,65.9 L210,70.1 L210,80"],["p","M210,80 L210,89.9 L205.5,94.1 L214.5,98.3 L205.5,102.5 L214.5,106.7 L205.5,110.9 L210,115.1 L210,125"],["c",210,125,2.4,"F"],["t",220,62,"2 \u03A9"],["t",220,108,"2 \u03A9 (3 \u03A9 \u2225 6 \u03A9)"]]}},{pn:109,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 23 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"crc",st:"Charging and discharging of a capacitor",q:"At steady state the charge on the capacitor, as shown in the circuit below, is ___ \u03BCC.",fig:{w:300,h:190,alt:"A 5 V cell in series with 10 ohm and 15 ohm; an 8 microfarad capacitor is connected across the 10 ohm resistor",d:[["l",50,40,50,150],["l",50,40,97,40],["l",97,30,97,50],["l",103,30,103,50],["l",103,40,150,40],["l",150,40,150,90],["c",150,90,2.4,"F"],["c",50,90,2.4,"F"],["p","M50,90 L72,90 L81.3,94.5 L90.7,85.5 L100,94.5 L109.3,85.5 L118.7,94.5 L128,90 L150,90"],["p","M150,90 L172,90 L181.3,94.5 L190.7,85.5 L200,94.5 L209.3,85.5 L218.7,94.5 L228,90 L250,90"],["l",250,90,250,150],["l",50,150,107,150],["l",107,140,107,160],["l",113,144.5,113,155.5,"B"],["l",113,150,250,150],["t",100,25,"8 \u03BCF","m"],["t",100,78,"10 \u03A9","m"],["t",200,78,"15 \u03A9","m"],["t",110,174,"5 V","m"]]},n:16,s:`At steady state no current flows through the capacitor branch, so the 10 \u03A9 and 15 \u03A9 resistors carry the whole current in series.
I = 5/(10 + 15) = 0.2 A.
The capacitor is connected across the 10 \u03A9 resistor, so its voltage equals the voltage across that resistor: V = 0.2 \xD7 10 = 2 V.
Q = CV = 8 \u03BCF \xD7 2 V = 16 \u03BCC.
So the charge is 16 \u03BCC.`},{pn:110,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ccmb",st:"Equivalent resistance of networks",q:"A wire of resistance 9 \u03A9 is bent to form an equilateral triangle. Then the equivalent resistance across any two vertices will be ___ ohm.",n:2,s:`The three sides have equal length, so each has resistance 9/3 = 3 \u03A9 (see the figure below).
Between two vertices, one side (3 \u03A9) is directly joined; the other two sides (3 + 3 = 6 \u03A9) are in series, and this path is in parallel with the first.
R = (3 \xD7 6)/(3 + 6) = 18/9 = 2 \u03A9.
So the equivalent resistance is 2 \u03A9.`,sfig:{w:240,h:195,alt:"An equilateral triangle made from the wire; each side has resistance 3 ohm; two vertices P and Q are marked",d:[["p","M40,160 L75.2,160 L90.1,164.5 L105.1,155.5 L120,164.5 L134.9,155.5 L149.9,164.5 L164.8,160 L200,160"],["p","M40,160 L57.6,129.4 L69,118.7 L68.6,101.2 L83.9,92.7 L83.6,75.3 L98.8,66.8 L102.4,51.6 L120,21"],["p","M120,21 L137.6,51.6 L141.2,66.8 L156.4,75.3 L156.1,92.7 L171.4,101.2 L171,118.7 L182.4,129.4 L200,160"],["c",40,160,2.4,"F"],["c",200,160,2.4,"F"],["c",120,21,2.4,"F"],["t",30,166,"P","e"],["t",210,166,"Q"],["t",120,11,"R","m"],["t",120,182,"3 \u03A9","m"],["t",70,84,"3 \u03A9","e"],["t",176,84,"3 \u03A9"]]}},{pn:111,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ccmb",st:"Infinite and cube networks",q:"Twelve wires each having resistance 2 \u03A9 are joined to form a cube. A battery of 6 V emf is joined across points a and c. The voltage difference between e and f is ___ V.",fig:{w:250,h:190,alt:"A wire cube a b c d on the top, h g f e on the bottom, each edge 2 ohm; a 6 V battery joined between a and c",d:[["l",60,85,130,85],["l",130,85,130,155],["l",130,155,60,155],["l",60,155,60,85],["l",105,50,175,50],["l",175,50,175,120],["l",175,120,105,120],["l",105,120,105,50],["l",60,85,105,50],["l",130,85,175,50],["l",130,155,175,120],["l",60,155,105,120],["l",60,85,60,22],["l",60,22,112,22],["l",112,12,112,32],["l",118,16.5,118,27.5,"B"],["l",118,22,175,22],["l",175,22,175,50],["t",115,12,"6 V","m"],["c",60,85,2.4,"F"],["c",175,50,2.4,"F"],["t",52,82,"a","e"],["t",100,44,"b","e"],["t",183,46,"c"],["t",138,100,"d"],["t",130,172,"g","m"],["t",60,172,"h","m"],["t",99,113,"e","e"],["t",183,124,"f"]]},n:1,s:`Take V_a = 6 V and V_c = 0. The cube is symmetric, so use symmetry to find the other potentials (the resistance value does not matter because all wires are equal).
b and d are each joined to both a and c, and are alike, so V_b = V_d = 3 V.
The corners e and g lie directly below b and d, so by symmetry V_e = V_g = 3 V.
Corner h is joined to a (6 V), e (3 V) and g (3 V). No charge builds up at h, so V_h is the average of its three neighbours: (6 + 3 + 3)/3 = 4 V.
By the symmetry a \u2194 c, V_f = 6 \u2212 V_h = 2 V (check: f is joined to c (0), e (3) and g (3): (0 + 3 + 3)/3 = 2 V).
V_e \u2212 V_f = 3 \u2212 2 = 1 V (see the figure below).`,sfig:{w:250,h:190,alt:"The cube with the potential of each corner written: a 6 V, c 0, b, d, e, g 3 V, h 4 V, f 2 V",d:[["l",60,85,130,85],["l",130,85,130,155],["l",130,155,60,155],["l",60,155,60,85],["l",105,50,175,50],["l",175,50,175,120],["l",175,120,105,120],["l",105,120,105,50],["l",60,85,105,50],["l",130,85,175,50],["l",130,155,175,120],["l",60,155,105,120],["l",60,85,60,22],["l",60,22,112,22],["l",112,12,112,32],["l",118,16.5,118,27.5,"B"],["l",118,22,175,22],["l",175,22,175,50],["t",115,12,"6 V","m"],["c",60,85,2.4,"F"],["c",175,50,2.4,"F"],["t",52,82,"a 6 V","e"],["t",100,44,"b 3 V","e"],["t",183,46,"c 0"],["t",138,100,"d 3 V"],["t",130,172,"g 3 V","m"],["t",60,172,"h 4 V","m"],["t",99,113,"e 3 V","e"],["t",183,124,"f 2 V"]]}},{pn:112,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cres",st:"Stretching and shape change of wires",q:"Two wires A and B are made up of the same material and have the same mass. Wire A has radius 2.0 mm and wire B has radius of 4.0 mm. The resistance of wire B is 2 \u03A9. The resistance of wire A is ___ \u03A9.",n:32,s:`Same material means the same density and resistivity; same mass means the same volume V = A\xB7l.
Volume = \u03C0r\xB2l is fixed, so l \u221D 1/r\xB2.
R = \u03C1l/A = \u03C1l/(\u03C0r\xB2) \u221D 1/r\u2074 (one 1/r\xB2 from l and another from the area).
R_A/R_B = (r_B/r_A)\u2074 = (4/2)\u2074 = 16.
R_A = 16 \xD7 2 = 32 \u03A9.`},{pn:113,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 5 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ccmb",st:"Series and parallel combinations",q:"A wire of resistance 20 \u03A9 is divided into 10 equal parts, resulting pairs. A combination of two parts are connected in parallel and so on. Now resulting pairs of parallel combination are connected in series. The equivalent resistance of final combination is ___ \u03A9.",n:5,s:`Ten equal parts of a 20 \u03A9 wire have 20/10 = 2 \u03A9 each.
The parts are taken two at a time, giving 5 pairs. Each pair in parallel: (2 \xD7 2)/(2 + 2) = 1 \u03A9.
The five pairs are then joined in series: 5 \xD7 1 = 5 \u03A9.
So the equivalent resistance is 5 \u03A9.`},{pn:114,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cres",st:"Stretching and shape change of wires",q:"A wire of resistance R and radius r is stretched till its radius became r/2. If new resistance of the stretched wire is xR, then value of x is ___.",n:16,s:`Stretching does not change the volume: \u03C0r\xB2l = \u03C0(r/2)\xB2l\u2032, so l\u2032 = 4l.
The new area is A\u2032 = \u03C0(r/2)\xB2 = A/4.
R\u2032 = \u03C1l\u2032/A\u2032 = \u03C1(4l)/(A/4) = 16 \u03C1l/A = 16R.
So x = 16.
Note: when a wire is stretched at constant volume, R \u221D l\xB2 (or \u221D 1/r\u2074), here (4)\xB2 = 16.`},{pn:115,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cres",st:"Temperature dependence",q:"Resistance of a wire at 0\xB0C, 100\xB0C and t \xB0C is found to be 10 \u03A9, 10.2 \u03A9 and 10.95 \u03A9, respectively. The temperature t in Kelvin scale is ___.",n:748,s:`For a wire, R_t = R\u2080(1 + \u03B1t). Use the first two readings to find \u03B1.
10.2 = 10(1 + \u03B1 \xD7 100), so 1 + 100\u03B1 = 1.02 and \u03B1 = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 per \xB0C.
Now for the third reading: 10.95 = 10(1 + \u03B1 t), so \u03B1t = 0.095.
t = 0.095/(2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074) = 475 \xB0C.
In kelvin: T = 475 + 273 = 748 K.`},{pn:116,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cres",st:"Temperature dependence",q:"At room temperature (27\xB0C), the resistance of a heating element is 50 \u03A9. The temperature coefficient of the material is 2.4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \xB0C\u207B\xB9. The temperature of the element, when its resistance is 62 \u03A9, is ___ \xB0C.",n:1027,s:`Use R = R\u2081(1 + \u03B1\u0394T), where R\u2081 = 50 \u03A9 is the resistance at 27 \xB0C.
62 = 50(1 + 2.4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \xD7 \u0394T), so 1 + 2.4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \u0394T = 1.24.
2.4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \u0394T = 0.24, so \u0394T = 1000 \xB0C.
The final temperature is 27 + 1000 = 1027 \xB0C.`},{pn:117,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cres",st:"Ohm's law and V-I graphs",q:"To determine the resistance (R) of a wire, a circuit is designed below. The V-I characteristic curve for this circuit is plotted for the voltmeter and the ammeter readings as shown in figure. The value of R is ___ \u03A9.",fig:{w:450,h:215,alt:"Left: a variable cell E, a milliammeter and the wire R in series, with a voltmeter and a 10 kilo-ohm resistor in parallel across R. Right: an I against V graph, a straight line from the origin through (4 V, 2 mA), (6 V, 3 mA) and (8 V, 4 mA)",d:[["l",25,100,25,185],["l",25,100,65,100],["p","M65,100 L81,100 L89,104 L97,96 L105,104 L113,96 L121,104 L129,100 L145,100"],["l",145,100,185,100],["l",185,100,185,127],["c",185,138,11],["t",185,142,"mA","ms"],["l",185,149,185,185],["l",185,185,122,185],["l",116,175,116,195],["l",122,179.5,122,190.5,"B"],["l",116,185,25,185],["l",106,197,130,174,"a"],["t",140,205,"E","m"],["l",65,100,65,40],["l",65,40,96,40],["c",105,40,9],["t",105,44,"V","m"],["l",114,40,145,40],["l",145,40,145,100],["p","M65,70 L81,70 L89,73.5 L97,66.5 L105,73.5 L113,66.5 L121,73.5 L129,70 L145,70"],["c",65,70,2.4,"F"],["c",145,70,2.4,"F"],["c",65,100,2.4,"F"],["c",145,100,2.4,"F"],["t",105,59,"10 k\u03A9","m"],["t",105,122,"R","m"],["l",260,170,260,25,"a"],["l",260,170,395,170,"a"],["t",262,18,"I (mA)","s"],["t",398,174,"V (volt)","s"],["t",252,183,"O","e"],["l",260,170,384,37],["l",316,170,316,110,"d"],["l",260,110,316,110,"d"],["l",344,170,344,80,"d"],["l",260,80,344,80,"d"],["l",372,170,372,50,"d"],["l",260,50,372,50,"d"],["t",316,184,"4","m"],["t",344,184,"6","m"],["t",372,184,"8","m"],["t",253,114,"2","e"],["t",253,84,"3","e"],["t",253,54,"4","e"]]},n:2500,s:`The voltmeter is in parallel with R and also with the 10 k\u03A9 resistor, so the ammeter reads the current through both together.
From the graph, I = 2 mA when V = 4 V (and 4 mA at 8 V), so the straight line has V/I = 4 V/2 mA = 2000 \u03A9.
This 2000 \u03A9 is the combined resistance of R in parallel with 10 k\u03A9: 1/2000 = 1/R + 1/10000.
1/R = 1/2000 \u2212 1/10000 = (5 \u2212 1)/10000 = 4/10000.
R = 10000/4 = 2500 \u03A9.`},{pn:118,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cdrf",st:"Current and charge",q:"The current in a conductor is expressed as I = 3t\xB2 + 4t\xB3, where I is in Ampere and t is in second. The amount of electric charge that flows through a section of the conductor during t = 1 s to t = 2 s is ___ C.",n:22,s:`Current is the rate of flow of charge, I = dq/dt, so the charge is the integral of I over time.
q = \u222B from 1 to 2 of (3t\xB2 + 4t\xB3) dt = [t\xB3 + t\u2074] from 1 to 2.
At t = 2: 8 + 16 = 24. At t = 1: 1 + 1 = 2.
q = 24 \u2212 2 = 22 C.`},{pn:119,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"crc",st:"Charging and discharging of a capacitor",q:"In the given figure, the charge stored in 6 \u03BCF capacitor, when points A and B are joined by a connecting wire is ___ \u03BCC.",fig:{w:290,h:235,alt:"Top terminal at 9 V. Left branch: 6 ohm resistor then point A then 3 microfarad capacitor to earth. Right branch: 6 microfarad capacitor then point B then 3 ohm resistor to earth",d:[["c",130,22,2.4,"F"],["t",140,26,"9 V"],["l",130,22,130,50],["l",60,50,200,50],["l",60,50,60,55],["p","M60,55 L60,62.5 L55.5,68.3 L64.5,74.2 L55.5,80 L64.5,85.8 L55.5,91.7 L60,97.5 L60,105"],["l",60,105,60,115],["c",60,115,2.4,"F"],["l",60,115,60,147],["l",50,147,70,147],["l",50,153,70,153],["l",60,153,60,200],["l",200,50,200,77],["l",190,77,210,77],["l",190,83,210,83],["l",200,83,200,115],["c",200,115,2.4,"F"],["l",200,115,200,125],["p","M200,125 L200,134.8 L195.5,142.3 L204.5,149.9 L195.5,157.5 L204.5,165.1 L195.5,172.7 L200,180.3 L200,190"],["l",200,190,200,200],["l",60,200,200,200],["l",130,200,130,212],["l",118,212,142,212],["l",123,217,137,217],["l",128,222,132,222],["t",48,84,"6 \u03A9","e"],["t",48,119,"A","e"],["t",46,154,"3 \u03BCF","e"],["t",216,84,"6 \u03BCF"],["t",210,119,"B"],["t",209,162,"3 \u03A9"]]},n:36,s:`In steady state the capacitors carry no current. After A and B are joined, the current can flow from the 9 V terminal through the 6 \u03A9 resistor, the connecting wire, and the 3 \u03A9 resistor to earth (see the figure below).
I = 9/(6 + 3) = 1 A.
The potential at the joined point is the voltage across the 3 \u03A9 resistor: V = 1 \xD7 3 = 3 V.
The 6 \u03BCF capacitor is between the 9 V terminal and the joined point, so its voltage is 9 \u2212 3 = 6 V.
Q = CV = 6 \u03BCF \xD7 6 V = 36 \u03BCC.`,sfig:{w:290,h:235,alt:"The same circuit with A and B joined by a wire: both nodes are at 3 V",d:[["c",130,22,2.4,"F"],["t",140,26,"9 V"],["l",130,22,130,50],["l",60,50,200,50],["l",60,50,60,55],["p","M60,55 L60,62.5 L55.5,68.3 L64.5,74.2 L55.5,80 L64.5,85.8 L55.5,91.7 L60,97.5 L60,105"],["l",60,105,60,115],["c",60,115,2.4,"F"],["l",60,115,60,147],["l",50,147,70,147],["l",50,153,70,153],["l",60,153,60,200],["l",200,50,200,77],["l",190,77,210,77],["l",190,83,210,83],["l",200,83,200,115],["c",200,115,2.4,"F"],["l",200,115,200,125],["p","M200,125 L200,134.8 L195.5,142.3 L204.5,149.9 L195.5,157.5 L204.5,165.1 L195.5,172.7 L200,180.3 L200,190"],["l",200,190,200,200],["l",60,200,200,200],["l",130,200,130,212],["l",118,212,142,212],["l",123,217,137,217],["l",128,222,132,222],["t",48,84,"6 \u03A9","e"],["t",48,119,"A","e"],["t",46,154,"3 \u03BCF","e"],["t",216,84,"6 \u03BCF"],["t",210,119,"B"],["t",209,162,"3 \u03A9"],["l",60,115,200,115],["t",130,108,"3 V","m"]]}},{pn:120,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ccmb",st:"Equivalent resistance of networks",q:"Equivalent resistance of the following network is ___ \u03A9.",fig:{w:400,h:170,alt:"Network between terminals A and B: R1 6 ohm shorted by the wire at the left; R2 2 ohm on top; R3 2 ohm rung; R4 2 ohm on top; R5 and R6 3 ohm rungs",d:[["l",30,50,80,50],["l",30,50,30,140],["l",30,140,356,140],["p","M80,50 L80,69.8 L75.5,78.2 L84.5,86.6 L75.5,95 L84.5,103.4 L75.5,111.8 L80,120.2 L80,140"],["c",80,50,2.4,"F"],["c",80,140,2.4,"F"],["p","M80,50 L94,50 L101,54.5 L108,45.5 L115,54.5 L122,45.5 L129,54.5 L136,50 L150,50"],["c",150,50,2.4,"F"],["p","M150,50 L150,69.8 L145.5,78.2 L154.5,86.6 L145.5,95 L154.5,103.4 L145.5,111.8 L150,120.2 L150,140"],["c",150,140,2.4,"F"],["p","M150,50 L172,50 L181.3,54.5 L190.7,45.5 L200,54.5 L209.3,45.5 L218.7,54.5 L228,50 L250,50"],["l",250,50,357,50],["c",260,50,2.4,"F"],["p","M260,50 L260,69.8 L255.5,78.2 L264.5,86.6 L255.5,95 L264.5,103.4 L255.5,111.8 L260,120.2 L260,140"],["c",260,140,2.4,"F"],["c",320,50,2.4,"F"],["p","M320,50 L320,69.8 L315.5,78.2 L324.5,86.6 L315.5,95 L324.5,103.4 L315.5,111.8 L320,120.2 L320,140"],["c",320,140,2.4,"F"],["c",360,50,3.5],["c",360,140,3.5],["t",370,54,"A"],["t",370,144,"B"],["t",115,38,"2 \u03A9","m"],["t",115,67,"R\u2082","m"],["t",72,90,"R\u2081","e"],["t",88,96,"6 \u03A9"],["t",142,118,"R\u2083","e"],["t",158,96,"2 \u03A9"],["t",200,38,"2 \u03A9","m"],["t",200,67,"R\u2084","m"],["t",252,86,"R\u2085","e"],["t",252,104,"3 \u03A9","e"],["t",312,86,"R\u2086","e"],["t",312,104,"3 \u03A9","e"]]},n:1,s:`Look at the left end: the wire along the left side joins the top of R\u2081 to the bottom line. So R\u2081 (6 \u03A9) has both ends at the same potential and is shorted; no current goes through it.
Then the left end of R\u2082 is on the bottom line, and R\u2083 also connects the right end of R\u2082 to the bottom line. R\u2082 and R\u2083 are in parallel: (2 \xD7 2)/4 = 1 \u03A9.
This 1 \u03A9 is in series with R\u2084 (2 \u03A9): 3 \u03A9.
R\u2085 and R\u2086 are in parallel: (3 \xD7 3)/6 = 1.5 \u03A9.
The 3 \u03A9 path and the 1.5 \u03A9 pair are both across A and B (see the figure below): R = (3 \xD7 1.5)/(3 + 1.5) = 4.5/4.5 = 1 \u03A9.
So the equivalent resistance is 1 \u03A9.`,sfig:{w:240,h:155,alt:"Reduced circuit: 3 ohm in parallel with 1.5 ohm between A and B",d:[["c",30,45,3.5],["c",30,115,3.5],["t",22,49,"A","e"],["t",22,119,"B","e"],["l",33.5,45,160,45],["l",33.5,115,160,115],["c",80,45,2.4,"F"],["c",160,45,2.4,"F"],["c",80,115,2.4,"F"],["c",160,115,2.4,"F"],["p","M80,45 L80,60.4 L75.5,66.9 L84.5,73.5 L75.5,80 L84.5,86.5 L75.5,93.1 L80,99.6 L80,115"],["p","M160,45 L160,60.4 L155.5,66.9 L164.5,73.5 L155.5,80 L164.5,86.5 L155.5,93.1 L160,99.6 L160,115"],["t",88,84,"3 \u03A9"],["t",168,84,"1.5 \u03A9"],["t",80,137,"2 \u03A9 + 1 \u03A9","ms"],["t",160,137,"3 \u03A9 \u2225 3 \u03A9","ms"]]}},{pn:121,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cres",st:"Stretching and shape change of wires",q:"The length of a metallic wire is increased by 20% and its area of cross-section is reduced by 4%. The percentage change in resistance of the metallic wire is ____.",n:25,s:`Resistance of a wire is R = \u03C1l/A, so only l and A matter here.
New length = 1.20 l (20% more). New area = 0.96 A (4% less).
R\u2032 = \u03C1(1.20 l)/(0.96 A) = (1.20/0.96)R = 1.25 R.
The resistance rises to 1.25 times, a change of 25%.`},{pn:122,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cdrf",st:"Drift velocity and mobility",q:"A current of 2 A flows through a wire of cross-sectional area 25.0 mm\xB2. The number of free electrons in a cubic metre are 2.0 \xD7 10\xB2\u2078. The drift velocity of the electrons is ____ \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m s\u207B\xB9. (given, charge on electron = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 C)",n:25,s:`Current in a conductor is I = nAev_d, so v_d = I/(nAe).
Convert the area: A = 25.0 mm\xB2 = 25 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB2.
nAe = 2.0 \xD7 10\xB2\u2078 \xD7 25 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 = 8 \xD7 10\u2074.
v_d = 2/(8 \xD7 10\u2074) = 2.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 m s\u207B\xB9.
In units of 10\u207B\u2076 m s\u207B\xB9 this is 25.`},{pn:123,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cdrf",st:"Drift velocity and mobility",q:"The number density of free electrons in copper is nearly 8 \xD7 10\xB2\u2078 m\u207B\xB3. A copper wire has its area of cross section = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB2 and is carrying a current of 3.2 A. The drift speed of the electrons is ____ \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m s\u207B\xB9.",n:125,s:`Use I = nAev_d, so v_d = I/(nAe).
nAe = 8 \xD7 10\xB2\u2078 \xD7 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 = 2.56 \xD7 10\u2074.
v_d = 3.2/(2.56 \xD7 10\u2074) = 1.25 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 m s\u207B\xB9.
In units of 10\u207B\u2076 m s\u207B\xB9 this is 125.`},{pn:124,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ccmb",st:"Series and parallel combinations",q:"10 resistors each of resistance 10 \u03A9 can be connected in such a way as to get maximum and minimum equivalent resistance. The ratio of maximum and minimum equivalent resistance will be ____.",n:100,s:`Series connection gives the largest resistance: add them all.
R_max = 10 \xD7 10 = 100 \u03A9.
Parallel connection gives the smallest: 1/R = 10 \xD7 (1/10), so R_min = 10/10 = 1 \u03A9.
Ratio = 100/1 = 100.`},{pn:125,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cres",st:"Resistance and resistivity",q:"A rectangular parallelopiped is measured as 1 cm \xD7 1 cm \xD7 100 cm. If its specific resistance is 3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \u03A9 m, then the resistance between its two opposite rectangular faces will be ____ \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \u03A9.",n:3,s:`R = \u03C1l/A, where l is the distance between the two faces and A is the area of a face.
The two opposite rectangular faces are the large ones, 1 cm \xD7 100 cm each, so A = 0.01 m \xD7 1 m = 10\u207B\xB2 m\xB2.
The distance between them is the remaining side, l = 1 cm = 10\u207B\xB2 m.
R = 3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2/10\u207B\xB2 = 3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \u03A9.`},{pn:126,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cres",st:"Temperature dependence",q:"The current flowing through a conductor connected across a source is 2 A and 1.2 A at 0\xB0C and 100\xB0C respectively. The current flowing through the conductor at 50\xB0C will be ____ \xD7 10\xB2 mA.",n:15,s:`The source voltage V is the same, so the resistance is R = V/I at each temperature.
R\u2080 = V/2 at 0\xB0C and R\u2081\u2080\u2080 = V/1.2 at 100\xB0C.
Resistance of a conductor rises linearly with temperature, so at 50\xB0C (halfway) R\u2085\u2080 is the average of the two: R\u2085\u2080 = (V/2 + V/1.2)/2 = V(0.5 + 0.8333)/2 = 0.6667 V.
I\u2085\u2080 = V/R\u2085\u2080 = 1/0.6667 = 1.5 A = 1500 mA.
In units of 10\xB2 mA this is 15.`},{pn:127,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cres",st:"Stretching and shape change of wires",q:"A hollow cylindrical conductor has length of 3.14 m, while its inner and outer diameters are 4 mm and 8 mm respectively. The resistance of the conductor is n \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \u03A9. If the resistivity of the material is 2.4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 \u03A9 m. The value of n is ____.",n:2,s:`Current flows along the length, so R = \u03C1l/A with A the area of the ring (annulus).
Radii: inner 2 mm, outer 4 mm. A = \u03C0(4\xB2 \u2212 2\xB2) mm\xB2 = 12\u03C0 mm\xB2 = 12\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB2.
With \u03C0 = 3.14, A = 3.768 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 m\xB2.
R = 2.4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 \xD7 3.14/(3.14 \xD7 12 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076) = 2.4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078/(1.2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075) = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \u03A9.
So n = 2.`},{pn:128,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cres",st:"Stretching and shape change of wires",q:"If a copper wire is stretched to increase its length by 20%. The percentage increase in resistance of the wire is ____ %.",n:44,s:`Stretching keeps the volume constant, so area falls as length rises and R = \u03C1l\xB2/V is proportional to l\xB2.
New length = 1.2 l, so R\u2032 = (1.2)\xB2 R = 1.44 R.
The resistance rises by 0.44 of its value, which is 44%.
Note: the shortcut 2 \xD7 20% = 40% is only valid for tiny changes; here use the exact square.`},{pn:129,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 25 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ccmb",st:"Balanced networks and symmetry",q:"In the given circuit, the equivalent resistance between the terminal A and B is ____ \u03A9.",n:10,s:`First look for wires that join two points: the plain vertical wire on the right joins the top node and the bottom node of the right part.
So the 2 \u03A9 (right vertical) is shorted and the 4 \u03A9 + 6 \u03A9 loop starts and ends at the same node, so no current flows in it (solution figure).
The top 2 \u03A9 resistor now connects the first node to the bottom wire, so it is in parallel with the left vertical 2 \u03A9.
2 \u03A9 \u2225 2 \u03A9 = 1 \u03A9.
Remaining chain in series: 3 \u03A9 + 1 \u03A9 + 6 \u03A9.
R_eq = 10 \u03A9.`,fig:{w:340,h:170,alt:"Circuit between A and B: A, 3 ohm, a node with a vertical 2 ohm to the bottom wire, 2 ohm, a node with a vertical 2 ohm to the bottom wire, a plain vertical wire joining top and bottom, a 4 ohm and 6 ohm loop on the right, and 6 ohm to B",d:[["c",20,45,2.6,"F"],["c",20,125,2.6,"F"],["t",14,40,"A","e"],["t",14,129,"B","e"],["p","M20 45 L42 45 L45 40 L51 50 L57 40 L63 50 L69 40 L75 50 L78 45 L100 45"],["t",60,35,"3 \u03A9","m"],["p","M100 45 L127 45 L130 40 L136 50 L142 40 L148 50 L154 40 L160 50 L163 45 L190 45"],["t",145,35,"2 \u03A9","m"],["l",190,45,205,45],["p","M205 45 L227 45 L230 40 L236 50 L242 40 L248 50 L254 40 L260 50 L263 45 L285 45"],["t",245,35,"4 \u03A9","m"],["p","M100 45 L100 67 L105 70 L95 76 L105 82 L95 88 L105 94 L95 100 L100 103 L100 125"],["t",90,89,"2 \u03A9","e"],["p","M190 45 L190 67 L195 70 L185 76 L195 82 L185 88 L195 94 L185 100 L190 103 L190 125"],["t",180,89,"2 \u03A9","e"],["l",285,45,285,125],["l",205,45,205,125],["c",205,45,2.6,"F"],["c",205,125,2.6,"F"],["p","M20 125 L42 125 L45 120 L51 130 L57 120 L63 130 L69 120 L75 130 L78 125 L100 125"],["t",60,146,"6 \u03A9","m"],["l",100,125,205,125],["p","M205 125 L227 125 L230 120 L236 130 L242 120 L248 130 L254 120 L260 130 L263 125 L285 125"],["t",245,146,"6 \u03A9","m"]]},sfig:{w:340,h:150,alt:"Equivalent circuit: 3 ohm in series with two 2 ohm resistors in parallel and then 6 ohm; the 4 ohm and 6 ohm loop carries no current",d:[["c",20,80,2.6,"F"],["c",320,80,2.6,"F"],["t",20,100,"A","m"],["t",320,100,"B","m"],["p","M20 80 L47 80 L50 75 L56 85 L62 75 L68 85 L74 75 L80 85 L83 80 L110 80"],["t",65,70,"3 \u03A9","m"],["l",110,80,110,50],["l",110,50,150,50],["p","M110 50 L132 50 L135 45 L141 55 L147 45 L153 55 L159 45 L165 55 L168 50 L190 50"],["t",150,40,"2 \u03A9","m"],["l",110,80,110,110],["p","M110 110 L132 110 L135 105 L141 115 L147 105 L153 115 L159 105 L165 115 L168 110 L190 110"],["t",150,130,"2 \u03A9","m"],["l",190,50,190,110],["l",190,80,230,80],["c",110,80,2.6,"F"],["c",190,80,2.6,"F"],["p","M190 80 L235 80 L238.3 75 L245 85 L251.7 75 L258.3 85 L265 75 L271.7 85 L275 80 L320 80"],["t",255,70,"6 \u03A9","m"]]}},{pn:130,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cres",st:"Stretching and shape change of wires",q:"Eight copper wires of length l and diameter d are joined in parallel to form a single composite conductor of resistance R. If a single copper wire of length 2l has the same resistance (R) then its diameter will be ____ d.",n:4,s:`Resistance is R = \u03C1l/A with A = \u03C0d\xB2/4.
Eight identical wires in parallel behave like one wire of length l and 8 times the area: R = \u03C1l/(8\u03C0d\xB2/4) = \u03C1l/(2\u03C0d\xB2).
Single wire of length 2l and diameter D: R = \u03C1(2l)/(\u03C0D\xB2/4) = 8\u03C1l/(\u03C0D\xB2).
Equate: 1/(2d\xB2) = 8/D\xB2, so D\xB2 = 16d\xB2.
D = 4d, so the answer is 4.`},{pn:131,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cres",st:"Ohm's law and V-I graphs",q:"A 1 m long copper wire carries a current of 1 A. If the cross section of the wire is 2.0 mm\xB2 and the resistivity of copper is 1.7 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 \u03A9 m, the force experienced by a moving electron in the wire is ____ \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB3 N. (charge on electron = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 C)",n:136,s:`The electric field in the wire drives each electron: F = eE.
Field from the point form of Ohm's law: E = \u03C1J = \u03C1I/A.
A = 2.0 mm\xB2 = 2.0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB2, so E = 1.7 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 \xD7 1/(2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076) = 8.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 V/m.
F = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 \xD7 8.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 = 1.36 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB9 N.
In units of 10\u207B\xB2\xB3 N this is 136.`},{pn:132,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ccmb",st:"Balanced networks and symmetry",q:"The current I flowing through the given circuit will be ____ A.",n:2,s:`Name the nodes: a (left end of the first 9 \u03A9), b (between the first and second), c (between the second and third), d (right end of the third).
The wire over the top joins a and c, so a and c are the same point. The lower wire joins b and d, so b and d are the same point.
First 9 \u03A9 is between a and b; second between b and c = a; third between c = a and d = b.
So all three 9 \u03A9 resistors are between the same two points a and b: they are in parallel (solution figure).
R_eq = 9/3 = 3 \u03A9.
I = 6/3 = 2 A.`,fig:{w:340,h:185,alt:"Three 9 ohm resistors in a row; a wire joins the left end of the first to the right end of the second, another wire joins the right end of the first to the right end of the third; a 6 V battery completes the circuit",d:[["l",40,75,60,75],["p","M60 75 L80 75 L83.3 70 L90 80 L96.7 70 L103.3 80 L110 70 L116.7 80 L120 75 L140 75"],["t",100,65,"9 \u03A9","m"],["p","M140 75 L160 75 L163.3 70 L170 80 L176.7 70 L183.3 80 L190 70 L196.7 80 L200 75 L220 75"],["t",180,65,"9 \u03A9","m"],["p","M220 75 L237 75 L240 70 L246 80 L252 70 L258 80 L264 70 L270 80 L273 75 L290 75"],["t",255,65,"9 \u03A9","m"],["l",60,75,60,40],["l",60,40,220,40],["l",220,40,220,75],["l",140,75,140,105],["l",140,105,280,105],["l",280,105,280,75],["c",60,75,2.6,"F"],["c",140,75,2.6,"F"],["c",220,75,2.6,"F"],["l",290,75,315,75],["l",315,75,315,155],["l",40,75,40,155],["t",22,118,"I","e"],["l",30,125,30,108,"a"],["l",40,155,172,155],["l",172,144,172,166],["l",178,149,178,161,"B"],["l",178,155,315,155],["t",175,178,"6 V","m"]]},sfig:{w:340,h:170,alt:"The three 9 ohm resistors are all between the same two nodes, so they are in parallel",d:[["l",60,25,60,125],["l",260,25,260,125],["p","M60 25 L135 25 L139.2 20 L147.5 30 L155.8 20 L164.2 30 L172.5 20 L180.8 30 L185 25 L260 25"],["t",160,15,"9 \u03A9","m"],["p","M60 75 L135 75 L139.2 70 L147.5 80 L155.8 70 L164.2 80 L172.5 70 L180.8 80 L185 75 L260 75"],["t",160,65,"9 \u03A9","m"],["p","M60 125 L135 125 L139.2 120 L147.5 130 L155.8 120 L164.2 130 L172.5 120 L180.8 130 L185 125 L260 125"],["t",160,115,"9 \u03A9","m"],["c",60,75,2.6,"F"],["c",260,75,2.6,"F"],["l",30,75,60,75],["l",260,75,290,75],["t",30,92,"a = c","m"],["t",290,92,"b = d","m"]]}},{pn:133,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ccmb",st:"Balanced networks and symmetry",q:"The total current supplied to the circuit by the 5 V battery is ____ A.",n:2,s:`The top arc of the circle is a plain wire, so the two top nodes (left and right) are at the same potential: call them one node L.
The two 5 \u03A9 resistors of the star both run from L to the centre, so they are in parallel: 2.5 \u03A9.
This is in series with the 2.5 \u03A9 to the bottom node: 2.5 + 2.5 = 5 \u03A9 between L and the bottom (solution figure).
The 5 \u03A9 on the right arc also joins L to the bottom, so it is in parallel with this 5 \u03A9: 2.5 \u03A9.
The battery is across this 2.5 \u03A9, so I = 5/2.5 = 2 A.`,fig:{w:340,h:210,alt:"Circle circuit: 5 V battery on the left arc; the top arc is a plain wire from the left node to the right node; a star of 5 ohm, 5 ohm and 2.5 ohm joins the two top nodes and the bottom node; a 5 ohm resistor sits on the right arc",d:[["p","M120.5,50.5 A70 70 0 0 1 219.5,50.5"],["p","M219.5,50.5 A70 70 0 0 1 235.8,76.1"],["p","M235.8 76.1 L233.8 91.3 L238.4 94.8 L227.7 99.1 L236.9 106 L226.2 110.3 L235.4 117.2 L224.7 121.5 L229.3 125 L227.3 140.2"],["p","M227.3,140.2 A70 70 0 0 1 170,170"],["p","M120.5,50.5 A70 70 0 0 0 101.1,87.8"],["p","M101.1,112.2 A70 70 0 0 0 170,170"],["l",100,87.8,100,97],["l",89,97,111,97],["l",94,103,106,103,"B"],["l",100,103,100,112.2],["p","M120.5 50.5 L129.9 58.4 L135.7 56.7 L134.4 68.7 L145.9 65.3 L144.6 77.2 L156.1 73.8 L154.8 85.8 L160.6 84.1 L170 92"],["p","M219.5 50.5 L210.1 58.4 L210.7 64.4 L199.2 61 L200.5 72.9 L189 69.6 L190.3 81.5 L178.8 78.1 L179.4 84.1 L170 92"],["p","M170 92 L170 106 L175 110.2 L165 118.5 L175 126.8 L165 135.2 L175 143.5 L165 151.8 L170 156 L170 170"],["c",170,92,2.6,"F"],["t",62,104,"5 V","e"],["t",132,80,"5 \u03A9","e"],["t",210,80,"5 \u03A9",""],["c",120.5,50.5,2.6,"F"],["c",219.5,50.5,2.6,"F"],["c",170,170,2.6,"F"],["t",243,112,"5 \u03A9",""],["t",162,150,"2.5 \u03A9","e"]]},sfig:{w:340,h:170,alt:"Equivalent: the two 5 ohm resistors from the joined top nodes are in parallel (2.5 ohm) and in series with 2.5 ohm, giving 5 ohm, which is in parallel with the 5 ohm on the right arc",d:[["l",50,60,70,60],["l",50,60,50,110],["l",50,110,70,110],["p","M70 60 L95 60 L98.3 55 L105 65 L111.7 55 L118.3 65 L125 55 L131.7 65 L135 60 L160 60"],["t",115,50,"2.5 \u03A9","m"],["p","M160 60 L185 60 L188.3 55 L195 65 L201.7 55 L208.3 65 L215 55 L221.7 65 L225 60 L250 60"],["t",205,50,"2.5 \u03A9","m"],["l",250,60,290,60],["l",290,60,290,110],["p","M70 110 L155 110 L159.2 105 L167.5 115 L175.8 105 L184.2 115 L192.5 105 L200.8 115 L205 110 L290 110"],["t",180,130,"5 \u03A9","m"],["c",50,60,2.6,"F"],["c",290,60,2.6,"F"],["c",50,110,2.6,"F"],["c",290,110,2.6,"F"]]}},{pn:134,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cres",st:"Stretching and shape change of wires",q:"The length of a given cylindrical wire is increased to double of its original length. The percentage increase in the resistance of the wire will be ____ %.",n:300,s:`Stretching keeps the volume the same, so R = \u03C1l\xB2/V is proportional to l\xB2.
New length = 2l, so R\u2032 = 2\xB2 R = 4R.
The increase is 4R \u2212 R = 3R, which is 300% of R.`},{pn:135,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cdrf",st:"Current density",q:"The current density in a cylindrical wire of radius 4 mm is 4 \xD7 10\u2076 A m\u207B\xB2. The current through the outer portion of the wire between radial distances R/2 and R is ____ \u03C0 A.",n:48,s:`The current density is uniform, so current = J \xD7 area of the region.
The outer portion is a ring between radii R/2 and R: area = \u03C0(R\xB2 \u2212 R\xB2/4) = (3/4)\u03C0R\xB2.
R = 4 mm = 4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m, so R\xB2 = 16 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB2.
Area = (3/4)\u03C0 \xD7 16 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 = 12\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB2.
I = 4 \xD7 10\u2076 \xD7 12\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 = 48\u03C0 A.
So the blank is 48.`},{pn:136,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cdrf",st:"Current density",q:"The current density in a cylindrical wire of radius r = 4.0 mm is 1.0 \xD7 10\u2076 A/m\xB2. The current through the outer portion of the wire between radial distance r/2 and r is x \u03C0 A; where x is ____.",n:12,s:`Uniform current density: current = J \xD7 area.
The outer portion is a ring between r/2 and r: area = \u03C0(r\xB2 \u2212 r\xB2/4) = (3/4)\u03C0r\xB2.
r = 4.0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m, so r\xB2 = 16 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB2 and area = 12\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB2.
I = 1.0 \xD7 10\u2076 \xD7 12\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 = 12\u03C0 A.
So x = 12.`},{pn:137,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ccmb",st:"Series and parallel combinations",q:"In the given circuit 'a' is an arbitrary constant. The value of m for which the equivalent circuit resistance is minimum, will be \u221A(x/2). The value of x is ____.",n:3,s:`Three resistors of ma in parallel: ma/3. Two resistors of a/m in parallel: a/(2m).
The two groups are in series: R(m) = ma/3 + a/(2m).
R is minimum where dR/dm = 0: a/3 \u2212 a/(2m\xB2) = 0.
So m\xB2 = 3/2, hence m = \u221A(3/2).
Compare with \u221A(x/2): x = 3.
Note: the second derivative a/m\xB3 is positive, so this is a minimum.`,fig:{w:340,h:170,alt:"Circuit with a battery V: three resistors of ma in parallel, in series with two resistors of a/m in parallel",d:[["l",30,60,70,60],["l",70,30,70,90],["l",150,30,150,90],["p","M70 30 L90 30 L93.3 25 L100 35 L106.7 25 L113.3 35 L120 25 L126.7 35 L130 30 L150 30"],["t",110,20,"ma","m"],["p","M70 60 L90 60 L93.3 55 L100 65 L106.7 55 L113.3 65 L120 55 L126.7 65 L130 60 L150 60"],["t",110,51,"ma","m"],["p","M70 90 L90 90 L93.3 85 L100 95 L106.7 85 L113.3 95 L120 85 L126.7 95 L130 90 L150 90"],["t",110,108,"ma","m"],["l",150,60,200,60],["l",200,40,200,80],["l",280,40,280,80],["p","M200 40 L220 40 L223.3 35 L230 45 L236.7 35 L243.3 45 L250 35 L256.7 45 L260 40 L280 40"],["t",240,30,"a/m","m"],["p","M200 80 L220 80 L223.3 75 L230 85 L236.7 75 L243.3 85 L250 75 L256.7 85 L260 80 L280 80"],["t",240,98,"a/m","m"],["l",280,60,310,60],["c",70,60,2.6,"F"],["c",150,60,2.6,"F"],["c",200,60,2.6,"F"],["c",280,60,2.6,"F"],["l",30,60,30,135],["l",310,60,310,135],["l",30,135,167,135],["l",167,124,167,146],["l",173,129,173,141,"B"],["l",173,135,310,135],["t",170,158,"V","m"]]}},{pn:138,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ccmb",st:"Infinite and cube networks",q:"All resistances in the figure are 1 \u03A9 each. The value of current 'I' is a/5 A. The value of a is ____.",n:8,s:`Work from the far end (the common node) back to the battery.
The last 8 resistors are in pairs: each pair of 1 \u03A9 in parallel is 0.5 \u03A9, and each pair feeds from a 1 \u03A9 resistor: 1 + 0.5 = 1.5 \u03A9.
Two such 1.5 \u03A9 branches are in parallel: 0.75 \u03A9; add the next 1 \u03A9 in series: 1.75 \u03A9.
Two such 1.75 \u03A9 branches are in parallel: 0.875 \u03A9; add the first 1 \u03A9 next to the battery: 1.875 \u03A9 = 15/8 \u03A9.
I = 3/(15/8) = 8/5 A = a/5 A.
So a = 8.`,fig:{w:340,h:232,alt:"Binary tree of 1 ohm resistors: one resistor from the 3 V battery, then 2 branches, then 4, then 8 resistors, all ending at one common node that returns to the battery",d:[["p","M50 102 L60 102 L62.5 97 L67.5 107 L72.5 97 L77.5 107 L82.5 97 L87.5 107 L90 102 L100 102"],["l",100,102,100,58],["p","M100 58 L110 58 L112.5 53 L117.5 63 L122.5 53 L127.5 63 L132.5 53 L137.5 63 L140 58 L150 58"],["l",100,102,100,146],["p","M100 146 L110 146 L112.5 141 L117.5 151 L122.5 141 L127.5 151 L132.5 141 L137.5 151 L140 146 L150 146"],["l",100,58,100,146],["l",150,58,150,36],["p","M150 36 L160 36 L162.5 31 L167.5 41 L172.5 31 L177.5 41 L182.5 31 L187.5 41 L190 36 L200 36"],["l",150,58,150,80],["p","M150 80 L160 80 L162.5 75 L167.5 85 L172.5 75 L177.5 85 L182.5 75 L187.5 85 L190 80 L200 80"],["l",150,146,150,124],["p","M150 124 L160 124 L162.5 119 L167.5 129 L172.5 119 L177.5 129 L182.5 119 L187.5 129 L190 124 L200 124"],["l",150,146,150,168],["p","M150 168 L160 168 L162.5 163 L167.5 173 L172.5 163 L177.5 173 L182.5 163 L187.5 173 L190 168 L200 168"],["l",150,36,150,80],["l",150,124,150,168],["l",200,36,200,25],["p","M200 25 L212.5 25 L215 20 L220 30 L225 20 L230 30 L235 20 L240 30 L242.5 25 L255 25"],["l",255,25,310,102],["l",200,36,200,47],["p","M200 47 L212.5 47 L215 42 L220 52 L225 42 L230 52 L235 42 L240 52 L242.5 47 L255 47"],["l",255,47,310,102],["l",200,80,200,69],["p","M200 69 L212.5 69 L215 64 L220 74 L225 64 L230 74 L235 64 L240 74 L242.5 69 L255 69"],["l",255,69,310,102],["l",200,80,200,91],["p","M200 91 L212.5 91 L215 86 L220 96 L225 86 L230 96 L235 86 L240 96 L242.5 91 L255 91"],["l",255,91,310,102],["l",200,124,200,113],["p","M200 113 L212.5 113 L215 108 L220 118 L225 108 L230 118 L235 108 L240 118 L242.5 113 L255 113"],["l",255,113,310,102],["l",200,124,200,135],["p","M200 135 L212.5 135 L215 130 L220 140 L225 130 L230 140 L235 130 L240 140 L242.5 135 L255 135"],["l",255,135,310,102],["l",200,168,200,157],["p","M200 157 L212.5 157 L215 152 L220 162 L225 152 L230 162 L235 152 L240 162 L242.5 157 L255 157"],["l",255,157,310,102],["l",200,168,200,179],["p","M200 179 L212.5 179 L215 174 L220 184 L225 174 L230 184 L235 174 L240 184 L242.5 179 L255 179"],["l",255,179,310,102],["l",200,36,200,47],["l",310,102,310,215],["l",50,215,310,215],["l",50,102,50,163],["l",40,163,60,163],["l",44,172,56,172,"B"],["l",50,172,50,215],["t",40,168,"3 V","e"],["l",66,185,66,150,"a"],["t",72,170,"I",""]]}},{pn:139,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cres",st:"Ohm's law and V-I graphs",q:"The variation of applied potential and current flowing through a given wire is shown in figure. The length of wire is 31.4 cm. The diameter of wire is measured as 2.4 cm. The resistivity of the given wire is measured as x \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \u03A9 cm. The value of x is ____. [Take \u03C0 = 3.14]",n:144,s:`The V-I graph is a straight line at 45\xB0 to the current axis, so its slope V/I = tan 45\xB0 = 1: the resistance is R = 1 \u03A9.
Resistivity from R = \u03C1l/A, so \u03C1 = RA/l.
Area A = \u03C0(d/2)\xB2 = 3.14 \xD7 (1.2)\xB2 = 3.14 \xD7 1.44 = 4.5216 cm\xB2.
\u03C1 = 1 \xD7 4.5216/31.4 = 0.144 \u03A9 cm.
0.144 = 144 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \u03A9 cm, so x = 144.`,fig:{w:260,h:195,alt:"Graph of potential in volt against current in ampere: a straight line through the origin at 45 degrees to the current axis",d:[["l",50,160,235,160,"a"],["l",50,160,50,22,"a"],["t",58,24,"Potential (volt)",""],["t",235,178,"I (A)","e"],["t",44,174,"O","e"],["l",50,160,195,15],["p","M95 160 A45 45 0 0 0 82 128"],["t",100,150,"45\xB0",""]]}},{pn:140,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cpot",st:"Galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"Two resistors 500 \u03A9 and 600 \u03A9 are connected in series across a 20 V battery as shown in figure. If a voltmeter of resistance 2000 \u03A9 is used to measure the potential difference across the 500 \u03A9 resistor, the reading of the voltmeter will be ____ V.",n:8,s:`The voltmeter is connected across the 500 \u03A9 resistor, so it is in parallel with it, and it changes the resistance of that part.
500 \u03A9 \u2225 2000 \u03A9 = (500 \xD7 2000)/2500 = 400 \u03A9.
Total resistance = 400 + 600 = 1000 \u03A9, so the battery current is 20/1000 = 0.02 A.
The voltmeter reads the voltage across the 400 \u03A9 part: 0.02 \xD7 400 = 8 V.
Note: without the voltmeter the drop across 500 \u03A9 would be 9.09 V; the meter's finite resistance lowers the reading.`,fig:{w:340,h:180,alt:"Series circuit: a 20 V battery with a 500 ohm resistor and a 600 ohm resistor",d:[["p","M50 45 L88 45 L91.7 40 L99 50 L106.3 40 L113.7 50 L121 40 L128.3 50 L132 45 L170 45"],["t",110,35,"500 \u03A9","m"],["p","M170 45 L208 45 L211.7 40 L219 50 L226.3 40 L233.7 50 L241 40 L248.3 50 L252 45 L290 45"],["t",230,35,"600 \u03A9","m"],["l",50,45,50,135],["l",290,45,290,135],["l",50,135,167,135],["l",167,124,167,146],["l",173,129,173,141,"B"],["l",173,135,290,135],["t",170,160,"20 V","m"]]}},{pn:141,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Aug \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ccmb",st:"Series and parallel combinations",q:"The ratio of the equivalent resistance of the network (shown in figure) between the points a and b when switch is open and switch is closed is x : 8. The value of x is ____.",n:9,s:`Switch open: the upper arm is R + 2R = 3R and the lower arm is 2R + R = 3R, and they are in parallel.
R_open = (3R \xD7 3R)/(6R) = 1.5R.
Switch closed: it joins the middle node of the upper arm to the middle node of the lower arm.
Now the left part is R \u2225 2R = 2R/3 and the right part is 2R \u2225 R = 2R/3, in series.
The equivalent circuit is drawn in the solution figure.
R_closed = 4R/3.
Ratio = 1.5R/(4R/3) = 9/8, so x = 9.`,fig:{w:340,h:170,alt:"Network between a and b: upper arm R then 2R, lower arm 2R then R, and an open switch joining the middle nodes of the two arms",d:[["c",20,92,2.6,"F"],["c",320,92,2.6,"F"],["t",20,112,"a","m"],["t",320,112,"b","m"],["l",20,92,60,92],["l",60,45,60,130],["p","M60 45 L90 45 L93.3 40 L100 50 L106.7 40 L113.3 50 L120 40 L126.7 50 L130 45 L160 45"],["t",110,35,"R","m"],["p","M160 45 L195 45 L198.3 40 L205 50 L211.7 40 L218.3 50 L225 40 L231.7 50 L235 45 L270 45"],["t",215,35,"2R","m"],["p","M60 130 L90 130 L93.3 125 L100 135 L106.7 125 L113.3 135 L120 125 L126.7 135 L130 130 L160 130"],["t",110,150,"2R","m"],["p","M160 130 L195 130 L198.3 125 L205 135 L211.7 125 L218.3 135 L225 125 L231.7 135 L235 130 L270 130"],["t",215,150,"R","m"],["l",270,45,270,130],["l",270,92,320,92],["l",160,45,160,68],["l",160,68,175,88],["c",178,92,2.6,"F"],["c",160,100,2.6,"F"],["l",160,100,160,130]]},sfig:{w:340,h:110,alt:"Switch closed: the left block R in parallel with 2R (2R/3) in series with the right block 2R in parallel with R (2R/3), between a and b",d:[["c",30,50,2.6,"F"],["c",310,50,2.6,"F"],["t",30,72,"a","m"],["t",310,72,"b","m"],["l",30,50,70,50],["p","M70 50 L82.0 50 L86.7 45 L96.0 55 L105.3 45 L114.7 55 L124.0 45 L133.3 55 L138.0 50 L150 50"],["l",150,50,170,50],["t",110,36,"2R/3","m"],["c",170,50,2.6,"F"],["p","M170 50 L182.0 50 L186.7 45 L196.0 55 L205.3 45 L214.7 55 L224.0 45 L233.3 55 L238.0 50 L250 50"],["t",210,36,"2R/3","m"],["l",250,50,310,50],["t",170,84,"R\u2032 = 2R/3 + 2R/3 = 4R/3","m"]]}},{pn:142,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 31 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ccmb",st:"Balanced networks and symmetry",q:"A square shaped wire with resistance of each side 3 \u03A9 is bent to form a complete circle. The resistance between two diametrically opposite points of the circle in unit of \u03A9 will be ____.",n:3,s:`The square has four sides of 3 \u03A9, so the wire has 12 \u03A9 in total; bending it into a circle does not change this.
Two diametrically opposite points split the circle into two equal halves, each of 6 \u03A9.
The two halves are in parallel between the points.
R = (6 \xD7 6)/(6 + 6) = 3 \u03A9.`},{pn:143,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 20 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ccmb",st:"Balanced networks and symmetry",q:"In the given figure switches S\u2081 and S\u2082 are in open condition. The resistance across ab when the switches S\u2081 and S\u2082 are closed is ____ \u03A9.",n:10,s:`A closed switch joins the top node and the bottom node at its place, making them one point.
Left part: top 12 \u03A9 and bottom 6 \u03A9 both run from a to the S\u2081 node: 12 \u2225 6 = 4 \u03A9.
Middle part: the two 4 \u03A9 resistors run between the S\u2081 node and the S\u2082 node: 4 \u2225 4 = 2 \u03A9.
Right part: 6 \u03A9 and 12 \u03A9 both run from the S\u2082 node to b: 6 \u2225 12 = 4 \u03A9.
These three parts are in series (solution figure): R = 4 + 2 + 4 = 10 \u03A9.`,fig:{w:380,h:165,alt:"Network between a and b: top arm 12, 4, 6 ohm and bottom arm 6, 4, 12 ohm, with switches S1 and S2 joining the middle nodes of the two arms",d:[["c",20,88,2.6,"F"],["c",360,88,2.6,"F"],["t",20,108,"a","m"],["t",360,108,"b","m"],["l",20,88,60,88],["l",60,45,60,130],["p","M60 45 L85 45 L88.3 40 L95 50 L101.7 40 L108.3 50 L115 40 L121.7 50 L125 45 L150 45"],["t",105,35,"12 \u03A9","m"],["p","M150 45 L175 45 L178.3 40 L185 50 L191.7 40 L198.3 50 L205 40 L211.7 50 L215 45 L240 45"],["t",195,35,"4 \u03A9","m"],["p","M240 45 L262 45 L265 40 L271 50 L277 40 L283 50 L289 40 L295 50 L298 45 L320 45"],["t",280,35,"6 \u03A9","m"],["p","M60 130 L85 130 L88.3 125 L95 135 L101.7 125 L108.3 135 L115 125 L121.7 135 L125 130 L150 130"],["t",105,150,"6 \u03A9","m"],["p","M150 130 L175 130 L178.3 125 L185 135 L191.7 125 L198.3 135 L205 125 L211.7 135 L215 130 L240 130"],["t",195,150,"4 \u03A9","m"],["p","M240 130 L262 130 L265 125 L271 135 L277 125 L283 135 L289 125 L295 135 L298 130 L320 130"],["t",280,150,"12 \u03A9","m"],["l",320,45,320,130],["l",320,88,360,88],["l",150,45,150,78],["c",150,80,2.6,"F"],["c",164,84,2.6,"F"],["l",150,130,150,108],["l",150,108,164,88],["t",176,88,"S\u2081",""],["l",240,45,240,78],["c",240,80,2.6,"F"],["c",254,84,2.6,"F"],["l",240,130,240,108],["l",240,108,254,88],["t",266,88,"S\u2082",""]]},sfig:{w:340,h:130,alt:"With both switches closed: 12 ohm in parallel with 6 ohm gives 4 ohm, two 4 ohm in parallel give 2 ohm, and again 4 ohm; total 10 ohm",d:[["c",20,60,2.6,"F"],["c",320,60,2.6,"F"],["t",20,80,"a","m"],["t",320,80,"b","m"],["p","M20 60 L45 60 L48.3 55 L55 65 L61.7 55 L68.3 65 L75 55 L81.7 65 L85 60 L110 60"],["t",65,50,"4 \u03A9","m"],["t",65,98,"12 \u2225 6","m"],["p","M110 60 L145 60 L148.3 55 L155 65 L161.7 55 L168.3 65 L175 55 L181.7 65 L185 60 L220 60"],["t",165,50,"2 \u03A9","m"],["t",165,98,"4 \u2225 4","m"],["p","M220 60 L250 60 L253.3 55 L260 65 L266.7 55 L273.3 65 L280 55 L286.7 65 L290 60 L320 60"],["t",270,50,"4 \u03A9","m"],["t",270,98,"6 \u2225 12","m"]]}},{pn:144,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ckir",st:"Kirchhoff's laws",q:"For the circuit shown, the value of current at time t = 3.2 s will be ____ A. [Voltage distribution V(t) is shown by figure 1 and the circuit is shown in figure 2.]",n:1,s:`Read V at t = 3.2 s from the graph. From 3 s to 4 s the voltage rises from 5 V to 10 V, a slope of 5 V/s.
V(3.2) = 5 + 5 \xD7 (3.2 \u2212 3) = 6 V.
The source V(t) and the 5 V battery act against each other in the loop with R = 1 \u03A9.
Net emf = 6 \u2212 5 = 1 V, so I = 1/1 = 1 A.
Note: the polarity of the 5 V battery is read as opposing V(t), which agrees with the key.`,fig:{w:470,h:190,alt:"Left: graph of V(t): rises from 0 to 5 V in 2 s, constant 5 V from 2 to 3 s, rises to 10 V at 4 s, then constant at 10 V. Right: circuit with source V(t), a 1 ohm resistor and a 5 V battery in a loop, current I",d:[["l",50,150,240,150,"a"],["l",50,150,50,18,"a"],["t",58,22,"V(t)",""],["t",240,170,"t (s)","e"],["t",44,164,"O","e"],["l",82,150,82,154],["t",82,166,"1","m"],["l",114,150,114,154],["t",114,166,"2","m"],["l",146,150,146,154],["t",146,166,"3","m"],["l",178,150,178,154],["t",178,166,"4","m"],["l",46,90,50,90],["t",42,94,"5","e"],["l",46,30,50,30],["t",42,34,"10","e"],["l",50,150,114,90],["l",114,90,146,90],["l",146,90,178,30],["l",178,30,225,30],["l",50,90,114,90,"d"],["l",50,30,178,30,"d"],["l",114,90,114,150,"d"],["l",146,90,146,150,"d"],["l",178,30,178,150,"d"],["p","M290 50 L328 50 L331.7 45 L339 55 L346.3 45 L353.7 55 L361 45 L368.3 55 L372 50 L410 50"],["t",350,40,"R = 1 \u03A9","m"],["l",290,50,290,80],["c",290,92,12],["t",290,96,"\xB1","ms"],["l",290,104,290,145],["l",290,145,410,145],["l",410,50,410,92],["l",401,95,419,95],["l",405,101,415,101,"B"],["l",410,101,410,145],["t",427,100,"5 V",""],["p","M340 105 A15 15 0 1 1 352 93","a"],["t",346,100,"I","m"]]}},{pn:145,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 17 Mar \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ccmb",st:"Series and parallel combinations",q:"The equivalent resistance of series combination of two resistors is s. When they are connected in parallel, the equivalent resistance is p. If s = np, then the minimum value for n is ______. (Round off to the nearest integer)",n:4,s:`Let the resistors be R\u2081 and R\u2082. Then s = R\u2081 + R\u2082 and p = R\u2081R\u2082/(R\u2081 + R\u2082).
So n = s/p = (R\u2081 + R\u2082)\xB2/(R\u2081R\u2082) = R\u2081/R\u2082 + R\u2082/R\u2081 + 2.
For positive numbers x + 1/x is at least 2, with equality when x = 1.
So n is at least 2 + 2 = 4, reached when R\u2081 = R\u2082.
The minimum value of n is 4.`},{pn:146,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 18 Mar \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ccmb",st:"Equivalent resistance of networks",q:"The voltage across the 10 \u03A9 resistor in the given circuit is x volt. The value of x to the nearest integer is ______.",n:70,s:`The 50 \u03A9 and 20 \u03A9 resistors are connected between the same two points, so they are in parallel.
Their equivalent is (50 \xD7 20)/(50 + 20) = 1000/70 = 100/7 \u03A9.
This is in series with the 10 \u03A9 resistor, so the total resistance is 10 + 100/7 = 170/7 \u03A9.
Current from the 170 V source: I = 170/(170/7) = 7 A.
The whole current passes through the 10 \u03A9 resistor, so its voltage is 7 \xD7 10 = 70 V.
So x = 70.`,fig:{w:300,h:170,alt:"A 170 volt cell in series with a 10 ohm resistor, then a 50 ohm and a 20 ohm resistor in parallel",d:[["l",30,40,63,40],["p","M63 40 L65.8 44 L71.5 36 L77.2 44 L82.8 36 L88.5 44 L94.2 36 L97 40"],["l",97,40,130,40],["c",130,40,2.2,"F"],["l",130,40,170,40],["p","M170 40 L173.3 44 L180 36 L186.7 44 L193.3 36 L200 44 L206.7 36 L210 40"],["l",210,40,250,40],["l",250,40,250,130],["l",130,40,130,75],["l",130,75,170,75],["p","M170 75 L173.3 79 L180 71 L186.7 79 L193.3 71 L200 79 L206.7 71 L210 75"],["l",210,75,250,75],["c",250,75,2.2,"F"],["l",30,40,30,130],["l",30,130,140,130],["l",140,118,140,142],["l",147,124,147,136,"B"],["l",147,130,250,130],["t",80,58,"10 \u03A9","m"],["t",190,28,"50 \u03A9","m"],["t",190,93,"20 \u03A9","m"],["t",143,152,"170 V","m"]]}},{pn:147,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 18 Mar \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cres",st:"Resistance and resistivity",q:"Two wires of same length and thickness having specific resistances 6 \u03A9 cm and 3 \u03A9 cm respectively are connected in parallel. The effective resistivity is \u03C1 \u03A9 cm. The value of \u03C1, to the nearest integer, is ______.",n:4,s:`Both wires have the same length l and area A, so their resistances are R\u2081 = 6l/A and R\u2082 = 3l/A.
In parallel: R = R\u2081R\u2082/(R\u2081 + R\u2082) = (6 \xD7 3)/(6 + 3) \xD7 (l/A) = 2l/A.
The combination behaves like one wire of the same length l, but with twice the area 2A (the two wires side by side).
So \u03C1_eff \xD7 l/(2A) = 2l/A, which gives \u03C1_eff = 4 \u03A9 cm.
Note: the same result follows from \u03C1 = 2\u03C1\u2081\u03C1\u2082/(\u03C1\u2081 + \u03C1\u2082) = 2 \xD7 18/9 = 4.
So \u03C1 = 4.`},{pn:148,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 24 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cdrf",st:"Current density",q:"A cylindrical wire of radius 0.5 mm and conductivity 5 \xD7 10\u2077 S/m is subjected to an electric field of 10 mV/m. The expected value of current in the wire will be x \u03C0 mA. The value of x is ______.",n:125,s:`Note: the printed key says 5; working gives 125.
Current density j = \u03C3E, where \u03C3 is the conductivity and E the field.
j = 5 \xD7 10\u2077 \xD7 10 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 = 5 \xD7 10\u2075 A/m\xB2.
Cross-section area A = \u03C0 r\xB2 = \u03C0 \xD7 (0.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3)\xB2 = \u03C0 \xD7 2.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 m\xB2.
I = jA = 5 \xD7 10\u2075 \xD7 2.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 \u03C0 = 0.125\u03C0 A = 125\u03C0 mA.
So x = 125.`},{pn:149,y:"JEE Advanced 2015",tp:"ccmb",st:"Balanced networks and symmetry",q:"In the following circuit, the current through the resistor R (= 2 \u03A9) is I amperes. The value of I is ______.",n:1,s:`Look for balanced bridges. Take the top-left node, the top-right node, the centre node and the lower-right node. The 1 \u03A9 then 2 \u03A9 arm and the 2 \u03A9 then 4 \u03A9 arm, both from the top-left node, have the same ratio, 1 : 2 = 2 : 4.
So the 8 \u03A9 resistor joins two points at the same potential and carries no current; remove it.
The two remaining paths to the lower-right junction are 1 + 2 = 3 \u03A9 and 2 + 4 = 6 \u03A9 in parallel, giving (3 \xD7 6)/(3 + 6) = 2 \u03A9.
Now the same idea again: the arm made of the 2 \u03A9 equivalent found above followed by the 4 \u03A9 resistor, and the arm 6 \u03A9 then 12 \u03A9 (both going to ground), have the same ratio 1 : 2, so the 10 \u03A9 resistor carries no current as well.
The solution figure shows the reduced circuit. The two paths to ground are 2 + 4 = 6 \u03A9 and 6 + 12 = 18 \u03A9 in parallel: (6 \xD7 18)/(6 + 18) = 4.5 \u03A9.
Total resistance = 2 (resistor R) + 4.5 = 6.5 \u03A9, so I = 6.5 V/6.5 \u03A9 = 1 A.
Note: the junction potentials come out as 4.5 V, 4 V, 3 V from the top, which confirms that no current flows in the 8 \u03A9 and 10 \u03A9 resistors.`,fig:{w:360,h:270,alt:"A 6.5 volt cell in series with R of 2 ohm feeding a network of resistors 1, 2, 4, 8, 6, 10, 12, 2 and 4 ohm",d:[["l",30,40,30,120],["l",18,120,42,120],["l",24,127,36,127,"B"],["l",30,127,30,250],["l",30,250,324,250],["l",30,40,64,40],["l",30,40,75,40],["p","M75 40 L77.8 44 L83.5 36 L89.2 44 L94.8 36 L100.5 44 L106.2 36 L109 40"],["l",109,40,154,40],["t",92,28,"R (= 2) \u03A9","m"],["l",154,40,222,40],["p","M222 40 L224.8 44 L230.5 36 L236.2 44 L241.8 36 L247.5 44 L253.2 36 L256 40"],["l",256,40,324,40],["t",239,28,"1 \u03A9","m"],["l",154,40,154,75],["p","M154 75 L150 78.3 L158 85 L150 91.7 L158 98.3 L150 105 L158 111.7 L154 115"],["l",154,115,154,150],["t",142,100,"6 \u03A9","e"],["l",154,150,154,180],["p","M154 180 L150 183.3 L158 190 L150 196.7 L158 203.3 L150 210 L158 216.7 L154 220"],["l",154,220,154,250],["t",142,205,"12 \u03A9","e"],["l",324,40,324,75],["p","M324 75 L320 78.3 L328 85 L320 91.7 L328 98.3 L320 105 L328 111.7 L324 115"],["l",324,115,324,150],["t",336,100,"2 \u03A9",""],["l",324,150,324,180],["p","M324 180 L320 183.3 L328 190 L320 196.7 L328 203.3 L320 210 L328 216.7 L324 220"],["l",324,220,324,250],["t",336,205,"4 \u03A9",""],["l",154,150,219,150],["p","M219 150 L222.3 154 L229 146 L235.7 154 L242.3 146 L249 154 L255.7 146 L259 150"],["l",259,150,324,150],["t",239,168,"10 \u03A9","m"],["l",154,40,179.7,56.6],["p","M179.7 56.6 L180.3 61.8 L190.3 58.7 L191.5 69 L201.5 66 L202.7 76.3 L212.7 73.2 L213.3 78.4"],["l",213.3,78.4,239,95],["t",184,82,"2 \u03A9","e"],["l",239,95,264.7,111.6],["p","M264.7 111.6 L265.3 116.8 L275.3 113.7 L276.5 124 L286.5 121 L287.7 131.3 L297.7 128.2 L298.3 133.4"],["l",298.3,133.4,324,150],["t",260,138,"4 \u03A9","e"],["l",324,40,300,55.5],["p","M300 55.5 L294.7 54.2 L292.9 64.9 L282.4 62.1 L280.6 72.9 L270.1 70.1 L268.3 80.8 L263 79.5"],["l",263,79.5,239,95],["t",292,84,"8 \u03A9","e"],["c",154,40,2.2,"F"],["c",324,40,2.2,"F"],["c",324,150,2.2,"F"],["c",154,150,2.2,"F"],["c",239,95,2.2,"F"],["c",154,250,2.2,"F"],["c",324,250,2.2,"F"],["t",44,138,"6.5 V"],["t",44,116,"+"]]},sfig:{w:340,h:235,alt:"Reduced circuit: 6.5 V cell and R of 2 ohm feed a node from which two paths go to ground: 2 ohm then 4 ohm, and 6 ohm then 12 ohm",d:[["l",30,30,30,100],["l",18,100,42,100],["l",24,107,36,107,"B"],["l",30,107,30,200],["t",44,118,"6.5 V"],["t",44,96,"+"],["l",30,30,50,30],["p","M50 30 L59.0 30 L62.5 25 L69.5 35 L76.5 25 L83.5 35 L90.5 25 L97.5 35 L101.0 30 L110 30"],["t",80,16,"R = 2 \u03A9","m"],["l",110,30,160,30],["c",160,30,2.4,"F"],["l",160,30,160,50],["p","M160.0 50.0 L160.0 56.8 L155.0 59.4 L165.0 64.6 L155.0 69.9 L165.0 75.1 L155.0 80.4 L165.0 85.6 L160.0 88.2 L160.0 95.0"],["t",172,72,"2 \u03A9",""],["l",160,95,160,100],["p","M160.0 100.0 L160.0 106.8 L155.0 109.4 L165.0 114.6 L155.0 119.9 L165.0 125.1 L155.0 130.4 L165.0 135.6 L160.0 138.2 L160.0 145.0"],["t",172,122,"4 \u03A9",""],["l",160,145,160,200],["l",160,30,260,30],["l",260,30,260,50],["p","M260.0 50.0 L260.0 57.5 L255.0 60.4 L265.0 66.2 L255.0 72.1 L265.0 77.9 L255.0 83.8 L265.0 89.6 L260.0 92.5 L260.0 100.0"],["t",272,75,"6 \u03A9",""],["l",260,100,260,105],["p","M260.0 105.0 L260.0 111.8 L255.0 114.4 L265.0 119.6 L255.0 124.9 L265.0 130.1 L255.0 135.4 L265.0 140.6 L260.0 143.2 L260.0 150.0"],["t",272,128,"12 \u03A9",""],["l",260,150,260,200],["l",30,200,260,200],["t",100,220,"ground","m"]]}},{pn:150,y:"JEE Advanced 2016",tp:"cdrf",st:"Current and charge",q:`Consider an evacuated cylindrical chamber of height h having rigid conducting plates at the ends and an insulating curved surface. A number of spherical balls, made of a light-weight, soft material and coated with a conducting material, are placed on the bottom plate. The balls have radius r << h. A high-voltage source (HV) is connected across the conducting plates so that the bottom plate is at +V\u2080 and the top plate at \u2212V\u2080. Due to their conducting surface, the balls get charged, become equipotential with the plate and are repelled by it. The balls eventually collide with the top plate, where the coefficient of restitution is taken as zero because of the soft material. The electric field in the chamber can be considered to be that of a parallel plate capacitor. Assume there are no collisions between the balls and that the interaction between them is negligible. (Ignore gravity.)
Which one of the following statements is correct?`,o:["The balls will bounce back to the bottom plate carrying the opposite charge they went up with","The balls will stick to the top plate and remain there","The balls will execute simple harmonic motion between the two plates","The balls will bounce back to the bottom plate carrying the same charge they went up with"],c:0,s:`Note: the key table prints 2 but the printed solution says (a); working gives (a).
A ball on the bottom plate (+V\u2080) takes a positive charge and is pushed up to the top plate (\u2212V\u2080).
On touching the top plate the ball shares its potential \u2212V\u2080, so its positive charge goes away and it takes a negative charge.
The coefficient of restitution is zero, so the ball does not bounce off the plate, but now it is negative like the plate and is repelled by it, and it is attracted by the positive bottom plate.
So it goes back down, carrying a charge of opposite sign to the one it went up with.
Once it touches the bottom plate it becomes positive again and the trip repeats, so it does not stick and it is not simple harmonic motion.
Option (a).`,fig:{w:320,h:215,alt:"Evacuated cylinder with conducting end plates, balls on the bottom plate, an ammeter A in the wire to the top plate and a high-voltage source HV",d:[["l",70,50,190,50,"B"],["l",70,170,190,170,"B"],["l",70,50,70,170],["l",190,50,190,170],["l",130,50,130,22],["l",130,22,168,22],["c",180,22,12],["t",180,26,"A","m"],["l",192,22,262,22],["l",262,22,262,100],["l",262,100,262,106],["l",256,106,268,106,"B"],["l",250,113,274,113],["l",262,113,262,200],["l",262,200,130,200],["l",130,200,130,170],["t",282,112,"HV"],["t",270,98,"\u2212"],["t",270,126,"+"],["l",50,52,50,168,"b"],["t",44,114,"h","e"],["t",60,46,"\u2212V\u2080","e"],["t",60,190,"+V\u2080","e"],["c",84,164,5],["c",101,164,5],["c",118,164,5],["c",135,164,5],["c",152,164,5],["c",169,164,5],["c",186,164,5]]}},{pn:151,y:"JEE Advanced 2016",tp:"cdrf",st:"Current and charge",q:"In the same set-up (evacuated chamber with conducting end plates at +V\u2080 and \u2212V\u2080, light conducting balls on the bottom plate, and an ammeter in the circuit), the average current in the steady state registered by the ammeter will be",o:["proportional to V\u2080^(1/2)","zero","proportional to V\u2080\xB2","proportional to the potential V\u2080"],c:2,s:`A ball is at the same potential as the plate, so the charge it carries is proportional to V\u2080: q \u221D V\u2080.
The field between the plates is E = 2V\u2080/h, so the force on a ball is qE \u221D V\u2080 \xD7 V\u2080 = V\u2080\xB2, and its acceleration is a \u221D V\u2080\xB2.
From h = \xBDat\xB2, the time to cross is t \u221D 1/\u221Aa \u221D 1/V\u2080.
Each crossing carries charge of size \u221D V\u2080 from one plate to the other, so the current of one ball is q/t \u221D V\u2080 \xD7 V\u2080 = V\u2080\xB2.
The number of balls is fixed, so the average current is proportional to V\u2080\xB2.
Option (c).`,fig:{w:320,h:215,alt:"Evacuated cylinder with conducting end plates, balls on the bottom plate, an ammeter A in the wire to the top plate and a high-voltage source HV",d:[["l",70,50,190,50,"B"],["l",70,170,190,170,"B"],["l",70,50,70,170],["l",190,50,190,170],["l",130,50,130,22],["l",130,22,168,22],["c",180,22,12],["t",180,26,"A","m"],["l",192,22,262,22],["l",262,22,262,100],["l",262,100,262,106],["l",256,106,268,106,"B"],["l",250,113,274,113],["l",262,113,262,200],["l",262,200,130,200],["l",130,200,130,170],["t",282,112,"HV"],["t",270,98,"\u2212"],["t",270,126,"+"],["l",50,52,50,168,"b"],["t",44,114,"h","e"],["t",60,46,"\u2212V\u2080","e"],["t",60,190,"+V\u2080","e"],["c",84,164,5],["c",101,164,5],["c",118,164,5],["c",135,164,5],["c",152,164,5],["c",169,164,5],["c",186,164,5]]}},{pn:152,y:"JEE Advanced 2016",tp:"cdrf",st:"Current density",q:"A block of conducting material of resistivity \u03C1 has four points A, B, C, D in a line on its top face, with AB = a, BC = b and CD = a. Current I enters at A and leaves at D, spreading through the block in hemispheres (superposition principle). \u0394V measured between B and C is",o:["\u03C1I/(2\u03C0(a \u2212 b))","\u03C1I/(\u03C0a) \u2212 \u03C1I/(\u03C0(a + b))","\u03C1I/a \u2212 \u03C1I/(a + b)","\u03C1I/(2\u03C0a) \u2212 \u03C1I/(2\u03C0(a + b))"],c:1,s:`Note: the printed key says (d); working gives (b).
Current I from one point spreads over a hemisphere of area 2\u03C0r\xB2, so j = I/(2\u03C0r\xB2) and E = \u03C1j = \u03C1I/(2\u03C0r\xB2).
The potential at distance r (taking zero far away) is V = \u03C1I/(2\u03C0r). Current entering at A gives +, current leaving at D gives \u2212 of the same form.
At B: V_B = \u03C1I/(2\u03C0a) \u2212 \u03C1I/(2\u03C0(a + b)), since B is a from A and a + b from D.
At C: V_C = \u03C1I/(2\u03C0(a + b)) \u2212 \u03C1I/(2\u03C0a), which is exactly \u2212V_B by symmetry.
\u0394V = V_B \u2212 V_C = 2V_B = \u03C1I/(\u03C0a) \u2212 \u03C1I/(\u03C0(a + b)).
Note: option (d) is only V_B, half of the answer, because it forgets the contribution of the second terminal at C.
Option (b).`,fig:{w:280,h:175,alt:"A block of conducting material with four points A, B, C and D in a line on the top face, spaced a, b and a; current I enters at A and leaves at D; the voltmeter reading \u0394V is taken between B and C",d:[["p","M30 134 L210 134 L250 90 L70 90 Z"],["p","M30 134 L30 156 L210 156 L210 134"],["p","M210 156 L250 112 L250 90"],["l",70,50,70,96,"a"],["l",215,96,215,50,"a"],["l",115,100,115,40],["l",165,100,165,40],["l",115,46,165,46,"b"],["t",140,36,"\u0394V","m"],["t",62,66,"I","e"],["t",224,66,"I"],["c",70,100,2.2,"F"],["c",115,100,2.2,"F"],["c",165,100,2.2,"F"],["c",215,100,2.2,"F"],["t",70,116,"A","m"],["t",115,116,"B","m"],["t",165,116,"C","m"],["t",215,116,"D","m"],["t",92,128,"a","m"],["t",140,128,"b","m"],["t",190,128,"a","m"]]}},{pn:153,y:"AIEEE 2008",tp:"cdrf",st:"Current density",q:"Current I enters a block of resistivity \u03C1 at A and spreads out through the block in a hemispherical pattern. The electric field at a distance r from A is",o:["\u03C1I/(4\u03C0r\xB2)","\u03C1I/(8\u03C0r\xB2)","\u03C1I/r\xB2","\u03C1I/(2\u03C0r\xB2)"],c:3,s:`At distance r the current has spread over a hemisphere, whose surface area is half of 4\u03C0r\xB2, that is 2\u03C0r\xB2.
Current density there: j = I/(2\u03C0r\xB2).
Ohm's law in the form E = \u03C1j gives E = \u03C1I/(2\u03C0r\xB2).
Option (d).`},{pn:154,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 15 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cres",st:"Temperature dependence",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I: The equivalent resistance of resistors in a series combination is smaller than the least resistance used in the combination.
Statement II: The resistivity of the material is independent of temperature.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["Statement I is true but Statement II is false.","Both Statement I and Statement II are false.","Statement I is false but Statement II is true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are true."],c:1,s:`Statement I: in series the resistances add, R = R\u2081 + R\u2082 + ..., so the total is larger than every single resistance. The statement says smaller, so it is false.
Statement II: resistivity changes with temperature (it rises for metals, falls for semiconductors), so it is not independent of temperature. This is false too.
Both statements are false, option (b).`},{pn:155,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ccmb",st:"Series and parallel combinations",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I: A uniform wire of resistance 80 \u03A9 is cut into four equal parts. These parts are now connected in parallel. The equivalent resistance of the combination will be 5 \u03A9.
Statement II: Two resistances 2R and 3R are connected in parallel in an electric circuit. The value of thermal energy developed in 3R and 2R will be in the ratio 3 : 2.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.`,o:["Both statement I and statement II are correct.","Both statement I and statement II are incorrect.","Statement I is correct but statement II is incorrect.","Statement I is incorrect but statement II is correct."],c:2,s:`Statement I: each of the four equal parts has resistance 80/4 = 20 \u03A9.
Four equal resistors of 20 \u03A9 in parallel give 20/4 = 5 \u03A9, so statement I is correct.
Statement II: in parallel both resistors have the same voltage V, so the heat in time t is H = V\xB2t/R, which is proportional to 1/R.
H(3R) : H(2R) = (1/3R) : (1/2R) = 2 : 3, not 3 : 2, so statement II is incorrect.
Option (c).`},{pn:156,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cres",st:"Temperature dependence",q:`Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.
Assertion A: Alloys such as constantan and manganin are used in making standard resistance coils.
Reason R: Constantan and manganin have a very small value of temperature coefficient of resistance.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","A is false but R is true."],c:0,s:`A standard resistance coil must keep the same value even when its temperature changes a little.
Constantan and manganin have a very small temperature coefficient of resistance, so their resistance hardly changes with temperature. So R is true.
This is exactly why they are chosen for standard coils, so A is true and R explains A.
Option (a).`},{pn:157,y:"AIEEE 2009",tp:"cres",st:"Temperature dependence",q:`Statement-1: The temperature dependence of resistance is usually given as R = R\u2080(1 + \u03B1\u0394t). The resistance of a wire changes from 100 \u03A9 to 150 \u03A9 when its temperature is increased from 27\xB0C to 227\xB0C. This implies that \u03B1 = 2.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3/\xB0C.
Statement-2: R = R\u2080(1 + \u03B1\u0394t) is valid only when the change in the temperature \u0394T is small and \u0394R = (R \u2212 R\u2080) << R\u2080.`,o:["Statement-1 is true, statement-2 is false","Statement-1 is true, statement-2 is true; statement-2 is the correct explanation of statement-1","Statement-1 is true, statement-2 is true; statement-2 is not the correct explanation of statement-1","Statement-1 is false, statement-2 is true"],c:0,s:`Statement-1: take R\u2080 = 100 \u03A9 at 27\xB0C, so R = 150 \u03A9 at \u0394t = 200\xB0C.
150 = 100(1 + \u03B1 \xD7 200), so 200\u03B1 = 0.5 and \u03B1 = 2.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 /\xB0C. Statement-1 is true.
Statement-2: the formula is just the definition of the average coefficient \u03B1 over the range taken; there is no need for \u0394R << R\u2080 for it to hold. The very example in statement-1 has \u0394R = 50 \u03A9, which is half of R\u2080, yet it is used. So statement-2 is false.
Note: the printed key agrees; only the linear law is an approximation (\u03B1 itself changes with temperature), and we use it as given.
Option (a).`},{pn:158,y:"IIT-JEE 1993",tp:"cres",st:"Temperature dependence",q:`Statement-1: The resistivity of a semiconductor increases with increase of temperature.
Statement-2: In a conducting solid, the rate of collisions between free electrons and ions increases with increase of temperature.`,o:["Statements 1 and 2 are true and statement-2 is a correct explanation for statement-1","Statements 1 and 2 are true and statement-2 is not a correct explanation for statement-1","Statement-1 is true, statement-2 is false","Statement-1 is false, statement-2 is true"],c:3,s:`In a semiconductor, heating frees many more charge carriers, so the number of carriers rises sharply. This beats the extra collisions, so the resistivity falls with temperature. Statement-1 is false.
In any conducting solid the ions vibrate more at higher temperature, so electrons collide with them more often. Statement-2 is true.
Statement-1 false, statement-2 true: option (d).`},{pn:159,y:"IIT-JEE 2004",tp:"cpot",st:"Galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"Draw the circuit diagram to verify Ohm's law with the help of a main resistance of 100 \u03A9 and two galvanometers of resistances 10\u2076 \u03A9 and 10\u207B\xB3 \u03A9 and a source of varying emf. Show the correct positions of voltmeter and ammeter.",a:"The galvanometer of 10\u2076 \u03A9 (very large) is the voltmeter: connect it in parallel with the 100 \u03A9 resistor. The galvanometer of 10\u207B\xB3 \u03A9 (very small) is the ammeter: connect it in series with the resistor and the variable source (see the figure).",s:`To verify Ohm's law we measure the voltage across the 100 \u03A9 resistor and the current through it for several settings of the source, then check that V/I stays constant.
A voltmeter must draw almost no current, so it needs a very large resistance and goes in PARALLEL with the resistor. The 10\u2076 \u03A9 galvanometer is used for this.
An ammeter must not change the current, so it needs a very small resistance and goes in SERIES. The 10\u207B\xB3 \u03A9 galvanometer is used for this.
In the figure the source, ammeter and 100 \u03A9 resistor form the main loop, and the voltmeter is connected across the resistor.
Vary the source, note V and I each time, and V/I comes out the same, 100 \u03A9.`,sfig:{w:320,h:190,alt:"Circuit: a variable source, the 100 ohm resistor in series with the low-resistance galvanometer as ammeter, and the high-resistance galvanometer as voltmeter across the resistor",d:[["l",40,40,56,40],["c",56,40,2.2,"F"],["l",56,40,90,40],["p","M90 40 L93 44 L99 36 L105 44 L111 36 L117 44 L123 36 L126 40"],["l",126,40,160,40],["c",160,40,2.2,"F"],["t",108,58,"R = 100 \u03A9","m"],["l",160,40,194,40],["c",210,40,16],["t",210,44,"A","m"],["t",210,16,"ammeter G\u2082 (10\u207B\xB3 \u03A9)","m"],["l",226,40,270,40],["l",270,40,270,150],["l",40,40,40,150],["l",40,150,150,150],["l",150,138,150,162],["l",157,144,157,156,"B"],["l",157,150,270,150],["t",154,172,"variable emf","m"],["l",56,40,56,90],["l",56,90,86,90],["c",102,90,16],["t",102,94,"V","m"],["l",118,90,160,90],["l",160,90,160,40],["t",150,122,"voltmeter G\u2081 (10\u2076 \u03A9)","m"]]}},{pn:160,y:"IIT-JEE 1982",tp:"cpot",st:"Galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"Two resistors, 400 ohm and 800 ohm are connected in series with a 6 volt battery. It is desired to measure the current in the circuit. An ammeter of 10 ohm resistance is used for this purpose. What will be the reading in the ammeter? Similarly, if a voltmeter of 10,000 ohm resistance is used to measure the potential difference across the 400 ohm resistor, what will be the reading in the voltmeter?",a:"Ammeter: about 4.96 mA (4.96 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 A). Voltmeter: about 1.95 V.",s:`Ammeter: it goes in series, so its 10 \u03A9 adds to the circuit. Total resistance = 400 + 800 + 10 = 1210 \u03A9.
Current I = 6/1210 = 4.96 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 A = 4.96 mA (the true current without the ammeter would be 6/1200 = 5 mA).
Voltmeter: it goes in parallel with the 400 \u03A9 resistor, so the pair has resistance (400 \xD7 10000)/(400 + 10000) = 4000000/10400 = 384.6 \u03A9.
Total resistance = 384.6 + 800 = 1184.6 \u03A9, so the battery current is 6/1184.6 = 5.065 mA.
Reading of the voltmeter = 5.065 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 384.6 = 1.948 V \u2248 1.95 V.
Note: the voltmeter reads a little less than the true 2 V across the 400 \u03A9 resistor, because it draws some current.
So the ammeter reads 4.96 mA and the voltmeter reads 1.95 V.`},{pn:161,y:"IIT-JEE 1982",tp:"cdrf",st:"Current density",q:"A steady current passes through a cylindrical conductor. Is there an electric field inside the conductor?",a:"Yes. A steady current needs an electric field along the conductor, E = \u03C1j.",s:`The free electrons of a conductor drift in a direction only if a force acts on them.
That force is the electric force on the charges, so a current needs an electric field in the conductor.
Ohm's law in its field form is E = \u03C1j, so with a steady current density j \u2260 0 the field is E = \u03C1j, not zero (E = V/l along the length).
Note: electrostatic equilibrium (E = 0 inside a conductor) is true only when no current flows.
So yes, there is an electric field inside the conductor, along the current.`},{pn:162,y:"IIT-JEE 1980",tp:"cpot",st:"Galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"In the circuit shown in the figure, a voltmeter reads 30 volts when it is connected across the 400 ohm resistance. Calculate what the same voltmeter will read when it is connected across the 300 ohm resistance.",a:"22.5 V",s:`The voltmeter is not ideal: when it is across a resistor it takes some current, so we must find its resistance R_v first.
With it across the 400 \u03A9 resistor the reading is 30 V, so the other 30 V of the 60 V supply is across the 300 \u03A9 resistor. The same current flows through 300 \u03A9: I = 30/300 = 0.1 A.
This current splits between the 400 \u03A9 resistor and the voltmeter, which share 30 V: the pair must have resistance 30/0.1 = 300 \u03A9.
400 R_v/(400 + R_v) = 300, so 400R_v = 120000 + 300R_v and R_v = 1200 \u03A9.
Now put the voltmeter across the 300 \u03A9 resistor: 300 \u03A9 and 1200 \u03A9 in parallel is (300 \xD7 1200)/1500 = 240 \u03A9.
This is in series with the 400 \u03A9 resistor, so total = 640 \u03A9 and the voltage across the parallel pair is 60 \xD7 240/640 = 22.5 V.
The voltmeter reads 22.5 V.`,fig:{w:300,h:165,alt:"A 60 volt battery in series with a 300 ohm and a 400 ohm resistor; a voltmeter is connected across the 400 ohm resistor",d:[["l",30,40,50,40],["l",50,40,72,40],["p","M72 40 L75 44 L81 36 L87 44 L93 36 L99 44 L105 36 L108 40"],["l",108,40,130,40],["t",90,58,"300 \u03A9","m"],["l",130,40,150,40],["c",150,40,2.2,"F"],["l",150,40,177,40],["p","M177 40 L180 44 L186 36 L192 44 L198 36 L204 44 L210 36 L213 40"],["l",213,40,240,40],["c",240,40,2.2,"F"],["t",195,58,"400 \u03A9","m"],["l",150,40,150,16],["l",150,16,183,16],["c",195,16,12],["t",195,20,"V","m"],["l",207,16,240,16],["l",240,16,240,40],["l",240,40,265,40],["l",265,40,265,130],["l",265,130,130,130],["l",30,40,30,130],["l",30,130,118,130],["l",118,118,118,142],["l",125,124,125,136,"B"],["t",122,152,"60 V","m"]]}},{pn:163,y:"IIT-JEE 1979",tp:"ccmb",st:"Equivalent resistance of networks",q:"All resistances in the diagram are in ohm. Find the effective resistance between the points A and B.",n:2,s:`The direct 3 \u03A9 resistor between A and B is in parallel with the rest of the network, so first find the resistance R\u2032 of the rest between A and B.
Between A and D there are two routes in parallel: the 6 \u03A9 resistor, and A to C to D with 3 + 3 = 6 \u03A9. Together they give 3 \u03A9.
The reduced network is drawn in the solution figure: A to D is 3 \u03A9, then D-E, E-F and F-B are 3 \u03A9 each, with 6 \u03A9 from A to E and from A to F.
Now send current from A to B with A at 1 V and B at 0 V, and find the potentials of D, E, F (call them V_D, V_E, V_F), applying the junction law at each point (the currents into a node equal the currents out).
At D: (1 \u2212 V_D)/3 = (V_D \u2212 V_E)/3, so V_E = 2V_D \u2212 1. At E: (1 \u2212 V_E)/6 + (V_D \u2212 V_E)/3 = (V_E \u2212 V_F)/3, which gives V_F = 4V_D \u2212 3.
At F: (1 \u2212 V_F)/6 + (V_E \u2212 V_F)/3 = V_F/3, which gives V_D = 7/8, V_E = 3/4, V_F = 1/2.
The current leaving through F to B is V_F/3 = 1/6 A, so the rest of the network has R\u2032 = 1/(1/6) = 6 \u03A9.
Now 3 \u03A9 (direct) in parallel with 6 \u03A9 gives (3 \xD7 6)/(3 + 6) = 2 \u03A9.
The effective resistance between A and B is 2 \u03A9.`,fig:{w:340,h:265,alt:"Six-sided network with centre point A joined to the outer points C, D, E, F and B: resistances 3 ohm on the outer sides and 6 ohm from A to D, E and F",d:[["l",170,230,122.5,198.3],["p","M122.5 198.3 L122.6 193.6 L114 197.5 L114.3 188.1 L105.7 191.9 L106 182.5 L97.4 186.4 L97.5 181.7"],["l",97.5,181.7,50,150],["t",102.8,204.8,"3","m"],["l",50,150,63.3,123.4],["p","M63.3 123.4 L68 123 L63.1 114.9 L72.5 114 L67.5 106 L76.9 105.1 L72 97 L76.7 96.6"],["l",76.7,96.6,90,70],["t",58.4,108.2,"3","m"],["l",90,70,116,60.3],["p","M116 60.3 L119.7 63.1 L121.6 53.9 L129.1 59.6 L130.9 50.4 L138.4 56.1 L140.3 46.9 L144 49.7"],["l",144,49.7,170,40],["t",125.4,46.8,"3","m"],["l",170,40,196,49.7],["p","M196 49.7 L196.9 54.4 L204.4 48.6 L206.3 57.9 L213.7 52.1 L215.6 61.4 L223.1 55.6 L224 60.3"],["l",224,60.3,250,70],["t",214.6,46.8,"3","m"],["l",250,70,263.3,96.6],["p","M263.3 96.6 L260.8 100.6 L270.2 101.5 L265.3 109.6 L274.7 110.4 L269.8 118.5 L279.2 119.4 L276.7 123.4"],["l",276.7,123.4,290,150],["t",281.6,108.2,"3","m"],["l",290,150,242.5,181.7],["p","M242.5 181.7 L238.2 179.7 L238.5 189.2 L229.9 185.3 L230.1 194.7 L221.5 190.8 L221.8 200.3 L217.5 198.3"],["l",217.5,198.3,170,230],["t",237.2,204.8,"3","m"],["l",170,230,138.9,167.9],["p","M138.9 167.9 L141 163.1 L130.9 160.7 L135.1 151.2 L124.9 148.8 L129.1 139.3 L119 136.9 L121.1 132.1"],["l",121.1,132.1,90,70],["t",141.6,148.2,"6","m"],["l",170,230,170,155],["p","M170 155 L174 151.7 L166 145 L174 138.3 L166 131.7 L174 125 L166 118.3 L170 115"],["l",170,115,170,40],["t",183,139,"6","m"],["l",170,230,201.1,167.9],["p","M201.1 167.9 L206.1 166.7 L202 157.2 L212.1 154.8 L207.9 145.2 L218 142.8 L213.9 133.3 L218.9 132.1"],["l",218.9,132.1,250,70],["t",198.4,148.2,"6","m"],["c",170,230,2.2,"F"],["c",50,150,2.2,"F"],["c",90,70,2.2,"F"],["c",170,40,2.2,"F"],["c",250,70,2.2,"F"],["c",290,150,2.2,"F"],["t",170,252,"A","m"],["t",40,152,"C","e"],["t",80,64,"D","e"],["t",170,28,"E","m"],["t",260,64,"F"],["t",300,152,"B"]]},sfig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"Reduced network: A to D 3 ohm (6 and 6 in parallel), then D to E, E to F and F to B each 3 ohm; 6 ohm from A to E and from A to F",d:[["c",40,60,2.4,"F"],["p","M40 60 L49.0 60 L52.5 55 L59.5 65 L66.5 55 L73.5 65 L80.5 55 L87.5 65 L91.0 60 L100 60"],["t",70,46,"3","m"],["c",100,60,2.4,"F"],["p","M100 60 L109.0 60 L112.5 55 L119.5 65 L126.5 55 L133.5 65 L140.5 55 L147.5 65 L151.0 60 L160 60"],["t",130,46,"3","m"],["c",160,60,2.4,"F"],["p","M160 60 L169.0 60 L172.5 55 L179.5 65 L186.5 55 L193.5 65 L200.5 55 L207.5 65 L211.0 60 L220 60"],["t",190,46,"3","m"],["c",220,60,2.4,"F"],["p","M220 60 L229.0 60 L232.5 55 L239.5 65 L246.5 55 L253.5 65 L260.5 55 L267.5 65 L271.0 60 L280 60"],["t",250,46,"3","m"],["c",280,60,2.4,"F"],["t",40,44,"A","m"],["t",100,44,"D","m"],["t",160,44,"E","m"],["t",220,44,"F","m"],["t",280,44,"B","m"],["l",40,60,40,150],["l",40,150,160,150],["l",160,150,160,115],["l",160,60,160,75],["p","M160.0 75.0 L160.0 81.8 L155.0 84.4 L165.0 89.6 L155.0 94.9 L165.0 100.1 L155.0 105.4 L165.0 110.6 L160.0 113.2 L160.0 120.0"],["t",172,98,"6",""],["l",160,120,160,150],["l",160,150,220,150],["l",220,60,220,75],["p","M220.0 75.0 L220.0 81.8 L215.0 84.4 L225.0 89.6 L215.0 94.9 L225.0 100.1 L215.0 105.4 L225.0 110.6 L220.0 113.2 L220.0 120.0"],["t",232,98,"6",""],["l",220,120,220,150]]}},{pn:164,y:"IIT-JEE 1978",tp:"cres",st:"Stretching and shape change of wires",q:"A copper wire is stretched to make it 0.1% longer. What is the percentage change in its resistance?",n:.2,s:`When a wire is stretched its volume stays the same, so as the length grows the area shrinks: A \u221D 1/l.
R = \u03C1l/A, so R \u221D l\xB2.
For a small change, \u0394R/R \u2248 2 \xD7 \u0394l/l = 2 \xD7 0.1% = 0.2%.
The resistance increases by 0.2%.`},{pn:165,y:"IIT-JEE 1985",tp:"cres",st:"Ohm's law and V-I graphs",q:"The current-voltage graphs for a given metallic wire at two different temperatures T\u2081 and T\u2082 are shown in the figure. The temperature T\u2082 is greater than T\u2081.",o:["True","False"],c:0,s:`On an I-V graph the slope is I/V = 1/R, so a steeper line means a smaller resistance.
The line T\u2081 is steeper, so the wire has the smaller resistance at T\u2081; the line T\u2082 is less steep, so the resistance is larger at T\u2082.
The resistance of a metal rises with temperature, so the higher resistance belongs to the higher temperature: T\u2082 > T\u2081.
So the statement is True.`,fig:{w:260,h:190,alt:"Current against voltage: two straight lines from the origin, the upper steeper line is T1 and the lower line is T2",d:[["l",50,160,50,20,"a"],["l",50,160,230,160,"a"],["t",42,28,"I","e"],["t",234,164,"V"],["t",42,172,"O","e"],["l",50,160,190,40],["l",50,160,200,92],["t",194,36,"T\u2081"],["t",204,92,"T\u2082"]]}},{pn:166,y:"IIT-JEE 1982",tp:"cdrf",st:"Drift velocity and mobility",q:"Electrons in a conductor have no motion in the absence of a potential difference across it.",o:["True","False"],c:1,s:`The free electrons of a conductor are always moving at random with large thermal speeds (about 10\u2075 m/s at room temperature), even with no battery connected.
These random motions cancel on average, so there is no net current.
A potential difference only adds a very small drift to this random motion.
So the statement is False.`},{pn:167,y:"IIT-JEE 1997",tp:"ccmb",st:"Series and parallel combinations",q:"The equivalent resistance between points A and B of the circuit given is ______.",a:"R/2",s:`Follow the wires and name the junctions: A, then the first 2R, junction P, the second 2R, junction Q, then R, ending at B.
The top wire joins P directly to B, so P and B are the same point electrically.
The bottom wire joins A directly to Q, so A and Q are the same point.
So the first 2R connects A to P = B, the second 2R connects P = B to Q = A, and R connects Q = A to B.
All three resistors are therefore between the same two points A and B, that is, in parallel (see the redrawn figure).
1/R_eq = 1/(2R) + 1/(2R) + 1/R = 2/R, so R_eq = R/2.`,fig:{w:300,h:165,alt:"Between A and B: a 2R, a 2R and an R resistor in series, with a wire from the junction after the first 2R to B and a wire from A to the junction after the second 2R",d:[["t",16,104,"A","m"],["l",24,100,40,100],["c",40,100,2.2,"F"],["l",40,100,55,100],["p","M55 100 L58.3 104 L65 96 L71.7 104 L78.3 96 L85 104 L91.7 96 L95 100"],["l",95,100,110,100],["t",75,88,"2R","m"],["c",110,100,2.2,"F"],["l",110,100,125,100],["p","M125 100 L128.3 104 L135 96 L141.7 104 L148.3 96 L155 104 L161.7 96 L165 100"],["l",165,100,180,100],["t",145,88,"2R","m"],["c",180,100,2.2,"F"],["l",180,100,195,100],["p","M195 100 L198.3 104 L205 96 L211.7 104 L218.3 96 L225 104 L231.7 96 L235 100"],["l",235,100,250,100],["t",215,88,"R","m"],["c",250,100,2.2,"F"],["l",250,100,268,100],["t",280,104,"B","m"],["l",110,100,110,55],["l",110,55,250,55],["l",250,55,250,100],["l",40,100,40,145],["l",40,145,180,145],["l",180,145,180,100]]},sfig:{w:240,h:150,alt:"Redrawn: three resistors 2R, 2R and R all in parallel between A and B",d:[["t",16,84,"A","m"],["l",24,80,50,80],["l",50,40,50,120],["l",50,40,90,40],["l",50,40,90,40],["p","M90 40 L93.3 44 L100 36 L106.7 44 L113.3 36 L120 44 L126.7 36 L130 40"],["l",130,40,170,40],["l",50,80,90,80],["l",50,120,90,120],["l",50,80,90,80],["p","M90 80 L93.3 84 L100 76 L106.7 84 L113.3 76 L120 84 L126.7 76 L130 80"],["l",130,80,170,80],["l",50,120,90,120],["p","M90 120 L93.3 124 L100 116 L106.7 124 L113.3 116 L120 124 L126.7 116 L130 120"],["l",130,120,170,120],["l",170,40,170,120],["l",170,80,196,80],["t",108,30,"2R","m"],["t",108,70,"2R","m"],["t",108,110,"R","m"],["t",208,84,"B","m"],["c",50,80,2.2,"F"],["c",170,80,2.2,"F"]]}},{pn:168,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ccel",st:"EMF and internal resistance",q:"In the given circuit, the terminal potential difference of the cell is",o:["3 V","2 V","4 V","1.5 V"],c:1,s:`The 3 V cell has an internal resistance of 1 \u03A9 (the dotted box), in series with the outside circuit.
The two 4 \u03A9 resistors are in parallel: (4 \xD7 4)/(4 + 4) = 2 \u03A9.
Total resistance in the loop = 1 + 2 = 3 \u03A9, so the current is I = 3/3 = 1 A.
The terminal potential difference is V = \u03B5 \u2212 Ir = 3 \u2212 1 \xD7 1 = 2 V.
Check: it is also the voltage across the outside 2 \u03A9, I \xD7 2 = 2 V.
Option (b).`,fig:{w:300,h:170,alt:"A 3 volt cell with 1 ohm internal resistance (inside the dotted box) connected across two 4 ohm resistors in parallel",d:[["l",60,30,250,30],["l",60,30,60,56],["l",48,56,72,56],["l",54,63,66,63,"B"],["l",60,63,60,80],["l",60,80,60,87],["p","M60 87 L56 89.8 L64 95.5 L56 101.2 L64 106.8 L56 112.5 L64 118.2 L60 121"],["l",60,121,60,128],["l",60,128,60,150],["l",60,150,250,150],["t",76,54,"+"],["t",72,108,"1 \u03A9"],["t",44,36,"3 V","e"],["r",36,46,64,92,"d"],["l",140,30,140,60],["l",140,60,140,72],["p","M140 72 L136 75 L144 81 L136 87 L144 93 L136 99 L144 105 L140 108"],["l",140,108,140,120],["l",140,120,140,150],["c",140,30,2.2,"F"],["c",140,150,2.2,"F"],["t",152,94,"4 \u03A9"],["l",250,30,250,60],["l",250,60,250,72],["p","M250 72 L246 75 L254 81 L246 87 L254 93 L246 99 L254 105 L250 108"],["l",250,108,250,120],["l",250,120,250,150],["t",262,94,"4 \u03A9"]]}},{pn:169,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ccel",st:"Grouping of cells",q:"The reading in the ideal voltmeter (V) shown in the given circuit diagram is",fig:{w:420,h:200,alt:"Loop of seven 5 V cells, each with 0.2 ohm internal resistance: three along the top, one on the left, one on the right and three along the bottom, all pushing current the same way round; an ideal voltmeter is joined across the right-hand cell",d:[["l",110,29,110,51],["l",115,34,115,46,"B"],["l",110,154,110,166,"B"],["l",115,149,115,171],["t",113,28,"0.2 \u03A9","m"],["t",113,70,"5 V","m"],["t",113,142,"0.2 \u03A9","m"],["t",113,190,"5 V","m"],["l",170,29,170,51],["l",175,34,175,46,"B"],["l",170,154,170,166,"B"],["l",175,149,175,171],["t",173,28,"0.2 \u03A9","m"],["t",173,70,"5 V","m"],["t",173,142,"0.2 \u03A9","m"],["t",173,190,"5 V","m"],["l",230,29,230,51],["l",235,34,235,46,"B"],["l",230,154,230,166,"B"],["l",235,149,235,171],["t",233,28,"0.2 \u03A9","m"],["t",233,70,"5 V","m"],["t",233,142,"0.2 \u03A9","m"],["t",233,190,"5 V","m"],["l",40,40,110,40],["l",115,40,170,40],["l",175,40,230,40],["l",235,40,375,40],["l",40,160,110,160],["l",115,160,170,160],["l",175,160,230,160],["l",235,160,375,160],["l",40,40,40,97],["l",34,97,46,97,"B"],["l",29,103,51,103],["l",40,103,40,160],["l",320,40,320,97],["l",309,97,331,97],["l",314,103,326,103,"B"],["l",320,103,320,160],["l",375,40,375,88],["c",375,100,12],["t",375,104,"V","ms"],["l",375,112,375,160],["c",320,40,2.5,"F"],["c",320,160,2.5,"F"],["l",90,40,66,40,"a"],["l",66,160,90,160,"a"],["t",54,92,"0.2 \u03A9",""],["t",54,118,"5 V",""],["t",304,106,"5 V","e"],["t",328,92,"0.2 \u03A9",""]]},o:["10 V","0 V","5 V","3 V"],c:1,s:`An ideal voltmeter draws no current, so the seven cells form one closed loop and the voltmeter reads the potential difference across the right-hand cell.
Read the cell directions: the long plate is positive. Following the arrows, the three cells on top, the one on the left, the one on the right and the three at the bottom all push the current the same way round, so their emfs add: 7 \xD7 5 = 35 V.
Each cell has an internal resistance of 0.2 \u03A9, so the loop resistance is 7 \xD7 0.2 = 1.4 \u03A9.
Current in the loop: I = 35/1.4 = 25 A.
The voltmeter reads the terminal voltage of the right-hand cell: V = E \u2212 Ir = 5 \u2212 25 \xD7 0.2.
V = 5 \u2212 5 = 0 V.
So the reading is 0 V, option (b).`},{pn:170,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 27 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cpot",st:"Galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"Three voltmeters, all having different internal resistances are joined as shown in figure. When some potential difference is applied across A and B, their readings are V\u2081, V\u2082 and V\u2083. Choose the correct option.",fig:{w:320,h:150,alt:"Voltmeters V1 and V2 in series on the upper branch, voltmeter V3 on the lower branch in parallel with them, between A and B",d:[["c",20,75,3,"F"],["l",23,75,60,75],["c",60,75,2.5,"F"],["l",60,40,60,110],["l",60,40,107,40],["c",120,40,12],["t",120,44,"V\u2081","ms"],["l",133,40,167,40],["c",180,40,12],["t",180,44,"V\u2082","ms"],["l",193,40,260,40],["l",60,110,147,110],["c",160,110,12],["t",160,114,"V\u2083","ms"],["l",173,110,260,110],["l",260,40,260,110],["c",260,75,2.5,"F"],["l",260,75,297,75],["c",300,75,3,"F"],["t",20,61,"A","m"],["t",300,61,"B","m"]]},o:["V\u2081 \u2260 V\u2083 \u2212 V\u2082","V\u2081 = V\u2082","V\u2081 + V\u2082 = V\u2083","V\u2081 + V\u2082 > V\u2083"],c:2,s:`A voltmeter reads the potential difference between its own two terminals.
V\u2081 and V\u2082 are in series, so together they connect point A to point B. The potential difference across this series pair is V\u2081 + V\u2082.
V\u2083 is joined directly between A and B, so it reads the full potential difference between A and B.
Both paths join the same two points, so the potential difference across them is the same: V\u2081 + V\u2082 = V\u2083.
Note: since the internal resistances are different, V\u2081 and V\u2082 are not equal (the larger resistance takes the larger share), so (b) is wrong.
The correct option is (c).`},{pn:171,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ckir",st:"Kirchhoff's laws",q:"Figure shows a part of an electric circuit. The potentials at points a, b and c are 30 V, 12 V and 2 V respectively. The current through the 20 \u03A9 resistor will be,",fig:{w:290,h:160,alt:"Part of a circuit: point a joined through 10 ohm to a junction, which splits into 20 ohm leading to b and 30 ohm leading to c",d:[["c",30,85,3,"F"],["p","M30 85 L52 85 L55 80 L61 90 L67 80 L73 90 L79 80 L85 90 L88 85 L110 85"],["c",110,85,2.5,"F"],["l",110,85,110,40],["l",110,85,110,130],["p","M110 40 L152 40 L155 35 L161 45 L167 35 L173 45 L179 35 L185 45 L188 40 L230 40"],["p","M110 130 L152 130 L155 125 L161 135 L167 125 L173 135 L179 125 L185 135 L188 130 L230 130"],["l",230,40,240,40],["l",230,130,240,130],["c",243,40,3,"F"],["c",243,130,3,"F"],["t",30,72,"a","m"],["t",250,36,"b",""],["t",250,136,"c",""],["t",70,74,"10 \u03A9","m"],["t",170,30,"20 \u03A9","m"],["t",170,120,"30 \u03A9","m"]]},o:["0.2 A","1.0 A","0.6 A","0.4 A"],c:3,s:`Let the potential of the junction (the point where the three resistors meet) be x volts. The current coming in through 10 \u03A9 must equal the currents going out through 20 \u03A9 and 30 \u03A9 (Kirchhoff's junction law).
Current in from a: (30 \u2212 x)/10. Current out to b: (x \u2212 12)/20. Current out to c: (x \u2212 2)/30.
Equate: (30 \u2212 x)/10 = (x \u2212 12)/20 + (x \u2212 2)/30.
Multiply by 60 to clear the fractions: 6(30 \u2212 x) = 3(x \u2212 12) + 2(x \u2212 2), so 180 \u2212 6x = 5x \u2212 40.
220 = 11x, so x = 20 V.
Current through the 20 \u03A9 resistor: (20 \u2212 12)/20 = 0.4 A.
So the current is 0.4 A, option (d).`},{pn:172,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"crc",st:"Charging and discharging of a capacitor",q:"In this figure, the resistance of the coil of galvanometer G is 2 \u03A9. The emf of the cell is 4 V. The ratio of potential difference across C\u2081 and C\u2082 is",fig:{w:300,h:240,alt:"Bridge with 6 ohm between A and B, capacitor C2 between B and D, capacitor C1 between A and C, 8 ohm between C and D, galvanometer G between B and C, and a 4 V cell joined across A and D",d:[["c",150,30,2.5,"F"],["c",60,100,2.5,"F"],["c",240,100,2.5,"F"],["c",150,170,2.5,"F"],["p","M60 100 L90.8 76.1 L90.1 70.3 L101 74.5 L99.6 62.9 L110.4 67.1 L109 55.5 L119.9 59.7 L119.2 53.9 L150 30"],["l",60,100,102.6,133.2],["l",107.4,136.8,150,170],["l",108.2,126.1,97.1,140.3],["l",112.9,129.7,101.8,143.9],["l",150,30,192.6,63.2],["l",197.4,66.8,240,100],["l",198.2,56.1,187.1,70.3],["l",202.9,59.7,191.8,73.9],["p","M240 100 L209.2 123.9 L209.9 129.7 L199 125.5 L200.4 137.1 L189.6 132.9 L191 144.5 L180.1 140.3 L180.8 146.1 L150 170"],["l",150,30,150,88],["c",150,100,12],["t",150,104,"G","ms"],["l",150,112,150,170],["l",60,100,20,100],["l",20,100,20,210],["l",20,210,140,210],["l",140,199,140,221],["l",145,204,145,216,"B"],["l",145,210,280,210],["l",280,210,280,100],["l",280,100,240,100],["l",110,210,86,210,"a"],["t",60,92,"A","e"],["t",150,20,"B","m"],["t",248,98,"D",""],["t",150,188,"C","m"],["t",88,52,"6 \u03A9","e"],["t",98,152,"C\u2081","e"],["t",198,52,"C\u2082",""],["t",205,162,"8 \u03A9",""],["t",142,236,"4 V","m"]]},o:["(4)/(5)","1","(5)/(4)","(3)/(4)"],c:0,s:`In the steady state the capacitors are fully charged and carry no current, so the only path for current from the cell is A \u2192 6 \u03A9 \u2192 B \u2192 G \u2192 C \u2192 8 \u03A9 \u2192 D.
Total resistance = 6 + 2 + 8 = 16 \u03A9, so I = 4/16 = 0.25 A.
Potential drops: across 6 \u03A9: 0.25 \xD7 6 = 1.5 V; across G: 0.25 \xD7 2 = 0.5 V; across 8 \u03A9: 0.25 \xD7 8 = 2 V.
C\u2081 is connected between A and C, so its potential difference is V_AC = 1.5 + 0.5 = 2 V.
C\u2082 is connected between B and D, so its potential difference is V_BD = 0.5 + 2 = 2.5 V.
Ratio V\u2081 : V\u2082 = 2/2.5 = 4/5.
So the ratio is 4/5, option (a).`},{pn:173,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 13 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"crc",st:"Charging and discharging of a capacitor",q:"In the network shown below, the charge accumulated in the capacitor in steady state will be",fig:{w:300,h:195,alt:"Three branches in parallel: a 3 V cell in series with 4 ohm; a 4 microfarad capacitor in series with 6 ohm; and a 6 ohm resistor",d:[["l",40,40,40,160],["l",260,40,260,160],["l",40,40,100,40],["l",100,29,100,51],["l",105,34,105,46,"B"],["l",105,40,170,40],["p","M170 40 L197 40 L200 35 L206 45 L212 35 L218 45 L224 35 L230 45 L233 40 L260 40"],["l",40,90,100,90],["l",100,81,100,99],["l",106,81,106,99],["l",106,90,170,90],["p","M170 90 L197 90 L200 85 L206 95 L212 85 L218 95 L224 85 L230 95 L233 90 L260 90"],["l",40,160,130,160],["p","M130 160 L140 160 L143.3 155 L150 165 L156.7 155 L163.3 165 L170 155 L176.7 165 L180 160 L190 160"],["l",190,160,260,160],["c",40,90,2.5,"F"],["c",260,90,2.5,"F"],["t",102,26,"3 V","m"],["t",215,32,"4 \u03A9","m"],["t",103,112,"4 \u03BCF","m"],["t",215,82,"6 \u03A9","m"],["t",160,182,"6 \u03A9","m"]]},o:["4.8 \u03BCC","12 \u03BCC","7.2 \u03BCC","10.3 \u03BCC"],c:2,s:`In steady state no current flows in the capacitor branch, so the 6 \u03A9 resistor in series with the capacitor has no voltage drop across it.
The current flows only in the loop made by the 3 V cell, the 4 \u03A9 resistor and the lower 6 \u03A9 resistor: I = 3/(4 + 6) = 0.3 A.
Voltage across the lower 6 \u03A9: V = 0.3 \xD7 6 = 1.8 V.
The capacitor branch is connected across the same two junction points, so the capacitor has this same voltage, 1.8 V.
Charge Q = CV = 4 \u03BCF \xD7 1.8 V = 7.2 \u03BCC.
So the charge is 7.2 \u03BCC, option (c).`},{pn:174,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ccel",st:"EMF and internal resistance",q:"A cell of emf 90 V is connected across series combination of two resistors each of 100 \u03A9 resistance. A voltmeter of resistance 400 \u03A9 is used to measure the potential difference across each resistor. The reading of the voltmeter will be",fig:{w:340,h:190,alt:"A 90 V cell in series with two 100 ohm resistors; a voltmeter of resistance 400 ohm is joined across the first resistor",d:[["l",40,40,40,85],["l",29,89,51,89],["l",34,95,46,95,"B"],["l",40,95,40,150],["l",40,150,300,150],["l",300,40,300,150],["l",40,40,70,40],["p","M70 40 L87 40 L90 35 L96 45 L102 35 L108 45 L114 35 L120 45 L123 40 L140 40"],["l",140,40,200,40],["p","M200 40 L217 40 L220 35 L226 45 L232 35 L238 45 L244 35 L250 45 L253 40 L270 40"],["l",270,40,300,40],["c",70,40,2.5,"F"],["c",140,40,2.5,"F"],["l",70,40,70,95],["l",70,95,93,95],["c",105,95,12],["t",105,99,"V","ms"],["l",117,95,140,95],["l",140,95,140,40],["t",30,96,"90 V","e"],["t",105,26,"100 \u03A9","m"],["t",235,26,"100 \u03A9","m"],["t",105,125,"400 \u03A9","m"]]},o:["45 V","40 V","80 V","90 V"],c:1,s:`Note: the book prints this question without the voltmeter's resistance; it is completed here with the original wording (400 \u03A9), the only value that gives one of the options.
The voltmeter is not ideal: connected across one 100 \u03A9 resistor it is in parallel with it, and the combination replaces that resistor (see the equivalent circuit).
Parallel part: R' = (400 \xD7 100)/(400 + 100) = 80 \u03A9.
Total resistance: R = 80 + 100 = 180 \u03A9, so the current is I = 90/180 = 0.5 A.
The voltmeter reads the potential difference across the parallel part: V = I R' = 0.5 \xD7 80 = 40 V.
So the reading is 40 V, option (b).`,sfig:{w:340,h:190,alt:"Equivalent circuit: a 90 V cell in series with 80 ohm (the 400 ohm voltmeter in parallel with 100 ohm) and the other 100 ohm; a 0.5 ampere current flows",d:[["l",40,40,40,85],["l",29,89,51,89],["l",34,95,46,95,"B"],["l",40,95,40,150],["l",40,150,300,150],["l",300,40,300,150],["l",40,40,70,40],["p","M70 40 L87 40 L90 35 L96 45 L102 35 L108 45 L114 35 L120 45 L123 40 L140 40"],["l",140,40,200,40],["p","M200 40 L217 40 L220 35 L226 45 L232 35 L238 45 L244 35 L250 45 L253 40 L270 40"],["l",270,40,300,40],["l",160,40,185,40,"a"],["t",30,96,"90 V","e"],["t",105,26,"80 \u03A9","m"],["t",235,26,"100 \u03A9","m"],["t",172,180,"I = 0.5 A","m"]]}},{pn:175,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ccmb",st:"Series and parallel combinations",q:"A current of 15 mA flows in the circuit as shown in figure. The value of potential difference between the points A and B will be",fig:{w:320,h:205,alt:"A 15 mA current enters at A through 5 kilo-ohm, then 10 kilo-ohm and 5 kilo-ohm in parallel, then 10 kilo-ohm to B",d:[["l",40,40,40,170],["p","M40 40 L115 40 L118.3 35 L125 45 L131.7 35 L138.3 45 L145 35 L151.7 45 L155 40 L230 40"],["p","M40 90 L115 90 L118.3 85 L125 95 L131.7 85 L138.3 95 L145 85 L151.7 95 L155 90 L230 90"],["l",230,40,230,110],["p","M230 110 L230 115 L235 118.3 L225 125 L235 131.7 L225 138.3 L235 145 L225 151.7 L230 155 L230 160"],["c",230,168,3,"F"],["l",40,170,60,170],["p","M40 170 L57 170 L60 165 L66 175 L72 165 L78 175 L84 165 L90 175 L93 170 L110 170"],["l",110,170,172,170],["l",150,170,128,170,"a"],["c",175,170,3,"F"],["c",40,90,2.5,"F"],["c",230,90,2.5,"F"],["t",135,28,"10 k\u03A9","m"],["t",135,78,"5 k\u03A9","m"],["t",240,140,"10 k\u03A9",""],["t",75,190,"5 k\u03A9","m"],["t",139,158,"15 mA","m"],["t",175,190,"A","m"],["t",230,190,"B","m"]]},o:["50 V","75 V","150 V","275 V"],c:3,s:`The 15 mA current from A passes through the 5 k\u03A9 resistor, then through the pair (10 k\u03A9 and 5 k\u03A9) in parallel, then through the 10 k\u03A9 resistor to B, so three parts are in series.
The two horizontal branches share the same two junctions, so they are in parallel: (10 \xD7 5)/(10 + 5) = 10/3 k\u03A9.
Total resistance = 5 + 10/3 + 10 = 55/3 k\u03A9.
Potential difference V = I R = 15 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 A \xD7 (55/3) \xD7 10\xB3 \u03A9 = 15 \xD7 55/3 = 275 V.
Note: k\u03A9 and mA multiply to give volts directly (10\xB3 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 = 1).
So V_AB = 275 V, option (d).`},{pn:176,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ccel",st:"EMF and internal resistance",q:"Two sources of equal emfs are connected in series. This combination is connected to an external resistance R. The internal resistance of the two sources are r\u2081 and r\u2082 (r\u2081 > r\u2082). If the potential difference across the source of internal resistance r\u2081 is zero, then the value of R will be",o:["r\u2081 \u2212 r\u2082","r\u2081r\u2082/(r\u2081 + r\u2082)","(r\u2081 + r\u2082)/2","r\u2082 \u2212 r\u2081"],c:0,s:`Each source has emf E. In series the total emf is 2E and the total resistance is R + r\u2081 + r\u2082, so I = 2E/(R + r\u2081 + r\u2082).
The potential difference across a source (terminal voltage) is V = E \u2212 I r.
For the first source this is zero: E \u2212 I r\u2081 = 0, so I = E/r\u2081.
Equate the two expressions for I: 2E/(R + r\u2081 + r\u2082) = E/r\u2081.
So R + r\u2081 + r\u2082 = 2r\u2081.
R = r\u2081 \u2212 r\u2082 (this is positive because r\u2081 > r\u2082).
So R = r\u2081 \u2212 r\u2082, option (a).`},{pn:177,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ccel",st:"EMF and internal resistance",q:"Two cells of same emf but different internal resistances r\u2081 and r\u2082 are connected in series with a resistance R. The value of resistance R, for which the potential difference across second cell is zero is",o:["r\u2082 \u2212 r\u2081","r\u2081 \u2212 r\u2082","r\u2081","r\u2082"],c:0,s:`Let each cell have emf E. The current in the series circuit is I = 2E/(R + r\u2081 + r\u2082).
The potential difference across the second cell is V\u2082 = E \u2212 I r\u2082, and it is given to be zero, so I = E/r\u2082.
Equate: 2E/(R + r\u2081 + r\u2082) = E/r\u2082, which gives R + r\u2081 + r\u2082 = 2r\u2082.
R = r\u2082 \u2212 r\u2081.
Note: this needs r\u2082 > r\u2081 so that R is positive; the cell with the larger internal resistance is the one that can show zero terminal voltage.
So R = r\u2082 \u2212 r\u2081, option (a).`},{pn:178,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"crc",st:"Charging and discharging of a capacitor",q:"A capacitor is discharging through a resistor R. Consider in time t\u2081, the energy stored in the capacitor reduces to half of its initial value and in time t\u2082, the charge stored reduces to one eighth of its initial value. The ratio t\u2081/t\u2082 will be",o:["1/2","1/3","1/4","1/6"],c:3,s:`During discharge the charge falls as Q = Q\u2080e^(\u2212t/RC).
Energy stored is U = Q\xB2/(2C), so U = U\u2080e^(\u22122t/RC).
For the energy to become half: e^(\u22122t\u2081/RC) = 1/2, so 2t\u2081/RC = ln 2 and t\u2081 = (RC/2) ln 2.
For the charge to become one eighth: e^(\u2212t\u2082/RC) = 1/8, so t\u2082 = RC ln 8 = 3RC ln 2.
Ratio: t\u2081/t\u2082 = [(RC/2) ln 2]/[3RC ln 2] = 1/6.
So t\u2081/t\u2082 = 1/6, option (d).`},{pn:179,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ccel",st:"Grouping of cells",q:"The combination of two identical cells, whether connected in series or parallel combination provides the same current through an external resistance of 2 \u03A9. The value of internal resistance of each cell is",o:["2 \u03A9","4 \u03A9","6 \u03A9","8 \u03A9"],c:0,s:`Let each cell have emf E and internal resistance r, and the external resistance be R = 2 \u03A9.
Series: emf 2E, total resistance R + 2r, so I_s = 2E/(2 + 2r).
Parallel: emf E, internal resistance r/2, total resistance R + r/2, so I_p = E/(2 + r/2).
Set the two currents equal: 2/(2 + 2r) = 1/(2 + r/2).
Cross-multiply: 2(2 + r/2) = 2 + 2r, so 4 + r = 2 + 2r.
r = 2 \u03A9.
Note: in general the two groupings give the same current when R = r, so the internal resistance equals the external resistance, 2 \u03A9.
So r = 2 \u03A9, option (a).`},{pn:180,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"crc",st:"Charging and discharging of a capacitor",q:"Calculate the amount of charge on capacitor of 4 \u03BCF. The internal resistance of battery is 1 \u03A9.",fig:{w:340,h:205,alt:"A 5 V battery of internal resistance 1 ohm in the middle branch; the top branch has a 4 microfarad capacitor, 6 ohm and two 2 microfarad capacitors in parallel; the bottom branch has 4 ohm",d:[["l",40,40,40,170],["l",300,40,300,170],["l",40,100,90,100],["l",90,89,90,111],["l",95,94,95,106,"B"],["l",95,100,300,100],["l",40,170,160,170],["p","M130 170 L150 170 L153.3 165 L160 175 L166.7 165 L173.3 175 L180 165 L186.7 175 L190 170 L210 170"],["l",210,170,300,170],["c",40,100,2.5,"F"],["c",300,100,2.5,"F"],["l",40,40,70,40],["l",70,31,70,49],["l",76,31,76,49],["l",76,40,100,40],["p","M100 40 L117 40 L120 35 L126 45 L132 35 L138 45 L144 35 L150 45 L153 40 L170 40"],["l",170,40,190,40],["l",190,22,190,58],["l",250,22,250,58],["l",190,22,217,22],["l",217,13,217,31],["l",223,13,223,31],["l",223,22,250,22],["l",190,58,217,58],["l",217,49,217,67],["l",223,49,223,67],["l",223,58,250,58],["l",250,40,300,40],["c",190,40,2.5,"F"],["c",250,40,2.5,"F"],["t",73,26,"4 \u03BCF","m"],["t",135,28,"6 \u03A9","m"],["t",220,10,"2 \u03BCF","m"],["t",220,84,"2 \u03BCF","m"],["t",93,88,"5 V","m"],["t",170,190,"4 \u03A9","m"]]},o:["4 \u03BCC","8 \u03BCC","16 \u03BCC","zero"],c:1,s:`In steady state no current flows in the top branch (it contains capacitors), so the battery sends current only through the 4 \u03A9 resistor.
Current: I = E/(R + r) = 5/(4 + 1) = 1 A.
Voltage across the 4 \u03A9 resistor: V = 1 \xD7 4 = 4 V. The top branch is connected across the same two points, so it also has 4 V across it.
No current means no drop across the 6 \u03A9, so the whole 4 V is shared by the capacitors.
The two 2 \u03BCF capacitors in parallel make 2 + 2 = 4 \u03BCF. This is in series with the 4 \u03BCF capacitor: C = (4 \xD7 4)/(4 + 4) = 2 \u03BCF.
Charge on the series combination: Q = C V = 2 \u03BCF \xD7 4 V = 8 \u03BCC, and in a series chain every part carries this same charge.
So the 4 \u03BCF capacitor has 8 \u03BCC, option (b).`},{pn:181,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ccel",st:"Grouping of cells",q:"Five identical cells each of internal resistance 1 \u03A9 and emf 5 V are connected in series and in parallel with an external resistance R. For what value of R, current in series and parallel combination will remain the same?",o:["1 \u03A9","5 \u03A9","25 \u03A9","10 \u03A9"],c:0,s:`Series of 5 cells: emf = 5 \xD7 5 = 25 V, internal resistance = 5 \xD7 1 = 5 \u03A9, so I_s = 25/(R + 5).
Parallel of 5 cells: emf = 5 V, internal resistance = 1/5 = 0.2 \u03A9, so I_p = 5/(R + 0.2).
Set I_s = I_p: 25/(R + 5) = 5/(R + 0.2).
Cross-multiply: 25(R + 0.2) = 5(R + 5), so 25R + 5 = 5R + 25.
20R = 20, so R = 1 \u03A9.
Note: this is the usual result: both groupings give equal current when the external resistance equals the internal resistance of one cell (1 \u03A9).
So R = 1 \u03A9, option (a).`},{pn:182,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 20 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ckir",st:"Kirchhoff's laws",q:"The value of current in the 6 \u03A9 resistance is",fig:{w:340,h:185,alt:"Three branches between a top wire and a bottom wire: a 140 V cell with 20 ohm on the left, a 6 ohm resistor in the middle, and a 90 V cell with 5 ohm on the right",d:[["l",50,40,50,90],["l",39,90,61,90],["l",44,96,56,96,"B"],["l",50,96,50,160],["p","M50 40 L90 40 L93.3 35 L100 45 L106.7 35 L113.3 45 L120 35 L126.7 45 L130 40 L170 40"],["p","M170 40 L212 40 L215 35 L221 45 L227 35 L233 45 L239 35 L245 45 L248 40 L290 40"],["l",290,40,290,90],["l",279,90,301,90],["l",284,96,296,96,"B"],["l",290,96,290,160],["l",50,160,290,160],["p","M170 40 L170 80 L175 83.3 L165 90 L175 96.7 L165 103.3 L175 110 L165 116.7 L170 120 L170 160"],["c",170,40,2.5,"F"],["c",170,160,2.5,"F"],["t",30,98,"140 V","e"],["t",312,98,"90 V",""],["t",110,28,"20 \u03A9","m"],["t",230,28,"5 \u03A9","m"],["t",178,104,"6 \u03A9",""]]},o:["8 A","6 A","4 A","10 A"],c:3,s:`The three branches (the 140 V cell with 20 \u03A9, the 6 \u03A9 resistor, the 90 V cell with 5 \u03A9) join the same two points, so call the top junction potential V and the bottom wire 0 V.
Both cells push the top wire to a higher potential (their positive plates are on top). Current from the left cell into the junction: (140 \u2212 V)/20; from the right cell: (90 \u2212 V)/5.
These two currents together flow down the 6 \u03A9 resistor (Kirchhoff's junction law): (140 \u2212 V)/20 + (90 \u2212 V)/5 = V/6.
Multiply by 60: 3(140 \u2212 V) + 12(90 \u2212 V) = 10V, so 420 + 1080 \u2212 15V = 10V.
1500 = 25V, so V = 60 V.
Current in the 6 \u03A9 resistor: I = 60/6 = 10 A (check: 4 A + 6 A).
So the current is 10 A, option (d).`},{pn:183,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"crc",st:"Charging and discharging of a capacitor",q:"A capacitor C = 1 \u03BCF is suddenly connected to a battery of 100 volt through a resistance R = 100 \u03A9. The time taken for the capacitor to be charged to get 50 V is (Take ln 2 = 0.69)",fig:{w:340,h:175,alt:"A 100 V battery, a 100 ohm resistor and a 1 microfarad capacitor in series",d:[["l",40,40,40,87],["l",29,87,51,87],["l",34,93,46,93,"B"],["l",40,93,40,150],["p","M40 40 L125 40 L129.2 35 L137.5 45 L145.8 35 L154.2 45 L162.5 35 L170.8 45 L175 40 L260 40"],["l",260,40,260,87],["l",251,87,269,87],["l",251,93,269,93],["l",260,93,260,150],["l",40,150,260,150],["t",54,100,"100 V",""],["t",150,28,"R = 100 \u03A9","m"],["t",274,96,"C = 1 \u03BCF",""]]},o:["1.44 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 s","0.30 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 s","3.33 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 s","0.69 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 s"],c:3,s:`While a capacitor charges through a resistor, its voltage grows as V = V\u2080(1 \u2212 e^(\u2212t/RC)).
Here V\u2080 = 100 V and V = 50 V, so 50 = 100(1 \u2212 e^(\u2212t/RC)), which gives e^(\u2212t/RC) = 1/2.
Take the natural log: t/RC = ln 2, so t = RC ln 2.
RC = 100 \u03A9 \xD7 1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 F = 10\u207B\u2074 s.
t = 10\u207B\u2074 \xD7 0.69 = 0.69 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 s.
Note: this is the time to reach half of the final voltage, called the half-life of the charging, 0.69 RC.
So t = 0.69 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 s, option (d).`},{pn:184,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 24 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ccel",st:"EMF and internal resistance",q:"A cell E\u2081 of emf 6 V and internal resistance 2 \u03A9 is connected with another cell E\u2082 of emf 4 V and internal resistance 8 \u03A9 (as shown in the figure). The potential difference across points X and Y is",fig:{w:330,h:145,alt:"Two cells in series between P and Y with their polarities opposed: E1 of 6 V and 2 ohm between P and X, E2 of 4 V and 8 ohm between X and Y, and a plain wire from Y back to P",d:[["l",30,40,90,40],["l",90,29,90,51],["l",95,34,95,46,"B"],["l",95,40,240,40],["l",240,34,240,46,"B"],["l",245,29,245,51],["l",245,40,300,40],["l",300,40,300,120],["l",300,120,30,120],["l",30,120,30,40],["c",30,40,2.5,"F"],["c",170,40,2.5,"F"],["c",300,40,2.5,"F"],["t",30,28,"P","m"],["t",92,28,"E\u2081","m"],["t",170,28,"X","m"],["t",245,28,"E\u2082","m"],["t",300,28,"Y","m"],["t",92,64,"6 V, 2 \u03A9","m"],["t",245,64,"4 V, 8 \u03A9","m"]]},o:["10.0 V","5.6 V","3.6 V","2.0 V"],c:1,s:`The figure shows the two cells joined so that their emfs oppose each other (the positive plate of E\u2081 is on the P side, that of E\u2082 on the Y side), and the wire from Y to P closes the loop.
Net emf = 6 \u2212 4 = 2 V (E\u2081 is stronger and decides the direction). Total resistance = 2 + 8 = 10 \u03A9.
Current: I = 2/10 = 0.2 A. It flows through E\u2082 from its positive to its negative plate, so E\u2082 is being charged.
For a cell that is being charged, the potential difference across it is V = E + Ir.
V_XY = 4 + 0.2 \xD7 8 = 4 + 1.6 = 5.6 V.
Note: the printed figure label for E\u2082 looks like 7 V, but the question text says 4 V, and only 4 V gives an option (5.6 V); the answer is worked with 4 V.
So the potential difference is 5.6 V, option (b).`},{pn:185,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 3 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cpot",st:"Galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"Two resistors 400 \u03A9 and 800 \u03A9 are connected in series across a 6 V battery. The potential difference measured by a voltmeter of 10 k\u03A9 across 400 \u03A9 resistor is close to",o:["2 V","1.8 V","2.05 V","1.95 V"],c:3,s:`A real voltmeter of 10 k\u03A9 in parallel with the 400 \u03A9 resistor lowers the resistance of that part.
Parallel resistance: (400 \xD7 10000)/(400 + 10000) = 4 000 000/10 400 \u2248 384.6 \u03A9.
Total resistance in the circuit = 384.6 + 800 = 1184.6 \u03A9, so the battery current is 6/1184.6 \u2248 5.065 mA.
The voltmeter reads the voltage across the parallel part: V = 5.065 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 384.6 \u2248 1.948 V.
Note: without the voltmeter the drop would be exactly 2 V; the meter loads the circuit and reads slightly less.
So the reading is close to 1.95 V, option (d).`},{pn:186,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 5 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ckir",st:"Kirchhoff's laws",q:"In the circuit, given in the figure currents in different branches and value of one resistor are shown. Then potential at point B with respect to the point A is",fig:{w:350,h:180,alt:"Circuit with corners E, D, B on the top and A, C, F on the bottom: a resistor between E and D, a 2 V cell between D and B, a resistor between E and A with 1 A flowing down, a 1 V cell between A and C, a 2 ohm resistor between D and C, and a resistor between C and F with 2 A flowing towards F; B and F are joined by a wire",d:[["p","M60 40 L105 40 L108.3 35 L115 45 L121.7 35 L128.3 45 L135 35 L141.7 45 L145 40 L190 40"],["c",190,40,2.5,"F"],["l",190,40,235,40],["l",235,29,235,51],["l",240,34,240,46,"B"],["l",240,40,300,40],["l",300,40,300,140],["l",60,40,60,50],["p","M60 50 L60 70 L65 73.3 L55 80 L65 86.7 L55 93.3 L65 100 L55 106.7 L60 110 L60 130"],["l",60,130,60,140],["l",60,140,105,140],["l",105,129,105,151],["l",110,134,110,146,"B"],["l",110,140,190,140],["c",190,140,2.5,"F"],["p","M190 140 L225 140 L228.3 135 L235 145 L241.7 135 L248.3 145 L255 135 L261.7 145 L265 140 L300 140"],["p","M190 40 L190 70 L195 73.3 L185 80 L195 86.7 L185 93.3 L195 100 L185 106.7 L190 110 L190 140"],["l",60,52,60,68,"a"],["l",140,140,162,140,"a"],["l",262,140,280,140,"a"],["t",48,72,"1 A","e"],["t",237,28,"2 V","m"],["t",107,160,"1 V","m"],["t",280,160,"2 A","m"],["t",200,94,"2 \u03A9",""],["t",52,36,"E","e"],["t",190,28,"D","m"],["t",308,36,"B",""],["t",308,148,"F",""],["t",190,160,"C","m"],["t",52,148,"A","e"]]},o:["+2 V","\u22122 V","\u22121 V","+1 V"],c:3,s:`Find V_B \u2212 V_A by walking from A to B through C and D, adding the potential change of each part.
Current in the 2 \u03A9 resistor: 2 A leaves C towards F and 1 A arrives at C from the 1 V cell, so the remaining 1 A must come up from D through the 2 \u03A9 resistor: it flows D \u2192 C.
A \u2192 C through the 1 V cell: the current runs through the cell in the direction of its emf (long plate on the C side), so the potential rises: V_C \u2212 V_A = +1 V.
C \u2192 D through the 2 \u03A9 resistor: the current flows from D to C, so D is higher than C: V_D \u2212 V_C = I R = 1 \xD7 2 = +2 V.
D \u2192 B through the 2 V cell: the long (positive) plate is on the D side, so going from D to B we cross the cell from + to \u2212: V_B \u2212 V_D = \u22122 V.
Add: V_B \u2212 V_A = +1 + 2 \u2212 2 = +1 V.
So B is at +1 V with respect to A, option (d).`},{pn:187,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 6 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ckir",st:"Kirchhoff's laws",q:"In the figure shown, the current in the 10 V battery is close to",fig:{w:380,h:190,alt:"A rectangle: 5 ohm on the top left, 10 ohm vertical in the middle, a 10 V cell on the right side, a 20 V cell and 2 ohm on the bottom left and 4 ohm on the bottom right; the long plates are on the left bottom cell and on top of the right cell",d:[["l",40,40,40,150],["p","M40 40 L85 40 L88.3 35 L95 45 L101.7 35 L108.3 45 L115 35 L121.7 45 L125 40 L170 40"],["c",170,40,2.5,"F"],["l",170,40,320,40],["l",320,40,320,90],["l",309,90,331,90],["l",314,96,326,96,"B"],["l",320,96,320,150],["p","M170 40 L170 75 L175 78.3 L165 85 L175 91.7 L165 98.3 L175 105 L165 111.7 L170 115 L170 150"],["c",170,150,2.5,"F"],["l",40,150,90,150],["l",90,139,90,161],["l",95,144,95,156,"B"],["l",95,150,100,150],["p","M95 150 L112.5 150 L115.8 145 L122.5 155 L129.2 145 L135.8 155 L142.5 145 L149.2 155 L152.5 150 L170 150"],["p","M170 150 L225 150 L228.3 145 L235 155 L241.7 145 L248.3 155 L255 145 L261.7 155 L265 150 L320 150"],["t",105,28,"5 \u03A9","m"],["t",180,98,"10 \u03A9",""],["t",342,98,"10 V",""],["t",90,126,"20 V","m"],["t",133,172,"2 \u03A9","m"],["t",245,172,"4 \u03A9","m"]]},o:["0.71 A from positive to negative terminal","0.42 A from positive to negative terminal","0.21 A from positive to negative terminal","0.36 A from negative to positive terminal"],c:2,s:`Take the bottom junction of the 10 \u03A9 resistor as 0 V and let the top junction be at x volts. Three branches meet at the top junction.
Left branch (20 V cell, 2 \u03A9 and 5 \u03A9): the cell pushes the top up by 20 V, so the current flowing up into the junction is (20 \u2212 x)/(5 + 2) = (20 \u2212 x)/7.
Right branch (10 V cell and 4 \u03A9): the positive plate of the 10 V cell is at the top, so the current it would send into the junction is (10 \u2212 x)/4.
Middle branch: current down through 10 \u03A9 is x/10.
Kirchhoff's junction law: (20 \u2212 x)/7 + (10 \u2212 x)/4 = x/10.
Multiply by 140: 20(20 \u2212 x) + 35(10 \u2212 x) = 14x, so 750 \u2212 55x = 14x, 69x = 750, so x \u2248 10.87 V.
Current from the 10 V cell: (10 \u2212 10.87)/4 \u2248 \u22120.217 A. The minus sign means the current actually flows into the positive terminal, i.e. through the battery from the positive to the negative terminal.
So the current is about 0.21 A from positive to negative terminal, option (c).`},{pn:188,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 7 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ccmb",st:"Equivalent resistance of networks",q:"The current I\u2081 (in A) flowing through 1 \u03A9 resistor in the following circuit is",fig:{w:360,h:220,alt:"A 1 volt cell drives two branches in parallel: one has two 1 ohm resistors in parallel followed by a 2 ohm resistor, the other has a single 2 ohm resistor; I1 flows through the upper 1 ohm resistor",d:[["c",40,95,2.5,"F"],["l",40,95,40,60],["l",40,60,90,60],["l",90,40,90,80],["c",90,60,2.5,"F"],["p","M90 40 L127 40 L130 35 L136 45 L142 35 L148 45 L154 35 L160 45 L163 40 L200 40"],["p","M90 80 L127 80 L130 75 L136 85 L142 75 L148 85 L154 75 L160 85 L163 80 L200 80"],["l",200,40,200,80],["c",200,60,2.5,"F"],["p","M200 60 L232 60 L235 55 L241 65 L247 55 L253 65 L259 55 L265 65 L268 60 L300 60"],["l",300,60,300,95],["c",300,95,2.5,"F"],["l",40,95,40,140],["p","M40 140 L150 140 L153.3 135 L160 145 L166.7 135 L173.3 145 L180 135 L186.7 145 L190 140 L300 140"],["l",300,140,300,95],["l",300,95,340,95],["l",340,95,340,190],["l",340,190,175,190],["l",170,179,170,201],["l",175,184,175,196,"B"],["l",170,190,10,190],["l",10,190,10,95],["l",10,95,40,95],["l",98,40,116,40,"a"],["t",98,28,"I\u2081","m"],["t",145,28,"1 \u03A9","m"],["t",145,102,"1 \u03A9","m"],["t",250,50,"2 \u03A9","m"],["t",170,128,"2 \u03A9","m"],["t",172,170,"1 V","m"]]},o:["0.4","0.25","0.2","0.5"],c:2,s:`The 1 V cell is joined across two parallel branches, so each branch has the full 1 V across it (the cell has no internal resistance).
Upper branch: the two 1 \u03A9 resistors are in parallel, 1 \u03A9 \u2225 1 \u03A9 = 0.5 \u03A9, and this is in series with 2 \u03A9, so the branch resistance is 0.5 + 2 = 2.5 \u03A9.
Current in the upper branch: 1/2.5 = 0.4 A.
This 0.4 A reaches the pair of equal 1 \u03A9 resistors and splits equally between them.
I\u2081 = 0.4/2 = 0.2 A.
Note: the lower 2 \u03A9 branch carries 1/2 = 0.5 A, but it does not affect I\u2081.
So I\u2081 = 0.2 A, option (c).`},{pn:189,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ckir",st:"Wheatstone bridge",q:"In the given circuit diagram, a wire is joining points B and D. The current in this wire is",fig:{w:360,h:215,alt:"Bridge: A to B is 1 ohm, B to C is 2 ohm, A to D is 4 ohm, D to C is 3 ohm, with a wire joining B and D; a 20 V cell is connected between A and C",d:[["l",60,60,60,130],["p","M60 60 L92 60 L95 55 L101 65 L107 55 L113 65 L119 55 L125 65 L128 60 L160 60"],["p","M160 60 L192 60 L195 55 L201 65 L207 55 L213 65 L219 55 L225 65 L228 60 L260 60"],["l",260,60,260,130],["p","M60 130 L92 130 L95 125 L101 135 L107 125 L113 135 L119 125 L125 135 L128 130 L160 130"],["p","M160 130 L192 130 L195 125 L201 135 L207 125 L213 135 L219 125 L225 135 L228 130 L260 130"],["l",160,60,160,130],["c",160,60,2.5,"F"],["c",160,130,2.5,"F"],["l",60,95,20,95],["c",60,95,2.5,"F"],["l",20,95,20,180],["l",20,180,150,180],["l",150,169,150,191],["l",155,174,155,186,"B"],["l",155,180,330,180],["l",330,180,330,95],["l",330,95,260,95],["c",260,95,2.5,"F"],["t",60,48,"A","m"],["t",160,48,"B","m"],["t",264,48,"C",""],["t",160,148,"D","m"],["t",110,52,"1 \u03A9","m"],["t",210,52,"2 \u03A9","m"],["t",110,152,"4 \u03A9","m"],["t",210,152,"3 \u03A9","m"],["t",152,200,"20 V","m"]]},o:["0.4 A","zero","2 A","4 A"],c:2,s:`Because the wire joins B and D, these two points are at the same potential, so the circuit is (1 \u03A9 \u2225 4 \u03A9) in series with (2 \u03A9 \u2225 3 \u03A9).
1 \u03A9 \u2225 4 \u03A9 = (1 \xD7 4)/5 = 0.8 \u03A9 and 2 \u03A9 \u2225 3 \u03A9 = (2 \xD7 3)/5 = 1.2 \u03A9, so the total is 2 \u03A9.
Battery current: I = 20/2 = 10 A.
Voltage across the left pair (A to B/D): 10 \xD7 0.8 = 8 V; across the right pair (B/D to C): 10 \xD7 1.2 = 12 V.
Currents arriving at the BD joint: through 1 \u03A9: 8/1 = 8 A and through 4 \u03A9: 8/4 = 2 A, which is 10 A in total.
Currents leaving the joint: through 2 \u03A9: 12/2 = 6 A and through 3 \u03A9: 12/3 = 4 A, which is also 10 A.
Note: the bridge is not balanced (1/4 is not 2/3), so the wire carries a current. At B, 8 A arrives and 6 A leaves, so 2 A must flow along the wire from B to D.
So the current in the wire is 2 A, option (c).`},{pn:190,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ckir",st:"Kirchhoff's laws",q:"For the circuit shown, with R\u2081 = 1.0 \u03A9, R\u2082 = 2.0 \u03A9, E\u2081 = 2 V and E\u2082 = E\u2083 = 4 V, the potential difference between the points a and b is approximately (in V)",fig:{w:390,h:230,alt:"Circuit with two nodes a (top) and b (bottom): left branch with E1 and two R1; middle branch with R2 and E2; right branch with E3 and R1 and the top R1; the positive plate of each cell is the upper one",d:[["l",40,40,40,100],["l",29,100,51,100],["l",34,106,46,106,"B"],["l",40,106,40,190],["p","M40 40 L100 40 L103.3 35 L110 45 L116.7 35 L123.3 45 L130 35 L136.7 45 L140 40 L200 40"],["c",200,40,2.5,"F"],["p","M200 40 L250 40 L253.3 35 L260 45 L266.7 35 L273.3 45 L280 35 L286.7 45 L290 40 L340 40"],["l",340,40,340,70],["l",329,70,351,70],["l",334,76,346,76,"B"],["l",340,76,340,100],["p","M340 100 L340 125 L345 128.3 L335 135 L345 141.7 L335 148.3 L345 155 L335 161.7 L340 165 L340 190"],["l",340,190,200,190],["p","M40 190 L100 190 L103.3 185 L110 195 L116.7 185 L123.3 195 L130 185 L136.7 195 L140 190 L200 190"],["c",200,190,2.5,"F"],["l",200,40,200,70],["p","M200 70 L200 74.5 L205 77.5 L195 83.5 L205 89.5 L195 95.5 L205 101.5 L195 107.5 L200 110.5 L200 115"],["l",200,115,200,135],["l",189,135,211,135],["l",194,141,206,141,"B"],["l",200,141,200,190],["t",120,28,"R\u2081","m"],["t",270,28,"R\u2081","m"],["t",120,212,"R\u2081","m"],["t",208,96,"R\u2082",""],["t",219,140,"E\u2082",""],["t",58,108,"E\u2081",""],["t",358,76,"E\u2083",""],["t",350,150,"R\u2081",""],["t",200,28,"a","m"],["t",200,210,"b","m"],["l",200,62,200,50,"a"]]},o:["2.7","3.7","2.3","3.3"],c:3,s:`There are three branches between the points a and b. Take b as 0 V and let a be at x volts. Each cell has its positive (long) plate at the top, so every branch tries to raise a.
Left branch: E\u2081 = 2 V with two R\u2081 in series (1 + 1 = 2 \u03A9), so the current into a is (2 \u2212 x)/2.
Middle branch: E\u2082 = 4 V with R\u2082 = 2 \u03A9, so the current into a is (4 \u2212 x)/2.
Right branch: E\u2083 = 4 V with two R\u2081 in series (2 \u03A9), so the current into a is (4 \u2212 x)/2.
No current is stored at a, so the three currents add up to zero (Kirchhoff's junction law): (2 \u2212 x)/2 + (4 \u2212 x)/2 + (4 \u2212 x)/2 = 0.
So 2 \u2212 x + 4 \u2212 x + 4 \u2212 x = 0, giving 10 = 3x and x = 10/3 \u2248 3.3 V.
So V_ab \u2248 3.3 V, option (d).`},{pn:191,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"crc",st:"Charging and discharging of a capacitor",q:"Determine the charge on the capacitor in the following circuit.",fig:{w:400,h:190,alt:"A 72 V cell drives 6 ohm and 2 ohm in series along the top; 4 ohm, 10 ohm and a 10 microfarad capacitor are each connected between the top and bottom wires",d:[["l",40,40,40,87],["l",29,87,51,87],["l",34,93,46,93,"B"],["l",40,93,40,160],["p","M40 40 L62 40 L65 35 L71 45 L77 35 L83 45 L89 35 L95 45 L98 40 L120 40"],["c",120,40,2.5,"F"],["p","M120 40 L157 40 L160 35 L166 45 L172 35 L178 45 L184 35 L190 45 L193 40 L230 40"],["c",230,40,2.5,"F"],["l",230,40,330,40],["l",330,40,330,92],["l",321,92,339,92],["l",321,98,339,98],["l",330,98,330,160],["l",40,160,330,160],["p","M120 40 L120 80 L125 83.3 L115 90 L125 96.7 L115 103.3 L125 110 L115 116.7 L120 120 L120 160"],["p","M230 40 L230 80 L235 83.3 L225 90 L235 96.7 L225 103.3 L235 110 L225 116.7 L230 120 L230 160"],["c",120,160,2.5,"F"],["c",230,160,2.5,"F"],["t",28,96,"72 V","e"],["t",80,28,"6 \u03A9","m"],["t",175,28,"2 \u03A9","m"],["t",128,104,"4 \u03A9",""],["t",238,104,"10 \u03A9",""],["t",342,100,"10 \u03BCF",""]]},o:["60 \u03BCC","10 \u03BCC","2 \u03BCC","200 \u03BCC"],c:3,s:`In steady state the capacitor passes no current, so it is just a voltmeter across the 10 \u03A9 resistor: its voltage equals the voltage across 10 \u03A9.
The current from the cell goes through 6 \u03A9, then splits between the 4 \u03A9 branch and the (2 \u03A9 + 10 \u03A9) branch.
The lower branch is 2 + 10 = 12 \u03A9 in series. It is in parallel with 4 \u03A9: (4 \xD7 12)/(4 + 12) = 3 \u03A9.
Total resistance = 6 + 3 = 9 \u03A9, so I = 72/9 = 8 A.
Voltage across the parallel part = 8 \xD7 3 = 24 V, so the current in the 12 \u03A9 branch is 24/12 = 2 A.
Voltage across 10 \u03A9 = 2 \xD7 10 = 20 V, which is also the capacitor voltage.
Q = CV = 10 \u03BCF \xD7 20 V = 200 \u03BCC.
So the charge is 200 \u03BCC, option (d).`},{pn:192,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ckir",st:"Kirchhoff's laws",q:"When the switch S, in the circuit shown is closed, then the value of current i will be",fig:{w:340,h:225,alt:"Junction C joined to A (20 V) through 2 ohm, to B (10 V) through 4 ohm, and to earth (V = 0) through a 2 ohm resistor and a switch S; current i flows down from C",d:[["c",40,50,3,"F"],["l",46,50,64,50,"a"],["p","M60 50 L95 50 L98.3 45 L105 55 L111.7 45 L118.3 55 L125 45 L131.7 55 L135 50 L170 50"],["c",170,50,2.5,"F"],["p","M170 50 L205 50 L208.3 45 L215 55 L221.7 45 L228.3 55 L235 45 L241.7 55 L245 50 L280 50"],["c",300,50,3,"F"],["l",292,50,278,50,"a"],["l",170,50,170,58],["l",170,58,170,74,"a"],["p","M170 76 L170 93 L175 96.3 L165 103 L175 109.7 L165 116.3 L175 123 L165 129.7 L170 133 L170 150"],["l",170,150,170,164],["c",170,164,2.5,"F"],["l",170,164,182,182],["c",170,190,2.5,"F"],["l",170,190,170,200],["l",155,200,185,200],["l",160,206,180,206],["l",165,212,175,212],["t",40,36,"20 V","m"],["t",40,68,"A","m"],["t",300,36,"10 V","m"],["t",300,68,"B","m"],["t",170,38,"C","m"],["t",115,68,"2 \u03A9","m"],["t",225,68,"4 \u03A9","m"],["t",180,72,"i",""],["t",180,118,"2 \u03A9",""],["t",192,184,"S",""],["t",194,214,"V = 0",""],["t",58,40,"i\u2081",""],["t",278,40,"i\u2082",""]]},o:["5 A","3 A","2 A","4 A"],c:0,s:`With the switch closed, point C is connected to the earth (0 V) through the lower 2 \u03A9 resistor. Let the potential of C be x volts.
Current arriving from A: i\u2081 = (20 \u2212 x)/2. Current arriving from B: i\u2082 = (10 \u2212 x)/4.
Kirchhoff's junction law at C: i = i\u2081 + i\u2082, and the current down the lower resistor is i = x/2.
So x/2 = (20 \u2212 x)/2 + (10 \u2212 x)/4.
Multiply by 4: 2x = 2(20 \u2212 x) + (10 \u2212 x), so 2x = 50 \u2212 3x.
5x = 50, so x = 10 V.
Current through the 2 \u03A9 resistor to earth: i = 10/2 = 5 A (check: i\u2081 = 5 A, i\u2082 = 0).
So i = 5 A, option (a).`},{pn:193,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ccmb",st:"Series and parallel combinations",q:"In the given circuit the internal resistance of the 18 V cell is negligible. If R\u2081 = 400 \u03A9, R\u2083 = 100 \u03A9 and R\u2084 = 500 \u03A9 and the reading of an ideal voltmeter across R\u2084 is 5 V, then the value of R\u2082 will be",o:["450 \u03A9","550 \u03A9","230 \u03A9","300 \u03A9"],c:3,s:`R\u2083 and R\u2084 are in series, and this pair is in parallel with R\u2082; the whole combination is in series with R\u2081 (see the second figure).
The voltmeter reads 5 V across R\u2084 = 500 \u03A9, so the current in R\u2083 and R\u2084 is 5/500 = 0.01 A.
Voltage across the pair: 0.01 \xD7 (100 + 500) = 6 V, and R\u2082 has the same 6 V because it is in parallel with the pair.
The cell gives 18 V, so the voltage across R\u2081 is 18 \u2212 6 = 12 V and the main current is 12/400 = 0.03 A.
The main current splits: 0.03 \u2212 0.01 = 0.02 A goes through R\u2082.
R\u2082 = 6/0.02 = 300 \u03A9, option (d).`,fig:{w:340,h:190,alt:"A 18 V cell drives R1 in series with a parallel pair: R2 on one path and R3 followed by R4 on the other",d:[["l",40,150,40,70],["l",40,70,63,70],["l",97,70,120,70],["p","M63 70L68.7 75L74.3 65L80 75L85.7 65L91.3 75L97 70"],["l",120,70,120,25],["l",120,25,145.5,25],["l",179.5,25,205,25],["p","M145.5 25L151.2 30L156.8 20L162.5 30L168.2 20L173.8 30L179.5 25"],["l",205,25,235.5,25],["l",269.5,25,300,25],["p","M235.5 25L241.2 30L246.8 20L252.5 30L258.2 20L263.8 30L269.5 25"],["l",300,25,300,150],["l",120,70,190,70],["l",230,70,300,70],["p","M190 70L196.7 75L203.3 65L210 75L216.7 65L223.3 75L230 70"],["l",40,150,154,150],["l",154,150,167,150],["l",173,150,186,150],["l",167,140,167,160],["l",173,145,173,155,"B"],["l",186,150,300,150],["c",120,70,2.6,"F"],["c",300,70,2.6,"F"],["t",80,58,"R\u2081","m"],["t",162,13,"R\u2083","m"],["t",252,13,"R\u2084","m"],["t",210,58,"R\u2082","m"],["t",170,176,"18 V","m"]]},sfig:{w:340,h:190,alt:"Equivalent circuit: the 18 V cell, R1 of 400 ohm, and R2 in parallel with 600 ohm (R3 plus R4)",d:[["l",40,150,40,70],["l",40,70,63,70],["l",97,70,120,70],["p","M63 70L68.7 75L74.3 65L80 75L85.7 65L91.3 75L97 70"],["l",120,70,120,25],["l",120,25,185,25],["l",235,25,300,25],["p","M185 25L193.3 30L201.7 20L210 30L218.3 20L226.7 30L235 25"],["l",300,25,300,150],["l",120,70,190,70],["l",230,70,300,70],["p","M190 70L196.7 75L203.3 65L210 75L216.7 65L223.3 75L230 70"],["l",40,150,154,150],["l",154,150,167,150],["l",173,150,186,150],["l",167,140,167,160],["l",173,145,173,155,"B"],["l",186,150,300,150],["c",120,70,2.6,"F"],["c",300,70,2.6,"F"],["t",80,58,"400 \u03A9","m"],["t",210,13,"100 + 500 = 600 \u03A9","m"],["t",210,58,"R\u2082","m"],["t",170,176,"18 V","m"]]}},{pn:194,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ckir",st:"Kirchhoff's laws",q:"In the given circuit the cells have zero internal resistance. The currents (in amperes) passing through resistance R\u2081 and R\u2082 respectively, are",o:["0, 1","1, 2","2, 2","0.5, 0"],c:3,s:`Name the top wire T. The left end of the bottom wire is joined to T by the plain left wire, so the first cell sits between T and the bottom end of R\u2081.
This cell has no resistance, so it fixes the voltage across R\u2081 at 10 V: I\u2081 = 10/20 = 0.5 A.
The second 10 V cell faces the other way and is between the bottom end of R\u2081 and the bottom end of R\u2082. Going from T through the first cell the potential rises 10 V, through the second it falls 10 V, so the bottom end of R\u2082 is again at the potential of T.
The voltage across R\u2082 is therefore 0, so I\u2082 = 0.
The currents are 0.5 A and 0, option (d).`,fig:{w:300,h:180,alt:"Two 10 V cells on the bottom wire and two 20 ohm resistors R1 and R2 as vertical branches joined to the top wire",d:[["l",40,40,205,40],["l",40,40,40,140],["l",40,140,64,140],["l",64,140,83,140],["l",77,140,96,140],["l",83,130,83,150],["l",77,135,77,145,"B"],["l",96,140,120,140],["l",120,140,146,140],["l",146,140,159,140],["l",165,140,178,140],["l",159,130,159,150],["l",165,135,165,145,"B"],["l",178,140,205,140],["l",120,40,120,72],["l",120,108,120,140],["p","M120 72L115 78L125 84L115 90L125 96L115 102L120 108"],["l",205,40,205,72],["l",205,108,205,140],["p","M205 72L200 78L210 84L200 90L210 96L200 102L205 108"],["c",120,40,2.6,"F"],["c",120,140,2.6,"F"],["c",205,40,2.6,"F"],["c",205,140,2.6,"F"],["t",112,85,"R\u2081","e"],["t",112,99,"20 \u03A9","e"],["t",213,85,"R\u2082"],["t",213,99,"20 \u03A9"],["t",80,166,"10 V","m"],["t",162,166,"10 V","m"]]}},{pn:195,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 11 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ccel",st:"Grouping of cells",q:"In the circuit shown, the potential difference between A and B is",o:["6 V","3 V","2 V","1 V"],c:2,s:`Nothing is connected to A and B except the 5 \u03A9 and 10 \u03A9 resistors, so no current flows in them and they cause no voltage drop.
So the voltage between A and B equals the voltage between D and C, which is the voltage across the three cells joined in parallel.
The three branches (1 \u03A9 with 1 V, 1 \u03A9 with 2 V, 1 \u03A9 with 3 V) have equal resistance, so their common voltage is the mean of the emfs: (1 + 2 + 3)/3 = 2 V.
Note: this agrees with the general rule V = (\u03A3E/r)/(\u03A31/r); the equal r values cancel.
The potential difference is 2 V, option (c).`,fig:{w:400,h:230,alt:"Three branches in parallel between a left wire through M, D, N and a right wire through C: 1 ohm with a 1 V cell, 1 ohm with a 2 V cell, 1 ohm with a 3 V cell; a 5 ohm resistor joins A to D and a 10 ohm resistor joins C to B",d:[["c",24,115,3.2],["t",24,100,"A","m"],["l",27,115,56.5,115],["l",90.5,115,120,115],["p","M56.5 115L62.2 120L67.8 110L73.5 120L79.2 110L84.8 120L90.5 115"],["t",72,102,"5 \u03A9","m"],["l",120,40,120,190],["c",120,115,2.6,"F"],["t",112,134,"D","e"],["t",112,36,"M","e"],["t",112,202,"N","e"],["l",120,40,140,40],["l",140,40,158,40],["l",192,40,210,40],["p","M158 40L163.7 45L169.3 35L175 45L180.7 35L186.3 45L192 40"],["l",210,40,234,40],["l",234,40,247,40],["l",253,40,266,40],["l",247,30,247,50],["l",253,35,253,45,"B"],["l",266,40,290,40],["t",175,29,"1 \u03A9","m"],["t",250,24,"1 V","m"],["l",120,115,140,115],["l",140,115,158,115],["l",192,115,210,115],["p","M158 115L163.7 120L169.3 110L175 120L180.7 110L186.3 120L192 115"],["l",210,115,234,115],["l",234,115,247,115],["l",253,115,266,115],["l",247,105,247,125],["l",253,110,253,120,"B"],["l",266,115,290,115],["t",175,104,"1 \u03A9","m"],["t",250,99,"2 V","m"],["l",120,190,140,190],["l",140,190,158,190],["l",192,190,210,190],["p","M158 190L163.7 195L169.3 185L175 195L180.7 185L186.3 195L192 190"],["l",210,190,234,190],["l",234,190,247,190],["l",253,190,266,190],["l",247,180,247,200],["l",253,185,253,195,"B"],["l",266,190,290,190],["t",175,179,"1 \u03A9","m"],["t",250,174,"3 V","m"],["l",290,40,290,190],["c",290,115,2.6,"F"],["t",298,134,"C"],["l",290,115,300,115],["l",300,115,320.5,115],["l",354.5,115,375,115],["p","M320.5 115L326.2 120L331.8 110L337.5 120L343.2 110L348.8 120L354.5 115"],["t",338,102,"10 \u03A9","m"],["c",378,115,3.2],["t",378,100,"B","m"]]}},{pn:196,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ckir",st:"Kirchhoff's laws",q:"In the given circuit diagram, the currents, I\u2081 = 0.3 A, I\u2084 = 0.8 A and I\u2085 = 0.4 A, are flowing as shown. The currents I\u2082, I\u2083 and I\u2086 respectively, are",o:["1.1 A, 0.4 A, 0.4 A","\u22120.4 A, 0.4 A, 1.1 A","1.1 A, \u22120.4 A, 0.4 A","0.4 A, 1.1 A, 0.4 A"],c:0,s:`Note: the printed key (a) 1.1 A, 0.4 A, 0.4 A needs I\u2081 = \u22120.3 A, that is, the current in the right branch really flows up; the book prints 0.3 A and drops the minus sign. With the +0.3 A printed, working gives I\u2082 = 0.5 A, which is not an option.
Kirchhoff's junction law: current entering a node equals current leaving it.
Node S: I\u2084 comes in; I\u2085 and I\u2083 go out, so I\u2083 = I\u2084 \u2212 I\u2085 = 0.8 \u2212 0.4 = 0.4 A.
Node P: I\u2085 comes in and I\u2086 goes out, so I\u2086 = I\u2085 = 0.4 A.
Node Q: I\u2086 + I\u2083 = 0.8 A comes in; I\u2082 and I\u2081 go out, so I\u2082 = 0.8 \u2212 I\u2081.
With I\u2081 = \u22120.3 A (the current of 0.3 A actually flows up the right branch): I\u2082 = 0.8 + 0.3 = 1.1 A (see the second figure).
Check at R: I\u2082 + I\u2081 = 1.1 \u2212 0.3 = 0.8 A = I\u2084, so the numbers are consistent.
So I\u2082, I\u2083, I\u2086 = 1.1 A, 0.4 A, 0.4 A, option (a).`,fig:{w:380,h:200,alt:"A rectangle P Q R S with a diagonal from S to Q and a second branch on the right. Currents: I6 from P to Q, I3 from S to Q along the diagonal, I2 from Q down to R, I1 down the right branch, I4 from R to S along the bottom, I5 from S up to P",d:[["l",50,40,70,40],["l",70,40,113,40],["l",147,40,190,40],["p","M113 40L118.7 45L124.3 35L130 45L135.7 35L141.3 45L147 40"],["l",190,40,230,40],["l",230,40,320,40],["l",50,150,50,112],["l",50,78,50,40],["p","M50 112L55 106.3L45 100.7L55 95L45 89.3L55 83.7L50 78"],["l",50,150,125.5,103.9],["l",154.5,86.1,230,40],["p","M125.5 103.9L132.9 105.2L132.6 93.7L142.6 99.3L142.2 87.8L152.3 93.4L154.5 86.1"],["l",230,40,230,78],["l",230,112,230,150],["p","M230 78L225 83.7L235 89.3L225 95L235 100.7L225 106.3L230 112"],["l",320,40,320,78],["l",320,112,320,150],["p","M320 78L315 83.7L325 89.3L315 95L325 100.7L315 106.3L320 112"],["l",320,150,230,150],["l",230,150,157,150],["l",123,150,50,150],["p","M157 150L151.3 145L145.7 155L140 145L134.3 155L128.7 145L123 150"],["c",230,150,2.6,"F"],["c",230,40,2.6,"F"],["l",75,40,95,40,"a"],["l",50,142,50,130,"a"],["l",190,60,205,51,"a"],["l",230,48,230,64,"a"],["l",320,48,320,64,"a"],["l",205,150,190,150,"a"],["t",42,36,"P","e"],["t",236,32,"Q"],["t",234,168,"R"],["t",42,168,"S","e"],["t",122,30,"I\u2086","m"],["t",42,138,"I\u2085","e"],["t",190,82,"I\u2083","m"],["t",238,100,"I\u2082"],["t",328,100,"I\u2081"],["t",140,168,"I\u2084","m"]]},sfig:{w:380,h:200,alt:"A rectangle P Q R S with a diagonal from S to Q and a second branch on the right. Currents: I6 from P to Q, I3 from S to Q along the diagonal, I2 from Q down to R, 0.3 A up the right branch (I1 = -0.3 A), I4 from R to S along the bottom, I5 from S up to P",d:[["l",50,40,70,40],["l",70,40,113,40],["l",147,40,190,40],["p","M113 40L118.7 45L124.3 35L130 45L135.7 35L141.3 45L147 40"],["l",190,40,230,40],["l",230,40,320,40],["l",50,150,50,112],["l",50,78,50,40],["p","M50 112L55 106.3L45 100.7L55 95L45 89.3L55 83.7L50 78"],["l",50,150,125.5,103.9],["l",154.5,86.1,230,40],["p","M125.5 103.9L132.9 105.2L132.6 93.7L142.6 99.3L142.2 87.8L152.3 93.4L154.5 86.1"],["l",230,40,230,78],["l",230,112,230,150],["p","M230 78L225 83.7L235 89.3L225 95L235 100.7L225 106.3L230 112"],["l",320,40,320,78],["l",320,112,320,150],["p","M320 78L315 83.7L325 89.3L315 95L325 100.7L315 106.3L320 112"],["l",320,150,230,150],["l",230,150,157,150],["l",123,150,50,150],["p","M157 150L151.3 145L145.7 155L140 145L134.3 155L128.7 145L123 150"],["c",230,150,2.6,"F"],["c",230,40,2.6,"F"],["l",75,40,95,40,"a"],["l",50,142,50,130,"a"],["l",190,60,205,51,"a"],["l",230,48,230,64,"a"],["l",320,64,320,48,"a"],["l",205,150,190,150,"a"],["t",42,36,"P","e"],["t",236,32,"Q"],["t",234,168,"R"],["t",42,168,"S","e"],["t",122,30,"0.4 A","m"],["t",42,138,"0.4 A","e"],["t",190,82,"0.4 A","m"],["t",238,100,"1.1 A"],["t",328,100,"0.3 A"],["t",140,168,"0.8 A","m"]]}},{pn:197,y:"JEE Main 2018",tp:"ccel",st:"Grouping of cells",q:"Two batteries with e.m.f. 12 V and 13 V are connected in parallel across a load resistor of 10 \u03A9. The internal resistances of the two batteries are 1 \u03A9 and 2 \u03A9 respectively. The voltage across the load lies between",o:["11.6 V and 11.7 V","11.5 V and 11.6 V","11.4 V and 11.5 V","11.7 V and 11.8 V"],c:1,s:`Let V be the voltage across the load. Each battery supplies current (E \u2212 V)/r, and the load takes V/10.
Junction rule: (12 \u2212 V)/1 + (13 \u2212 V)/2 = V/10.
Multiply out: 12 \u2212 V + 6.5 \u2212 0.5V = 0.1V, so 18.5 = 1.6V.
V = 18.5/1.6 \u2248 11.56 V.
This lies between 11.5 V and 11.6 V, option (b).`},{pn:198,y:"JEE Main 2017",tp:"crc",st:"Charging and discharging of a capacitor",q:"In the given circuit diagram when the current reaches steady state in the circuit, the charge on the capacitor of capacitance C will be",o:["CE","CE r\u2081/(r\u2082 + r)","CE r\u2082/(r + r\u2082)","CE r\u2081/(r\u2081 + r)"],c:2,s:`In steady state a capacitor carries no current, so the branch with C and r\u2081 has no current and no drop across r\u2081.
So the voltage across C equals the voltage across the parallel branch r\u2082.
Current flows only in the loop E, r and r\u2082: I = E/(r + r\u2082).
Voltage across r\u2082 = I r\u2082 = E r\u2082/(r + r\u2082).
Charge Q = C \xD7 V = CE r\u2082/(r + r\u2082), option (c).`,fig:{w:330,h:180,alt:"Three parallel branches between two vertical wires: a cell E in series with resistance r; a capacitor C in series with r1; and a resistor r2 alone",d:[["l",50,30,50,140],["l",280,30,280,140],["l",50,30,79,30],["l",79,30,92,30],["l",98,30,111,30],["l",92,20,92,40],["l",98,25,98,35,"B"],["l",111,30,140,30],["l",140,30,172,30],["l",208,30,240,30],["p","M172 30L178 35L184 25L190 35L196 25L202 35L208 30"],["l",240,30,280,30],["l",50,85,86.5,85],["l",93.5,85,130,85],["l",86.5,76,86.5,94],["l",93.5,76,93.5,94],["l",130,85,167,85],["l",203,85,240,85],["p","M167 85L173 90L179 80L185 90L191 80L197 90L203 85"],["l",240,85,280,85],["l",50,140,145,140],["l",185,140,280,140],["p","M145 140L151.7 145L158.3 135L165 145L171.7 135L178.3 145L185 140"],["c",50,85,2.6,"F"],["c",280,85,2.6,"F"],["t",95,14,"E","m"],["t",190,18,"r","m"],["t",90,74,"C","m"],["t",190,73,"r\u2081","m"],["t",165,158,"r\u2082","m"]]}},{pn:199,y:"JEE Main 2017",tp:"ckir",st:"Kirchhoff's laws",q:"In the above circuit the current in each resistance is",o:["1 A","0.25 A","0.5 A","0 A"],c:3,s:`The three cells on the top wire and the three on the bottom wire all face the same way, and each 1 \u03A9 resistor joins a point of the top wire to the matching point of the bottom wire.
Take the plain left wire as the common starting point of the top and bottom wires. After the first cell, the top and bottom wires have both risen by 2 V, so the two ends of the first resistor are at the same potential.
After the second cell both wires have risen by 2 V more, and after the third cell by 2 V again, so the two ends of every resistor stay at equal potential.
No voltage across a resistor means no current: I = 0 in each, option (d).`,fig:{w:420,h:190,alt:"Three loops side by side; the top wire and the bottom wire each contain three 2 V cells of the same orientation, and 1 ohm resistors join top to bottom after the first and second cell and at the right end",d:[["l",30,40,64,40],["l",96,40,169,40],["l",201,40,274,40],["l",306,40,370,40],["l",64,40,77,40],["l",83,40,96,40],["l",77,30,77,50],["l",83,35,83,45,"B"],["t",80,24,"2 V","m"],["l",169,40,182,40],["l",188,40,201,40],["l",182,30,182,50],["l",188,35,188,45,"B"],["t",185,24,"2 V","m"],["l",274,40,287,40],["l",293,40,306,40],["l",287,30,287,50],["l",293,35,293,45,"B"],["t",290,24,"2 V","m"],["l",30,140,64,140],["l",96,140,169,140],["l",201,140,274,140],["l",306,140,370,140],["l",64,140,77,140],["l",83,140,96,140],["l",77,130,77,150],["l",83,135,83,145,"B"],["t",80,164,"2 V","m"],["l",169,140,182,140],["l",188,140,201,140],["l",182,130,182,150],["l",188,135,188,145,"B"],["t",185,164,"2 V","m"],["l",274,140,287,140],["l",293,140,306,140],["l",287,130,287,150],["l",293,135,293,145,"B"],["t",290,164,"2 V","m"],["l",30,40,30,140],["l",370,40,370,140],["l",130,40,130,72],["l",130,108,130,140],["p","M130 72L125 78L135 84L125 90L135 96L125 102L130 108"],["l",240,40,240,72],["l",240,108,240,140],["p","M240 72L235 78L245 84L235 90L245 96L235 102L240 108"],["l",370,40,370,72],["l",370,108,370,140],["p","M370 72L365 78L375 84L365 90L375 96L365 102L370 108"],["c",130,40,2.6,"F"],["c",130,140,2.6,"F"],["c",240,40,2.6,"F"],["c",240,140,2.6,"F"],["t",138,95,"1 \u03A9"],["t",248,95,"1 \u03A9"],["t",378,95,"1 \u03A9"]]}},{pn:200,y:"JEE Main 2017 \xB7 Online",tp:"ccmb",st:"Infinite and cube networks",q:"A 9 V battery with internal resistance of 0.5 \u03A9 is connected across an infinite network as shown in the figure. All ammeters A\u2081, A\u2082, A\u2083 and voltmeter V are ideal. Choose correct statement.",o:["Reading of V is 9 V.","Reading of A\u2081 is 2 A.","Reading of V is 7 V.","Reading of A\u2081 is 18 A."],c:1,s:`Cut off one repeating unit (1 \u03A9 on top, 1 \u03A9 at the bottom and a 4 \u03A9 shunt) and call the resistance of the whole network R. The rest of the ladder after it is the same network again, so it also has resistance R.
R = 2 + (4 \xD7 R)/(4 + R). Multiply out: 4R + R\xB2 = 8 + 2R + 4R, so R\xB2 \u2212 2R \u2212 8 = 0.
Note: an infinite ladder looks the same after removing one unit, which is why the same R appears on both sides.
R = 4 \u03A9 (the other root is negative and is rejected).
Current: I = 9/(4 + 0.5) = 2 A, so A\u2081 reads 2 A.
Voltmeter reads the terminal voltage: 9 \u2212 2 \xD7 0.5 = 8 V, so (a) and (c) are wrong.
The correct statement is (b).`,fig:{w:500,h:200,alt:"A 9 V battery B with internal resistance 0.5 ohm feeds a ladder: ammeter A1 and 1 ohm in the top wire, 1 ohm in the bottom wire, and 4 ohm shunts (with ammeters A2 and A3 in the first two) repeating to infinity; a voltmeter V is across the battery",d:[["l",40,40,53,40],["c",62,40,9],["t",62,44,"A\u2081","m"],["l",71,40,90,40],["l",90,40,103,40],["l",137,40,150,40],["p","M103 40L108.7 45L114.3 35L120 45L125.7 35L131.3 45L137 40"],["l",150,40,180,40],["l",220,40,250,40],["p","M180 40L186.7 45L193.3 35L200 45L206.7 35L213.3 45L220 40"],["l",250,40,280,40],["l",320,40,350,40],["p","M280 40L286.7 45L293.3 35L300 45L306.7 35L313.3 45L320 40"],["l",350,40,420,40,"d"],["l",40,140,150,140],["l",40,140,40,108],["l",40,76,40,40],["l",40,140,40,140],["l",40,140,78,140],["l",112,140,150,140],["p","M78 140L83.7 145L89.3 135L95 145L100.7 135L106.3 145L112 140"],["l",150,140,180,140],["l",220,140,250,140],["p","M180 140L186.7 145L193.3 135L200 145L206.7 135L213.3 145L220 140"],["l",250,140,280,140],["l",320,140,350,140],["p","M280 140L286.7 145L293.3 135L300 145L306.7 135L313.3 145L320 140"],["l",350,140,420,140,"d"],["l",40,76,40,89],["l",40,95,40,108],["l",30,89,50,89],["l",35,95,45,95,"B"],["l",150,40,150,56],["c",150,65,9],["t",150,69,"A\u2082","m"],["l",150,74,150,80],["l",150,80,150,93],["l",150,127,150,140],["p","M150 93L145 98.7L155 104.3L145 110L155 115.7L145 121.3L150 127"],["c",150,40,2.6,"F"],["c",150,140,2.6,"F"],["t",158,114,"4 \u03A9"],["l",250,40,250,56],["c",250,65,9],["t",250,69,"A\u2083","m"],["l",250,74,250,80],["l",250,80,250,93],["l",250,127,250,140],["p","M250 93L245 98.7L255 104.3L245 110L255 115.7L245 121.3L250 127"],["c",250,40,2.6,"F"],["c",250,140,2.6,"F"],["t",258,114,"4 \u03A9"],["l",350,40,350,73],["l",350,107,350,140],["p","M350 73L345 78.7L355 84.3L345 90L355 95.7L345 101.3L350 107"],["c",350,40,2.6,"F"],["c",350,140,2.6,"F"],["t",358,94,"4 \u03A9"],["t",440,94,"\u221E ","m"],["l",40,58,85,58,"d"],["l",85,58,85,83,"d"],["c",85,92,9],["t",85,96,"V","m"],["l",85,101,85,124,"d"],["l",85,124,40,124,"d"],["t",120,30,"1 \u03A9","m"],["t",200,30,"1 \u03A9","m"],["t",300,30,"1 \u03A9","m"],["t",100,160,"1 \u03A9","m"],["t",200,160,"1 \u03A9","m"],["t",300,160,"1 \u03A9","m"],["t",54,76,"B"],["t",54,93,"9 V"],["t",54,112,"0.5 \u03A9","s"]]},sfig:{w:300,h:170,alt:"Working figure: the 9 V battery with 0.5 ohm internal resistance connected to the whole ladder, which acts as a single 4 ohm resistor, with a current of 2 A",d:[["l",50,30,50,62],["l",50,62,50,75],["l",50,81,50,94],["l",40,75,60,75],["l",45,81,55,81,"B"],["l",50,94,50,140],["l",50,140,230,140],["l",50,30,90,30],["l",90,30,108,30],["l",142,30,160,30],["p","M108 30L113.7 35L119.3 25L125 35L130.7 25L136.3 35L142 30"],["l",160,30,230,30],["l",230,30,230,65],["l",230,105,230,140],["p","M230 65L225 71.7L235 78.3L225 85L235 91.7L225 98.3L230 105"],["t",125,18,"0.5 \u03A9","m"],["t",240,90,"4 \u03A9"],["t",240,104,"(ladder)"],["t",32,74,"9 V","e"],["l",70,160,130,160,"a"],["t",160,164,"I = 2 A","m"]]}},{pn:201,y:"JEE Main 2015",tp:"ckir",st:"Kirchhoff's laws",q:"In the circuit shown, the current in the 1 \u03A9 resistor is",o:["0.13 A, from Q to P","0.13 A, from P to Q","0.3 A, from P to Q","0 A"],c:0,s:`P and Q are joined by three branches: left (6 V cell with 3 \u03A9), middle (1 \u03A9) and right (2 \u03A9 with 9 V cell and 3 \u03A9, total 5 \u03A9). Find the voltage between P and Q.
Let V = V_P \u2212 V_Q. The cells are placed so that the 6 V cell pushes current away from P in the left branch and the 9 V cell pushes it towards P in the right branch (as drawn).
Junction rule at P (currents leaving P add to zero): (V + 6)/3 + V/1 + (V \u2212 9)/5 = 0.
Multiply by 15: 5(V + 6) + 15V + 3(V \u2212 9) = 0, so 23V + 30 \u2212 27 = 0 and V = \u22123/23 V.
The negative sign means Q is higher than P, so the current in the 1 \u03A9 is 3/23 \u2248 0.13 A from Q to P, option (a).`,fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"Two loops sharing the 1 ohm resistor between P and Q: the left loop has a 6 V cell and a 3 ohm resistor, the right loop has a 2 ohm resistor, a 9 V cell and a 3 ohm resistor",d:[["l",40,50,84,50],["l",84,50,97,50],["l",103,50,116,50],["l",97,40,97,60],["l",103,45,103,55,"B"],["l",116,50,180,50],["l",40,50,40,150],["l",40,150,93,150],["l",127,150,180,150],["p","M93 150L98.7 155L104.3 145L110 155L115.7 145L121.3 155L127 150"],["l",180,50,180,83],["l",180,117,180,150],["p","M180 83L175 88.7L185 94.3L175 100L185 105.7L175 111.3L180 117"],["l",180,50,208,50],["l",242,50,270,50],["p","M208 50L213.7 55L219.3 45L225 55L230.7 45L236.3 55L242 50"],["l",270,50,270,85],["l",270,85,270,98],["l",270,104,270,117],["l",260,98,280,98],["l",265,104,275,104,"B"],["l",270,117,270,150],["l",270,150,242,150],["l",208,150,180,150],["p","M242 150L236.3 145L230.7 155L225 145L219.3 155L213.7 145L208 150"],["c",180,50,2.6,"F"],["c",180,150,2.6,"F"],["t",100,34,"6 V","m"],["t",180,40,"P","m"],["t",180,170,"Q","m"],["t",188,105,"1 \u03A9"],["t",110,138,"3 \u03A9","m"],["t",225,138,"3 \u03A9","m"],["t",225,38,"2 \u03A9","m"],["t",284,105,"9 V"]]}},{pn:202,y:"JEE Main 2015 \xB7 Online",tp:"ccel",st:"Grouping of cells",q:"A 10 V battery with internal resistance 1 \u03A9 and a 15 V battery with internal resistance 0.6 \u03A9 are connected in parallel to a voltmeter (see figure). The reading in the voltmeter will be close to",o:["11.9 V","12.5 V","13.1 V","24.5 V"],c:2,s:`An ideal voltmeter draws no current, so the two batteries drive a current round the loop made of the two of them.
Loop current: I = (15 \u2212 10)/(1 + 0.6) = 3.125 A, flowing out of the 15 V battery.
Voltage across the 15 V battery terminals (which is also the voltmeter reading): V = 15 \u2212 I \xD7 0.6 = 15 \u2212 1.875 = 13.125 V.
Check with the 10 V battery: 10 + 3.125 \xD7 1 = 13.125 V, the same.
The reading is about 13.1 V, option (c).`,fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"A 10 V cell with 1 ohm internal resistance, a 15 V cell with 0.6 ohm internal resistance and a voltmeter, all joined in parallel between two vertical wires",d:[["l",40,40,40,150],["l",290,40,290,150],["l",40,40,94,40],["l",94,40,107,40],["l",113,40,126,40],["l",107,30,107,50],["l",113,35,113,45,"B"],["l",126,40,170,40],["l",170,40,212,40],["l",248,40,290,40],["p","M212 40L218 45L224 35L230 45L236 35L242 45L248 40"],["t",110,24,"10 V","m"],["t",230,29,"1 \u03A9","m"],["l",40,95,94,95],["l",94,95,107,95],["l",113,95,126,95],["l",107,85,107,105],["l",113,90,113,100,"B"],["l",126,95,170,95],["l",170,95,212,95],["l",248,95,290,95],["p","M212 95L218 100L224 90L230 100L236 90L242 100L248 95"],["t",110,79,"15 V","m"],["t",230,84,"0.6 \u03A9","m"],["l",40,150,155,150],["c",165,150,10],["t",165,154,"V","m"],["l",175,150,290,150],["c",40,95,2.6,"F"],["c",290,95,2.6,"F"]]}},{pn:203,y:"JEE Main 2015 \xB7 Online",tp:"ckir",st:"Kirchhoff's laws",q:"In the electric network shown, when no current flows through the 4 \u03A9 resistor in the arm EB, the potential difference between the points A and D will be",o:["3 V","4 V","5 V","6 V"],c:2,s:`No current flows in the arm EB, so there is no drop across the 4 \u03A9, and the voltage between E and B is just the emf of the 4 V cell: V_E \u2212 V_B = 4 V (E is the higher end as drawn).
Between E and D there is only a plain wire, so V_D = V_E.
The 9 V cell sits between A and B and has no internal resistance, so the voltage across it is 9 V whatever the current: V_A \u2212 V_B = 9 V, with A the positive end.
Then V_A \u2212 V_D = (V_A \u2212 V_B) \u2212 (V_D \u2212 V_B) = 9 \u2212 4 = 5 V.
The potential difference is 5 V, option (c).
Note: the cell polarities were read from the printed figure and agree with the printed key (c).`,fig:{w:420,h:230,alt:"A network: top wire F to D has a 2 V cell and a 2 ohm resistor and a node E; left side F to A has a 2 ohm resistor; bottom wire A to C has a 9 V cell, a node B and a 3 V cell; right side D to C has a resistor R; the arm E to B has a 4 ohm resistor and a 4 V cell",d:[["l",60,50,84,50],["l",84,50,103,50],["l",97,50,116,50],["l",97,40,97,60],["l",103,45,103,55,"B"],["l",116,50,130,50],["l",130,50,168,50],["l",202,50,240,50],["p","M168 50L173.7 55L179.3 45L185 55L190.7 45L196.3 55L202 50"],["l",240,50,340,50],["l",60,50,60,83],["l",60,117,60,150],["p","M60 83L55 88.7L65 94.3L55 100L65 105.7L55 111.3L60 117"],["l",60,150,84,150],["l",84,150,97,150],["l",103,150,116,150],["l",97,140,97,160],["l",103,145,103,155,"B"],["l",116,150,274,150],["l",274,150,287,150],["l",293,150,306,150],["l",293,140,293,160],["l",287,145,287,155,"B"],["l",306,150,340,150],["l",340,50,340,83],["l",340,117,340,150],["p","M340 83L335 88.7L345 94.3L335 100L345 105.7L335 111.3L340 117"],["l",240,50,240,56],["l",240,86,240,92],["p","M240 56L235 61L245 66L235 71L245 76L235 81L240 86"],["l",240,92,240,99],["l",240,99,240,112],["l",240,118,240,131],["l",230,112,250,112],["l",235,118,245,118,"B"],["l",240,131,240,150],["c",240,50,2.6,"F"],["c",240,150,2.6,"F"],["t",60,38,"F","m"],["t",240,38,"E","m"],["t",340,38,"D","m"],["t",60,170,"A","m"],["t",240,170,"B","m"],["t",340,170,"C","m"],["t",100,34,"2 V","m"],["t",185,38,"2 \u03A9","m"],["t",48,105,"2 \u03A9","e"],["t",100,134,"9 V","m"],["t",290,134,"3 V","m"],["t",350,105,"R"],["t",232,78,"4 \u03A9","e"],["t",226,120,"4 V","e"]]}},{pn:204,y:"JEE Main 2014 \xB7 Online",tp:"ckir",st:"Kirchhoff's laws",q:"In the circuit shown, current (in A) through the 50 V and 30 V batteries are, respectively",o:["2.5 and 3","3.5 and 2","4.5 and 1","3 and 2.5"],c:2,s:`The ideal 50 V cell is directly across the 20 \u03A9, so the left pair of nodes is held at 50 V. Likewise the ideal 30 V cell holds the right pair of nodes at 30 V across the 10 \u03A9.
The two 5 \u03A9 resistors (top and bottom) are in series, 10 \u03A9 in all, between these two pairs of nodes.
Current in this 10 \u03A9: (50 \u2212 30)/10 = 2 A, flowing from the 50 V side to the 30 V side.
Current through the 20 \u03A9: 50/20 = 2.5 A. So the 50 V battery supplies 2.5 + 2 = 4.5 A.
Current through the 10 \u03A9: 30/10 = 3 A. The 2 A arriving from the left supplies part of it, so the 30 V battery supplies 3 \u2212 2 = 1 A (it is being asked for less than the 10 \u03A9 needs).
The currents are 4.5 A and 1 A, option (c).`,fig:{w:420,h:210,alt:"A 50 V cell on the left edge with a 20 ohm resistor across it, a 30 V cell on the right edge with a 10 ohm resistor across it, joined by a 5 ohm resistor in the top wire and a 5 ohm resistor in the bottom wire",d:[["l",40,40,110,40],["l",110,40,182,40],["l",218,40,290,40],["p","M182 40L188 45L194 35L200 45L206 35L212 45L218 40"],["l",290,40,360,40],["l",40,160,110,160],["l",110,160,182,160],["l",218,160,290,160],["p","M182 160L188 165L194 155L200 165L206 155L212 165L218 160"],["l",290,160,360,160],["l",40,40,40,84],["l",40,84,40,97],["l",40,103,40,116],["l",30,97,50,97],["l",35,103,45,103,"B"],["l",40,116,40,160],["l",360,40,360,84],["l",360,84,360,97],["l",360,103,360,116],["l",350,97,370,97],["l",355,103,365,103,"B"],["l",360,116,360,160],["l",110,40,110,80],["l",110,120,110,160],["p","M110 80L105 86.7L115 93.3L105 100L115 106.7L105 113.3L110 120"],["l",290,40,290,80],["l",290,120,290,160],["p","M290 80L285 86.7L295 93.3L285 100L295 106.7L285 113.3L290 120"],["c",110,40,2.6,"F"],["c",110,160,2.6,"F"],["c",290,40,2.6,"F"],["c",290,160,2.6,"F"],["t",200,28,"5 \u03A9","m"],["t",200,184,"5 \u03A9","m"],["t",28,104,"50 V","e"],["t",372,104,"30 V"],["t",100,104,"20 \u03A9","e"],["t",300,104,"10 \u03A9"]]}},{pn:205,y:"AIEEE 2008",tp:"ckir",st:"Kirchhoff's laws",q:"A 5 V battery with internal resistance 2 \u03A9 and 2 V battery with internal resistance 1 \u03A9 are connected to a 10 \u03A9 resistor as shown in the figure. The current in the 10 \u03A9 resistor is",o:["0.27 A P\u2081 to P\u2082","0.27 A P\u2082 to P\u2081","0.03 A P\u2081 to P\u2082","0.03 A P\u2082 to P\u2081"],c:3,s:`The two cells face opposite ways: the 5 V cell pushes current up towards P\u2082, the 2 V cell pushes it down towards P\u2081.
Let V = V(P\u2082) \u2212 V(P\u2081). The junction rule at P\u2082 with currents leaving P\u2082: (V \u2212 5)/2 + V/10 + (V + 2)/1 = 0.
Multiply by 10: 5(V \u2212 5) + V + 10(V + 2) = 0, so 16V \u2212 25 + 20 = 0 and V = 5/16 V.
V is positive, so P\u2082 is higher and the current in the 10 \u03A9 is V/10 = 0.03125 A \u2248 0.03 A from P\u2082 to P\u2081.
The answer is option (d).`,fig:{w:380,h:190,alt:"A 5 V cell with 2 ohm internal resistance, a 10 ohm resistor and a 2 V cell with 1 ohm internal resistance in parallel between a top wire at P2 and a bottom wire at P1; the cells face opposite ways",d:[["l",70,40,310,40],["l",70,150,310,150],["l",70,40,70,79],["l",70,79,70,92],["l",70,98,70,111],["l",60,92,80,92],["l",65,98,75,98,"B"],["l",70,111,70,150],["l",190,40,190,75],["l",190,115,190,150],["p","M190 75L185 81.7L195 88.3L185 95L195 101.7L185 108.3L190 115"],["l",310,40,310,79],["l",310,79,310,98],["l",310,92,310,111],["l",300,98,320,98],["l",305,92,315,92,"B"],["l",310,111,310,150],["c",190,40,2.6,"F"],["c",190,150,2.6,"F"],["t",190,28,"P\u2082","m"],["t",190,168,"P\u2081","m"],["t",198,100,"10 \u03A9"],["t",54,90,"5 V","e"],["t",54,104,"2 \u03A9","e"],["t",328,90,"2 V"],["t",328,104,"1 \u03A9"]]}},{pn:206,y:"AIEEE 2008",tp:"crc",st:"Charging and discharging of a capacitor",q:"A parallel plate capacitor C with plates of unit area and separation d is filled with a liquid of dielectric constant K = 2. The level of liquid is d/3 initially. Suppose the liquid level decreases at a constant speed V, the time constant as a function of time t is",o:["6\u03B5\u2080R/(5d + 3Vt)","(15d + 9Vt)\u03B5\u2080R/(2d\xB2 \u2212 3dVt \u2212 9V\xB2t\xB2)","6\u03B5\u2080R/(5d \u2212 3Vt)","(15d \u2212 9Vt)\u03B5\u2080R/(2d\xB2 + 3dVt \u2212 9V\xB2t\xB2)"],c:0,s:`The capacitor is two layers in series: a liquid layer of thickness h and an air layer of thickness d \u2212 h. The time constant is \u03C4 = RC.
The liquid level falls at speed V from d/3, so h = d/3 \u2212 Vt and the air layer is d \u2212 h = 2d/3 + Vt.
For layers in series with unit area, 1/C = (d \u2212 h)/\u03B5\u2080 + h/(K\u03B5\u2080). With K = 2 this is 1/C = [ (2d/3 + Vt) + (d/3 \u2212 Vt)/2 ]/\u03B5\u2080.
Inside the bracket: 2d/3 + d/6 + Vt \u2212 Vt/2 = 5d/6 + Vt/2 = (5d + 3Vt)/6.
So C = 6\u03B5\u2080/(5d + 3Vt) and \u03C4 = RC = 6\u03B5\u2080R/(5d + 3Vt), option (a).`,fig:{w:340,h:210,alt:"A parallel plate capacitor C with horizontal plates separated by d; the lower third of the gap, d/3, is filled with liquid; the plates are joined in a loop with a resistor R and a cell",d:[["l",60,60,170,60,"B"],["l",60,140,170,140,"B"],["r",60,113,110,27,"f"],["l",115,60,115,28],["l",115,28,270,28],["l",270,28,270,72],["l",270,72,270,83],["l",270,117,270,128],["p","M270 83L265 88.7L275 94.3L265 100L275 105.7L265 111.3L270 117"],["l",270,128,270,175],["l",270,175,205,175],["l",174,175,187,175],["l",193,175,206,175],["l",187,165,187,185],["l",193,170,193,180,"B"],["l",174,175,115,175],["l",115,175,115,140],["l",40,62,40,138,"b"],["t",32,104,"d","e"],["l",185,115,185,138,"b"],["t",193,130,"d/3"],["t",122,52,"C"],["t",280,104,"R"]]}},{pn:207,y:"IIT-JEE 2007",tp:"crc",st:"Charging and discharging of a capacitor",q:"A circuit is connected as shown in the figure with the switch S open. When the switch is closed, the total amount of charge that flows from Y to X is",o:["0","54 \u03BCC","27 \u03BCC","81 \u03BCC"],c:2,s:`Switch open: the two capacitors are in series across 9 V, so X has equal and opposite charges on its two plates and the net charge at X is zero.
Switch closed, steady state: no current flows in the capacitors, so the 9 V drives current 9/(3 + 6) = 1 A through the resistors. The voltage across 3 \u03A9 is 3 V and across 6 \u03A9 is 6 V, so Y (and now X) is at 6 V when the left end is at 9 V and the right end at 0 V (see the second figure).
The 3 \u03BCF capacitor now has 9 \u2212 6 = 3 V across it, so its plate on X carries \u22123 \xD7 3 = \u22129 \u03BCC.
The 6 \u03BCF capacitor has 6 \u2212 0 = 6 V across it, so its plate on X carries +6 \xD7 6 = +36 \u03BCC.
Net charge now at X: +36 \u2212 9 = +27 \u03BCC. Before it was 0, so 27 \u03BCC must have flowed to X through the switch from Y.
The charge is 27 \u03BCC, option (c).`,fig:{w:340,h:250,alt:"A 3 microfarad and a 6 microfarad capacitor in series in the top wire with the point X between them; a 3 ohm and a 6 ohm resistor in series in the lower wire with the point Y between them; a switch S joins X to Y; a 9 V cell joins the left and right ends",d:[["l",60,50,60,130],["l",280,50,280,130],["l",60,50,94,50],["l",60,50,106.5,50],["l",113.5,50,160,50],["l",106.5,42,106.5,58],["l",113.5,42,113.5,58],["l",160,50,216.5,50],["l",223.5,50,280,50],["l",216.5,42,216.5,58],["l",223.5,42,223.5,58],["l",60,130,93,130],["l",127,130,160,130],["p","M93 130L98.7 135L104.3 125L110 135L115.7 125L121.3 135L127 130"],["l",160,130,203,130],["l",237,130,280,130],["p","M203 130L208.7 135L214.3 125L220 135L225.7 125L231.3 135L237 130"],["l",160,50,160,82],["l",160,82,172,94],["l",160,106,160,130],["c",160,50,2.6,"F"],["c",160,130,2.6,"F"],["c",160,84,3.2],["c",160,106,3.2],["l",60,130,60,200],["l",60,200,144,200],["l",144,200,157,200],["l",163,200,176,200],["l",157,190,157,210],["l",163,195,163,205,"B"],["l",176,200,280,200],["l",280,200,280,130],["t",110,38,"3 \u03BCF","m"],["t",220,38,"6 \u03BCF","m"],["t",160,38,"X","m"],["t",160,150,"Y","m"],["t",110,118,"3 \u03A9","m"],["t",220,118,"6 \u03A9","m"],["t",180,98,"S"],["t",160,222,"9 V","m"]]},sfig:{w:340,h:190,alt:"Switch closed: the left end at 9 V and the right end at 0 V; Y and X at 6 V; the 3 microfarad capacitor has 3 V across it and the 6 microfarad capacitor has 6 V across it",d:[["t",60,20,"9 V","m"],["t",280,20,"0 V","m"],["t",160,20,"6 V","m"],["l",60,40,60,140],["l",280,40,280,140],["l",60,50,106.5,50],["l",113.5,50,160,50],["l",106.5,42,106.5,58],["l",113.5,42,113.5,58],["l",160,50,216.5,50],["l",223.5,50,280,50],["l",216.5,42,216.5,58],["l",223.5,42,223.5,58],["l",60,130,93,130],["l",127,130,160,130],["p","M93 130L98.7 135L104.3 125L110 135L115.7 125L121.3 135L127 130"],["l",160,130,203,130],["l",237,130,280,130],["p","M203 130L208.7 135L214.3 125L220 135L225.7 125L231.3 135L237 130"],["l",160,50,160,130],["c",160,50,2.6,"F"],["c",160,130,2.6,"F"],["t",110,38,"3 \u03BCF: 3 V","m"],["t",220,38,"6 \u03BCF: 6 V","m"],["t",110,118,"3 \u03A9: 3 V","m"],["t",220,118,"6 \u03A9: 6 V","m"],["t",160,40,"X"],["t",168,150,"Y"]]}},{pn:208,y:"AIEEE 2006",tp:"ckir",st:"Kirchhoff's laws",q:"The Kirchhoff's first law (\u03A3i = 0) and second law (\u03A3iR = \u03A3E), where the symbols have their usual meanings, are respectively based on",o:["conservation of charge, conservation of energy","conservation of charge, conservation of momentum","conservation of energy, conservation of charge","conservation of momentum, conservation of charge"],c:0,s:`The first law says the total current into a junction is zero: charge cannot pile up at a junction, so it is based on conservation of charge.
The second law says the sum of emfs round a loop equals the sum of iR drops: a unit charge going round a closed loop gains and loses energy in total zero, so it is based on conservation of energy.
The pair is conservation of charge and conservation of energy, option (a).`},{pn:209,y:"IIT-JEE 2006",tp:"crc",st:"Charging and discharging of a capacitor",q:"Find the time constant (in \u03BCs) for the given R-C circuits in the given order respectively. R\u2081 = 1 \u03A9, R\u2082 = 2 \u03A9; C\u2081 = 4 \u03BCF, C\u2082 = 2 \u03BCF.",o:["18, 4, 8/9","18, 8/9, 4","4, 18, 8/9","4, 8/9, 18"],c:1,s:`The time constant is \u03C4 = R(eq) \xD7 C(eq), found by combining resistors and capacitors separately.
(a) R\u2081 and R\u2082 are in series: 3 \u03A9. C\u2081 and C\u2082 are in parallel: 6 \u03BCF. \u03C4 = 3 \xD7 6 = 18 \u03BCs.
(b) C\u2081 and C\u2082 are in series: (4 \xD7 2)/6 = 4/3 \u03BCF. R\u2081 and R\u2082 are in parallel: (1 \xD7 2)/3 = 2/3 \u03A9. \u03C4 = (2/3)(4/3) = 8/9 \u03BCs.
(c) R\u2081 and R\u2082 are in parallel: 2/3 \u03A9. C\u2081 and C\u2082 are in parallel: 6 \u03BCF. \u03C4 = (2/3) \xD7 6 = 4 \u03BCs.
The order is 18, 8/9, 4, option (b).`,fig:{w:520,h:200,alt:"Three R-C circuits with a voltmeter in each loop: (a) R1 and R2 in series with C1 and C2 in parallel; (b) C2 and C1 in series with R1 and R2 in parallel; (c) R1 and R2 in parallel, in series with C1 and C2 in parallel",d:[["l",30,30,81,30],["c",90,30,9],["t",90,34,"V","m"],["l",99,30,150,30],["l",30,30,30,30],["l",30,30,30,40],["l",30,70,30,80],["p","M30 40L25 45L35 50L25 55L35 60L25 65L30 70"],["l",30,80,30,90],["l",30,120,30,130],["p","M30 90L25 95L35 100L25 105L35 110L25 115L30 120"],["l",30,130,150,130],["l",150,30,150,55],["l",150,105,150,130],["l",120,55,180,55],["l",120,105,180,105],["l",120,55,120,77],["l",120,83,120,105],["l",127,77,113,77],["l",127,83,113,83],["l",180,55,180,77],["l",180,83,180,105],["l",187,77,173,77],["l",187,83,173,83],["c",150,55,2.6,"F"],["c",150,105,2.6,"F"],["t",22,58,"R\u2081","e"],["t",22,108,"R\u2082","e"],["t",110,84,"C\u2081","e"],["t",190,84,"C\u2082"],["t",90,160,"(a)","m"],["l",205,30,256,30],["c",265,30,9],["t",265,34,"V","m"],["l",274,30,325,30],["l",205,30,205,52],["l",205,58,205,80],["l",212,52,198,52],["l",212,58,198,58],["l",205,80,205,102],["l",205,108,205,130],["l",212,102,198,102],["l",212,108,198,108],["l",205,130,325,130],["l",325,30,325,55],["l",325,105,325,130],["l",295,55,355,55],["l",295,105,355,105],["l",295,55,295,65],["l",295,95,295,105],["p","M295 65L290 70L300 75L290 80L300 85L290 90L295 95"],["l",355,55,355,65],["l",355,95,355,105],["p","M355 65L350 70L360 75L350 80L360 85L350 90L355 95"],["c",325,55,2.6,"F"],["c",325,105,2.6,"F"],["t",197,58,"C\u2082","e"],["t",197,108,"C\u2081","e"],["t",285,84,"R\u2081","e"],["t",365,84,"R\u2082"],["t",265,160,"(b)","m"],["l",391,30,442,30],["c",451,30,9],["t",451,34,"V","m"],["l",460,30,507,30],["l",391,30,391,80],["l",391,80,407,80],["l",407,55,407,105],["l",407,55,415,55],["p","M415 55 L419 59 L423 51 L427 59 L431 51 L435 59 L439 55"],["l",439,55,447,55],["l",407,105,415,105],["p","M415 105 L419 109 L423 101 L427 109 L431 101 L435 109 L439 105"],["l",439,105,447,105],["l",447,55,447,105],["l",447,80,467,80],["l",467,55,467,105],["l",467,55,484,55],["l",490,55,507,55],["l",484,49,484,61],["l",490,49,490,61],["l",467,105,484,105],["l",490,105,507,105],["l",484,99,484,111],["l",490,99,490,111],["l",507,30,507,105],["t",427,45,"R\u2081","m"],["t",427,122,"R\u2082","m"],["t",487,45,"C\u2081","m"],["t",487,122,"C\u2082","m"],["t",451,160,"(c)","m"]]}},{pn:210,y:"AIEEE 2005",tp:"ccel",st:"EMF and internal resistance",q:"Two sources of equal emf are connected to an external resistance R. The internal resistances of the two sources are R\u2081 and R\u2082 (R\u2082 > R\u2081). If the potential difference across the source having internal resistance R\u2082 is zero, then",o:["R = R\u2081R\u2082/(R\u2081 + R\u2082)","R = R\u2081R\u2082/(R\u2082 \u2212 R\u2081)","R = R\u2082(R\u2081 + R\u2082)/(R\u2082 \u2212 R\u2081)","R = R\u2082 \u2212 R\u2081"],c:3,s:`The two sources are in series with R (this is the only way the p.d. across one source can be zero). The current is I = 2E/(R + R\u2081 + R\u2082).
Terminal voltage of source 2: V = E \u2212 I R\u2082 = 0, so I R\u2082 = E.
Put in the current: 2E R\u2082/(R + R\u2081 + R\u2082) = E, so 2R\u2082 = R + R\u2081 + R\u2082.
R = R\u2082 \u2212 R\u2081, option (d).`},{pn:211,y:"AIEEE 2005",tp:"cpot",st:"Potentiometer",q:"In the circuit, the galvanometer G shows zero deflection. If the batteries A and B have negligible internal resistance, the value of the resistor R will be",o:["500 \u03A9","1000 \u03A9","200 \u03A9","100 \u03A9"],c:3,s:`Zero deflection means no current in the branch with G, so I\u2082 = 0 and cell A (2 V) just balances the voltage across R.
So the voltage across R is 2 V.
The 12 V cell B drives current through 500 \u03A9 and R in series: I\u2081 = 12/(500 + R).
Voltage across R: I\u2081 R = 12R/(500 + R) = 2, so 12R = 1000 + 2R and 10R = 1000.
R = 100 \u03A9, option (d).`,fig:{w:380,h:200,alt:"A 12 V cell B with a 500 ohm resistor on the top wire, a resistor R from the junction to the bottom wire, and a branch with a galvanometer G and a 2 V cell A to the bottom wire",d:[["l",50,45,50,84],["l",50,84,50,97],["l",50,103,50,116],["l",40,97,60,97],["l",45,103,55,103,"B"],["l",50,116,50,150],["l",50,150,290,150],["l",50,45,83,45],["l",117,45,150,45],["p","M83 45L88.7 50L94.3 40L100 50L105.7 40L111.3 50L117 45"],["l",150,45,150,77.5],["l",150,117.5,150,150],["p","M150 77.5L145 84.2L155 90.8L145 97.5L155 104.2L145 110.8L150 117.5"],["l",150,45,210,45],["c",220,45,10],["t",220,49,"G","m"],["l",230,45,290,45],["l",290,45,290,84],["l",290,84,290,97],["l",290,103,290,116],["l",280,97,300,97],["l",285,103,295,103,"B"],["l",290,116,290,150],["c",150,45,2.6,"F"],["c",150,150,2.6,"F"],["l",160,32,186,32,"a"],["t",173,24,"I\u2081","m"],["l",270,32,246,32,"a"],["t",258,24,"I\u2082","m"],["t",100,33,"500 \u03A9","m"],["t",158,100,"R"],["t",40,96,"12 V","e"],["t",40,110,"B","e"],["t",302,96,"2 V"],["t",302,110,"A"]]}},{pn:212,y:"AIEEE 2005",tp:"ccel",st:"EMF and internal resistance",q:"An energy source will supply a constant current into the load if its internal resistance is",o:["zero","non-zero but less than the resistance of the load","equal to the resistance of the load","very large as compared to the load resistance."],c:3,s:`Note: the printed key says (a) zero; working gives (d), because zero internal resistance gives a constant voltage, not a constant current.
The current from a source with emf E and internal resistance r into a load R is I = E/(r + R).
If r is very large compared with R, then r + R \u2248 r and I \u2248 E/r, which does not change when the load changes: a constant-current source.
If r = 0 instead, I = E/R changes with R, while the terminal voltage stays at E (a constant-voltage source).
The answer is (d).`},{pn:213,y:"IIT-JEE 2005",tp:"crc",st:"Charging and discharging of a capacitor",q:"A 4 \u03BCF capacitor and a resistance of 2.5 M\u03A9 are in series with 12 V battery. Find the time after which the potential difference across the capacitor is 3 times the potential difference across the resistor. [Given ln(2) = 0.693].",o:["13.86 s","6.93 s","7 s","14 s"],c:0,s:`At any moment V_C + V_R = 12 V. If V_C = 3V_R, then V_C = 9 V and V_R = 3 V.
Charging a capacitor: V_C = E(1 \u2212 e^(\u2212t/\u03C4)), so 9 = 12(1 \u2212 e^(\u2212t/\u03C4)) and e^(\u2212t/\u03C4) = 1/4.
Time constant \u03C4 = RC = 2.5 \xD7 10\u2076 \xD7 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 = 10 s.
t = \u03C4 ln 4 = 10 \xD7 2 ln 2 = 20 \xD7 0.693 = 13.86 s.
The time is 13.86 s, option (a).`},{pn:214,y:"AIEEE 2004",tp:"ccmb",st:"Equivalent resistance of networks",q:"The total current supplied to the circuit by the battery is",o:["1 A","2 A","4 A","6 A"],c:2,s:`The cell and the 3 \u03A9 resistor sit on the circle, which splits it into an upper and a lower wire. The 2 \u03A9 and 6 \u03A9 join the upper wire to the centre; the 1.5 \u03A9 joins the lower wire to the centre.
The 2 \u03A9 and 6 \u03A9 are in parallel: (2 \xD7 6)/8 = 1.5 \u03A9. This is in series with the 1.5 \u03A9 to the lower wire: 3 \u03A9 in all.
So the cell sees two 3 \u03A9 paths in parallel (see the second figure): 3/2 = 1.5 \u03A9.
I = 6/1.5 = 4 A, option (c).`,fig:{w:300,h:210,alt:"A circular wire with a 6 V cell on its left and a 3 ohm resistor on its right; three resistors 2 ohm, 6 ohm and 1.5 ohm join points of the circle to a centre point",d:[["p","M81.9 89A70 70 0 0 1 216.5 83"],["p","M81.9 121A70 70 0 0 0 216.5 127"],["l",81.9,89,81.9,102],["l",81.9,108,81.9,121],["l",71.9,102,91.9,102],["l",76.9,108,86.9,108,"B"],["l",216.5,83,216.5,90],["l",216.5,120,216.5,127],["p","M216.5 90L211.5 95L221.5 100L211.5 105L221.5 110L211.5 115L216.5 120"],["l",115,44.4,125,61.7],["l",140,87.7,150,105],["p","M125 61.7L123.2 68.5L134.3 67.9L128.2 77.2L139.3 76.5L133.2 85.8L140 87.7"],["c",115,44.4,2.6,"F"],["t",120.5,78.7,"2 \u03A9","e"],["l",203.6,60,188.3,72.9],["l",165.3,92.1,150,105],["p","M188.3 72.9L181.3 72.2L183.9 83.1L173.6 78.7L176.2 89.5L165.9 85.1L165.3 92.1"],["c",203.6,60,2.6,"F"],["t",188.8,86.5,"6 \u03A9"],["l",96.4,150,111.7,137.1],["l",134.7,117.9,150,105],["p","M111.7 137.1L118.7 137.8L116.1 126.9L126.4 131.3L123.8 120.5L134.1 124.9L134.7 117.9"],["c",96.4,150,2.6,"F"],["t",122,151,"1.5 \u03A9"],["c",150,105,2.6,"F"],["t",67.9,109,"6 V","e"],["t",228.5,109,"3 \u03A9"]]},sfig:{w:380,h:170,alt:"Equivalent circuit: the 6 V cell with two 3 ohm paths in parallel; one is the 3 ohm resistor, the other is 2 ohm in parallel with 6 ohm (1.5 ohm) in series with 1.5 ohm",d:[["l",50,30,50,62],["l",50,62,50,75],["l",50,81,50,94],["l",40,75,60,75],["l",45,81,55,81,"B"],["l",50,94,50,140],["l",50,30,260,30],["l",50,140,260,140],["l",160,30,160,65],["l",160,105,160,140],["p","M160 65L155 71.7L165 78.3L155 85L165 91.7L155 98.3L160 105"],["l",260,30,260,65],["l",260,105,260,140],["p","M260 65L255 71.7L265 78.3L255 85L265 91.7L255 98.3L260 105"],["c",160,30,2.6,"F"],["c",160,140,2.6,"F"],["t",152,90,"3 \u03A9","e"],["t",268,90,"1.5 + 1.5 = 3 \u03A9"],["t",40,74,"6 V","e"]]}},{pn:215,y:"IIT-JEE 2004",tp:"crc",st:"Charging and discharging of a capacitor",q:"A capacitor is charged using an external battery with a resistance x in series. The dashed line shows the variation of ln I with respect to time. If the resistance is changed to 2x, the new graph will be",o:["P","Q","R","S"],c:1,s:`While charging, I = (E/x) e^(\u2212t/xC), so ln I = ln(E/x) \u2212 t/(xC): a straight line with intercept ln(E/x) and slope \u22121/(xC).
With 2x the starting current E/(2x) is half as big, so the intercept is lower by ln 2: the new line starts below the dashed line.
The slope becomes \u22121/(2xC), half as steep as before, so the new line falls more gently than the dashed line.
Lines P and Q both start low; Q is the gentle one, P is steeper. S and R start too high.
So the new graph is Q, option (b).`,fig:{w:340,h:240,alt:"A graph of ln I against t with five lines: S and R start at the same high point, S falling slowly and R faster; a dashed line starts a little lower and falls about as fast as R and P; Q and P start at the same lower point, Q falling slowly and P faster",d:[["l",70,215,70,15,"a"],["l",30,205,310,205,"a"],["t",62,108,"ln I","e"],["t",316,209,"t"],["l",70,40,250,47],["l",70,40,250,90],["l",70,58,250,102,"d"],["l",70,133,250,143],["l",70,133,250,183],["t",258,51,"S"],["t",258,94,"R"],["t",258,147,"Q"],["t",258,187,"P"]]}},{pn:216,y:"IIT-JEE 2001",tp:"crc",st:"Charging and discharging of a capacitor",q:"In the given circuit, with steady current, the potential drop across the capacitor must be",o:["V","V/2","V/3","2V/3"],c:2,s:`In steady current the capacitor branch carries no current, so only the top branch (V with R) and the bottom branch (2V with 2R) form a loop.
All the cells face the same way, so the loop emf is 2V \u2212 V = V, and the loop resistance is R + 2R = 3R: I = V/(3R).
Take the left wire as reference. Going along the top branch, the right wire is at V + I R = V + V/3 = 4V/3 (the current runs against the V cell there).
Check with the bottom branch: 2V \u2212 I(2R) = 2V \u2212 2V/3 = 4V/3, the same value.
The middle branch has the cell V, so the voltage across the capacitor is 4V/3 \u2212 V = V/3.
The answer is V/3, option (c).`,fig:{w:400,h:200,alt:"Three parallel branches between two vertical wires: a cell V in series with R; a cell V in series with a capacitor C; a cell 2V in series with 2R; all cells face the same way",d:[["l",60,40,60,160],["l",300,40,300,160],["l",60,40,94,40],["l",94,40,107,40],["l",113,40,126,40],["l",113,30,113,50],["l",107,35,107,45,"B"],["l",126,40,170,40],["t",110,24,"V","m"],["l",60,100,94,100],["l",94,100,107,100],["l",113,100,126,100],["l",113,90,113,110],["l",107,95,107,105,"B"],["l",126,100,170,100],["t",110,84,"V","m"],["l",60,160,94,160],["l",94,160,107,160],["l",113,160,126,160],["l",113,150,113,170],["l",107,155,107,165,"B"],["l",126,160,170,160],["t",110,144,"2 V","m"],["l",170,40,217,40],["l",253,40,300,40],["p","M217 40L223 45L229 35L235 45L241 35L247 45L253 40"],["t",235,28,"R","m"],["l",170,100,218,100],["l",218,100,255,100],["l",263,100,300,100],["l",255,91,255,109],["l",263,91,263,109],["t",260,86,"C","m"],["l",170,160,217,160],["l",253,160,300,160],["p","M217 160L223 165L229 155L235 165L241 155L247 165L253 160"],["t",235,148,"2R","m"],["c",60,100,2.6,"F"],["c",300,100,2.6,"F"]]}},{pn:217,y:"IIT-JEE 1997",tp:"crc",st:"Charging and discharging of a capacitor",q:"A parallel combination of 0.1 M\u03A9 resistor and a 10 mF capacitor is connected across a 1.5 V source of negligible resistance. The time required for the capacitor to get charged up to 0.75 V is approximately (in seconds)",o:["\u221E","ln 2","log\u2081\u2080 2","zero"],c:3,s:`The capacitor and the resistor are in parallel, so both are connected directly across the source.
The source has negligible resistance, so there is no resistor in series with the capacitor that could slow the charging.
The time constant of the charging path is \u03C4 = (resistance in series) \xD7 C = 0 \xD7 C = 0.
So the capacitor reaches the source voltage (and therefore 0.75 V on the way) at once.
The time required is zero, option (d).
Note: the 0.1 M\u03A9 resistor only draws a steady current from the source; it does not affect the charging time.`},{pn:218,y:"JEE Advanced 2022",tp:"ckir",st:"Kirchhoff's laws",q:"The figure shows a circuit having eight resistances of 1 \u03A9 each, labelled R\u2081 to R\u2088, and two ideal batteries with voltages \u03B5\u2081 = 12 V and \u03B5\u2082 = 6 V. Which of the following statement(s) is (are) correct?",fig:{w:340,h:280,alt:"A square-shaped network: centre node C joined to top and bottom corners by R2 and R4; left corner joined to C through cell E2 and R3 and right corner joined to C through cell E1 and R1; R6 and R7 on the upper sides and R5 and R8 on the lower sides",d:[["p","M40,140 L68.6,115.8 L83,108.2 L90.6,92.6 L107.3,87.7 L114.9,72.1 L131.5,67.1 L141.4,54.2 L170,30"],["p","M170,30 L198.6,54.2 L208.5,67.1 L225.1,72.1 L232.7,87.7 L249.4,92.6 L257,108.2 L271.4,115.8 L300,140"],["p","M40,140 L68.6,164.2 L78.5,177.1 L95.1,182.1 L102.7,197.7 L119.4,202.6 L127,218.2 L141.4,225.8 L170,250"],["p","M300,140 L271.4,164.2 L257,171.8 L249.4,187.4 L232.7,192.3 L225.1,207.9 L208.5,212.9 L198.6,225.8 L170,250"],["p","M170,30 L170,54.2 L165.5,64.5 L174.5,74.7 L165.5,85 L174.5,95.3 L165.5,105.5 L170,115.8 L170,140"],["p","M170,140 L170,164.2 L165.5,174.5 L174.5,184.7 L165.5,195 L174.5,205.3 L165.5,215.5 L170,225.8 L170,250"],["l",40,140,80,140],["l",80,140,87,140],["l",87,130,87,150],["l",93,134.5,93,145.5,"B"],["l",93,140,100,140],["l",100,140,108,140],["p","M108,140 L117.3,140 L126,144 L134.7,136 L143.3,144 L152,136 L160.7,140 L170,140"],["l",170,140,190,140],["l",190,140,197,140],["l",197,130,197,150],["l",203,134.5,203,145.5,"B"],["l",203,140,210,140],["l",210,140,218,140],["p","M218,140 L230.3,140 L241.8,144 L253.3,136 L264.7,144 L276.2,136 L287.7,140 L300,140"],["c",40,140,2.4,"F"],["c",300,140,2.4,"F"],["c",170,30,2.4,"F"],["c",170,250,2.4,"F"],["c",170,140,2.4,"F"],["t",92,78,"R\u2086","e"],["t",250,78,"R\u2087"],["t",92,212,"R\u2085","e"],["t",250,212,"R\u2088"],["t",180,82,"R\u2082"],["t",180,202,"R\u2084"],["t",139,128,"R\u2083","m"],["t",90,162,"\u03B5\u2082","m"],["t",200,162,"\u03B5\u2081","m"],["t",259,128,"R\u2081","m"]]},o:["The magnitude of current flowing through R\u2081 is 7.2 A.","The magnitude of current flowing through R\u2082 is 1.2 A.","The magnitude of current flowing through R\u2083 is 4.8 A.","The magnitude of current flowing through R\u2085 is 2.4 A."],c:[0,1,2,3],s:`Call the centre point C (0 V). The top and bottom corners are joined to the left and right corners by identical pairs of 1 \u03A9 resistors, and both are joined to C by 1 \u03A9, so by symmetry the top and bottom corners are at the same potential X.
Then the left corner, the right corner and C are each joined to X by two 1 \u03A9 in parallel, 0.5 \u03A9 each. The cells act in opposite directions, so \u03B5\u2081 and \u03B5\u2082 send currents with opposite signs: take the left branch (\u03B5\u2082 with R\u2083) and the right branch (\u03B5\u2081 with R\u2081).
Write I\u2081 and I\u2083 for the currents in R\u2081 and R\u2083 leaving C. Right corner: V = 12 \u2212 I\u2081 (measured from C) and I\u2081 = (V \u2212 X)/0.5, so 3I\u2081 = 24 \u2212 2X.
Left corner: V = \u22126 \u2212 I\u2083 and I\u2083 = (V \u2212 X)/0.5, so 3I\u2083 = \u221212 \u2212 2X.
At C the three currents leaving it add to zero: I\u2081 + I\u2083 = 2X (the current taken by the 0.5 \u03A9 to X). Adding the two equations: 3(I\u2081 + I\u2083) = 12 \u2212 4X, so 6X = 12 \u2212 4X and X = 1.2 V.
I\u2081 = (24 \u2212 2.4)/3 = 7.2 A, and I\u2083 = (\u221212 \u2212 2.4)/3 = \u22124.8 A, magnitude 4.8 A.
R\u2082 carries the current from X to C: (1.2 \u2212 0)/1 = 1.2 A. R\u2085 joins the left corner (V = \u22126 + 4.8 = \u22121.2 V) to X: (1.2 \u2212 (\u22121.2))/1 = 2.4 A.
So all four statements (a), (b), (c), (d) are correct.
Note: the cells must be drawn opposite in direction as seen from C, as the printed figure shows; with the same direction the numbers would not match any option.`},{pn:219,y:"JEE Advanced 2021",tp:"cdrf",st:"Current and charge",q:"A long straight wire carries a current, I = 2 ampere. A semi-circular conducting rod is placed beside it on two conducting parallel rails of negligible resistance. Both the rails are parallel to the wire. The wire, the rod and the rails lie in the same horizontal plane, as shown in the figure. Two ends of the semi-circular rod are at distances 1 cm and 4 cm from the wire. At time t = 0, the rod starts moving on the rails with a speed v = 3.0 m s\u207B\xB9 (see the figure). A resistor R = 1.4 \u03A9 and a capacitor C\u2080 = 5.0 \u03BCF are connected in series between the rails. At time t = 0, C\u2080 is uncharged. Which of the following statement(s) is(are) correct? [\u03BC\u2080 = 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 SI units. Take ln 2 = 0.7]",fig:{w:330,h:220,alt:"A long straight wire with upward current I beside two vertical rails 1 cm and 4 cm from it; a semicircular rod across the rails moves up with speed v; a resistor R and capacitor C0 in series join the rails at the bottom",d:[["l",55,205,55,30,"d"],["l",55,70,55,45,"a"],["t",45,62,"I","e"],["l",105,20,105,125],["l",255,20,255,125],["p","M105,125 A75,75 0 0 1 255,125"],["l",105,125,105,190],["l",255,125,255,190],["l",55,140,105,140,"b"],["t",80,134,"1 cm","ms"],["l",55,160,255,160,"b"],["t",180,154,"4 cm","ms"],["c",105,190,2.4,"F"],["l",105,190,150,190],["p","M150,190 L156.8,190 L164.6,193.5 L172.5,186.5 L180.4,193.5 L188.3,190 L195,190"],["l",195,190,215,190],["l",215,180,215,200],["l",221,180,221,200],["l",221,190,255,190],["c",255,190,2.4,"F"],["t",172,208,"R","m"],["t",218,208,"C\u2080","m"],["l",295,90,295,55,"a"],["t",305,75,"v"]]},o:["Maximum current through R is 1.2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 ampere.","Maximum current through R is 3.8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 ampere.","Maximum charge on capacitor C\u2080 is 8.4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 coulomb.","Maximum charge on capacitor C\u2080 is 2.4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 coulomb."],c:[0,2],s:`Note: the printed key says (c) only; working gives (a) and (c).
The rod moves parallel to the wire, so only the horizontal part of the rod (its projection between the two rails) matters: the emf of the curved rod equals the emf of a straight rod joining its two ends.
The field of the wire at distance x is B = \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0x). A small piece dx of the rod gives d\u03B5 = vB dx.
\u03B5 = \u222B from 0.01 to 0.04 m of v \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0x) dx = v \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0) \xB7 ln 4.
\u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0) = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 2 = 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 T m, and ln 4 = 2 ln 2 = 1.4, so \u03B5 = 3 \xD7 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 1.4 = 1.68 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 V (constant, since v and the geometry do not change).
The current is largest at t = 0 when the capacitor is uncharged: I = \u03B5/R = 1.68 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076/1.4 = 1.2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 A, so (a) is true and (b) is false.
After a long time the capacitor holds the full emf: Q = C\u03B5 = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 1.68 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 = 8.4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 C, so (c) is true and (d) is false.`},{pn:220,y:"JEE Advanced 2019",tp:"crc",st:"Charging and discharging of a capacitor",q:`In the circuit shown, initially there is no charge on capacitors and keys S\u2081 and S\u2082 are open. The values of the capacitors are C\u2081 = 10 \u03BCF, C\u2082 = 30 \u03BCF and C\u2083 = C\u2084 = 80 \u03BCF. Which statements is/are correct?
(a) The key S\u2081 is kept closed for long time such that capacitors are fully charged. Now key S\u2082 is closed, at this time, the instantaneous current across 30 \u03A9 resistor (between points P and Q) will be 0.2 A (round off to 1st decimal place).
(b) If key S\u2081 is kept closed for long time such that capacitors are fully charged, the voltage across capacitor C\u2081 will be 4 V.
(c) At time t = 0, the key S\u2081 is closed, the instantaneous current in the closed circuit will be 25 mA.
(d) If S\u2081 is kept closed for long time such that capacitors are fully charged, the voltage difference between P and Q will be 10 V.`,fig:{w:410,h:240,alt:"Circuit with keys S1 and S2: left branch has S1, C1 and 70 ohm; top branch has C4, a 5 V cell and 30 ohm; right branch C3; bottom has 100 ohm; middle branch from P has S2, C2, 30 ohm and a 10 V cell down to Q",d:[["l",40,50,65,50],["l",65,50,95,33],["c",100,50,2.4,"F"],["l",100,50,200,50],["t",82,26,"S\u2081","m"],["l",40,50,40,97],["l",30,97,50,97],["l",30,103,50,103],["l",40,103,40,118],["p","M40,118 L40,130.5 L35.5,135.9 L44.5,141.2 L35.5,146.5 L44.5,151.8 L35.5,157.1 L40,162.5 L40,175"],["l",40,175,40,190],["t",28,104,"C\u2081","e"],["t",48,148,"70 \u03A9"],["c",200,50,2.4,"F"],["t",192,44,"P","e"],["l",200,50,222,50],["l",222,40,222,60],["l",228,40,228,60],["l",228,50,252,50],["l",252,40,252,60],["l",258,44.5,258,55.5,"B"],["l",258,50,275,50],["p","M275,50 L281,50 L290.6,54 L300.2,46 L309.8,54 L319.4,46 L329,50 L335,50"],["l",335,50,360,50],["t",225,38,"C\u2084","m"],["t",256,38,"5 V","m"],["t",305,38,"30 \u03A9","m"],["l",360,50,360,97],["l",350,97,370,97],["l",350,103,370,103],["l",360,103,360,190],["t",372,104,"C\u2083"],["p","M230,190 L248,190 L262,194 L276,186 L290,194 L304,186 L318,194 L332,190 L350,190"],["l",350,190,360,190],["t",290,210,"100 \u03A9","m"],["l",40,190,230,190],["c",200,190,2.4,"F"],["t",200,208,"Q","m"],["l",200,50,200,68],["l",200,68,214,86],["c",200,92,3],["l",200,95,200,105],["t",222,86,"S\u2082"],["l",190,105,210,105],["l",190,111,210,111],["l",200,111,200,118],["p","M200,118 L200,121.4 L196,126.8 L204,132.3 L196,137.7 L204,143.2 L200,148.6 L200,152"],["l",200,152,200,165],["l",190,165,210,165],["l",194.5,171,205.5,171,"B"],["l",200,174,200,190],["t",190,108,"C\u2082","e"],["t",208,138,"30 \u03A9"],["t",210,172,"10 V"]]},o:["(a)","(b)","(c)","(d)"],c:[1,2],s:`Part (c): at t = 0 the capacitors are uncharged and act like plain wires. Closing S\u2081 puts the 5 V cell in a loop with 70 \u03A9, 30 \u03A9 and 100 \u03A9 in series: I = 5/200 = 0.025 A = 25 mA. True.
Part (b): after a long time no current flows, so C\u2081, C\u2084 and C\u2083 are in series with the cell and hold the same charge. Series capacitance: 1/C = 1/10 + 1/80 + 1/80 = 1/8, so C = 8 \u03BCF and Q = 8 \xD7 5 = 40 \u03BCC.
V across C\u2081 = 40/10 = 4 V. True.
Part (d): V between P and Q equals the voltage across C\u2081 (P to Q through C\u2081 and 70 \u03A9 with no current), so it is 4 V, not 10 V. False.
Part (a): just after S\u2082 is closed, C\u2082 is uncharged (like a wire), while the other capacitors still hold their charges (like cells). So the node P settles by the current law, with the 10 V cell and 30 \u03A9 in the new branch.
Branch 1 (C\u2081 and 70 \u03A9) acts as a 4 V source with 70 \u03A9, branch 2 (C\u2084, cell, C\u2083, 30 \u03A9, 100 \u03A9) acts as a 4 V source with 130 \u03A9, and branch 3 is 10 V with 30 \u03A9. Potential of P: (4\u2212V)/70 + (4\u2212V)/130 + (10\u2212V)/30 = 0, so V \u2248 7.6 V.
Current in 30 \u03A9 = (10 \u2212 7.6)/30 \u2248 0.08 A, about 0.1 A, not 0.2 A. False.
So (b) and (c) are correct.`},{pn:221,y:"JEE Advanced 2014",tp:"ckir",st:"Kirchhoff's laws",q:"Two ideal batteries of emf V\u2081 and V\u2082 and three resistances R\u2081, R\u2082 and R\u2083 are connected as shown in the figure. The current in resistance R\u2082 would be zero if",fig:{w:260,h:225,alt:"Left branch: cell V1; right branch: R1; both meet at nodes joined by R2; below, R3 and cell V2 form a second loop",d:[["l",60,25,180,25],["l",60,25,60,47],["l",50,47,70,47],["l",54.5,53,65.5,53,"B"],["l",60,53,60,105],["l",60,105,60,185],["p","M180,25 L180,40.4 L175.5,46.9 L184.5,53.5 L175.5,60 L184.5,66.5 L175.5,73.1 L180,79.6 L180,95"],["l",180,95,180,105],["l",180,105,180,140],["l",170,146,190,146],["l",174.5,140,185.5,140,"B"],["l",180,146,180,185],["p","M60,105 L86.4,105 L97.6,109.5 L108.8,100.5 L120,109.5 L131.2,100.5 L142.4,109.5 L153.6,105 L180,105"],["p","M60,185 L86.4,185 L97.6,189.5 L108.8,180.5 L120,189.5 L131.2,180.5 L142.4,189.5 L153.6,185 L180,185"],["c",60,105,2.4,"F"],["c",180,105,2.4,"F"],["t",48,54,"V\u2081","e"],["t",192,65,"R\u2081"],["t",120,92,"R\u2082","m"],["t",192,147,"V\u2082"],["t",120,208,"R\u2083","m"]]},o:["V\u2081 = V\u2082 and R\u2081 = R\u2082 = R\u2083","V\u2081 = V\u2082 and R\u2081 = 2R\u2082 = R\u2083","V\u2081 = 2V\u2082 and 2R\u2081 = 2R\u2082 = R\u2083","2V\u2081 = V\u2082 and 2R\u2081 = R\u2082 = R\u2083"],c:[0,1,3],s:`If R\u2082 carries no current, then its two ends are at the same potential, so the two outer paths between those ends carry one common current I (R\u2081 and R\u2083 are effectively in series with the cells).
Upper loop: the cell V\u2081 drives I through R\u2081 alone, because the voltage across R\u2082 is zero: V\u2081 = I R\u2081.
Lower loop: the cell V\u2082 drives the same current through R\u2083: V\u2082 = I R\u2083.
Dividing: V\u2081/R\u2081 = V\u2082/R\u2083. This is the condition (R\u2082 does not appear).
(a) V\u2081 = V\u2082 and R\u2081 = R\u2083: satisfied.
(b) V\u2081 = V\u2082 and R\u2081 = R\u2083 (R\u2082 is anything): satisfied.
(c) V\u2081 = 2V\u2082, R\u2083 = 2R\u2081: V\u2081/R\u2081 = 2V\u2082/R\u2081 but V\u2082/R\u2083 = V\u2082/(2R\u2081); not equal.
(d) V\u2082 = 2V\u2081, R\u2083 = 2R\u2081: V\u2082/R\u2083 = 2V\u2081/(2R\u2081) = V\u2081/R\u2081; satisfied.
So (a), (b) and (d) are correct.`},{pn:222,y:"JEE Advanced 2014",tp:"crc",st:"Charging and discharging of a capacitor",q:"At time t = 0, terminal A in the circuit shown in the figure is connected to B by a key and an alternating current I(t) = I\u2080cos(\u03C9t), with I\u2080 = 1 A and \u03C9 = 500 rad s\u207B\xB9 starts flowing in it with the initial direction shown in the figure. At t = 7\u03C0/(6\u03C9), the key is switched from B to D. Now onwards only A and D are connected. A total charge Q flows from the battery to charge the capacitor fully. If C = 20 \u03BCF, R = 10 \u03A9 and the battery is ideal with emf of 50 V, identify the correct statement(s).",fig:{w:330,h:215,alt:"An ac source, a key that joins A to B or D, a capacitor of 20 microfarad, a resistor of 10 ohm and a 50 V battery",d:[["l",40,45,70,45],["l",70,45,90,45,"a"],["l",90,45,130,45],["c",130,45,2.4,"F"],["l",130,45,155,75],["c",155,75,2.4,"F"],["c",190,45,3],["l",193,45,240,45],["c",40,115,14],["p","M32,115 C36,108 40,108 40,115 S44,122 48,115"],["l",40,45,40,101],["l",40,129,40,175],["l",155,75,155,103],["l",145,103,165,103],["l",145,109,165,109],["l",155,109,155,175],["l",40,175,155,175],["p","M155,175 L169.4,175 L179.6,179 L189.9,171 L200.1,179 L210.4,171 L220.6,175 L235,175"],["l",235,175,240,175],["l",240,175,240,124],["l",230,118,250,118],["l",234.5,124,245.5,124,"B"],["l",240,118,240,45],["t",130,36,"B","m"],["t",190,36,"D","m"],["t",166,80,"A"],["t",172,112,"C = 20 \u03BCF"],["t",252,124,"50 V"],["t",195,198,"R = 10 \u03A9","m"]]},o:["Magnitude of the maximum charge on the capacitor before t = 7\u03C0/(6\u03C9) is 1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 C.","The current in the left part of the circuit just before t = 7\u03C0/(6\u03C9) is clockwise.","Immediately after A is connected to D, the current in R is 10 A.","Q = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 C."],c:[2,3],s:`With the ac source connected, the charge on the capacitor is q = \u222BI dt = (I\u2080/\u03C9) sin \u03C9t = (1/500) sin \u03C9t = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 sin \u03C9t C.
Before t = 7\u03C0/(6\u03C9), \u03C9t goes up to 7\u03C0/6, passing \u03C0/2 where sin = 1. So the maximum charge is 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 C, not 1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 C. (a) is false.
At \u03C9t = 7\u03C0/6, I = I\u2080 cos(7\u03C0/6) is negative, so the current is opposite to the initial (clockwise) direction: it is anticlockwise. (b) is false.
At that instant q = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 sin(7\u03C0/6) = \u22121 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 C. The capacitor voltage has magnitude 10\u207B\xB3/(20 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076) = 50 V and its polarity is such that it adds to the battery.
Just after A is joined to D, the battery (50 V) and the capacitor (50 V) act together on R: I = (50 + 50)/10 = 10 A. (c) is true.
The capacitor finally holds +50 V, i.e. +10\u207B\xB3 C, so the charge that flows from the battery is Q = 10\u207B\xB3 \u2212 (\u221210\u207B\xB3) = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 C. (d) is true.
So (c) and (d) are correct.`},{pn:223,y:"IIT-JEE 1989",tp:"crc",st:"Charging and discharging of a capacitor",q:"Capacitor C\u2081 of capacitance 1 micro-farad and capacitor C\u2082 of capacitance 2 microfarad are separately charged fully by a common battery. The two capacitors are then separately allowed to discharge through equal resistors at time t = 0.",o:["The current in each of the two discharging circuits is zero at t = 0.","The currents in the two discharging circuits are equal but not zero.","The currents in the two discharging circuits at t = 0 are unequal.","Capacitor C\u2081 loses 50% of its initial charge sooner than C\u2082 loses 50% of its initial charge."],c:[1,3],s:`Both capacitors are charged by the same battery, so each has voltage V across it before discharge.
At t = 0 the discharge current is I\u2080 = V/R. V and R are equal for both circuits, so the currents are equal and not zero. (a) and (c) are false, (b) is true.
The charge falls as q = q\u2080e^(\u2212t/RC). The charge halves when e^(\u2212t/RC) = 1/2, i.e. at t = RC ln 2.
C\u2081 is smaller than C\u2082, so RC is smaller for C\u2081 and it loses 50% of its charge sooner. (d) is true.
So (b) and (d) are correct.`},{pn:224,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 3 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ccel",st:"Grouping of cells",q:"Two cells of emfs 1 V and 2 V and internal resistances 2 \u03A9 and 1 \u03A9, respectively, are connected in series with an external resistance of 6 \u03A9. The total current in the circuit is I\u2081. Now the same two cells in parallel configuration are connected to same external resistance. In this case, the total current drawn is I\u2082. The value of (I\u2081/I\u2082) is x/3. The value of x is ___.",n:4,s:`Series: the emfs add and the internal resistances add. I\u2081 = (1 + 2)/(6 + 2 + 1) = 3/9 = 1/3 A.
Parallel: the equivalent emf is E = (E\u2081/r\u2081 + E\u2082/r\u2082)/(1/r\u2081 + 1/r\u2082) = (1/2 + 2/1)/(1/2 + 1) = 2.5/1.5 = 5/3 V.
The equivalent internal resistance is r = (r\u2081r\u2082)/(r\u2081 + r\u2082) = (2 \xD7 1)/3 = 2/3 \u03A9.
I\u2082 = E/(R + r) = (5/3)/(6 + 2/3) = (5/3)/(20/3) = 1/4 A.
I\u2081/I\u2082 = (1/3)/(1/4) = 4/3 = x/3, so x = 4.`},{pn:225,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ckir",st:"Kirchhoff's laws",q:"The current flowing through the 1 \u03A9 resistor is n/10 A. The value of n is ___.",fig:{w:340,h:245,alt:"Points A, B, C, D: A to B 4 ohm, A to D 4 ohm, B to C 2 ohm, C to D 2 ohm, A to C a 10 V cell with 1 ohm and current I1; a 5 V cell joins B and D",d:[["p","M40,125 L68.6,105.2 L82.7,99.7 L90.9,85.5 L107,82.9 L115.1,68.7 L131.3,66.1 L141.4,54.8 L170,35"],["p","M40,125 L68.6,144.8 L78.7,156.1 L94.9,158.7 L103,172.9 L119.1,175.5 L127.3,189.7 L141.4,195.2 L170,215"],["p","M170,35 L170,54.8 L165.5,63.2 L174.5,71.6 L165.5,80 L174.5,88.4 L165.5,96.8 L170,105.2 L170,125"],["p","M170,125 L170,144.8 L165.5,153.2 L174.5,161.6 L165.5,170 L174.5,178.4 L165.5,186.8 L170,195.2 L170,215"],["l",40,125,46,125],["l",66,125,46,125,"a"],["l",66,125,90,125],["l",90,125,97,125],["l",97,115,97,135],["l",103,119.5,103,130.5,"B"],["l",103,125,110,125],["l",110,125,118,125],["p","M118,125 L123.2,125 L133.6,129 L144,121 L154.4,129 L164.8,125 L170,125"],["l",170,35,260,122],["l",250,122,270,122],["l",254.5,128,265.5,128,"B"],["l",260,128,170,215],["c",40,125,2.4,"F"],["c",170,35,2.4,"F"],["c",170,125,2.4,"F"],["c",170,215,2.4,"F"],["t",32,129,"A","e"],["t",170,26,"B","m"],["t",180,121,"C"],["t",170,232,"D","m"],["t",92,66,"4 \u03A9","e"],["t",92,186,"4 \u03A9","e"],["t",178,85,"2 \u03A9"],["t",178,172,"2 \u03A9"],["t",144,143,"1 \u03A9","m"],["t",100,114,"10 V","m"],["t",272,129,"5 V"],["t",75,142,"I\u2081","m"]]},n:25,s:`Take the potential of C as 0 and let s = V_B + V_D. The 5 V cell fixes the difference V_B \u2212 V_D but not the sum, so only s matters.
Current law for the block B and D: the current arriving from A through the two 4 \u03A9 resistors equals the current leaving to C through the two 2 \u03A9 resistors.
(a \u2212 V_B)/4 + (a \u2212 V_D)/4 = (V_B + V_D)/2, so 2a \u2212 s = 2s and a = 1.5 s.
That current is (2a \u2212 s)/4 = s/2, and the same current must come back to A through the branch with the 10 V cell and the 1 \u03A9 from C.
Going from C to A: a drop of (s/2) \xD7 1 across the 1 \u03A9, then a rise of 10 V across the cell: a = 10 \u2212 s/2.
Substitute a = 1.5 s: 2s = 10, so s = 5 V and a = 7.5 V.
The current in the 1 \u03A9 is s/2 = 2.5 A = 25/10 A, so n = 25.
Note: the polarity of the 5 V cell only decides whether V_B \u2212 V_D is +5 or \u22125; the sum s is the same, so the answer does not depend on it.`},{pn:226,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"crc",st:"Charging and discharging of a capacitor",q:"In an electrical circuit drawn below the amount of charge stored in the capacitor is ___ \u03BCC.",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"Three branches between the same two side wires: a 10 V cell with 4 ohm, a 10 microfarad capacitor with 5 ohm, and a 6 ohm resistor",d:[["l",40,40,40,160],["l",300,40,300,160],["c",40,100,2.4,"F"],["c",300,100,2.4,"F"],["l",40,40,90,40],["l",90,40,97,40],["l",97,30,97,50],["l",103,34.5,103,45.5,"B"],["l",103,40,110,40],["l",110,40,200,40],["p","M200,40 L215,40 L229,44 L243,36 L257,44 L271,36 L285,40 L300,40"],["l",40,100,97,100],["l",97,90,97,110],["l",103,90,103,110],["l",103,100,200,100],["p","M200,100 L215,100 L229,104 L243,96 L257,104 L271,96 L285,100 L300,100"],["l",40,160,110,160],["p","M110,160 L129.8,160 L138.2,164.5 L146.6,155.5 L155,164.5 L163.4,155.5 L171.8,164.5 L180.2,160 L200,160"],["l",200,160,300,160],["t",100,27,"10 V","m"],["t",80,52,"+","m"],["t",122,52,"\u2212","m"],["t",250,28,"R\u2081 = 4 \u03A9","m"],["t",100,88,"C","m"],["t",100,122,"10 \u03BCF","m"],["t",250,88,"R\u2082 = 5 \u03A9","m"],["t",155,182,"R\u2083 = 6 \u03A9","m"]]},n:60,s:`In steady state no current flows in the branch with the capacitor, so R\u2082 has no voltage across it.
The current flows through the cell, R\u2081 (4 \u03A9) and R\u2083 (6 \u03A9) in series: I = 10/(4 + 6) = 1 A.
The capacitor branch is connected between the same two side wires as R\u2083, so its voltage equals the voltage across R\u2083: V = 1 \xD7 6 = 6 V.
Q = CV = 10 \u03BCF \xD7 6 V = 60 \u03BCC.`},{pn:227,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 27 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"crc",st:"Charging and discharging of a capacitor",q:"The charge accumulated on the capacitor connected in the following circuit is ___ \u03BCC. (Given C = 150 \u03BCF)",fig:{w:330,h:215,alt:"A 10 V cell feeds a bridge: R1 1 ohm and R3 6 ohm from the top, R2 2 ohm and R4 4 ohm to the bottom; the capacitor joins A and B",d:[["l",45,30,190,30],["l",45,30,45,87],["l",35,87,55,87],["l",39.5,93,50.5,93,"B"],["l",45,93,45,180],["l",45,180,190,180],["p","M190,30 L172.4,46.5 L162.5,50.9 L159.9,63.1 L147.6,64.9 L144.9,77.1 L132.7,78.9 L127.6,88.5 L110,105"],["p","M190,30 L207.6,46.5 L212.7,56.1 L224.9,57.9 L227.6,70.1 L239.9,71.9 L242.5,84.1 L252.4,88.5 L270,105"],["p","M110,105 L127.6,121.5 L132.7,131.1 L144.9,132.9 L147.6,145.1 L159.9,146.9 L162.5,159.1 L172.4,163.5 L190,180"],["p","M270,105 L252.4,121.5 L242.5,125.9 L239.9,138.1 L227.6,139.9 L224.9,152.1 L212.7,153.9 L207.6,163.5 L190,180"],["l",110,105,187,105],["l",187,95,187,115],["l",193,95,193,115],["l",193,105,270,105],["c",110,105,2.4,"F"],["c",270,105,2.4,"F"],["c",190,30,2.4,"F"],["c",190,180,2.4,"F"],["t",38,100,"10 V","e"],["t",55,70,"+","m"],["t",143,56,"R\u2081 1 \u03A9","e"],["t",237,56,"R\u2083 6 \u03A9"],["t",143,156,"R\u2082 2 \u03A9","e"],["t",237,156,"R\u2084 4 \u03A9"],["t",106,126,"A","e"],["t",280,109,"B"]]},n:400,s:`In steady state the capacitor carries no current, so the circuit is two parallel paths across the 10 V cell: R\u2081 + R\u2082 = 1 + 2 = 3 \u03A9 and R\u2083 + R\u2084 = 6 + 4 = 10 \u03A9.
Left path: I = 10/3 A. The potential of A below the top: drop across R\u2081 = (10/3) \xD7 1 = 10/3 V, so V_A = 10 \u2212 10/3 = 20/3 V (taking the bottom as 0), as in the figure below.
Right path: I = 10/10 = 1 A, drop across R\u2083 = 6 V, so V_B = 10 \u2212 6 = 4 V.
The capacitor is between A and B: V = 20/3 \u2212 4 = 8/3 V.
Q = CV = 150 \u03BCF \xD7 8/3 V = 400 \u03BCC.`,sfig:{w:330,h:215,alt:"The same bridge with the potential of A written as 20/3 V and of B as 4 V",d:[["l",45,30,190,30],["l",45,30,45,87],["l",35,87,55,87],["l",39.5,93,50.5,93,"B"],["l",45,93,45,180],["l",45,180,190,180],["p","M190,30 L172.4,46.5 L162.5,50.9 L159.9,63.1 L147.6,64.9 L144.9,77.1 L132.7,78.9 L127.6,88.5 L110,105"],["p","M190,30 L207.6,46.5 L212.7,56.1 L224.9,57.9 L227.6,70.1 L239.9,71.9 L242.5,84.1 L252.4,88.5 L270,105"],["p","M110,105 L127.6,121.5 L132.7,131.1 L144.9,132.9 L147.6,145.1 L159.9,146.9 L162.5,159.1 L172.4,163.5 L190,180"],["p","M270,105 L252.4,121.5 L242.5,125.9 L239.9,138.1 L227.6,139.9 L224.9,152.1 L212.7,153.9 L207.6,163.5 L190,180"],["l",110,105,187,105],["l",187,95,187,115],["l",193,95,193,115],["l",193,105,270,105],["c",110,105,2.4,"F"],["c",270,105,2.4,"F"],["c",190,30,2.4,"F"],["c",190,180,2.4,"F"],["t",38,100,"10 V","e"],["t",55,70,"+","m"],["t",143,56,"R\u2081 1 \u03A9","e"],["t",237,56,"R\u2083 6 \u03A9"],["t",143,156,"R\u2082 2 \u03A9","e"],["t",237,156,"R\u2084 4 \u03A9"],["t",106,126,"A 20/3 V","e"],["t",280,109,"B 4 V"]]}},{pn:228,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ccel",st:"EMF and internal resistance",q:"A 16 \u03A9 wire is bend to form a square loop. A 9 V battery with internal resistance 1 \u03A9 is connected across one of its sides. If a 4 \u03BCF capacitor is connected across one of its diagonals, the energy stored by the capacitor will be (x/2) \u03BCJ, where x = ___.",n:81,s:`The wire is split into four equal sides, so each side is 16/4 = 4 \u03A9 (see the figure below).
The battery is across one side (4 \u03A9). That side is in parallel with the other three sides in series (12 \u03A9): R = (4 \xD7 12)/(4 + 12) = 3 \u03A9.
Total resistance with the internal 1 \u03A9: 3 + 1 = 4 \u03A9, so the battery current is 9/4 A.
The voltage across the loop (terminal voltage) is 9 \u2212 (9/4) \xD7 1 = 27/4 V.
Current in the three-side path: (27/4)/12 = 9/16 A.
The capacitor on a diagonal sees the drop across two sides in series: V = (9/16) \xD7 (4 + 4) = 4.5 V.
Energy U = \xBDCV\xB2 = \xBD \xD7 4 \xD7 (4.5)\xB2 = 2 \xD7 20.25 = 40.5 \u03BCJ = 81/2 \u03BCJ, so x = 81.`,sfig:{w:300,h:215,alt:"A square loop of four 4 ohm sides ABCD; a 9 V cell with 1 ohm internal resistance joined across AB; a 4 microfarad capacitor across the diagonal AC",d:[["p","M70,70 L100.8,70 L113.9,74 L126.9,66 L140,74 L153.1,66 L166.1,74 L179.2,70 L210,70"],["p","M210,70 L210,92 L206,101.3 L214,110.7 L206,120 L214,129.3 L206,138.7 L210,148 L210,170"],["p","M210,170 L179.2,170 L166.1,166 L153.1,174 L140,166 L126.9,174 L113.9,166 L100.8,170 L70,170"],["p","M70,170 L70,148 L74,138.7 L66,129.3 L74,120 L66,110.7 L74,101.3 L70,92 L70,70"],["l",70,70,70,25],["l",70,25,100,25],["l",100,25,107,25],["l",107,15,107,35],["l",113,19.5,113,30.5,"B"],["l",113,25,120,25],["p","M120,25 L130.5,25 L142.8,29 L155,21 L167.3,29 L179.5,25 L190,25"],["l",190,25,210,25],["l",210,25,210,70],["l",70,70,137.5581395348837,118.25581395348837],["l",142.4418604651163,121.74418604651163,210,170],["l",131.7441860465116,126.3953488372093,143.37209302325581,110.11627906976744],["l",136.62790697674419,129.88372093023256,148.2558139534884,113.6046511627907],["c",70,70,2.4,"F"],["c",210,70,2.4,"F"],["t",62,66,"A","e"],["t",218,66,"B"],["t",218,178,"C"],["t",62,178,"D","e"],["t",110,14,"9 V","m"],["t",155,16,"1 \u03A9","m"],["t",140,58,"4 \u03A9","m"],["t",224,124,"4 \u03A9"],["t",140,192,"4 \u03A9","m"],["t",56,124,"4 \u03A9","e"],["t",172,102,"4 \u03BCF","s"]]}},{pn:229,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ccmb",st:"Series and parallel combinations",q:"In the given circuit, the current flowing through the resistance 20 \u03A9 is 0.3 A, while the ammeter reads 0.9 A. The value of R\u2081 is ___ \u03A9.",fig:{w:320,h:160,alt:"Three parallel branches R1, 20 ohm and 15 ohm; the main current enters from the left and leaves through an ammeter on the right",d:[["l",20,80,36,80],["l",36,80,66,80,"a"],["l",66,80,90,80],["l",90,40,90,120],["l",230,40,230,120],["p","M90,40 L120.8,40 L133.9,44.5 L146.9,35.5 L160,44.5 L173.1,35.5 L186.1,44.5 L199.2,40 L230,40"],["p","M90,80 L120.8,80 L133.9,84.5 L146.9,75.5 L160,84.5 L173.1,75.5 L186.1,84.5 L199.2,80 L230,80"],["p","M90,120 L120.8,120 L133.9,124.5 L146.9,115.5 L160,124.5 L173.1,115.5 L186.1,124.5 L199.2,120 L230,120"],["c",90,80,2.4,"F"],["c",230,80,2.4,"F"],["l",230,80,250,80],["l",250,80,256,80],["c",266,80,10],["t",266,84,"A","m"],["l",276,80,282,80],["l",282,80,308,80,"a"],["t",160,27,"R\u2081","m"],["t",160,69,"20 \u03A9","m"],["t",160,109,"15 \u03A9","m"]]},n:30,s:`The three branches are in parallel, so each has the same voltage.
V = 0.3 A \xD7 20 \u03A9 = 6 V.
Current in the 15 \u03A9 branch = 6/15 = 0.4 A.
The ammeter is on the main line, so it reads the total: 0.9 A = 0.3 + 0.4 + I\u2081, giving I\u2081 = 0.2 A.
R\u2081 = V/I\u2081 = 6/0.2 = 30 \u03A9.`},{pn:230,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ccel",st:"EMF and internal resistance",q:"Two cells are connected in opposition as shown. Cell E\u2081 is of 8 V emf and 2 \u03A9 internal resistance, the cell E\u2082 is of 2 V emf and 4 \u03A9 internal resistance. The terminal potential difference of cell E\u2082 is ___ V.",fig:{w:330,h:150,alt:"A loop with points A, B, C: cell E1 between A and B with its positive plate on the left, cell E2 between B and C with its positive plate on the right",d:[["l",40,50,92,50],["l",92,40,92,60],["l",98,44.5,98,55.5,"B"],["l",98,50,160,50],["c",160,50,2.4,"F"],["l",160,50,212,50],["l",218,40,218,60],["l",212,44.5,212,55.5,"B"],["l",218,50,280,50],["l",280,50,280,125],["l",280,125,40,125],["l",40,125,40,50],["t",30,44,"A","e"],["t",160,38,"B","m"],["t",290,44,"C"],["t",95,32,"E\u2081","m"],["t",215,32,"E\u2082","m"]]},n:6,s:`The cells face each other, so the net emf is the difference: 8 \u2212 2 = 6 V. The stronger cell E\u2081 drives the current.
Total resistance = 2 + 4 = 6 \u03A9, so I = 6/6 = 1 A.
The current flows into the positive terminal of E\u2082 (it is being charged), so its terminal voltage is larger than its emf: V = E\u2082 + Ir\u2082 = 2 + 1 \xD7 4 = 6 V.
Check with E\u2081: V = 8 \u2212 1 \xD7 2 = 6 V, the same, as it must be since they are joined together.`},{pn:231,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cpot",st:"Galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"As shown in the figure, the voltmeter reads 2 V across 5 \u03A9 resistor. The resistance of the voltmeter is ___ \u03A9.",fig:{w:300,h:175,alt:"A 3 V cell and a 2 ohm resistor in series with a 5 ohm resistor; a voltmeter is connected across the 5 ohm resistor",d:[["l",40,30,92,30],["l",92,30,99,30],["l",99,20,99,40],["l",105,24.5,105,35.5,"B"],["l",105,30,112,30],["l",112,30,250,30],["l",40,30,40,130],["l",40,130,60,130],["p","M60,130 L77.6,130 L85.1,134.5 L92.5,125.5 L100,134.5 L107.5,125.5 L114.9,134.5 L122.4,130 L140,130"],["l",140,130,250,130],["l",170,30,170,40],["p","M170,30 L170,52 L165.5,61.3 L174.5,70.7 L165.5,80 L174.5,89.3 L165.5,98.7 L170,108 L170,130"],["c",170,30,2.4,"F"],["c",170,130,2.4,"F"],["l",250,30,250,70],["c",250,80,10],["t",250,84,"V","m"],["l",250,90,250,130],["t",102,18,"3 V","m"],["t",180,84,"5 \u03A9"],["t",100,152,"2 \u03A9","m"]]},n:20,s:`The 2 \u03A9 resistor is in series with the parallel combination of 5 \u03A9 and the voltmeter.
The voltage across the parallel part is 2 V, so the voltage across the 2 \u03A9 resistor is 3 \u2212 2 = 1 V.
Current in the circuit = 1/2 = 0.5 A.
The parallel part has 2 V and carries 0.5 A, so its resistance is 2/0.5 = 4 \u03A9.
For the parallel pair, 1/4 = 1/5 + 1/R_V, so 1/R_V = 1/4 \u2212 1/5 = 1/20.
The voltmeter resistance is 20 \u03A9.`},{pn:232,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 11 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ckir",st:"Kirchhoff's laws",q:"In the circuit diagram shown in figure given below, the current flowing through resistance 3 \u03A9 is (x/3) A. The value of x is ___.",fig:{w:320,h:195,alt:"A 4 V cell with 0.5 ohm and an 8 V cell with 1 ohm in opposition in series with 4.5 ohm and a parallel pair of 3 ohm and 6 ohm",d:[["l",40,40,92,40],["l",98,30,98,50],["l",92,34.5,92,45.5,"B"],["l",98,40,192,40],["l",192,30,192,50],["l",198,34.5,198,45.5,"B"],["l",198,40,280,40],["l",280,40,280,130],["l",280,130,250,130],["l",40,40,40,130],["l",40,130,55,130],["p","M55,130 L72.6,130 L80.1,134.5 L87.5,125.5 L95,134.5 L102.5,125.5 L109.9,134.5 L117.4,130 L135,130"],["l",135,130,160,130],["c",160,130,2.4,"F"],["l",160,110,160,150],["l",250,110,250,150],["c",250,130,2.4,"F"],["p","M160,110 L179.8,110 L188.2,114.5 L196.6,105.5 L205,114.5 L213.4,105.5 L221.8,114.5 L230.2,110 L250,110"],["p","M160,150 L179.8,150 L188.2,154.5 L196.6,145.5 L205,154.5 L213.4,145.5 L221.8,154.5 L230.2,150 L250,150"],["t",95,28,"4 V","m"],["t",95,62,"r\u2081 = 0.5 \u03A9","m"],["t",195,28,"8 V","m"],["t",195,62,"r\u2082 = 1 \u03A9","m"],["t",95,152,"4.5 \u03A9","m"],["t",205,100,"3 \u03A9","m"],["t",205,172,"6 \u03A9","m"]]},n:1,s:`The two cells are in opposition (their positive plates face each other), so the net emf is 8 \u2212 4 = 4 V.
Total resistance: 0.5 + 1 + 4.5 + (3 \xD7 6)/(3 + 6) = 0.5 + 1 + 4.5 + 2 = 8 \u03A9.
Main current I = 4/8 = 0.5 A.
The 3 \u03A9 and 6 \u03A9 are in parallel, so the current divides in the inverse ratio of resistances: the 3 \u03A9 gets I \xD7 6/(3 + 6) = 0.5 \xD7 2/3 = 1/3 A.
So x/3 = 1/3 and x = 1.`},{pn:233,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 11 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ccel",st:"EMF and internal resistance",q:"Two identical cells each of emf 1.5 V are connected in series across a 10 \u03A9 resistance. An ideal voltmeter connected across 10 \u03A9 resistance reads 1.5 V. The internal resistance of each cell is ___ \u03A9.",n:5,s:`Two cells in series give a total emf of 3 V and a total internal resistance of 2r.
The voltmeter across the 10 \u03A9 reads 1.5 V, so the current is I = 1.5/10 = 0.15 A.
The emf is shared between the external resistor and the internal resistances: 3 = 1.5 + 0.15 \xD7 2r.
0.3r = 1.5, so r = 5 \u03A9.`},{pn:234,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 13 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"crc",st:"Charging and discharging of a capacitor",q:"In the circuit shown, the energy stored in the capacitor is n \u03BCJ. The value of n is ___.",fig:{w:340,h:230,alt:"A 12 V cell across a diamond: 3 ohm and 4 ohm from the top, 9 ohm and 2 ohm to the bottom; a 6 microfarad capacitor joins the left and right corners",d:[["l",40,30,210,30],["l",40,30,40,102],["l",30,102,50,102],["l",34.5,108,45.5,108,"B"],["l",40,108,40,195],["l",40,195,210,195],["p","M210,30 L194.6,48 L185.4,53.4 L184.2,65.6 L172.3,68.7 L171.1,80.9 L159.3,84 L155.4,94 L140,112"],["p","M210,30 L225.4,48 L229.3,58 L241.1,61.1 L242.3,73.3 L254.2,76.4 L255.4,88.6 L264.6,94 L280,112"],["p","M140,112 L155.4,130.3 L159.3,140.3 L171.1,143.5 L172.3,155.8 L184.2,159 L185.4,171.2 L194.6,176.7 L210,195"],["p","M280,112 L264.6,130.3 L255.4,135.8 L254.2,148 L242.3,151.2 L241.1,163.5 L229.3,166.7 L225.4,176.7 L210,195"],["l",140,112,207,112],["l",207,102,207,122],["l",213,102,213,122],["l",213,112,280,112],["c",140,112,2.4,"F"],["c",280,112,2.4,"F"],["c",210,30,2.4,"F"],["c",210,195,2.4,"F"],["t",32,108,"12 V","e"],["t",160,62,"3 \u03A9","e"],["t",260,62,"4 \u03A9"],["t",160,168,"9 \u03A9","e"],["t",260,168,"2 \u03A9"],["t",210,134,"6 \u03BCF","m"]]},n:75,s:`In steady state no current flows through the capacitor, so there are two parallel paths across the 12 V cell: 3 \u03A9 + 9 \u03A9 = 12 \u03A9 on the left and 4 \u03A9 + 2 \u03A9 = 6 \u03A9 on the right.
Left current = 12/12 = 1 A; the potential of the left corner (above the 9 \u03A9) is 1 \xD7 9 = 9 V (taking the bottom as 0).
Right current = 12/6 = 2 A; the right corner potential is 2 \xD7 2 = 4 V (see the figure below).
The capacitor voltage is 9 \u2212 4 = 5 V.
U = \xBDCV\xB2 = \xBD \xD7 6 \u03BCF \xD7 25 V\xB2 = 75 \u03BCJ, so n = 75.`,sfig:{w:340,h:230,alt:"The same circuit with the left corner at 9 V and the right corner at 4 V",d:[["l",40,30,210,30],["l",40,30,40,102],["l",30,102,50,102],["l",34.5,108,45.5,108,"B"],["l",40,108,40,195],["l",40,195,210,195],["p","M210,30 L194.6,48 L185.4,53.4 L184.2,65.6 L172.3,68.7 L171.1,80.9 L159.3,84 L155.4,94 L140,112"],["p","M210,30 L225.4,48 L229.3,58 L241.1,61.1 L242.3,73.3 L254.2,76.4 L255.4,88.6 L264.6,94 L280,112"],["p","M140,112 L155.4,130.3 L159.3,140.3 L171.1,143.5 L172.3,155.8 L184.2,159 L185.4,171.2 L194.6,176.7 L210,195"],["p","M280,112 L264.6,130.3 L255.4,135.8 L254.2,148 L242.3,151.2 L241.1,163.5 L229.3,166.7 L225.4,176.7 L210,195"],["l",140,112,207,112],["l",207,102,207,122],["l",213,102,213,122],["l",213,112,280,112],["c",140,112,2.4,"F"],["c",280,112,2.4,"F"],["c",210,30,2.4,"F"],["c",210,195,2.4,"F"],["t",32,108,"12 V","e"],["t",160,62,"3 \u03A9","e"],["t",260,62,"4 \u03A9"],["t",160,168,"9 \u03A9","e"],["t",260,168,"2 \u03A9"],["t",210,134,"6 \u03BCF","m"],["t",132,116,"9 V","e"],["t",288,116,"4 V"],["t",210,218,"0","m"]]}},{pn:235,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 15 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ckir",st:"Wheatstone bridge",q:"A network of four resistances is connected to 9 V battery, as shown in figure. The magnitude of voltage difference between the points A and B is ___ V.",fig:{w:320,h:300,alt:"A diamond with A at the top and B at the bottom: 2 ohm and 4 ohm from A, 4 ohm and 2 ohm to B; the 9 V battery is joined between the left and right corners",d:[["p","M160,35 L138,54.8 L126.3,60.6 L121.7,74.2 L107.7,77.4 L103,91 L89,94.2 L82,105.2 L60,125"],["p","M160,35 L182,54.8 L189,65.8 L203,69 L207.7,82.6 L221.7,85.8 L226.3,99.4 L238,105.2 L260,125"],["p","M60,125 L82,144.8 L89,155.8 L103,159 L107.7,172.6 L121.7,175.8 L126.3,189.4 L138,195.2 L160,215"],["p","M260,125 L238,144.8 L226.3,150.6 L221.7,164.2 L207.7,167.4 L203,181 L189,184.2 L182,195.2 L160,215"],["l",60,125,60,255],["l",60,255,147,255],["l",147,245,147,265],["l",153,249.5,153,260.5,"B"],["l",153,255,260,255],["l",260,255,260,125],["c",160,35,2.4,"F"],["c",160,215,2.4,"F"],["c",60,125,2.4,"F"],["c",260,125,2.4,"F"],["t",100,70,"2 \u03A9","e"],["t",222,70,"4 \u03A9"],["t",100,186,"4 \u03A9","e"],["t",222,186,"2 \u03A9"],["t",150,280,"9 V","m"],["t",160,26,"A","m"],["t",160,236,"B","m"]]},n:3,s:`The battery is across the left and right corners (long plate, the + terminal, on the left), and each of the two paths from one corner to the other has resistance 2 + 4 = 6 \u03A9.
Each path carries I = 9/6 = 1.5 A.
Take the right corner as 0 V, so the left corner is at 9 V. Going over the top path, A is after the 2 \u03A9: V_A = 9 \u2212 1.5 \xD7 2 = 6 V.
Going over the bottom path, B is after the 4 \u03A9: V_B = 9 \u2212 1.5 \xD7 4 = 3 V (see the figure below).
The magnitude of the difference is |6 \u2212 3| = 3 V.`,sfig:{w:320,h:300,alt:"Taking the right corner as 0 V: the left corner is at 9 V, A is at 6 V and B is at 3 V",d:[["p","M160,35 L138,54.8 L126.3,60.6 L121.7,74.2 L107.7,77.4 L103,91 L89,94.2 L82,105.2 L60,125"],["p","M160,35 L182,54.8 L189,65.8 L203,69 L207.7,82.6 L221.7,85.8 L226.3,99.4 L238,105.2 L260,125"],["p","M60,125 L82,144.8 L89,155.8 L103,159 L107.7,172.6 L121.7,175.8 L126.3,189.4 L138,195.2 L160,215"],["p","M260,125 L238,144.8 L226.3,150.6 L221.7,164.2 L207.7,167.4 L203,181 L189,184.2 L182,195.2 L160,215"],["l",60,125,60,255],["l",60,255,147,255],["l",147,245,147,265],["l",153,249.5,153,260.5,"B"],["l",153,255,260,255],["l",260,255,260,125],["c",160,35,2.4,"F"],["c",160,215,2.4,"F"],["c",60,125,2.4,"F"],["c",260,125,2.4,"F"],["t",100,70,"2 \u03A9","e"],["t",222,70,"4 \u03A9"],["t",100,186,"4 \u03A9","e"],["t",222,186,"2 \u03A9"],["t",150,280,"9 V","m"],["t",160,26,"A  6 V","m"],["t",160,236,"B  3 V","m"],["t",52,129,"9 V","e"],["t",268,129,"0"]]}},{pn:236,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ckir",st:"Kirchhoff's laws",q:"In the given circuit, the value of |(I\u2081 + I\u2083)/I\u2082| is ___.",fig:{w:370,h:195,alt:"Two cells, 10 V and 20 V, with three 10 ohm resistors: two in parallel (currents I1 and I2) and one in the outer loop (current I3)",d:[["l",40,40,127,40],["l",133,30,133,50],["l",127,34.5,127,45.5,"B"],["l",133,40,190,40],["c",190,40,2.4,"F"],["l",190,40,247,40],["l",247,30,247,50],["l",253,34.5,253,45.5,"B"],["l",253,40,330,40],["l",330,40,330,150],["l",40,40,40,150],["l",40,150,60,150],["l",60,150,98,150,"a"],["p","M95,150 L106.3,150 L119.4,154 L132.5,146 L145.6,154 L158.8,150 L170,150"],["l",170,150,330,150],["c",40,95,2.4,"F"],["l",40,95,70,95],["l",70,80,70,110],["l",160,80,160,110],["l",190,40,190,95],["l",190,95,160,95],["c",160,95,2.4,"F"],["c",70,95,2.4,"F"],["p","M70,80 L77.2,80 L88.7,84 L100.2,76 L111.8,84 L123.3,76 L134.8,80 L142,80"],["l",142,80,160,80],["l",158,80,142,80,"a"],["p","M70,110 L77.2,110 L88.7,114 L100.2,106 L111.8,114 L123.3,106 L134.8,110 L142,110"],["l",142,110,160,110],["l",158,110,142,110,"a"],["t",122,28,"10 V","m"],["t",252,28,"20 V","m"],["t",106,70,"10 \u03A9","m"],["t",106,128,"10 \u03A9","m"],["t",150,72,"I\u2081","m"],["t",150,128,"I\u2082","m"],["t",133,172,"10 \u03A9","m"],["t",70,168,"I\u2083","m"]]},n:2,s:`The two 10 \u03A9 resistors carrying I\u2081 and I\u2082 are in parallel with each other, so they have the same voltage and the same current: I\u2081 = I\u2082.
The 10 V cell is joined directly across this parallel pair, so each of them has 10 V across it: I\u2081 = I\u2082 = 10/10 = 1 A.
Now take the other path between the same two points: the 20 V cell and the 10 \u03A9 resistor of I\u2083. The same 10 V must appear across it: 20 \u2212 10 I\u2083 = 10, so I\u2083 = 1 A.
Then |(I\u2081 + I\u2083)/I\u2082| = (1 + 1)/1 = 2.
Note: this uses the polarity of the page (the + plate of each cell on the junction side).`},{pn:237,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 25 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ccel",st:"Grouping of cells",q:"Two cells are connected between points A and B as shown. Cell 1 has emf of 12 V and internal resistance of 3 \u03A9. Cell 2 has emf of 6 V and internal resistance of 6 \u03A9. An external resistor R of 4 \u03A9 is connected across A and B. The current flowing through R will be ___ A.",fig:{w:340,h:215,alt:"Two cells in parallel between A and B: cell 1 of 12 V with 3 ohm and cell 2 of 6 V with 6 ohm, joined with opposite polarity",d:[["c",25,108,3],["l",28,108,55,108],["l",55,108,90,55],["l",90,55,115,55],["l",115,55,122,55],["l",122,45,122,65],["l",128,49.5,128,60.5,"B"],["l",128,55,135,55],["l",135,55,160,55],["p","M160,55 L172,55 L186,59 L200,51 L214,59 L228,55 L240,55"],["l",240,55,262,55],["l",262,55,295,108],["l",55,108,90,160],["l",90,160,115,160],["l",115,160,122,160],["l",128,150,128,170],["l",122,154.5,122,165.5,"B"],["l",128,160,135,160],["l",135,160,160,160],["p","M160,160 L172,160 L186,164 L200,156 L214,164 L228,160 L240,160"],["l",240,160,262,160],["l",262,160,295,108],["l",295,108,318,108],["c",321,108,3],["t",25,128,"A","m"],["t",321,128,"B","m"],["t",125,42,"12 V","m"],["t",200,43,"3 \u03A9","m"],["t",190,72,"Cell 1","m"],["t",125,148,"6 V","m"],["t",200,148,"6 \u03A9","m"],["t",190,185,"Cell 2","m"]]},n:1,s:`The two cells are in parallel between A and B but face opposite ways (the figure shows opposite polarity), so one emf counts as +12 V and the other as \u22126 V.
For cells in parallel across a point pair, V_AB = (\u03A3 E/r)/(\u03A3 1/r) when R is connected too: V_AB = (12/3 \u2212 6/6)/(1/3 + 1/6 + 1/4).
Numerator = 4 \u2212 1 = 3, denominator = (4 + 2 + 3)/12 = 3/4, so V_AB = 3/(3/4) = 4 V.
The current through R = V_AB/R = 4/4 = 1 A.
Note: this uses the junction law at A: (12 \u2212 V)/3 + (\u22126 \u2212 V)/6 = V/4, which gives the same V = 4 V.`},{pn:238,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ckir",st:"Kirchhoff's laws",q:"In the following circuit, the magnitude of current I\u2081 is ___.",fig:{w:330,h:235,alt:"A network with 2 ohm resistors at the top and bottom left, two 1 ohm resistors in the middle branch, a 5 V cell with 1 ohm, and a 2 V cell on the right carrying current I1",d:[["l",40,40,40,200],["p","M40,40 L57.6,40 L65.1,44.5 L72.5,35.5 L80,44.5 L87.5,35.5 L94.9,44.5 L102.4,40 L120,40"],["l",120,40,142,40,"a"],["l",142,40,260,40],["p","M40,200 L57.6,200 L65.1,204.5 L72.5,195.5 L80,204.5 L87.5,195.5 L94.9,204.5 L102.4,200 L120,200"],["l",120,200,260,200],["l",260,40,260,117],["l",260,123,260,200],["l",250,117,270,117],["l",254.5,123,265.5,123,"B"],["l",260,95,260,70,"a"],["l",260,175,260,150,"a"],["p","M150,40 L150,52.1 L145.5,57.2 L154.5,62.4 L145.5,67.5 L154.5,72.6 L145.5,77.8 L150,82.9 L150,95"],["l",150,95,150,135],["p","M150,135 L150,147.1 L145.5,152.2 L154.5,157.4 L145.5,162.5 L154.5,167.6 L145.5,172.8 L150,177.9 L150,190"],["l",150,190,150,200],["c",150,40,2.4,"F"],["c",150,120,2.4,"F"],["c",150,200,2.4,"F"],["l",150,120,145,120],["l",125,120,132,120],["l",132,110,132,130],["l",138,114.5,138,125.5,"B"],["l",138,120,145,120],["p","M70,120 L75.5,120 L84.3,124 L93.1,116 L101.9,124 L110.7,116 L119.5,120 L125,120"],["l",70,120,64,120],["l",64,120,44,120,"a"],["t",80,29,"2 \u03A9","m"],["t",133,31,"I\u2083","m"],["t",158,72,"1 \u03A9"],["t",158,168,"1 \u03A9"],["t",48,112,"I\u2082","m"],["t",95,140,"1 \u03A9","m"],["t",135,108,"5 V","m"],["t",80,219,"2 \u03A9","m"],["t",272,125,"2 V"],["t",272,80,"I\u2081"],["t",272,168,"I\u2081"]]},n:1.5,s:`Note: the printed key says 2; working gives 1.5 for I\u2081 (the current in the 2 V cell). 2 A is the current I\u2082 in the 5 V branch.
Let X be the left wire, T the top end of the middle branch, M the middle junction and B its bottom end. The ideal 2 V cell fixes V_T \u2212 V_B = 2 V.
Current law at M, with I\u2082 flowing from M to X through the 1 \u03A9 and the 5 V cell, and at X (I\u2082 splits into the two 2 \u03A9 resistors), solved with V_T \u2212 V_B = 2:
V_T = V_X \u2212 1 V and V_M = V_B = V_X \u2212 3 V (positive plate of the 5 V cell towards X, as drawn).
Then I\u2083 = (V_X \u2212 V_T)/2 = 0.5 A, the lower 2 \u03A9 carries (V_X \u2212 V_B)/2 = 1.5 A, and the lower middle 1 \u03A9 has equal ends, so it carries no current.
All of the 1.5 A from the lower 2 \u03A9 must go up the 2 V cell branch: I\u2081 = 1.5 A. (Check at T: 0.5 A in from I\u2083 plus 1.5 A from the cell = 2 A down the upper 1 \u03A9, which has 2 V across it.)
The value I\u2081 = 0.75 \xD7 2 V = 1.5 A does not depend on the polarity of the 5 V cell.`},{pn:239,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ccel",st:"Grouping of cells",q:"Two identical cells, when connected either in parallel or in series gives same current in an external resistance 5 \u03A9. The internal resistance of each cell will be ___ \u03A9.",n:5,s:`Let each cell have emf E and internal resistance r.
Series: I = 2E/(5 + 2r).
Parallel: the emf stays E and the internal resistance becomes r/2: I = E/(5 + r/2).
The currents are equal: 2/(5 + 2r) = 1/(5 + r/2), so 2(5 + r/2) = 5 + 2r.
10 + r = 5 + 2r, so r = 5 \u03A9.`},{pn:240,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ckir",st:"Kirchhoff's laws",q:"In the given figure, the value of V\u2080 will be ___ V.",fig:{w:300,h:200,alt:"Three branches, each with a cell (2 V, 4 V, 6 V) and a 1 kilo-ohm resistor, from earth to one common point V0",d:[["l",40,35,40,165],["l",28,165,52,165],["l",33,171,47,171],["l",38,177,42,177],["c",40,85,2.4,"F"],["c",40,135,2.4,"F"],["l",40,35,80,35],["l",80,35,87,35],["l",93,25,93,45],["l",87,29.5,87,40.5,"B"],["l",93,35,100,35],["l",100,35,130,35],["p","M130,35 L147.6,35 L155.1,39.5 L162.5,30.5 L170,39.5 L177.5,30.5 L184.9,39.5 L192.4,35 L210,35"],["l",210,35,230,35],["t",90,23,"2 V","m"],["t",170,23,"1 k\u03A9","m"],["l",40,85,80,85],["l",80,85,87,85],["l",93,75,93,95],["l",87,79.5,87,90.5,"B"],["l",93,85,100,85],["l",100,85,130,85],["p","M130,85 L147.6,85 L155.1,89.5 L162.5,80.5 L170,89.5 L177.5,80.5 L184.9,89.5 L192.4,85 L210,85"],["l",210,85,230,85],["t",90,73,"4 V","m"],["t",170,73,"1 k\u03A9","m"],["l",40,135,80,135],["l",80,135,87,135],["l",93,125,93,145],["l",87,129.5,87,140.5,"B"],["l",93,135,100,135],["l",100,135,130,135],["p","M130,135 L147.6,135 L155.1,139.5 L162.5,130.5 L170,139.5 L177.5,130.5 L184.9,139.5 L192.4,135 L210,135"],["l",210,135,230,135],["t",90,123,"6 V","m"],["t",170,123,"1 k\u03A9","m"],["l",230,35,230,135],["c",230,35,2.4,"F"],["c",230,85,2.4,"F"],["c",230,135,2.4,"F"],["l",230,135,262,135],["c",265,135,3],["t",275,139,"V\u2080"]]},n:4,s:`The three branches (cell + 1 k\u03A9) join the earth on one side and the point V\u2080 on the other. Nothing else is connected to V\u2080, so the net current into V\u2080 from the three branches is zero.
Current law at V\u2080: (2 \u2212 V\u2080)/1000 + (4 \u2212 V\u2080)/1000 + (6 \u2212 V\u2080)/1000 = 0.
Because all three resistors are equal: 12 \u2212 3V\u2080 = 0.
V\u2080 = 4 V.`},{pn:241,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"crc",st:"Charging and discharging of a capacitor",q:"As shown in the figure, in steady state, the charge stored in the capacitor is ____ \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 C. (E = 10 V, r = 10 \u03A9, R = 100 \u03A9, C = 1.1 \u03BCF, R\u2032 = 200 \u03A9)",n:10,s:`In steady state a capacitor carries no current, so the branch with C and R\u2032 has zero current and R\u2032 has no voltage drop.
The current flows only through the cell (with r) and R: I = E/(r + R) = 10/(10 + 100) = 1/11 A.
The capacitor branch is in parallel with R, so the capacitor voltage equals the voltage across R: V = IR = (1/11) \xD7 100 = 100/11 V.
Q = CV = 1.1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 100/11 = 10 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 C.
So the blank is 10.`,fig:{w:340,h:195,alt:"Three parallel branches between two vertical wires: a 10 V cell with r = 10 ohm, a resistor R = 100 ohm, and a 1.1 microfarad capacitor in series with R-dash = 200 ohm",d:[["l",50,40,50,150],["l",290,40,290,150],["l",50,40,97,40],["l",97,29,97,51],["l",103,34,103,46,"B"],["l",103,40,150,40],["t",100,24,"E = 10 V","m"],["p","M150 40 L198 40 L201.7 35 L209 45 L216.3 35 L223.7 45 L231 35 L238.3 45 L242 40 L290 40"],["t",220,60,"r = 10 \u03A9","m"],["p","M50 95 L148 95 L151.7 90 L159 100 L166.3 90 L173.7 100 L181 90 L188.3 100 L192 95 L290 95"],["t",170,85,"R = 100 \u03A9","m"],["l",50,150,106,150],["l",106,140,106,160],["l",114,140,114,160],["p","M114 150 L180 150 L183.7 145 L191 155 L198.3 145 L205.7 155 L213 145 L220.3 155 L224 150 L290 150"],["t",110,134,"C = 1.1 \u03BCF","m"],["t",220,172,"R\u2032 = 200 \u03A9","m"]]}},{pn:242,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ckir",st:"Kirchhoff's laws",q:"For the network shown below, the value of V_B \u2212 V_A is ____ V.",n:10,s:`All three elements are in one loop, so the same current flows through each.
Current: I = 15/(1 + 2) = 5 A.
Going from A to B we cross the cell (a rise of 15 V, because its long plate, the + terminal, is on the B side) and then the 1 \u03A9 resistor in the direction of the current (a drop of I \xD7 1 = 5 V).
V_B \u2212 V_A = 15 \u2212 5 = 10 V.`,fig:{w:340,h:170,alt:"Series loop: A, a 15 volt cell with its positive plate towards B, a 1 ohm resistor, B, and a 2 ohm resistor back to A",d:[["c",30,50,2.6,"F"],["c",300,50,2.6,"F"],["t",30,38,"A","m"],["t",300,38,"B","m"],["l",30,50,97,50],["l",97,44,97,56,"B"],["l",103,39,103,61],["l",103,50,150,50],["t",100,34,"15 V","m"],["p","M150 50 L203 50 L206.7 45 L214 55 L221.3 45 L228.7 55 L236 45 L243.3 55 L247 50 L300 50"],["t",225,38,"1 \u03A9","m"],["l",300,50,300,130],["l",30,50,30,130],["p","M30 130 L143 130 L146.7 125 L154 135 L161.3 125 L168.7 135 L176 125 L183.3 135 L187 130 L300 130"],["t",165,152,"2 \u03A9","m"]]}},{pn:243,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ccel",st:"EMF and internal resistance",q:"First, a set of n equal resistors of 10 \u03A9 each are connected in series to a battery of emf 20 V and internal resistance 10 \u03A9. A current I is observed to flow. Then, the n resistors are connected in parallel to the same battery. It is observed that the current is increased 20 times. The value of n is ____.",n:20,s:`Current from a battery is I = E/(external resistance + internal resistance).
Series: I = 20/(10n + 10).
Parallel: the n resistors give 10/n, so I\u2032 = 20/(10/n + 10) = 20n/(10 + 10n).
Ratio I\u2032/I = [20n/(10 + 10n)] \xD7 [(10n + 10)/20] = n.
The ratio is given as 20, so n = 20.`},{pn:244,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 31 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"crc",st:"Charging and discharging of a capacitor",q:"A capacitor of 50 \u03BCF is connected in a circuit as shown in the figure. The charge on the upper plate of the capacitor is ____ \u03BCC.",n:100,s:`When the capacitor is fully charged no current flows into it, so the three 2 k\u03A9 resistors carry the same current.
I = 6 V/(2 + 2 + 2) k\u03A9 = 1 mA.
The capacitor is in parallel with the last 2 k\u03A9 resistor, so its voltage is V = 1 mA \xD7 2 k\u03A9 = 2 V.
Q = CV = 50 \u03BCF \xD7 2 V = 100 \u03BCC.
The upper plate is joined to the higher potential side (the node above the last resistor), so its charge is +100 \u03BCC.`,fig:{w:340,h:215,alt:"6 V cell, two 2 kilo-ohm resistors in series, a third 2 kilo-ohm resistor to earth, and a 50 microfarad capacitor connected across the third resistor",d:[["l",60,30,160,30],["p","M160 30 L160 30 L165 33.3 L155 40 L165 46.7 L155 53.3 L165 60 L155 66.7 L160 70 L160 70"],["p","M160 70 L160 70 L165 73.3 L155 80 L165 86.7 L155 93.3 L165 100 L155 106.7 L160 110 L160 110"],["p","M160 110 L160 122.5 L165 126.7 L155 135 L165 143.3 L155 151.7 L165 160 L155 168.3 L160 172.5 L160 185"],["l",160,185,160,194],["l",148,194,172,194],["l",153,199,167,199],["l",158,204,162,204],["t",150,56,"2 k\u03A9","e"],["t",150,96,"2 k\u03A9","e"],["t",150,152,"2 k\u03A9","e"],["l",160,110,240,110],["l",240,110,240,140],["l",230,140,250,140],["l",230,148,250,148],["l",240,148,240,185],["l",60,185,240,185],["c",160,110,2.6,"F"],["l",60,30,60,97],["l",49,97,71,97],["l",54,103,66,103,"B"],["l",60,103,60,185],["t",50,104,"6 V","e"],["t",256,148,"C = 50 \u03BCF",""]]}},{pn:245,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 31 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ccmb",st:"Equivalent resistance of networks",q:"The voltage drop across 15 \u03A9 resistance in the given figure will be ____ V.",n:6,s:`Find each arm of the parallel network between the two ends of the cell branch.
Upper arm: (4 \u2225 4) = 2 \u03A9, plus 2 \u03A9, plus (15 \u2225 10) = 6 \u03A9, so 2 + 2 + 6 = 10 \u03A9.
Lower arm: (8 \u2225 8) = 4 \u03A9 plus (12 \u2225 12) = 6 \u03A9, so 10 \u03A9.
The two arms are in parallel: 5 \u03A9. Add the 1 \u03A9 in the cell branch: R_total = 6 \u03A9.
Current from the cell: I = 12/6 = 2 A. The voltage across the 5 \u03A9 parallel part is 2 \xD7 5 = 10 V.
Each arm is 10 \u03A9, so the upper arm carries 1 A. The 15 \u03A9 and 10 \u03A9 pair has 6 \u03A9, so its voltage is 1 \xD7 6 = 6 V.
The 15 \u03A9 resistor is in parallel with the 10 \u03A9, so it has the same 6 V across it.`,fig:{w:370,h:215,alt:"Two arms in parallel then 1 ohm and a 12 volt cell in series. Upper arm: two 4 ohm in parallel, 2 ohm, then 15 ohm and 10 ohm in parallel. Lower arm: two 8 ohm in parallel, then two 12 ohm in parallel",d:[["l",40,45,40,185],["l",330,45,330,185],["l",40,45,70,45],["l",70,31,70,59],["l",130,31,130,59],["p","M70 31 L86 31 L88.3 26 L93 36 L97.7 26 L102.3 36 L107 26 L111.7 36 L114 31 L130 31"],["p","M70 59 L86 59 L88.3 54 L93 64 L97.7 54 L102.3 64 L107 54 L111.7 64 L114 59 L130 59"],["t",100,24,"4 \u03A9","m"],["t",100,76,"4 \u03A9","m"],["p","M130 45 L140 45 L143.3 40 L150 50 L156.7 40 L163.3 50 L170 40 L176.7 50 L180 45 L190 45"],["t",160,37,"2 \u03A9","m"],["l",190,45,200,45],["l",200,31,200,59],["l",260,31,260,59],["p","M200 31 L216 31 L218.3 26 L223 36 L227.7 26 L232.3 36 L237 26 L241.7 36 L244 31 L260 31"],["p","M200 59 L216 59 L218.3 54 L223 64 L227.7 54 L232.3 64 L237 54 L241.7 64 L244 59 L260 59"],["t",230,24,"15 \u03A9","m"],["t",230,76,"10 \u03A9","m"],["l",260,45,330,45],["l",40,125,70,125],["l",70,111,70,139],["l",130,111,130,139],["p","M70 111 L86 111 L88.3 106 L93 116 L97.7 106 L102.3 116 L107 106 L111.7 116 L114 111 L130 111"],["p","M70 139 L86 139 L88.3 134 L93 144 L97.7 134 L102.3 144 L107 134 L111.7 144 L114 139 L130 139"],["t",100,104,"8 \u03A9","m"],["t",100,156,"8 \u03A9","m"],["l",130,125,200,125],["l",200,111,200,139],["l",260,111,260,139],["p","M200 111 L216 111 L218.3 106 L223 116 L227.7 106 L232.3 116 L237 106 L241.7 116 L244 111 L260 111"],["p","M200 139 L216 139 L218.3 134 L223 144 L227.7 134 L232.3 144 L237 134 L241.7 144 L244 139 L260 139"],["t",230,104,"12 \u03A9","m"],["t",230,156,"12 \u03A9","m"],["l",260,125,330,125],["l",40,185,92,185],["l",92,174,92,196],["l",98,179,98,191,"B"],["l",98,185,150,185],["t",95,208,"12 V","m"],["p","M150 185 L218 185 L221.7 180 L229 190 L236.3 180 L243.7 190 L251 180 L258.3 190 L262 185 L330 185"],["t",240,208,"1 \u03A9","m"]]}},{pn:246,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 22 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ccel",st:"EMF and internal resistance",q:"In an electric circuit, a cell of certain emf provides a potential difference of 1.25 V across a load resistance of 5 \u03A9. However, it provides a potential difference of 1 V across a load resistance of 2 \u03A9. The emf of the cell is given by (x/10) V. Then the value of x is ____.",n:15,s:`For a cell with internal resistance r, the terminal voltage is V = E \u2212 Ir, and I = V/R for the load.
First case: I = 1.25/5 = 0.25 A, so E = 1.25 + 0.25r.
Second case: I = 1/2 = 0.5 A, so E = 1 + 0.5r.
Equate: 1.25 + 0.25r = 1 + 0.5r, so 0.25 = 0.25r and r = 1 \u03A9.
E = 1 + 0.5 \xD7 1 = 1.5 V = 15/10 V.
So x = 15.`},{pn:247,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ccmb",st:"Equivalent resistance of networks",q:"A 16 \u03A9 wire is bent to form a square loop. A 9 V supply having internal resistance of 1 \u03A9 is connected across one of its sides. The potential drop across the diagonals of the square loop is ____ \xD7 10\u207B\xB9 V.",n:45,s:`The wire is uniform, so each side of the square is 16/4 = 4 \u03A9.
The supply is across side AB (4 \u03A9). This side is in parallel with the other three sides in series (12 \u03A9): 4 \u2225 12 = 3 \u03A9.
Add the internal resistance: total = 3 + 1 = 4 \u03A9, so I = 9/4 A.
Terminal voltage across AB = I \xD7 3 = 6.75 V; this is the voltage across the 12 \u03A9 path A \u2192 D \u2192 C \u2192 B.
Current in that path = 6.75/12 = 0.5625 A, so each 4 \u03A9 side drops 2.25 V (solution figure).
A to C (or D to B) crosses two sides: 2 \xD7 2.25 = 4.5 V = 45 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9 V.`,sfig:{w:340,h:215,alt:"Square loop ABCD with 4 ohm sides; the 9 V supply with 1 ohm internal resistance is across side AB; potentials A 6.75 V, B 0, C 2.25 V, D 4.5 V",d:[["l",100,50,100,24],["l",250,50,250,24],["l",100,24,172,24],["l",172,13,172,35],["l",178,18,178,30,"B"],["l",178,24,250,24],["t",175,12,"9 V, r = 1 \u03A9","m"],["p","M100 50 L155 50 L158.3 45 L165 55 L171.7 45 L178.3 55 L185 45 L191.7 55 L195 50 L250 50"],["p","M250 50 L250 85 L255 88.3 L245 95 L255 101.7 L245 108.3 L255 115 L245 121.7 L250 125 L250 160"],["p","M250 160 L195 160 L191.7 165 L185 155 L178.3 165 L171.7 155 L165 165 L158.3 155 L155 160 L100 160"],["p","M100 160 L100 125 L95 121.7 L105 115 L95 108.3 L105 101.7 L95 95 L105 88.3 L100 85 L100 50"],["c",100,50,2.6,"F"],["c",250,50,2.6,"F"],["c",250,160,2.6,"F"],["c",100,160,2.6,"F"],["t",92,38,"A  6.75 V","e"],["t",272,38,"B  0 V","m"],["t",258,176,"C  2.25 V","m"],["t",92,176,"D  4.5 V","e"],["t",175,70,"4 \u03A9","m"],["t",175,150,"4 \u03A9","m"],["t",88,108,"4 \u03A9","e"],["t",270,108,"4 \u03A9","m"]]}},{pn:248,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 16 Mar \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ccmb",st:"Series and parallel combinations",q:"In the figure given, the electric current flowing through the 5 k\u03A9 resistor is x mA. The value of x to the nearest integer is ____.",n:3,s:`The 5 k\u03A9 resistor, the group of three 3 k\u03A9 resistors and the cell with its 1 k\u03A9 internal resistance are all in one loop, so the same current flows through each part.
Three 3 k\u03A9 in parallel: 3/3 = 1 k\u03A9.
Total resistance = 5 + 1 + 1 = 7 k\u03A9.
I = 21 V/7 k\u03A9 = 3 mA.
So x = 3.`,fig:{w:350,h:205,alt:"A 21 V cell with 1 kilo-ohm internal resistance, a 5 kilo-ohm resistor, and three 3 kilo-ohm resistors in parallel, all in one loop",d:[["l",40,70,40,160],["l",320,70,320,160],["p","M40 70 L83 70 L86.7 65 L94 75 L101.3 65 L108.7 75 L116 65 L123.3 75 L127 70 L170 70"],["t",105,58,"5 k\u03A9","m"],["l",170,30,170,110],["l",290,30,290,110],["p","M170 30 L208 30 L211.7 25 L219 35 L226.3 25 L233.7 35 L241 25 L248.3 35 L252 30 L290 30"],["t",230,20,"3 k\u03A9","m"],["p","M170 70 L208 70 L211.7 65 L219 75 L226.3 65 L233.7 75 L241 65 L248.3 75 L252 70 L290 70"],["t",230,60,"3 k\u03A9","m"],["p","M170 110 L208 110 L211.7 105 L219 115 L226.3 105 L233.7 115 L241 105 L248.3 115 L252 110 L290 110"],["t",230,100,"3 k\u03A9","m"],["l",290,70,320,70],["c",170,70,2.6,"F"],["c",290,70,2.6,"F"],["l",40,160,147,160],["l",147,149,147,171],["l",153,154,153,166,"B"],["l",153,160,320,160],["t",150,185,"21 V, 1 k\u03A9","m"],["l",40,120,40,102,"a"],["l",320,100,320,118,"a"]]}},{pn:249,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ckir",st:"Kirchhoff's laws",q:"A current of 6 A enters one corner P of an equilateral triangle PQR having 3 wires of resistance 2 \u03A9 each and leaves by the corner R. The current i\u2081 in ampere is ____.",n:2,s:`From P to R there are two paths: the direct side PR (2 \u03A9), and the path P \u2192 Q \u2192 R (2 + 2 = 4 \u03A9).
The two paths are in parallel, so the current divides in the inverse ratio of resistance.
Current in the 4 \u03A9 path (i\u2081) : current in the 2 \u03A9 path (i\u2082) = 2 : 4 = 1 : 2.
i\u2081 + i\u2082 = 6 A, so i\u2081 = 6/3 = 2 A and i\u2082 = 4 A.
Check: the voltage across both paths is 2 \xD7 4 = 4 \xD7 2 = 8 V.
So i\u2081 = 2 A.`,fig:{w:340,h:215,alt:"Equilateral triangle PQR of three 2 ohm wires; 6 A enters through a 2 ohm lead at P, currents i1 in side PQ and i2 in side PR, and the current leaves at R",d:[["l",170,6,170,20,"a"],["t",200,16,"6 A","m"],["p","M170 20 L170 27 L175 30 L165 36 L175 42 L165 48 L175 54 L165 60 L170 63 L170 70"],["t",160,50,"2 \u03A9","e"],["p","M170 70 L152.9 98.6 L155 104.7 L142.1 106.7 L146.4 119 L133.6 121 L137.9 133.3 L125 135.3 L127.1 141.4 L110 170"],["p","M170 70 L187.1 98.6 L193.6 99.6 L189.3 111.9 L202.1 113.9 L197.9 126.1 L210.7 128.1 L206.4 140.4 L212.9 141.4 L230 170"],["p","M110 170 L145 170 L149.2 165 L157.5 175 L165.8 165 L174.2 175 L182.5 165 L190.8 175 L195 170 L230 170"],["c",170,70,2.6,"F"],["t",182,76,"P","m"],["t",100,186,"Q","m"],["t",240,186,"R","m"],["t",118,114,"2 \u03A9","e"],["t",222,114,"2 \u03A9","m"],["t",170,198,"2 \u03A9","m"],["l",155,82,148,94,"a"],["t",140,92,"i\u2081","e"],["l",185,82,192,94,"a"],["t",198,92,"i\u2082","m"],["l",230,170,262,170,"a"]]}},{pn:250,y:"JEE Advanced 2021",tp:"crc",st:"Charging and discharging of a capacitor",q:"In the circuit shown below, the switch S is connected to position P for a long time so that the charge on the capacitor becomes q\u2081 \u03BCC. Then S is switched to position Q. After a long time, the charge on the capacitor is q\u2082 \u03BCC. The magnitude of q\u2081 is ____.",n:1.33,s:`At steady state no current flows into the capacitor, but current can flow in the loop 1 V cell, 1 \u03A9, 2 \u03A9 and the 2 V cell.
Call the node after the 1 \u03A9 resistor N, and the bottom wire 0 V. The two cells have their top terminals at +1 V and +2 V.
Current through 1 \u03A9 towards N: (1 \u2212 V)/1. Current through 2 \u03A9 away from N: (V \u2212 2)/2.
The capacitor takes no current, so these are equal: 2(1 \u2212 V) = V \u2212 2, so 4 = 3V and V = 4/3 V.
q\u2081 = CV = 1 \u03BCF \xD7 4/3 V = 1.33 \u03BCC.`,fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"Circuit: a switch S with contacts P and Q; contact P connects a 1 V cell, contact Q connects straight to the bottom wire; then a 1 ohm resistor, a node with a 1 microfarad capacitor to the bottom wire, a 2 ohm resistor and a 2 V cell. Both cells have their positive plates up",d:[["l",40,62,60,62],["c",60,62,2.6,"F"],["c",95,62,2.6,"F"],["l",95,62,95,160],["l",112,40,66,60],["t",88,30,"S","m"],["t",56,78,"P","m"],["t",100,78,"Q","m"],["l",40,62,40,107],["l",29,107,51,107],["l",34,113,46,113,"B"],["l",40,113,40,160],["t",24,114,"1 V","e"],["p","M112 40 L136 40 L139.3 35 L146 45 L152.7 35 L159.3 45 L166 35 L172.7 45 L176 40 L200 40"],["t",156,30,"1 \u03A9","m"],["p","M200 40 L230 40 L233.3 35 L240 45 L246.7 35 L253.3 45 L260 35 L266.7 45 L270 40 L300 40"],["t",250,30,"2 \u03A9","m"],["c",200,40,2.6,"F"],["l",200,40,200,100],["l",190,100,210,100],["l",190,108,210,108],["l",200,108,200,160],["t",224,112,"1 \u03BCF","m"],["l",300,40,300,160],["l",300,40,300,97],["l",289,97,311,97],["l",294,103,306,103,"B"],["l",300,103,300,160],["t",326,104,"2 V","m"],["l",40,160,300,160]]}},{pn:251,y:"JEE Advanced 2021",tp:"crc",st:"Charging and discharging of a capacitor",q:"In the circuit shown below, the switch S is connected to position P for a long time so that the charge on the capacitor becomes q\u2081 \u03BCC. Then S is switched to position Q. After a long time, the charge on the capacitor is q\u2082 \u03BCC. The magnitude of q\u2082 is ____.",n:.67,s:`With S at Q the left end of the 1 \u03A9 resistor is joined straight to the bottom wire (0 V).
In the long run no current flows into the capacitor; the loop is 1 \u03A9, 2 \u03A9 and the 2 V cell.
Let the node N between the resistors be at V. Current through 1 \u03A9 towards N: (0 \u2212 V)/1. Current through 2 \u03A9 away from N: (V \u2212 2)/2.
Equate: \u22122V = V \u2212 2, so V = 2/3 V.
q\u2082 = CV = 1 \u03BCF \xD7 2/3 V = 0.67 \u03BCC (magnitude).`,fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"Circuit: a switch S with contacts P and Q; contact P connects a 1 V cell, contact Q connects straight to the bottom wire; then a 1 ohm resistor, a node with a 1 microfarad capacitor to the bottom wire, a 2 ohm resistor and a 2 V cell. Both cells have their positive plates up",d:[["l",40,62,60,62],["c",60,62,2.6,"F"],["c",95,62,2.6,"F"],["l",95,62,95,160],["l",112,40,66,60],["t",88,30,"S","m"],["t",56,78,"P","m"],["t",100,78,"Q","m"],["l",40,62,40,107],["l",29,107,51,107],["l",34,113,46,113,"B"],["l",40,113,40,160],["t",24,114,"1 V","e"],["p","M112 40 L136 40 L139.3 35 L146 45 L152.7 35 L159.3 45 L166 35 L172.7 45 L176 40 L200 40"],["t",156,30,"1 \u03A9","m"],["p","M200 40 L230 40 L233.3 35 L240 45 L246.7 35 L253.3 45 L260 35 L266.7 45 L270 40 L300 40"],["t",250,30,"2 \u03A9","m"],["c",200,40,2.6,"F"],["l",200,40,200,100],["l",190,100,210,100],["l",190,108,210,108],["l",200,108,200,160],["t",224,112,"1 \u03BCF","m"],["l",300,40,300,160],["l",300,40,300,97],["l",289,97,311,97],["l",294,103,306,103,"B"],["l",300,103,300,160],["t",326,104,"2 V","m"],["l",40,160,300,160]]}},{pn:252,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 2 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"crc",st:"Charging and discharging of a capacitor",q:"An ideal cell of emf 10 V is connected in the circuit shown in figure. Each resistance is 2 \u03A9. The potential difference (in V) across the capacitor when it is fully charged is ____.",n:8,s:`When the capacitor is fully charged no current flows in the branch with C and R\u2085, so R\u2085 has no voltage drop and the capacitor voltage equals the voltage between node T (above R\u2082) and the right-hand node N.
Left node L joins the left wire. R\u2081 and R\u2082 in series (4 \u03A9) are in parallel with R\u2083 (2 \u03A9): 4 \u2225 2 = 4/3 \u03A9. R\u2084 is in series (solution figure).
Total R = 4/3 + 2 = 10/3 \u03A9, so the cell current is I = 10/(10/3) = 3 A.
Voltage across R\u2084 = 3 \xD7 2 = 6 V. Voltage across the parallel part = 3 \xD7 4/3 = 4 V.
The 4 \u03A9 branch (R\u2081 + R\u2082) carries 4/4 = 1 A, so R\u2082 drops 1 \xD7 2 = 2 V.
V_T \u2212 V_N = (drop across R\u2082) + (drop across R\u2084) = 2 + 6 = 8 V.`,fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"Circuit with a 10 V cell: R1 in the top wire to a node; from that node R2 goes down to the middle wire and a capacitor C in series with R5 goes to the right wire; R3 and R4 are in the middle wire; each resistance is 2 ohm",d:[["l",40,30,60,30],["p","M60 30 L83 30 L86.7 25 L94 35 L101.3 25 L108.7 35 L116 25 L123.3 35 L127 30 L150 30"],["t",105,20,"R\u2081","m"],["l",150,30,175,30],["l",175,20,175,40],["l",183,20,183,40],["l",183,30,300,30],["l",40,30,40,165],["p","M150 30 L150 48 L155 51.7 L145 59 L155 66.3 L145 73.7 L155 81 L145 88.3 L150 92 L150 110"],["t",140,74,"R\u2082","e"],["l",300,30,300,40],["p","M300 40 L300 53 L305 56.7 L295 64 L305 71.3 L295 78.7 L305 86 L295 93.3 L300 97 L300 110"],["t",290,76,"R\u2085","e"],["t",190,52,"C","m"],["p","M40 110 L73 110 L76.7 105 L84 115 L91.3 105 L98.7 115 L106 105 L113.3 115 L117 110 L150 110"],["t",95,100,"R\u2083","m"],["p","M150 110 L203 110 L206.7 105 L214 115 L221.3 105 L228.7 115 L236 105 L243.3 115 L247 110 L300 110"],["t",225,128,"R\u2084","m"],["l",300,110,300,165],["c",150,30,2.6,"F"],["c",150,110,2.6,"F"],["c",300,110,2.6,"F"],["c",40,110,2.6,"F"],["l",40,165,167,165],["l",167,154,167,176],["l",173,159,173,171,"B"],["l",173,165,300,165],["t",170,188,"10 V","m"]]},sfig:{w:340,h:170,alt:"Steady state: R1 and R2 in series (4 ohm) in parallel with R3 (2 ohm), then R4 in series; the capacitor branch carries no current",d:[["c",30,70,2.6,"F"],["l",30,70,60,70],["l",60,40,60,100],["p","M60 40 L88 40 L91.7 35 L99 45 L106.3 35 L113.7 45 L121 35 L128.3 45 L132 40 L160 40"],["t",110,30,"R\u2081 + R\u2082 = 4 \u03A9","m"],["p","M60 100 L88 100 L91.7 95 L99 105 L106.3 95 L113.7 105 L121 95 L128.3 105 L132 100 L160 100"],["t",110,122,"R\u2083 = 2 \u03A9","m"],["l",160,40,160,100],["l",160,70,190,70],["p","M190 70 L218 70 L221.7 65 L229 75 L236.3 65 L243.7 75 L251 65 L258.3 75 L262 70 L290 70"],["t",240,60,"R\u2084 = 2 \u03A9","m"],["c",290,70,2.6,"F"],["t",30,56,"L","m"],["t",290,56,"N","m"],["t",160,148,"4 \u03A9 \u2225 2 \u03A9 = 4/3 \u03A9, in series with 2 \u03A9","m"],["c",60,70,2.6,"F"]]}},{pn:253,y:"JEE Advanced 2018",tp:"crc",st:"Charging and discharging of a capacitor",q:"Three identical capacitors C\u2081, C\u2082 and C\u2083 have a capacitance of 1.0 \u03BCF each and they are uncharged initially. They are connected in a circuit as shown in the figure and C\u2081 is then filled completely with a dielectric material of relative permittivity \u03B5_r. The cell electromotive force (emf) V\u2080 = 8 V. First the switch S\u2081 is closed while the switch S\u2082 is kept open. When the capacitor C\u2083 is fully charged, S\u2081 is opened and S\u2082 is closed simultaneously. When all the capacitors reach equilibrium, the charge on C\u2083 is found to be 5 \u03BCC. The value of \u03B5_r = ____.",n:1.5,s:`With S\u2081 closed and S\u2082 open, only C\u2083 is connected to the cell. It charges to 8 V: Q = 1 \u03BCF \xD7 8 V = 8 \u03BCC.
Now S\u2081 is opened (the cell is disconnected) and S\u2082 is closed. The 8 \u03BCC is shared between C\u2083 and the series pair C\u2081, C\u2082; the total charge is conserved.
Final charge on C\u2083 is 5 \u03BCC, so the series branch carries 8 \u2212 5 = 3 \u03BCC.
C\u2083 is in parallel with the series branch, so both have the same voltage: V = 5 \u03BCC/1 \u03BCF = 5 V.
Series branch: C_s = 3 \u03BCC/5 V = 0.6 \u03BCF.
C\u2081 = \u03B5_r \xD7 1 \u03BCF and C\u2082 = 1 \u03BCF in series: C_s = \u03B5_r/(\u03B5_r + 1) = 0.6, so \u03B5_r = 0.6\u03B5_r + 0.6 and \u03B5_r = 1.5.`,fig:{w:360,h:215,alt:"Circuit: cell V0 and switch S1 on the left, switch S2 with capacitors C1 and C2 in series in the middle branch, capacitor C3 on the right",d:[["l",50,30,300,30],["l",50,30,50,66],["l",50,30,50,63],["l",39,63,61,63],["l",44,69,56,69,"B"],["l",50,69,50,100],["t",40,70,"V\u2080","e"],["c",50,104,2.6,"F"],["c",50,122,2.6,"F"],["l",50,122,64,102],["t",38,118,"S\u2081","e"],["l",50,122,50,190],["l",170,30,170,62],["c",170,62,2.6,"F"],["l",170,90,158,66],["t",150,80,"S\u2082","e"],["c",170,90,2.6,"F"],["l",170,90,170,112],["l",158,112,182,112],["l",158,120,182,120],["r",158,113,24,6,"f"],["t",192,120,"C\u2081","m"],["l",170,120,170,142],["l",158,142,182,142],["l",158,150,182,150],["t",192,150,"C\u2082","m"],["l",170,150,170,190],["l",300,30,300,100],["l",288,100,312,100],["l",288,108,312,108],["l",300,108,300,190],["t",270,108,"C\u2083","e"],["l",50,190,300,190]]}},{pn:254,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 8 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ccel",st:"EMF and internal resistance",q:"The series combination of two batteries, both of the same emf 10 V, but different internal resistance of 20 \u03A9 and 5 \u03A9, is connected to the parallel combination of two resistors 30 \u03A9 and R \u03A9. The voltage difference across the battery of internal resistance 20 \u03A9 is zero, the value of R (in \u03A9) is ____.",n:30,s:`Terminal voltage of a battery is V = E \u2212 Ir. It is zero for the 20 \u03A9 battery, so I = E/r = 10/20 = 0.5 A.
The same 0.5 A flows through both batteries (they are in series), so the total emf is 20 V.
Total resistance of the loop = 20 + 5 + R_ext, so 20 = 0.5(25 + R_ext) and R_ext = 15 \u03A9.
R_ext is 30 \u03A9 in parallel with R: 30R/(30 + R) = 15, so 30R = 450 + 15R and R = 30 \u03A9.`},{pn:255,y:"IIT-JEE 2011",tp:"ccel",st:"Grouping of cells",q:"Two batteries of different emfs and different internal resistances are connected as shown. The voltage across AB in volts is ____.",n:5,s:`The two batteries are connected in a closed loop, and they are drawn the same way along the loop (A to B), so they act against each other.
Net emf = 6 \u2212 3 = 3 V, and total resistance = 1 + 2 = 3 \u03A9, so I = 1 A in the loop (flowing in the 6 V battery's direction).
Voltage across AB through the upper branch: V_A \u2212 V_B = 6 \u2212 I \xD7 1 = 6 \u2212 1 = 5 V.
Check with the lower branch: 3 + I \xD7 2 = 3 + 2 = 5 V. Both agree.`,fig:{w:360,h:180,alt:"Two branches between A and B: a 6 V cell with a 1 ohm resistor, and a 3 V cell with a 2 ohm resistor, both with the long plate towards A",d:[["c",20,95,2.6,"F"],["c",340,95,2.6,"F"],["t",20,82,"A","m"],["t",340,82,"B","m"],["l",20,95,60,95],["l",60,45,60,145],["l",300,45,300,145],["l",300,95,340,95],["l",60,45,97,45],["l",97,34,97,56],["l",103,39,103,51,"B"],["l",103,45,150,45],["t",100,30,"6 V","m"],["p","M150 45 L203 45 L206.7 40 L214 50 L221.3 40 L228.7 50 L236 40 L243.3 50 L247 45 L300 45"],["t",225,34,"1 \u03A9","m"],["l",60,145,97,145],["l",97,134,97,156],["l",103,139,103,151,"B"],["l",103,145,150,145],["t",100,172,"3 V","m"],["p","M150 145 L203 145 L206.7 140 L214 150 L221.3 140 L228.7 150 L236 140 L243.3 150 L247 145 L300 145"],["t",225,170,"2 \u03A9","m"]]}},{pn:256,y:"IIT-JEE 2010",tp:"crc",st:"Charging and discharging of a capacitor",q:"At time t = 0, a battery of 10 V is connected across points A and B in the given circuit. If the capacitors have no charge initially, at what time (in seconds) does the voltage across them become 4 V? [Take: ln 5 = 1.6, ln 3 = 1.1]",n:2,s:`Two 2 M\u03A9 resistors in parallel give R = 1 M\u03A9. Two 2 \u03BCF capacitors in parallel give C = 4 \u03BCF.
They are in series with each other, so this is a simple RC charging circuit with time constant \u03C4 = RC = 10\u2076 \xD7 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 = 4 s.
Capacitor voltage: V = V\u2080(1 \u2212 e^(\u2212t/\u03C4)), so 4 = 10(1 \u2212 e^(\u2212t/4)).
e^(\u2212t/4) = 0.6 = 3/5, so t/4 = ln(5/3) = ln 5 \u2212 ln 3 = 1.6 \u2212 1.1 = 0.5.
t = 4 \xD7 0.5 = 2 s.`,fig:{w:360,h:150,alt:"Between A and B: two 2 megohm resistors in parallel in series with two 2 microfarad capacitors in parallel",d:[["c",20,75,2.6,"F"],["c",340,75,2.6,"F"],["t",20,62,"A","m"],["t",340,62,"B","m"],["l",20,75,50,75],["l",50,45,50,105],["l",150,45,150,105],["p","M50 45 L80 45 L83.3 40 L90 50 L96.7 40 L103.3 50 L110 40 L116.7 50 L120 45 L150 45"],["t",100,35,"2 M\u03A9","m"],["p","M50 105 L80 105 L83.3 100 L90 110 L96.7 100 L103.3 110 L110 100 L116.7 110 L120 105 L150 105"],["t",100,127,"2 M\u03A9","m"],["l",150,75,200,75],["l",200,45,200,105],["l",310,45,310,105],["l",200,45,251,45],["l",251,35,251,55],["l",259,35,259,55],["l",259,45,310,45],["l",200,105,251,105],["l",251,95,251,115],["l",259,95,259,115],["l",259,105,310,105],["t",255,31,"2 \u03BCF","m"],["t",255,127,"2 \u03BCF","m"],["l",310,75,340,75]]}},{pn:257,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 22 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ccel",st:"Grouping of cells",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement-I: The equivalent emf of two non-ideal batteries connected in parallel is smaller than either of the two emfs.
Statement-II: The equivalent internal resistance of two non-ideal batteries connected in parallel is smaller than the internal resistance of either of the two batteries.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["Both statement-I and statement-II are true","Both statement-I and statement-II are false","Statement-I is true but statement-II is false","Statement-I is false but statement-II is true"],c:3,s:`For batteries (E\u2081, r\u2081) and (E\u2082, r\u2082) in parallel, the equivalent emf is E = (E\u2081r\u2082 + E\u2082r\u2081)/(r\u2081 + r\u2082) and 1/r = 1/r\u2081 + 1/r\u2082.
E is a weighted average of E\u2081 and E\u2082 (the weights r\u2082/(r\u2081 + r\u2082) and r\u2081/(r\u2081 + r\u2082) add to 1), so it lies between E\u2081 and E\u2082. It is not smaller than both, so Statement-I is false.
r = r\u2081r\u2082/(r\u2081 + r\u2082) is smaller than each of r\u2081 and r\u2082, because the reciprocal 1/r is bigger than 1/r\u2081 and 1/r\u2082. So Statement-II is true.
This is option (d).`},{pn:258,y:"IIT-JEE 2005",tp:"crc",st:"Charging and discharging of a capacitor",q:"In the given circuit, the switch S is closed at time t = 0. The charge Q on the capacitor at any instant t is given by Q(t) = Q\u2080(1 \u2212 e^(\u2212\u03B1t)). Find the value of Q\u2080 and \u03B1 in terms of given parameters as shown in the circuit.",a:"Q\u2080 = CVR\u2082/(R\u2081 + R\u2082),  \u03B1 = (R\u2081 + R\u2082)/(R\u2081R\u2082C)",s:`Note: the book does not print the circuit figure; it is drawn here as the standard circuit of this question (R\u2081 in series with C \u2225 R\u2082).
Q\u2080 is the final charge: after a long time the capacitor is full and takes no current, so the current flows only through R\u2081 and R\u2082.
Steady current: I = V/(R\u2081 + R\u2082).
The capacitor is in parallel with R\u2082, so its final voltage is V_C = I R\u2082 = VR\u2082/(R\u2081 + R\u2082).
Q\u2080 = C V_C = CVR\u2082/(R\u2081 + R\u2082).
\u03B1 = 1/\u03C4, where \u03C4 is the time constant. Seen from the capacitor, the battery (ideal, so it acts as a plain wire) leaves R\u2081 and R\u2082 in parallel: R_th = R\u2081R\u2082/(R\u2081 + R\u2082).
\u03C4 = R_th C = R\u2081R\u2082C/(R\u2081 + R\u2082), so \u03B1 = 1/\u03C4 = (R\u2081 + R\u2082)/(R\u2081R\u2082C).
Check: Q(t) = Q\u2080(1 \u2212 e^(\u2212\u03B1t)) is 0 at t = 0 and tends to Q\u2080 for large t, as it should.
Result: Q\u2080 = CVR\u2082/(R\u2081 + R\u2082) and \u03B1 = (R\u2081 + R\u2082)/(R\u2081R\u2082C).`,fig:{w:380,h:190,alt:"A battery V in series with the switch S and resistor R1; then the capacitor C and the resistor R2 are connected in parallel across the remaining wires",d:[["l",40,40,40,87],["l",29,87,51,87],["l",34,93,46,93,"B"],["l",40,93,40,160],["t",22,92,"V","e"],["l",40,40,90,40],["c",90,40,2.6,"F"],["c",125,40,2.6,"F"],["l",90,40,120,24],["t",108,18,"S","m"],["l",125,40,150,40],["p","M150 40 L156 40 L159 35 L165 45 L171 35 L177 45 L183 35 L189 45 L192 40 L198 40"],["l",198,40,320,40],["t",174,28,"R\u2081","m"],["c",240,40,2.6,"F"],["l",240,40,240,92],["l",228,92,252,92],["l",228,100,252,100],["l",240,100,240,160],["t",262,100,"C","m"],["l",320,40,320,70],["p","M320 70 L325 76 L315 82 L325 88 L315 94 L325 100 L320 106"],["l",320,106,320,160],["t",340,92,"R\u2082","m"],["c",240,160,2.6,"F"],["l",40,160,320,160]]}},{pn:259,y:"IIT-JEE 1997",tp:"ccel",st:"Grouping of cells",q:"Find the emf (V) and internal resistance (r) of a single battery which is equivalent to a parallel combination of two batteries of emfs V\u2081 and V\u2082 and internal resistances r\u2081 and r\u2082 respectively, with polarities as shown in figure.",a:"V = (V\u2081r\u2082 + V\u2082r\u2081)/(r\u2081 + r\u2082), r = r\u2081r\u2082/(r\u2081 + r\u2082)",s:`Let the terminal voltage between A and B be V_t. For each battery, V_t = V \u2212 I\xB7r, so I\u2081 = (V\u2081 \u2212 V_t)/r\u2081 and I\u2082 = (V\u2082 \u2212 V_t)/r\u2082, both flowing out of the A terminals.
The total current out of the combination is I = I\u2081 + I\u2082 = V\u2081/r\u2081 + V\u2082/r\u2082 \u2212 V_t(1/r\u2081 + 1/r\u2082).
A single battery of emf V and resistance r gives I = V/r \u2212 V_t/r.
Compare the coefficients of V_t: 1/r = 1/r\u2081 + 1/r\u2082, so r = r\u2081r\u2082/(r\u2081 + r\u2082).
Compare the constant terms: V/r = V\u2081/r\u2081 + V\u2082/r\u2082, so V = r(V\u2081/r\u2081 + V\u2082/r\u2082) = (V\u2081r\u2082 + V\u2082r\u2081)/(r\u2081 + r\u2082).
Note: the plates are small in the crop; both batteries are read with the long (positive) plate on the A side. If one were reversed, change the sign of its emf in the formulas.`,fig:{w:360,h:190,alt:"Two batteries in parallel between A and B: V2 with internal resistance r2 in the upper branch, V1 with r1 in the lower branch; both with the long (positive) plate on the A side",d:[["c",20,95,2.6,"F"],["c",340,95,2.6,"F"],["t",20,82,"A","m"],["t",340,82,"B","m"],["l",20,95,60,95],["l",60,40,60,150],["l",300,40,300,150],["l",300,95,340,95],["p","M60 40 L83 40 L86.7 35 L94 45 L101.3 35 L108.7 45 L116 35 L123.3 45 L127 40 L150 40"],["t",105,30,"r\u2082","m"],["l",150,40,227,40],["l",227,29,227,51],["l",233,34,233,46,"B"],["l",233,40,300,40],["t",230,28,"V\u2082","m"],["p","M60 150 L83 150 L86.7 145 L94 155 L101.3 145 L108.7 155 L116 145 L123.3 155 L127 150 L150 150"],["t",105,140,"r\u2081","m"],["l",150,150,227,150],["l",227,139,227,161],["l",233,144,233,156,"B"],["l",233,150,300,150],["t",230,176,"V\u2081","m"]]}},{pn:260,y:"IIT-JEE 1997",tp:"crc",st:"Charging and discharging of a capacitor",q:"A leaky parallel plate capacitor is filled completely with a material having dielectric constant K = 5 and electrical conductivity \u03C3 = 7.4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 \u03A9\u207B\xB9 m\u207B\xB9. If the charge on the plate at instant t = 0 is q = 8.85 \u03BCC, then calculate the leakage current at the instant t = 12 s.",a:"\u2248 0.2 \u03BCA (about 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 A)",s:`The leaky capacitor is like a capacitor C in parallel with a resistance R, so it discharges through itself: q = q\u2080e^(\u2212t/\u03C4) with \u03C4 = RC.
For plates of area A and gap d: C = K\u03B5\u2080A/d and R = d/(\u03C3A), so \u03C4 = K\u03B5\u2080/\u03C3 (area and gap cancel).
\u03C4 = 5 \xD7 8.85 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2/(7.4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2) = 5.98 s \u2248 6 s.
Leakage current = rate of loss of charge: I = q/\u03C4 = (q\u2080/\u03C4)e^(\u2212t/\u03C4).
At t = 12 s = 2\u03C4: I = (8.85 \u03BCC/6 s) \xD7 e\u207B\xB2 = 1.475 \xD7 0.135 \u03BCA \u2248 0.2 \u03BCA.`},{pn:261,y:"IIT-JEE 1994",tp:"crc",st:"Charging and discharging of a capacitor",q:"Two square metal plates of side 1 m are kept 0.01 m apart like a parallel plate capacitor in air in such a way that one of their edges is perpendicular to an oil surface in a tank filled with an insulating oil. The plates are connected to a battery of emf 500 V. The plates are then lowered vertically into the oil at a speed of 0.001 m s\u207B\xB9. Calculate the current drawn from the battery during the process. (Dielectric constant of oil = 11, \u03B5\u2080 = 8.85 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 C\xB2 N\u207B\xB9 m\u207B\xB2.)",a:"\u2248 4.4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 A",s:`When the plates are dipped by a depth x, a part of width 1 m and height x is in oil (K = 11) and the rest (height 1 \u2212 x) is in air. These two parts are in parallel.
C = \u03B5\u2080(1)(1 \u2212 x)/d + K\u03B5\u2080(1)x/d = (\u03B5\u2080/d)[1 + (K \u2212 1)x].
The plates stay connected to the 500 V battery, so Q = CV changes as C changes, and the battery supplies current I = dQ/dt = V dC/dt.
dC/dt = (\u03B5\u2080/d)(K \u2212 1)(dx/dt) = (8.85 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2/0.01) \xD7 10 \xD7 0.001 = 8.85 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 F/s.
I = 500 \xD7 8.85 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 = 4.4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 A.`},{pn:262,y:"IIT-JEE 1988",tp:"ckir",st:"Kirchhoff's laws",q:"In the given circuit, E\u2081 = 3E\u2082 = 2E\u2083 = 6 volt, R\u2081 = 2R\u2084 = 6 ohm, R\u2083 = 2R\u2082 = 4 ohm, C = 5 \u03BCF. Find the current in R\u2083 and the energy stored in the capacitor.",a:"Current in R\u2083 = 1.5 A; energy stored = 14.4 \u03BCJ",s:`Values: E\u2081 = 6 V, E\u2082 = 2 V, E\u2083 = 3 V; R\u2081 = 6 \u03A9, R\u2084 = 3 \u03A9, R\u2083 = 4 \u03A9, R\u2082 = 2 \u03A9.
In steady state the capacitor carries no current, so R\u2081 has no drop. Name the node after R\u2081 and R\u2082 as M, the right wire as W and the left wire as L.
The wire joins M to the node after R\u2081, and E\u2081 is connected directly between M and W. R\u2083 is also between M and W, so R\u2083 has the full 6 V of E\u2081 across it.
Current in R\u2083 = 6/4 = 1.5 A.
Take V_W = 0, so V_M = \u22126 V (E\u2081 has its positive plate on the W side). Let V_L = u. Through E\u2083 and R\u2084 (E\u2083 positive on the W side) the current towards W is (u + 3)/3; through E\u2082 (positive plate on the L side) and R\u2082 the current towards M is (u \u2212 2 + 6)/2.
The capacitor takes no current, so at L the two currents add to zero: (u + 3)/3 + (u + 4)/2 = 0, which gives u = \u22123.6 V.
The capacitor is between L and M (through R\u2081), so V_C = |\u22126 \u2212 (\u22123.6)| = 2.4 V.
U = (1/2)CV\xB2 = (1/2)(5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076)(2.4)\xB2 = 14.4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 J = 14.4 \u03BCJ.
Note: the current in R\u2083 does not depend on E\u2082, E\u2083. The energy depends on how E\u2082 and E\u2083 are turned; their plates are small in the crop and are read as: E\u2082 long plate on the L side, E\u2083 long plate on the W side.`,fig:{w:380,h:215,alt:"Circuit: R1 and cell E1 in the top wire; a capacitor C in the left wire; the middle wire holds E2 and R2 then R3; the bottom wire holds E3 and R4; a wire joins the node after R1 to the node between R2 and R3",d:[["l",60,30,60,56],["l",50,60,70,60],["l",50,68,70,68],["l",60,68,60,175],["t",46,68,"C","e"],["l",60,30,60,30],["p","M60 30 L83 30 L86.7 25 L94 35 L101.3 25 L108.7 35 L116 25 L123.3 35 L127 30 L150 30"],["t",105,20,"R\u2081","m"],["l",150,30,170,30],["l",170,30,232,30],["l",232,24,232,36,"B"],["l",238,19,238,41],["l",238,30,300,30],["t",235,16,"E\u2081","m"],["l",300,30,300,175],["l",170,30,170,95],["c",170,95,2.6,"F"],["l",60,95,82,95],["l",82,84,82,106],["l",88,89,88,101,"B"],["l",88,95,110,95],["t",85,117,"E\u2082","m"],["p","M110 95 L120 95 L123.3 90 L130 100 L136.7 90 L143.3 100 L150 90 L156.7 100 L160 95 L170 95"],["t",140,85,"R\u2082","m"],["p","M170 95 L210 95 L214.2 90 L222.5 100 L230.8 90 L239.2 100 L247.5 90 L255.8 100 L260 95 L300 95"],["t",235,85,"R\u2083","m"],["c",300,95,2.6,"F"],["l",60,175,97,175],["l",97,169,97,181,"B"],["l",103,164,103,186],["l",103,175,140,175],["t",100,198,"E\u2083","m"],["p","M140 175 L198 175 L201.7 170 L209 180 L216.3 170 L223.7 180 L231 170 L238.3 180 L242 175 L300 175"],["t",220,198,"R\u2084","m"]]}},{pn:263,y:"IIT-JEE 1987",tp:"ccmb",st:"Infinite and cube networks",q:`An infinite ladder network of resistances is constructed with 1 ohm and 2 ohm resistances, as shown in figure. The 6 volt battery between A and B has negligible internal resistance.
(i) Show that the effective resistance between A and B is 2 ohm.
(ii) What is the current that passes through the 2 ohm resistance nearest to the battery?`,a:"(i) 2 \u03A9; (ii) 1.5 A",s:`Let R be the resistance of the whole infinite ladder seen from A and B. Remove the first section (one 1 \u03A9 and one 2 \u03A9): what remains is again the same infinite ladder, so it also has resistance R.
The first section looks like: 1 \u03A9 in series with (2 \u03A9 in parallel with R). So R = 1 + 2R/(2 + R).
Multiply out: R(2 + R) = (2 + R) + 2R, so R\xB2 + 2R = 2 + 3R and R\xB2 \u2212 R \u2212 2 = 0.
(R \u2212 2)(R + 1) = 0, and a resistance is positive, so R = 2 \u03A9.
The battery current is I = 6/2 = 3 A, and all of it passes through the first 1 \u03A9 resistor, which drops 3 V.
The first 2 \u03A9 resistor has the remaining 6 \u2212 3 = 3 V across it, so its current is 3/2 = 1.5 A.`,fig:{w:380,h:190,alt:"Infinite ladder: a 6 V cell between A and B; each step has a 1 ohm resistor in the top wire followed by a 2 ohm resistor across the two wires",d:[["l",60,40,60,87],["l",49,87,71,87],["l",54,93,66,93,"B"],["l",60,93,60,140],["t",44,94,"6 V","e"],["t",60,32,"A","m"],["t",60,162,"B","m"],["p","M60 40 L85 40 L88.3 35 L95 45 L101.7 35 L108.3 45 L115 35 L121.7 45 L125 40 L150 40"],["t",105,30,"1 \u03A9","m"],["p","M150 40 L175 40 L178.3 35 L185 45 L191.7 35 L198.3 45 L205 35 L211.7 45 L215 40 L240 40"],["t",195,30,"1 \u03A9","m"],["p","M240 40 L265 40 L268.3 35 L275 45 L281.7 35 L288.3 45 L295 35 L301.7 45 L305 40 L330 40"],["t",285,30,"1 \u03A9","m"],["p","M150 40 L150 68 L155 71.7 L145 79 L155 86.3 L145 93.7 L155 101 L145 108.3 L150 112 L150 140"],["t",140,94,"2 \u03A9","e"],["p","M240 40 L240 68 L245 71.7 L235 79 L245 86.3 L235 93.7 L245 101 L235 108.3 L240 112 L240 140"],["t",230,94,"2 \u03A9","e"],["p","M330 40 L330 68 L335 71.7 L325 79 L335 86.3 L325 93.7 L335 101 L325 108.3 L330 112 L330 140"],["t",320,94,"2 \u03A9","e"],["l",60,140,350,140],["l",330,40,365,40,"d"],["l",350,140,365,140,"d"],["c",150,40,2.6,"F"],["c",240,40,2.6,"F"],["c",330,40,2.6,"F"],["c",150,140,2.6,"F"],["c",240,140,2.6,"F"],["c",330,140,2.6,"F"]]}},{pn:264,y:"IIT-JEE 1986",tp:"crc",st:"Charging and discharging of a capacitor",q:"A part of a circuit in a steady state along with the currents flowing in the branches, the values of resistances etc., is shown in the figure. Calculate the energy stored in the capacitor C (4 \u03BCF).",a:"8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 J (800 \u03BCJ)",s:`In steady state the capacitor carries no current, so its voltage is V_X \u2212 V_Y, found from the marked currents.
At X: 1 A enters from above and 2 A enters from the 4 V branch, so 3 A flows through 5 \u03A9 and 1 \u03A9 to Z.
V_X \u2212 V_Z = 3 \xD7 (5 + 1) = 18 V, so V_X = V_Z + 18.
At Y: 2 A leaves along the 3 V branch and 1 A enters from below, so 1 A must flow from Z to Y through the 2 \u03A9.
V_Y = V_Z \u2212 1 \xD7 2 = V_Z \u2212 2.
V = V_X \u2212 V_Y = 18 \u2212 (\u22122) = 20 V.
U = (1/2)CV\xB2 = (1/2)(4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076)(20)\xB2 = 8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 J.`,fig:{w:420,h:285,alt:"Part of a circuit in steady state: node X joined to node Y by a 4 microfarad capacitor; X has 1 A entering through 3 ohm, a 4 V cell with 3 ohm carrying 2 A, and 5 ohm then 1 ohm to node Z; Y has a 3 V cell with 2 ohm carrying 2 A, 2 ohm to Z, and 3 ohm carrying 1 A away; Z leaves through 4 ohm",d:[["l",200,8,200,20,"a"],["t",216,16,"1 A","m"],["p","M200 20 L200 35 L205 39.2 L195 47.5 L205 55.8 L195 64.2 L205 72.5 L195 80.8 L200 85 L200 100"],["t",216,64,"3 \u03A9","m"],["l",60,100,82,100],["l",82,89,82,111],["l",88,94,88,106,"B"],["l",88,100,110,100],["t",85,86,"4 V","m"],["p","M110 100 L130 100 L134.2 95 L142.5 105 L150.8 95 L159.2 105 L167.5 95 L175.8 105 L180 100 L200 100"],["t",155,90,"3 \u03A9","m"],["t",88,134,"2 A","m"],["p","M200 100 L220 100 L225 95 L235 105 L245 95 L255 105 L265 95 L275 105 L280 100 L300 100"],["t",250,90,"5 \u03A9","m"],["p","M300 100 L300 120 L305 124.2 L295 132.5 L305 140.8 L295 149.2 L305 157.5 L295 165.8 L300 170 L300 190"],["t",318,148,"1 \u03A9","m"],["l",200,100,200,136],["l",190,136,210,136],["l",190,144,210,144],["l",200,144,200,190],["t",224,148,"4 \u03BCF","m"],["l",60,190,82,190],["l",82,179,82,201],["l",88,184,88,196,"B"],["l",88,190,110,190],["t",85,176,"3 V","m"],["p","M110 190 L130 190 L134.2 185 L142.5 195 L150.8 185 L159.2 195 L167.5 185 L175.8 195 L180 190 L200 190"],["t",155,180,"2 \u03A9","m"],["t",88,224,"2 A","m"],["p","M200 190 L220 190 L225 185 L235 195 L245 185 L255 195 L265 185 L275 195 L280 190 L300 190"],["t",250,208,"2 \u03A9","m"],["p","M300 190 L317.5 190 L321.7 185 L330 195 L338.3 185 L346.7 195 L355 185 L363.3 195 L367.5 190 L385 190"],["t",342,208,"4 \u03A9","m"],["p","M200 190 L200 201 L205 205.2 L195 213.5 L205 221.8 L195 230.2 L205 238.5 L195 246.8 L200 251 L200 262"],["t",218,230,"3 \u03A9","m"],["l",200,276,200,262,"a"],["t",216,276,"1 A","m"],["c",200,100,2.6,"F"],["c",200,190,2.6,"F"],["c",300,190,2.6,"F"],["c",300,100,2.6,"F"],["t",190,120,"X","e"],["t",190,210,"Y","e"],["t",308,184,"Z",""],["l",72,113,106,113,"a"],["l",106,203,72,203,"a"]]}},{pn:265,y:"IIT-JEE 1984",tp:"ckir",st:"Kirchhoff's laws",q:"In the circuit shown in figure E, F, G, H are cells of emf 2, 1, 3 and 1 volt respectively, and their internal resistances are 2, 1, 3 and 1 ohm respectively. Calculate: (i) the potential difference between B and D and (ii) the potential difference across the terminals of each of the cells G and H.",a:"(i) V_B \u2212 V_D = 2/13 V \u2248 0.15 V (B above D). (ii) Terminal voltage of G = 21/13 V \u2248 1.6 V; of H = 19/13 V \u2248 1.5 V (H is being charged).",s:`Note: the printed key says 0 for part (i). Reading the plate lengths in the figure (long plate = positive: E and F positive at A, G and H positive at C) working gives 2/13 V. The key of 0 appears only if cells F and H are drawn the other way round.
Take D as 0 V and call the potentials of A, B, C as V_A, V_B, V_C. A cell with its + at node p and its \u2212 at node n gives a current (V_p \u2212 V_n \u2212 e)/r from p towards n through the outside circuit.
Current leaving A: through E, (V_A \u2212 V_B \u2212 2)/2; through F, V_A \u2212 1 (r = 1 \u03A9, D at 0 V). Nothing enters or leaves A, so (V_A \u2212 V_B \u2212 2)/2 + V_A \u2212 1 = 0, which gives 3V_A \u2212 V_B = 4.
At C: current leaving C through H towards B is V_C \u2212 V_B \u2212 1 and through G towards D is (V_C \u2212 3)/3. They add to zero: 4V_C \u2212 3V_B = 6.
At B: current coming in from A, (V_A \u2212 V_B \u2212 2)/2, equals V_B/2 through the 2 \u03A9 resistor to D, plus V_B \u2212 V_C + 1 going on to C. This gives V_A \u2212 4V_B + 2V_C = 4.
Solving the three equations: V_B = 2/13 V, V_A = 18/13 V, V_C = 21/13 V.
(i) V_B \u2212 V_D = 2/13 V, about 0.15 V.
(ii) Cell G delivers 6/13 A, so its terminal voltage is 3 \u2212 3 \xD7 6/13 = 21/13 V \u2248 1.6 V. Cell H is pushed backwards by 6/13 A, so its terminal voltage is 1 + 1 \xD7 6/13 = 19/13 V \u2248 1.5 V.`,fig:{w:290,h:250,alt:"A square ABCD with cells E on AB, H on BC, G on DC and F on AD, and a 2 ohm resistor along the diagonal DB",d:[["l",50,50,133,50],["l",133,38,133,62],["l",140,44,140,56,"B"],["l",140,50,230,50],["l",230,50,230,124],["l",224,124,236,124,"B"],["l",218,131,242,131],["l",230,131,230,210],["l",50,210,133,210],["l",133,204,133,216,"B"],["l",140,198,140,222],["l",140,210,230,210],["l",50,50,50,124],["l",38,124,62,124],["l",44,131,56,131,"B"],["l",50,131,50,210],["l",50,210,123.6,144.6],["p","M123.6 144.6 L129.6 145.9 L128.5 133.6 L140.6 136.2 L139.4 123.8 L151.5 126.4 L150.4 114.1 L156.4 115.4"],["l",156.4,115.4,230,50],["c",50,50,2.2,"F"],["c",230,50,2.2,"F"],["c",230,210,2.2,"F"],["c",50,210,2.2,"F"],["t",40,44,"A","e"],["t",240,44,"B",""],["t",240,222,"C",""],["t",40,222,"D","e"],["t",137,36,"E","m"],["t",137,232,"G","m"],["t",34,132,"F","e"],["t",244,132,"H",""],["t",160,152,"2 \u03A9",""]]}},{pn:266,y:"IIT-JEE 1982",tp:"crc",st:"Charging and discharging of a capacitor",q:"Calculate the steady state current in the 2 ohm resistor shown in the circuit in the figure. The internal resistance of the battery is negligible and the capacitance of the condenser C is 0.2 microfarad.",a:"About 0.123 A (0.12 A)",s:`In the steady state a capacitor is fully charged and carries no current, so the branch with C and the 4 \u03A9 resistor can be ignored.
The current then flows only through the 6 V battery, the 28 \u03A9 resistor, and the 2 \u03A9 and 3 \u03A9 resistors, which are in parallel.
Parallel part: (2 \xD7 3)/(2 + 3) = 6/5 = 1.2 \u03A9.
Total resistance = 28 + 1.2 = 29.2 \u03A9, so the battery current is I = 6/29.2 = 0.2055 A.
This current divides between 2 \u03A9 and 3 \u03A9 in the inverse ratio of their resistances, so the 2 \u03A9 resistor gets 3/5 of it.
Current in the 2 \u03A9 resistor = 0.2055 \xD7 3/5 = 0.123 A.
Note: the 0.2 \u03BCF value is not needed; it only matters for the charge on C, not for the steady current.`,fig:{w:300,h:235,alt:"A 6 volt battery with a 28 ohm resistor, a branch with capacitor C and 4 ohm resistor, and a 2 ohm and 3 ohm resistor in parallel, all joined between two rails",d:[["l",30,70,95,70],["l",95,40,95,100],["l",95,40,135,40],["p","M135 40 L138.3 44 L145 36 L151.7 44 L158.3 36 L165 44 L171.7 36 L175 40"],["l",175,40,215,40],["t",155,30,"2 \u03A9","m"],["l",95,100,135,100],["p","M135 100 L138.3 104 L145 96 L151.7 104 L158.3 96 L165 104 L171.7 96 L175 100"],["l",175,100,215,100],["t",155,88,"3 \u03A9","m"],["l",215,40,215,100],["l",215,70,270,70],["l",30,70,30,200],["l",270,70,270,200],["l",30,140,60,140],["l",60,131,60,149],["l",66,131,66,149],["l",66,140,100,140],["l",100,140,165,140],["p","M165 140 L168.3 144 L175 136 L181.7 144 L188.3 136 L195 144 L201.7 136 L205 140"],["l",205,140,270,140],["t",63,126,"C","m"],["t",180,128,"4 \u03A9","m"],["c",30,140,2.2,"F"],["c",270,140,2.2,"F"],["l",30,200,62,200],["l",62,188,62,212],["l",69,194,69,206,"B"],["l",69,200,100,200],["l",100,200,165,200],["p","M165 200 L168.3 204 L175 196 L181.7 204 L188.3 196 L195 204 L201.7 196 L205 200"],["l",205,200,270,200],["t",180,188,"28 \u03A9","m"],["t",66,222,"6 V","m"]]}},{pn:267,y:"IIT-JEE 1981",tp:"ckir",st:"Kirchhoff's laws",q:`In the circuit shown in figure, E\u2081 = 3 volt, E\u2082 = 2 volt, E\u2083 = 1 volt and R = r\u2081 = r\u2082 = r\u2083 = 1 ohm.
(i) Find the potential difference between the points A and B and the currents through each branch.
(ii) If r\u2082 is short circuited and the point A is connected to point B, find the currents through E\u2081, E\u2082, E\u2083 and the resistor R.`,a:"(i) V_A \u2212 V_B = 2 V; i\u2081 = 1 A (D to C, as marked), i\u2082 = 0, i\u2083 = 1 A (C to D, as marked). (ii) E\u2081: 1 A (D to C), E\u2082: 2 A (D to C), E\u2083: 1 A (C to D), R: 2 A (C to D).",s:`Take i\u2081 and i\u2082 towards C and i\u2083 away from C, as marked. Let x = V_C \u2212 V_D. A and B are open, so R carries no current and V_A \u2212 V_B = x.
Branch 1 (current i\u2081 from D to C): x = E\u2081 \u2212 i\u2081r\u2081, so i\u2081 = 3 \u2212 x.
Branch 2 (i\u2082 from D to C): x = E\u2082 \u2212 i\u2082r\u2082, so i\u2082 = 2 \u2212 x.
Branch 3 (i\u2083 from C to D): x = i\u2083r\u2083 + E\u2083, so i\u2083 = x \u2212 1.
Kirchhoff's current law at C: i\u2081 + i\u2082 = i\u2083, so (3 \u2212 x) + (2 \u2212 x) = x \u2212 1.
3x = 6, so x = 2 V. Then i\u2081 = 1 A, i\u2082 = 0, i\u2083 = 1 A.
(i) V_A \u2212 V_B = 2 V; i\u2081 = 1 A (D to C), i\u2082 = 0, i\u2083 = 1 A (C to D).
(ii) With r\u2082 shorted, the E\u2082 branch fixes x = E\u2082 = 2 V, and joining A to B puts R across C and D, so R carries x/R = 2 A (C to D).
Branch 1: i\u2081 = 3 \u2212 x = 1 A (D to C). Branch 3: i\u2083 = x \u2212 1 = 1 A (C to D).
Check at C: in 1 + 2 = 3 A (E\u2081 and E\u2082 branches), out 1 + 2 = 3 A (E\u2083 branch and R).
So E\u2081: 1 A, E\u2082: 2 A, E\u2083: 1 A and R: 2 A.`,fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"Resistor R from A to junction C; from C three branches to D, each with a resistor and a cell (r1 with E1, r2 with E2, r3 with E3); B is joined to D",d:[["c",8,100,2.2,"F"],["l",8,100,28,100],["l",28,100,44,100],["p","M44 100 L47.3 104 L54 96 L60.7 104 L67.3 96 L74 104 L80.7 96 L84 100"],["l",84,100,100,100],["t",64,86,"R","m"],["c",100,100,2.2,"F"],["l",100,100,135,45],["l",135,45,155,45],["p","M155 45 L158.3 49 L165 41 L171.7 49 L178.3 41 L185 49 L191.7 41 L195 45"],["l",195,45,215,45],["t",175,34,"r\u2081","m"],["l",225,33,225,57],["l",232,39,232,51,"B"],["l",232,45,255,45],["l",255,45,290,100],["t",221,30,"+","m"],["t",240,30,"\u2212","m"],["t",234,73,"E\u2081","m"],["l",100,100,140,100],["l",140,100,157.5,100],["p","M157.5 100 L160.8 104 L167.5 96 L174.2 104 L180.8 96 L187.5 104 L194.2 96 L197.5 100"],["l",197.5,100,215,100],["t",175,114,"r\u2082","m"],["l",225,88,225,112],["l",232,94,232,106,"B"],["l",232,100,290,100],["t",221,86,"+","m"],["t",240,86,"\u2212","m"],["t",234,128,"E\u2082","m"],["l",100,100,135,155],["l",135,155,155,155],["p","M155 155 L158.3 159 L165 151 L171.7 159 L178.3 151 L185 159 L191.7 151 L195 155"],["l",195,155,215,155],["t",175,144,"r\u2083","m"],["l",225,143,225,167],["l",232,149,232,161,"B"],["l",232,155,255,155],["l",255,155,290,100],["t",221,141,"+","m"],["t",240,141,"\u2212","m"],["t",234,183,"E\u2083","m"],["c",290,100,2.2,"F"],["l",290,100,310,100],["c",310,100,2.2,"F"],["t",4,92,"A",""],["t",316,96,"B",""],["t",94,116,"C","e"],["t",298,116,"D",""],["t",128,38,"P","e"],["t",264,38,"Q",""],["t",128,172,"T","e"],["t",264,172,"S",""],["l",127,72,118,86,"a"],["t",112,70,"i\u2081","e"],["l",148,100,134,100,"a"],["t",146,92,"i\u2082",""],["l",118,114,127,128,"a"],["t",112,136,"i\u2083","e"]]}},{pn:268,y:"IIT-JEE 1980",tp:"ccel",st:"EMF and internal resistance",q:"A battery of emf 2 volt and internal resistance 0.1 ohm is being charged with a current of 5 ampere. In what direction will the current flow inside the battery? What is the potential difference between the two terminals of the battery?",a:"Inside the battery the current flows from the positive terminal to the negative terminal; the terminal potential difference is 2.5 V.",s:`While the battery is being charged the outside source forces current into its positive terminal. Inside the battery the current therefore flows from the positive terminal to the negative terminal, opposite to the direction when it is discharging.
The outside source has to overcome the battery emf and also the voltage drop inside it.
Terminal voltage = \u03B5 + Ir.
V = 2 + 5 \xD7 0.1 = 2 + 0.5 = 2.5 V.
Note: in discharging it would be \u03B5 \u2212 Ir, which is less than \u03B5; charging makes the terminal voltage larger than \u03B5.`},{pn:269,y:"IIT-JEE 1979",tp:"crc",st:"Charging and discharging of a capacitor",q:"Find the potential difference between the points A and B and between the points B and C in the steady state.",a:"V_A \u2212 V_B = 25 V and V_B \u2212 V_C = 75 V (A is the higher potential end of the 100 V source).",s:`In the steady state no current flows through capacitors, so none flows through the 20 \u03A9 and 10 \u03A9 resistors and they have no voltage drop. So D is at the potential of A, and E is at the potential of C: 100 V is across D and E.
The wires F to D and G to E join F with D and G with E. Between D and E the capacitors are: 1 \u03BCF directly; and, through B, 3 \u03BCF (F to B) and 3 \u03BCF (B to D) in parallel on the D side, and 1 \u03BCF (B to G) and 1 \u03BCF (B to E) in parallel on the E side.
D side of B: 3 + 3 = 6 \u03BCF. E side of B: 1 + 1 = 2 \u03BCF. They are in series between D and E.
For capacitors in series the charge is the same, so the voltage divides in the inverse ratio of capacitance: V_DB : V_BE = 2 : 6 = 1 : 3.
V_DB = 100 \xD7 1/4 = 25 V and V_BE = 100 \xD7 3/4 = 75 V.
The 1 \u03BCF directly between D and E has 100 V across it and does not change this.
So V_A \u2212 V_B = 25 V and V_B \u2212 V_C = 75 V.`,fig:{w:330,h:240,alt:"A network of capacitors 3, 1, 3, 1 and 1 microfarad between points F, B, G, D and E, with a 20 ohm resistor and 100 volt source from A to C and a 10 ohm resistor from E to C",d:[["l",30,30,60,30],["l",60,21,60,39],["l",66,21,66,39],["l",66,30,120,30],["c",120,30,2.2,"F"],["l",120,30,170,30],["c",170,30,2.2,"F"],["l",170,30,215,30],["c",215,30,2.2,"F"],["l",215,30,240,30],["l",240,21,240,39],["l",246,21,246,39],["l",246,30,300,30],["l",300,30,300,100],["l",30,30,30,200],["l",120,30,120,64],["l",111,64,129,64],["l",111,70,129,70],["l",120,76,120,110],["l",215,30,215,64],["l",206,64,224,64],["l",206,70,224,70],["l",215,76,215,110],["l",30,110,150,110],["l",150,101,150,119],["l",156,101,156,119],["l",156,110,300,110],["c",120,110,2.2,"F"],["c",215,110,2.2,"F"],["c",30,110,2.2,"F"],["c",300,110,2.2,"F"],["l",300,110,300,140],["l",300,140,300,147.5],["p","M300 147.5 L296 150 L304 155 L296 160 L304 165 L296 170 L304 175 L300 177.5"],["l",300,177.5,300,185],["l",300,185,300,200],["l",30,200,52,200],["l",52,200,62,200],["p","M62 200 L65.2 204 L71.5 196 L77.8 204 L84.2 196 L90.5 204 L96.8 196 L100 200"],["l",100,200,110,200],["l",110,200,140,200],["l",140,188,140,212],["l",147,194,147,206,"B"],["l",147,200,156,200],["l",156,188,156,212],["l",163,194,163,206,"B"],["l",163,200,300,200],["t",36,24,"F","e"],["t",170,22,"B","m"],["t",306,24,"G",""],["t",20,104,"D","e"],["t",306,104,"E",""],["t",20,206,"A","e"],["t",306,206,"C",""],["t",60,18,"3 \u03BCF","m"],["t",243,18,"1 \u03BCF","m"],["t",112,72,"3 \u03BCF","e"],["t",232,72,"1 \u03BCF",""],["t",153,128,"1 \u03BCF","m"],["t",80,190,"20 \u03A9","m"],["t",276,166,"10 \u03A9","e"],["t",150,224,"100 V","m"]]}},{pn:270,y:"IIT-JEE 1997",tp:"ccel",st:"Grouping of cells",q:"In the circuit shown each battery is 5 V and has an internal resistance of 0.2 ohm. The reading in the ideal voltmeter V is ______ V.",n:0,s:`There are 8 batteries in one loop: 1 on the left, 3 on top, 1 on the right and 3 on the bottom. The voltmeter is ideal, so it draws no current and the loop is the only path.
Tracing the plates, every battery points the same way round the loop, so their emfs add: total emf = 8 \xD7 5 = 40 V.
There is no outside resistor, so the total resistance is the internal resistance of 8 batteries: 8 \xD7 0.2 = 1.6 \u03A9.
Current I = 40/1.6 = 25 A.
The voltmeter is connected across the one battery on the right, so it reads that battery's terminal voltage: V = \u03B5 \u2212 Ir = 5 \u2212 25 \xD7 0.2 = 5 \u2212 5 = 0 V.
Note: by symmetry every identical battery has the same terminal voltage, and the eight of them have to add up to 0 because the loop has no other resistance.
So the reading is 0 V.`,fig:{w:380,h:240,alt:"A single loop of eight 5 volt batteries: one on the left, three on top, one on the right and three on the bottom; an ideal voltmeter V is connected across the battery on the right",d:[["l",40,50,100,50],["l",100,38,100,62],["l",107,44,107,56,"B"],["l",107,50,160,50],["l",160,38,160,62],["l",167,44,167,56,"B"],["l",167,50,220,50],["l",220,38,220,62],["l",227,44,227,56,"B"],["l",227,50,290,50],["c",290,50,2.2,"F"],["l",290,50,340,50],["l",340,50,340,118],["c",340,130,12],["t",340,134,"V","m"],["l",340,142,340,210],["l",40,50,40,100],["l",34,108,46,108,"B"],["l",28,115,52,115],["l",40,115,40,210],["l",290,50,290,106],["l",278,106,302,106],["l",284,113,296,113,"B"],["l",290,113,290,210],["c",290,210,2.2,"F"],["l",340,210,290,210],["l",40,210,100,210],["l",100,204,100,216,"B"],["l",107,198,107,222],["l",107,210,160,210],["l",160,204,160,216,"B"],["l",167,198,167,222],["l",167,210,220,210],["l",220,204,220,216,"B"],["l",227,198,227,222],["l",227,210,290,210]]}},{pn:271,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 7 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ccmb",st:"Infinite and cube networks",q:"A wire of resistance R is bent into a triangular pyramid as shown in figure with each segment having same length. The resistance between points A and B is R/n. The value of n is",o:["16","10","14","12"],c:3,s:`A triangular pyramid (tetrahedron) has 6 edges of equal length, so each edge has resistance R/6.
By symmetry, the top corner and the corner in the middle are at the same potential when current goes from A to B, so the resistor joining them carries no current.
Three routes join A and B: the direct edge, R/6; the route through the top, R/6 + R/6 = R/3; the route through the middle corner, also R/3.
These three are in parallel: 1/R_AB = 6/R + 3/R + 3/R = 12/R.
So R_AB = R/12 and n = 12, option (d).`,fig:{w:290,h:230,alt:"A triangular pyramid drawn flat: base corners A and B, apex at the top and a fourth corner in the middle, six equal resistors on the six edges",d:[["l",40,190,120,190],["p","M120 190 L123.3 194 L130 186 L136.7 194 L143.3 186 L150 194 L156.7 186 L160 190"],["l",160,190,240,190],["l",40,190,79.2,129.3],["p","M79.2 129.3 L84.3 128.7 L81.2 118.7 L91.6 117.5 L88.4 107.5 L98.8 106.3 L95.7 96.3 L100.8 95.7"],["l",100.8,95.7,140,35],["l",240,190,200.8,129.3],["p","M200.8 129.3 L202.4 124.3 L192.1 123.1 L195.2 113.1 L184.8 111.9 L187.9 101.9 L177.6 100.7 L179.2 95.7"],["l",179.2,95.7,140,35],["l",140,35,140,60],["p","M140 60 L136 63.3 L144 70 L136 76.7 L144 83.3 L136 90 L144 96.7 L140 100"],["l",140,100,140,125],["l",40,190,73.2,168.4],["p","M73.2 168.4 L78.2 169.9 L79.4 159.6 L89.4 162.7 L90.6 152.3 L100.6 155.4 L101.8 145.1 L106.8 146.6"],["l",106.8,146.6,140,125],["l",240,190,206.8,168.4],["p","M206.8 168.4 L206.2 163.2 L196.2 166.3 L195 156 L185 159 L183.8 148.7 L173.8 151.8 L173.2 146.6"],["l",173.2,146.6,140,125],["c",40,190,2.2,"F"],["c",240,190,2.2,"F"],["c",140,35,2.2,"F"],["c",140,125,2.2,"F"],["t",28,196,"A","e"],["t",252,196,"B",""]]}},{pn:272,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 22 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ccmb",st:"Series and parallel combinations",q:"Sliding contact of a potentiometer is in the middle of the potentiometer wire having resistance R_p = 1 \u03A9 as shown in the figure. An external resistance of R_e = 2 \u03A9 is connected via the sliding contact. The electric current in the circuit is",o:["0.9 A","1.0 A","0.3 A","1.35 A"],c:1,s:`The 0.9 V battery is joined across the whole potentiometer wire (1 \u03A9). The sliding contact is at the middle, so the wire is split into two halves of 0.5 \u03A9 each.
The external 2 \u03A9 resistor is connected between one end of the wire and the sliding contact, so it is in parallel with the half on that side.
Parallel part: (2 \xD7 0.5)/(2 + 0.5) = 1/2.5 = 0.4 \u03A9.
This is in series with the other half, 0.5 \u03A9, so the total resistance is 0.4 + 0.5 = 0.9 \u03A9.
Current drawn from the battery: I = 0.9/0.9 = 1.0 A, option (b).`,fig:{w:290,h:190,alt:"A 0.9 volt cell across a potentiometer wire of 1 ohm with a sliding contact in the middle; a 2 ohm resistor joins the left end of the wire to the sliding contact",d:[["l",40,40,130,40],["l",130,28,130,52],["l",137,34,137,46,"B"],["l",137,40,250,40],["l",250,40,250,110],["l",40,40,40,165],["l",40,110,125,110],["l",125,110,140,110],["p","M140 110 L144.2 114 L152.5 106 L160.8 114 L169.2 106 L177.5 114 L185.8 106 L190 110"],["l",190,110,205,110],["l",205,110,250,110],["c",40,110,2.2,"F"],["l",40,165,70,165],["l",70,165,97.5,165],["p","M97.5 165 L100.8 169 L107.5 161 L114.2 169 L120.8 161 L127.5 169 L134.2 161 L137.5 165"],["l",137.5,165,165,165],["l",165,165,165,111,"a"],["t",160,30,"0.9 V","m"],["t",133,94,"R",""],["t",142,97,"p","s"],["t",148,94,"= 1 \u03A9",""],["t",78,152,"R",""],["t",87,155,"e","s"],["t",93,152,"= 2 \u03A9",""]]}},{pn:273,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ckir",st:"Wheatstone bridge",q:"The value of unknown resistance (x) for which the potential difference between B and D will be zero in the arrangement shown, is",o:["42 \u03A9","9 \u03A9","6 \u03A9","3 \u03A9"],c:2,s:`The network is a Wheatstone bridge. B and D are at the same potential (no p.d.) when the ratio of resistances along A-B-C equals the ratio along A-D-C: R_AB/R_BC = R_AD/R_DC.
Arm AB: 24 \u03A9 in parallel with 12 + 12 = 24 \u03A9, which gives 12 \u03A9.
Arm BC: two 1 \u03A9 resistors in parallel give 0.5 \u03A9.
Arm DC: 0.5 \u03A9.
Arm AD: x in series with two 12 \u03A9 resistors in parallel (6 \u03A9), so R_AD = x + 6.
Balance: 12/0.5 = (x + 6)/0.5, so x + 6 = 12 and x = 6 \u03A9, option (c).`,fig:{w:380,h:350,alt:"A bridge ABCD: arm AB is 24 ohm in parallel with two 12 ohm in series, arm BC two 1 ohm in parallel, arm AD x ohm in series with two 12 ohm in parallel, arm DC 0.5 ohm; a 14.5 volt cell joins A and C",d:[["l",40,150,64.2,132.3],["l",161,61.3,190,40],["c",64.2,132.3,2.2,"F"],["c",161,61.3,2.2,"F"],["l",64.2,132.3,52.4,116.1],["l",149.1,45.2,161,61.3],["l",52.4,116.1,88.7,89.5],["p","M88.7 89.5 L93 91.3 L92.3 81.9 L101.1 85.4 L100.4 75.9 L109.2 79.4 L108.5 70 L112.8 71.8"],["l",112.8,71.8,149.1,45.2],["t",91.9,72.6,"24 \u03A9","m"],["l",64.2,132.3,76,148.4],["l",172.8,77.4,161,61.3],["l",76,148.4,88.1,139.5],["p","M88.1 139.5 L92.5 141.3 L91.8 131.9 L100.5 135.4 L99.9 125.9 L108.6 129.4 L107.9 120 L112.3 121.8"],["l",112.3,121.8,124.4,112.9],["t",109.1,146.7,"12 \u03A9","m"],["l",124.4,112.9,136.5,104],["p","M136.5 104 L140.9 105.8 L140.2 96.4 L148.9 99.9 L148.3 90.4 L157 93.9 L156.3 84.5 L160.7 86.3"],["l",160.7,86.3,172.8,77.4],["t",157.5,111.3,"12 \u03A9","m"],["l",190,40,222.3,63.7],["l",302.9,122.8,340,150],["c",222.3,63.7,2.2,"F"],["c",302.9,122.8,2.2,"F"],["l",222.3,63.7,230.5,52.4],["l",311.2,111.5,302.9,122.8],["l",230.5,52.4,258.7,73.1],["p","M258.7 73.1 L258.4 77.8 L267.2 74.3 L266.5 83.7 L275.2 80.2 L274.5 89.6 L283.3 86.1 L283 90.8"],["l",283,90.8,311.2,111.5],["t",279.7,73.8,"1 \u03A9","m"],["l",222.3,63.7,214,74.9],["l",294.6,134.1,302.9,122.8],["l",214,74.9,242.2,95.6],["p","M242.2 95.6 L241.9 100.3 L250.6 96.8 L249.9 106.2 L258.7 102.8 L258 112.2 L266.7 108.7 L266.4 113.4"],["l",266.4,113.4,294.6,134.1],["t",245.4,120.6,"1 \u03A9","m"],["l",40,150,54.5,160.6],["p","M54.5 160.6 L55.5 166.3 L67 164.8 L68.9 176.2 L80.4 174.7 L82.4 186 L93.8 184.5 L94.8 190.2"],["l",94.8,190.2,109.35082964359069,200.8572750719665],["t",84.13723343983563,166.52615760229196,"x \u03A9","e"],["l",109.35082964359069,200.8572750719665,119,208],["l",177.1,250.5,190,260],["c",119,208,2.2,"F"],["c",177.1,250.5,2.2,"F"],["l",119,208,127.3,196.7],["l",185.4,239.2,177.1,250.5],["l",127.3,196.7,144.2,209.1],["p","M144.2 209.1 L143.9 213.8 L152.7 210.3 L152 219.7 L160.7 216.2 L160 225.6 L168.8 222.1 L168.5 226.8"],["l",168.5,226.8,185.4,239.2],["t",165.2,209.9,"12 \u03A9","m"],["l",119,208,110.7,219.2],["l",168.8,261.8,177.1,250.5],["l",110.7,219.2,127.7,231.6],["p","M127.7 231.6 L127.3 236.3 L136.1 232.8 L135.4 242.2 L144.1 238.8 L143.4 248.2 L152.2 244.7 L151.8 249.4"],["l",151.8,249.4,168.8,261.8],["t",130.9,256.6,"12 \u03A9","m"],["l",190,260,248.9,216.8],["p","M248.9 216.8 L253.9 218.1 L254.6 207.7 L264.7 210.2 L265.3 199.8 L275.4 202.3 L276.1 191.9 L281.1 193.2"],["l",281.1,193.2,340,150],["t",290,226,"0.5 \u03A9",""],["c",40,150,2.2,"F"],["c",190,40,2.2,"F"],["c",340,150,2.2,"F"],["c",190,260,2.2,"F"],["t",26,146,"A","e"],["t",190,26,"B","m"],["t",354,146,"C",""],["t",190,282,"D","m"],["l",40,150,14,150],["l",14,150,14,310],["l",14,310,172,310],["l",172,298,172,322],["l",179,304,179,316,"B"],["l",179,310,366,310],["l",366,310,366,150],["l",366,150,340,150],["t",176,334,"14.5 V","m"]]}},{pn:274,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ckir",st:"Metre bridge",q:"In a metre-bridge when a resistance in the left gap is 2 \u03A9 and unknown resistance in the right gap, the balance length is found to be 40 cm. On shunting the unknown resistance with 2 \u03A9, the balance length changes by",o:["62.5 cm","22.5 cm","20 cm","65 cm"],c:1,s:`At balance, (left resistance)/(right resistance) = (length on left)/(length on right).
First balance: 2/X = 40/60, so X = 3 \u03A9.
Shunting X with 2 \u03A9 gives X\u2032 = (3 \xD7 2)/(3 + 2) = 1.2 \u03A9.
New balance: 2/1.2 = l/(100 \u2212 l), so 5/3 = l/(100 \u2212 l), 5(100 \u2212 l) = 3l, and l = 500/8 = 62.5 cm.
Change in balance length = 62.5 \u2212 40 = 22.5 cm, option (b).`},{pn:275,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ckir",st:"Wheatstone bridge",q:"To measure the temperature coefficient of resistivity \u03B1 of a semiconductor, an electrical arrangement shown in the figure is prepared. The arm BC is made up of the semiconductor. The experiment is being conducted at 25\xB0C and resistance of the semiconductor arm is 3 m\u03A9. Arm BC is cooled at a constant rate of 2\xB0C/s. If the galvanometer G shows no deflection after 10 s, then \u03B1 is",o:["\u22121 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 \xB0C\u207B\xB9","\u22122 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 \xB0C\u207B\xB9","\u22122.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 \xB0C\u207B\xB9","\u22121.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 \xB0C\u207B\xB9"],c:0,s:`Note: the printed key is (a) \u22121 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 \xB0C\u207B\xB9. Working gives +1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 \xB0C\u207B\xB9 with R = R\u2080(1 + \u03B1\u0394T) and \u0394T = \u221220 \xB0C; option (a) is the only one of magnitude 1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 \xB0C\u207B\xB9, so (a) is chosen.
No deflection means the bridge is balanced: R_AB/R_BC = R_AD/R_DC.
With R_AB = 0.8 m\u03A9, R_AD = 1 m\u03A9 and R_DC = 3 m\u03A9, the balanced value of the semiconductor arm is R_BC = 0.8 \xD7 3/1 = 2.4 m\u03A9.
In 10 s at 2 \xB0C/s the temperature falls by 20 \xB0C, so \u0394T = \u221220 \xB0C.
With R = R\u2080(1 + \u03B1\u0394T): 2.4 = 3(1 + \u03B1(\u221220)), so 1 \u2212 20\u03B1 = 0.8 and \u03B1 = +0.01 = 1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 \xB0C\u207B\xB9.
The magnitude 1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 \xB0C\u207B\xB9 matches only option (a) (printed key, sign as printed), so the answer is (a).`,fig:{w:310,h:260,alt:"A bridge: A to B 0.8 milliohm, A to D 1 milliohm, B to C a plain wire (the semiconductor arm), D to C 3 milliohm, a galvanometer G between B and D, and a 5 millivolt source between A and C",d:[["l",60,100,93.6,76.5],["p","M93.6 76.5 L98.6 77.8 L99.5 67.5 L109.6 70.2 L110.4 59.8 L120.5 62.5 L121.4 52.2 L126.4 53.5"],["l",126.4,53.5,160,30],["t",88,48,"0.8 m\u03A9","e"],["l",60,100,93.6,123.5],["p","M93.6 123.5 L94.1 128.7 L104.1 126 L105 136.4 L115 133.6 L115.9 144 L125.9 141.3 L126.4 146.5"],["l",126.4,146.5,160,170],["t",86,148,"1 m\u03A9","e"],["l",160,30,260,100],["l",160,170,193.6,146.5],["p","M193.6 146.5 L198.6 147.8 L199.5 137.5 L209.6 140.2 L210.4 129.8 L220.5 132.5 L221.4 122.2 L226.4 123.5"],["l",226.4,123.5,260,100],["t",246,148,"3 m\u03A9",""],["l",160,30,160,86],["c",160,100,14],["t",160,104,"G","m"],["l",160,114,160,170],["c",60,100,2.2,"F"],["c",160,30,2.2,"F"],["c",260,100,2.2,"F"],["c",160,170,2.2,"F"],["t",46,96,"A","e"],["t",160,20,"B","m"],["t",274,96,"C",""],["t",160,186,"D","m"],["l",60,100,30,100],["l",30,100,30,225],["l",30,225,152,225],["l",152,213,152,237],["l",159,219,159,231,"B"],["l",159,225,290,225],["l",290,225,290,100],["l",290,100,260,100],["t",155,246,"V = 5 mV","m"]]}},{pn:276,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 27 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ckir",st:"Metre bridge",q:"A wire of length 10 cm and radius \u221A7 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 m is connected across the right gap of a meter bridge. When a resistance of 4.5 \u03A9 is connected on the left gap by using a resistance box, the balance length is found to be at 60 cm from the left end. If the resistivity of the wire is R \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \u03A9m, then the value of R is",o:["66","35","63","70"],c:0,s:`In a metre bridge, (left resistance)/(right resistance) = (balance length)/(100 \u2212 balance length).
4.5/X = 60/40, so the wire resistance is X = 4.5 \xD7 40/60 = 3 \u03A9.
For the wire, X = \u03C1l/A, with l = 10 cm = 0.1 m and A = \u03C0r\xB2 = \u03C0 \xD7 7 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 m\xB2.
\u03C1 = XA/l = (3 \xD7 \u03C0 \xD7 7 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078)/0.1 = 21\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \u03A9m.
Taking \u03C0 = 22/7: \u03C1 = 21 \xD7 22/7 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 = 66 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \u03A9m.
So R = 66, option (a).`},{pn:277,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 27 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ckir",st:"Wheatstone bridge",q:"Wheatstone bridge principle is used to measure the specific resistance (S\u2081) of a given wire, having length L, radius r. If X is the resistance of the wire, then the specific resistance is S\u2081 = X(\u03C0r\xB2/L). If the length of the wire gets doubled then the value of specific resistance will be:",o:["S\u2081/2","S\u2081","S\u2081/4","2S\u2081"],c:1,s:`Specific resistance (resistivity) is a property of the material of the wire, not of its size.
If the length is doubled, the resistance X doubles as well (X = \u03C1L/A), so the ratio X(\u03C0r\xB2/L) stays the same.
Check with the formula: X becomes 2X and L becomes 2L, so S = (2X)(\u03C0r\xB2)/(2L) = X(\u03C0r\xB2/L) = S\u2081.
So the specific resistance remains S\u2081, option (b).`},{pn:278,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ckir",st:"Metre bridge",q:"The resistance per centimeter of a meter bridge wire is r, with X \u03A9 resistance in the left gap. Balancing length from the left end is at 40 cm with 25 \u03A9 resistance in the right gap. Now the wire is replaced by another wire of 2r resistance per centimeter. The new balancing length for the same settings will be at",o:["20 cm","40 cm","80 cm","10 cm"],c:1,s:`At balance, X/25 = (resistance of the left part of the wire)/(resistance of the right part) = (r \xD7 l)/(r \xD7 (100 \u2212 l)) = l/(100 \u2212 l).
First wire: X/25 = 40/60. The factor r cancels.
With the new wire the resistance per cm is 2r everywhere, so the left and right parts both double, and their ratio is still l/(100 \u2212 l).
The gap resistances X and 25 \u03A9 have not changed, so X/25 = l/(100 \u2212 l) gives the same l = 40 cm.
Option (b).`},{pn:279,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ccmb",st:"Balanced networks and symmetry",q:"The equivalent resistance of the circuit shown below between points a and b is",o:["16 \u03A9","20 \u03A9","3.2 \u03A9","24 \u03A9"],c:2,s:`Name the inner corner O and the top corner T. The resistors are: a-T, T-b, a-O, O-b and T-O, all 4 \u03A9, and a-b, 16 \u03A9.
The two paths from a to b through T and through O are exactly alike (4 \u03A9 then 4 \u03A9), so T and O are at the same potential.
The T-O resistor therefore carries no current, and we can remove it.
Path a-T-b: 4 + 4 = 8 \u03A9. Path a-O-b: 4 + 4 = 8 \u03A9. In parallel these give 4 \u03A9.
This 4 \u03A9 is in parallel with the 16 \u03A9 resistor: (4 \xD7 16)/(4 + 16) = 64/20 = 3.2 \u03A9.
Option (c).`,fig:{w:340,h:275,alt:"A triangle with corners a, b and a top corner joined to a central point by 4 ohm resistors, all five 4 ohm resistors as shown, and a 16 ohm resistor joining a and b",d:[["l",40,230,140,230],["p","M140 230 L143.8 235 L151.3 225 L158.8 235 L166.3 225 L173.8 235 L181.3 225 L188.8 235 L196.3 225 L200 230"],["l",200,230,300,230],["t",170,254,"16 \u03A9","m"],["l",40,230,93,148.4],["p","M93 148.4 L98.4 147.6 L95.7 137 L106.4 135.3 L103.6 124.7 L114.3 123 L111.6 112.4 L117 111.6"],["l",117,111.6,170,30],["t",86,124,"4 \u03A9","e"],["l",170,30,223,111.6],["p","M223 111.6 L221.7 116.8 L232.4 118.6 L229.6 129.1 L240.4 130.9 L237.6 141.4 L248.3 143.2 L247 148.4"],["l",247,148.4,300,230],["t",256,124,"4 \u03A9",""],["l",170,30,170,68],["p","M170 68 L166 71.7 L174 79 L166 86.3 L174 93.7 L166 101 L174 108.3 L170 112"],["l",170,112,170,150],["t",182,92,"4 \u03A9",""],["l",40,230,86.3,201.5],["p","M86.3 201.5 L91.5 203 L93.5 192.4 L104 195.3 L106 184.7 L116.5 187.6 L118.5 177 L123.7 178.5"],["l",123.7,178.5,170,150],["t",134,198,"4 \u03A9",""],["l",300,230,253.7,201.5],["p","M253.7 201.5 L252.7 196.2 L242.3 199.2 L240.2 188.5 L229.8 191.5 L227.7 180.8 L217.3 183.8 L216.3 178.5"],["l",216.3,178.5,170,150],["t",206,198,"4 \u03A9","e"],["c",40,230,2.2,"F"],["c",300,230,2.2,"F"],["c",170,30,2.2,"F"],["c",170,150,2.2,"F"],["t",26,244,"a","e"],["t",314,244,"b",""]]}},{pn:280,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cpot",st:"Potentiometer",q:`With the help of a potentiometer, we can determine the value of emf of a given cell. The sensitivity of the potentiometer is
A. directly proportional to the length of the potentiometer wire.
B. directly proportional to the potential gradient of the wire.
C. inversely proportional to the potential gradient of the wire.
D. inversely proportional to the length of the potentiometer wire.
Choose the correct option for the above statements.`,o:["A only","B and D only","A and C only","C only"],c:2,s:`Sensitivity means how small a change in emf the potentiometer can detect, that is, how far the balance point moves for a small change of emf.
The potential gradient k = (voltage across the wire)/(length of wire) is the voltage per cm of wire. A balance length l measures emf = k l.
If k is small, a tiny change in emf moves the balance point by a large, easily measured length. So sensitivity is inversely proportional to the potential gradient. That makes C correct and B wrong.
For the same voltage across the wire, a longer wire gives a smaller gradient (k = V/L), so a longer wire is more sensitive: sensitivity is directly proportional to length. That makes A correct and D wrong.
So A and C are correct, option (c).`},{pn:281,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ccmb",st:"Balanced networks and symmetry",q:"The current I in the given circuit will be",o:["10 A","20 A","4 A","40 A"],c:0,s:`Label the points: L (left), M (between the first 4 \u03A9 and the 2 \u03A9), N (between the 2 \u03A9 and the last 4 \u03A9), and R (right end).
The resistors are: L-M 4 \u03A9, M-N 2 \u03A9, L-N 4 \u03A9 (top), N-R 4 \u03A9, M-R 4 \u03A9 (lower one).
Now compare the two routes from L to R: through M the arm L-M is 4 \u03A9 and M-R is 4 \u03A9; through N the arm L-N is 4 \u03A9 and N-R is 4 \u03A9. The ratios are equal, 4/4 = 4/4, so this is a balanced bridge and M and N are at the same potential.
So the 2 \u03A9 between M and N carries no current and can be ignored.
The two routes are 4 + 4 = 8 \u03A9 each, in parallel: 8/2 = 4 \u03A9.
I = V/R = 40/4 = 10 A, option (a).`,fig:{w:330,h:200,alt:"A 40 volt source feeding a network of four 4 ohm resistors and one 2 ohm resistor arranged as a bridge",d:[["l",20,70,50,70],["c",50,70,2.2,"F"],["l",50,70,67.5,70],["p","M67.5 70 L70.8 74 L77.5 66 L84.2 74 L90.8 66 L97.5 74 L104.2 66 L107.5 70"],["l",107.5,70,125,70],["t",88,58,"4 \u03A9","m"],["c",125,70,2.2,"F"],["l",125,70,147.5,70],["p","M147.5 70 L150.8 74 L157.5 66 L164.2 74 L170.8 66 L177.5 74 L184.2 66 L187.5 70"],["l",187.5,70,210,70],["t",168,58,"2 \u03A9","m"],["c",210,70,2.2,"F"],["l",210,70,227.5,70],["p","M227.5 70 L230.8 74 L237.5 66 L244.2 74 L250.8 66 L257.5 74 L264.2 66 L267.5 70"],["l",267.5,70,285,70],["t",248,58,"4 \u03A9","m"],["c",285,70,2.2,"F"],["l",285,70,300,70],["l",50,70,50,35],["l",50,35,105,35],["p","M105 35 L109.2 39 L117.5 31 L125.8 39 L134.2 31 L142.5 39 L150.8 31 L155 35"],["l",155,35,210,35],["t",130,24,"4 \u03A9","m"],["l",210,35,210,70],["l",125,70,125,110],["l",125,110,180,110],["p","M180 110 L184.2 114 L192.5 106 L200.8 114 L209.2 106 L217.5 114 L225.8 106 L230 110"],["l",230,110,285,110],["t",205,130,"4 \u03A9","m"],["l",285,110,285,70],["l",20,70,20,150],["l",300,70,300,150],["l",20,150,142,150],["c",160,150,18],["t",160,153,"+ \u2212","m"],["l",178,150,300,150],["t",160,184,"40 V","m"]]}},{pn:282,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ccmb",st:"Balanced networks and symmetry",q:"A battery of 6 V is connected to the circuit as shown below. The current I drawn from the battery is",o:["1 A","2 A","6/11 A","4/3 A"],c:0,s:`The 5 \u03A9 resistor joins the middle point of the top path (3 \u03A9 and 3 \u03A9) to the middle point of the bottom path (6 \u03A9 and 6 \u03A9).
The two middle points are at the same potential, because each path is split into two equal halves (3 : 3 on top, 6 : 6 below), so the voltage divides in the same ratio.
Then no current flows through the 5 \u03A9 resistor, and we can ignore it.
Top path: 3 + 3 = 6 \u03A9. Bottom path: 6 + 6 = 12 \u03A9. In parallel: (6 \xD7 12)/(6 + 12) = 4 \u03A9.
This is in series with the 2 \u03A9 resistor, so the total resistance is 4 + 2 = 6 \u03A9.
I = 6/6 = 1 A, option (a).`,fig:{w:290,h:245,alt:"A 6 volt battery with a 2 ohm resistor feeding a bridge of 3, 3, 6 and 6 ohm resistors with a 5 ohm resistor across the middle",d:[["l",40,40,40,125],["l",230,40,230,125],["l",40,40,65,40],["p","M65 40 L68.3 44 L75 36 L81.7 44 L88.3 36 L95 44 L101.7 36 L105 40"],["l",105,40,130,40],["t",85,28,"3 \u03A9","m"],["l",130,40,160,40],["p","M160 40 L163.3 44 L170 36 L176.7 44 L183.3 36 L190 44 L196.7 36 L200 40"],["l",200,40,230,40],["t",180,28,"3 \u03A9","m"],["c",130,40,2.2,"F"],["l",40,125,65,125],["p","M65 125 L68.3 129 L75 121 L81.7 129 L88.3 121 L95 129 L101.7 121 L105 125"],["l",105,125,130,125],["t",85,148,"6 \u03A9","m"],["l",130,125,160,125],["p","M160 125 L163.3 129 L170 121 L176.7 129 L183.3 121 L190 129 L196.7 121 L200 125"],["l",200,125,230,125],["t",180,148,"6 \u03A9","m"],["c",130,125,2.2,"F"],["l",130,40,130,125],["l",130,40,130,62.5],["p","M130 62.5 L126 65.8 L134 72.5 L126 79.2 L134 85.8 L126 92.5 L134 99.2 L130 102.5"],["l",130,102.5,130,125],["t",142,86,"5 \u03A9",""],["l",40,82,14,82],["l",14,82,14,200],["l",14,200,105,200],["l",105,188,105,212],["l",112,194,112,206,"B"],["l",112,200,150,200],["l",150,200,184,200],["p","M184 200 L187.3 204 L194 196 L200.7 204 L207.3 196 L214 204 L220.7 196 L224 200"],["l",224,200,258,200],["t",205,222,"2 \u03A9","m"],["l",258,200,258,82],["l",258,82,230,82],["l",70,200,52,200,"a"],["t",58,190,"I","m"],["t",108,222,"6 V",""],["c",40,82,2.2,"F"],["c",230,82,2.2,"F"]]}},{pn:283,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cpot",st:"Potentiometer",q:"In the given figure, there is a circuit of potentiometer of length AB = 10 m. The resistance per unit length is 0.1 \u03A9 per cm. Across AB, a battery of emf E and internal resistance r is connected. The maximum value of emf measured by this potentiometer is",o:["2.75 V","2.25 V","6 V","5 V"],c:3,s:`The potentiometer wire is 10 m = 1000 cm long, so its resistance is 1000 \xD7 0.1 = 100 \u03A9.
The driver circuit is the 6 V battery, the 20 \u03A9 resistor and the wire in series (the key K is closed). The current is I = 6/(20 + 100) = 0.05 A.
The voltage across the whole wire AB is I \xD7 100 = 0.05 \xD7 100 = 5 V.
A potentiometer can only balance an emf that is smaller than the voltage across its whole wire, since the balance point must lie on the wire.
So the largest emf that can be measured is 5 V, option (d).`,fig:{w:340,h:225,alt:"A potentiometer wire AB with a 6 volt battery and 20 ohm resistor through a key K, a cell E with resistor r and galvanometer G leading to a sliding jockey; the jockey is 550 cm from A and 450 cm from B",d:[["l",30,120,300,120],["l",30,120,30,45],["l",30,45,52,45],["l",52,33,52,57],["l",59,39,59,51,"B"],["l",59,45,95,45],["l",95,45,102.5,45],["p","M102.5 45 L105.8 49 L112.5 41 L119.2 49 L125.8 41 L132.5 49 L139.2 41 L142.5 45"],["l",142.5,45,150,45],["l",150,45,210,45],["l",210,45,210,68],["c",210,80,12],["t",210,84,"G","m"],["l",210,92,210,119,"a"],["l",30,120,30,190],["l",30,190,52,190],["l",52,178,52,202],["l",59,184,59,196,"B"],["l",59,190,95,190],["l",95,190,102.5,190],["p","M102.5 190 L105.8 194 L112.5 186 L119.2 194 L125.8 186 L132.5 194 L139.2 186 L142.5 190"],["l",142.5,190,150,190],["l",150,190,178,190],["l",184,182,208,170],["l",212,190,300,190],["c",179,190,2.2,"F"],["c",212,190,2.2,"F"],["l",300,190,300,120],["t",30,112,"A","e"],["t",308,114,"B",""],["t",220,112,"J",""],["t",44,34,"+","e"],["t",68,34,"\u2212","m"],["t",62,216,"6 V","m"],["t",122,208,"20 \u03A9","m"],["t",196,208,"K","m"],["t",60,22,"E","m"],["t",122,34,"r","m"],["t",120,150,"550 cm","m"],["t",262,164,"450 cm","m"],["l",30,134,208,134,"b"],["l",216,146,300,146,"b"],["t",45,174,"+","e"],["t",67,174,"\u2212","m"]]}},{pn:284,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cpot",st:"Potentiometer",q:"The given potentiometer has its wire of resistance 10 \u03A9. When the sliding contact is in the middle of the potentiometer wire, the potential drop across the 2 \u03A9 resistor is",o:["10 V","40/11 V","40/9 V","5 V"],c:2,s:`The 20 V battery is connected across the whole potentiometer wire AB (10 \u03A9). With the sliding contact in the middle, the wire is two halves of 5 \u03A9 each.
The 2 \u03A9 resistor joins A to the sliding contact, so it is in parallel with the half of the wire between A and the contact.
Parallel part: (2 \xD7 5)/(2 + 5) = 10/7 \u03A9.
This is in series with the other half, 5 \u03A9: total R = 10/7 + 5 = 45/7 \u03A9.
Total current I = 20/(45/7) = 140/45 = 28/9 A.
Voltage across the parallel part (which is the voltage across the 2 \u03A9 resistor) = I \xD7 10/7 = (28/9)(10/7) = 40/9 V.
Option (c).`,fig:{w:290,h:190,alt:"A 20 volt cell across a potentiometer wire AB of 10 ohm with the sliding contact at the middle, joined back to A through a 2 ohm resistor",d:[["l",30,40,100,40],["l",100,28,100,52],["l",107,34,107,46,"B"],["l",107,40,240,40],["l",240,40,240,100],["c",240,100,2.2,"F"],["l",30,100,50,100],["p","M50 100 L55.3 104 L65.9 96 L76.6 104 L87.2 96 L97.8 104 L108.4 96 L119.1 104 L129.7 96 L140.3 104 L150.9 96 L161.6 104 L172.2 96 L182.8 104 L193.4 96 L204.1 104 L214.7 96 L220 100"],["l",220,100,240,100],["c",30,100,2.2,"F"],["l",30,100,30,165],["l",30,165,52,165],["l",52,165,75.5,165],["p","M75.5 165 L78.5 169 L84.5 161 L90.5 169 L96.5 161 L102.5 169 L108.5 161 L111.5 165"],["l",111.5,165,135,165],["t",93,154,"2 \u03A9","m"],["l",135,165,135,104,"a"],["l",30,40,30,100],["t",100,20,"20 V","m"],["t",18,104,"A","e"],["t",252,104,"B",""]]}},{pn:285,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 17 Mar \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ckir",st:"Wheatstone bridge",q:"The four arms of a Wheatstone bridge have resistances as shown in the figure. A galvanometer of 15 \u03A9 resistance is connected across BD. Calculate the current through the galvanometer when a potential difference of 10 V is maintained across AC.",o:["4.87 mA","4.87 \u03BCA","2.44 mA","2.44 \u03BCA"],c:0,s:`The bridge is not balanced (100/10 is not 60/5), so the galvanometer carries a small current. Find the potentials of B and D and use the Thevenin idea.
Take C as 0 V and A as 10 V. Voltage divider on the B side: V_B = 10 \xD7 10/(100 + 10) = 0.909 V.
On the D side: V_D = 10 \xD7 5/(60 + 5) = 0.769 V.
Voltage across the galvanometer branch, V_B \u2212 V_D = 0.909 \u2212 0.769 = 0.140 V.
The resistance seen between B and D with the source replaced by a wire: (100 \xD7 10)/110 + (60 \xD7 5)/65 = 9.09 + 4.62 = 13.71 \u03A9.
Add the galvanometer: 13.71 + 15 = 28.71 \u03A9.
Current I = 0.140/28.71 = 4.87 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 A = 4.87 mA, option (a).`,fig:{w:360,h:300,alt:"A Wheatstone bridge ABCD: A to B 100 ohm, B to C 10 ohm, A to D 60 ohm, D to C 5 ohm, a galvanometer G of 15 ohm between B and D, and a 10 volt cell between A and C",d:[["l",60,120,101.7,92.2],["p","M101.7 92.2 L107 93.5 L108.6 82.8 L119.2 85.4 L120.8 74.6 L131.4 77.2 L133 66.5 L138.3 67.8"],["l",138.3,67.8,180,40],["t",98,58,"100 \u03A9","e"],["l",180,40,221.7,67.8],["p","M221.7 67.8 L222.5 73.2 L233.1 70.6 L234.7 81.3 L245.3 78.7 L246.9 89.4 L257.5 86.8 L258.3 92.2"],["l",258.3,92.2,300,120],["t",264,58,"10 \u03A9",""],["l",60,120,101.7,147.8],["p","M101.7 147.8 L102.5 153.2 L113.1 150.6 L114.7 161.3 L125.3 158.7 L126.9 169.4 L137.5 166.8 L138.3 172.2"],["l",138.3,172.2,180,200],["t",98,190,"60 \u03A9","e"],["l",180,200,221.7,172.2],["p","M221.7 172.2 L227 173.5 L228.6 162.8 L239.2 165.4 L240.8 154.6 L251.4 157.2 L253 146.5 L258.3 147.8"],["l",258.3,147.8,300,120],["t",264,190,"5 \u03A9",""],["l",180,40,180,98],["c",180,112,14],["l",172,118,188,106],["t",200,118,"G",""],["l",180,126,180,200],["c",60,120,2.2,"F"],["c",180,40,2.2,"F"],["c",300,120,2.2,"F"],["c",180,200,2.2,"F"],["t",46,116,"A","e"],["t",180,28,"B","m"],["t",314,116,"C",""],["t",180,218,"D","m"],["l",60,120,30,120],["l",30,120,30,260],["l",30,260,172,260],["l",172,248,172,272],["l",179,254,179,266,"B"],["l",179,260,330,260],["l",330,260,330,120],["l",330,120,300,120],["t",176,282,"10 V","m"]]}},{pn:286,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ccmb",st:"Balanced networks and symmetry",q:"Five equal resistances are connected in a network as shown in figure. The net resistance between the points A and B is",o:["R","2R","R/2","3R/2"],c:0,s:`Note: the printed key says 2, which would mean option (b) 2R; working gives R, option (a).
Follow the wires. Point A is joined by a plain wire to the middle point O, and point B is joined by a plain wire to the corner C. So we need the resistance between O and C.
The resistors are: O-D, O-E, D-E, D-C and E-C, all of resistance R.
The network is symmetrical about the line from O to C: D and E are at the same potential, so the resistor D-E carries no current and can be removed.
Path O-D-C: R + R = 2R. Path O-E-C: R + R = 2R. In parallel: 2R/2 = R.
So the net resistance between A and B is R, option (a).`,fig:{w:340,h:245,alt:"A triangle with corners D, E and C and a centre point O joined to D and E by resistors R; resistors R on DE, DC and EC; terminal A is wired to O and terminal B is wired to C",d:[["l",170,30,118.4,97.5],["p","M118.4 97.5 L113 98 L114.9 108.7 L104 109.7 L106 120.3 L95.1 121.3 L97 132 L91.6 132.5"],["l",91.6,132.5,40,200],["t",88,104,"R","e"],["l",170,30,221.6,97.5],["p","M221.6 97.5 L220.7 102.9 L231.5 103.8 L229.6 114.5 L240.4 115.5 L238.5 126.2 L249.3 127.1 L248.4 132.5"],["l",248.4,132.5,300,200],["t",252,104,"R",""],["l",40,200,148,200],["p","M148 200 L151.7 204 L159 196 L166.3 204 L173.7 196 L181 204 L188.3 196 L192 200"],["l",192,200,300,200],["t",170,224,"R","m"],["l",170,30,170,58],["p","M170 58 L166 61.7 L174 69 L166 76.3 L174 83.7 L166 91 L174 98.3 L170 102"],["l",170,102,170,130],["t",182,80,"R",""],["l",40,200,85.6,175.4],["p","M85.6 175.4 L90.8 177.2 L93.4 166.7 L103.7 170.3 L106.3 159.7 L116.6 163.3 L119.2 152.8 L124.4 154.6"],["l",124.4,154.6,170,130],["t",118,184,"R",""],["l",170,130,225,160],["c",225,160,2.2,"F"],["c",170,30,2.2,"F"],["c",40,200,2.2,"F"],["c",300,200,2.2,"F"],["c",170,130,2.2,"F"],["l",300,200,268,176],["c",268,176,2.2,"F"],["t",172,22,"D","m"],["t",28,206,"E","e"],["t",312,206,"C",""],["t",225,150,"A",""],["t",262,172,"B","e"]]}},{pn:287,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 2 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cpot",st:"Potentiometer",q:"A potentiometer wire PQ of 1 m length is connected to a standard cell E\u2081. Another cell E\u2082 of emf 1.02 V is connected with resistance r and switch S (as shown in figure). With switch S open, the null position is obtained at a distance of 49 cm from Q. The potential gradient in the potentiometer wire is",o:["0.03 V/cm","0.02 V/cm","0.01 V/cm","0.04 V/cm"],c:1,s:`At the null point no current flows through the galvanometer, so the potential difference along the wire from the end to the jockey equals the emf of cell E\u2082.
The emf of E\u2082 is 1.02 V and the null point is 49 cm from the end Q.
Potential gradient = (potential difference)/(length) = 1.02 V/49 cm = 0.0208 V/cm.
This is close to 0.02 V/cm, option (b).
Note: with the switch open the resistance r is in the circuit of E\u2082, but at the null point there is no current in that circuit, so r makes no difference.`,fig:{w:350,h:260,alt:"A potentiometer wire PQ with a standard cell E1 across it; a second cell E2 in series with a resistor r that has a switch S across it, a galvanometer G and a jockey J on the wire",d:[["l",30,90,320,90],["l",30,90,30,40],["l",30,40,165,40],["l",165,28,165,52],["l",172,34,172,46,"B"],["l",172,40,320,40],["l",320,40,320,90],["l",30,90,30,200],["l",30,200,70,200],["l",70,188,70,212],["l",77,194,77,206,"B"],["l",77,200,120,200],["c",120,200,2.2,"F"],["l",120,200,120,165],["l",120,165,152.5,165],["p","M152.5 165 L156.7 169 L165 161 L173.3 169 L181.7 161 L190 169 L198.3 161 L202.5 165"],["l",202.5,165,235,165],["t",177,153,"r","m"],["l",235,165,235,200],["c",235,200,2.2,"F"],["l",120,200,120,225],["l",120,225,160,225],["l",190,213,160,225],["l",195,225,235,225],["l",235,225,235,200],["c",160,225,2.2,"F"],["c",195,225,2.2,"F"],["t",178,243,"S","m"],["l",235,200,290,200],["l",290,200,290,150],["c",290,138,12],["t",290,142,"G","m"],["l",290,126,290,115],["l",290,115,200,115],["l",200,115,200,91,"a"],["t",176,28,"E\u2081","m"],["t",74,222,"E\u2082","m"],["t",16,94,"P","e"],["t",332,94,"Q",""],["t",210,106,"J",""]]}},{pn:288,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 4 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ccmb",st:"Balanced networks and symmetry",q:"The value of current i\u2081 flowing from A to C in the circuit diagram is",o:["5 A","2 A","4 A","1 A"],c:3,s:`Let O be the central point where the two 4 \u03A9 resistors and the two 5 \u03A9 resistors meet. The 8 V battery is connected across A and C.
The path A-B-C has 2 \u03A9 + 2 \u03A9, and the path A-D-C is the same. The path A-O-C has 4 \u03A9 + 4 \u03A9.
The network is symmetrical: from A, the point B is one 2 \u03A9 step along and the point O is one 4 \u03A9 step along, and B-C is 2 \u03A9 while O-C is 4 \u03A9. Along both A-B-C and A-O-C the voltage divides in equal halves, so B, O and D are at the same potential (half of 8 V, 4 V below A).
So the two 5 \u03A9 resistors carry no current and can be removed.
The 4 \u03A9 resistor from A to O now carries the current of the A-O-C route: i\u2081 = 8 V/(4 + 4) \u03A9 = 1 A.
Option (d).`,fig:{w:390,h:310,alt:"An 8 volt cell across A and C of a bridge with 2 ohm resistors on AB, BC, AD and DC, 4 ohm resistors on AO and OC, and 5 ohm resistors on BO and OD, with i1 flowing from A to O",d:[["l",60,170,107.6,133.4],["p","M107.6 133.4 L112.9 134.3 L113.8 123.5 L124.5 125.4 L125.5 114.6 L136.2 116.5 L137.1 105.7 L142.4 106.6"],["l",142.4,106.6,190,70],["t",112,100,"2 \u03A9","e"],["l",190,70,237.6,106.6],["p","M237.6 106.6 L238 112 L248.7 110.1 L249.7 120.9 L260.3 119.1 L261.3 129.9 L272 128 L272.4 133.4"],["l",272.4,133.4,320,170],["t",262,100,"2 \u03A9",""],["l",60,170,107.6,206.6],["p","M107.6 206.6 L108 212 L118.7 210.1 L119.7 220.9 L130.3 219.1 L131.3 229.9 L142 228 L142.4 233.4"],["l",142.4,233.4,190,270],["t",112,248,"2 \u03A9","e"],["l",190,270,237.6,233.4],["p","M237.6 233.4 L242.9 234.3 L243.8 223.5 L254.5 225.4 L255.5 214.6 L266.2 216.5 L267.1 205.7 L272.4 206.6"],["l",272.4,206.6,320,170],["t",262,248,"2 \u03A9",""],["l",60,170,95,170],["p","M95 170 L100 174 L110 166 L120 174 L130 166 L140 174 L150 166 L155 170"],["l",155,170,190,170],["t",124,158,"4 \u03A9","m"],["l",190,170,225,170],["p","M225 170 L230 174 L240 166 L250 174 L260 166 L270 174 L280 166 L285 170"],["l",285,170,320,170],["t",256,158,"4 \u03A9","m"],["l",190,70,190,98],["p","M190 98 L186 101.7 L194 109 L186 116.3 L194 123.7 L186 131 L194 138.3 L190 142"],["l",190,142,190,170],["t",204,126,"5 \u03A9",""],["l",190,170,190,198],["p","M190 198 L186 201.7 L194 209 L186 216.3 L194 223.7 L186 231 L194 238.3 L190 242"],["l",190,242,190,270],["t",204,222,"5 \u03A9",""],["l",104,170,128,170,"a"],["t",116,190,"i\u2081","m"],["c",60,170,2.2,"F"],["c",190,70,2.2,"F"],["c",320,170,2.2,"F"],["c",190,270,2.2,"F"],["c",190,170,2.2,"F"],["t",46,188,"A","e"],["t",190,58,"B","m"],["t",328,190,"C",""],["t",190,290,"D","m"],["l",60,170,20,170],["l",20,170,20,25],["l",20,25,120,25],["l",120,13,120,37],["l",127,19,127,31,"B"],["l",127,25,360,25],["l",360,25,360,170],["l",360,170,320,170],["t",130,14,"8 V","m"],["l",20,100,20,124,"a"],["l",360,124,360,100,"a"],["t",8,114,"i","e"],["t",370,114,"i",""]]}},{pn:289,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 8 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cpot",st:"Potentiometer",q:"The length of a potentiometer wire is 1200 cm and it carries a current of 60 mA. For a cell of emf 5 V and internal resistance of 20 \u03A9, the null point on it is found to be at 1000 cm. The resistance of whole wire is",o:["120 \u03A9","80 \u03A9","60 \u03A9","100 \u03A9"],c:3,s:`At the null point no current flows through the cell, so its emf equals the potential drop along the wire up to the null point; the internal resistance of 20 \u03A9 does not matter.
The potential drop is proportional to length, so if 1000 cm gives 5 V, the whole 1200 cm wire has V = 5 \xD7 (1200/1000) = 6 V.
This 6 V is the drop across the whole wire when it carries 60 mA.
Note: use Ohm's law on the whole wire: R = V/I.
R = 6/(60 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3) = 6/0.06 = 100 \u03A9.
So the resistance of the wire is 100 \u03A9, option (d).`},{pn:290,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cpot",st:"Potentiometer",q:"In the circuit shown, a four-wire potentiometer is made of a 400 cm long wire, which extends between A and B. The resistance per unit length of the potentiometer wire is r = 0.01 \u03A9/cm. If an ideal voltmeter is connected as shown with jockey J at 50 cm from end A, the expected reading of the voltmeter will be",fig:{w:340,h:205,alt:"Four-wire potentiometer: a 400 cm wire folded into four 100 cm rows from A (top left) to B (bottom left); two 1.5 V cells with 0.5 ohm internal resistance each and a 1 ohm resistor in series with it; a voltmeter between A and the jockey J at 50 cm along the top row",d:[["l",40,50,70,50],["l",70,39,70,61],["l",75,44,75,56,"B"],["l",75,50,90,50],["l",90,39,90,61],["l",95,44,95,56,"B"],["l",95,50,130,50],["l",40,50,40,95],["p","M40 95 L40 100 L45 102.5 L35 107.5 L45 112.5 L35 117.5 L45 122.5 L35 127.5 L40 130 L40 135"],["l",40,135,40,150],["l",40,150,130,150],["l",130,50,300,50],["l",300,50,300,80],["l",300,80,130,80],["l",130,80,130,110],["l",130,110,300,110],["l",300,110,300,140],["l",300,140,130,140],["l",130,140,130,150],["l",130,50,130,22],["l",130,22,164,22],["c",176,22,11],["t",176,26,"V","ms"],["l",187,22,215,22],["l",215,22,215,48,"a"],["t",80,20,"1.5 V, 1.5 V","m"],["t",80,34,"0.5 \u03A9, 0.5 \u03A9","m"],["t",60,118,"1 \u03A9",""],["t",124,64,"A","e"],["t",124,162,"B","e"],["t",200,16,"J","m"],["t",215,66,"50 cm","m"],["l",130,178,300,178,"b"],["t",215,194,"100 cm","m"],["l",124,50,136,50]]},o:["0.25 V","0.20 V","0.50 V","0.75 V"],c:0,s:`The wire carries a steady current from the two cells, so first find that current.
The two 1.5 V cells are in series: emf = 3 V, internal resistance = 0.5 + 0.5 = 1 \u03A9.
Wire resistance = 400 cm \xD7 0.01 \u03A9/cm = 4 \u03A9. Total resistance = 1 (cells) + 1 (resistor) + 4 (wire) = 6 \u03A9.
Current I = 3/6 = 0.5 A.
The ideal voltmeter draws no current, so it reads the potential drop along 50 cm of wire from A to J: resistance = 50 \xD7 0.01 = 0.5 \u03A9.
V = I \xD7 R = 0.5 \xD7 0.5 = 0.25 V.
So the reading is 0.25 V, option (a).`},{pn:291,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cpot",st:"Potentiometer",q:"A potentiometer wire AB having length L and resistance 12r is joined to a cell D of emf \u03B5 and internal resistance r. A cell C having emf \u03B5/2 and internal resistance 3r is connected. The length AJ at which the galvanometer as shown in figure shows no deflection is",fig:{w:340,h:215,alt:"Potentiometer wire AB joined to cell D of emf epsilon and internal resistance r at the top; cell C of emf epsilon over 2 and internal resistance 3r in series with a galvanometer G joins A to the jockey J at the bottom; positive plates face A",d:[["l",40,30,170,30],["l",170,19,170,41],["l",175,24,175,36,"B"],["l",175,30,300,30],["l",300,30,300,100],["l",40,30,40,170],["l",40,100,300,100],["c",40,100,2.5,"F"],["c",300,100,2.5,"F"],["l",40,170,170,170],["l",170,159,170,181],["l",175,164,175,176,"B"],["l",175,170,215,170],["c",227,170,12],["t",227,174,"G","ms"],["l",239,170,260,170],["l",260,170,260,102],["l",260,118,260,101,"a"],["t",34,92,"A","e"],["t",306,92,"B",""],["t",262,92,"J","m"],["t",170,16,"D","m"],["t",205,24,"\u03B5, r",""],["t",158,196,"C","m"],["t",215,200,"\u03B5/2, 3r","m"],["t",150,24,"+","e"],["t",160,164,"+","e"],["t",185,24,"\u2212","m"],["t",190,164,"\u2212","m"]]},o:["(11)/(12) L","(5)/(12) L","(11)/(24) L","(13)/(24) L"],c:3,s:`Cell D sends a steady current through the whole wire: I = \u03B5/(12r + r) = \u03B5/(13r).
The potential drop along a length AJ = x of the wire (resistance (x/L) \xD7 12r) is V = I \xD7 (x/L) \xD7 12r = (\u03B5/13r)(12r x/L) = (12/13)(x/L) \u03B5.
At the null point no current flows through cell C, so its emf (not affected by its internal resistance 3r) equals this drop. The positive plates of both cells face A, so they oppose each other correctly.
Set V = \u03B5/2: (12/13)(x/L) \u03B5 = \u03B5/2.
x/L = 13/24.
So AJ = (13/24) L, option (d).`},{pn:292,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ckir",st:"Wheatstone bridge",q:"The Wheatstone bridge shown in figure here gets balanced when the carbon resistor used as R\u2081 has the colour code (Orange, Red, Brown). The resistors R\u2082 and R\u2084 are 80 \u03A9 and 40 \u03A9, respectively. Assume that the colour code for the carbon resistors gives their accurate values, the colour code for the carbon resistor, used as R\u2083, would be",fig:{w:320,h:230,alt:"Wheatstone bridge: R1 top left, R2 top right, R4 bottom left, R3 bottom right, galvanometer G joining the top and bottom corners, and a cell across the left and right corners",d:[["c",160,40,2.5,"F"],["c",60,100,2.5,"F"],["c",260,100,2.5,"F"],["c",160,160,2.5,"F"],["p","M60 100 L94.6 79.3 L94.6 73.4 L104.9 78.9 L104.9 67.3 L115.1 72.7 L115.1 61.1 L125.4 66.6 L125.4 60.7 L160 40"],["p","M160 40 L194.6 60.7 L199.7 58 L199.7 69.7 L210 64.2 L210 75.8 L220.3 70.3 L220.3 82 L225.4 79.3 L260 100"],["p","M60 100 L94.6 120.7 L99.7 118 L99.7 129.7 L110 124.2 L110 135.8 L120.3 130.3 L120.3 142 L125.4 139.3 L160 160"],["p","M160 160 L194.6 139.3 L194.6 133.4 L204.9 138.9 L204.9 127.3 L215.1 132.7 L215.1 121.1 L225.4 126.6 L225.4 120.7 L260 100"],["l",160,40,160,88],["c",160,100,12],["t",160,104,"G","ms"],["l",160,112,160,160],["l",60,100,30,100],["l",30,100,30,200],["l",30,200,125,200],["l",125,189,125,211],["l",130,194,130,206,"B"],["l",130,200,290,200],["l",290,200,290,100],["l",290,100,260,100],["t",76,62,"R\u2081","e"],["t",246,62,"R\u2082",""],["t",76,142,"R\u2084","e"],["t",246,142,"R\u2083",""]]},o:["Brown, Blue, Brown","Brown, Blue, Black","Grey, Black, Brown","Red, Green, Brown"],c:0,s:`Colour code of R\u2081: Orange = 3, Red = 2, Brown = \xD710, so R\u2081 = 32 \xD7 10 = 320 \u03A9.
For the figure as drawn (R\u2081 and R\u2082 in the upper arms, R\u2084 and R\u2083 in the lower arms, galvanometer between the two middle corners) the balance condition is R\u2081/R\u2082 = R\u2084/R\u2083.
This gives R\u2083 = R\u2082R\u2084/R\u2081 = 80 \xD7 40/320 = 10 \u03A9, which is Brown, Black, Black. None of the options is 10 \u03A9.
Note: the printed key says (a); working with the figure as drawn gives 10 \u03A9, which is not an option. The key (a) = Brown, Blue, Brown = 16 \xD7 10 = 160 \u03A9 follows only if the condition is taken as R\u2081/R\u2082 = R\u2083/R\u2084 (R\u2083 = R\u2081R\u2084/R\u2082 = 320 \xD7 40/80 = 160 \u03A9), so option (a) is marked as the intended answer.
The intended answer is (a), Brown, Blue, Brown (160 \u03A9).`},{pn:293,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 11 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ckir",st:"Metre bridge",q:"The resistance of the meter bridge AB in given figure is 4 \u03A9. With a cell of emf \u03B5 = 0.5 V and rheostat resistance R\u2095 = 2 \u03A9, the null point is obtained at some point J. When the cell is replaced by another one of emf \u03B5 = \u03B5\u2082 the same null point J is found for R\u2095 = 6 \u03A9. The emf \u03B5\u2082 is",fig:{w:340,h:190,alt:"Metre bridge wire AB with a cell of emf epsilon and a galvanometer joining A to the jockey J on the wire; a 6 V cell and rheostat Rh in series across A and B",d:[["l",40,30,120,30],["l",120,19,120,41],["l",125,24,125,36,"B"],["l",125,30,190,30],["l",190,30,190,63],["c",190,75,12],["t",190,79,"G","ms"],["l",190,87,190,93],["l",190,86,190,96,"a"],["l",40,30,40,150],["l",40,95,300,95],["c",40,95,2.5,"F"],["l",300,95,300,150],["t",32,88,"A","e"],["t",306,88,"B",""],["t",204,106,"J",""],["t",120,18,"\u03B5","m"],["l",40,150,95,150],["l",95,139,95,161],["l",100,144,100,156,"B"],["l",100,150,170,150],["p","M170 150 L210 150 L214.2 145 L222.5 155 L230.8 145 L239.2 155 L247.5 145 L255.8 155 L260 150 L300 150"],["t",97,172,"6 V","m"],["l",207,166,248,140,"a"],["t",232,176,"R",""],["t",241,179,"h","s"]]},o:["0.5 V","0.4 V","0.6 V","0.3 V"],c:3,s:`The 6 V cell, the rheostat and the bridge wire form the main circuit; the cell of emf \u03B5 is balanced against the potential drop between A and J.
Case 1: current I\u2081 = 6/(R\u2095 + 4) = 6/(2 + 4) = 1 A. At the null point \u03B5 = I\u2081 \xD7 R_AJ, so R_AJ = 0.5/1 = 0.5 \u03A9.
The point J is the same in the second case, so R_AJ is still 0.5 \u03A9.
Case 2: current I\u2082 = 6/(6 + 4) = 0.6 A.
\u03B5\u2082 = I\u2082 \xD7 R_AJ = 0.6 \xD7 0.5 = 0.3 V.
So \u03B5\u2082 = 0.3 V, option (d).`},{pn:294,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 11 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ckir",st:"Wheatstone bridge",q:"In a Wheatstone bridge (see figure), Resistances P and Q are approximately equal. When R = 400 \u03A9, the bridge is balanced. On interchanging P and Q, the value of R, for balance, is 405 \u03A9. The value of X is close to",fig:{w:320,h:230,alt:"Wheatstone bridge with P and Q in the upper arms and R and X in the lower arms; galvanometer G with key K2 between B and D; cell with key K1 across A and C",d:[["c",160,40,2.5,"F"],["c",60,100,2.5,"F"],["c",260,100,2.5,"F"],["c",160,160,2.5,"F"],["p","M60 100 L94.6 79.3 L94.6 73.4 L104.9 78.9 L104.9 67.3 L115.1 72.7 L115.1 61.1 L125.4 66.6 L125.4 60.7 L160 40"],["p","M160 40 L194.6 60.7 L199.7 58 L199.7 69.7 L210 64.2 L210 75.8 L220.3 70.3 L220.3 82 L225.4 79.3 L260 100"],["p","M60 100 L94.6 120.7 L99.7 118 L99.7 129.7 L110 124.2 L110 135.8 L120.3 130.3 L120.3 142 L125.4 139.3 L160 160"],["p","M160 160 L194.6 139.3 L194.6 133.4 L204.9 138.9 L204.9 127.3 L215.1 132.7 L215.1 121.1 L225.4 126.6 L225.4 120.7 L260 100"],["l",160,40,160,68],["c",160,80,12],["t",160,84,"G","ms"],["l",160,92,160,108],["c",160,108,2.5,"F"],["l",160,108,170,124],["c",160,130,2.5,"F"],["l",160,130,160,160],["l",60,100,30,100],["l",30,100,30,200],["l",30,200,100,200],["l",100,189,100,211],["l",105,194,105,206,"B"],["l",105,200,190,200],["c",196,200,5],["c",196,200,2.5,"F"],["l",202,200,290,200],["l",290,200,290,100],["l",290,100,260,100],["t",76,62,"P","e"],["t",246,62,"Q",""],["t",76,142,"R","e"],["t",246,142,"X",""],["t",52,92,"A","e"],["t",160,28,"B","m"],["t",270,92,"C",""],["t",160,176,"D","m"],["t",180,122,"K\u2082",""],["t",196,222,"K\u2081","m"]]},o:["401.5 ohm","404.5 ohm","403.5 ohm","402.5 ohm"],c:3,s:`Balance of the bridge: P/Q = R/X.
First balance with R = 400 \u03A9: P/Q = 400/X.
After P and Q are interchanged the ratio becomes Q/P, and the new balance (R = 405 \u03A9) gives Q/P = 405/X.
Multiply the two equations: (P/Q)(Q/P) = 1 = (400 \xD7 405)/X\xB2.
X\xB2 = 400 \xD7 405 = 162000, so X = \u221A162000 \u2248 402.5 \u03A9.
Note: X is the geometric mean of the two balancing values, a useful way to remove the unknown ratio P/Q.
So X \u2248 402.5 \u03A9, option (d).`},{pn:295,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 11 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ckir",st:"Metre bridge",q:"In the experimental set up of metre bridge shown in the figure, the null point is obtained at a distance of 40 cm from A. If a 10 \u03A9 resistor is connected in series with R\u2081, the null point shifts by 10 cm. The resistance that should be connected in parallel with (R\u2081 + 10) \u03A9 such that the null point shifts back to its initial position is",fig:{w:340,h:235,alt:"Metre bridge with R1 in the left gap and R2 in the right gap, galvanometer to the jockey on wire AB (null point 40 cm from A), cell and key below",d:[["p","M40 40 L85 40 L88.3 35 L95 45 L101.7 35 L108.3 45 L115 35 L121.7 45 L125 40 L170 40"],["t",105,28,"R\u2081","m"],["p","M170 40 L215 40 L218.3 35 L225 45 L231.7 35 L238.3 45 L245 35 L251.7 45 L255 40 L300 40"],["t",235,28,"R\u2082","m"],["c",170,40,2.5,"F"],["l",170,40,170,58],["l",170,58,144,58],["l",144,58,144,66.5],["c",144,78.5,12],["t",144,82.5,"G","ms"],["l",144,90.5,144,108],["l",144,98,144,110,"a"],["l",40,110,300,110],["c",40,110,2.5,"F"],["c",300,110,2.5,"F"],["t",36,102,"A","e"],["t",304,102,"B",""],["t",144,124,"","m"],["l",40,40,40,185],["l",300,40,300,185],["l",40,185,110,185],["l",110,174,110,196],["l",115,179,115,191,"B"],["l",115,185,230,185],["l",230,185,238,185],["c",244,185,5],["c",244,185,2.5,"F"],["l",250,185,300,185],["t",112,209,"E","m"],["t",244,209,"K","m"],["l",40,132,144,132,"b"],["l",144,132,300,132,"b"],["t",92,148,"40 cm","m"],["t",222,148,"60 cm","m"]]},o:["40 \u03A9","20 \u03A9","60 \u03A9","30 \u03A9"],c:2,s:`For a metre bridge, R\u2081/R\u2082 = l/(100 \u2212 l), where l is the null length from A.
Initially l = 40: R\u2081/R\u2082 = 40/60 = 2/3.
With 10 \u03A9 added to R\u2081 the null point moves by 10 cm towards B (larger left resistance), to 50 cm: (R\u2081 + 10)/R\u2082 = 50/50 = 1.
So R\u2082 = R\u2081 + 10. Put this in R\u2081/R\u2082 = 2/3: 3R\u2081 = 2(R\u2081 + 10), which gives R\u2081 = 20 \u03A9 and R\u2082 = 30 \u03A9.
To bring the null point back to 40 cm the left arm must again be 20 \u03A9. The left arm is now 30 \u03A9 (R\u2081 + 10); a resistance X in parallel must reduce it to 20 \u03A9: (30 \xD7 X)/(30 + X) = 20.
30X = 600 + 20X, so X = 60 \u03A9.
So the resistance is 60 \u03A9, option (c).`},{pn:296,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cpot",st:"Potentiometer",q:"An ideal battery of 4 V and resistance R are connected in series in the primary circuit of a potentiometer of length 1 m and resistance 5 \u03A9. The value of R, to give a potential difference of 5 mV across 10 cm of potentiometer wire is",o:["490 \u03A9","495 \u03A9","395 \u03A9","480 \u03A9"],c:2,s:`The potential gradient is the potential drop per unit length: 5 mV across 10 cm means 0.5 mV per cm, so across the whole 100 cm wire the drop is 50 mV.
This 50 mV is the drop across the wire of resistance 5 \u03A9, so the current in the primary circuit is I = 50 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3/5 = 10 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 A = 10 mA.
The same current flows through R and the wire in series from the ideal 4 V battery: 4 = I (R + 5).
R + 5 = 4/0.01 = 400 \u03A9.
R = 395 \u03A9, option (c).`},{pn:297,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ckir",st:"Metre bridge",q:"In a meter bridge, the wire of length 1 m has a non-uniform cross-section such that the variation dR/dl of its resistance R with length l is dR/dl \u221D 1/\u221Al. Two equal resistances are connected as shown in the figure. The galvanometer has zero deflection when the jockey is at point P. What is the length AP?",fig:{w:340,h:205,alt:"Metre bridge with two equal resistances R prime in the gaps, a cell on top, galvanometer to jockey P on wire AB; AP = l and PB = 1 minus l",d:[["p","M40 55 L85 55 L88.3 50 L95 60 L101.7 50 L108.3 60 L115 50 L121.7 60 L125 55 L170 55"],["t",105,43,"R\u2032","m"],["p","M170 55 L215 55 L218.3 50 L225 60 L231.7 50 L238.3 60 L245 50 L251.7 60 L255 55 L300 55"],["t",235,43,"R\u2032","m"],["c",170,55,2.5,"F"],["l",170,55,170,73],["l",170,73,105,73],["l",105,73,105,81.5],["c",105,93.5,12],["t",105,97.5,"G","ms"],["l",105,105.5,105,123],["l",105,113,105,125,"a"],["l",40,125,300,125],["c",40,125,2.5,"F"],["c",300,125,2.5,"F"],["t",36,117,"A","e"],["t",304,117,"B",""],["t",105,139,"P","m"],["l",40,20,165,20],["l",165,9,165,31],["l",170,14,170,26,"B"],["l",170,20,300,20],["l",40,20,40,125],["l",300,20,300,125],["l",40,55,40,55],["c",40,55,2.5,"F"],["c",300,55,2.5,"F"],["l",40,147,105,147,"b"],["l",105,147,300,147,"b"],["t",72.5,163,"l","m"],["t",202.5,163,"(1 \u2212 l)","m"]]},o:["0.35 m","0.2 m","0.25 m","0.3 m"],c:2,s:`The bridge is balanced with equal resistances in the two gaps, so the resistance of the wire part AP must equal the resistance of the part PB.
The resistance of a length l from A is the sum (integral) of dR = k dl/\u221Al: R(l) = 2k\u221Al.
So R_AP = 2k\u221Al, and R_PB = R(1) \u2212 R(l) = 2k(1 \u2212 \u221Al), with the total wire length 1 m.
Balance: R_AP = R_PB, so \u221Al = 1 \u2212 \u221Al.
2\u221Al = 1, so \u221Al = 1/2 and l = 1/4 = 0.25 m.
So AP = 0.25 m, option (c).`},{pn:298,y:"JEE Main 2018",tp:"ckir",st:"Metre bridge",q:"On interchanging the resistances, the balance point of meter bridge shifts to the left by 10 cm. The resistance of their series combinations is 1 k\u03A9. How much was the resistance on the left slot before the interchange?",o:["990 \u03A9","505 \u03A9","550 \u03A9","910 \u03A9"],c:2,s:`Let the balance point before interchange be l cm from the left end. Then (left resistance)/(right resistance) = l/(100 \u2212 l).
When the two resistances are interchanged, the ratio becomes inverted, so the new balance point is at (100 \u2212 l) cm.
It has shifted left by 10 cm: (100 \u2212 l) = l \u2212 10, so 2l = 110 and l = 55 cm.
So left/right = 55/45 = 11/9.
The sum is 1000 \u03A9: left = 1000 \xD7 11/(11 + 9) = 550 \u03A9 (and right = 450 \u03A9).
The left resistance before the interchange was 550 \u03A9, option (c).`},{pn:299,y:"JEE Main 2018 \xB7 Online",tp:"ckir",st:"Metre bridge",q:"In a meter bridge, as shown in the figure, it is given that resistance Y = 12.5 \u03A9 and that the balance is obtained at a distance 39.5 cm from end A (by Jockey J). After interchanging the resistances X and Y, a new balance point is found at a distance l\u2082 from end A. What are the values of X and l\u2082?",fig:{w:340,h:235,alt:"Metre bridge: X in the left gap and Y in the right gap, galvanometer to jockey J on the wire AC, balance at l1 from A, with a cell and key",d:[["p","M40 40 L85 40 L88.3 35 L95 45 L101.7 35 L108.3 45 L115 35 L121.7 45 L125 40 L170 40"],["t",105,28,"X","m"],["p","M170 40 L215 40 L218.3 35 L225 45 L231.7 35 L238.3 45 L245 35 L251.7 45 L255 40 L300 40"],["t",235,28,"Y","m"],["c",170,40,2.5,"F"],["l",170,40,170,58],["l",170,58,143,58],["l",143,58,143,66.5],["c",143,78.5,12],["t",143,82.5,"G","ms"],["l",143,90.5,143,108],["l",143,98,143,110,"a"],["l",40,110,300,110],["c",40,110,2.5,"F"],["c",300,110,2.5,"F"],["t",36,102,"A","e"],["t",304,102,"C",""],["t",143,124,"J","m"],["l",40,40,40,185],["l",300,40,300,185],["l",40,185,110,185],["l",110,174,110,196],["l",115,179,115,191,"B"],["l",115,185,230,185],["l",230,185,238,185],["c",244,185,5],["c",244,185,2.5,"F"],["l",250,185,300,185],["t",112,209,"E","m"],["t",244,209,"K","m"],["l",40,132,143,132,"b"],["l",143,132,300,132,"b"],["t",91.5,148,"l\u2081","m"],["t",221.5,148,"100 \u2212 l\u2081","m"]]},o:["19.15 \u03A9 and 60.5 cm","8.16 \u03A9 and 60.5 cm","8.16 \u03A9 and 39.5 cm","19.15 \u03A9 and 39.5 cm"],c:1,s:`Metre bridge condition: X/Y = l\u2081/(100 \u2212 l\u2081), with X in the left gap (next to A).
X/12.5 = 39.5/60.5, so X = 12.5 \xD7 39.5/60.5 \u2248 8.16 \u03A9.
After interchanging, Y is in the left gap: Y/X = l\u2082/(100 \u2212 l\u2082).
Since Y/X = 60.5/39.5 (the inverse of before), l\u2082/(100 \u2212 l\u2082) = 60.5/39.5, which gives l\u2082 = 60.5 cm.
Note: interchanging the resistances always moves the balance point to (100 \u2212 l\u2081), here 100 \u2212 39.5 = 60.5 cm.
So X \u2248 8.16 \u03A9 and l\u2082 = 60.5 cm, option (b).`},{pn:300,y:"JEE Main 2017",tp:"ckir",st:"Wheatstone bridge",q:"Which of the following statement is false ?",o:["Wheatstone bridge is the most sensitive when all the four resistances are of the same order of magnitude.","In a balanced Wheatstone bridge if the cell and the galvanometer are exchanged, the null point is disturbed.","A rheostat can be used as a potential divider.","Kirchhoff's second law represents energy conservation."],c:1,s:`Check each statement.
(a) A bridge is most sensitive when all four arms are of the same order, so a small change in one arm gives a noticeable galvanometer current. True.
(b) In a balanced bridge the cell arm and the galvanometer arm are "conjugate" arms: the balance condition P/Q = R/S is the same after they are exchanged, so the null point stays and is not disturbed. This statement is false.
(c) A rheostat connected at its two ends across a source and tapped at a slider works as a potential divider. True.
(d) Kirchhoff's loop law (sum of potential changes around a loop is zero) is the conservation of energy. True.
The false statement is (b), option (b).`},{pn:301,y:"JEE Main 2017 \xB7 Online",tp:"cpot",st:"Potentiometer",q:"A potentiometer PQ is set up to compare two resistances as shown in the figure. The ammeter A in the circuit reads 1.0 A when two way key K\u2083 is open. The balance point is at a length l\u2081 cm from P when two way key K\u2083 is plugged in between 2 and 1, while the balance points is at a length l\u2082 cm from P when key K\u2083 is plugged in between 3 and 1. The ratio of two resistances R\u2081/R\u2082, is found to be",fig:{w:360,h:295,alt:"Potentiometer PQ comparing R1 and R2: primary loop E1, Rh1, K1 across P and Q; secondary loop with ammeter A, cell E2, rheostat Rh2 and key K2 sending current through R1 and R2 in series; a two-way key joins terminal 1 (to the galvanometer and jockey) to terminal 2 (between R1 and R2) or terminal 3 (right end of R2); P is joined to the left end of R1",d:[["l",40,30,30,30],["c",55,30,10],["t",55,34,"A","ms"],["l",65,30,90,30],["l",90,19,90,41],["l",95,24,95,36,"B"],["l",95,30,130,30],["p","M130 30 L147 30 L150 25 L156 35 L162 25 L168 35 L174 25 L180 35 L183 30 L200 30"],["l",150,38,170,22,"a"],["l",200,30,225,30],["c",233,30,4],["l",237,30,310,30],["t",92,16,"E\u2082","m"],["t",165,16,"Rh\u2082","m"],["t",250,16,"K\u2082","m"],["l",30,30,30,75],["l",310,30,310,75],["l",30,75,40,75],["p","M40 75 L75 75 L78.3 70 L85 80 L91.7 70 L98.3 80 L105 70 L111.7 80 L115 75 L150 75"],["c",150,75,2.5,"F"],["p","M150 75 L210 75 L213.3 70 L220 80 L226.7 70 L233.3 80 L240 70 L246.7 80 L250 75 L310 75"],["t",95,64,"R\u2081","m"],["t",230,64,"R\u2082","m"],["l",150,75,150,105],["c",150,105,2.5,"F"],["l",310,75,310,105],["l",310,105,235,105],["c",235,105,2.5,"F"],["c",190,125,2.5,"F"],["l",150,105,190,125,"d"],["l",235,105,190,125,"d"],["t",150,98,"2","e"],["t",240,98,"3",""],["t",194,140,"1",""],["l",190,125,190,150],["c",190,165,12],["t",190,169,"G","ms"],["l",190,177,190,198],["l",190,188,190,214,"a"],["l",40,215,330,215],["t",32,222,"P","e"],["t",338,222,"Q",""],["l",40,215,40,260],["l",40,260,100,260],["l",100,249,100,271],["l",105,254,105,266,"B"],["l",105,260,150,260],["p","M150 260 L162 260 L165 255 L171 265 L177 255 L183 265 L189 255 L195 265 L198 260 L210 260"],["l",162,268,182,252,"a"],["l",210,260,260,260],["c",266,260,5],["c",266,260,2.5,"F"],["l",272,260,330,260],["l",330,260,330,215],["t",100,280,"E\u2081","m"],["t",180,284,"Rh\u2081","m"],["t",266,284,"K\u2081","m"],["l",40,75,40,215]]},o:["l\u2082/(l\u2082 \u2212 l\u2081)","l\u2081/(l\u2082 \u2212 l\u2081)","l\u2081/(l\u2081 + l\u2082)","l\u2081/(l\u2081 \u2212 l\u2082)"],c:1,s:`The same current I = 1 A flows through R\u2081 and R\u2082 in series, so the voltage across R\u2081 is IR\u2081 and the voltage across R\u2081 + R\u2082 is I(R\u2081 + R\u2082).
Plug between 2 and 1: the potentiometer compares the voltage across R\u2081 (from the P side to terminal 2). The balance length is proportional to this voltage: l\u2081 \u221D I R\u2081.
Plug between 3 and 1: it compares the voltage across both resistors: l\u2082 \u221D I (R\u2081 + R\u2082).
Divide: l\u2081/l\u2082 = R\u2081/(R\u2081 + R\u2082).
Cross-multiply: l\u2081R\u2081 + l\u2081R\u2082 = l\u2082R\u2081, so R\u2081(l\u2082 \u2212 l\u2081) = l\u2081R\u2082.
R\u2081/R\u2082 = l\u2081/(l\u2082 \u2212 l\u2081), option (b).`},{pn:302,y:"JEE Main 2017 \xB7 Online",tp:"ckir",st:"Metre bridge",q:"In meter bridge experiment resistances are connected as shown in the figure. Initially resistance P = 4 \u03A9 and the neutral point N is at 60 cm from A. Now an unknown resistance R is connected in series to P and the new position of the neutral point is at 80 cm from A. The value of unknown resistance R is",fig:{w:340,h:225,alt:"Metre bridge: P in the left gap and Q in the right gap, galvanometer to the jockey at N on wire AB, cell E, rheostat Rh and key K below",d:[["l",40,40,70,40],["r",70,30,40,20],["t",90,44,"P","ms"],["l",110,40,170,40],["c",170,40,2.5,"F"],["l",170,40,170,40],["l",170,40,230,40],["r",230,30,40,20],["t",250,44,"Q","ms"],["l",270,40,300,40],["l",170,40,170,56],["l",170,56,196,56],["l",196,56,196,64],["c",196,76,12],["t",196,80,"G","ms"],["l",196,88,196,96],["l",196,90,196,109,"a"],["l",40,40,40,185],["l",300,40,300,185],["l",40,110,300,110],["c",40,110,2.5,"F"],["c",300,110,2.5,"F"],["t",34,102,"A","e"],["t",306,102,"B",""],["t",196,126,"N","m"],["l",40,185,110,185],["l",110,174,110,196],["l",115,179,115,191,"B"],["l",115,185,150,185],["p","M150 185 L172 185 L175 180 L181 190 L187 180 L193 190 L199 180 L205 190 L208 185 L230 185"],["l",172,193,192,177,"a"],["l",230,185,238,185],["c",244,185,5],["c",244,185,2.5,"F"],["l",250,185,300,185],["t",112,209,"E","m"],["t",190,211,"Rh","m"],["t",244,209,"K","m"]]},o:["(20)/(3) \u03A9","(33)/(5) \u03A9","6 \u03A9","7 \u03A9"],c:0,s:`P is in the left gap (next to A) and Q in the right gap. Metre bridge condition: P/Q = l/(100 \u2212 l).
Initially l = 60: 4/Q = 60/40 = 3/2, so Q = 8/3 \u03A9.
Now R is in series with P, so the left arm is 4 + R and the null point is at 80 cm: (4 + R)/Q = 80/20 = 4.
4 + R = 4 \xD7 8/3 = 32/3.
R = 32/3 \u2212 4 = 20/3 \u03A9.
So R = 20/3 \u03A9 \u2248 6.67 \u03A9, option (a).`},{pn:303,y:"IIT-JEE 2011",tp:"ckir",st:"Metre bridge",q:"A meter bridge is set-up as shown, to determine an unknown resistance X using a standard 10 ohm resistor. The galvanometer shows null point when tapping-key is at 52 cm mark. The end-corrections are 1 cm and 2 cm respectively for the ends A and B. The determined value of X is",fig:{w:340,h:185,alt:"Metre bridge: unknown X in the left gap, standard 10 ohm in the right gap, cell across the ends, galvanometer to the tapping key at the 52 cm mark of wire AB",d:[["l",40,70,70,70],["p","M40 70 L77 70 L80 65 L86 75 L92 65 L98 75 L104 65 L110 75 L113 70 L150 70"],["l",150,70,180,70],["c",180,70,2.5,"F"],["p","M180 70 L222 70 L225 65 L231 75 L237 65 L243 75 L249 65 L255 75 L258 70 L300 70"],["l",40,70,40,130],["l",300,70,300,130],["l",40,130,300,130],["c",40,130,2.5,"F"],["c",300,130,2.5,"F"],["l",40,70,40,30],["l",40,30,165,30],["l",165,19,165,41],["l",170,24,170,36,"B"],["l",170,30,300,30],["l",300,30,300,70],["l",180,70,180,92],["c",180,104,12],["t",180,108,"G","ms"],["l",180,116,180,121],["l",180,114,180,130,"a"],["t",34,122,"A","e"],["t",306,122,"B",""],["t",95,58,"X","m"],["t",240,58,"10 \u03A9","m"],["l",40,150,180,150,"b"],["t",110,166,"52 cm","m"]]},o:["10.2 ohm","10.6 ohm","10.8 ohm","11.1 ohm"],c:1,s:`The end corrections mean that the effective length of wire on each side is longer than the scale reading by the end correction at that end.
Left side (towards A): l\u2081 = 52 + 1 = 53 cm.
Right side (towards B): l\u2082 = (100 \u2212 52) + 2 = 50 cm.
Bridge balance: X/10 = l\u2081/l\u2082 = 53/50.
X = 10 \xD7 53/50 = 10.6 \u03A9.
So X = 10.6 \u03A9, option (b).`},{pn:304,y:"AIEEE 2008",tp:"ckir",st:"Metre bridge",q:"Shown in the figure below is a meter-bridge set up with null deflection in the galvanometer. The value of the unknown resistance R is",fig:{w:340,h:235,alt:"Metre bridge: 55 ohm in the left gap and unknown R in the right gap, balance 20 cm from the left end, cell and key below",d:[["p","M40 40 L85 40 L88.3 35 L95 45 L101.7 35 L108.3 45 L115 35 L121.7 45 L125 40 L170 40"],["t",105,28,"55 \u03A9","m"],["p","M170 40 L215 40 L218.3 35 L225 45 L231.7 35 L238.3 45 L245 35 L251.7 45 L255 40 L300 40"],["t",235,28,"R","m"],["c",170,40,2.5,"F"],["l",170,40,170,58],["l",170,58,92,58],["l",92,58,92,66.5],["c",92,78.5,12],["t",92,82.5,"G","ms"],["l",92,90.5,92,108],["l",92,98,92,110,"a"],["l",40,110,300,110],["c",40,110,2.5,"F"],["c",300,110,2.5,"F"],["t",36,102,"A","e"],["t",304,102,"B",""],["t",92,124,"","m"],["l",40,40,40,185],["l",300,40,300,185],["l",40,185,110,185],["l",110,174,110,196],["l",115,179,115,191,"B"],["l",115,185,230,185],["l",230,185,238,185],["c",244,185,5],["c",244,185,2.5,"F"],["l",250,185,300,185],["t",112,209,"E","m"],["t",244,209,"K","m"],["l",40,132,92,132,"b"],["l",92,132,300,132,"b"],["t",66,148,"20 cm","m"],["t",196,148,"80 cm","m"]]},o:["55 \u03A9","13.75 \u03A9","220 \u03A9","110 \u03A9"],c:2,s:`The null point is 20 cm from the left end, so the right part of the wire is 100 \u2212 20 = 80 cm.
Metre bridge condition: (left resistance)/(right resistance) = (left length)/(right length).
55/R = 20/80 = 1/4.
R = 55 \xD7 4 = 220 \u03A9.
So R = 220 \u03A9, option (c).`},{pn:305,y:"IIT-JEE 2007",tp:"ckir",st:"Metre bridge",q:"A resistance of 2 \u03A9 is connected across one gap of a metre-bridge (the length of the wire is 100 cm) and an unknown resistance, greater than 2 \u03A9, is connected across the other gap. When these resistances are interchanged, the balance point shifts by 20 cm. Neglecting any corrections, the unknown resistance is",o:["3 \u03A9","4 \u03A9","5 \u03A9","6 \u03A9"],c:0,s:`Let the unknown be R > 2 \u03A9, with the 2 \u03A9 on the left. The balance length from the left end is l: 2/R = l/(100 \u2212 l).
Since R > 2, the left length is shorter: l < 50.
After the interchange the ratio is inverted, so the new balance point is at (100 \u2212 l). The shift is (100 \u2212 l) \u2212 l = 20, so l = 40 cm.
Put back: 2/R = 40/60 = 2/3.
R = 3 \u03A9.
So the unknown resistance is 3 \u03A9, option (a).`},{pn:306,y:"AIEEE 2006",tp:"ckir",st:"Wheatstone bridge",q:"In a Wheatstone's bridge, three resistances P, Q and R connected in the three arms and the fourth arm is formed by two resistances S\u2081 and S\u2082 connected in parallel. The condition for bridge to be balanced will be",o:["P/Q = R/(S\u2081 + S\u2082)","P/Q = 2R/(S\u2081 + S\u2082)","P/Q = R(S\u2081 + S\u2082)/(S\u2081S\u2082)","P/Q = R(S\u2081 + S\u2082)/(2S\u2081S\u2082)"],c:2,s:`Balance of a Wheatstone bridge needs P/Q = R/S, where S is the resistance of the fourth arm.
The fourth arm has S\u2081 and S\u2082 in parallel: S = S\u2081S\u2082/(S\u2081 + S\u2082).
Put this in: P/Q = R/S = R(S\u2081 + S\u2082)/(S\u2081S\u2082).
So the condition is P/Q = R(S\u2081 + S\u2082)/(S\u2081S\u2082), option (c).`},{pn:307,y:"AIEEE 2006",tp:"ccmb",st:"Balanced networks and symmetry",q:"The current I drawn from the 5 volt source will be",fig:{w:350,h:200,alt:"Five resistors and a 5 V source: from the positive terminal, 5 ohm then 10 ohm then 20 ohm in series along the middle row, a 10 ohm resistor from the first node over the top to the node between 10 and 20 ohm, and a 10 ohm resistor from the node between 5 and 10 ohm along the bottom to the far end",d:[["l",40,80,40,30],["p","M40 30 L115 30 L118.3 25 L125 35 L131.7 25 L138.3 35 L145 25 L151.7 35 L155 30 L230 30"],["l",230,30,230,80],["l",40,80,50,80],["p","M40 80 L67 80 L70 75 L76 85 L82 75 L88 85 L94 75 L100 85 L103 80 L130 80"],["c",130,80,2.5,"F"],["p","M130 80 L162 80 L165 75 L171 85 L177 75 L183 85 L189 75 L195 85 L198 80 L230 80"],["c",230,80,2.5,"F"],["p","M230 80 L257 80 L260 75 L266 85 L272 75 L278 85 L284 75 L290 85 L293 80 L320 80"],["l",320,80,320,170],["l",130,80,130,130],["p","M130 130 L205 130 L208.3 125 L215 135 L221.7 125 L228.3 135 L235 125 L241.7 135 L245 130 L320 130"],["c",320,130,2.5,"F"],["l",40,80,40,170],["l",40,115,40,98,"a"],["l",40,170,150,170],["l",150,159,150,181],["l",155,164,155,176,"B"],["l",155,170,320,170],["t",135,18,"10 \u03A9","m"],["t",85,70,"5 \u03A9","m"],["t",180,70,"10 \u03A9","m"],["t",275,70,"20 \u03A9","m"],["t",225,122,"10 \u03A9","m"],["t",28,110,"I","e"],["t",152,192,"5 V","m"],["t",143,164,"+","e"],["t",160,164,"\u2212",""]]},o:["0.17 A","0.33 A","0.5 A","0.67 A"],c:2,s:`Name the nodes: N\u2081 (positive end), N\u2082 (between 5 \u03A9 and 10 \u03A9), N\u2083 (between the middle 10 \u03A9 and 20 \u03A9), N\u2084 (negative end).
The resistors are: N\u2081N\u2082 = 5 \u03A9, N\u2082N\u2083 = 10 \u03A9, N\u2083N\u2084 = 20 \u03A9, N\u2081N\u2083 = 10 \u03A9 (top), N\u2082N\u2084 = 10 \u03A9 (bottom).
Check the bridge balance: N\u2081N\u2082 : N\u2082N\u2084 = 5 : 10 = 1 : 2 and N\u2081N\u2083 : N\u2083N\u2084 = 10 : 20 = 1 : 2. The ratios are equal, so N\u2082 and N\u2083 are at the same potential.
Note: no current flows through the 10 \u03A9 resistor between N\u2082 and N\u2083, so it can be removed.
Upper path N\u2081 \u2192 N\u2083 \u2192 N\u2084: 10 + 20 = 30 \u03A9. Lower path N\u2081 \u2192 N\u2082 \u2192 N\u2084: 5 + 10 = 15 \u03A9. In parallel: (30 \xD7 15)/(30 + 15) = 10 \u03A9.
I = 5/10 = 0.5 A.
So I = 0.5 A, option (c).`},{pn:308,y:"AIEEE 2005",tp:"cpot",st:"Potentiometer",q:"In a potentiometer experiment the balancing point with a cell is at length 240 cm. On shunting the cell with a resistance of 2 \u03A9, the balancing length becomes 120 cm. The internal resistance of the cell is",o:["4 \u03A9","2 \u03A9","1 \u03A9","0.5 \u03A9"],c:1,s:`With the cell open, no current is drawn, so the balance length gives its emf: E \u221D l\u2081 = 240 cm.
When the cell is shunted by R = 2 \u03A9, the balance length gives its terminal voltage: V \u221D l\u2082 = 120 cm.
For a cell with internal resistance r: V = E R/(R + r), so E/V = (R + r)/R = l\u2081/l\u2082.
So (2 + r)/2 = 240/120 = 2, giving 2 + r = 4.
r = 2 \u03A9.
So the internal resistance is 2 \u03A9, option (b).`},{pn:309,y:"AIEEE 2004",tp:"ckir",st:"Metre bridge",q:"In a metre bridge experiment null point is obtained at 20 cm from one end of the wire when resistance X is balanced against another resistance Y. If X < Y, then where will be the new position of the null point from the same end, if one decides to balance a resistance of 4X against Y?",o:["50 cm","80 cm","40 cm","70 cm"],c:0,s:`Because X < Y the null point is near the X end: X/Y = 20/80 = 1/4, so Y = 4X.
Now balance 4X against Y: the ratio is 4X/Y = 4X/(4X) = 1.
At balance, (left resistance)/(right resistance) = l/(100 \u2212 l) = 1, so l = 50 cm.
Note: equal resistances in the two gaps always balance at the middle of the wire.
The null point is at 50 cm, option (a).`},{pn:310,y:"IIT-JEE 2004",tp:"ckir",st:"Wheatstone bridge",q:"Shown in figure is a post office box. In order to calculate the value of external resistance, it should be connected between",fig:{w:330,h:195,alt:"Post office box drawn as an S-shaped strip of plug-key rows with terminals C, B and A on the top row, D at the left of the bottom row, and terminals B-prime and C-prime below the bottom row",d:[["p","M40 20 H290 V45 H65 V70 H290 V145 H40 V120 H265 V95 H40 Z"],["l",60,20,60,45],["l",60,70,60,95],["l",60,120,60,145],["l",80,20,80,45],["l",80,70,80,95],["l",80,120,80,145],["l",100,20,100,45],["l",100,70,100,95],["l",100,120,100,145],["l",120,20,120,45],["l",120,70,120,95],["l",120,120,120,145],["l",140,20,140,45],["l",140,70,140,95],["l",140,120,140,145],["l",160,20,160,45],["l",160,70,160,95],["l",160,120,160,145],["l",180,20,180,45],["l",180,70,180,95],["l",180,120,180,145],["l",200,20,200,45],["l",200,70,200,95],["l",200,120,200,145],["l",220,20,220,45],["l",220,70,220,95],["l",220,120,220,145],["l",240,20,240,45],["l",240,70,240,95],["l",240,120,240,145],["l",260,20,260,45],["l",260,70,260,95],["l",260,120,260,145],["c",65,20,2.5,"F"],["c",105,20,2.5,"F"],["c",280,20,2.5,"F"],["t",65,12,"C","m"],["t",105,12,"B","m"],["t",280,12,"A","m"],["t",26,112,"D","m"],["c",50,150,2.5,"F"],["c",220,150,2.5,"F"],["l",50,150,50,162],["l",220,150,220,162],["t",50,176,"C\u2032","m"],["t",220,176,"B\u2032","m"]]},o:["B\u2032 and C\u2032","A and D","C and D","B and D"],c:1,s:`A post office box is a Wheatstone bridge: the top row gives the two ratio arms AB (P) and BC (Q), the bottom row (between C and D) is the adjustable rheostat arm R, and the remaining arm, between A and D, is the gap for the unknown resistance X.
The cell is connected across A and C, and the galvanometer across B and D, so the four arms are AB, BC, CD and DA.
At balance, P/Q = X/R, so X is found from the plugs pulled out in R.
Therefore the external (unknown) resistance goes between A and D.
So the answer is A and D, option (b).`},{pn:311,y:"AIEEE 2003",tp:"cpot",st:"Potentiometer",q:"The length of a wire of a potentiometer is 100 cm, and the e.m.f. of its standard cell is E volt. It is employed to measure the e.m.f. of a battery whose internal resistance is 0.5 \u03A9. If the balance point is obtained at l = 30 cm from the positive end, the e.m.f. of the battery is",o:["30E/100.5","30E/(100 \u2212 0.5)","30E/100 \u2212 0.5i, where i is the current in the potentiometer wire","30E/100"],c:2,s:`At balance no current flows through the battery being measured, so its emf equals the potential drop along 30 cm of the wire.
If the wire carried exactly E over 100 cm, the drop over 30 cm would be 30E/100.
The question is flawed: it does not say how the 0.5 \u03A9 enters. Only option (c) contains a correction term in the wire current i, namely 30E/100 \u2212 0.5i.
Note: the printed key is (c), and (c) is the only option with the 0.5i correction term, so it is taken as the intended answer.
So the answer is option (c).`},{pn:312,y:"IIT-JEE 2003",tp:"ckir",st:"Metre bridge",q:"In the shown arrangement of the experiment of the meter bridge if AC corresponding to null deflection of galvanometer is x, what would be its value if the radius of the wire AB is doubled?",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"Metre bridge: a cell at the top, R1 and R2 in the two gaps, galvanometer from between them to the jockey C on wire AB; AC = x",d:[["l",40,30,165,30],["l",165,19,165,41],["l",170,24,170,36,"B"],["l",170,30,300,30],["l",300,30,300,150],["l",40,30,40,150],["p","M40 70 L77 70 L80 65 L86 75 L92 65 L98 75 L104 65 L110 75 L113 70 L150 70"],["l",150,70,170,70],["c",170,70,2.5,"F"],["p","M170 70 L217 70 L220 65 L226 75 L232 65 L238 75 L244 65 L250 75 L253 70 L300 70"],["c",40,70,2.5,"F"],["c",300,70,2.5,"F"],["l",40,150,300,150],["c",40,150,2.5,"F"],["c",300,150,2.5,"F"],["l",170,70,170,100],["c",170,112,12],["t",170,116,"G","ms"],["l",170,124,170,136],["l",170,128,170,150,"a"],["t",95,58,"R\u2081","m"],["t",240,58,"R\u2082","m"],["t",34,142,"A","e"],["t",306,142,"B",""],["t",170,166,"C","m"],["l",44,172,166,172,"b"],["t",105,188,"x","m"]]},o:["x","x/4","4x","2x"],c:0,s:`At balance the bridge condition is R\u2081/R\u2082 = (resistance of AC)/(resistance of CB).
For a uniform wire, the resistance of a part is R = \u03C1l/A, where l is its length and A its cross-section area.
Doubling the radius makes the area 4 times, so the resistance of every part of the wire becomes 1/4 of its old value. The ratio of the resistances of AC and CB does not change, because both parts are divided by the same factor 4.
The ratio R\u2081/R\u2082 is fixed by the resistors outside, so the balance length is still given by the same ratio of lengths.
So AC stays x, option (a).`},{pn:313,y:"IIT-JEE 2002",tp:"ccmb",st:"Equivalent resistance of networks",q:"The effective resistance between points P and Q of the electrical circuit shown in the figure is",o:["2Rr/(R+r)","8R(R+r)/(3R+r)","2r + 4R","5R/2 + 2r"],c:0,s:`Note: the printed key says (a); working with the figure as printed gives R(eq) = Rr(10R + 3r)/(5R\xB2 + 5Rr + r\xB2), which matches no option, so the question should be checked against the original paper.
The upper and lower halves are identical, so the two nodes on the top and bottom wires (call T) are at the same potential and can be treated together.
Between P and T the two 2R resistors are in parallel: R. Between T and the middle node M the two vertical 2R resistors are in parallel: R. Between T and Q the two 4R paths are in parallel: 2R.
P to M and M to Q are the two resistors r. Write the node equations with P at V and Q at 0: T = 2(V + M)/5 and M(2R + r) = RV + rT.
Solving gives M = V(5R + 2r)/(10R + 3r) and T = 2V(3R + r)/(10R + 3r).
The current from P is I = (V \u2212 T)/R + (V \u2212 M)/r, and R(eq) = V/I = Rr(10R + 3r)/(5R\xB2 + 5Rr + r\xB2).
The printed key (a) is kept in the option list.`,fig:{w:420,h:250,alt:"Network between P and Q: top row 2R then 2R; middle row r then r; bottom row 2R then 2R; a vertical 2R at the centre above and below the middle row, and a vertical 2R at the right end above and below Q",d:[["c",30,125,3.2],["t",30,113,"P","m"],["l",33,125,60,125],["l",60,50,60,200],["l",60,50,70,50],["l",70,50,112,50],["l",148,50,190,50],["p","M112 50L118 55L124 45L130 55L136 45L142 55L148 50"],["l",200,50,252,50],["l",288,50,340,50],["p","M252 50L258 55L264 45L270 55L276 45L282 55L288 50"],["l",340,50,340,50],["t",130,40,"2R","m"],["t",270,40,"2R","m"],["l",60,200,70,200],["l",70,200,112,200],["l",148,200,190,200],["p","M112 200L118 205L124 195L130 205L136 195L142 205L148 200"],["l",200,200,252,200],["l",288,200,340,200],["p","M252 200L258 205L264 195L270 205L276 195L282 205L288 200"],["l",340,200,340,200],["t",130,218,"2R","m"],["t",270,218,"2R","m"],["l",190,50,200,50],["l",190,200,200,200],["l",60,125,112,125],["l",148,125,200,125],["p","M112 125L118 130L124 120L130 130L136 120L142 130L148 125"],["l",200,125,252,125],["l",288,125,340,125],["p","M252 125L258 130L264 120L270 130L276 120L282 130L288 125"],["t",130,115,"r","m"],["t",270,115,"r","m"],["l",200,50,200,69.5],["l",200,105.5,200,125],["p","M200 69.5L195 75.5L205 81.5L195 87.5L205 93.5L195 99.5L200 105.5"],["l",200,125,200,144.5],["l",200,180.5,200,200],["p","M200 144.5L195 150.5L205 156.5L195 162.5L205 168.5L195 174.5L200 180.5"],["l",340,50,340,69.5],["l",340,105.5,340,125],["p","M340 69.5L335 75.5L345 81.5L335 87.5L345 93.5L335 99.5L340 105.5"],["l",340,125,340,144.5],["l",340,180.5,340,200],["p","M340 144.5L335 150.5L345 156.5L335 162.5L345 168.5L335 174.5L340 180.5"],["c",200,50,2.6,"F"],["c",200,125,2.6,"F"],["c",200,200,2.6,"F"],["c",60,125,2.6,"F"],["c",340,125,2.6,"F"],["l",340,125,372,125],["c",375,125,3.2],["t",386,117,"Q","m"],["t",208,90,"2R"],["t",208,166,"2R"],["t",348,90,"2R"],["t",348,166,"2R"]]}},{pn:314,y:"IIT-JEE 2001",tp:"ckir",st:"Wheatstone bridge",q:"In the given circuit, it is observed that the current I is independent of the value of the resistance R\u2086. Then the resistance values must satisfy",o:["R\u2081R\u2082R\u2085 = R\u2083R\u2084R\u2086","1/R\u2085 + 1/R\u2086 = 1/(R\u2081 + R\u2082) + 1/(R\u2083 + R\u2084)","R\u2081R\u2084 = R\u2082R\u2083","R\u2081R\u2083 = R\u2082R\u2084 = R\u2085R\u2086"],c:2,s:`R\u2081, R\u2082 form one branch and R\u2083, R\u2084 the other; R\u2086 is the bridge arm joining the middle points of the two branches.
The current drawn from the battery does not depend on R\u2086 only if no current flows in R\u2086, whatever its value.
That happens when the two middle points are at the same potential, which is the balance condition of a Wheatstone bridge.
The ratio of the upper resistors must equal the ratio of the lower ones: R\u2081/R\u2082 = R\u2083/R\u2084.
Cross-multiplying, R\u2081R\u2084 = R\u2082R\u2083, which is option (c).`,fig:{w:340,h:220,alt:"A battery drives R5 in the top wire; R1 above R2 and R3 above R4 are two vertical branches between the top and bottom wires; R6 joins the middle point of R1, R2 to the middle point of R3, R4; the current I leaves the battery upwards",d:[["l",40,50,40,85],["l",40,85,40,98],["l",40,104,40,117],["l",30,98,50,98],["l",35,104,45,104,"B"],["l",40,117,40,180],["l",40,180,270,180],["l",40,50,60,50],["l",60,50,77,50],["l",113,50,130,50],["p","M77 50L83 55L89 45L95 55L101 45L107 55L113 50"],["l",130,50,270,50],["l",130,50,130,64.5],["l",130,100.5,130,115],["p","M130 64.5L125 70.5L135 76.5L125 82.5L135 88.5L125 94.5L130 100.5"],["l",130,115,130,129.5],["l",130,165.5,130,180],["p","M130 129.5L125 135.5L135 141.5L125 147.5L135 153.5L125 159.5L130 165.5"],["l",270,50,270,64.5],["l",270,100.5,270,115],["p","M270 64.5L265 70.5L275 76.5L265 82.5L275 88.5L265 94.5L270 100.5"],["l",270,115,270,129.5],["l",270,165.5,270,180],["p","M270 129.5L265 135.5L275 141.5L265 147.5L275 153.5L265 159.5L270 165.5"],["l",130,115,175,115],["l",225,115,270,115],["p","M175 115L183.3 120L191.7 110L200 120L208.3 110L216.7 120L225 115"],["c",130,50,2.6,"F"],["c",130,115,2.6,"F"],["c",270,115,2.6,"F"],["l",22,118,22,90,"a"],["t",14,104,"I","e"],["t",95,40,"R\u2085","m"],["t",120,85,"R\u2081","e"],["t",120,150,"R\u2082","e"],["t",280,85,"R\u2083"],["t",280,150,"R\u2084"],["t",200,106,"R\u2086","m"]]}},{pn:315,y:"IIT-JEE 1999",tp:"ckir",st:"Wheatstone bridge",q:"In the circuit P \u2260 R, the reading of the galvanometer is same with switch S open or closed. Then (one or more options correct)",o:["I_R = I_G","I_P = I_G","I_Q = I_G","I_Q = I_R"],c:0,s:`Closing S joins the top and bottom corners of the bridge. The galvanometer reading does not change only if there was no current in S, that is, the two corners are already at the same potential.
So the bridge is balanced: no current passes through S, whether it is open or closed.
Then the current in P goes on entirely into Q, so I_P = I_Q, and the current in R goes on entirely into G, so I_R = I_G.
Since P \u2260 R, the currents in the two sides are different, so I_P = I_G, I_Q = I_G and I_Q = I_R are not true.
Only I_R = I_G holds, option (a).`,fig:{w:330,h:250,alt:"A bridge: P from the top corner to the left corner, Q from the top corner to the right corner, R from the left corner to the bottom corner, galvanometer G from the bottom corner to the right corner; a switch S joins the top corner to the bottom corner; the battery V joins the left and right corners",d:[["l",65,105,99,79.5],["l",131,55.5,165,30],["p","M99 79.5L107.3 79.5L106.7 67.5L118 71.5L117.3 59.5L128.7 63.5L131 55.5"],["l",165,30,199,55.5],["l",231,79.5,265,105],["p","M199 55.5L201.3 63.5L212.7 59.5L212 71.5L223.3 67.5L222.7 79.5L231 79.5"],["l",65,105,99,130.5],["l",131,154.5,165,180],["p","M99 130.5L101.3 138.5L112.7 134.5L112 146.5L123.3 142.5L122.7 154.5L131 154.5"],["l",165,180,200,180],["c",210,180,10],["t",210,184,"G","m"],["l",220,180,265,180],["l",265,180,265,105],["l",165,30,165,88],["l",165,88,178,112],["c",165,120,3.2],["l",165,124,165,180],["c",65,105,2.6,"F"],["c",265,105,2.6,"F"],["c",165,180,2.6,"F"],["l",65,105,65,225],["l",65,225,150,225],["l",150,225,163,225],["l",169,225,182,225],["l",163,215,163,235],["l",169,220,169,230,"B"],["l",182,225,265,225],["l",265,225,265,105],["t",166,245,"V","m"],["t",100,55,"P","e"],["t",235,55,"Q"],["t",105,160,"R","e"],["t",180,82,"S"]]}},{pn:316,y:"JEE Advanced 2016",tp:"crc",st:"Charging and discharging of a capacitor",q:"In the circuit shown below, the key is pressed at time t = 0. Which of the following statement(s) is(are) true?",o:["The voltmeter displays \u22125 V as soon as the key is pressed, and displays +5 V after a long time","The voltmeter will display 0 V at time t = ln 2 seconds","The current in the ammeter becomes 1/e of the initial value after 1 second","The current in the ammeter becomes zero after a long time"],c:[0,1,2,3],s:`The 5 V cell has its + side towards the key, so the left corner is at 5 V and the right corner at 0 V.
At t = 0 an uncharged capacitor acts as a wire: the top corner is at 5 V (through 40 \u03BCF) and the bottom corner is at 0 V (through 20 \u03BCF). The voltmeter (+ at the bottom) reads V(bottom) \u2212 V(top) = \u22125 V.
There are two RC paths from the left corner to the right corner: 40 \u03BCF with 25 k\u03A9, and 50 k\u03A9 with 20 \u03BCF. Both have \u03C4 = 1 s, because 40 \u03BCF \xD7 25 k\u03A9 = 1 s and 50 k\u03A9 \xD7 20 \u03BCF = 1 s.
So both currents decay as e^(\u2212t), the total current becomes 1/e of its initial value after 1 s, and tends to zero after a long time. (c) and (d) are true.
The top corner is at 5e^(\u2212t) V and the bottom corner at 5 \u2212 5e^(\u2212t) V, so the reading is 5 \u2212 10e^(\u2212t) V. It is 0 at e^(\u2212t) = 1/2, i.e. t = ln 2 s, and +5 V after a long time. (a) and (b) are true.
All four statements are true: options (a), (b), (c) and (d).`,fig:{w:400,h:260,alt:"A bridge: 40 microfarad capacitor from the left corner to the top corner, 25 kilo-ohm resistor from the top corner to the right corner, 50 kilo-ohm resistor from the left corner to the bottom corner, 20 microfarad capacitor from the right corner to the bottom corner; a voltmeter between top and bottom corners with its plus at the bottom; an ammeter, key and 5 V cell connect the left and right corners",d:[["l",110,105,152.3,69.7],["l",157.7,65.3,200,30],["l",147.2,63.6,157.4,75.9],["l",152.6,59.1,162.8,71.4],["l",200,30,229.6,54.7],["l",260.4,80.3,290,105],["p","M229.6 54.7L231.6 62.8L243.1 59.4L241.8 71.3L253.3 67.9L252 79.9L260.4 80.3"],["l",110,105,139.6,129.7],["l",170.4,155.3,200,180],["p","M139.6 129.7L141.6 137.8L153.1 134.4L151.8 146.3L163.3 142.9L162 154.9L170.4 155.3"],["l",290,105,247.7,140.3],["l",242.3,144.7,200,180],["l",252.8,146.4,242.6,134.1],["l",247.4,150.9,237.2,138.6],["l",200,30,200,80],["c",200,92,12],["t",200,96,"V","m"],["l",200,104,200,180],["t",190,78,"\u2212","e"],["t",190,124,"+","e"],["c",110,105,2.6,"F"],["c",290,105,2.6,"F"],["l",110,105,60,105],["l",60,105,60,125],["c",60,137,12],["t",60,141,"A","m"],["l",60,149,60,225],["l",60,225,100,225],["c",104,225,3.2],["l",108,225,128,215],["l",128,225,205,225],["l",205,225,218,225],["l",224,225,237,225],["l",218,215,218,235],["l",224,220,224,230,"B"],["l",237,225,340,225],["l",340,225,340,105],["l",340,105,290,105],["t",140,52,"40 \u03BCF","e"],["t",262,52,"25 k\u03A9"],["t",125,150,"50 k\u03A9","e"],["t",262,160,"20 \u03BCF"],["t",100,246,"Key","m"],["t",221,246,"5 V","m"]]}},{pn:317,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 28 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ckir",st:"Wheatstone bridge",q:"The value of current I in the electrical circuit as given below, when potential at A is equal to the potential at B, will be ______ A.",n:2,s:`If A and B are at the same potential, no current flows in the 30 \u03A9 resistor, so the circuit is a balanced Wheatstone bridge.
Balance condition: 10/20 = R/40, which gives R = 20 \u03A9.
Top path: 10 + 20 = 30 \u03A9. Bottom path: R + 40 = 60 \u03A9. They are in parallel: (30 \xD7 60)/90 = 20 \u03A9.
The 40 V cell is connected across this 20 \u03A9, so I = 40/20 = 2 A.`,fig:{w:400,h:230,alt:"A bridge between a left and a right wire: 10 ohm and 20 ohm in the top row with node A between them, R and 40 ohm in the bottom row with node B between them, and a 30 ohm resistor joining A to B; a 40 V cell and a key close the outer loop; the current I enters on the left and leaves on the right",d:[["l",100,50,100,130],["l",300,50,300,130],["l",100,50,105,50],["l",100,50,132,50],["l",168,50,200,50],["p","M132 50L138 55L144 45L150 55L156 45L162 55L168 50"],["l",200,50,232,50],["l",268,50,300,50],["p","M232 50L238 55L244 45L250 55L256 45L262 55L268 50"],["l",100,130,132,130],["l",168,130,200,130],["p","M132 130L138 135L144 125L150 135L156 125L162 135L168 130"],["l",200,130,232,130],["l",268,130,300,130],["p","M232 130L238 135L244 125L250 135L256 125L262 135L268 130"],["l",200,50,200,72],["l",200,108,200,130],["p","M200 72L195 78L205 84L195 90L205 96L195 102L200 108"],["c",200,50,2.6,"F"],["c",200,130,2.6,"F"],["c",100,90,2.6,"F"],["c",300,90,2.6,"F"],["l",100,90,60,90],["l",60,90,60,190],["l",60,190,150,190],["l",150,190,163,190],["l",169,190,182,190],["l",163,180,163,200],["l",169,185,169,195,"B"],["l",182,190,210,190],["c",214,190,3.2],["l",218,190,340,190],["l",340,190,340,90],["l",340,90,300,90],["l",70,82,86,82,"a"],["t",66,72,"I","e"],["l",318,82,334,82,"a"],["t",340,72,"I"],["t",150,38,"10 \u03A9","m"],["t",250,38,"20 \u03A9","m"],["t",150,148,"R","m"],["t",250,148,"40 \u03A9","m"],["t",208,94,"30 \u03A9"],["t",200,28,"A","m"],["t",200,170,"B","m"],["t",166,212,"40 V","m"],["t",214,180,"K","m"]]}},{pn:318,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ckir",st:"Metre bridge",q:"In a metre bridge experiment the balance point is obtained if the gaps are closed by 2 \u03A9 and 3 \u03A9. A shunt of X \u03A9 is added to 3 \u03A9 resistor to shift the balancing point by 22.5 cm. The value of X is ______.",n:2,s:`Put 2 \u03A9 in the left gap and 3 \u03A9 in the right gap. At balance 2/3 = l/(100 \u2212 l), so l = 40 cm.
A shunt on the 3 \u03A9 lowers the right-hand resistance, so the balance point moves towards the right, to 40 + 22.5 = 62.5 cm.
Let R\u2032 be the new right-hand resistance. Then 2/R\u2032 = 62.5/(100 \u2212 62.5), so R\u2032/2 = 37.5/62.5.
R\u2032 = 2 \xD7 37.5/62.5 = 1.2 \u03A9.
3 \u03A9 in parallel with X gives 1.2: 3X/(3 + X) = 1.2, so 3X = 3.6 + 1.2X and 1.8X = 3.6.
X = 2 \u03A9.`},{pn:319,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ckir",st:"Metre bridge",q:"When two resistances R\u2081 and R\u2082 connected in series and introduced into the left gap of a meter bridge and a resistance of 10 \u03A9 is introduced into the right gap, a null point is found at 60 cm from left side. When R\u2081 and R\u2082 are connected in parallel and introduced into the left gap, a resistance of 3 \u03A9 is introduced into the right-gap to get null point at 40 cm from left end. The product of R\u2081R\u2082 is ______ \u03A9\xB2.",n:30,s:`Metre bridge: left resistance / right resistance = l/(100 \u2212 l).
Series: (R\u2081 + R\u2082)/10 = 60/40, so R\u2081 + R\u2082 = 15 \u03A9.
Parallel: R\u2081R\u2082/(R\u2081 + R\u2082) = 3 \xD7 40/60 = 2 \u03A9.
So R\u2081R\u2082 = 2 \xD7 (R\u2081 + R\u2082) = 2 \xD7 15 = 30 \u03A9\xB2.`},{pn:320,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cpot",st:"Potentiometer",q:"A null point is found at 200 cm in potentiometer when cell in secondary circuit is shunted by 5 \u03A9. When a resistance of 15 \u03A9 is used for shunting, null point moves to 300 cm. The internal resistance of the cell is ______ \u03A9.",n:5,s:`When a cell of emf E and internal resistance r is shunted by R, the potential difference across it is V = ER/(R + r), and the balancing length is proportional to V.
So 200/300 = [5/(5 + r)] \xF7 [15/(15 + r)] = (15 + r)/(3(5 + r)).
Cross-multiplying: 2 \xD7 3(5 + r) = 3(15 + r), i.e. 2(5 + r) = 15 + r.
10 + 2r = 15 + r, so r = 5 \u03A9.`},{pn:321,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ckir",st:"Wheatstone bridge",q:"If the potential difference between B and D is zero, the value of x is 1/n \u03A9. The value of n is ______.",n:2,s:`Note: the resistor on the upper right is printed blurred; it is read as \xBD \u03A9, because only this value gives x = 1/n with a whole number n, matching the printed key 2.
Between the left corner and B: 6 \u03A9 and 3 \u03A9 in parallel = 2 \u03A9. Between B and the right corner: \xBD \u03A9 and 1 \u03A9 in parallel = (\xBD \xD7 1)/(3/2) = 1/3 \u03A9.
Between the left corner and D: 1 \u03A9 + 2 \u03A9 = 3 \u03A9 in series. Between D and the right corner: x.
V(B) = V(D) means the bridge is balanced: 2/(1/3) = 3/x.
6 = 3/x, so x = 1/2 \u03A9, i.e. n = 2.`,fig:{w:420,h:270,alt:"A bridge with corners left, B (top), right and D (bottom): 6 ohm and 3 ohm in parallel from left to B, 1/2 ohm and 1 ohm in parallel from B to right, 1 ohm then 2 ohm in series from left to D, and x from D to right; a cell joins left and right",d:[["l",90,120,115.5,127],["l",90,120,93.3,93.8],["l",210,40,206.7,66.2],["l",210,40,184.5,33],["l",115.5,127,148.6,105],["l",173.6,88.3,206.7,66.2],["p","M148.6 105L155.5 106.3L154.2 95.3L163.9 100.8L162.5 89.7L172.2 95.3L173.6 88.3"],["l",93.3,93.8,126.4,71.7],["l",151.4,55,184.5,33],["p","M126.4 71.7L133.4 73.1L132 62L141.7 67.5L140.3 56.4L150 62L151.4 55"],["l",210,40,213.3,66.2],["l",210,40,235.5,33],["l",330,120,304.5,127],["l",330,120,326.7,93.8],["l",213.3,66.2,246.4,88.3],["l",271.4,105,304.5,127],["p","M246.4 88.3L247.8 95.3L257.5 89.7L256.1 100.8L265.8 95.3L264.5 106.3L271.4 105"],["l",235.5,33,268.6,55],["l",293.6,71.7,326.7,93.8],["p","M268.6 55L270 62L279.7 56.4L278.3 67.5L288 62L286.6 73.1L293.6 71.7"],["t",182,114,"3 \u03A9"],["t",117.4,60.1,"6 \u03A9","e"],["t",238,114,"1 \u03A9","e"],["t",302.6,60.1,"\xBD \u03A9"],["l",90,120,129.2,146.1],["l",170.8,173.9,210,200],["p","M129.2 146.1L133.4 154.9L145.8 151.2L147.2 164.2L159.7 160.5L161.1 173.4L170.8 173.9"],["l",210,200,249.2,173.9],["l",290.8,146.1,330,120],["p","M249.2 173.9L258.9 173.4L260.3 160.5L272.8 164.2L274.2 151.2L286.6 154.9L290.8 146.1"],["c",90,120,2.6,"F"],["c",330,120,2.6,"F"],["c",210,40,3.2],["c",210,200,3.2],["t",210,26,"B","m"],["t",210,218,"D","m"],["t",135,175,"1 \u03A9 + 2 \u03A9","e"],["t",300,175,"x \u03A9"],["l",90,120,60,120],["l",60,120,60,245],["l",60,245,190,245],["l",190,245,203,245],["l",209,245,222,245],["l",203,235,203,255],["l",209,240,209,250,"B"],["t",206,266,"","m"],["l",222,245,360,245],["l",360,245,360,120],["l",360,120,330,120]]}},{pn:322,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"crc",st:"Charging and discharging of a capacitor",q:"For the given circuit, in the steady state, |V_B \u2212 V_D| = ______ V.",n:1,s:`In the steady state no current flows through the capacitor, so the branch 2 \u03A9 + 1 \u03BCF between B and C carries nothing; only the 1 \u03A9 between B and C carries the current.
Path A-B-C: 2 \u03A9 + 1 \u03A9 = 3 \u03A9 across 6 V gives I = 2 A. The drop from A to B is 2 \xD7 2 = 4 V.
Path A-D-C: 10 \u03A9 + 2 \u03A9 = 12 \u03A9 across 6 V gives I = 0.5 A. The drop from A to D is 0.5 \xD7 10 = 5 V.
|V_B \u2212 V_D| = |4 \u2212 5| = 1 V.`,fig:{w:420,h:290,alt:"Corners A (left), B (top), C (right), D (bottom): 2 ohm from A to B; from B to C two parallel branches, a 1 ohm resistor and a 2 ohm resistor in series with a 1 microfarad capacitor; 10 ohm from A to D; 2 ohm from D to C; a 6 V cell joins A and C",d:[["l",80,125,124.1,96.2],["l",165.9,68.8,210,40],["p","M124.1 96.2L133.8 95.8L135.3 82.9L147.7 86.7L149.2 73.8L161.7 77.6L165.9 68.8"],["l",80,125,124.1,153.8],["l",165.9,181.2,210,210],["p","M124.1 153.8L128.3 162.6L140.8 158.8L142.3 171.7L154.7 167.9L156.2 180.8L165.9 181.2"],["l",210,210,254.1,181.2],["l",295.9,153.8,340,125],["p","M254.1 181.2L263.8 180.8L265.3 167.9L277.7 171.7L279.2 158.8L291.7 162.6L295.9 153.8"],["c",80,125,2.6,"F"],["c",340,125,2.6,"F"],["c",210,40,3.2],["c",210,210,3.2],["l",210,40,226.7,50.9],["l",323.3,114.1,340,125],["l",226.7,50.9,220.7,60.2],["l",226.7,50.9,232.8,41.7],["l",323.3,114.1,317.2,123.3],["l",323.3,114.1,329.3,104.8],["l",220.7,60.2,250.6,79.7],["l",287.4,103.7,317.2,123.3],["p","M250.6 79.7L254 87.9L265.6 83.5L266.2 95.9L277.9 91.5L278.5 103.9L287.4 103.7"],["l",232.8,41.7,242.7,48.2],["l",267.8,64.6,277.7,71.1],["p","M242.7 48.2L244.1 55.1L253.8 49.5L252.5 60.6L262.1 55L260.8 66.1L267.8 64.6"],["l",277.7,71.1,301,86.3],["l",306,89.6,329.3,104.8],["l",304.8,80.5,297.1,92.2],["l",309.8,83.8,302.2,95.5],["c",226.7,50.9,2.6,"F"],["c",323.3,114.1,2.6,"F"],["t",125,70,"2 \u03A9","e"],["t",125,185,"10 \u03A9","e"],["t",285,190,"2 \u03A9"],["t",290,50,"2 \u03A9"],["t",228,100,"1 \u03A9","e"],["t",336,92,"1 \u03BCF","ms"],["t",210,28,"B","m"],["t",354,120,"C"],["t",66,120,"A","e"],["t",210,232,"D","m"],["l",80,125,40,125],["l",40,125,40,255],["l",40,255,190,255],["l",190,255,203,255],["l",209,255,222,255],["l",203,245,203,265],["l",209,250,209,260,"B"],["l",222,255,380,255],["l",380,255,380,125],["l",380,125,340,125],["t",206,278,"6 V","m"],["t",240,232,"","m"]]}},{pn:323,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cpot",st:"Potentiometer",q:"In a potentiometer arrangement, a cell of emf 1.20 V gives a balance point at 36 cm length of wire. This cell is now replaced by another cell of emf 1.80 V. The difference in balancing length of potentiometer wire in above conditions will be ______ cm.",n:18,s:`On a potentiometer wire the balancing length is proportional to the emf: l \u221D E.
l\u2082 = 36 \xD7 1.80/1.20 = 54 cm.
Difference = 54 \u2212 36 = 18 cm.`},{pn:324,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ckir",st:"Metre bridge",q:"Resistance are connected in a meter bridge circuit as shown in the figure. The balancing length l\u2081 is 40 cm. Now an unknown resistance x is connected in series with P and new balancing length is found to be 80 cm measured from the same end. Then the value of x will be ______ \u03A9.",n:200,s:`Note: the printed key says 20; working with P = 40 \u03A9 as printed gives 200 \u03A9, so the question should be checked against the original paper.
Metre bridge: P/Q = l/(100 \u2212 l). With l\u2081 = 40: 40/Q = 40/60, so Q = 60 \u03A9.
With x in series with P: (40 + x)/60 = 80/20 = 4.
40 + x = 240, so x = 200 \u03A9.`,fig:{w:380,h:215,alt:"A metre bridge: P = 40 ohm in the left gap and Q in the right gap, galvanometer G, jockey at distance l1 from A",d:[["l",50,130,330,130,"B"],["l",50,130,50,55],["l",330,130,330,55],["l",50,55,60,55],["l",60,55,102,55],["l",138,55,180,55],["p","M102 55L108 60L114 50L120 60L126 50L132 60L138 55"],["l",180,55,200,55],["l",200,55,242,55],["l",278,55,320,55],["p","M242 55L248 60L254 50L260 60L266 50L272 60L278 55"],["l",320,55,330,55],["c",190,55,2.6,"F"],["l",190,55,190,80],["c",190,90,10],["t",190,94,"G","m"],["l",190,100,190,108],["l",190,108,162,108],["l",162,108,162,127,"a"],["t",44,146,"A","m"],["t",336,146,"C","m"],["t",120,44,"P = 40 \u03A9","m"],["t",260,44,"Q","m"],["t",162,148,"J","m"],["l",50,130,50,180],["l",50,180,160,180],["l",160,180,173,180],["l",179,180,192,180],["l",173,170,173,190],["l",179,175,179,185,"B"],["l",192,180,220,180],["c",224,180,3.2],["l",228,180,330,180],["l",330,180,330,130],["t",176,202,"E","m"],["t",224,170,"K","m"],["l",50,160,162,160,"b"],["t",106,174,"l\u2081","m"],["t",190,42,"B","m"]]}},{pn:325,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cpot",st:"Potentiometer",q:"A potentiometer wire of length 300 cm is connected in series with a resistance 780 \u03A9 and a standard cell of emf 4 V. A constant current flows through potentiometer wire. The length of the null point for cell of emf 20 mV is found to be 60 cm. The resistance of the potentiometer wire is ______ \u03A9.",n:20,s:`Let the wire resistance be r. The current is I = 4/(780 + r).
The potential difference across 60 cm of the wire is I \xD7 r \xD7 (60/300) = 0.2 I r, and this equals the emf 20 mV = 0.02 V.
So I r = 0.1, i.e. 4r/(780 + r) = 0.1.
4r = 78 + 0.1r, so 3.9r = 78 and r = 20 \u03A9.`},{pn:326,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ckir",st:"Metre bridge",q:"In a meter bridge experiment, for measuring unknown resistance S, the null point is obtained at a distance 30 cm from the left side as shown at point D. If R is 5.6 k\u03A9, then the value of unknown resistance S will be ______ \u03A9.",n:2400,s:`S is in the left gap and R in the right gap, so S/R = l/(100 \u2212 l) = 30/70.
S = 5600 \xD7 3/7 = 2400 \u03A9.`,fig:{w:380,h:215,alt:"A metre bridge: unknown S in the left gap, R in the right gap, null point D at 30 cm from the left end",d:[["l",50,130,330,130,"B"],["l",50,130,50,55],["l",330,130,330,55],["l",50,55,60,55],["l",60,55,102,55],["l",138,55,180,55],["p","M102 55L108 60L114 50L120 60L126 50L132 60L138 55"],["l",180,55,200,55],["l",200,55,242,55],["l",278,55,320,55],["p","M242 55L248 60L254 50L260 60L266 50L272 60L278 55"],["l",320,55,330,55],["c",190,55,2.6,"F"],["l",190,55,190,80],["c",190,90,10],["t",190,94,"G","m"],["l",190,100,190,108],["l",190,108,134,108],["l",134,108,134,127,"a"],["t",44,146,"A","m"],["t",336,146,"B","m"],["t",120,44,"S","m"],["t",260,44,"R","m"],["t",134,148,"D","m"],["l",50,130,50,180],["l",50,180,160,180],["l",160,180,173,180],["l",179,180,192,180],["l",173,170,173,190],["l",179,175,179,185,"B"],["l",192,180,220,180],["c",224,180,3.2],["l",228,180,330,180],["l",330,180,330,130],["t",176,202,"E","m"],["t",224,170,"K","m"],["l",50,160,134,160,"b"],["t",92,174,"30 cm","m"]]}},{pn:327,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ckir",st:"Metre bridge",q:"In the given figure of meter bridge experiment, the balancing length AC corresponding to null deflection of the galvanometer is 40 cm. The balancing length, if the radius of the wire AB is doubled, will be ______ cm.",n:40,s:`At balance R\u2081/R\u2082 = l/(100 \u2212 l), where l and 100 \u2212 l are the lengths of the two wire parts. The wire resistance per unit length cancels from this ratio.
Doubling the radius changes the resistance per unit length of the whole wire equally, so the ratio of the two parts is unchanged.
The balancing length stays 40 cm.`,fig:{w:380,h:215,alt:"A metre bridge with R1 in the left gap and R2 in the right gap, 5 V cell, balance point C at 40 cm from A",d:[["l",50,130,330,130,"B"],["l",50,130,50,55],["l",330,130,330,55],["l",50,55,60,55],["l",60,55,102,55],["l",138,55,180,55],["p","M102 55L108 60L114 50L120 60L126 50L132 60L138 55"],["l",180,55,200,55],["l",200,55,242,55],["l",278,55,320,55],["p","M242 55L248 60L254 50L260 60L266 50L272 60L278 55"],["l",320,55,330,55],["c",190,55,2.6,"F"],["l",190,55,190,80],["c",190,90,10],["t",190,94,"G","m"],["l",190,100,190,108],["l",190,108,162,108],["l",162,108,162,127,"a"],["t",44,146,"A","m"],["t",336,146,"B","m"],["t",120,44,"R\u2081","m"],["t",260,44,"R\u2082","m"],["t",162,148,"C","m"],["l",50,130,50,180],["l",50,180,160,180],["l",160,180,173,180],["l",179,180,192,180],["l",173,170,173,190],["l",179,175,179,185,"B"],["l",192,180,220,180],["c",224,180,3.2],["l",228,180,330,180],["l",330,180,330,130],["t",176,202,"5 V","m"],["t",224,170,"K","m"],["l",50,160,162,160,"b"],["t",106,174,"AC = 40 cm","m"]]}},{pn:328,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cpot",st:"Potentiometer",q:"As shown in the figure, a potentiometer wire of resistance 20 \u03A9 and length 300 cm is connected with resistance box (R.B.) and a standard cell of emf 4 V. For a resistance R of resistance box introduced into the circuit, the null point for a cell of 20 mV is found to be 60 cm. The value of R is ______ \u03A9.",n:780,s:`The potential difference across the 60 cm of wire equals 20 mV: I \xD7 20 \xD7 (60/300) = 0.02 V.
So I \xD7 4 = 0.02 and I = 0.005 A.
The same current flows through R and the wire: I = 4/(20 + R) = 0.005.
20 + R = 800, so R = 780 \u03A9.`,fig:{w:380,h:230,alt:"Potentiometer: wire AB of 20 ohm and 300 cm in series with a resistance box R and a 4 V cell; the 20 mV cell and a galvanometer between A and a jockey on the wire",d:[["l",60,100,330,100,"B"],["t",52,96,"A","e"],["t",338,104,"B"],["l",60,100,60,40],["l",60,40,110,40],["l",110,40,123,40],["l",129,40,142,40],["l",123,30,123,50],["l",129,35,129,45,"B"],["l",142,40,170,40],["c",174,40,3.2],["l",178,40,220,40],["r",220,30,44,20],["l",264,40,330,40],["l",330,40,330,100],["t",126,24,"4 V","m"],["t",174,28,"K","m"],["t",242,24,"R.B.","m"],["t",242,62,"R","m"],["l",60,100,60,170],["l",60,170,100,170],["l",100,170,113,170],["l",119,170,132,170],["l",113,160,113,180],["l",119,165,119,175,"B"],["l",132,170,170,170],["c",184,170,10],["t",184,174,"G","m"],["l",194,170,240,170],["l",240,170,240,108,"a"],["t",116,194,"20 mV","m"],["l",60,128,240,128,"b"],["t",150,146,"60 cm","m"]]}},{pn:329,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 24 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cpot",st:"Potentiometer",q:"In a potentiometer arrangement, a cell gives a balancing point at 75 cm length of wire. This cell is now replaced by another cell of unknown emf. If the ratio of the emf's of two cells respectively is 3 : 2, the difference in the balancing length of the potentiometer wire in above two cases will be ______ cm.",n:25,s:`Balancing length is proportional to emf: l\u2081/l\u2082 = E\u2081/E\u2082 = 3/2.
l\u2082 = 75 \xD7 2/3 = 50 cm.
Difference = 75 \u2212 50 = 25 cm.`},{pn:330,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 24 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cpot",st:"Potentiometer",q:"A potentiometer wire of length 10 m and resistance 20 \u03A9 is connected in series with a 25 V battery and an external resistance 30 \u03A9. A cell of emf E in secondary circuit is balanced by 250 cm long potentiometer wire. The value of E (in volt) is x/10. The value of x is ______.",n:25,s:`Current in the wire: I = 25/(20 + 30) = 0.5 A.
Potential difference along the whole wire = 0.5 \xD7 20 = 10 V over 10 m, so the gradient is 1 V per metre.
E = 1 \xD7 2.5 m = 2.5 V = x/10, so x = 25.`},{pn:331,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cpot",st:"Potentiometer",q:"A cell, shunted by a 8 \u03A9 resistance, is balanced across a potentiometer wire of length 3 m. The balancing length is 2 m, when the cell is shunted by 4 \u03A9 resistance. The value of internal resistance of the cell will be ______ \u03A9.",n:8,s:`The terminal voltage of a shunted cell is V = ER/(R + r), and the balancing length is proportional to V.
With the 8 \u03A9 shunt the balancing length is 3 m, with the 4 \u03A9 shunt it is 2 m: 3/2 = [8/(8 + r)] \xF7 [4/(4 + r)] = 2(4 + r)/(8 + r).
3(8 + r) = 4(4 + r), so 24 + 3r = 16 + 4r.
r = 8 \u03A9.`},{pn:332,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ckir",st:"Metre bridge",q:"A meter bridge setup is shown in the figure. It is used to determine an unknown resistance R using a given resistor of 15 \u03A9. The galvanometer (G) shows null deflection when tapping key is at 43 cm mark from end A. If the end correction for end A is 2 cm, then the determined value of R will be ______ \u03A9.",n:19,s:`The end correction adds 2 cm to the length measured from A: the effective length is 43 + 2 = 45 cm, and the other part is 100 \u2212 43 = 57 cm.
The 15 \u03A9 resistor is in the gap next to A, so 15/R = 45/57.
R = 15 \xD7 57/45 = 19 \u03A9.`,fig:{w:380,h:215,alt:"A metre bridge with a 15 ohm resistor in the left gap and unknown R in the right gap, 5 V cell and key K, null point 43 cm from A",d:[["l",50,130,330,130,"B"],["l",50,130,50,55],["l",330,130,330,55],["l",50,55,60,55],["l",60,55,102,55],["l",138,55,180,55],["p","M102 55L108 60L114 50L120 60L126 50L132 60L138 55"],["l",180,55,200,55],["l",200,55,242,55],["l",278,55,320,55],["p","M242 55L248 60L254 50L260 60L266 50L272 60L278 55"],["l",320,55,330,55],["c",190,55,2.6,"F"],["l",190,55,190,80],["c",190,90,10],["t",190,94,"G","m"],["l",190,100,190,108],["l",190,108,170.4,108],["l",170.4,108,170.4,127,"a"],["t",44,146,"A","m"],["t",336,146,"B","m"],["t",120,44,"15 \u03A9","m"],["t",260,44,"R","m"],["t",170.4,148,"J","m"],["l",50,130,50,180],["l",50,180,160,180],["l",160,180,173,180],["l",179,180,192,180],["l",173,170,173,190],["l",179,175,179,185,"B"],["l",192,180,220,180],["c",224,180,3.2],["l",228,180,330,180],["l",330,180,330,130],["t",176,202,"5 V","m"],["t",224,170,"K","m"],["l",50,160,170.4,160,"b"],["t",110.2,174,"43 cm","m"]]}},{pn:333,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ckir",st:"Wheatstone bridge",q:"Current measured by the ammeter A in the reported circuit when no current flows through 10 \u03A9 resistance, will be ______ A.",n:10,s:`No current in the 10 \u03A9 means the bridge is balanced: R/3 = 4/6, so R = 2 \u03A9.
The 10 \u03A9 can be ignored. The two arms are R + 4 = 6 \u03A9 and 3 + 6 = 9 \u03A9 in parallel: (6 \xD7 9)/15 = 3.6 \u03A9.
The ammeter reads the total current: I = 36/3.6 = 10 A.`,fig:{w:380,h:230,alt:"A bridge: R from the left corner to the top corner, 4 ohm from the top corner to the right corner, 3 ohm from the left corner to the bottom corner, 6 ohm from the bottom corner to the right corner, 10 ohm between top and bottom corners; an ammeter and a 36 V cell connect the left and right corners",d:[["l",110,100,138.4,81.1],["l",171.6,58.9,200,40],["p","M138.4 81.1L146.7 81.6L146.7 69.5L157.8 74.2L157.8 62.1L168.9 66.8L171.6 58.9"],["l",200,40,228.4,58.9],["l",261.6,81.1,290,100],["p","M228.4 58.9L231.1 66.8L242.2 62.1L242.2 74.2L253.3 69.5L253.3 81.6L261.6 81.1"],["l",110,100,138.4,118.9],["l",171.6,141.1,200,160],["p","M138.4 118.9L141.1 126.8L152.2 122.1L152.2 134.2L163.3 129.5L163.3 141.6L171.6 141.1"],["l",200,160,228.4,141.1],["l",261.6,118.9,290,100],["p","M228.4 141.1L236.7 141.6L236.7 129.5L247.8 134.2L247.8 122.1L258.9 126.8L261.6 118.9"],["l",200,40,200,75],["l",200,125,200,160],["p","M200 75L195 83.3L205 91.7L195 100L205 108.3L195 116.7L200 125"],["c",110,100,2.6,"F"],["c",290,100,2.6,"F"],["l",110,100,60,100],["l",60,100,60,200],["l",60,200,100,200],["c",116,200,10],["t",116,204,"A","m"],["l",126,200,210,200],["l",210,200,223,200],["l",229,200,242,200],["l",223,190,223,210],["l",229,195,229,205,"B"],["l",242,200,340,200],["l",340,200,340,100],["l",340,100,290,100],["t",140,52,"R","e"],["t",262,52,"4 \u03A9"],["t",140,150,"3 \u03A9","e"],["t",262,150,"6 \u03A9"],["t",208,104,"10 \u03A9"],["t",226,222,"36 V","m"]]}},{pn:334,y:"JEE Advanced 2022",tp:"ckir",st:"Wheatstone bridge",q:"Two resistances R\u2081 = X \u03A9 and R\u2082 = 1 \u03A9 are connected to a wire AB of uniform resistivity, as shown in the figure. The radius of the wire varies linearly along its axis from 0.2 mm at A to 1 mm at B. A galvanometer (G) connected to the center of the wire, 50 cm from each end along its axis, shows zero deflection when A and B are connected to a battery. The value of X is ______.",n:5,s:`Zero deflection means a balanced bridge: R\u2081/R\u2082 = R_A-mid / R_mid-B, the resistances of the two halves of the wire.
Take the length as 1 m with radius r(x) = 0.2 + 0.8x mm (x in metres). A thin slice has resistance \u03C1 dx/(\u03C0r\xB2).
First half: \u222B\u2080^0.5 dx/r\xB2 = (1/0.8)(1/0.2 \u2212 1/0.6) = 4.167. Second half: \u222B\u2080.\u2085^1 dx/r\xB2 = (1/0.8)(1/0.6 \u2212 1/1) = 0.833, in the same units.
Ratio = 4.167/0.833 = 5.
So X/1 = 5, X = 5.`,fig:{w:380,h:260,alt:"A triangle: R1 on the left side and R2 on the right side from the top corner down to the ends A and B of a wire that is thin at A and thick at B; a galvanometer joins the top corner to the middle of the wire; a battery joins A and B below the wire",d:[["l",190,40,139.7,86.4],["l",110.3,113.6,60,160],["p","M139.7 86.4L131.4 87.3L133.3 99.2L121.6 96.3L123.5 108.2L111.8 105.4L110.3 113.6"],["l",190,40,240.3,86.4],["l",269.7,113.6,320,160],["p","M240.3 86.4L241.8 94.6L253.5 91.8L251.6 103.7L263.3 100.8L261.4 112.7L269.7 113.6"],["l",190,40,190,85],["c",190,100,12],["t",190,104,"G","m"],["l",190,112,190,160],["c",190,40,2.6,"F"],["c",190,160,2.6,"F"],["p","M60 156L320 152L320 168L60 164Z"],["t",52,164,"A","e"],["t",328,164,"B"],["t",120,90,"R\u2081","e"],["t",262,90,"R\u2082"],["t",190,190,"radius 0.2 mm at A, 1 mm at B","ms"],["l",60,164,60,225],["l",60,225,170,225],["l",170,225,183,225],["l",189,225,202,225],["l",183,215,183,235],["l",189,220,189,230,"B"],["l",202,225,320,225],["l",320,225,320,164]]}},{pn:335,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 18 Mar \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ckir",st:"Wheatstone bridge",q:"Consider a 72 cm long 12 \u03A9 wire AB as shown in the figure. The galvanometer jockey is placed at P on AB at a distance x cm from A. The galvanometer shows zero deflection. The value of x, to the nearest integer is ______.",n:48,s:`Zero deflection: the potential at C (between 12 \u03A9 and 6 \u03A9) equals the potential at P on the wire.
Balance condition: 12/6 = (resistance of AP)/(resistance of PB).
The wire is uniform, so the resistances are proportional to lengths: x/(72 \u2212 x) = 2.
x = 144 \u2212 2x, so 3x = 144 and x = 48 cm.`,fig:{w:380,h:220,alt:"A 12 ohm resistor and a 6 ohm resistor in series on the top with node C between them; a galvanometer joins C to the jockey at P on wire AB, 72 cm long; a cell joins A and B below; x is the distance AP",d:[["l",50,110,330,110,"B"],["c",50,110,2.6,"F"],["c",330,110,2.6,"F"],["t",44,126,"A","m"],["t",336,126,"B","m"],["l",50,110,50,40],["l",50,40,60,40],["l",60,40,92,40],["l",128,40,160,40],["p","M92 40L98 45L104 35L110 45L116 35L122 45L128 40"],["l",160,40,175,40],["l",175,40,224.5,40],["l",260.5,40,310,40],["p","M224.5 40L230.5 45L236.5 35L242.5 45L248.5 35L254.5 45L260.5 40"],["l",310,40,330,40],["l",330,40,330,110],["c",167,40,2.6,"F"],["l",167,40,167,60],["c",167,72,10],["t",167,76,"G","m"],["l",167,82,167,90],["l",167,90,220,90],["l",220,90,220,107,"a"],["t",220,128,"P","m"],["t",108,30,"12 \u03A9","m"],["t",240,30,"6 \u03A9","m"],["t",167,30,"C","m"],["l",50,140,220,140,"b"],["t",135,156,"x","m"],["l",50,110,50,185],["l",50,185,170,185],["l",170,185,183,185],["l",189,185,202,185],["l",183,175,183,195],["l",189,180,189,190,"B"],["l",202,185,330,185],["l",330,185,330,110]]}},{pn:336,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cpot",st:"Potentiometer",q:"In the given circuit of potentiometer, the potential difference E across AB (10 m length) is larger than E\u2081 and E\u2082 as well. For key K\u2081 (closed), the jockey is adjusted to touch the wire at point J\u2081 so that there is no deflection in the galvanometer. Now the first battery (E\u2081) is replaced by second battery (E\u2082) for working by making K\u2081 open and K\u2082 closed. The galvanometer gives then null deflection at J\u2082. The value of E\u2081/E\u2082 is a/b, where a = ______.",n:1,s:`Note: the printed figure folds the 10 m wire into rows of 1 m. J\u2081 is read as 20 cm along the 2nd row and J\u2082 as 60 cm along the 4th row, as printed; the folded drawing in this page is only a sketch of it.
The balancing length is proportional to the emf, so E\u2081/E\u2082 = l\u2081/l\u2082.
l\u2081 = 1 m + 0.2 m = 1.2 m (one full row, then 20 cm).
l\u2082 = 3 m + 0.6 m = 3.6 m (three full rows, then 60 cm).
E\u2081/E\u2082 = 1.2/3.6 = 1/3, so a/b = 1/3 and a = 1.`,fig:{w:440,h:320,alt:"A potentiometer wire of 10 m folded into five tracks of 2 m, joined alternately at the ends, a driver cell E with a rheostat Rh between A and B, cells E1 and E2 with keys K1 and K2 and a galvanometer G to a jockey; J1 is 20 cm along the second track and J2 is 60 cm along the fourth track",d:[["l",150,116,330,116],["l",150,150,330,150],["l",150,184,330,184],["l",150,218,330,218],["l",150,252,330,252],["l",330,116,330,150],["l",150,150,150,184],["l",330,184,330,218],["l",150,218,150,252],["c",150,116,2.6,"F"],["t",160,104,"A","m"],["c",330,252,2.6,"F"],["t",342,256,"B"],["l",294,168,294,153,"a"],["t",282,168,"J\u2081","e"],["l",330,140,294,140,"b"],["t",312,134,"20 cm","ms"],["l",222,236,222,221,"a"],["t",210,236,"J\u2082","e"],["l",330,208,222,208,"b"],["t",276,202,"60 cm","ms"],["l",150,116,100,116],["l",100,116,100,76],["l",100,76,70,76],["l",38,76,51,76],["l",57,76,70,76],["l",51,66,51,86],["l",57,71,57,81,"B"],["l",38,76,20,76],["t",54,60,"E","m"],["l",20,76,20,150],["l",20,150,20,173],["l",20,217,20,240],["p","M20 173L15 180.3L25 187.7L15 195L25 202.3L15 209.7L20 217"],["l",20,240,20,290],["l",20,290,330,290],["l",330,290,330,252],["t",34,198,"Rh"],["l",294,168,294,300],["l",294,300,420,300],["l",420,300,420,40],["l",420,40,390,40],["c",380,40,10],["t",380,44,"G","m"],["l",370,40,350,40],["l",350,40,350,20],["l",350,20,300,20],["l",268,20,281,20],["l",287,20,300,20],["l",281,10,281,30],["l",287,15,287,25,"B"],["l",268,20,240,20],["c",236,20,3.2],["t",236,36,"K\u2081","m"],["t",284,46,"E\u2081","m"],["l",350,40,350,60],["l",350,60,300,60],["l",268,60,281,60],["l",287,60,300,60],["l",281,50,281,70],["l",287,55,287,65,"B"],["l",268,60,240,60],["c",236,60,3.2],["t",236,76,"K\u2082","m"],["t",284,86,"E\u2082","m"]]}},{pn:337,y:"JEE Advanced 2021",tp:"ckir",st:"Metre bridge",q:"In order to measure the internal resistance r\u2081 of a cell of emf E, a meter bridge of wire resistance R\u2080 = 50 \u03A9, a resistance R\u2080/2, another cell of emf E/2 (internal resistance r) and a galvanometer G are used in a circuit, as shown in figure. If the null point is found at l = 72 cm, then the value of r\u2081 = ____ \u03A9.",n:3,s:`The cell E (with r\u2081), the resistor R\u2080/2 = 25 \u03A9 and the bridge wire (50 \u03A9) form one loop, so the same current flows through all three: I = E/(r\u2081 + 25 + 50) = E/(r\u2081 + 75).
At the null point no current flows in the branch with the cell E/2 and G, so r does not matter and the branch only compares potentials: the potential difference between the node N (after r\u2081) and the jockey equals E/2.
The cell E sends current along the wire from A to B. The wire part from A to the jockey is 72 cm, which is 0.72 \xD7 50 = 36 \u03A9.
Potential of N relative to A: V_N \u2212 V_A = \u2212E + I r\u2081. Potential of the jockey: V_J \u2212 V_A = \u221236 I.
V_N \u2212 V_J = \u2212E + I(r\u2081 + 36) = \u2212E + E(r\u2081 + 36)/(r\u2081 + 75) = \u221239E/(r\u2081 + 75).
The cell E/2 opposes this (its + plate is on the jockey side), so 39E/(r\u2081 + 75) = E/2, which gives r\u2081 + 75 = 78 and r\u2081 = 3 \u03A9.
Note: the plates of both cells were read on the 400 dpi page: E has its long plate on the A side and E/2 has its long plate on the jockey side. This agrees with the printed key 3.`,fig:{w:340,h:215,alt:"Meter bridge with a wire AB of resistance R0. A cell E with internal resistance r1 and a resistor R0/2 are connected in series across the wire. From the node between r1 and R0/2 a cell E/2 with internal resistance r and a galvanometer G go to a jockey on the wire at distance l from A",d:[["c",40,160,2.6,"F"],["c",320,160,2.6,"F"],["l",40,160,320,160],["t",40,178,"A","m"],["t",320,178,"B","m"],["l",40,160,70,60],["l",70,60,92,60],["l",92,49,92,71],["l",98,54,98,66,"B"],["l",98,60,120,60],["t",95,44,"E","m"],["p","M120 60 L122 60 L125 55 L131 65 L137 55 L143 65 L149 55 L155 65 L158 60 L160 60"],["t",140,48,"r\u2081","m"],["c",160,60,2.6,"F"],["p","M160 60 L197.5 60 L201.7 55 L210 65 L218.3 55 L226.7 65 L235 55 L243.3 65 L247.5 60 L285 60"],["t",222,48,"R\u2080/2","m"],["l",285,60,320,160],["l",160,60,160,105],["l",160,105,177,105],["l",177,99,177,111,"B"],["l",183,94,183,116],["l",183,105,200,105],["t",180,128,"E/2","m"],["p","M200 105 L205.5 105 L208.3 100 L214 110 L219.7 100 L225.3 110 L231 100 L236.7 110 L239.5 105 L245 105"],["t",222,128,"r","m"],["c",262,105,12],["t",262,109,"G","ms"],["l",274,105,290,105],["l",290,105,290,152,"a"],["l",40,190,290,190,"b"],["t",165,208,"l","m"]]}},{pn:338,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 4 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ckir",st:"Wheatstone bridge",q:"Four resistances 40 \u03A9, 60 \u03A9, 90 \u03A9 and 110 \u03A9 make the arms AB, BC, AD and DC of a quadrilateral ABCD. Across AC is a battery of emf 40 V and internal resistance negligible. The potential difference across BD in V is ____.",n:2,s:`The battery is across A and C, so there are two voltage dividers between A and C: A\u2013B\u2013C (40 \u03A9 and 60 \u03A9) and A\u2013D\u2013C (90 \u03A9 and 110 \u03A9).
Take V_C = 0 and V_A = 40 V.
Potential of B: V_B = 40 \xD7 60/(40 + 60) = 24 V (the drop across BC).
Potential of D: V_D = 40 \xD7 110/(90 + 110) = 22 V (the drop across DC).
V_B \u2212 V_D = 24 \u2212 22 = 2 V.`,fig:{w:300,h:226,alt:"Quadrilateral ABCD with 40 ohm in AB, 60 ohm in BC, 90 ohm in AD and 110 ohm in DC; a 40 V battery is connected across A and C",d:[["p","M60 90 L80 76.6 L81.4 69.7 L95.3 72.5 L98.1 58.6 L111.9 61.4 L114.7 47.5 L128.6 50.3 L130 43.4 L150 30"],["p","M150 30 L170 43.4 L177 42 L179.7 55.8 L193.6 53.1 L196.4 66.9 L210.3 64.2 L213 78 L220 76.6 L240 90"],["p","M60 90 L80 103.4 L87 102 L89.7 115.8 L103.6 113.1 L106.4 126.9 L120.3 124.2 L123 138 L130 136.6 L150 150"],["p","M150 150 L170 136.6 L171.4 129.7 L185.3 132.5 L188.1 118.6 L201.9 121.4 L204.7 107.5 L218.6 110.3 L220 103.4 L240 90"],["t",150,18,"B","m"],["t",48,94,"A","e"],["t",252,94,"C",""],["t",150,168,"D","m"],["t",92,52,"40 \u03A9","e"],["t",210,52,"60 \u03A9",""],["t",92,134,"90 \u03A9","e"],["t",226,152,"110 \u03A9","e"],["l",60,90,60,185],["l",240,90,240,185],["l",60,185,147,185],["l",147,174,147,196],["l",153,179,153,191,"B"],["l",153,185,240,185],["t",150,219,"40 V","m"]]}},{pn:339,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 7 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cpot",st:"Potentiometer",q:"The balancing length for a cell is 560 cm in a potentiometer experiment. When an external resistance of 10 \u03A9 is connected in parallel to the cell, the balancing length changes by 60 cm. If the internal resistance of the cell is N/10 \u03A9, where N is an integer then value of N is ____.",n:12,s:`On a potentiometer the balancing length is proportional to the potential difference being measured.
Open cell: it balances its emf E at l\u2081 = 560 cm.
With 10 \u03A9 in parallel, the cell gives current, so the terminal voltage V = E \u2212 Ir is smaller, so the balancing length falls: l\u2082 = 560 \u2212 60 = 500 cm.
E/V = l\u2081/l\u2082 = 560/500 = 1.12.
For a cell with load R: E/V = (R + r)/R = 1 + r/R, so r/R = 0.12.
r = 0.12 \xD7 10 = 1.2 \u03A9 = 12/10 \u03A9, so N = 12.`},{pn:340,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 8 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ckir",st:"Wheatstone bridge",q:"Four resistances of 15 \u03A9, 12 \u03A9, 4 \u03A9 and 10 \u03A9 respectively in cyclic order to form Wheatstone's network. The resistance that is to be connected in parallel with the resistance of 10 \u03A9 to balance the network is ____ \u03A9.",n:10,s:`Arms in order around the bridge: P = 15 \u03A9, Q = 12 \u03A9, R = 4 \u03A9, S = 10 \u03A9. A Wheatstone bridge is balanced when P/Q = S/R for arms taken around the loop (the two arms on one side of the galvanometer give the same ratio).
Here P/Q = 15/12 = 1.25.
The arm that must change is the 10 \u03A9 one: we need S\u2032/R = 1.25 with R = 4 \u03A9, so S\u2032 = 5 \u03A9.
A resistance x in parallel with 10 \u03A9 gives 10x/(10 + x) = 5.
10x = 50 + 5x, so x = 10 \u03A9.`},{pn:341,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ckir",st:"Metre bridge",q:"In a meter bridge experiment, S is a standard resistance. R is a resistance wire. It is found that balancing length is l = 25 cm. If R is replaced by a wire of half length and half diameter that of R of same material, then the balancing distance l\u2032 (in cm) will now be ____.",n:40,s:`In a meter bridge with R in the left gap and S in the right gap, R/S = l/(100 \u2212 l).
First case: R/S = 25/75 = 1/3.
The resistance of a wire is R = \u03C1L/A = 4\u03C1L/(\u03C0d\xB2). With half the length and half the diameter, R\u2032 = 4\u03C1(L/2)/(\u03C0(d/2)\xB2) = 2R.
So R\u2032/S = 2/3.
l\u2032/(100 \u2212 l\u2032) = 2/3, so 3l\u2032 = 200 \u2212 2l\u2032 and l\u2032 = 40 cm.`,fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"Meter bridge: resistance wire R in the left gap and standard resistance S in the right gap; a galvanometer G from the middle strip to a jockey on the bridge wire at distance l from the left end; a battery across the bridge wire",d:[["l",40,130,300,130],["l",40,130,40,55],["l",40,55,60,55],["p","M60 55 A35 35 0 0 1 130 55"],["r",128,50,40,10],["l",168,55,225,55],["r",225,48,34,14],["l",259,55,300,55],["l",300,55,300,130],["t",95,18,"R","m"],["t",242,40,"S","m"],["l",148,60,148,83],["c",148,93,10],["t",148,97,"G","ms"],["l",148,103,148,126,"a"],["l",40,150,148,150,"b"],["t",94,166,"l","m"],["l",40,130,40,185],["l",40,185,167,185],["l",167,174,167,196],["l",173,179,173,191,"B"],["l",173,185,300,185],["t",170,200,"V","m"],["l",300,185,300,130]]}},{pn:342,y:"JEE Advanced 2020",tp:"ckir",st:"Wheatstone bridge",q:"In the balanced condition, the values of the resistances of the four arms of a Wheatstone bridge are shown in the figure (R\u2081 = 60 \u03A9, R\u2082 = 100 \u03A9, R\u2083 = 300 \u03A9, R\u2084 = 500 \u03A9, with a 50 V cell). The resistance R\u2083 has temperature coefficient 0.0004 \xB0C\u207B\xB9. If the temperature of R\u2083 is increased by 100 \xB0C, the voltage developed between S and T will be ____ volt.",n:.27,s:`Before heating the bridge is balanced: R\u2081/R\u2083 = 60/300 = 1/5 and R\u2082/R\u2084 = 100/500 = 1/5, so V_S = V_T and the voltmeter reads 0.
After heating, R\u2083 = 300(1 + 0.0004 \xD7 100) = 300 \xD7 1.04 = 312 \u03A9.
Take V_Q = 0 and V_P = 50 V. Each side is a voltage divider: V_S = 50 \xD7 R\u2083/(R\u2081 + R\u2083) = 50 \xD7 312/372 = 41.94 V.
V_T = 50 \xD7 R\u2084/(R\u2082 + R\u2084) = 50 \xD7 500/600 = 41.67 V (unchanged).
V_S \u2212 V_T = 41.94 \u2212 41.67 = 0.269 V \u2248 0.27 V.
Note: the cell label is rotated in the crop; on the page it reads 50 V, which agrees with the printed key 0.27 (a 5 V cell would give 0.027 V).`,fig:{w:340,h:185,alt:"Wheatstone bridge: R1 = 60 ohm and R2 = 100 ohm in the top arms, R3 = 300 ohm and R4 = 500 ohm in the bottom arms, a 50 V cell between P and Q, and a voltmeter between S and T",d:[["p","M50 70 L88 70 L91.7 65 L99 75 L106.3 65 L113.7 75 L121 65 L128.3 75 L132 70 L170 70"],["t",110,60,"R\u2081 = 60 \u03A9","m"],["p","M170 70 L208 70 L211.7 65 L219 75 L226.3 65 L233.7 75 L241 65 L248.3 75 L252 70 L290 70"],["t",230,60,"R\u2082 = 100 \u03A9","m"],["p","M50 130 L88 130 L91.7 125 L99 135 L106.3 125 L113.7 135 L121 125 L128.3 135 L132 130 L170 130"],["t",110,152,"R\u2083 = 300 \u03A9","m"],["p","M170 130 L208 130 L211.7 125 L219 135 L226.3 125 L233.7 135 L241 125 L248.3 135 L252 130 L290 130"],["t",230,152,"R\u2084 = 500 \u03A9","m"],["l",50,70,50,130],["l",290,70,290,130],["l",170,70,170,92],["l",160,95,180,95],["l",165,102,175,102,"B"],["l",170,102,170,130],["t",192,104,"50 V",""],["c",170,70,2.6,"F"],["c",170,130,2.6,"F"],["t",170,58,"P","m"],["t",170,172,"Q","m"],["t",38,104,"S","e"],["t",302,104,"T",""],["l",50,70,50,25],["l",50,25,150,25],["c",162,25,12],["t",162,29,"V","ms"],["l",174,25,290,25],["l",290,25,290,70],["c",50,100,2.6,"F"],["c",290,100,2.6,"F"]]}},{pn:343,y:"IIT-JEE 2008",tp:"ckir",st:"Metre bridge",q:`Statement-1: In a Meter Bridge experiment, null point for an unknown resistance is measured. Now the unknown resistance is put inside an enclosure maintained at a higher temperature. The null point can be obtained at the same point as before by decreasing the value of the standard resistance.
Statement-2: Resistance of a metal increases with increase in temperature.`,o:["Statements-1 and 2 are true and statement-2 is a correct explanation for statement-1","Statements-1 and 2 are true and statement-2 is not a correct explanation for statement-1","Statement-1 is true, statement-2 is false","Statement-1 is false, statement-2 is true."],c:3,s:`Statement-2 is true: the resistance of a metal rises with temperature.
At the null point X/S = l/(100 \u2212 l). If the null point is to stay at the same place, the ratio X/S must stay the same.
Heating the unknown resistance increases X, so S must also be increased in the same proportion, not decreased.
So Statement-1 (decrease S) is false.
This is option (d).`},{pn:344,y:"IIT-JEE 2005",tp:"ckir",st:"Metre bridge",q:"The circuit shown in the figure is used to measure the value of an unknown resistance X by comparing it with a known resistance R with three different known resistances. R = R\u2081, R = R\u2082 and R = R\u2083. The corresponding null points are obtained at A, B and C. Find which of the above will give the most accurate reading and why?",a:"R = R\u2082 (null point at B, near the middle of the wire), because then the percentage error in X is smallest.",s:`At the null point X/R = l/(100 \u2212 l), so X = R l/(100 \u2212 l).
If the length l has a small error \u0394l, the fractional error in X is \u0394X/X = \u0394l/l + \u0394l/(100 \u2212 l) = 100 \u0394l/[l(100 \u2212 l)].
For a given \u0394l this is smallest when the product l(100 \u2212 l) is largest, that is when l = 50 cm (the middle of the wire).
In the figure B is near the middle of the wire, while A and C are near the ends where the error would be large.
So the reading with R = R\u2082 (null point at B) is the most accurate.`,fig:{w:340,h:225,alt:"Circuit with a battery and rheostat feeding a bridge wire; unknown resistance X and known resistance R in series, a galvanometer from their junction to a jockey touching the bridge wire; points A, B and C marked along the wire",d:[["l",40,50,40,190],["l",40,50,60,50],["l",60,38,60,62],["l",66,44,66,56,"B"],["l",66,50,80,50],["p","M80 50 L105 50 L110.8 45 L122.5 55 L134.2 45 L145.8 55 L157.5 45 L169.2 55 L175 50 L200 50"],["l",200,50,215,50],["l",300,14,190,14],["l",300,14,300,190],["l",190,14,190,45,"a"],["l",40,110,70,110],["r",70,97,40,26],["t",90,114,"X","ms"],["l",110,110,150,110],["r",210,97,40,26],["t",230,114,"R","ms"],["l",150,110,210,110],["l",250,110,300,110],["l",150,110,150,135],["c",150,147,11],["t",150,151,"G","ms"],["l",150,158,138,184,"a"],["l",40,190,300,190],["c",90,190,2.6,"F"],["c",170,190,2.6,"F"],["c",250,190,2.6,"F"],["t",90,208,"A","m"],["t",170,208,"B","m"],["t",250,208,"C","m"],["c",150,110,2.6,"F"]]}},{pn:345,y:"IIT-JEE 2003",tp:"cpot",st:"Potentiometer",q:"How a battery is to be connected so that the shown rheostat will behave like a potential divider? Also indicate the points about which output can be taken.",a:"Connect the battery across the two fixed ends A and B of the rheostat; take the output between the sliding contact C and either end (A or B).",s:`A rheostat has two fixed end terminals A and B (the full resistance R between them) and a sliding contact C.
To work as a potential divider the whole resistance must be connected across the battery, so the battery goes across A and B (see the figure below). The same current then flows along the whole length.
The voltage between the slider C and one end is a fraction of the battery voltage, set by the slider position, so the output is taken between C and A (or between C and B).
Moving the slider changes this output smoothly from 0 to the full battery voltage.`,fig:{w:300,h:170,alt:"A rheostat: resistance R between terminals A and B, with a sliding contact connected to terminal C",d:[["c",30,120,2.6,"F"],["p","M30 120 L95 120 L100.8 115 L112.5 125 L124.2 115 L135.8 125 L147.5 115 L159.2 125 L165 120 L230 120"],["c",230,120,2.6,"F"],["t",30,142,"A","m"],["t",230,142,"B","m"],["t",130,142,"R","m"],["c",260,35,2.6,"F"],["l",260,35,110,35],["l",110,35,110,100,"a"],["t",272,39,"C",""]]},sfig:{w:300,h:200,alt:"The battery is joined across the two fixed ends A and B of the rheostat. The output is taken between the sliding contact C and end A.",d:[["c",30,100,2.6,"F"],["p","M30 100 L95 100 L100.8 95 L112.5 105 L124.2 95 L135.8 105 L147.5 95 L159.2 105 L165 100 L230 100"],["c",230,100,2.6,"F"],["t",17,122,"A","m"],["t",230,122,"B","m"],["t",130,122,"R","m"],["l",30,100,30,165],["l",30,165,142,165],["l",142,153,142,177],["l",148,158,148,172,"B"],["l",148,165,230,165],["l",230,165,230,100],["t",145,192,"battery","m"],["l",130,28,130,95,"a"],["c",130,28,2.6,"F"],["c",30,28,2.6,"F"],["l",30,28,30,100],["t",130,16,"C","m"],["t",80,22,"output","m"]]}},{pn:346,y:"IIT-JEE 2002",tp:"ckir",st:"Metre bridge",q:`A thin uniform wire AB of length 1 m, an unknown resistance X and a resistance of 12 \u03A9 are connected by thick conducting strips, as shown in the figure. A battery and a galvanometer (with a sliding jockey connected to it) are also available. Connections are to be made to measure the unknown resistance X using the principle of Wheatstone bridge. Answer the following questions.
(a) Are there positive and negative terminals on the galvanometer?
(b) Copy the figure in your answer book and show the battery and the galvanometer (with jockey) connected at appropriate points.
(c) After appropriate connections are made, it is found that no deflection takes place in the galvanometer when the sliding jockey touches the wire at a distance of 60 cm from A. Obtain the value of the resistance of X.`,a:"(a) No fixed positive or negative terminals: it is a centre-zero null detector. (b) Battery across A and B (A is joined to D by the thick strips); galvanometer from the junction C of X and 12 \u03A9 to the jockey on wire AB. (c) X = 8 \u03A9.",s:`(a) The galvanometer is only used to see whether current flows, and in which direction. It is a centre-zero instrument with no marked + and \u2212 terminals; the pointer deflects either way.
(b) The thick strips join A and D, so A and D are the same point. Connect the battery between B and A. Then there are two paths from B to A: along the wire B \u2192 A, and through X and then 12 \u03A9 (B \u2192 C \u2192 D = A).
Connect the galvanometer between C (the junction of X and 12 \u03A9) and the jockey on wire AB (see the figure below).
(c) At balance the bridge condition is R_BJ/R_JA = X/12. The jockey is 60 cm from A, so JA = 60 cm and BJ = 40 cm of the same uniform wire.
X/12 = 40/60, so X = 12 \xD7 2/3 = 8 \u03A9.`,fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"A thin wire from A to B, then unknown resistance X from B to C and 12 ohm from C to D; thick strips join A and D along the top",d:[["r",30,30,16,115],["r",290,30,16,115],["r",30,30,276,12],["l",46,125,126,125],["r",126,121,8,8],["p","M134 125 L147 125 L150.3 120 L157 130 L163.7 120 L170.3 130 L177 120 L183.7 130 L187 125 L200 125"],["t",167,113,"X","m"],["r",200,121,8,8],["p","M208 125 L228 125 L231.3 120 L238 130 L244.7 120 L251.3 130 L258 120 L264.7 130 L268 125 L288 125"],["t",248,113,"12 \u03A9","m"],["c",291,125,2.6,"F"],["t",38,163,"A","m"],["t",130,143,"B","m"],["t",204,143,"C","m"],["t",298,163,"D","m"]]},sfig:{w:340,h:210,alt:"Battery joined between A and B; galvanometer G joined from the junction C of X and 12 ohm to a sliding jockey touching wire AB",d:[["r",30,30,16,115],["r",290,30,16,115],["r",30,30,276,12],["l",46,125,126,125],["r",126,121,8,8],["p","M134 125 L147 125 L150.3 120 L157 130 L163.7 120 L170.3 130 L177 120 L183.7 130 L187 125 L200 125"],["t",167,113,"X","m"],["r",200,121,8,8],["p","M208 125 L228 125 L231.3 120 L238 130 L244.7 120 L251.3 130 L258 120 L264.7 130 L268 125 L288 125"],["t",248,113,"12 \u03A9","m"],["c",291,125,2.6,"F"],["t",20,163,"A","m"],["t",120,143,"B","m"],["t",204,143,"C","m"],["t",298,163,"D","m"],["l",38,145,38,175],["l",38,175,78,175],["l",78,167,78,183],["l",84,170,84,180,"B"],["l",84,175,130,175],["l",130,129,130,175],["t",107,198,"battery","m"],["l",204,121,204,70],["l",204,70,160,70],["c",150,70,10],["t",150,74,"G","m"],["l",140,70,94,70],["l",94,70,94,121,"a"],["t",100,60,"jockey","m"]]}},{pn:347,y:"IIT-JEE 1996",tp:"ckir",st:"Kirchhoff's laws",q:"An electrical circuit is shown in the figure. Calculate the potential difference across the resistor of 400 ohm, as will be measured by the voltmeter V of resistance 400 ohm, either by applying Kirchhoff's rules or otherwise.",a:"About 2.22 V (20/9 V)",s:`Name the nodes: L (left), R (right), P (between the two upper 100 \u03A9 resistors) and F (far right end of the 200 \u03A9). The 10 V cell is across L and R and is treated as ideal, as no internal resistance is given.
Between P and F there are two paths in parallel: the 400 \u03A9 resistor and the voltmeter of 400 \u03A9. 400 \u2225 400 = 200 \u03A9.
From P to R there are two paths: the direct 100 \u03A9, and the path through (200 \u03A9) + the 200 \u03A9 resistor = 400 \u03A9. 100 \u2225 400 = 80 \u03A9 (solution figure).
The middle 100 \u03A9 resistor (L to R) is directly across the cell and carries its own current, which does not affect the others.
Upper arm: 100 + 80 = 180 \u03A9, so I\u2082 = 10/180 = 1/18 A, and V_P \u2212 V_R = (1/18) \xD7 80 = 4.44 V.
The path P \u2192 F \u2192 R is 400 \u03A9 in all, so its current is 4.44/400 = 11.1 mA.
The voltage across the 400 \u03A9 resistor (and the voltmeter in parallel with it) is 11.1 mA \xD7 200 \u03A9 = 2.22 V.`,fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"Circuit: a 10 V cell across a left node and a right node. Between them a 100 ohm resistor; and a 100 ohm resistor in series with a second 100 ohm resistor, the junction of these two connected through a 400 ohm resistor and a 400 ohm voltmeter in parallel to the far end of a 200 ohm resistor joined to the right node",d:[["p","M40 70 L53 70 L56.7 65 L64 75 L71.3 65 L78.7 75 L86 65 L93.3 75 L97 70 L110 70"],["t",75,60,"100 \u03A9","m"],["p","M110 70 L133 70 L136.7 65 L144 75 L151.3 65 L158.7 75 L166 65 L173.3 75 L177 70 L200 70"],["t",155,60,"100 \u03A9","m"],["p","M200 70 L228 70 L231.7 65 L239 75 L246.3 65 L253.7 75 L261 65 L268.3 75 L272 70 L300 70"],["t",250,60,"200 \u03A9","m"],["l",110,70,110,35],["p","M110 35 L155 35 L163.3 30 L180 40 L196.7 30 L213.3 40 L230 30 L246.7 40 L255 35 L300 35"],["t",150,27,"400 \u03A9","m"],["l",300,35,300,70],["c",110,70,2.6,"F"],["l",110,35,110,10],["l",110,10,193,10],["c",205,10,12],["t",205,14,"V","ms"],["l",217,10,300,10],["l",300,10,300,35],["c",110,35,2.6,"F"],["c",300,35,2.6,"F"],["p","M40 120 L90 120 L95 115 L105 125 L115 115 L125 125 L135 115 L145 125 L150 120 L200 120"],["t",120,110,"100 \u03A9","m"],["l",40,70,40,165],["l",200,70,200,165],["c",40,120,2.6,"F"],["c",200,120,2.6,"F"],["c",200,70,2.6,"F"],["l",40,165,117,165],["l",117,154,117,176],["l",123,159,123,171,"B"],["l",123,165,200,165],["t",120,188,"10 V","m"]]},sfig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"Reduced circuit: 100 ohm in series with 80 ohm in one arm, 100 ohm in the other arm, across the 10 V cell",d:[["p","M40 50 L60 50 L63.3 45 L70 55 L76.7 45 L83.3 55 L90 45 L96.7 55 L100 50 L120 50"],["t",80,40,"100 \u03A9","m"],["p","M120 50 L160 50 L165 45 L175 55 L185 45 L195 55 L205 45 L215 55 L220 50 L260 50"],["t",190,40,"80 \u03A9","m"],["p","M40 105 L120 105 L125 100 L135 110 L145 100 L155 110 L165 100 L175 110 L180 105 L260 105"],["t",150,95,"100 \u03A9","m"],["l",40,50,40,160],["l",260,50,260,160],["c",120,50,2.6,"F"],["c",40,105,2.6,"F"],["c",260,105,2.6,"F"],["l",40,160,147,160],["l",147,149,147,171],["l",153,154,153,166,"B"],["l",153,160,260,160],["t",150,184,"10 V","m"],["t",190,72,"(100 \u03A9 \u2225 400 \u03A9)","m"]]}},{pn:348,y:"IIT-JEE 1978",tp:"ccmb",st:"Balanced networks and symmetry",q:"If each of the resistances in the network shown in the figure is R, what is the resistance between the terminals A and B?",a:"R",s:`The network is a tetrahedron P, Q, V and O made of resistors, but the edge between O and V is open at the terminals A and B. So five resistors R are present: PQ, PO, PV, QO and QV.
Terminal A is on O and B is on V, so we need the resistance between O and V.
By symmetry P and Q are at the same potential: each is joined to O by R and to V by R. So no current flows in the resistor PQ.
Remove PQ: there are two paths from O to V, O \u2192 P \u2192 V (2R) and O \u2192 Q \u2192 V (2R).
They are in parallel: (2R \xD7 2R)/(2R + 2R) = R.
Note: the same result follows from a balanced Wheatstone bridge with PQ as the galvanometer arm.`,fig:{w:260,h:200,alt:"Tetrahedron of five equal resistors R between four corners P, Q, V and O; the sixth edge between O and V is open at the terminals A and B",d:[["p","M50 150 L70.3 113.7 L68.4 106.9 L82 103 L78.2 89.4 L91.8 85.6 L88 72 L101.6 68.1 L99.7 61.3 L120 25"],["p","M120 25 L143.8 62.2 L150.7 63.7 L147.7 77.6 L161.5 80.6 L158.5 94.4 L172.3 97.4 L169.3 111.3 L176.2 112.8 L200 150"],["p","M50 150 L95 150 L100 145 L110 155 L120 145 L130 155 L140 145 L150 155 L155 150 L200 150"],["p","M120 25 L119.1 40 L123.8 44.5 L113.3 52.2 L122.8 61.2 L112.2 68.8 L121.7 77.8 L111.2 85.5 L115.9 90 L115 105"],["p","M50 150 L61.9 141.7 L62.5 135.2 L75.1 138.7 L76.2 125.8 L88.8 129.2 L89.9 116.3 L102.5 119.8 L103.1 113.3 L115 105"],["l",115,105,150,121],["l",168,129,200,150],["c",150,121,2.6,"F"],["c",168,129,2.6,"F"],["t",148,138,"A","e"],["t",179,124,"B","m"],["t",42,156,"P","e"],["t",120,14,"Q","m"],["t",208,156,"V",""],["t",108,104,"O","e"],["t",68,84,"R","e"],["t",170,84,"R",""],["t",125,174,"R","m"],["t",128,64,"R",""],["t",58,128,"R","e"]]}},{pn:349,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 2 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"The battery of a mobile phone is rated as 4.2 V, 5800 mAh. How much energy is stored in it when fully charged?",o:["87.7 kJ","24.4 kJ","48.7 kJ","43.8 kJ"],c:0,s:`Energy stored = voltage \xD7 charge, U = V q.
Charge: 5800 mAh = 5.8 A h = 5.8 \xD7 3600 C = 20880 C (1 h = 3600 s).
U = 4.2 \xD7 20880 = 87696 J \u2248 87.7 kJ.
This is option (1).`},{pn:350,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"There are 'n' number of identical electric bulbs, each is designed to draw a power p independently from the mains supply. They are now joined in series across the mains supply. The total power drawn by the combination is",o:["p/n","p/n\xB2","p","np"],c:0,s:`A bulb designed for power p on the mains voltage V has resistance R = V\xB2/p.
n bulbs in series have resistance nR = nV\xB2/p.
Power drawn from the same supply: P = V\xB2/(nR) = V\xB2p/(nV\xB2) = p/n.
This is option (1).`},{pn:351,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"An electric bulb rated 50 W - 200 V is connected across a 100 V supply. The power dissipation of the bulb is",o:["50 W","100 W","12.5 W","25 W"],c:2,s:`The rating gives the resistance of the filament: R = V\xB2/P = 200\xB2/50 = 800 \u03A9.
We assume R stays the same at the lower voltage.
Power at 100 V: P\u2032 = V\u2032\xB2/R = 100\xB2/800 = 12.5 W.
Shortcut: P \u221D V\xB2, and 100 V is half of 200 V, so P is a quarter: 50/4 = 12.5 W.
This is option (3).`},{pn:352,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 5 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"The ratio of heat dissipated per second through the resistance 5 \u03A9 and 10 \u03A9 in the circuit given below is",o:["2 : 1","1 : 2","1 : 1","4 : 1"],c:0,s:`The 5 \u03A9 and 10 \u03A9 resistors are in parallel, so the same voltage V acts across both.
Heat per second in a resistor is P = V\xB2/R.
P\u2085 : P\u2081\u2080 = (V\xB2/5) : (V\xB2/10) = 10 : 5 = 2 : 1.
The 20 \u03A9 resistor and the 10 V value are not needed for this ratio.
This is option (1).`,fig:{w:330,h:190,alt:"A 10 V cell, a 20 ohm resistor in series with a parallel pair of 5 ohm and 10 ohm resistors",d:[["l",40,60,60,60],["p","M60 60 L75 60 L78.3 55 L85 65 L91.7 55 L98.3 65 L105 55 L111.7 65 L115 60 L130 60"],["t",95,50,"20 \u03A9","m"],["l",130,60,150,60],["l",150,40,150,80],["l",240,40,240,80],["p","M150 40 L181 40 L183.3 35 L188 45 L192.7 35 L197.3 45 L202 35 L206.7 45 L209 40 L240 40"],["p","M150 80 L181 80 L183.3 75 L188 85 L192.7 75 L197.3 85 L202 75 L206.7 85 L209 80 L240 80"],["t",195,33,"5 \u03A9","m"],["t",195,97,"10 \u03A9","m"],["c",150,60,2.6,"F"],["c",240,60,2.6,"F"],["l",240,60,280,60],["l",40,60,40,160],["l",280,60,280,160],["l",40,160,147,160],["l",147,149,147,171],["l",153,154,153,166,"B"],["l",153,160,280,160],["t",150,184,"10 V","m"]]}},{pn:353,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"The number of electrons flowing per second in the filament of a 110 W bulb operating at 220 V is (Given e = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 C)",o:["1.25 \xD7 10\xB9\u2079","6.25 \xD7 10\xB9\u2078","6.25 \xD7 10\xB9\u2077","31.25 \xD7 10\xB9\u2077"],c:3,s:`Power P = VI, so the current is I = P/V = 110/220 = 0.5 A.
Current is charge per second, so electrons per second n = I/e.
n = 0.5/(1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079) = 3.125 \xD7 10\xB9\u2078 = 31.25 \xD7 10\xB9\u2077.
This is option (4).`},{pn:354,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cheat",st:"Joule heating and fuses",q:"Water boils in an electric kettle in 20 minutes after being switched on. Using the same main supply, the length of the heating element should be ____ to ____ times of its initial length if the water is to be boiled in 15 minutes.",o:["increased, 3/4","decreased, 3/4","increased, 4/3","decreased, 4/3"],c:1,s:`The same water must receive the same heat H, and H = (V\xB2/R) t with V fixed. So t is proportional to R.
The heating wire has R = \u03C1l/A, so R is proportional to its length l (same wire and area).
l\u2032/l = R\u2032/R = t\u2032/t = 15/20 = 3/4.
The time is shorter, so the element must be shorter: decreased to 3/4 of its length.
This is option (2).`},{pn:355,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cheat",st:"Joule heating and fuses",q:"When a potential difference V is applied across a wire of resistance R, it dissipates energy at a rate W. If the wire is cut into two halves and these halves are connected mutually parallel across the same supply, the energy dissipation rate will become",o:["4W","2W","(1)/(4)W","(1)/(2)W"],c:0,s:`The rate of energy dissipation is W = V\xB2/R.
Each half has R/2 (resistance is proportional to length).
Two halves in parallel: R\u2032 = (R/2)/2 = R/4.
W\u2032 = V\xB2/(R/4) = 4V\xB2/R = 4W.
This is option (1).`},{pn:356,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"By what percentage will the illumination of the lamp decrease if the current drops by 20%?",o:["56%","46%","26%","36%"],c:3,s:`The brightness (illumination) of a lamp depends on its power, P = I\xB2R, and R is taken as constant.
The current becomes 0.8 I, so P\u2032 = (0.8 I)\xB2R = 0.64 P.
The power falls by 1 \u2212 0.64 = 0.36, that is 36%.
This is option (4).
The question does not give the lamp data, so we take the lamp resistance as constant.`},{pn:357,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 11 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cheat",st:"Joule heating and fuses",q:"Two identical heater filaments are connected first in parallel and then in series. At the same applied voltage, the ratio of heat produced in same time for parallel to series will be",o:["4 : 1","2 : 1","1 : 2","1 : 4"],c:0,s:`Heat in time t at voltage V is H = V\xB2t/R_eq.
Parallel: R_eq = R/2, so H_p = 2V\xB2t/R.
Series: R_eq = 2R, so H_s = V\xB2t/(2R).
H_p/H_s = (2/R)/(1/(2R)) = 4.
The ratio is 4 : 1, option (1).`},{pn:358,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 13 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"Different combinations of 3 resistors of equal resistance R are shown in the figures (the same current I enters each). The increasing order for power dissipation is",o:["P_C < P_D < P_A < P_B","P_A < P_B < P_C < P_D","P_B < P_C < P_D < P_A","P_C < P_B < P_A < P_D"],c:3,s:`The same current I flows into each combination, so the power is P = I\xB2R_eq: it is larger for a larger equivalent resistance.
(A) Two R in parallel give R/2, plus R in series: R_eq = 3R/2.
(B) Two R in series (2R) in parallel with R: (2R \xD7 R)/(3R) = 2R/3.
(C) Three R in parallel: R/3.
(D) Three R in series: 3R.
Order of R_eq: C (R/3) < B (2R/3) < A (3R/2) < D (3R).
So P_C < P_B < P_A < P_D, option (4).`,fig:{w:300,h:300,alt:"Four combinations of three equal resistors R with the same current I: (A) two in parallel then one in series, (B) two in series in parallel with one, (C) three in parallel, (D) three in series",d:[["l",8,40,30,40,"a"],["t",14,32,"I","m"],["t",8,14,"(A)",""],["l",30,40,60,40],["l",60,26,60,54],["l",150,26,150,54],["p","M60 26 L91 26 L93.3 21 L98 31 L102.7 21 L107.3 31 L112 21 L116.7 31 L119 26 L150 26"],["p","M60 54 L91 54 L93.3 49 L98 59 L102.7 49 L107.3 59 L112 49 L116.7 59 L119 54 L150 54"],["t",105,19,"R","m"],["t",105,71,"R","m"],["l",150,40,190,40],["p","M190 40 L210 40 L213.3 35 L220 45 L226.7 35 L233.3 45 L240 35 L246.7 45 L250 40 L270 40"],["t",230,30,"R","m"],["l",8,110,30,110,"a"],["t",14,102,"I","m"],["t",8,80,"(B)",""],["l",30,110,60,110],["l",60,95,60,125],["l",190,95,190,125],["p","M60 95 L78.5 95 L80.8 90 L85.5 100 L90.2 90 L94.8 100 L99.5 90 L104.2 100 L106.5 95 L125 95"],["p","M125 95 L143.5 95 L145.8 90 L150.5 100 L155.2 90 L159.8 100 L164.5 90 L169.2 100 L171.5 95 L190 95"],["t",92,87,"R","m"],["t",158,87,"R","m"],["p","M60 125 L105 125 L108.3 120 L115 130 L121.7 120 L128.3 130 L135 120 L141.7 130 L145 125 L190 125"],["t",125,117,"R","m"],["l",190,110,230,110],["c",230,110,2.6,"F"],["l",8,195,30,195,"a"],["t",14,187,"I","m"],["t",8,160,"(C)",""],["l",30,195,60,195],["l",60,167,60,223],["l",190,167,190,223],["p","M60 167 L105 167 L108.3 162 L115 172 L121.7 162 L128.3 172 L135 162 L141.7 172 L145 167 L190 167"],["p","M60 195 L105 195 L108.3 190 L115 200 L121.7 190 L128.3 200 L135 190 L141.7 200 L145 195 L190 195"],["p","M60 223 L105 223 L108.3 218 L115 228 L121.7 218 L128.3 228 L135 218 L141.7 228 L145 223 L190 223"],["t",125,159,"R","m"],["t",125,187,"R","m"],["t",125,215,"R","m"],["l",190,195,230,195],["c",230,195,2.6,"F"],["l",8,270,30,270,"a"],["t",14,262,"I","m"],["t",8,244,"(D)",""],["p","M30 270 L52 270 L55 265 L61 275 L67 265 L73 275 L79 265 L85 275 L88 270 L110 270"],["p","M110 270 L132 270 L135 265 L141 275 L147 265 L153 275 L159 265 L165 275 L168 270 L190 270"],["p","M190 270 L212 270 L215 265 L221 275 L227 265 L233 275 L239 265 L245 275 L248 270 L270 270"],["t",70,262,"R","m"],["t",150,262,"R","m"],["t",230,262,"R","m"],["c",270,40,2.6,"F"]]}},{pn:359,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cheat",st:"Joule heating and fuses",q:"Ratio of thermal energy released in two resistors R and 3R connected in parallel in an electric circuit is",o:["1 : 3","3 : 1","1 : 1","1 : 27"],c:1,s:`In parallel both resistors have the same voltage V, and the same time t.
Thermal energy H = V\xB2t/R, so H is inversely proportional to R.
H_R : H_3R = (1/R) : (1/3R) = 3 : 1.
This is option (2).`},{pn:360,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cheat",st:"Joule heating and fuses",q:"The H amount of thermal energy is developed by a resistor in 10 s when a current of 4 A is passed through it. If the current is increased to 16 A, the thermal energy developed by the resistor in 10 s will be",o:["H/4","H","4H","16H"],c:3,s:`Joule heating is H = I\xB2Rt, with R and t unchanged.
So H is proportional to I\xB2.
The current becomes 16/4 = 4 times, so H becomes 4\xB2 = 16 times.
This is option (4): 16H.`},{pn:361,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"An electric appliance supplies 6000 J per min heat to the system. If the system delivers a power of 90 W, how long it would take to increase the internal energy by 2.5 \xD7 10\xB3 J?",o:["2.5 \xD7 10\xB9 s","2.5 \xD7 10\xB2 s","2.4 \xD7 10\xB3 s","4.1 \xD7 10\xB9 s"],c:1,s:`Heat supplied per second = 6000 J / 60 s = 100 W.
The system gives out 90 W, so the net power going into its internal energy is 100 \u2212 90 = 10 W.
Time = energy/power = 2.5 \xD7 10\xB3 J / 10 W = 250 s = 2.5 \xD7 10\xB2 s.
So the answer is option (b).`},{pn:362,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"In the given figure, the emf of the cell is 2.2 V and if internal resistance is 0.6 \u03A9. Calculate the power dissipated in the whole circuit.",fig:{w:330,h:240,alt:"The two left corners are joined (terminal A) and the two right corners are joined (terminal B). Between A and B: 4 ohm on top, 8 ohm at the bottom, and two crossing diagonals: 4 ohm then 8 ohm, and 2 ohm then 4 ohm. A 2.2 V cell with internal resistance 0.6 ohm drives them.",d:[["l",70,40,70,130],["l",250,40,250,130],["p","M70,40 L109.6,40 L126.4,44.5 L143.2,35.5 L160,44.5 L176.8,35.5 L193.6,44.5 L210.4,40 L250,40"],["p","M70,130 L109.6,130 L126.4,134.5 L143.2,125.5 L160,134.5 L176.8,125.5 L193.6,134.5 L210.4,130 L250,130"],["p","M70,40 L87.6,48.8 L97.2,57.5 L111.6,56.9 L119.6,68.7 L132.4,71.2 L150,80"],["l",150,80,170,90],["p","M170,90 L187.6,98.8 L197.2,107.5 L211.6,106.9 L219.6,118.7 L232.4,121.2 L250,130"],["p","M70,130 L87.6,121.2 L100.4,118.7 L108.4,106.9 L122.8,107.5 L132.4,98.8 L150,90"],["l",150,90,155,87.5],["p","M155,87.5 A6,6 0 0 1 165,82.5"],["l",165,82.5,170,80],["p","M170,80 L187.6,71.2 L200.4,68.7 L208.4,56.9 L222.8,57.5 L232.4,48.8 L250,40"],["l",70,40,30,40],["l",30,40,30,190],["l",30,190,147,190],["l",147,180,147,200],["l",153,184.5,153,195.5,"B"],["l",153,190,290,190],["l",290,190,290,40],["l",290,40,250,40],["t",45,32,"A","m"],["t",275,32,"B","m"],["t",160,30,"4 \u03A9","m"],["t",160,152,"8 \u03A9","m"],["t",110,84,"4 \u03A9","m"],["t",112,104,"2 \u03A9","m"],["t",212,78,"4 \u03A9","m"],["t",212,100,"8 \u03A9","m"],["t",160,214,"2.2 V, r = 0.6 \u03A9","m"]]},o:["1.32 W","4.4 W","0.65 W","2.2 W"],c:3,sfig:{w:300,h:210,alt:"Equivalent circuit: four paths in parallel between A and B with resistances 4, 8, 12 and 6 ohm",d:[["c",25,100,3],["t",25,88,"A","m"],["l",28,100,60,100],["l",60,30,60,170],["l",220,30,220,170],["l",220,100,247,100],["c",250,100,3],["t",250,88,"B","m"],["c",60,100,2.4,"F"],["c",220,100,2.4,"F"],["p","M60,30 L95.2,30 L110.1,34.5 L125.1,25.5 L140,34.5 L154.9,25.5 L169.9,34.5 L184.8,30 L220,30"],["p","M60,77 L95.2,77 L110.1,81.5 L125.1,72.5 L140,81.5 L154.9,72.5 L169.9,81.5 L184.8,77 L220,77"],["p","M60,123 L95.2,123 L110.1,127.5 L125.1,118.5 L140,127.5 L154.9,118.5 L169.9,127.5 L184.8,123 L220,123"],["p","M60,170 L95.2,170 L110.1,174.5 L125.1,165.5 L140,174.5 L154.9,165.5 L169.9,174.5 L184.8,170 L220,170"],["t",140,22,"4 \u03A9","m"],["t",140,69,"8 \u03A9","m"],["t",140,115,"4 \u03A9 + 8 \u03A9 = 12 \u03A9","m"],["t",140,162,"2 \u03A9 + 4 \u03A9 = 6 \u03A9","m"]]},s:`The two left corners are joined by a wire and so are the two right corners, so all six resistors are connected between the same two points A and B.
The crossing diagonals are 4 \u03A9 + 8 \u03A9 = 12 \u03A9 and 2 \u03A9 + 4 \u03A9 = 6 \u03A9. Together with 4 \u03A9 (top) and 8 \u03A9 (bottom) there are four parallel paths (see the figure below).
1/R = 1/4 + 1/8 + 1/12 + 1/6 = (6 + 3 + 2 + 4)/24 = 15/24, so R = 1.6 \u03A9.
Total resistance = 1.6 + 0.6 (internal) = 2.2 \u03A9, so I = 2.2/2.2 = 1 A.
Power in the whole circuit = EI = 2.2 \xD7 1 = 2.2 W.
Note: if the two diagonals were joined at the centre instead, the result is the same 1.6 \u03A9, so the small arc where the two diagonals cross without touching does not change the answer.
So the answer is option (d).`},{pn:363,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Aug \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"An electric bulb of 500 watt at 100 volt is used in a circuit having a 200 V supply. Calculate the resistance R to be connected in series with the bulb so that the power delivered by the bulb is 500 W.",o:["30 \u03A9","5 \u03A9","20 \u03A9","10 \u03A9"],c:2,s:`For the bulb to give 500 W it must run at its rating: 100 V across it.
Bulb current I = P/V = 500/100 = 5 A (bulb resistance 100/5 = 20 \u03A9).
The series resistor must take the remaining 200 \u2212 100 = 100 V at the same 5 A.
R = 100/5 = 20 \u03A9.
So the answer is option (c).`},{pn:364,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 1 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cheat",st:"Joule heating and fuses",q:"Due to cold weather a 1 m water pipe of cross-sectional area 1 cm\xB2 is filled with ice at \u221210 \xB0C. Resistive heating is used to melt the ice. Current of 0.5 A is passed through 4 k\u03A9 resistance. Assuming that all the heat produced is used for melting, what is the minimum time required? (Given latent heat of fusion for water/ice = 3.33 \xD7 10\u2075 J kg\u207B\xB9, specific heat of ice = 2 \xD7 10\xB3 J kg\u207B\xB9 and density of ice = 10\xB3 kg/m\xB3)",o:["3.53 s","0.353 s","35.3 s","70.6 s"],c:2,s:`Volume of ice = 1 m \xD7 1 cm\xB2 = 1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 m\xB3, so mass m = 10\xB3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 = 0.1 kg.
Heat to warm the ice from \u221210 \xB0C to 0 \xB0C: Q\u2081 = mc\u0394T = 0.1 \xD7 2 \xD7 10\xB3 \xD7 10 = 2000 J.
Heat to melt it: Q\u2082 = mL = 0.1 \xD7 3.33 \xD7 10\u2075 = 33300 J.
Total heat Q = 35300 J.
Heating power = I\xB2R = (0.5)\xB2 \xD7 4000 = 1000 W.
Time = Q/P = 35300/1000 = 35.3 s, option (c).`},{pn:365,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 16 Mar \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cheat",st:"Joule heating and fuses",q:"A resistor develops 500 J of thermal energy in 20 s when a current of 1.5 A is passed through it. If the current is increased from 1.5 A to 3 A, what will be the energy developed in 20 s.",o:["1000 J","500 J","1500 J","2000 J"],c:3,s:`Heat developed H = I\xB2Rt. R and t stay the same, so H is proportional to I\xB2.
The current doubles (1.5 A to 3 A), so H becomes 2\xB2 = 4 times.
H = 4 \xD7 500 = 2000 J, option (d).`},{pn:366,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 3 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ccel",st:"Maximum power transfer",q:"Model a torch battery of length l to be made up of a thin cylindrical bar of radius a and a concentric thin cylindrical shell of radius b filled in between with an electrolyte of resistivity \u03C1 (see figure). If the battery is connected to a resistance of value R, the maximum Joule heating in R will take place for",fig:{w:300,h:210,alt:"A cylindrical battery of length l: a thin central rod of radius a and a coaxial outer shell of radius b with electrolyte of resistivity rho between them",d:[["p","M70,50 A70,14 0 1 1 210,50 A70,14 0 1 1 70,50"],["l",70,50,70,170],["l",210,50,210,170],["p","M70,170 A70,14 0 0 0 210,170"],["r",132,50,16,122,"f"],["p","M132,50 A8,3 0 1 1 148,50 A8,3 0 1 1 132,50"],["p","M132,172 A8,3 0 0 0 148,172"],["t",100,115,"\u03C1","m"],["t",185,115,"\u03C1","m"],["l",245,50,245,170,"b"],["t",258,114,"l"],["l",140,50,210,50,"a"],["t",224,42,"b","m"],["l",140,28,140,44],["t",140,22,"a","m"]]},o:["R = \u03C1/(2\u03C0l) \xB7 (b/a)","R = \u03C1/(2\u03C0l) \xB7 ln(b/a)","R = \u03C1/(\u03C0l) \xB7 ln(b/a)","R = 2\u03C1/(\u03C0l) \xB7 ln(b/a)"],c:1,s:`The electrolyte between the rod and the shell is the internal resistance r of the battery. Current flows radially outward.
Take a thin cylindrical layer of radius x and thickness dx: its resistance is dr = \u03C1 dx/(2\u03C0xl) (length dx, area 2\u03C0xl).
Add up all layers from a to b: r = (\u03C1/2\u03C0l) \u222B dx/x = (\u03C1/2\u03C0l) ln(b/a).
The power in the external resistor R is greatest when R equals the internal resistance (maximum power transfer).
So R = (\u03C1/2\u03C0l) ln(b/a), option (b).`},{pn:367,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 4 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ccel",st:"EMF and internal resistance",q:"A battery of 3.0 V is connected to a resistor dissipating 0.5 W of power. If the terminal voltage of the battery is 2.5 V, the power dissipated within the internal resistance is",o:["0.50 W","0.072 W","0.125 W","0.10 W"],c:3,s:`The terminal voltage is the voltage across the external resistor: V = 2.5 V.
Current I = P/V = 0.5/2.5 = 0.2 A.
The voltage lost inside the battery is 3.0 \u2212 2.5 = 0.5 V, so the internal resistance is r = 0.5/0.2 = 2.5 \u03A9.
Power in r: I\xB2r = (0.2)\xB2 \xD7 2.5 = 0.10 W (or 0.5 V \xD7 0.2 A = 0.10 W).
So the answer is option (d).`},{pn:368,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 5 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"An electrical power line, having a total resistance of 2 \u03A9, delivers 1 kW at 220 V. The efficiency of the transmission line is approximately",o:["72%","91%","85%","96%"],c:3,s:`Current delivered: I = P/V = 1000/220 = 4.55 A.
Power lost in the line: I\xB2R = (4.55)\xB2 \xD7 2 = 41.3 W.
Power put into the line = 1000 + 41.3 = 1041.3 W.
Efficiency = output/input = 1000/1041.3 = 0.96 = 96%.
So the answer is option (d).`},{pn:369,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 7 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cheat",st:"Joule heating and fuses",q:"In a building, there are 15 bulbs of 45 W, 15 bulbs of 100 W, 15 small fans of 10 W and 2 heaters of 1 kW. The voltage of electric main is 220 V. The minimum capacity (rated value) of the building fuse will be",o:["20 A","25 A","10 A","15 A"],c:0,s:`The fuse must carry the total current when everything is on, so add all the powers.
Bulbs: 15 \xD7 45 = 675 W and 15 \xD7 100 = 1500 W. Fans: 15 \xD7 10 = 150 W. Heaters: 2 \xD7 1000 = 2000 W.
Total = 675 + 1500 + 150 + 2000 = 4325 W.
I = P/V = 4325/220 = 19.66 A.
The fuse must be rated at least this, so the nearest rating is 20 A, option (a).`},{pn:370,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ccel",st:"Maximum power transfer",q:"A cell of internal resistance r drives current through an external resistance R. The power delivered by the cell to the external resistance will be maximum when",o:["R = 0.001 r","R = r","R = 2r","R = 1000 r"],c:1,s:`Current I = E/(R + r), so the power in R is P = I\xB2R = E\xB2R/(R + r)\xB2.
For the maximum, set dP/dR = 0: (R + r)\xB2 \u2212 2R(R + r) = 0, which gives (R + r) \u2212 2R = 0, so R = r.
The power is greatest when the external resistance equals the internal resistance, option (b).`},{pn:371,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"The resistive network shown below is connected to a D.C. source of 16 V. The power consumed by the network is 4 watt. The value of R is",fig:{w:370,h:200,alt:"A 16 V source feeding in series: a pair of 4R and 4R in parallel, a resistor R, a pair of 6R and 12R in parallel, and a resistor R",d:[["l",40,80,70,80],["l",40,80,40,150],["l",70,60,70,100],["l",130,60,130,100],["p","M70,60 L79,60 L89.5,63.5 L100,56.5 L110.5,63.5 L121,60 L130,60"],["p","M70,100 L79,100 L89.5,103.5 L100,96.5 L110.5,103.5 L121,100 L130,100"],["c",70,80,2.4,"F"],["c",130,80,2.4,"F"],["p","M130,80 L136,80 L148,83.5 L160,76.5 L172,83.5 L184,80 L190,80"],["l",190,60,190,100],["l",250,60,250,100],["p","M190,60 L199,60 L209.5,63.5 L220,56.5 L230.5,63.5 L241,60 L250,60"],["p","M190,100 L199,100 L209.5,103.5 L220,96.5 L230.5,103.5 L241,100 L250,100"],["c",190,80,2.4,"F"],["c",250,80,2.4,"F"],["p","M250,80 L257,80 L271,83.5 L285,76.5 L299,83.5 L313,80 L320,80"],["l",320,80,320,150],["l",40,150,177,150],["l",177,140,177,160],["l",183,144.5,183,155.5,"B"],["l",183,150,320,150],["t",100,52,"4R","m"],["t",100,120,"4R","m"],["t",160,70,"R","m"],["t",220,52,"6R","m"],["t",220,120,"12R","m"],["t",285,70,"R","m"],["t",180,178,"\u03B5 = 16 V","m"]]},o:["1 \u03A9","6 \u03A9","8 \u03A9","16 \u03A9"],c:2,s:`The network is four parts in series: (4R \u2225 4R), R, (6R \u2225 12R) and R.
4R \u2225 4R = (4R \xD7 4R)/(8R) = 2R.
6R \u2225 12R = (6R \xD7 12R)/(18R) = 4R.
Total resistance = 2R + R + 4R + R = 8R.
Power P = V\xB2/R_total, so 4 = 256/(8R), which gives 8R = 64 and R = 8 \u03A9.
So the answer is option (c).`},{pn:372,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cres",st:"Colour code of resistors",q:"A 2 W carbon resistor is colour coded with green, black, red and brown respectively. The maximum current which can be passed through this resistor is",o:["20 mA","0.4 mA","100 mA","63 mA"],c:0,s:`Read the colour bands: green = 5, black = 0, red = multiplier 10\xB2, brown = tolerance \xB11%.
So R = 50 \xD7 10\xB2 = 5000 \u03A9.
The power rating limits the current: P = I\xB2R, so I = \u221A(P/R) = \u221A(2/5000) = \u221A(4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074) = 0.02 A.
The maximum current is 20 mA, option (a).`},{pn:373,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"A current of 2 mA was passed through an unknown resistor which dissipated a power of 4.4 W. Dissipated power when an ideal power supply of 11 V is connected across it is",o:["11 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 W","11 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 W","11 \xD7 10\u2075 W","11 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 W"],c:1,s:`From P = I\xB2R: R = P/I\xB2 = 4.4/(2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3)\xB2 = 4.4/(4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076) = 1.1 \xD7 10\u2076 \u03A9.
With 11 V across it, P = V\xB2/R = 121/(1.1 \xD7 10\u2076) = 1.1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 W = 11 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 W.
So the answer is option (b).`},{pn:374,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 11 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"Two equal resistances when connected in series to a battery, consume electric power of 60 W. If these resistances are now connected in parallel combination to the same battery, the electric power consumed will be",o:["240 W","120 W","60 W","30 W"],c:0,s:`Let each resistance be R. In series the total is 2R, so P = V\xB2/(2R) = 60 W.
In parallel the total is R/2, so P\u2032 = V\xB2/(R/2) = 2V\xB2/R.
Compare: P\u2032/P = (2V\xB2/R)/(V\xB2/2R) = 4.
P\u2032 = 4 \xD7 60 = 240 W, option (a).`},{pn:375,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"Two electric bulbs, rated at (25 W, 220 V) and (100 W, 220 V), are connected in series across a 220 V voltage source. If the 25 W and 100 W bulbs draw powers P\u2081 and P\u2082 respectively, then",o:["P\u2081 = 9 W, P\u2082 = 16 W","P\u2081 = 16 W, P\u2082 = 4 W","P\u2081 = 4 W, P\u2082 = 16 W","P\u2081 = 16 W, P\u2082 = 9 W"],c:1,s:`Resistance of each bulb, R = V\xB2/P: R\u2081 = 220\xB2/25 = 1936 \u03A9 and R\u2082 = 220\xB2/100 = 484 \u03A9.
In series they carry the same current: I = 220/(1936 + 484) = 220/2420 = 1/11 A.
P\u2081 = I\xB2R\u2081 = 1936/121 = 16 W.
P\u2082 = I\xB2R\u2082 = 484/121 = 4 W.
So P\u2081 = 16 W and P\u2082 = 4 W, option (b).`},{pn:376,y:"JEE Main 2019",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"A current carrying wire heats a metal rod. The wire provides a constant power (P) to the rod. The metal rod is enclosed in an insulated container. It is observed that the temperature (T) in the metal rod changes with time (t) as T(t) = T\u2080(1 + \u03B2t^(1/4)), where \u03B2 is a constant with appropriate dimension while T\u2080 is a constant with dimension of temperature. The heat capacity of metal is,",o:["4P(T(t) \u2212 T\u2080)\u2074/(\u03B2\u2074T\u2080\u2075)","4P(T(t) \u2212 T\u2080)\xB3/(\u03B2\u2074T\u2080\u2074)","4P(T(t) \u2212 T\u2080)/(\u03B2\u2074T\u2080\xB2)","4P(T(t) \u2212 T\u2080)\xB2/(\u03B2\u2074T\u2080\xB3)"],c:1,s:`The power supplied is the heat capacity times the rate of rise of temperature: P = C dT/dt, so C = P/(dT/dt).
dT/dt = T\u2080\u03B2 \xD7 (1/4) t^(\u22123/4).
So C = 4P t^(3/4)/(\u03B2 T\u2080).
Express t in terms of T: T \u2212 T\u2080 = T\u2080\u03B2t^(1/4), so t^(1/4) = (T \u2212 T\u2080)/(\u03B2T\u2080) and t^(3/4) = (T \u2212 T\u2080)\xB3/(\u03B2\xB3T\u2080\xB3).
C = 4P (T \u2212 T\u2080)\xB3/(\u03B2\u2074T\u2080\u2074).
So the answer is option (b).`},{pn:377,y:"JEE Main 2018 \xB7 Online",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"A constant voltage is applied between two ends of a metallic wire. If the length is halved and the radius of the wire is doubled, the rate of heat developed in the wire will be",o:["increased 8 times","unchanged","doubled","halved"],c:0,s:`Rate of heat = V\xB2/R, and R = \u03C1l/A = \u03C1l/(\u03C0r\xB2).
The length becomes l/2 and the radius 2r, so the area becomes 4 times.
R\u2032 = \u03C1(l/2)/(4\u03C0r\xB2) = R/8.
With V fixed, power P = V\xB2/R\u2032 = 8 \xD7 V\xB2/R.
The heat rate increases 8 times, option (a).`},{pn:378,y:"JEE Main 2017 \xB7 Online",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"The figure shows three circuits I, II and III which are connected to a 3 V battery. If the powers dissipated by the configurations I, II and III are P\u2081, P\u2082 and P\u2083 respectively, then",fig:{w:580,h:200,alt:"Three circuits I, II and III of 1 ohm resistors, each connected to a 3 V battery",d:[["l",70,20,150,20],["l",150,20,150,70],["l",140,75,160,75],["l",144.5,81,155.5,81,"B"],["l",150,84,150,160],["l",70,160,150,160],["p","M70,20 L70,23.5 L66,30.5 L74,37.5 L66,44.5 L70,51.5 L70,55"],["p","M70,55 L70,59.5 L66,68.5 L74,77.5 L66,86.5 L70,95.5 L70,100"],["p","M70,100 L70,104.5 L66,113.5 L74,122.5 L66,131.5 L70,140.5 L70,145"],["l",70,145,70,160],["p","M110,20 L110,32 L106,43.2 L114,54.4 L106,65.6 L114,76.8 L110,88 L110,100"],["l",110,100,70,100],["l",70,55,40,55],["p","M40,55 L40,68.5 L36,81.1 L44,93.7 L36,106.3 L44,118.9 L40,131.5 L40,145"],["l",40,145,70,145],["c",70,20,2.4,"F"],["c",70,55,2.4,"F"],["c",70,100,2.4,"F"],["c",70,145,2.4,"F"],["t",80,42,"1 \u03A9"],["t",80,82,"1 \u03A9"],["t",80,130,"1 \u03A9"],["t",118,62,"1 \u03A9"],["t",32,104,"1 \u03A9","e"],["t",158,82,"3 V"],["t",90,185,"I","m"],["l",265,20,340,20],["l",340,20,340,70],["l",330,75,350,75],["l",334.5,81,345.5,81,"B"],["l",340,84,340,160],["l",265,145,265,160],["l",265,160,340,160],["p","M265,20 L259.6,27.4 L248.2,37.2 L245.3,53.1 L231.1,60.7 L225.4,74.6 L220,82"],["p","M220,82 L225.4,89.6 L231.1,103.6 L245.3,111.5 L248.2,127.5 L259.6,137.4 L265,145"],["p","M265,20 L265,38.8 L261,56.3 L269,73.8 L261,91.3 L269,108.8 L265,126.3 L265,145"],["p","M265,20 L270.4,27.4 L276.1,41.3 L290.3,48.9 L293.2,64.8 L304.6,74.6 L310,82"],["p","M310,82 L304.6,89.6 L293.2,99.5 L290.3,115.5 L276.1,123.4 L270.4,137.4 L265,145"],["c",265,20,2.4,"F"],["c",265,145,2.4,"F"],["t",213,50,"1 \u03A9","e"],["t",213,118,"1 \u03A9","e"],["t",273,86,"1 \u03A9"],["t",301,48,"1 \u03A9"],["t",301,122,"1 \u03A9"],["t",348,82,"3 V"],["t",275,185,"II","m"],["l",445,25,445,10],["l",445,10,525,10],["l",525,10,525,70],["l",515,75,535,75],["l",519.5,81,530.5,81,"B"],["l",525,84,525,160],["l",445,160,525,160],["p","M445,25 L440.2,29.8 L430.1,34.9 L427.5,47.5 L414.9,50.1 L409.8,60.2 L405,65"],["p","M445,25 L449.8,29.8 L454.9,39.9 L467.5,42.5 L470.1,55.1 L480.2,60.2 L485,65"],["p","M405,65 L409.8,69.8 L414.9,79.9 L427.5,82.5 L430.1,95.1 L440.2,100.2 L445,105"],["p","M485,65 L480.2,69.8 L470.1,74.9 L467.5,87.5 L454.9,90.1 L449.8,100.2 L445,105"],["p","M445,105 L445,111.8 L441,118.1 L449,124.4 L441,130.7 L449,137 L445,143.3 L445,150"],["l",445,150,445,160],["c",445,25,2.4,"F"],["c",445,105,2.4,"F"],["t",402,38,"1 \u03A9","e"],["t",488,38,"1 \u03A9"],["t",402,96,"1 \u03A9","e"],["t",488,96,"1 \u03A9"],["t",455,132,"1 \u03A9"],["t",533,82,"3 V"],["t",460,185,"III","m"]]},o:["P\u2083 > P\u2082 > P\u2081","P\u2082 > P\u2081 > P\u2083","P\u2081 > P\u2083 > P\u2082","P\u2081 > P\u2082 > P\u2083"],c:1,s:`All resistors are 1 \u03A9 and the voltage is 3 V, so P = V\xB2/R_eq = 9/R_eq: the smaller the equivalent resistance, the larger the power.
Circuit I (see the figure below): call the top node T, the bottom node B, and the two middle junctions A and M. The resistors are T\u2013A, A\u2013M, M\u2013B, T\u2013M and A\u2013B (1 \u03A9 each). Take V_T \u2212 V_B = 1 (a scale). By symmetry V_A = V_M = \xBD, so the two resistors leaving T (T\u2013A and T\u2013M) each carry \xBD and the total current is 1, so R_eq = 1 \u03A9 and P\u2081 = 9 W.
Circuit II: left path 1 + 1 = 2 \u03A9, middle 1 \u03A9, right path 1 + 1 = 2 \u03A9 in parallel: 1/R = \xBD + 1 + \xBD = 2, so R_eq = 0.5 \u03A9 and P\u2082 = 18 W.
Circuit III: the four 1 \u03A9 in the diamond make two parallel paths of 2 \u03A9 each = 1 \u03A9, in series with another 1 \u03A9: R_eq = 2 \u03A9, P\u2083 = 4.5 W.
So P\u2082 > P\u2081 > P\u2083, option (b).`,sfig:{w:300,h:195,alt:"Circuit I redrawn with its nodes: T at the top, A and M in the middle, B at the bottom; five 1 ohm resistors T-A, T-M, A-M, A-B and M-B",d:[["p","M150.0 28.0 L123.0 48.1 L117.0 46.3 L117.0 58.8 L105.0 55.3 L105.0 67.7 L93.0 64.2 L93.0 76.7 L87.0 74.9 L60.0 95.0"],["t",74.2,57.5,"1 \u03A9","m"],["p","M150.0 28.0 L177.0 48.1 L177.0 54.3 L189.0 50.8 L189.0 63.3 L201.0 59.7 L201.0 72.2 L213.0 68.7 L213.0 74.9 L240.0 95.0"],["t",225.8,57.5,"1 \u03A9","m"],["p","M60.0 95.0 L114.0 95.0 L120.0 100.0 L132.0 90.0 L144.0 100.0 L156.0 90.0 L168.0 100.0 L180.0 90.0 L186.0 95.0 L240.0 95.0"],["t",150,85,"1 \u03A9","m"],["p","M60.0 95.0 L87.0 115.1 L87.0 121.3 L99.0 117.8 L99.0 130.3 L111.0 126.7 L111.0 139.2 L123.0 135.7 L123.0 141.9 L150.0 162.0"],["t",74.2,142.5,"1 \u03A9","m"],["p","M240.0 95.0 L213.0 115.1 L207.0 113.3 L207.0 125.8 L195.0 122.3 L195.0 134.7 L183.0 131.2 L183.0 143.7 L177.0 141.9 L150.0 162.0"],["t",225.8,142.5,"1 \u03A9","m"],["c",150,28,2.6,"F"],["c",60,95,2.6,"F"],["c",240,95,2.6,"F"],["c",150,162,2.6,"F"],["t",150,16,"T","m"],["t",150,182,"B","m"],["t",46,99,"A","m"],["t",254,99,"M","m"]]}},{pn:379,y:"JEE Main 2016 \xB7 Online",tp:"ccel",st:"Maximum power transfer",q:"In the circuit shown, the resistance r is a variable resistance. If for r = fR, the heat generation in r is maximum then the value of f is",fig:{w:310,h:180,alt:"A battery drives a resistor R in series with a parallel pair: a resistor R and a variable resistor r",d:[["l",40,60,50,60],["p","M50,60 L59,60 L67.4,64 L75.8,56 L84.2,64 L92.6,56 L101,60 L110,60"],["l",110,60,130,60],["c",130,60,2.4,"F"],["l",130,40,130,80],["l",230,40,230,80],["p","M130,40 L152,40 L161.3,44.5 L170.7,35.5 L180,44.5 L189.3,35.5 L198.7,44.5 L208,40 L230,40"],["p","M130,80 L152,80 L161.3,84.5 L170.7,75.5 L180,84.5 L189.3,75.5 L198.7,84.5 L208,80 L230,80"],["l",155,92,205,68,"a"],["c",230,60,2.4,"F"],["l",230,60,250,60],["l",250,60,250,140],["l",40,60,40,140],["l",40,140,147,140],["l",147,130,147,150],["l",153,134.5,153,145.5,"B"],["l",153,140,250,140],["t",80,48,"R","m"],["t",180,30,"R","m"],["t",180,103,"r","m"]]},o:["1/2","1","1/4","3/4"],c:0,s:`Let the emf be E. The resistor R in series is joined to the parallel pair R and r.
Parallel part: R_p = Rr/(R + r). Voltage across it: V = E R_p/(R + R_p) = E R r/(R(R + r) + Rr) = E r/(R + 2r).
Power in r: P = V\xB2/r = E\xB2 r/(R + 2r)\xB2.
For the maximum, dP/dr = 0: (R + 2r)\xB2 \u2212 4r(R + 2r) = 0, so R + 2r \u2212 4r = 0, giving r = R/2.
So f = 1/2, option (a).`},{pn:380,y:"JEE Main 2016 \xB7 Online",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:`The resistance of an electrical toaster has a temperature dependence given by R(T) = R\u2080[1 + \u03B1(T \u2212 T\u2080)] in its range of operation. At T\u2080 = 300 K, R = 100 \u03A9 and at T = 500 K, R = 120 \u03A9. The toaster is connected to a voltage source at 200 V and its temperature is raised at a constant rate from 300 to 500 K in 30 s. The total work done in raising the temperature is
(a) 400 ln(5/6) J
(b) 200 ln(2/3) J
(c) 300 J
(d) 400 ln(1.5/1.3) J`,a:"None of the printed options matches: W = 6 \xD7 10\u2074 ln 1.2 \u2248 1.1 \xD7 10\u2074 J",s:`Note: no option matches; the printed key is blank. Working gives W = 6 \xD7 10\u2074 ln(1.2) J \u2248 1.09 \xD7 10\u2074 J.
From the two readings: 120 = 100[1 + \u03B1(200)], so \u03B1 = 1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 per K.
The temperature rises at a constant rate, dT/dt = 200/30 K/s, so dt = (30/200) dT = 0.15 dT.
The electrical work is W = \u222B (V\xB2/R) dt = \u222B V\xB2 dt/(R\u2080[1 + \u03B1(T \u2212 T\u2080)]).
Substitute: W = (V\xB2/R\u2080) \xD7 0.15 \xD7 \u222B from 0 to 200 of dx/(1 + \u03B1x), where x = T \u2212 T\u2080.
The integral is (1/\u03B1) ln(1 + 200\u03B1) = 1000 ln 1.2.
V\xB2/R\u2080 = 200\xB2/100 = 400 W, so W = 400 \xD7 0.15 \xD7 1000 \xD7 ln 1.2 = 6 \xD7 10\u2074 ln 1.2 \u2248 1.09 \xD7 10\u2074 J.`},{pn:381,y:"JEE Main 2014",tp:"cheat",st:"Joule heating and fuses",q:"In a large building, there are 15 bulbs of 40 W, 5 bulbs of 100 W, 5 fans of 80 W and 1 heater of 1 kW. The voltage of the electric mains is 220 V. The minimum capacity of the main fuse of the building will be",o:["14 A","8 A","10 A","12 A"],c:3,s:`The fuse must carry the current when all appliances are on, so add the powers.
Bulbs: 15 \xD7 40 = 600 W and 5 \xD7 100 = 500 W. Fans: 5 \xD7 80 = 400 W. Heater: 1000 W.
Total = 600 + 500 + 400 + 1000 = 2500 W.
I = P/V = 2500/220 = 11.4 A.
The smallest fuse rating above this is 12 A, option (d).`},{pn:382,y:"JEE Main 2014 \xB7 Online",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"Four bulbs B\u2081, B\u2082, B\u2083 and B\u2084 of 100 W each are connected to 220 V main as shown in the figure. The reading in an ideal ammeter (as shown) will be",fig:{w:360,h:190,alt:"A 220 V source across four 100 W bulbs B1 to B4 in parallel; an ammeter sits in the bottom wire between the first and second bulb",d:[["l",40,30,290,30],["l",40,130,110,130],["l",110,130,130,130],["c",140,130,10],["t",140,134,"A","m"],["l",150,130,170,130],["l",170,130,290,130],["l",40,30,40,66],["c",40,80,14],["p","M32,80 C36,73 40,73 40,80 S44,87 48,80"],["l",40,94,40,130],["l",110,30,110,70],["c",110,80,10],["l",103,73,117,87],["l",103,87,117,73],["l",110,90,110,130],["c",110,130,2.4,"F"],["t",124,100,"B\u2081"],["l",170,30,170,70],["c",170,80,10],["l",163,73,177,87],["l",163,87,177,73],["l",170,90,170,130],["c",170,130,2.4,"F"],["t",184,100,"B\u2082"],["l",230,30,230,70],["c",230,80,10],["l",223,73,237,87],["l",223,87,237,73],["l",230,90,230,130],["c",230,130,2.4,"F"],["t",244,100,"B\u2083"],["l",290,30,290,70],["c",290,80,10],["l",283,73,297,87],["l",283,87,297,73],["l",290,90,290,130],["c",290,130,2.4,"F"],["t",304,100,"B\u2084"],["t",50,112,"220 V"],["t",140,156,"Ammeter","m"]]},o:["0.45 A","0.90 A","1.35 A","1.80 A"],c:2,s:`The four bulbs are in parallel across 220 V, so each draws I = P/V = 100/220 = 0.4545 A.
The ammeter is in the bottom wire between the first and the second bulb. All the current returning from B\u2082, B\u2083 and B\u2084 passes through it, but the current of B\u2081 returns to the source before the ammeter.
Reading = 3 \xD7 0.4545 = 1.36 A, close to 1.35 A.
So the answer is option (c).`},{pn:383,y:"JEE Main 2013",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"The supply voltage to a room is 120 V. The resistance of the lead wires is 6 \u03A9. A 60 W bulb is already switched on. What is the decrease of voltage across the bulb, when a 240 W heater is switched on in parallel to the bulb?",o:["10.04 Volt","zero Volt","2.9 Volt","13.3 Volt"],c:0,s:`Note: the printed key is (a) 10.04 V; working gives about 10.4 V, and (a) is the nearest option.
Bulb resistance: R_b = V\xB2/P = 120\xB2/60 = 240 \u03A9. Heater resistance: R_h = 120\xB2/240 = 60 \u03A9.
Bulb alone: it is in series with the 6 \u03A9 lead wires, so V\u2081 = 120 \xD7 240/(240 + 6) = 117.07 V.
With the heater: 240 \u03A9 \u2225 60 \u03A9 = 48 \u03A9, so V\u2082 = 120 \xD7 48/(48 + 6) = 106.67 V.
Decrease = 117.07 \u2212 106.67 = 10.4 V, closest to option (a).`},{pn:384,y:"JEE Main 2013 \xB7 Online",tp:"cheat",st:"Joule heating and fuses",q:"Which of the four resistances P, Q, R and S generate the greatest amount of heat when a current flows from A to B? P = 2 \u03A9, Q = 4 \u03A9, R = 1 \u03A9, S = 2 \u03A9",fig:{w:330,h:170,alt:"From A the current splits into an upper path with P = 2 ohm and Q = 4 ohm in series and a lower path with R = 1 ohm and S = 2 ohm in series, which meet again at B",d:[["l",30,85,90,85,"a"],["l",90,85,120,55],["p","M120,55 L124,55 L132,59 L140,51 L148,59 L156,55 L160,55"],["l",160,55,180,55],["p","M180,55 L184,55 L192,59 L200,51 L208,59 L216,55 L220,55"],["l",220,55,240,55],["l",240,55,270,85],["l",90,85,120,115],["p","M120,115 L124,115 L132,119 L140,111 L148,119 L156,115 L160,115"],["l",160,115,180,115],["p","M180,115 L184,115 L192,119 L200,111 L208,119 L216,115 L220,115"],["l",220,115,240,115],["l",240,115,270,85],["l",270,85,310,85],["c",90,85,2.4,"F"],["c",270,85,2.4,"F"],["t",30,105,"A","m"],["t",305,105,"B","m"],["t",140,40,"P = 2 \u03A9","m"],["t",200,40,"Q = 4 \u03A9","m"],["t",140,140,"R = 1 \u03A9","m"],["t",200,140,"S = 2 \u03A9","m"]]},o:["Q","S","P","R"],c:1,s:`P and Q are in series on the upper path, R and S in series on the lower path; the two paths are in parallel between A and B and so have the same voltage V.
Upper path: 2 + 4 = 6 \u03A9, current I = V/6. Lower path: 1 + 2 = 3 \u03A9, current 2I = V/3.
Heat in each resistor H = I\xB2R: P = I\xB2 \xD7 2 = 2I\xB2, Q = I\xB2 \xD7 4 = 4I\xB2, R = (2I)\xB2 \xD7 1 = 4I\xB2, S = (2I)\xB2 \xD7 2 = 8I\xB2.
S produces the most heat, option (b).
Note: the straight line through the middle of the printed figure is only the A-to-B axis; if it were a real wire it would short the network and no resistor would heat.`},{pn:385,y:"JEE Main 2013",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"The supply voltage to a room is 120 V. The resistance of the lead wires is 6 \u03A9. A 60 W bulb is already switched on. What is the decrease of voltage across the bulb, when a 240 W heater is switched on in parallel to the bulb?",o:["10.04 Volt","zero Volt","2.9 Volt","13.3 Volt"],c:0,s:`Note: the printed option is 10.04 V; working gives 10.4 V, so (a) is the intended answer and the printed digits look like a misprint.
A bulb marked 60 W for 120 V has resistance R = V\xB2/P = 120\xB2/60 = 240 \u03A9. The heater (240 W) has R = 120\xB2/240 = 60 \u03A9. We assume these ratings are for 120 V.
Only the bulb on: the lead wires (6 \u03A9) and the bulb are in series, so the voltage across the bulb is 120 \xD7 240/(240 + 6) = 117.07 V.
With the heater in parallel: the bulb and heater together give (240 \xD7 60)/(240 + 60) = 48 \u03A9. Voltage across them = 120 \xD7 48/(48 + 6) = 106.67 V.
Decrease of voltage across the bulb = 117.07 \u2212 106.67 = 10.4 V.
This is closest to option (a).`},{pn:386,y:"AIEEE 2012",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"Two electric bulbs marked 25 W-220 V and 100 W-220 V are connected in series to a 440 V supply. Which of the bulbs will fuse?",o:["100 W","25 W","neither","both"],c:1,s:`Find the resistance of each bulb from R = V\xB2/P. The 25 W bulb: 220\xB2/25 = 1936 \u03A9. The 100 W bulb: 220\xB2/100 = 484 \u03A9.
In series the same current flows through both: I = 440/(1936 + 484) = 440/2420 = 0.18 A.
The safe (rated) current of each bulb is P/V: for 25 W, 25/220 = 0.114 A; for 100 W, 100/220 = 0.45 A.
The current 0.18 A is more than the 0.114 A that the 25 W bulb can carry, but less than the 0.45 A of the 100 W bulb.
Note: equivalently, the 25 W bulb has the larger resistance, so it gets the larger share of the 440 V (more than its rated 220 V).
So the 25 W bulb fuses, option (b).`},{pn:387,y:"IIT-JEE 2010",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"Incandescent bulbs are designed by keeping in mind that the resistance of their filament increases with the increase in temperature. If at room temperature, 100 W, 60 W and 40 W bulbs have filament resistances R\u2081\u2080\u2080, R\u2086\u2080 and R\u2084\u2080 respectively, the relation between these resistances is",o:["1/R\u2081\u2080\u2080 = 1/R\u2084\u2080 + 1/R\u2086\u2080","R\u2081\u2080\u2080 = R\u2084\u2080 + R\u2086\u2080","R\u2081\u2080\u2080 > R\u2086\u2080 > R\u2084\u2080","1/R\u2081\u2080\u2080 > 1/R\u2086\u2080 > 1/R\u2084\u2080"],c:3,s:`All three bulbs are designed for the same voltage V, so the power at working temperature is P = V\xB2/R, giving R = V\xB2/P: the larger the power, the smaller the hot resistance.
All three filaments are tungsten, and the higher-power bulb has the shorter and thicker filament. From R = \u03C1l/A, a shorter, thicker filament has less resistance at room temperature too.
So at room temperature R\u2081\u2080\u2080 < R\u2086\u2080 < R\u2084\u2080.
Taking reciprocals reverses the inequality: 1/R\u2081\u2080\u2080 > 1/R\u2086\u2080 > 1/R\u2084\u2080.
Option (d).`},{pn:388,y:"IIT-JEE 2008",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"Figure shows three resistor configurations R\u2081, R\u2082 and R\u2083 connected to a 3 V battery. If the power dissipated by the configurations R\u2081, R\u2082 and R\u2083 is P\u2081, P\u2082 and P\u2083 respectively, then",o:["P\u2081 > P\u2082 > P\u2083","P\u2081 > P\u2083 > P\u2082","P\u2082 > P\u2081 > P\u2083","P\u2083 > P\u2082 > P\u2081"],c:2,s:`The same 3 V battery is connected to each configuration, so the power is P = V\xB2/R = 9/R. The smaller the equivalent resistance, the larger the power.
R\u2081 (solution figure, redrawn as a bridge): call the nodes T (top), M\u2081 and M\u2082 (middle) and B (bottom). Resistors: T-M\u2081, M\u2081-M\u2082, M\u2082-B, T-M\u2082 and M\u2081-B, all 1 \u03A9. The route T-M\u2081-B is 2 \u03A9 and the route T-M\u2082-B is 2 \u03A9, so M\u2081 and M\u2082 are at the same potential and the M\u2081-M\u2082 resistor carries no current. Then 2 \u03A9 \u2225 2 \u03A9 = 1 \u03A9, so P\u2081 = 9/1 = 9 W.
R\u2082: the diamond gives two routes of 2 \u03A9 each (through the left and right corners), and the vertical 1 \u03A9 is in parallel: 1/R = 1/2 + 1/2 + 1/1 = 2, so R = 0.5 \u03A9 and P\u2082 = 9/0.5 = 18 W.
R\u2083: the diamond alone is 2 \u03A9 \u2225 2 \u03A9 = 1 \u03A9, plus the 1 \u03A9 in series gives 2 \u03A9, so P\u2083 = 9/2 = 4.5 W.
So P\u2082 (18 W) > P\u2081 (9 W) > P\u2083 (4.5 W), option (c).`,fig:{w:600,h:240,alt:"Three resistor networks R1, R2 and R3 made of 1 ohm resistors, each connected to a 3 volt battery",d:[["c",76,35,2.2,"F"],["c",76,85,2.2,"F"],["c",76,130,2.2,"F"],["c",76,175,2.2,"F"],["l",76,35,76,46],["p","M76 46 L72 48.3 L80 53 L72 57.7 L80 62.3 L72 67 L80 71.7 L76 74"],["l",76,74,76,85],["l",76,85,76,93.5],["p","M76 93.5 L72 95.8 L80 100.5 L72 105.2 L80 109.8 L72 114.5 L80 119.2 L76 121.5"],["l",76,121.5,76,130],["l",76,130,76,138.5],["p","M76 138.5 L72 140.8 L80 145.5 L72 150.2 L80 154.8 L72 159.5 L80 164.2 L76 166.5"],["l",76,166.5,76,175],["l",76,35,116,35],["l",116,35,116,65.5],["p","M116 65.5 L112 68.3 L120 74 L112 79.7 L120 85.3 L112 91 L120 96.7 L116 99.5"],["l",116,99.5,116,130],["l",116,130,76,130],["l",76,85,38,85],["l",38,85,38,113],["p","M38 113 L34 115.8 L42 121.5 L34 127.2 L42 132.8 L34 138.5 L42 144.2 L38 147"],["l",38,147,38,175],["l",38,175,76,175],["t",68,62,"1 \u03A9","e"],["t",68,112,"1 \u03A9","e"],["t",84,158,"1 \u03A9",""],["t",124,86,"1 \u03A9",""],["t",30,134,"1 \u03A9","e"],["l",76,35,76,20],["l",76,20,166,20],["l",166,20,166,95],["l",154,95,178,95],["l",160,102,172,102,"B"],["l",166,102,166,195],["l",166,195,76,195],["l",76,195,76,175],["t",182,100,"3 V",""],["t",76,222,"R\u2081","m"],["l",270,35,254.3,57],["p","M254.3 57 L249.5 56.8 L252.9 65.8 L243.3 65.5 L246.7 74.5 L237.1 74.2 L240.5 83.2 L235.7 83"],["l",235.7,83,220,105],["l",270,35,285.7,57],["p","M285.7 57 L284 61.5 L293.6 61.2 L290.2 70.2 L299.8 69.8 L296.4 78.8 L306 78.5 L304.3 83"],["l",304.3,83,320,105],["l",220,105,235.7,127],["p","M235.7 127 L234 131.5 L243.6 131.2 L240.2 140.2 L249.8 139.8 L246.4 148.8 L256 148.5 L254.3 153"],["l",254.3,153,270,175],["l",320,105,304.3,127],["p","M304.3 127 L299.5 126.8 L302.9 135.8 L293.3 135.5 L296.7 144.5 L287.1 144.2 L290.5 153.2 L285.7 153"],["l",285.7,153,270,175],["l",270,35,270,85],["p","M270 85 L266 88.3 L274 95 L266 101.7 L274 108.3 L266 115 L274 121.7 L270 125"],["l",270,125,270,175],["c",270,35,2.2,"F"],["c",270,175,2.2,"F"],["t",232,60,"1 \u03A9","e"],["t",312,60,"1 \u03A9",""],["t",232,158,"1 \u03A9","e"],["t",312,158,"1 \u03A9",""],["t",282,110,"1 \u03A9",""],["l",270,35,270,20],["l",270,20,350,20],["l",350,20,350,95],["l",338,95,362,95],["l",344,102,356,102,"B"],["l",350,102,350,195],["l",350,195,270,195],["l",270,195,270,175],["t",366,100,"3 V",""],["t",270,222,"R\u2082","m"],["l",455,35,440.2,52.7],["p","M440.2 52.7 L435.5 52.2 L438.2 61.4 L428.6 60.4 L431.4 69.6 L421.8 68.6 L424.5 77.8 L419.8 77.3"],["l",419.8,77.3,405,95],["l",455,35,469.8,52.7],["p","M469.8 52.7 L468.4 57.3 L478 56.3 L475.2 65.5 L484.8 64.5 L482 73.7 L491.6 72.7 L490.2 77.3"],["l",490.2,77.3,505,95],["l",405,95,419.8,112.7],["p","M419.8 112.7 L418.4 117.3 L428 116.3 L425.2 125.5 L434.8 124.5 L432 133.7 L441.6 132.7 L440.2 137.3"],["l",440.2,137.3,455,155],["l",505,95,490.2,112.7],["p","M490.2 112.7 L485.5 112.2 L488.2 121.4 L478.6 120.4 L481.4 129.6 L471.8 128.6 L474.5 137.8 L469.8 137.3"],["l",469.8,137.3,455,155],["l",455,155,455,160],["l",455,155,455,161],["p","M455 161 L451 163.3 L459 168 L451 172.7 L459 177.3 L451 182 L459 186.7 L455 189"],["l",455,189,455,195],["c",455,35,2.2,"F"],["c",455,155,2.2,"F"],["t",417,60,"1 \u03A9","e"],["t",497,60,"1 \u03A9",""],["t",417,148,"1 \u03A9","e"],["t",497,148,"1 \u03A9",""],["t",467,178,"1 \u03A9",""],["l",455,35,455,20],["l",455,20,535,20],["l",535,20,535,95],["l",523,95,547,95],["l",529,102,541,102,"B"],["l",535,102,535,210],["l",535,210,455,210],["l",455,210,455,195],["t",551,100,"3 V",""],["t",455,232,"R\u2083","m"]]},sfig:{w:300,h:220,alt:"R1 redrawn as a bridge: top node T, middle nodes M1 and M2, bottom node B, with 1 ohm between T-M1, T-M2, M1-M2, M1-B and M2-B",d:[["l",130,30,109,51],["p","M109.0 51.0 L103.1 49.8 L105.5 61.5 L93.8 59.1 L96.2 70.9 L84.5 68.5 L86.9 80.2 L81.0 79.0"],["l",81,79,60,100],["l",130,30,151,51],["p","M151.0 51.0 L149.8 56.9 L161.5 54.5 L159.1 66.2 L170.9 63.8 L168.5 75.5 L180.2 73.1 L179.0 79.0"],["l",179,79,200,100],["l",60,100,102,100],["p","M102.0 100.0 L106.7 105.0 L116.0 95.0 L125.3 105.0 L134.7 95.0 L144.0 105.0 L153.3 95.0 L158.0 100.0"],["l",158,100,200,100],["l",60,100,81,121],["p","M81.0 121.0 L79.8 126.9 L91.5 124.5 L89.1 136.2 L100.9 133.8 L98.5 145.5 L110.2 143.1 L109.0 149.0"],["l",109,149,130,170],["l",200,100,179,121],["p","M179.0 121.0 L173.1 119.8 L175.5 131.5 L163.8 129.1 L166.2 140.9 L154.5 138.5 L156.9 150.2 L151.0 149.0"],["l",151,149,130,170],["c",130,30,2.2,"F"],["c",60,100,2.2,"F"],["c",200,100,2.2,"F"],["c",130,170,2.2,"F"],["t",130,18,"T","m"],["t",50,104,"M\u2081","e"],["t",210,104,"M\u2082",""],["t",130,190,"B","m"],["t",82,56,"1 \u03A9","e"],["t",178,56,"1 \u03A9",""],["t",130,92,"1 \u03A9","m"],["t",82,150,"1 \u03A9","e"],["t",178,150,"1 \u03A9",""]]}},{pn:389,y:"AIEEE 2006",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"An electric bulb is rated 220 volt - 100 watt. The power consumed by it when operated on 110 volt will be",o:["50 watt","75 watt","40 watt","25 watt"],c:3,s:`First find the resistance of the filament from the rating: R = V\xB2/P = 220\xB2/100 = 484 \u03A9.
Assume R stays the same at 110 V.
Power at 110 V = V\xB2/R = 110\xB2/484 = 12100/484 = 25 W.
Note: the power depends on V\xB2, so halving the voltage cuts the power to one quarter: 100/4 = 25 W.
Option (d).`},{pn:390,y:"IIT-JEE 2006",tp:"cdrf",st:"Current density",q:"If a steady current I is flowing through a cylindrical element ABC, choose the correct relationship (AB has radius 2r and BC has radius r, each of length l/2).",o:["V_AB = 2V_BC","Power across BC is 4 times the power across AB","Current densities in AB and BC are equal","Electric field due to current inside AB and BC are equal"],c:1,s:`The same current I flows through both parts (steady current in series), but the cross-section areas differ: A_AB = \u03C0(2r)\xB2 = 4\u03C0r\xB2 and A_BC = \u03C0r\xB2. So A_AB = 4A_BC.
Both parts have length l/2 and the same material, so R = \u03C1L/A gives R_BC = 4R_AB.
Voltage V = IR, so V_BC = 4V_AB, which makes option (a) false.
Power P = I\xB2R, so P_BC = 4P_AB. Option (b) is true.
Current density j = I/A is 4 times larger in BC than in AB, so (c) is false; the field E = \u03C1j is also 4 times larger in BC, so (d) is false.
Option (b).`,fig:{w:280,h:170,alt:"A cylindrical element ABC: the part AB has radius 2r and the part BC has radius r, each of length l over 2, with current I flowing from A to C",d:[["l",40,30,140,30],["l",140,30,140,50],["l",140,50,240,50],["l",40,110,140,110],["l",140,110,140,90],["l",140,90,240,90],["l",40,30,40,110],["l",240,50,240,90],["l",40,70,240,70],["t",34,24,"A","e"],["t",140,22,"B","m"],["t",246,48,"C",""],["l",10,70,26,70],["l",26,70,36,70],["p","M36 70 L26 66 L26 74 Z","F"],["t",14,60,"I","e"],["l",90,34,90,66],["t",100,52,"2r",""],["l",190,54,190,66],["t",200,63,"r",""],["l",40,130,140,130],["t",90,148,"l/2","m"],["l",140,130,240,130],["t",190,148,"l/2","m"]]}},{pn:391,y:"AIEEE 2005",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"The resistance of hot tungsten filament is about 10 times the cold resistance. What will be the resistance of a 100 W and 200 V lamp when not in use?",o:["400 \u03A9","200 \u03A9","40 \u03A9","20 \u03A9"],c:2,s:`When the lamp works (hot), the rating gives R_hot = V\xB2/P = 200\xB2/100 = 400 \u03A9.
The hot resistance is about 10 times the cold resistance, so R_cold = 400/10 = 40 \u03A9.
Option (c).`},{pn:392,y:"AIEEE 2005",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"A heater coil is cut into two equal parts and only one part is now used in the heater. The heat generated will now be",o:["one fourth","halved","doubled","four times"],c:2,s:`The heater is connected to the same supply voltage V, so the heat produced per second is P = V\xB2/R.
The coil is cut into two equal parts, so one half has resistance R/2 (resistance is proportional to length).
New power P\u2032 = V\xB2/(R/2) = 2V\xB2/R = 2P.
So the heat generated (per unit time) is doubled, option (c).`},{pn:393,y:"IIT-JEE 2005",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"An ideal gas is filled in a closed rigid and thermally insulated container. A coil of 100 \u03A9 resistor carrying current 1 A for 5 minute supplied heat to the gas. The change in internal energy of the gas is",o:["10 kJ","30 kJ","20 kJ","0 kJ"],c:1,s:`Heat supplied by the coil: Q = I\xB2Rt = 1\xB2 \xD7 100 \xD7 (5 \xD7 60) = 100 \xD7 300 = 30000 J = 30 kJ.
The container is rigid, so the gas does no work (W = 0).
First law: \u0394U = Q \u2212 W = 30 kJ \u2212 0 = 30 kJ.
Note: "thermally insulated" only means no heat is lost to the surroundings; the heater coil itself supplies the heat.
Option (b).`},{pn:394,y:"AIEEE 2004",tp:"cheat",st:"Joule heating and fuses",q:"Time taken by a 836 W heater to heat one litre of water from 10\xB0C to 40\xB0C is",o:["50 s","100 s","150 s","200 s"],c:2,s:`One litre of water has mass 1 kg. The specific heat of water is 4180 J/(kg \xB0C).
Heat needed: Q = mc\u0394T = 1 \xD7 4180 \xD7 (40 \u2212 10) = 125400 J.
The heater supplies P = 836 J each second, so the time is t = Q/P = 125400/836 = 150 s.
Option (c).`},{pn:395,y:"AIEEE 2003",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"A 220 volt, 1000 watt bulb is connected across a 110 volt mains supply. The power consumed will be",o:["750 watt","500 watt","250 watt","1000 watt"],c:2,s:`Resistance from the rating: R = V\xB2/P = 220\xB2/1000 = 48.4 \u03A9.
On a 110 V supply the power is V\xB2/R = 110\xB2/48.4 = 12100/48.4 = 250 W.
Note: the voltage is halved, so the power falls to (1/2)\xB2 = 1/4 of 1000 W = 250 W.
Option (c).`},{pn:396,y:"IIT-JEE 2003",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"Three resistances of equal value are arranged in the different combinations as shown below. Arrange them in increasing order of power dissipation (the same current I flows into each combination).",o:["III < II < IV < I","II < III < IV < I","I < IV < III < II","I < III < II < IV"],c:0,s:`In every combination the same current I enters (this is marked on the figure), so the power is P = I\xB2R. A larger equivalent resistance means more power.
Let each resistance be R.
(I) Three in series: R_eq = 3R.
(II) Two in series (2R) in parallel with one R: (2R \xD7 R)/(2R + R) = 2R/3.
(III) Three in parallel: R/3.
(IV) Two in parallel (R/2) in series with one R: R/2 + R = 3R/2.
Increasing order of resistance (and so of power): R/3 < 2R/3 < 3R/2 < 3R, that is III < II < IV < I.
Option (a).`,fig:{w:470,h:260,alt:"Four arrangements of three equal resistors: (I) all in series, (II) two in series in parallel with one, (III) all three in parallel, (IV) two in parallel in series with one",d:[["l",10,40,40,40,"a"],["t",22,30,"I","m"],["l",40,40,60,40],["l",60,40,65,40],["p","M65 40 L67.5 44 L72.5 36 L77.5 44 L82.5 36 L87.5 44 L92.5 36 L95 40"],["l",95,40,100,40],["l",100,40,105,40],["p","M105 40 L107.5 44 L112.5 36 L117.5 44 L122.5 36 L127.5 44 L132.5 36 L135 40"],["l",135,40,140,40],["l",140,40,145,40],["p","M145 40 L147.5 44 L152.5 36 L157.5 44 L162.5 36 L167.5 44 L172.5 36 L175 40"],["l",175,40,180,40],["l",180,40,200,40],["t",100,70,"(I)","m"],["l",250,40,270,40,"a"],["t",258,56,"I","m"],["l",270,40,270,18],["l",270,18,285,18],["l",285,18,290,18],["p","M290 18 L292.5 22 L297.5 14 L302.5 22 L307.5 14 L312.5 22 L317.5 14 L320 18"],["l",320,18,325,18],["l",325,18,330,18],["p","M330 18 L332.5 22 L337.5 14 L342.5 22 L347.5 14 L352.5 22 L357.5 14 L360 18"],["l",360,18,365,18],["l",365,18,380,18],["l",380,18,380,40],["l",380,40,410,40],["l",270,40,270,62],["l",270,62,310,62],["l",310,62,315,62],["p","M315 62 L317.5 66 L322.5 58 L327.5 66 L332.5 58 L337.5 66 L342.5 58 L345 62"],["l",345,62,350,62],["l",350,62,380,62],["l",380,62,380,40],["t",330,86,"(II)","m"],["l",10,170,40,170,"a"],["t",18,186,"I","m"],["l",40,125,40,215],["l",170,125,170,215],["l",40,125,70,125],["l",70,125,89,125],["p","M89 125 L91.7 129 L97 121 L102.3 129 L107.7 121 L113 129 L118.3 121 L121 125"],["l",121,125,140,125],["l",140,125,170,125],["l",40,170,89,170],["p","M89 170 L91.7 174 L97 166 L102.3 174 L107.7 166 L113 174 L118.3 166 L121 170"],["l",121,170,170,170],["l",40,215,70,215],["l",70,215,89,215],["p","M89 215 L91.7 219 L97 211 L102.3 219 L107.7 211 L113 219 L118.3 211 L121 215"],["l",121,215,140,215],["l",140,215,170,215],["l",170,170,200,170],["t",105,238,"(III)","m"],["c",40,170,2.2,"F"],["c",170,170,2.2,"F"],["l",250,170,270,170,"a"],["t",258,186,"I","m"],["l",270,170,270,140],["l",270,140,285,140],["l",285,140,296.5,140],["p","M296.5 140 L299.2 144 L304.5 136 L309.8 144 L315.2 136 L320.5 144 L325.8 136 L328.5 140"],["l",328.5,140,340,140],["l",340,140,355,140],["l",355,140,355,170],["l",270,170,270,200],["l",270,200,285,200],["l",285,200,296.5,200],["p","M296.5 200 L299.2 204 L304.5 196 L309.8 204 L315.2 196 L320.5 204 L325.8 196 L328.5 200"],["l",328.5,200,340,200],["l",340,200,355,200],["l",355,200,355,170],["c",355,170,2.2,"F"],["l",355,170,370,170],["l",370,170,381.5,170],["p","M381.5 170 L384.2 174 L389.5 166 L394.8 174 L400.2 166 L405.5 174 L410.8 166 L413.5 170"],["l",413.5,170,425,170],["t",345,238,"(IV)","m"]]}},{pn:397,y:"AIEEE 2002",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"A wire when connected to 220 V mains supply has power dissipation P\u2081. Now the wire is cut into two equal pieces which are connected in parallel to the same supply. Power dissipation in this case is P\u2082. Then P\u2082 : P\u2081 is",o:["1","4","2","3"],c:1,s:`Power at fixed voltage V is P = V\xB2/R, so we need the new resistance.
Let the wire have resistance R. Cut into two equal pieces, each has resistance R/2 (resistance is proportional to length).
Two pieces of R/2 in parallel give (R/2)/2 = R/4.
P\u2082 = V\xB2/(R/4) = 4V\xB2/R = 4P\u2081.
So P\u2082 : P\u2081 = 4, option (b).`},{pn:398,y:"AIEEE 2002",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"If in the circuit, power dissipation is 150 W, then R is",o:["2 \u03A9","6 \u03A9","5 \u03A9","4 \u03A9"],c:1,s:`The resistors R and 2 \u03A9 are connected across the same 15 V battery, so they are in parallel.
Power from the battery = V\xB2/R_eq, so R_eq = V\xB2/P = 15\xB2/150 = 225/150 = 1.5 \u03A9.
For the parallel pair: 1/R_eq = 1/R + 1/2, so 1/R = 1/1.5 \u2212 1/2 = 2/3 \u2212 1/2 = 1/6.
So R = 6 \u03A9, option (b).`,fig:{w:290,h:190,alt:"A 15 volt battery connected across a resistor R in parallel with a 2 ohm resistor",d:[["l",30,60,60,60],["c",60,60,2.2,"F"],["l",60,60,60,30],["l",60,30,80,30],["l",80,30,117,30],["p","M117 30 L120 34 L126 26 L132 34 L138 26 L144 34 L150 26 L153 30"],["l",153,30,190,30],["t",135,18,"R","m"],["l",190,30,210,30],["c",210,60,2.2,"F"],["l",210,30,210,60],["l",60,60,60,90],["l",60,90,80,90],["l",80,90,117,90],["p","M117 90 L120 94 L126 86 L132 94 L138 86 L144 94 L150 86 L153 90"],["l",153,90,190,90],["t",135,112,"2 \u03A9","m"],["l",190,90,210,90],["l",210,90,210,60],["l",210,60,240,60],["l",30,60,30,150],["l",30,150,140,150],["l",140,138,140,162],["l",147,144,147,156,"B"],["l",147,150,240,150],["l",240,150,240,60],["t",145,174,"15 V","m"]]}},{pn:399,y:"IIT-JEE 2002",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"A 100 W bulb B\u2081 and two 60 W bulbs B\u2082 and B\u2083, are connected to a 250 V source, as shown in figure. Now W\u2081, W\u2082 and W\u2083 are the output powers of the bulbs B\u2081, B\u2082 and B\u2083, respectively. Then",o:["W\u2081 > W\u2082 = W\u2083","W\u2081 > W\u2082 > W\u2083","W\u2081 < W\u2082 = W\u2083","W\u2081 < W\u2082 < W\u2083"],c:3,s:`From the figure B\u2081 and B\u2082 are in series on one branch, and B\u2083 is on a separate branch directly across the 250 V source. The bulbs are rated for 250 V.
Resistance of each bulb from R = V\xB2/P: R\u2081 = 250\xB2/100 = 625 \u03A9, and R\u2082 = R\u2083 = 250\xB2/60 \u2248 1042 \u03A9.
B\u2083 has the full 250 V across it, so W\u2083 = 60 W.
B\u2081 and B\u2082 share the 250 V in series: current I = 250/(625 + 1042) = 0.15 A. Then W\u2081 = I\xB2R\u2081 = 14 W and W\u2082 = I\xB2R\u2082 = 23 W.
So W\u2081 (14 W) < W\u2082 (23 W) < W\u2083 (60 W).
Note: in series the bigger resistance gets more power, and a bulb with its full rated voltage is the brightest.
Option (d).`,fig:{w:300,h:190,alt:"A 250 volt source feeding two branches in parallel: one has bulbs B1 and B2 in series, the other has bulb B3",d:[["l",40,40,70,40],["c",82,40,12],["l",73.5,31.5,90.5,48.5],["l",73.5,48.5,90.5,31.5],["l",94,40,150,40],["c",162,40,12],["l",153.5,31.5,170.5,48.5],["l",153.5,48.5,170.5,31.5],["l",174,40,250,40],["t",82,20,"B\u2081","m"],["t",162,20,"B\u2082","m"],["l",40,95,70,95],["c",82,95,12],["l",73.5,86.5,90.5,103.5],["l",73.5,103.5,90.5,86.5],["l",94,95,250,95],["t",82,118,"B\u2083","m"],["l",40,40,40,150],["l",250,40,250,150],["c",40,95,2.2,"F"],["c",250,95,2.2,"F"],["l",40,150,115,150],["l",115,138,115,162],["l",122,144,122,156,"B"],["l",122,150,250,150],["t",125,174,"250 V","m"]]}},{pn:400,y:"IIT-JEE 2001",tp:"cheat",st:"Joule heating and fuses",q:"A wire of length L and three identical cells of negligible internal resistances are connected in series. Due to the current, the temperature of the wire is raised by \u0394T in a time t. A number N of similar cells are now connected in series with a wire of the same material and cross-section but of length 2L. The temperature of the wire is raised by the same amount \u0394T in the same time t. The value of N is",o:["4","6","8","9"],c:1,s:`Heat produced in time t is H = V\xB2t/R, where V is the total emf (negligible internal resistance).
The heat needed to raise the temperature by \u0394T is mc\u0394T, which is proportional to the mass, and so to the length of the wire (same cross-section and material).
First case: V = 3E, R \u221D L, heat needed \u221D L: (3E)\xB2t/R \u221D L ... call this (1).
Second case: V = NE, R\u2032 = 2R, heat needed \u221D 2L: (NE)\xB2t/(2R) \u221D 2L ... call this (2).
Divide (2) by (1): (N\xB2/9) \xD7 (R/2R) = 2, so N\xB2/18 = 2 and N\xB2 = 36.
So N = 6, option (b).`},{pn:401,y:"IIT-JEE 1995",tp:"ccel",st:"Maximum power transfer",q:"A battery of internal resistance 4 \u03A9 is connected to the network of resistances as shown in the figure. In order that the maximum power can be delivered to the network, the value of R in \u03A9 should be",o:["4/9","2","8/3","18"],c:1,s:`Maximum power is delivered to an outside circuit when its resistance equals the internal resistance of the battery. So the network must have a net resistance of 4 \u03A9.
Redraw the network as the bridge in the solution figure, with corners X (left), U (top), W (bottom), Z (right). Resistors: X-U R, X-W 2R (the two R in series), U-Z 2R (the two R in series), W-Z 4R and U-W 6R.
Check the bridge between X, U, W, Z: X-U/X-W = R/2R = 1/2 and U-Z/W-Z = 2R/4R = 1/2. The ratios are equal, so the bridge is balanced and the 6R resistor carries no current.
Path X-U-Z = R + 2R = 3R. Path X-W-Z = 2R + 4R = 6R. In parallel: (3R \xD7 6R)/(3R + 6R) = 2R.
Set 2R = 4 \u03A9, so R = 2 \u03A9, option (b).`,fig:{w:330,h:230,alt:"A battery E with 4 ohm internal resistance joined to a network of resistors R, R, R, R, R, 6R and 4R arranged as a bridge",d:[["l",50,40,90,40],["l",50,40,50,90],["l",38,96,62,96],["l",44,103,56,103,"B"],["l",50,103,50,125],["l",50,125,50,130.5],["p","M50 130.5 L47 133.5 L53 139.5 L47 145.5 L53 151.5 L50 154.5"],["l",50,154.5,50,160],["l",50,160,50,170],["t",34,100,"E","e"],["t",42,148,"4 \u03A9","e"],["l",50,170,50,200],["l",50,200,290,200],["l",290,200,290,125],["c",90,40,2.2,"F"],["l",90,40,90,40],["l",90,40,115.5,40],["p","M115.5 40 L118.3 44 L124 36 L129.7 44 L135.3 36 L141 44 L146.7 36 L149.5 40"],["l",149.5,40,175,40],["t",132,28,"R","m"],["c",175,40,2.2,"F"],["l",175,40,200.5,40],["p","M200.5 40 L203.3 44 L209 36 L214.7 44 L220.3 36 L226 44 L231.7 36 L234.5 40"],["l",234.5,40,260,40],["t",218,28,"R","m"],["l",260,40,290,40],["l",90,40,90,65.5],["p","M90 65.5 L86 68.3 L94 74 L86 79.7 L94 85.3 L86 91 L94 96.7 L90 99.5"],["l",90,99.5,90,125],["t",80,86,"R","e"],["c",90,125,2.2,"F"],["l",90,125,115.5,125],["p","M115.5 125 L118.3 129 L124 121 L129.7 129 L135.3 121 L141 129 L146.7 121 L149.5 125"],["l",149.5,125,175,125],["t",132,146,"R","m"],["c",175,125,2.2,"F"],["l",175,125,207.5,125],["p","M207.5 125 L211.7 129 L220 121 L228.3 129 L236.7 121 L245 129 L253.3 121 L257.5 125"],["l",257.5,125,290,125],["t",235,146,"4R","m"],["l",175,40,175,65.5],["p","M175 65.5 L171 68.3 L179 74 L171 79.7 L179 85.3 L171 91 L179 96.7 L175 99.5"],["l",175,99.5,175,125],["t",185,86,"6R",""],["l",290,40,290,65.5],["p","M290 65.5 L286 68.3 L294 74 L286 79.7 L294 85.3 L286 91 L294 96.7 L290 99.5"],["l",290,99.5,290,125],["t",300,86,"R",""]]},sfig:{w:300,h:220,alt:"Bridge from the solution: X to U is R, U to Z is 2R (R plus R), X to W is 2R (R plus R), W to Z is 4R, and U to W is 6R",d:[["l",40,100,67,82],["p","M67.0 82.0 L72.8 84.2 L73.2 71.8 L84.8 76.2 L85.2 63.8 L96.8 68.2 L97.2 55.8 L103.0 58.0"],["l",103,58,130,40],["l",130,40,157,58],["p","M157.0 58.0 L157.2 64.2 L168.8 59.8 L169.2 72.2 L180.8 67.8 L181.2 80.2 L192.8 75.8 L193.0 82.0"],["l",193,82,220,100],["l",40,100,67,118],["p","M67.0 118.0 L67.2 124.2 L78.8 119.8 L79.2 132.2 L90.8 127.8 L91.2 140.2 L102.8 135.8 L103.0 142.0"],["l",103,142,130,160],["l",130,160,157,142],["p","M157.0 142.0 L162.8 144.2 L163.2 131.8 L174.8 136.2 L175.2 123.8 L186.8 128.2 L187.2 115.8 L193.0 118.0"],["l",193,118,220,100],["l",130,40,130,76],["p","M130.0 76.0 L125.0 80.0 L135.0 88.0 L125.0 96.0 L135.0 104.0 L125.0 112.0 L135.0 120.0 L130.0 124.0"],["l",130,124,130,160],["c",40,100,2.2,"F"],["c",130,40,2.2,"F"],["c",130,160,2.2,"F"],["c",220,100,2.2,"F"],["t",30,104,"X","e"],["t",130,28,"U","m"],["t",130,182,"W","m"],["t",230,104,"Z",""],["t",70,56,"R","e"],["t",190,56,"2R",""],["t",70,150,"2R","e"],["t",190,150,"4R",""],["t",142,104,"6R",""]]}},{pn:402,y:"IIT-JEE 1981",tp:"cheat",st:"Joule heating and fuses",q:"In the circuit shown in fig the heat produced in the 5 ohm resistor due to the current flowing through it is 10 calorie per second. The heat generated in the 4 ohm resistor is",o:["1 calorie/sec","2 calorie/sec","3 calorie/sec","4 calorie/sec"],c:1,s:`The 4 \u03A9 and 6 \u03A9 resistors are in series on the upper branch (10 \u03A9), and this branch is in parallel with the 5 \u03A9 resistor on the lower branch. So both branches have the same voltage V across them.
For the same voltage, the heat rate P = V\xB2/R is inversely proportional to the resistance.
Upper branch (10 \u03A9) : lower branch (5 \u03A9) = 5 : 10, so the upper branch gets 5/10 of 10 cal/s = 5 cal/s.
In the upper branch the same current flows, so the heat is shared in proportion to the resistances: the 4 \u03A9 resistor gets 4/10 of 5 = 2 cal/s.
Option (b).`,fig:{w:270,h:140,alt:"Two parallel branches between two terminals: the upper branch has a 4 ohm and a 6 ohm resistor in series, the lower branch has a 5 ohm resistor",d:[["l",10,70,40,70],["l",40,70,50,70],["c",50,70,2.2,"F"],["l",50,70,50,40],["l",50,40,70,40],["l",70,40,84,40],["p","M84 40 L86.7 44 L92 36 L97.3 44 L102.7 36 L108 44 L113.3 36 L116 40"],["l",116,40,130,40],["t",100,28,"4 \u03A9","m"],["l",130,40,144,40],["p","M144 40 L146.7 44 L152 36 L157.3 44 L162.7 36 L168 44 L173.3 36 L176 40"],["l",176,40,190,40],["t",160,28,"6 \u03A9","m"],["l",190,40,210,40],["l",210,40,210,70],["c",210,70,2.2,"F"],["l",210,70,240,70],["l",50,70,50,100],["l",50,100,100,100],["l",100,100,114,100],["p","M114 100 L116.7 104 L122 96 L127.3 104 L132.7 96 L138 104 L143.3 96 L146 100"],["l",146,100,160,100],["t",130,120,"5 \u03A9","m"],["l",160,100,210,100],["l",210,100,210,70]]}},{pn:403,y:"IIT-JEE 1980",tp:"cheat",st:"Joule heating and fuses",q:"A constant voltage is applied between the two ends of a uniform metallic wire. Some heat is developed in it. The heat developed is doubled if",o:["both the length and the radius of the wire are halved.","both the length and the radius of the wire are doubled.","the radius of the wire is doubled.","the length of the wire is doubled."],c:1,s:`At constant voltage the heat produced in a given time is H = V\xB2t/R, with R = \u03C1L/(\u03C0r\xB2). So H = V\xB2t \u03C0r\xB2/(\u03C1L), that is H \u221D r\xB2/L.
(a) Length and radius halved: (\xBD)\xB2/(\xBD) = \xBD, so the heat is halved.
(b) Both doubled: (2)\xB2/2 = 2, so the heat is doubled.
(c) Radius doubled: 2\xB2 = 4, heat becomes 4 times.
(d) Length doubled: 1/2, heat is halved.
Only (b) doubles the heat.`},{pn:404,y:"JEE Advanced 2022",tp:"ccmb",st:"Series and parallel combinations",q:`In circuit-1 and circuit-2 shown in the figures, R\u2081 = 1 \u03A9, R\u2082 = 2 \u03A9 and R\u2083 = 3 \u03A9. P\u2081 and P\u2082 are the power dissipations in circuit-1 and circuit-2 when the switches S\u2081 and S\u2082 are in open conditions, respectively. Q\u2081 and Q\u2082 are the power dissipations in circuit-1 and circuit-2 when the switches S\u2081 and S\u2082 are in closed conditions, respectively.
Which of the following statement(s) is(are) correct?`,o:["When a voltage source of 6 V is connected across A and B in both circuits, P\u2081 < P\u2082.","When a constant current source of 2 amp is connected across A and B in both circuits, P\u2081 > P\u2082.","When a voltage source of 6 V is connected across A and B in Circuit-1, Q\u2081 > P\u2081.","When a constant current source of 2 amp is connected across A and B in both circuits, Q\u2082 < Q\u2081."],c:[0,1,2],s:`First find the resistance between A and B for each case.
Circuit-1: S\u2081 is connected across R\u2081. With S\u2081 open, R\u2081 is in the circuit. The lower part is R\u2082 + R\u2083 = 5 \u03A9 in parallel with R\u2081/2 = 0.5 \u03A9, which is (5 \xD7 0.5)/5.5 = 5/11 \u03A9. So with S\u2081 open: R = 1 + 5/11 = 16/11 \u03A9 \u2248 1.455 \u03A9. With S\u2081 closed it shorts R\u2081: R = 5/11 \u03A9 \u2248 0.455 \u03A9.
Circuit-2: R\u2081, R\u2082, R\u2083 are in parallel: 1/R = 1 + 1/2 + 1/3 = 11/6, so R = 6/11 \u03A9 \u2248 0.545 \u03A9 with S\u2082 open. With S\u2082 closed, 2R\u2083 = 6 \u03A9 is also in parallel: 1/R = 11/6 + 1/6 = 2, so R = 0.5 \u03A9.
(a) At fixed 6 V, P = V\xB2/R. P\u2081 = 36/1.455 \u2248 24.8 W and P\u2082 = 36/0.545 = 66 W, so P\u2081 < P\u2082. True.
(b) At a fixed current of 2 A, P = I\xB2R. P\u2081 = 4 \xD7 1.455 = 5.8 W and P\u2082 = 4 \xD7 0.545 = 2.2 W, so P\u2081 > P\u2082. True.
(c) At 6 V in circuit-1: Q\u2081 = 36/0.455 = 79 W > P\u2081 = 24.8 W. True.
(d) At 2 A: Q\u2081 = 4 \xD7 0.455 = 1.82 W and Q\u2082 = 4 \xD7 0.5 = 2 W, so Q\u2082 = 2 W is greater than Q\u2081 = 1.82 W. The printed statement Q\u2082 < Q\u2081 is therefore false.
So (a), (b) and (c) are correct.`,fig:{w:440,h:195,alt:"Circuit-1: R1 with switch S1 across it, in series with R2 plus R3 in parallel with R1 over 2; Circuit-2: R1, R2, R3 in parallel with a branch of switch S2 and 2R3",d:[["t",0,16,"Circuit-1",""],["l",25,45,25,140],["l",25,45,50.5,45],["p","M50.5 45 L53.3 49 L59 41 L64.7 49 L70.3 41 L76 49 L81.7 41 L84.5 45"],["l",84.5,45,110,45],["t",67,34,"R\u2081","m"],["c",110,45,2.2,"F"],["l",110,45,110,85],["l",110,45,124.5,45],["p","M124.5 45 L127.5 49 L133.5 41 L139.5 49 L145.5 41 L151.5 49 L157.5 41 L160.5 45"],["l",160.5,45,175,45],["t",142,34,"R\u2082","m"],["l",175,45,197,45],["p","M197 45 L200 49 L206 41 L212 49 L218 41 L224 49 L230 41 L233 45"],["l",233,45,255,45],["t",215,34,"R\u2083","m"],["l",255,45,255,140],["l",25,85,45,85],["l",45,85,64,68],["l",70,85,110,85],["c",110,85,2.2,"F"],["t",46,106,"S\u2081","m"],["l",110,85,162.5,85],["p","M162.5 85 L165.8 89 L172.5 81 L179.2 89 L185.8 81 L192.5 89 L199.2 81 L202.5 85"],["l",202.5,85,255,85],["t",182,106,"R\u2081/2","m"],["c",255,85,2.2,"F"],["l",25,140,126,140],["c",126,140,2.2,"F"],["c",148,140,2.2,"F"],["l",148,140,255,140],["t",126,158,"A","m"],["t",148,158,"B","m"],["t",305,184,"Circuit-2",""],["l",315,25,315,130],["l",405,25,405,130],["l",315,25,342,25],["p","M342 25 L345 29 L351 21 L357 29 L363 21 L369 29 L375 21 L378 25"],["l",378,25,405,25],["t",360,15,"R\u2081","m"],["l",315,55,342,55],["p","M342 55 L345 59 L351 51 L357 59 L363 51 L369 59 L375 51 L378 55"],["l",378,55,405,55],["t",360,45,"R\u2082","m"],["l",315,85,342,85],["p","M342 85 L345 89 L351 81 L357 89 L363 81 L369 89 L375 81 L378 85"],["l",378,85,405,85],["t",360,75,"R\u2083","m"],["l",315,115,327,115],["l",327,115,339,102],["l",345,115,353,115],["l",353,115,364,115],["p","M364 115 L366.5 119 L371.5 111 L376.5 119 L381.5 111 L386.5 119 L391.5 111 L394 115"],["l",394,115,405,115],["t",379,106,"2R\u2083","m"],["t",333,133,"S\u2082","m"],["l",315,130,315,150],["l",315,150,350,150],["l",405,130,405,150],["l",405,150,370,150],["c",350,150,2.2,"F"],["c",370,150,2.2,"F"],["t",350,168,"A","m"],["t",370,168,"B","m"]]}},{pn:405,y:"JEE Advanced 2016",tp:"cheat",st:"Joule heating and fuses",q:"An incandescent bulb has a thin filament of tungsten that is heated to high temperature by passing an electric current. The hot filament emits black-body radiation. The filament is observed to break up at random locations after a sufficiently long time of operation due to non-uniform evaporation of tungsten from the filament. If the bulb is powered at constant voltage, which of the following statement(s) is(are) true?",o:["The temperature distribution over the filament is uniform","The resistance over small sections of the filament decreases with time","The filament emits more light at higher band of frequencies before it breaks up","The filament consumes less electrical power towards the end of the life of the bulb"],c:[2,3],s:`Parts of the filament that are slightly thinner have a higher resistance, so more heat is produced there and they get hotter and lose tungsten faster. The temperature is therefore not uniform: (a) is false.
As tungsten evaporates, the filament gets thinner in places. Thinner means a smaller cross-section, so the resistance of those small sections increases with time: (b) is false.
The thin, hotter sections are at a higher temperature. A hotter black body emits more at higher frequencies (the peak of its spectrum shifts to higher frequency), so (c) is true.
The resistance of the whole filament increases with time, and at constant voltage P = V\xB2/R, so the power consumed falls towards the end of life: (d) is true.
So (c) and (d) are correct.`},{pn:406,y:"JEE Advanced 2014",tp:"cheat",st:"Joule heating and fuses",q:"Heater of an electric kettle is made of a wire of length L and diameter d. It takes 4 minutes to raise the temperature of 0.5 kg water by 40 K. This heater is replaced by a new heater having two wires of the same material, each of length L and diameter 2d. The way these wires are connected is given in the options. How much time in minutes will it take to raise the temperature of the same amount of water by 40 K?",o:["4 if wires are in parallel","2 if wires are in series","1 if wires are in series","0.5 if wires are in parallel"],c:[1,3],s:`The heat needed is the same, so the time is inversely proportional to the power, and P = V\xB2/R at the same supply voltage.
Original wire: resistance R = \u03C1L/(\u03C0d\xB2/4) and time 4 min.
Each new wire has diameter 2d, so its area is 4 times larger. Its resistance is R/4.
In series the heater has R/4 + R/4 = R/2, so the power is 2P and the time is 4/2 = 2 min. Option (b) is correct.
In parallel the heater has (R/4)/2 = R/8, so the power is 8P and the time is 4/8 = 0.5 min. Option (d) is correct.
So (b) and (d) are correct.`},{pn:407,y:"IIT-JEE 2009",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"For the circuit shown in the figure (R\u2081 = 2 k\u03A9, R\u2082 = 6 k\u03A9, R_L = 1.5 k\u03A9, battery 24 V),",o:["the current I through the battery is 7.5 mA","the potential difference across R_L is 18 V","ratio of powers dissipated in R\u2081 and R\u2082 is 3","if R\u2081 and R\u2082 are interchanged, magnitude of the power dissipated in R_L will decrease by a factor of 9"],c:[0,3],s:`R\u2082 and R_L are in parallel: (6 \xD7 1.5)/(6 + 1.5) = 1.2 k\u03A9. This is in series with R\u2081 = 2 k\u03A9, so the total is 3.2 k\u03A9.
(a) I = 24 V/3.2 k\u03A9 = 7.5 mA. True.
(b) The voltage across the parallel pair (and so across R_L) = I \xD7 1.2 k\u03A9 = 9 V, not 18 V. False.
(c) Power in R\u2081 = I\xB2R\u2081 = (7.5 mA)\xB2 \xD7 2 k\u03A9 = 112.5 mW. Power in R\u2082 = 9\xB2/6 k\u03A9 = 13.5 mW. The ratio is 8.33, not 3. False.
(d) Interchanged: R\u2081 = 6 k\u03A9 in series, and R\u2082 = 2 k\u03A9 in parallel with R_L: (2 \xD7 1.5)/3.5 = 6/7 k\u03A9. Total = 6 + 6/7 = 48/7 k\u03A9, so I = 24 \xD7 7/48 = 3.5 mA and the voltage across R_L = 3.5 mA \xD7 6/7 k\u03A9 = 3 V.
Original power in R_L = 9\xB2/1.5 k\u03A9 = 54 mW; new power = 3\xB2/1.5 k\u03A9 = 6 mW. The decrease is by a factor 54/6 = 9. True.
So (a) and (d) are correct.`,fig:{w:300,h:190,alt:"A 24 volt battery driving R1 of 2 kilo-ohm in series with R2 of 6 kilo-ohm and RL of 1.5 kilo-ohm in parallel",d:[["l",40,30,40,70],["l",28,76,52,76],["l",34,83,46,83,"B"],["l",40,83,40,160],["t",58,112,"24 V",""],["l",40,160,250,160],["l",40,30,60,30],["l",60,30,85,30],["p","M85 30 L88.3 34 L95 26 L101.7 34 L108.3 26 L115 34 L121.7 26 L125 30"],["l",125,30,150,30],["t",105,18,"R\u2081","m"],["t",105,50,"2 k\u03A9","m"],["l",150,30,250,30],["c",170,30,2.2,"F"],["c",170,160,2.2,"F"],["l",170,30,170,73],["p","M170 73 L166 76.7 L174 84 L166 91.3 L174 98.7 L166 106 L174 113.3 L170 117"],["l",170,117,170,160],["t",160,95,"R\u2082","e"],["t",160,111,"6 k\u03A9","e"],["l",250,30,250,50],["l",250,30,250,73],["p","M250 73 L246 76.7 L254 84 L246 91.3 L254 98.7 L246 106 L254 113.3 L250 117"],["l",250,117,250,160],["t",274,92,"R","e"],["t",275,95,"L","s"],["t",276,108,"1.5 k\u03A9","m"],["c",250,30,2.2,"F"],["c",250,160,2.2,"F"],["l",40,125,40,118],["p","M40 108 L36 118 L44 118 Z","F"],["t",30,116,"I","e"]]}},{pn:408,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"A heater is designed to operate with a power of 1000 W in a 100 V line. It is connected in combination with a resistance of 10 \u03A9 and a resistance R, to a 100 V mains as shown in figure. For the heater to operate at 62.5 W, the value of R should be ______ \u03A9.",n:5,s:`Resistance of the heater from its rating: R_h = V\xB2/P = 100\xB2/1000 = 10 \u03A9.
The heater and R are in parallel (between B and C), and this pair is in series with the 10 \u03A9 resistor.
For the heater to work at 62.5 W: V_h\xB2/10 = 62.5, so V_h\xB2 = 625 and V_h = 25 V.
The current through the heater is 25/10 = 2.5 A.
The 10 \u03A9 resistor must drop the rest of the 100 V: 100 \u2212 25 = 75 V, so the total current is 75/10 = 7.5 A.
The current through R is 7.5 \u2212 2.5 = 5 A, at 25 V, so R = 25/5 = 5 \u03A9.`,fig:{w:310,h:215,alt:"A 10 ohm resistor in series with a heater and a resistor R connected in parallel, all across a 100 volt mains",d:[["l",60,50,60,60],["l",60,60,60,93],["p","M60 93 L56 95.8 L64 101.5 L56 107.2 L64 112.8 L56 118.5 L64 124.2 L60 127"],["l",60,127,60,160],["t",46,112,"10 \u03A9","e"],["l",60,160,60,190],["l",60,50,110,50],["c",110,50,2.2,"F"],["l",110,50,125,50],["r",125,38,60,24],["t",155,54,"heater","m"],["l",185,50,205,50],["c",205,50,2.2,"F"],["l",205,50,260,50],["l",260,50,260,190],["l",110,50,110,110],["l",110,110,128,110],["l",128,110,143,110],["p","M143 110 L145.5 114 L150.5 106 L155.5 114 L160.5 106 L165.5 114 L170.5 106 L173 110"],["l",173,110,188,110],["t",158,130,"R","m"],["l",188,110,205,110],["l",205,110,205,50],["t",110,38,"B","e"],["t",210,38,"C",""],["l",60,190,120,190],["c",120,190,2.2,"F"],["c",200,190,2.2,"F"],["t",160,194,"100 V","m"],["l",200,190,260,190]]}},{pn:409,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"In the following circuit, the battery has an emf of 2 V and an internal resistance of (2)/(3) \u03A9. The power consumption in the entire circuit is ____ W.",n:3,s:`Power consumed in the whole circuit (external resistors plus the battery's own resistance) is P = EI, where I = E/(R_ext + r).
The left and right sides of the square are plain wires, so the two left corners are one point X and the two right corners are one point Y (solution figure).
Between X and Y there are three paths in parallel: the top 2 \u03A9, the bottom 2 \u03A9, and the path through the centre: two 2 \u03A9 in parallel to the centre (1 \u03A9) and two more (1 \u03A9) to Y, so 2 \u03A9.
R_ext = 2 \u2225 2 \u2225 2 = 2/3 \u03A9.
I = 2/(2/3 + 2/3) = 1.5 A, so P = EI = 2 \xD7 1.5 = 3 W.
Note: in the crop a long wire is drawn across the square at the level of the centre, and it crosses the sides without a dot. It is read as the two leads of the battery joining the left and right sides; the other reading would short the battery (P = E\xB2/r = 6 W). The reading used here agrees with the printed key 3.`,fig:{w:320,h:225,alt:"A 2 V battery with internal resistance 2/3 ohm across a network: a square frame with 2 ohm in the top and bottom sides and four 2 ohm resistors from the centre to the four corners",d:[["l",60,30,60,130],["l",240,30,240,130],["p","M60 30 L130 30 L133.3 25 L140 35 L146.7 25 L153.3 35 L160 25 L166.7 35 L170 30 L240 30"],["t",150,20,"2 \u03A9","m"],["p","M60 130 L130 130 L133.3 125 L140 135 L146.7 125 L153.3 135 L160 125 L166.7 135 L170 130 L240 130"],["t",150,152,"2 \u03A9","m"],["p","M150 80 L126.9 67.1 L120.8 69.5 L118.4 56.7 L106.2 61.4 L103.8 48.6 L91.6 53.3 L89.2 40.5 L83.1 42.9 L60 30"],["p","M150 80 L173.1 67.1 L174.4 60.7 L186.5 65.4 L188.9 52.7 L201.1 57.3 L203.5 44.6 L215.6 49.3 L216.9 42.9 L240 30"],["p","M150 80 L126.9 92.9 L125.6 99.3 L113.5 94.6 L111.1 107.3 L98.9 102.7 L96.5 115.4 L84.4 110.7 L83.1 117.1 L60 130"],["p","M150 80 L173.1 92.9 L179.2 90.5 L181.6 103.3 L193.8 98.6 L196.2 111.4 L208.4 106.7 L210.8 119.5 L216.9 117.1 L240 130"],["c",150,80,2.6,"F"],["l",20,80,60,80],["l",240,80,280,80],["c",60,80,2.6,"F"],["c",240,80,2.6,"F"],["l",20,80,20,195],["l",280,80,280,195],["l",20,195,77,195],["l",77,184,77,206],["l",83,189,83,201,"B"],["l",83,195,150,195],["t",80,218,"2 V","m"],["p","M150 195 L193 195 L196.7 190 L204 200 L211.3 190 L218.7 200 L226 190 L233.3 200 L237 195 L280 195"],["t",215,218,"2/3 \u03A9","m"],["t",122,50,"2 \u03A9","m"],["t",178,50,"2 \u03A9","m"],["t",122,120,"2 \u03A9","m"],["t",178,120,"2 \u03A9","m"]]},sfig:{w:300,h:200,alt:"Equivalent circuit: three 2 ohm resistors in parallel between the points X and Y",d:[["c",70,30,2.2,"F"],["c",230,30,2.2,"F"],["l",70,50,118,50],["p","M118.0 50.0 L123.3 55.0 L134.0 45.0 L144.7 55.0 L155.3 45.0 L166.0 55.0 L176.7 45.0 L182.0 50.0"],["l",182,50,230,50],["l",70,100,118,100],["p","M118.0 100.0 L123.3 105.0 L134.0 95.0 L144.7 105.0 L155.3 95.0 L166.0 105.0 L176.7 95.0 L182.0 100.0"],["l",182,100,230,100],["l",70,150,118,150],["p","M118.0 150.0 L123.3 155.0 L134.0 145.0 L144.7 155.0 L155.3 145.0 L166.0 155.0 L176.7 145.0 L182.0 150.0"],["l",182,150,230,150],["l",70,30,70,50],["l",70,50,70,150],["l",230,30,230,150],["t",150,42,"2 \u03A9","m"],["t",150,92,"2 \u03A9","m"],["t",150,142,"2 \u03A9","m"],["t",60,28,"X","e"],["t",240,28,"Y",""]]}},{pn:410,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 13 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"A potential V\u2080 is applied across a uniform wire of resistance R. The power dissipation is P\u2081. The wire is then cut into two equal halves and a potential of V\u2080 is applied across the length of each half. The total power dissipation across two wires is P\u2082. The ratio P\u2082 : P\u2081 is \u221Ax : 1. The value of x is ____.",n:16,s:`First case: P\u2081 = V\u2080\xB2/R.
Each half has resistance R/2, and each has V\u2080 across it: power in one half = V\u2080\xB2/(R/2) = 2V\u2080\xB2/R.
Two halves: P\u2082 = 2 \xD7 2V\u2080\xB2/R = 4V\u2080\xB2/R.
P\u2082 : P\u2081 = 4 : 1 = \u221A16 : 1, so x = 16.`},{pn:411,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"An electrical bulb rated 220 V, 100 W, is connected in series with another bulb rated 220 V, 60 W. If the voltage across the combination is 220 V, the power consumed by the 100 W bulb will be about ____ W.",n:14,s:`From P = V\xB2/R, the resistance of each bulb: R\u2081\u2080\u2080 = 220\xB2/100 = 484 \u03A9 and R\u2086\u2080 = 220\xB2/60 \u2248 806.7 \u03A9.
In series the same current flows: I = 220/(484 + 806.7) = 220/1290.7 \u2248 0.1704 A.
Power in the 100 W bulb: P = I\xB2R = (0.1704)\xB2 \xD7 484 \u2248 14.06 W.
So the power is about 14 W.`},{pn:412,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cheat",st:"Joule heating and fuses",q:"A resistor develops 300 J of thermal energy in 15 s, when a current of 2 A is passed through it. If the current increases to 3 A, the energy developed in 10 s is ____ J.",n:450,s:`Joule heating: H = I\xB2Rt.
First case: 300 = 2\xB2 \xD7 R \xD7 15 = 60R, so R = 5 \u03A9.
Second case: H\u2032 = 3\xB2 \xD7 5 \xD7 10 = 450 J.
Or by ratio: H\u2032/H = (3/2)\xB2 \xD7 (10/15) = 2.25 \xD7 2/3 = 1.5, so H\u2032 = 450 J.`},{pn:413,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cheat",st:"Joule heating and fuses",q:"Two coils require 20 minutes and 60 minutes respectively to produce same amount of heat energy when connected separately to the same source. If they are connected in parallel arrangement to the same source, the time required to produce same amount of heat by the combination of coils, will be ____ min.",n:15,s:`The same heat H = V\xB2t/R is needed with the same V, so t is proportional to R: R\u2081 \u221D 20 and R\u2082 \u221D 60.
In parallel 1/R = 1/R\u2081 + 1/R\u2082, so the time to give the same heat is given by 1/t = 1/t\u2081 + 1/t\u2082.
1/t = 1/20 + 1/60 = 4/60 = 1/15.
So t = 15 min.`},{pn:414,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 31 Aug \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cheat",st:"Joule heating and fuses",q:"A resistor dissipates 192 J of energy in 1 s when a current of 4 A is passed through it. Now, when the current is doubled, the amount of thermal energy dissipated in 5 s is ____ J.",n:3840,s:`Joule heating: H = I\xB2Rt.
First case: 192 = 4\xB2 \xD7 R \xD7 1, so R = 12 \u03A9.
Doubled current: I\u2032 = 8 A and t\u2032 = 5 s.
H\u2032 = 8\xB2 \xD7 12 \xD7 5 = 3840 J.`},{pn:415,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 1 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"A uniform heating wire of resistance 36 \u03A9 is connected across a potential difference of 240 V. The wire is then cut into half and a potential difference of 240 V is applied across each half separately. The ratio of power dissipation in first case to the total power dissipation in the second case would be 1 : x where x is ____.",n:4,s:`First case: P\u2081 = V\xB2/R = 240\xB2/36 = 1600 W.
Each half has R/2 = 18 \u03A9 and has 240 V across it: P = 240\xB2/18 = 3200 W each.
Total in the second case: P\u2082 = 2 \xD7 3200 = 6400 W.
P\u2081 : P\u2082 = 1600 : 6400 = 1 : 4, so x = 4.`},{pn:416,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"An electric bulb rated as 200 W at 100 V is used in a circuit having 200 V supply. The resistance R that must be put in series with the bulb so that the bulb delivers the same power is ____ \u03A9.",n:50,s:`The bulb's rating gives its working current: I = P/V = 200/100 = 2 A, and its resistance R_b = V\xB2/P = 100\xB2/200 = 50 \u03A9.
To deliver the same power the bulb must carry the same 2 A, so the whole circuit must have a total resistance of 200 V/2 A = 100 \u03A9.
R = 100 \u2212 50 = 50 \u03A9.`},{pn:417,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 16 Mar \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"cheat",st:"Joule heating and fuses",q:"The energy dissipated by a resistor is 10 mJ in 1 s when an electric current of 2 mA flows through it. The resistance is ____ \u03A9. (Round off to the nearest integer)",n:2500,s:`Joule heating: H = I\xB2Rt.
10 mJ = 10 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 J = 10\u207B\xB2 J and I = 2 mA = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 A.
R = H/(I\xB2t) = 10\u207B\xB2/(4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 1) = 2500 \u03A9.`},{pn:418,y:"IIT-JEE 2010",tp:"ccel",st:"Grouping of cells",q:"When two identical batteries of internal resistance 1 \u03A9 each are connected in series across a resistor R, the rate of heat produced in R is J\u2081. When the same batteries are connected in parallel across R, the rate is J\u2082. If J\u2081 = 2.25 J\u2082 then the value of R in \u03A9 is ____.",n:4,s:`Let each battery have emf E and internal resistance 1 \u03A9.
Series: emf 2E, internal resistance 2 \u03A9, so I\u2081 = 2E/(R + 2) and J\u2081 = I\u2081\xB2R = 4E\xB2R/(R + 2)\xB2.
Parallel: emf E, internal resistance 1/2 \u03A9, so I\u2082 = E/(R + 0.5) and J\u2082 = E\xB2R/(R + 0.5)\xB2.
J\u2081/J\u2082 = 4(R + 0.5)\xB2/(R + 2)\xB2 = 2.25, so 2(R + 0.5)/(R + 2) = 1.5.
2R + 1 = 1.5R + 3, so 0.5R = 2 and R = 4 \u03A9.`},{pn:419,y:"IIT-JEE 1979",tp:"cheat",st:"Joule heating and fuses",q:`A copper wire having cross-sectional area of 0.5 mm\xB2 and a length of 0.1 metre is initially at 25\xB0C and is thermally insulated from the surrounding. If a current of 10 ampere is set up in this wire, (i) find the time in which the wire will start melting. The change of resistance with the temperature of the wire may be neglected. (ii) What will this time be, if the length of the wire is doubled?
Melting point of copper = 1075\xB0C. Specific resistance of copper = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 \u03A9 m. Density of copper = 9 \xD7 10\xB3 kg/m\xB3. Specific heat of copper = 9 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 cal/kg\xB0C (as printed).`,a:"(i) about 558 s (9 min 18 s); (ii) the same, 558 s",s:`All the electrical heat I\xB2Rt goes into warming the wire from 25\xB0C to 1075\xB0C (\u0394T = 1050\xB0C), because it is insulated.
Resistance: R = \u03C1l/A = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 \xD7 0.1/(0.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076) = 3.2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \u03A9. Heating power = I\xB2R = 100 \xD7 3.2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 = 0.32 W.
Mass: m = (area)(length)(density) = 0.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 0.1 \xD7 9 \xD7 10\xB3 = 4.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 kg.
Specific heat: the crop prints 9 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 cal/kg\xB0C, but copper's value is 0.09 cal per gram per \xB0C = 90 cal/kg\xB0C = 378 J/kg\xB0C (4.2 J per cal). This is the value used.
Heat needed: Q = mc\u0394T = 4.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \xD7 378 \xD7 1050 \u2248 178.6 J.
t = Q/(I\xB2R) = 178.6/0.32 \u2248 558 s \u2248 9 min 18 s.
(ii) If the length is doubled, R doubles and the mass doubles too: t = mc\u0394T/(I\xB2R) is unchanged. So the time is again 558 s.
Note: the unit of c is misprinted in the crop; the answer 558 s agrees with the book's worked answer.`},{pn:420,y:"IIT-JEE 1979",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"A 25 watt and a 100 watt bulb are joined in series and connected to the mains. Which bulb will grow brighter?",a:"The 25 W bulb",s:`Both bulbs are rated for the same mains voltage V, so the resistance of each is R = V\xB2/P.
R\u2082\u2085 = V\xB2/25 is four times R\u2081\u2080\u2080 = V\xB2/100.
In series the same current flows through both, and the power in each is I\xB2R.
The larger resistance gets the larger power, so the 25 W bulb dissipates 4 times as much as the 100 W bulb.
The 25 W bulb glows brighter.`},{pn:421,y:"IIT-JEE 1978",tp:"cheat",st:"Joule heating and fuses",q:"A room is maintained at 20\xB0C by a heater of resistance 20 ohm connected to 200 volt mains. The temperature is uniform throughout the room and the heat is transmitted through a glass window of area 1 m\xB2 and thickness 0.2 cm. Calculate the temperature outside. Thermal conductivity of glass is 0.2 cal m\u207B\xB9 s\u207B\xB9 (\xB0C)\u207B\xB9 and mechanical equivalent of heat is 4.2 J cal\u207B\xB9.",a:"15.24\xB0C",s:`In the steady state, the heat given by the heater per second equals the heat conducted through the window per second.
Heater power: P = V\xB2/R = 200\xB2/20 = 2000 W = 2000 J/s = 2000/4.2 = 476.2 cal/s.
Conduction through the window: Q/t = KA\u0394T/d = 0.2 \xD7 1 \xD7 (20 \u2212 T)/0.002 = 100(20 \u2212 T) cal/s.
Equate: 100(20 \u2212 T) = 476.2, so 20 \u2212 T = 4.76.
T = 20 \u2212 4.76 = 15.24\xB0C.`},{pn:422,y:"IIT-JEE 1978",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"A heater is designed to operate with a power of 1000 watt in a 100 V line. It is connected in combination with a resistance of 10 \u03A9 and a resistance R, to a 100 V mains as shown in the figure. What will be the value of R so that the heater operates with a power of 62.5 W?",a:"5 \u03A9",s:`Heater resistance: R_h = V\xB2/P = 100\xB2/1000 = 10 \u03A9.
For 62.5 W, the heater current is I_h = \u221A(P/R_h) = \u221A(62.5/10) = 2.5 A, so the heater voltage is 2.5 \xD7 10 = 25 V.
The 10 \u03A9 resistor is in series with the heater\u2013R pair, so it has the remaining 100 \u2212 25 = 75 V: the total current is 75/10 = 7.5 A.
Current through R = 7.5 \u2212 2.5 = 5 A, and R has the same 25 V as the heater (they are in parallel).
R = 25/5 = 5 \u03A9.`,fig:{w:320,h:190,alt:"Circuit: a 10 ohm resistor in series with a heater and a resistor R in parallel, across a 100 V supply",d:[["l",40,40,40,150],["p","M40 40 L60 40 L63.3 35 L70 45 L76.7 35 L83.3 45 L90 35 L96.7 45 L100 40 L120 40"],["t",80,30,"10 \u03A9","m"],["c",130,40,2.6,"F"],["t",130,28,"B","m"],["l",120,40,130,40],["l",130,40,150,40],["r",150,30,80,20],["t",190,45,"Heater","m"],["l",230,40,260,40],["c",260,40,2.6,"F"],["t",260,28,"C","m"],["l",130,40,130,90],["p","M130 90 L165 90 L170 85 L180 95 L190 85 L200 95 L210 85 L220 95 L225 90 L260 90"],["t",195,112,"R","m"],["l",260,90,260,40],["l",260,40,290,40],["l",290,40,290,150],["c",40,150,2.6,"F"],["c",290,150,2.6,"F"],["t",165,155,"100 V","m"]]}},{pn:423,y:"IIT-JEE 1987",tp:"cheat",st:"Electrical power and energy",q:"An electric bulb rated for 500 watt at 100 volt is used in a circuit having a 200 volt supply. The resistance R that must be put in series with the bulb, so that the bulb delivers 500 watt is ______ ohm.",n:20,s:`At its rating the bulb draws I = P/V = 500/100 = 5 A, and its resistance is R_b = 100\xB2/500 = 20 \u03A9.
To deliver 500 W the bulb must still have 5 A flowing through it.
Total resistance on 200 V: 200/5 = 40 \u03A9.
R = 40 \u2212 20 = 20 \u03A9.`},{pn:424,y:"AIEEE 2005",tp:"cdrf",st:"Current and charge",q:"Two voltameters, one of copper and another of silver, are joined in parallel. When a total charge q flows through the voltameters, equal amount of metals are deposited. If the electrochemical equivalents of copper and silver are z\u2081 and z\u2082 respectively, the charge which flows through the silver voltameter is",o:["q (z\u2081)/(z\u2082)","q (z\u2082)/(z\u2081)","(q)/(1 + (z\u2081)/(z\u2082))","(q)/(1 + (z\u2082)/(z\u2081))"],c:3,s:`Faraday's first law: mass deposited m = zq, where q is the charge through that voltameter.
Let q\u2081 flow through copper and q\u2082 through silver, so q\u2081 + q\u2082 = q.
Equal masses: z\u2081q\u2081 = z\u2082q\u2082, so q\u2081 = (z\u2082/z\u2081)q\u2082.
q = q\u2082(1 + z\u2082/z\u2081), so q\u2082 = q/(1 + z\u2082/z\u2081).
This is option (d).`},{pn:425,y:"AIEEE 2004",tp:"cdrf",st:"Current and charge",q:"The electrochemical equivalent of a metal is 3.3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 kg per coulomb. The mass of the metal liberated at the cathode when a 3 A current is passed for 2 second will be",o:["19.8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 kg","9.9 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 kg","6.6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 kg","1.1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 kg"],c:0,s:`Faraday's law: m = zIt.
Charge: q = It = 3 \xD7 2 = 6 C.
m = 3.3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 6 = 19.8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 kg.
This is option (a).`},{pn:426,y:"AIEEE 2003",tp:"cdrf",st:"Current and charge",q:"The negative Zn pole of a Daniell cell, sending a constant current through a circuit, decreases in mass by 0.13 g in 30 minutes. If the electrochemical equivalent of Zn and Cu are 32.5 and 31.5 respectively, the increase in the mass of the positive Cu pole in this time is",o:["0.180 g","0.141 g","0.126 g","0.242 g"],c:2,s:`The same current flows for the same time through both poles, so the same charge q passes.
m = zq, so m_Cu/m_Zn = z_Cu/z_Zn = 31.5/32.5.
m_Cu = 0.13 \xD7 31.5/32.5 = 0.126 g.
This is option (c).`},{pn:427,y:"AIEEE 2002",tp:"cdrf",st:"Current and charge",q:"The mass of a product liberated on anode in an electrochemical cell depends on (where t is the time period for which the current is passed)",o:["(It)^(1/2)","It","I/t","I\xB2t"],c:1,s:`By Faraday's first law the mass liberated is m = zq, where q is the charge that passes.
Charge q = It, so m is proportional to It (for a constant current).
This is option (b).`},{pn:428,y:"IIT-JEE 1980",tp:"cdrf",st:"Current and charge",q:"In an electrolytic solution the electric current is mainly due to the movement of free electrons. (True or False)",o:["True","False"],c:1,s:`An electrolyte conducts by ions: positive ions move towards the cathode and negative ions towards the anode.
Free electrons move in metals, not in the solution.
So the statement is False.`},{pn:429,y:"AIEEE 2006",tp:"ccel",st:"EMF and internal resistance",q:"A thermocouple is made from two metals, antimony and bismuth. If one junction of the couple is kept hot and the other is kept cold then, an electric current will",o:["flow from antimony to bismuth at the cold junction","flow from antimony to bismuth at the hot junction","flow from bismuth to antimony at the cold junction","not flow through the thermocouple"],c:0,s:`A temperature difference between the two junctions of different metals produces a thermo-emf (Seebeck effect), so a current flows.
Thermoelectric series: Bi, ..., Cu, ..., Sb (bismuth at one end, antimony at the other).
Rule: at the hot junction the current goes from the metal earlier in the series to the later one, that is from Bi to Sb.
The current goes round the loop, so through the cold junction it goes from Sb to Bi.
So the current flows from antimony to bismuth at the cold junction, option (a).`},{pn:430,y:"AIEEE 2004",tp:"ccel",st:"EMF and internal resistance",q:"The thermo emf of a thermocouple varies with the temperature \u03B8 of the hot junction as E = a\u03B8 + b\u03B8\xB2 in volt where the ratio a/b is 700\xB0C. If the cold junction is kept at 0\xB0C, then the neutral temperature is",o:["700\xB0C","350\xB0C","1400\xB0C","no neutral temperature is possible for this thermocouple."],c:3,s:`The neutral temperature is where the thermo emf is maximum: dE/d\u03B8 = 0.
dE/d\u03B8 = a + 2b\u03B8 = 0, so \u03B8_n = \u2212a/(2b).
With a/b = +700\xB0C this gives \u03B8_n = \u2212350\xB0C.
A negative temperature on this scale (the hot junction is above the 0\xB0C cold junction) is not possible, so there is no neutral temperature.
This is option (d).`}]})();
